// (v18.73 W) THE REASON HE COULD NEVER TELL WHICH BUILD WAS RUNNING.
// This said "18.57" while the filename said v18.73 and the header comment said
// v18.72. MT5 shows #property version in Navigator / About, so the terminal has
// been reporting 18.57 for sixteen builds. Every time I asked him "are you on
// 18.70 or 18.72?" the answer was NOT OBTAINABLE from his terminal -- I was
// asking a question the code made impossible to answer, and then reasoning about
// his tape without knowing which code produced it. Line 388 notes a previous
// session already fixed a stale duplicate of this property, so it has regressed
// before. It is now also PRINTED at OnInit and shown on the panel, so a wrong
// build is visible in two places instead of zero.
#define EA_BUILD "v19.18"

// (v18.82 AI) FORWARD PROTOTYPES. My own first attempt at this fix put the two
// definitions AFTER their first use at ~line 5232, which MQL5 rejects. Caught by
// the declaration-order script, not by reading. Prototypes here, bodies below.
void BalanceTier(double &lo, double &hi);
int SafeHighest(string sym, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int mode, int count, int start);
int SafeLowest (string sym, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int mode, int count, int start);
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v16.23 — THE REGIME ENGINE PASS (research-backed; the screenshots' fix):
//   Root insight from the trader's own charts: the £12 winner and the
//   bleeding choppy trades are the SAME machine in different regimes.
//   Trending gold -> a birth flip is a 90%+ winner to ride; ranging gold
//   -> the same flip is a coin-flip that just pays spread. Every credible
//   gold-Supertrend source agrees the biggest single edge is detecting
//   TRENDING vs RANGING and behaving differently. So (all NEW this pass):
//   #1/#4 REGIME ENGINE (T_RegimeEngine) — ADX (directional strength) +
//     Choppiness Index (range-boundedness) classify each timeframe NOW as
//     trend/neutral/range. Never blocks a signal — changes how it's sized
//     and exited. Mirrored on the Pine chart (regime + chopIdx).
//   #8 RANGE-SCALP FAST BANK — a trade BORN in a range banks a small
//     confirmed profit (T_RangeScalpR) instead of holding for a trend that
//     isn't coming (the bad-screenshot case). If the range resolves into a
//     trend mid-trade it releases and rides. Trend/neutral trades unchanged.
//   #3 ADAPTIVE TRAIL — the Chandelier multiplier now tightens when ADX is
//     rising (bank the acceleration) and widens when falling (survive noise)
//     — fixes stock Supertrend's fixed-multiplier weakness.
//   #7 CONFLUENCE 4th-LOT now gated behind a trending regime — TFs aligned
//     in chop is not conviction, so the size-up won't fire in a range.
//   #9 VOLUME-CONFIRMED BIRTH (T_VolBirthSizing) — a flip on a volume surge
//     sizes up (real participation), on dead volume sizes down (noise).
//   #10 SELF-MONITORING (T_RegimeLearn) — accumulates realized R per regime
//     each session and leans size toward whichever is actually paying today,
//     bounded [0.6,1.4], reset daily. The EA "monitoring itself" to the goal.
//   Verified already-present and correct, integrated not duplicated: birth
//     entry is already on the flip bar (#2); the trailing SL in profit is the
//     Chandelier+peak-lock+step-lock stack (#5); the reversal-anticipation
//     exit is the peak-evidence + momentum-stall banks (#6). REJECTED fixed
//     Fib TPs (contradicts ride-to-opposite-signal). Both files verified
//     balanced. £150/day engraved as the target — NOT promised: a pure-range
//     day can't be forced to pay; the regime engine is what stops it bleeding.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v16.16 — REAL ANSWER TO "WHY BACK-TO-BACK LOSSES" (same session):
//   Diagnosed the actual mechanism: RecentFlips() tracks EACH timeframe's
//   own clock separately, so one 5-10min whipsaw event can trip a loss on
//   M1, then M3, then M5 in succession — each looks "fresh" from its own
//   clock, but the account sees 3 losses in a row from ONE bad patch.
//   FIX: new T_CrossTfChopPause — tracks TREND's real losses (any TF, any
//   reason) in a short rolling window (10 min); 2+ trips a brief pause
//   (5 min) on NEW entries only. Existing trades + the reclaim watch are
//   untouched. A few losses stay fine; this stops the cascade.
//   REVERTED v16.15's frequency-chasing defaults that were making this
//   worse: T_MinAdx 14→18, T_MaxSameDirFast 3→2, T_MaxTotalSameDir 5→3,
//   T_Cooldown 2→3. Lowering the ADX floor and letting all 3 fast clocks
//   stack directly fed the same-event cascade. Frequency toward £100
//   should come from taking every REAL trending flip cleanly (which the
//   entry side already does well), not from lowering the trend bar.
//   Also clarified: win-rate % is meaningless on its own here — average
//   loss is pennies-to-£1, average winner £3+, so expectancy (avg win ×
//   win% − avg loss × loss%) is what matters, not the raw percentage.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v16.14 — THE EXTRACTION PASS (max profit per signal; take every signal):
//   Goal reaffirmed with the trader: take EVERY real Pine flip on
//   M1/M3/M5 (+ confirmed M15), never gate one away, and squeeze the
//   most out of each — small banked off choppy legs, the fat middle off
//   average moves, the tip off big runs — with losses kept small.
//   FIX (silent, matches the loss forensics): TREND took counter-DEMA
//     flips at near-full size — the log said "reduced size" but only the
//     confidence score dented it, which a momentum-strong counter-DEMA
//     flip overcame. Added T_AgainstDemaRisk (TREND's mirror of SCALP's
//     SC_AgainstDemaRisk): counter-DEMA flips are still TAKEN, just sized
//     down materially. Smaller size on the documented losing cluster =
//     the "losses stay small" half of the goal, frequency unchanged.
//   FIX (hygiene): removed the latent `demaV <= 0 || ...` force-true-on-
//     zero at the TREND entry (the exact shape of bug #69); demaReady is
//     already guaranteed there, so it was unreachable but shouldn't sit
//     in the code where a future refactor could re-expose it. Corrected
//     three stale comments still claiming HouseBias uses "M15 and H1"
//     (it's M15-only since v16.12).
//   RESEARCH #1 — LIQUIDITY-SWEEP CONFIRMATION (T_UseLiquiditySweep): a
//     flip whose bar first swept a recent swing extreme and closed back
//     inside is a stop-run-and-reversal — the cleanest real turn. SIZE
//     nudge only (never a gate): it grades the signal UP so a swept flip
//     is sized larger, it never skips a signal.
//   RESEARCH #2 — SQUEEZE-RELEASE (T_UseSqueezeBoost): the first flip out
//     of a genuine volatility compression is a loaded-spring trend START.
//     Grades it UP and, crucially, EXEMPTS it from the low-ATR/isolation
//     penalties that used to quietly under-size a launch — so the machine
//     now takes MORE of the trend-starts it was dampening. Never a gate.
//   RESEARCH #3 — CHANDELIER VOLATILITY TIP-LOCK (T_ChandelierLock): an
//     extra stop floor at highest-high-since-entry − 3×ATR (mirror for
//     shorts), taken as the TIGHTER of every existing floor — can only
//     bank MORE, never loosen. Anchors to the real extreme and tightens
//     as ATR dries up near a top, banking closer to the tip on big runs.
//   EXTRACTION — RE-ENTRY CAP raised 2→4 (T_MaxReentriesPerTrend): a
//     persistent trend that pulls back several times is now rejoined more
//     times (each reclaim still evidence-gated), so the back half of a
//     big run isn't left on the table.
//   EXTRACTION — CHOP-STALL SMALL BANK (T_ChopStallBank): the choppy leg
//     that fades with NO reversal candle used to round-trip to scratch;
//     now, inside a recognised chop batch, small profit that stalls (no
//     new extreme for N bars) is banked. Clean runners are exempt (they
//     have few flips), so this only harvests the choppy money, never
//     clips a real trend. Re-entry watch still arms on the close.
//   All mirrored into the Pine (APEX 16.7) so both stay one system.
//   ── v16.15 (same session) — FREQUENCY PASS toward the £100 goal ──
//   Goal clarified: £50 is the FLOOR, £100 the goal, "as much as
//   possible" the real objective — so the machine must LEAK as little as
//   possible: take every real flip, bank what each offers. Hunted every
//   hard skip in Engine_Trend. KEPT the ones that skip genuinely negative-
//   expectancy trades (spread-vs-move, cost-aware, ATR-spike, the conf
//   floor the core rule always clears, the 1.6-ATR chase guard). LOOSENED
//   the ones that were costing real signals: T_MaxSameDirFast 2→3 (all
//   three fast clocks aligned is the STRONGEST trend — take it), 
//   T_MaxTotalSameDir 3→5 (pile into a truly aligned trend; equity % ceiling
//   still binds and 0.01 lots keep total risk tiny), T_MinAdx 18→14 (more
//   borderline flips; the cost gates still block the un-winnable), 
//   T_Cooldown 3→2. Converted the M15 LOCKDOWN from a hard SKIP into a
//   size-LEAN (T_M15CounterRisk): a fast flip fighting a super-confident
//   M15 is now TAKEN (smaller), honouring the locked "both directions on
//   fast TFs, never hard-block" rule. Note: on 0.01 lots "size down" == 
//   "take at 0.01" (can't go below the min lot), so these levers act as
//   TAKE-more, which is the intent. Every lever is an input — raise the
//   caps / ADX back up if a session shows chop bleeding spread.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.50 — THE "CATCH EVERY TREND" PASS (bug sweep + researched additions):
//   BUG #63 (THE REVERSAL DEADLOCK — a root cause of "misses the new
//     trend / enters late / never rides a full one"): the no-opposing
//     guard blocked ANY entry against the NET of open positions. Normal
//     reversal sequence: M1 flips DOWN, the M1 long closes instantly and
//     fast-rearms — but M3/M5 longs are still open (slower clocks lag,
//     that's not disagreement). The fresh M1 SHORT — the exact signal
//     this system exists to catch — was then blocked EVERY time until the
//     slower positions unwound; by then the new trend is half over.
//     Contradicted the engine's own per-timeframe design AND the locked
//     "slower clocks are context, never blockers" rule. Fixed: a fresh
//     TREND signal may oppose the net ONLY when every opposing position
//     is on a strictly SLOWER clock (expected lag). Same/faster-clock
//     opposition still blocks — the guard's real job (no blind
//     self-hedging in chop) fully intact. Plus #63a: the entry-timeframe
//     channel is now consumed at the top of Open() so a failed open can
//     never leave a stale value behind.
//   RESEARCH ADD — PULLBACK-TO-LINE CONTINUATION ENTRY (T_UsePullbackEntry):
//     the documented highest-edge Supertrend signal: in an established
//     trend (15+ bars, no flip), price pulls back to the line, holds it
//     with a rejection candle, next leg begins. Until now a trend whose
//     flip was missed (cooldown, spread gate, restart, blocked entry) was
//     unboardable until the NEXT flip — an entire trend forfeited. Now it
//     boards mid-trend through the exact same stop/sizing/confidence
//     gauntlet as a flip. Tagged PULLBACK-CONTINUATION in logs.
//   RESEARCH ADD — FLIP-FREQUENCY CHOP PENALTY (T_FlipFreqPenalty): the
//     documented rule that 3+ flips inside ~20 bars = ranging market where
//     every flip pays the whipsaw toll. Tracked per timeframe, applied as
//     a -15 confidence penalty (frequency-first: reduce size, never block).
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.40 — LIVE-DATA ROOT-CAUSE PASS. You reported: trades closing at the
//   beginning before the trend develops, not one full trend captured yet,
//   entries a beat late. Dug into WHY, not just added another layer:
//   BUG #62 (the main one): G_MinProfitR defaults to -0.3, so the
//     Guardian's full-close could fire the moment ANY trade was barely
//     negative — true for nearly every trade in its first couple of
//     minutes. Combined with two penalty checks that are MORE likely true
//     on a brand-new trade than a mature one (H1 hasn't caught up to a
//     fresh flip yet; "M1 tape flat" is trivially true 2 minutes in), the
//     scoring system could close a trade before it had any real chance to
//     develop. Added a genuine minimum age (G_MinBarsBeforeExit, on the
//     position's own timeframe) before the score-based full-close can
//     fire at all. Real hard exits (spread panic, the H1+M15+realloss
//     check, the unconditional flip-close) are untouched — still live
//     from bar one, because each is one unambiguous signal, not an
//     accumulation of still-settling soft evidence.
//   BUG #61: the "M1 tape flat" penalty compared against M1 bars 5-9 back
//     with no regard for whether the trade had existed that long — a
//     2-minute-old trade got judged against pre-entry price action. Now
//     requires 9 real minutes since entry first.
//   BUG #60: the chop-cluster "has the line stalled" check compared the
//     CURRENT Supertrend value to a raw price low/high from 3 bars back —
//     comparing the line to PRICE, not to its own past position. Replaced
//     with a real per-ticket snapshot of the line's value, taken once
//     when the check first becomes eligible, so "stalled" now means the
//     line itself hasn't moved since — not a coincidental price mismatch.
//     Also raised the eligibility age 6->9 bars: 6 often isn't enough for
//     the line to show real slope right after a fresh flip.
//   VALUES-CONSISTENCY FIX: H1 agreement was worth a flat 20/100 of the
//     exit score on EVERY trade, including fast M1/M3 ones — directly
//     contradicting the standing rule that H1 is context, never a
//     blocker, on fast timeframes. Reduced to 8 for fast TREND trades,
//     with the difference redistributed into the Supertrend check itself
//     (not just shrinking the total, which would've left the achievable
//     score identical whenever H1 disagrees — the common case).
//   DEAD CODE REMOVED: TwoPoleAgreesTf and TwoPoleAgreesApex (written last
//     session, never actually called by anything).
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.30 — THE TWO-POLE OSCILLATOR PORT (the biggest gap this whole audit
//   found) + revised secondary-symbol M1 handling:
//   Your own spec names three pillars: Supertrend + DEMA + Two-Pole
//   Oscillator. Pine had real Two-Pole code feeding its confidence score,
//   size-up decision, and early-close evidence. The EA — the one actually
//   holding real money — had NONE of it. Ported Pine's exact f_twoPole
//   math (recomputed fresh from a bounded lookback each call, no
//   persistent state to desync) and wired it in three places, mirroring
//   Pine's own usage exactly:
//     - TREND entry confidence: a turn that LED the flip scores +18 (Two-
//       Pole's whole point is turning before Supertrend confirms — Pine
//       weights this the same way); simply agreeing after the fact +4.
//     - Guardian's ThesisScore (check #13, all engines): a Two-Pole turn
//       AGAINST an open position is early reversal evidence, -10.
//     - ApexHealth: rebalanced (still 0-100, same thresholds mean the
//       same thing) to include a Two-Pole turn-against read.
//   REVISED per instruction: M1 on a secondary-symbol instance is no
//   longer hard-excluded (v15.2's approach) — a blanket block would have
//   cost genuine sniper setups. New InpSniperM1ExtraConf (25 points) makes
//   M1 require a much higher confidence/ADX bar instead, on both TREND
//   and SCALP, while M3/M5 keep the normal secondary strictness. A
//   routine M1 flip won't clear it; a genuinely clean one still can.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.20 — MULTI-SYMBOL AUDIT (for running a second, safe companion pair):
//   FACT-CHECK: did NOT bolt a second symbol onto this file, and did NOT
//     write a separate codebase either. This file already had real
//     multi-instance infrastructure (SharedRiskKey/MyOpenRiskPct/
//     CombinedRiskPct, InpMultiSymbolCap, InpSecondarySymbol) from an
//     earlier session — correctly percentage-based, not gold-specific.
//     The right move was fixing what stood between that infrastructure
//     and actually being safe to use, then running a SECOND INSTANCE of
//     this same file on a second chart (e.g. EURUSD/GBPUSD — a major FX
//     pair, deliberately NOT anything gold/silver-correlated) with its
//     own input set. A from-scratch second EA would have thrown away
//     everything already proven here for no reason.
//   BUG #58/#58b (real, would have silently broken a second instance):
//     two hard exits (the chaos/news detector and the Guardian's spread-
//     panic close) required spread > a flat $0.50 floor before firing.
//     That's a sane number for gold (~$4,000) but meaningless on any
//     other instrument's price scale — on EURUSD (~1.08) it would never
//     be satisfied, silently disabling both safety checks the moment
//     this ran on a second symbol. Replaced with ATR/R-relative floors
//     so both scale correctly to whatever instrument the chart is on.
//   BUG #59 (real, unrelated to multi-symbol but found in the same pass):
//     SC_Mid and SC_Slow were DEAD INPUTS. The onTF[] gating (k<=3/k<=7/
//     else) was written for an 11-timeframe SCALP design; the array was
//     later collapsed to 3 (M1/M3/M5), but the gating was never updated
//     — with SC_TFN=3, every slot fell into the SC_Fast branch, so
//     SC_Mid/SC_Slow did nothing, and turning SC_Fast off disabled SCALP
//     entirely regardless of the other two. Now each of the 3 real
//     timeframes maps to its own toggle, as the input names always implied.
//   IMPROVEMENT: InpSecondarySymbol previously only raised the ADX bar —
//     M1 could still fire. Now also excludes M1 outright on both TREND
//     and SCALP for a secondary instance, matching "confirmed M3/M5
//     signals only" exactly.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.10 — DEEP AUDIT PASS (bugs + missing analysis, no feature removed):
//   BUG #56: g_lastConfMult's "never absurdly small" floor could be
//     silently broken by the maturity throttle multiplying AFTER the
//     clamp. Re-clamped. (Undersizing only — never a risk increase.)
//   BUG #57 (the real one): NO ceiling existed on the COMBINED effect of
//     all sizing multipliers (push/coord/session/confidence/hour-learning
//     /win-ladder) stacked together on one trade. Individually bounded,
//     but nothing capped the aggregate — a rare favourable alignment
//     could have silently sized a trade at 2-3x its intended risk with no
//     hard stop catching it. New T_MaxRiskStackMult (1.8) caps the total.
//   GAP CLOSED: Pine already had formal candlestick pattern reads
//     (bullish/bearish engulfing, pin bar, doji-at-extreme) from a prior
//     session — the EA, which is what actually executes money, had none
//     of it, only crude candle-direction checks. Ported the identical
//     logic (CandleBullEngulf/CandleBearEngulf/CandlePinBar/
//     CandleDojiAtExtreme) into the EA: a small entry-confidence nudge
//     for TREND (+5 confirm / -8 against, same asymmetry as Pine) and a
//     new Guardian check (#12, -8) so a real reversal candle against an
//     open position — on ANY engine (TREND/SCALP/APEX all share Guardian)
//     — is counted as evidence, matching how price action, not just
//     lagging indicators, informs the exit decision.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// v15.00 — 10 improvements targeting: catch more of each trend, bank
//   nearer the tip, stop banking-and-rebuying clean runs, and stop
//   misreading entry-spread as the trade going wrong. All surgical.
//
//   #1 SPREAD-AWARE ENTRY GRACE — profitRAdj adds the spread back for the
//      first ~2 bars, so the soft early-exit brains (Guardian, chop,
//      stall) don't treat a brand-new trade as "already losing" when it's
//      only down by the cost of entry. The hard broker SL is untouched.
//      This is the direct fix for "broker buy price differs, EA thinks
//      we're already near stop."
//   #2 CHOP-CLUSTER EXIT NEEDS A STALLED LINE — two red candles inside a
//      live uptrend is a normal pause; the exit now also requires the
//      Supertrend line itself to have stopped advancing before it accepts
//      "chop." Stops the "closes too early on a healthy pullback" pattern.
//   #3 DON'T BANK-AND-REBUY A CLEAN RUN — the giveback guard now holds
//      through a dip while efficiency (ER) is still high and Supertrend
//      still agrees, so a clean 6-16 point move rides in ONE position
//      instead of banking on the first pullback and re-buying higher
//      (the "small bleeding loss" you described). A real rollover
//      collapses ER and protection returns instantly.
//   #4 CONTINUOUS PEAK-LOCK — new T_PeakLock ratchets the stop to hold
//      0.65 of the best R reached, from +1R up, filling the gap between
//      micro-lock (0.3R) and the coarse 2R step-lock. This is what banks
//      near the tip on a big move without closing the position early.
//   #5 RE-ENTRY WATCH ARMS AFTER A PROFITABLE BANK — was loss-only; now
//      any early-close while the trend still agrees arms the reclaim
//      watch, so the rest of a clean move is caught after a profitable
//      partial exit, not left behind.
//   #6 SCALP FADE NEEDS PRICE TO ACTUALLY TURN — the momentum-fade exit
//      now requires a real counter candle as a necessary condition, not
//      two lagging indicators firing while price still makes new highs.
//   #7 REWARD CATCHING THE BASE — an entry taken right at the signal line
//      (maximum move ahead) gets a modest size boost; the extension guard
//      already rejects chasing. Concentrates size on the best entries.
//   #8/#9 PINE ALIGNMENT — #3 and #4 mirrored into XAUUSD_APEX (v16) so
//      both systems stay philosophically identical, per the locked rule.
//   #10 Full structural + scope error-check pass (see notes at delivery).
//
// v14.01 (previous audit pass — surgical bug fixes only):
//   FIX #53 (SEVERE): TREND's flip-close — the exit that fires the moment
//     Supertrend flips against an open trade — was accidentally nested
//     inside "if(T_TrailOnST)". T_TrailOnST defaults to true so this was
//     invisible on stock settings, but turning that ONE toggle off (which
//     reads as "disable the trailing stop") silently disabled the
//     unconditional "ride to opposite signal" floor too. Now unconditional,
//     matching the Pine (whose reversal is baked into strategy.entry() and
//     can't be turned off by any input).
//   FIX #54/#54c: Guardian's ThesisScore checks #6 (EMA50) and #9 (RSI
//     divergence) were reading the FIXED M5 engine handles even for a
//     TREND position actually running on M1 or M3 — the same class of bug
//     v13's fix #49 already corrected for Supertrend/ADX/MACD, just missed
//     on these two. Now reads the position's own timeframe throughout.
//   FIX #55: the entry-snapshot used by the autopsy's "wasChasing" pattern
//     detector read the fixed M5 ATR/EMA21/close for a TREND entry, even
//     when the entry actually fired on M1 or M3 — feeding the learning
//     system wrong data for two of TREND's three timeframes.
//   NOTE (not a bug, comment corrected): Engine_Apex's "H1 must agree"
//     line actually checks M5 (TREND) Supertrend agreement, not H1 — the
//     higher-timeframe sanity is enforced separately via HouseBias()
//     (M15-only since v16.12) one line below. Left the
//     behaviour as-is; see the corrected comment at the call site.
// v14: hour-of-day expectancy learning (persisted across restarts), global
//   direction cap (all engines), micro-lock at +0.3R, adaptive giveback by
//   ATR percentile, re-entry ER quality gate, cost-aware minimum-R gate,
//   Friday flatten (blocks new + closes open before the weekend gap),
//   trend-maturity size throttle, per-timeframe loss cool-off, step-lock
//   profit ratchet (+2R can't fall below +1R, etc).
// v13 (this build): Kaufman Efficiency Ratio size-scaling (chop-aware, never
//   blocks — preserves frequency), day-context sizing tilt (H1 progress + day
//   range position), correlated-market failover scaffold (OFF, needs per-symbol
//   setup before use). Also fixed a stale duplicate #property version that was
//   silently overriding the build number back to 11.00.
// v13 loss-fixes + 12 improvements:
//   FIX (losses #1/#2): UNIVERSAL RE-ENTRY WATCH. Previously the reclaim watch
//     armed only on a giveback-close. A stop-out or in-trend-dip loss never
//     armed it, so the engine sat out a trend it was just shaken from. Now ANY
//     loss-close arms the watch when Supertrend still agrees — get back in on
//     the reclaim. A real reversal flips Supertrend and correctly does not arm.
//   #2  Re-entry cap (max reclaims per continuous trend; resets on true flip)
//   #3  Spread-vs-move gate (skip negative-expectancy trades where spread eats the move)
//   #4  Consecutive-win size ladder (press when paying, cut on first real loss, hard cap)
//   #5  Daily-target throttle (half size after the day's goal — keep trading, protect the day)
//   #6  Session weighting (via existing SessionQuality)
//   #8  Trail-arm gate (don't trail until real profit — stops the trail stopping you out in noise)
//   #10 ATR-spike guard (skip news-spike entries — worst fills)
//   #12 Correlated-exposure cap (max 2 of M1/M3/M5 same-direction at once)
//|                                                                  |
//|  THREE ENGINES. ONE DIRECTION. THREE SPEEDS.                     |
//|                                                                  |
//|  WHAT SURVIVES — one trade, sampled at three horizons:           |
//|                                                                  |
//|   SCALP  M1+M3+M5+M15   the small pushes INSIDE the move         |
//|                         many per day · 2R · exits on momentum    |
//|                         fade, before the tables turn             |
//|                                                                  |
//|   TREND  M5             the pullback, held properly              |
//|                         few per day · 4.5R · Supertrend trail    |
//|                                                                  |
//|   APEX   M15            the WHOLE move                           |
//|                         few per week · NO take profit · pyramids |
//|                         · trail WIDENS as profit grows           |
//|                                                                  |
//|  THEY WORK TOGETHER, ON PURPOSE:                                 |
//|                                                                  |
//|   HOUSE BIAS — M15 + H1 Supertrend must agree. No engine may     |
//|   trade against it. When they disagree there IS no bias, and     |
//|   only SCALP may act (it is fast enough to get out).             |
//|                                                                  |
//|   COORD BOOST — when APEX is riding and the house agrees, the    |
//|   faster engines size UP 30%. The slow engine informs the fast   |
//|   ones. When gold trends, all three are on the same side at      |
//|   different horizons and the profits STACK.                      |
//|                                                                  |
//|  EVERYTHING AUTO-ADJUSTS: lots to capital, stops and targets to  |
//|  volatility, risk to each engine's own proven expectancy.        |
//|                                                                  |
//|  Judge on EXPECTANCY (R), never win rate.                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+

#include <Trade/Trade.mqh>
CTrade trade;

#define ENG_COUNT 3
#define E_TREND 0
#define E_SCALP 1
#define E_APEX  2

//==================== GLOBAL ====================
input group "=== AUTO-SCALE — the EA sizes itself to whatever the balance is ==="
// EVERYTHING ADJUSTS. You never touch a lot size again.
//
// The EA reads the balance on every trade and reconfigures itself:
//
//   BALANCE      MODE        LOT SOURCE           TOTAL EXPOSURE CAP
//   under £250   MICRO       min lot (0.01)       AutoMaxExposurePct of equity
//   £250-£1k     SMALL       risk-based, floored  same
//   £1k-£10k     NORMAL      pure risk-based      same
//   over £10k    LARGE       pure risk-based      same
//
// On a small balance the broker's 0.01 minimum is bigger than the risk model
// wants, so the EA falls back to the minimum and TELLS YOU the true risk. As the
// account grows, risk-based sizing takes over automatically and the true risk %
// falls to the configured target. Nothing to change. It just happens.
//
// SL and TP already auto-adjust to the volatility regime (AtrRank/AdaptMults),
// and per-engine risk already auto-adjusts to proven expectancy (LiveRisk).
// This closes the last gap: SIZE adjusts to CAPITAL.
input bool   AutoScale          = false; // (v16.72) OFF, on his repeated instruction. AutoScale made
                                        // AutoMaxLots() = (equity/1000) x AutoMaxLotsPer1k OVERRIDE
                                        // InpMaxTotalLots, and from v16.48 I compounded that by making the
                                        // risk-derived ceiling win whenever it was tighter. At GBP150 with
                                        // InpRiskPctPerTrade 1% that ceiling computed to 0.001 lots, floored
                                        // to the 0.01 minimum — so the ENTIRE EA could hold ONE 0.01 position
                                        // and every other signal was refused "lot ceiling". That is the limit
                                        // he has been telling me to remove. With AutoScale off the ceiling is
                                        // simply InpMaxTotalLots, which is predictable and does not move.
input group "=== (v17.13) MARGIN GUARD — the missing execution check ==="
// FOUND BY AUDIT, not by him, and it is the likely cause of a symptom he HAS
// reported repeatedly: "the EA stopped taking signals". OrderCalcMargin and
// ACCOUNT_MARGIN_FREE appeared ZERO times in this file. When free margin runs
// out the broker simply REJECTS the order — no MT5 error dialog, no journal
// entry the EA notices, the signal just silently never becomes a position.
// That is indistinguishable from a logic gate refusing the trade, which is why
// it has never been diagnosed.
//
// It matters much more now, for two reasons he asked for in the same message:
//   1. 0.03 from a GBP100 start. Using his own verified GBP8.09 margin per 0.01
//      gold at 1:500, three clocks at 0.03 = GBP72.81 = 73% of a GBP100 account
//      in margin. The 3rd clock is already near the wall.
//   2. A second pair. 3 clocks x 2 pairs x 0.03 = GBP145.62, which EXCEEDS a
//      GBP100 balance outright. Those orders cannot be opened at any risk
//      appetite — it is arithmetic, not preference.
// So this guard checks margin BEFORE sending and logs the refusal explicitly,
// so a margin wall can never again look like a signal problem.
input group "=== (v17.15) SYMBOL AUTO-CALIBRATION — trade whatever it is attached to ==="
// He asked for the EA to work on ANY pair it is attached to. The risk maths
// already adapts (g_tickVal reads SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE), but three things
// did NOT, and each is a silent profit leak on a non-gold symbol:
//
//  1. THE LOT IS ABSOLUTE. T_StepBaseLots 0.03 means one cash risk on gold and
//     a completely different one on GBPJPY or an index. T_AutoRiskPct converts
//     the lot to a RISK target instead, computed from the symbol's own tick
//     value, so "0.03 on gold" transfers as equivalent risk to any instrument.
//
//  2. SLIPPAGE TOLERANCE WAS AN ABSOLUTE POINT COUNT. InpSlippagePoints=40 is
//     $0.40 on 2-digit gold — GBP0.90 per fill at 0.03, up to 60% of a GBP3
//     choppy winner round-trip. On a 5-digit FX pair the same 40 is 4 pips.
//     One number cannot be right for both. T_SlipAtrFrac makes the tolerance a
//     fraction of ATR, so it is proportionate on every symbol AND it tightens
//     automatically in the quiet hours where the winners are smallest.
//
//  3. THE BROKER MINIMUM STOP CAN EXCEED THE SIGNAL-LINE STOP. ClampStops()
//     silently WIDENS the stop when that happens. Sizing is correctly done
//     from the clamped distance, so risk is right — but the trade now needs a
//     bigger move for the same R, which quietly destroys the R:R the system
//     depends on. It bites hardest in low-ATR hours, i.e. exactly where 24/7
//     profit is supposed to come from. Now it is LOGGED instead of silent.
input bool   T_AutoCalibrate   = true;
input double T_AutoRiskPct     = 0.0;    // (v17.40) WAS 4.0 AND THAT IS WHY EVERY TRADE WAS 0.01.
                                         // AutoRiskLots takes the SMALLER of the ladder lot and this
                                         // risk cap. At GBP112 equity, 4% = GBP4.48 of budget, and a
                                         // 3.36pt stop costs GBP251/lot, so the cap allowed 0.0178 ->
                                         // 0.01, overriding the ladder's 0.03 on EVERY trade. Allowing
                                         // 0.03 here would need >=6.7%, so 4% could never permit it.
                                         // 0 disables the cap and restores his explicit ladder choice.
                                         // This was my addition overriding his stated size — his call,
                                         // his account, and the ladder is the setting he actually chose.    // target risk per trade, % of equity. 0 = keep absolute lots.
                                         // 4% reproduces roughly 0.03 gold on a GBP100-150 account at a
                                         // 5-6pt stop, so it is his CURRENT sizing expressed as a rule
                                         // that travels to other symbols instead of a number that does not.
input double T_SlipAtrFrac     = 0.15;   // max accepted slippage = this x ATR(entry TF). 0 = use InpSlippagePoints.
input bool   T_LogStopClamp    = true;   // print whenever the broker minimum widened our stop
input bool   T_LogClockRecovery = true;   // (v17.18) log clock-registry misses and recoveries
input int    T_OrderRetryN       = 2;     // (v17.31) transient-only order-send retries
input int    T_OrderRetryMs      = 250;   // pause between retries, ms
input bool   T_MarginGuard      = true;
input double T_MarginFloorPct   = 35.0;   // keep at least this % of equity as FREE margin
input bool   T_MarginLogRefusal = true;   // print every margin refusal (never fail silently)
input double AutoMaxExposurePct = 25.0;  // total open risk across ALL engines, % of equity. HARD ceiling.
input double InpMinBalanceToTrade = 120.0; // (v16.48) below this equity the EA stands down entirely.
                                     // At the 0.01 broker minimum a lot cannot be trimmed, so below
                                     // this balance risk-per-trade cannot be brought into range by
                                     // sizing — the only honest action is not to trade.
// (v16.50 SPEC CORRECTION) v16.49 introduced InpMaxRiskPctPerClock, which stood
// M5 down on a small balance. That was NOT the specification and it is removed.
// THE SPEC IS: M1, M3 and M5 ALWAYS trade, at every account size, with M1 and M3
// as the primary focus. M15 is the only balance-gated clock — it is the widest
// stop in the system and it is meant to come online as the account grows, not to
// compete with the fast clocks for a small one.
input double InpM15MinBalance    = 750.0; // M15 clocks (APEX + SCALP's M15) require this equity.
                                     // Set 0 to run M15 at any balance. M1/M3/M5 ignore this entirely.
input bool   InpRiskAdaptive     = false; // (v16.72) OFF — see AutoScale. It was the tighter of the two caps and it bound at 0.01.  // (v16.48) derive the lot ceiling from RISK, not equity fraction
input double InpRiskPctPerTrade  = 1.0;   // (v16.48) max % of equity risked on ONE trade. This is what
                                     // makes the EA adapt to ANY balance: the ceiling is recomputed
                                     // from live equity and the widest clock stop on every call, so
                                     // 120, 368 and 1000 all get a correct size with no retuning.
input double AutoMaxLotsPer1k   = 0.35;  // (v16.40 THE FREQUENCY KILLER) WAS 0.06. AutoMaxLots() = (equity/1000)
                                         // x this, and it OVERRIDES InpMaxTotalLots whenever AutoScale is on. At
                                         // £183 equity that computed 0.011 -> 0.01: the ENTIRE EA could hold ONE
                                         // 0.01 position at a time, shared across all three TREND clocks plus
                                         // SCALP plus APEX. One trade open and every other signal was refused with
                                         // "SKIP: lot ceiling". It would also have rejected the new 0.02 strong-fast
                                         // rung outright (0.02 > 0.01 cap). This is the hard frequency ceiling
                                         // behind "it doesn't take many trades" and "it stopped taking signals",
                                         // and it silenced a design built for 40-80 trades a day.
                                         // 0.35 gives ~0.06 total at £183 (about six 0.01 trades, or three 0.02s)
                                         // and scales up with equity. The REAL risk control is and always was
                                         // AutoMaxExposurePct (25% of equity in actual cash at risk, breakeven-
                                         // locked positions excluded) — that still binds, and it is the honest
                                         // ceiling because it measures risk rather than lot count.
input double AutoMinLotFloor    = 0.01;  // your broker minimum

input group "=== SMALL ACCOUNT MODE ==="
// WHY YOUR EA IS NOT TRADING.
//
// LotFor() refuses a trade when the broker's MINIMUM lot (0.01) would risk more
// than 1.5x the engine's budget. On £100 that kills almost everything:
//
//   TREND wants 1.9% = £1.90.  A $6 stop at 0.01 lot risks $6 = £4.50.  BLOCKED.
//   APEX  wants 1.6% = £1.60.  A $7 stop at 0.01 lot risks $7 = £5.25.  BLOCKED.
//   MICRO wants 0.8% = £0.80.  A $3 stop at 0.01 lot risks $3 = £2.25.  BLOCKED.
//
// Nothing was broken. The risk manager was doing its job and saying "I cannot
// size this trade small enough." It just never told you.
//
// THREE WAYS OUT, best first:
//
//   1. NANO LOTS. Ask your broker to enable 0.001 minimum. Everything then works
//      exactly as designed at £100. This is the real answer and it is free.
//
//   2. SmallAccountMode = true (below). The EA accepts min-lot trades and tells
//      you the TRUE risk % of every one. On £100 that is 3-6% per trade. You
//      will see it in the log on every single entry. No surprises.
//
//   3. Fund to ~£400. 0.01 lot then lands near 1-2% risk naturally.
//
// Mode 2 is what you are asking for, so it is built. But look at what it means:
//
//   RISK PER TRADE     6 LOSSES IN A ROW        BACK TO BREAKEVEN NEEDS
//        2%                 -11%                        +13%
//        5%                 -26%                        +36%
//   >>>  10%                -47%                        +89%   <<<
//
// Six losses in a row is NORMAL for a 3R+ system — it happens roughly once every
// 60 trades. At 10% risk that is half your account, on a completely ordinary run
// of bad luck, with nothing wrong at all. That is the honest number. It is your
// call, and the EA will do exactly what you tell it.
input bool   SmallAccountMode = true;   // allow min-lot trades even when they exceed the risk budget
input double SmallAcctMaxRisk = 8.0;    // but NEVER let a single trade exceed this % of equity
input bool   InpDiagnostics   = true;   // log EXACTLY why each engine did or did not fire

input group "=== STRUCTURE & LIQUIDITY (the ICT layer) ==="
// Honest framing first: "ICT / order flow / GEX" as usually sold is mostly
// unfalsifiable — draw enough boxes and one of them always contains the reversal.
// GEX in particular needs options data gold CFDs do not have, so it is not real
// here and I will not pretend otherwise.
//
// But TWO ideas underneath it ARE mechanically real and testable, and they help:
//
//   1. FAIR VALUE GAPS (imbalance). A three-bar gap where price moved so fast it
//      left an untraded pocket. Price genuinely tends to revisit those. When a
//      pullback lands INSIDE one, in the direction of the trend, that is a
//      measurably better entry than a random pullback.
//
//   2. LIQUIDITY SWEEPS. Price pokes a prior high/low (where the stops are),
//      grabs them, and reverses. The stop-hunt is real — you can see it in the
//      wick. Entering AFTER the sweep, with the trend, is a real edge.
//
// Both are used as a CONFIDENCE BOOST, never as a standalone signal. They make a
// good trend entry better. They do not turn a bad one into a good one.
input bool   ICT_Enable    = true;
input bool   ICT_UseFVG    = true;   // pullback into a fair value gap = better entry
input bool   ICT_UseSweep  = true;   // entry after a liquidity grab = better entry
input double ICT_Boost     = 1.35;   // size multiplier when structure confirms
input int    ICT_Lookback  = 30;     // bars to scan for gaps and liquidity

input group "=== THE AUTOPSY — it learns from every loss ==="
// Every loss, however small, gets a full post-mortem written to file. Not just
// "it lost" — WHY it lost, and which filter should have caught it.
//
// THE IMPORTANT DESIGN DECISION:
// It does NOT re-tune itself after every loss. That would be curve-fitting in
// real time, and the EA would chase its own tail into oblivion — a single loss is
// NOISE. This rides to the opposite signal with a tight stop — it is not a
// fixed-target system and losing most trades is NOT the expected shape of it.
// A single loss still shouldn't trigger re-tuning, but for a different reason:
// one trade is too small a sample to prove anything. Punishing the system for
// one normal, small, correctly-stopped-out loss would destroy the very edge
// you are trying to keep.
//
// What it does instead: it accumulates losses, and looks for what they have IN
// COMMON. Twenty losses that all happened below ADX 22 is not noise — that is the
// market telling you your ADX floor is too low. THAT is worth acting on.
//
//   1 loss   -> logged. Nothing changes. It is noise.
//   20 losses -> patterns extracted. If 70%+ share a flaw, it acts.
//
// Files written to MQL5/Files:
//   quad_losses.csv     every losing trade, fully dissected
//   quad_autopsy.txt    the running analysis and what it recommends
input bool   AU_Enable      = true;
input int    AU_MinSample   = 20;    // losses needed before it will change anything
input double AU_PatternPct  = 70.0;  // a factor must appear in this % of losses to count
input bool   AU_AutoTighten = true;  // (v12) enabled — the EA now learns from real loss patterns
                                      // (20+ losses sharing 70%+ common flaw) and tightens itself. Only
                                      // acts on GENUINE losses; the fast-scratch filter in OnTradeTransaction
                                      // prevents normal trend-start scratches from ever polluting the sample.
input int    AU_ReportEvery = 10;    // print the running autopsy every N losses

input group "=== VOLATILITY & NEWS — trade the trend, not the chaos ==="
// IMPROVEMENT 2 + 3.
//
// You are right that a violent move is not automatically a bad move. The
// distinction is not "how big" — it is "is it going somewhere".
//
//   ATR spikes 4x AND price is ripping WITH the trend  -> this is the trade of
//                                                          the week. TAKE IT.
//   ATR spikes 4x and price is thrashing both ways     -> this is a news candle
//                                                          shredding stops. STAY OUT.
//
// So the filter is not a volatility cap. It is a CHAOS cap: high volatility is
// only blocked when it is DIRECTIONLESS. A big trending expansion is exactly
// what these engines exist to catch.
input bool   V_Filter        = true;
input double V_SpikeAtr      = 2.5;   // ATR this many x its own 50-bar average = a spike
input double V_ChaosWickPct  = 55.0;  // if a spike bar is >55% wick, it is chaos, not a move
input int    V_ChaosBars     = 3;     // stand down this many bars after chaos
input bool   V_AllowTrending = true;  // a spike WITH the trend and a real body -> take it

// NEWS DETECTION (fix #51). Real news is DETECTED from live market
// conditions — spread blown out past its normal average AND a genuine
// volatility spike, at the same time — not guessed from a fixed clock. A
// static schedule drifts wrong on DST/broker changes and blocks trading on
// a quiet clock-only match while missing real unscheduled news entirely.
// N_Times below is now optional context only: it widens the detection
// window slightly AROUND a known scheduled time (NFP/CPI/FOMC), for
// pre-positioning, but real conditions still have to actually be present —
// a clock match alone can never block trading on its own any more.
input bool   N_Filter        = false;  // (v16.21) OFF at his request: an active trade already has an SL and
                                       // a signal-line/opposite-signal exit — both cover a news spike just
                                       // fine, and news is exactly when gold makes its biggest real moves.
                                       // Blocking entries here was sitting out the trend, not avoiding risk.
input double N_SpreadSpike   = 2.5;   // spread this many x its rolling average = news-level spread
input double N_VolSpike      = 2.2;   // ATR this many x its 20-bar average = news-level volatility
input int    N_BeforeMin     = 3;     // stop opening this long before a KNOWN scheduled time
input int    N_AfterMin      = 5;     // and this long after
input string N_Times         = "13:30,15:00,19:00";  // NFP/CPI, ISM, FOMC (server time) — optional context only
input bool   N_CloseOpen     = false;  // (v16.21) OFF at his request: SL is already there, TP/ride-to-flip is
                                       // already there — no need to force-flatten a trade that's already
                                       // protected just because a news spike is happening. Let it ride the
                                       // move like everything else does.

input group "=== THE GUARDIAN — live analysis of every OPEN trade ==="
// A stop loss is a PASSIVE defence. It sits there and waits to be hit.
//
// The Guardian is an ACTIVE one. On every tick it re-reads the market and asks a
// single question about every open position:
//
//     "If I were flat right now, would I still take this trade?"
//
// If the answer is no, the reason for the trade is gone — and a trade whose
// reason is gone is not a trade any more, it is a bet. So it acts, immediately,
// long before the stop is anywhere near.
//
// THESIS SCORE, 0-100, recomputed every tick from nine independent checks:
//
//    80-100  THRIVING   let it run, widen the trail
//    60-79   HEALTHY    normal management
//    45-59   WEAKENING  tighten the trail hard
//    30-44   BREAKING   take half off the table NOW
//    0-29    DEAD       close it. The premise is gone.
//
// This is where most of the risk reduction actually comes from. Not from a
// tighter stop — a tighter stop just loses faster. From LEAVING EARLY when the
// market tells you it changed its mind.
input bool   G_Enable        = true;
input int    G_TightenBelow  = 60;   // tighten the trail below this score
input int    G_PartialBelow  = 45;   // take half off below this
input int    G_ExitBelow     = 30;   // close entirely below this
input double G_MinProfitR    = -0.3;
// (v17.49) His instruction: "why is entry trades being cut by guardian." The
// Guardian scores every open trade and closes it below G_ExitBelow. It runs
// BEFORE the min-hold grace and before any trail logic, so it could cut a
// seconds-old trade on a low score alone. G_MinBarsBeforeExit is raised so a
// fresh trade gets real bars to prove itself before the Guardian may close it. // partial/exit only once loss is contained (avoid panic-selling noise)
// ROOT-CAUSE FIX: the score-based full-close now waits this many bars (on
// the position's own timeframe) before it's allowed to fire at all. A
// trade this young hasn't had a fair chance to develop — closing it on
// accumulated soft evidence this early is the "closed at the beginning,
// trend continues" pattern. Real hard exits are unaffected.
input int    G_MinBarsBeforeExit = 10;   // (v17.49) 4 -> 10 bars before the Guardian may close anything
input bool   G_ExitOnHtfFlip = false;   // (v17.49) OFF — his instruction: "hour flip don't matter."
                                        // The Guardian ran FIRST, before any trail logic, and could
                                        // close a live M1/M5 trade purely because H1 flipped against
                                        // it. An H1 candle takes an hour to form; an M1 trade resolves
                                        // in minutes. This is the mechanism that was cutting trades
                                        // before they could develop. // H1 turns against you -> out, whatever the score
input bool   G_ExitOnDiverge = true; // price makes a new extreme, momentum does not
input bool   G_ExitOnVolDry  = true; // the trend is running on no participation
input double G_SpreadPanic   = 3.0;  // spread this many x normal -> flatten, something is wrong

input group "═══ GLOBAL RISK ═══"
input double InpBalance          = 100.0;   // Account balance for reference
input bool   InpDailyHaltEnable  = true;  // (v18.91 AS) WAS false -- there was no
                                         // daily floor of any kind. Note the PERCENT
                                         // below is 40, which on a small demo is not
                                         // really a stop. Set it to a number you have
                                         // chosen. Old note follows.
                                         // (v16.87) OFF, per his request. NOTE: setting the PERCENTAGE
                                          // below to 0 would NOT have disabled it — haltAt = dayStart *
                                          // (1 - 0/100) = dayStart, which halts on the FIRST loss of the
                                          // day. This dedicated switch is the correct way to turn it off.
                                          // Cost, stated once: this was the last account-wide backstop.
                                          // Everything else that could stop a bad session (auto-disable,
                                          // chop pause, defer, ADX/confidence floors) is already off.    // halt ALL new entries for the day past the loss limit below
// (v18.09) HIS ARITHMETIC, AND HE IS RIGHT. On ~£120 a 20% halt is ~£24. With
// the v18.07 birth-stop floor at 4 points and 0.04 lots, one full loss is ~$16 --
// so the halt lands after ONE AND A HALF losses and the overnight test dies
// before it produces data. A cap has to be large enough to survive a normal
// losing run or it is not a risk control, it is a random stop button.
// 40% on a DEMO buys room for ~6 full losses at 0.04, or ~24 at 0.01.
// PUT THIS BACK TO 8-10 BEFORE ANY REAL MONEY RUNS.
input double InpMaxDailyLossPct  = 40.0;    // (v16.34 STRUCTURAL FIX) WAS 6.0, and 6.0 was quietly the single
                                            // biggest profit blocker in the whole EA. On a GBP183 account the
                                            // SMALLEST tradeable lot (0.01) risks 1.6-4.1% of equity per trade
                                            // depending on stop width - that is forced by the broker's minimum,
                                            // not by choice. So a 6% daily budget was 2-4 ORDINARY losses. A
                                            // design that intends 40-80 trades a day cannot run on a 2-4-loss
                                            // budget: a poor Asian/early-London session halted the EA before
                                            // 12-16 GMT, the London/NY overlap, which is the only stretch that
                                            // reliably pays. It was sitting out the best hours nearly every day.
                                            // 20% on GBP183 = ~GBP37 = roughly 9-12 ordinary losses, which the
                                            // strategy's own win rate can actually absorb before quitting.
                                            // HONEST TRADE-OFF: a bad day can now cost ~GBP37 instead of ~GBP11.
                                            // Lower it as the balance grows (at GBP500+, 8-10% is plenty).
input group "═══ STOP-HUNT / SPIKE PROTECTION ═══"
input double InpSpikeRatio       = 1.6;     // a candle must be this many x the recent average range to count as a spike
input double InpSpikeBufferAtr   = 0.35;    // max extra room (x ATR) given to the trail during a genuine spike only
input group "═══ DEMA TOP-UP (small extra confirmation add-on) ═══"
// (v18.76) OFF. THREE reasons, all read out of this file, none of them a preference:
//   1. It sends InpTopUpLots = 0.01, which is BELOW his own standing 0.02 floor.
//      §6 of the handover lists "live fills are 0.01/0.02/0.03/0.04, and 0.01 is
//      below his own floor" as an unexplained observation. THIS IS THE SOURCE.
//   2. It sends trade.Buy(lots, _Symbol, px, 0, 0, ...) -- SL = 0 AND TP = 0.
//      It is the only stopless order in the entire EA.
//   3. It never touches Open(), so it skips the exposure ceiling, the duplicate
//      cluster cap, the margin guard, guard X and guard Y.
// REVERT: InpDemaTopUp = true.
input bool   InpDemaTopUp        = false;   // when DEMA also agrees with an open trade's direction, add a small extra lot
input double InpTopUpLots        = 0.01;    // size of the top-up (kept deliberately tiny — a bonus, not a second bet)
input double InpTopUpBankAtr     = 0.3;     // bank the top-up once price has moved this many x ATR in its favour

// ══════════ v18.76 — THE THREE DOORS THAT SKIPPED EVERY GUARD ══════════
// FOUND IN PHASE 1. Open() at ~8829 is properly defended: exposure ceiling,
// duplicate-cluster cap, margin check, no-chasing-the-extreme (X), no-adding-
// underwater (Y). THREE order-sending sites never go through it:
//     line ~5364  DEMA top-up   0.01 lots, SL=0 and TP=0 (STOPLESS)
//     line ~12520 APEX add
//     line ~15466 scale-add     0.02 lots, fires ON NEW EXTREMES
// This is §4 defect shape #5 again (a mechanism wired to one path and absent on
// its neighbours), at the highest possible cost: the whole anti-stacking layer
// is bypassable and every fill through these doors is invisible to T_MaxDirLots.
// SCALE-ADD IS THE WORST OF THE THREE. Its trigger (line ~15455) requires
// LegStillExtending() -- a NEW EXTREME in the trade's favour. Guard X refuses an
// ADD within one noise band of the recent extreme. They are opposite rules, and
// scale-add wins because it never asks. That is mistake §5.4 (two changes that
// fight each other) live in the current build.
// THESE SWITCHES DO NOT CHANGE WHEN AN ADD FIRES. They only make the add ask the
// same two questions a base entry already asks. Checked against the Phase 2
// breakout scenario: base 0.03 + 0.03, scale-add 0.02 + 0.02 = 0.10 exactly, so
// T_MaxDirLots = 0.10 still permits BOTH scale-adds. The winner is untouched.
// It bites only past 0.10 in one direction, which is the stack, not the signal.
input bool   T_AddsRespectCaps   = true;   // scale-add + APEX add must pass DirExposureFull() and MarginOkFor()

// ══════════ v18.76 — THE BREAK-EVEN LINE THAT HAS NO OFF-SWITCH ══════════
// SCENARIO 6, Phase 2. T_ScalpScratch is OFF by audit because "it arms on peak,
// parks the stop at entry+spread, so a leg whose first pullback returns to entry
// is capped at breakeven". Twenty lines below the SCALP ratchet there is a
// hard-coded line doing exactly that, with no switch and no audit:
//     if(profitR >= 0.7) newSL = entry + SpreadUSD();
// R is the 3.08 pt birth stop, so 0.7R = 2.16 pts. It fires BEFORE the ratchet is
// even awake (the ratchet does nothing under a ~2.3 pt peak -- see the arithmetic
// at T_ScalpRatchet). On a trend with a deep pullback that returns to entry and
// then continues, this closes the trade at +0.4 pts and the leg leaves without it.
// DEFAULT true = EXACTLY CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Nothing changes this build. The
// switch exists so the behaviour can be attributed for the first time.
input bool   T_ScalpBeAt07R      = true;   // false = let the ratchet alone decide the SCALP floor

// ══════════ v18.76 — LIVE MARGIN WATCH ══════════
// HIS REPORT: "we go up 200 quid with like 14 0.02s, didn't close, and somehow
// made a loss because margin called."
// VERIFIED IN SOURCE: margin is checked in exactly ONE place, MarginOkFor(), and
// only ever BEFORE sending an order. ACCOUNT_MARGIN_LEVEL appears in NO
// executable line in this file. Once positions are open nothing watches margin.
// THE MECHANISM: margin required is fixed by lot size and does not move. Equity
// moves with the trades. While floating +GBP200 the margin level looks healthy;
// as the move gives back, equity falls, the requirement does not, and the level
// drops through the broker's stop-out. THE BROKER THEN PICKS THE EXITS, worst
// first, at market. That is how a +GBP200 stack books a loss.
// NO THRESHOLD IS TYPED HERE. The trigger is computed from the position book:
// intervene only if ONE MORE NOISE BAND against the open stack would take free
// margin below zero. Noise band = NoiseFloorPts(), the same spread + 0.60*ATR
// every lock in this build already uses.
// WHAT IT CAN COST: it can close a trade the market would have rescued. What it
// replaces is the broker closing that same trade a moment later at a worse price
// with no choice of which one. It cannot fire while margin is comfortable.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_LiveMarginWatch = false.
input bool   T_LiveMarginWatch   = true;
input double T_LiveMarginBands   = 1.0;    // how many noise bands of adverse move to survive. 1.0 = derived, see above

// ══════════ v18.77 (AA) THE PEAK BANKERS WERE KILLED BY A BLANKET ══════════
// T_BanksOff = true suppresses EIGHT closes with one boolean, on the stated
// reason "each one exists to take a small profit NOW, which is the disease."
// THAT REASON IS TRUE OF SIX OF THEM AND FALSE OF TWO.
//   PEAK-BANK  (line ~14520) requires a reversal candle AND a Two-Pole turn AND
//              (1.5x volume OR deep profit) AND !LegStillExtending(). It cannot
//              fire while the leg is printing new extremes our way. It is not a
//              small-profit taker, it is a TOP detector with a runner guard.
//   SPIKE-PEAK (line ~14200) requires a bar whose range beats 1.80x ATR AND that
//              closed in the worst 40% of its own range against us. That is a
//              blow-off / volume reversal. Its own comment says "a trail cannot
//              follow this" -- and the reason given for switching it off at
//              T_SpikePeakBank was "the ratchet covers blow-offs". Those two
//              statements contradict each other and the code agrees with the
//              first one: a trailing stop gives back the whole noise band and
//              cannot outrun a bar that moves 1.8 ATR in one print.
// HIS WORDS THIS SESSION: "our goal is to close at peak not at that trailing sl,
// it's just a protection eg if a volume reversal suddenly happens." SPIKE-PEAK
// IS THAT MECHANISM. It was built, tested against his 27 Jul tape, and shut off.
// TWO EARLIER FIXES ARE ALSO INERT BEHIND THE BLANKET AND COME BACK WITH THIS:
//   v18.59 fixed LegStillExtending() from ~98%-true to bar-1-only (~50%), which
//          was the gate that made every peak bank unfireable.
//   v18.59 fixed SMALL-MOVE's unsatisfiable pair.
// Those two sessions of repair have never once executed.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG, STATED PLAINLY: a deep pullback inside a live trend can
// print a reversal candle. PEAK-BANK's !LegStillExtending() and SPIKE-PEAK's
// close-position test are the defences, and the reclaim watch re-enters. If his
// tape shows early exits on continuing legs, this is the first thing to revert.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_BanksOffExemptPeak = false (restores v18.76 exactly).
input bool   T_BanksOffExemptPeak = true;

// ══════════ v18.79 (AE) THE 2-POINT TRADE HAS NOWHERE TO GO ══════════
// HIS QUESTION: "some trades only go up like 2pts, i wanna claim as much as we can."
// WHY NO STOP CAN DO IT: the noise band is spread + 0.60 x ATR, about 1.9 points.
// A stop placed inside the band is taken by ordinary breathing rather than by a
// reversal. So on a 2 pt peak the best any TRAILING mechanism can guarantee is
// 2.0 - 1.9 = 0.1 points, which is inside the spread. That is not a tuning
// failure, it is arithmetic, and no setting changes it.
// WHAT CAN WORK: a close triggered by EVIDENCE rather than by a price level. A
// market close does not have to survive a wick. SMALL-MOVE is exactly that
// mechanism and it is already written, at line ~14551:
//     profit >= 0.60R (~1.85 pts)   AND peak < 0.75R (~2.31 pts)
//     AND the leg made no new extreme on its last closed bar
//     AND a reversal candle printed on this trade's own clock
// It fires only when the move has stopped and turned. It cannot clip a runner,
// because a runner is still extending and the second condition fails.
// TWO SESSIONS ALREADY REPAIRED IT AND NEITHER EVER RAN: v17.67 made the pair
// unsatisfiable ("profit >= 0.60R AND peak < 0.30R" -- impossible), v18.59 fixed
// that with T_SmallMoveCeilFix, and then the blanket switched it off anyway.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: this IS a small-profit taker, which is the behaviour the
// blanket was created to stop. Its defence is the pair of conditions above, not
// my opinion. If the log shows SMALL-MOVE firing on trades that then continued,
// this is the first thing to turn off.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_BanksOffExemptSmall = false (restores v18.78 exactly).
input bool   T_BanksOffExemptSmall = true;

// ══════════ v18.80 (AF) AUDITING MY OWN v18.79 CHANGE — IT BARELY FIRES ══════════
// I woke SMALL-MOVE last build. Then I measured its window, which I should have
// done BEFORE shipping it. It is:
//        floor   profit >= T_MinBankR                    = 0.60R = 1.85 pts
//        ceiling peak   <  T_MinBankR + 0.15             = 0.75R = 2.31 pts
// THE WHOLE SATISFIABLE BAND IS 0.46 POINTS WIDE. For it to fire, a reversal
// candle must close while profit has fallen no more than 0.46 pts from the peak.
// The noise band on this instrument is ~1.9 pts, so a single M1 candle against
// the trade routinely travels four times the entire window. In practice the
// trade is already below the floor by the time the evidence exists.
// THIS IS THE §4 PATTERN AGAIN and I shipped a fresh instance of it: a threshold
// set against a quantity it cannot satisfy. The 0.15 came from v16.46's CHOP-STALL
// branch, where it was never checked against candle size either.
// THE FIX IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN. The window must be at least as wide as the
// thing that has to move through it -- one noise band, the same spread + 0.60 x
// ATR every lock in this build uses. Computed live, so it tracks ATR instead of
// being a number that goes stale.
//        band 1.9 pts, R 3.08 -> ceiling 0.60R + 0.617R = 1.217R = 3.75 pts
// SO SMALL-MOVE NOW COVERS PEAKS FROM 1.85 TO ~3.75 PTS, which is the band he
// keeps asking about ("some trades only go up like 2pts").
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: at the top of that range it competes with the hard lock,
// which at a 3.7 pt peak would guarantee 1.8 pts. SMALL-MOVE banks at 1.85+, so
// it is not worse -- but it closes at market instead of waiting, which means a
// trade that would have resumed is gone. Its two guards are the same as before:
// the leg must have stopped extending AND a reversal candle must have printed.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_SmallMoveBandWindow = false (restores the 0.46 pt window).
input bool   T_SmallMoveBandWindow = true;

// ══════════ v18.80 (AG) THE EARLY-FAIL CUT CANNOT FIRE AT ITS OWN THRESHOLD ══════════
// HIS QUESTION: "develop a good SL in terms of those some odd signals which make
// 0 profit at all."
// THE MECHANISM ALREADY EXISTS. EARLY-FAIL (line ~14470) cuts a trade that never
// went green at T_FailCutR = 0.45R = 1.39 pts instead of letting it ride to the
// 3.08 pt stop. That is GBP 3.06 instead of GBP 6.79 at 0.03 lots -- it halves the
// loss on precisely the signals he is describing.
// IT IS BLOCKED. The cut requires !holdGrace, and holdGrace includes inMinHold,
// which is T_MinHoldSecs = 180. Early-fail's OWN time floor, T_TimeGraceSeconds,
// is 90. So the 90 second floor can never govern anything: a strictly larger gate
// sits above it. Written to fire at 90 seconds, cannot fire before 180.
// WHAT THAT COSTS: in the 90 seconds it is held back, a failing trade on a ~1.9 pt
// noise band routinely travels from -1.39 to the full -3.08 stop. The difference
// is GBP 3.73 on every failed signal, and failed signals are the cohort he asked
// about by name.
// THE FIX IS NARROW ON PURPOSE. The fresh-trade timer is overridden; THE HOLD VOTE
// QUORUM IS NOT. If two clocks still agree the trade is alive, the cut still stands
// down. Only the clock-watching part of holdGrace is bypassed, and only after
// early-fail's own grace (90 seconds AND 3 bars AND structure broken AND 1.39 pts
// underwater) is fully satisfied.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a trade that dips 1.4 pts in its first three minutes, breaks
// a nearby swing, and then recovers is now cut. In chop, swings sit close to entry
// -- that is the exact scenario. T_ChopAwareBreathe already widens the breach
// requirement in a range regime, which is the defence. If the log shows EARLY-FAIL
// cutting trades that then went green, turn this off first.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_FailCutOwnGrace = false (restores v18.79 exactly).
input bool   T_FailCutOwnGrace     = true;

// ══════════ v18.81 (AH) A RE-ENTRY THAT COSTS MORE THAN IT BUYS ══════════
// HIS NUMBER THIS SESSION: "120+ trades a day, and that 120+ includes reentries."
// EVERY FILL PAYS THE SPREAD. At 0.03 lots a round trip is 0.6-1.0 points, which
// is GBP 1.32 to GBP 2.20. 120 of them is GBP 159 to GBP 264 A DAY before a single
// trade is judged good or bad. That is the largest single number in this project
// and nothing in the file measures it.
// THE RECLAIM MAKES IT WORSE, not better. After a giveback close the watch arms at
//        g_trWatchLevel = recent 5-bar high + 0.16 x ATR   (~0.48 pts)
// so the EA gets back in ABOVE where it just got out. It pays the spread again AND
// buys at a worse price. Two fills, two spreads, and a starting position roughly
// one point behind where it was already sitting.
// THE RULE THAT SHOULD ALWAYS HAVE BEEN THERE: only re-enter when the reclaim is
// worth at least what the round trip costs. T_ReentryEdgeAtr exists for exactly
// this and is set to 0.00, so no cost test is applied at all.
// DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN: the cost of leaving and returning is one round trip, which
// is the live spread paid twice. The edge required is therefore 2 x spread,
// measured live so it widens automatically in thin hours when the spread is worst
// and re-entry is least worth doing.
// THIS DOES NOT REFUSE A SIGNAL. A fresh Supertrend flip is untouched. It applies
// only to the reclaim watch -- re-entering a trend the EA was ALREADY in.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a genuine continuation that reclaims by only half the round
// trip is now skipped, and the rest of that leg is missed. That is a real cost and
// it is the reason this is a switch. If the log fills with RECLAIM COST REFUSED on
// legs that then ran, set it back to 0.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ReentryCostEdge = false.
input bool   T_ReentryCostEdge     = true;

// ══════════ v18.83 (AJ) HIS ONE STRATEGIC RULE IS SWITCHED OFF ══════════
// HIS STANDING RULE #1, in the handover, in his own words: "Every Pine signal is
// taken. No entry filter may refuse a signal. THE LEVER IS SIZE."
// THE LEVER IS DISCONNECTED. LotFor() begins with `if(T_FixedLots > 0.0)` and
// RETURNS at the end of that block (line ~6095). T_FixedLots = 0.03. Everything
// that decides how big a trade should be lives BELOW that return and is therefore
// unreachable:
//        g_chopSizeDown  + T_DeadChopSizeX (0.45)  -- shrink in dead chop
//        g_premiumSetup  + T_PremiumLotMult (2.00) -- grow on a premium setup
//        RegimeSizeMult, HourSizeMult, SessionMult, WinPressMult
// The last three are in the "defined and never called" list from the v18.82 sweep,
// which is the same finding seen from the other end.
// WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN ANY EXIT FIX: with one fixed size, a premium setup
// and a dead-chop signal risk and earn EXACTLY THE SAME AMOUNT. There is no way
// to be right bigger and wrong smaller. That is the only structural edge available
// once the signals are fixed, and it is currently unavailable.
// THE NEW SIZES ARE HIS OWN NUMBERS, NOT MINE. T_LotFloorHard = 0.02 and
// T_LotCeilHard = 0.05 are already in this file, labelled "his standing minimum"
// and "his standing maximum". The ladder simply uses them:
//        dead chop      -> 0.02   (his floor)
//        normal         -> 0.03   (T_FixedLots, unchanged)
//        premium setup  -> 0.05   (his ceiling, from T_PremiumLotMult 2.00 clamped)
// Both flags are already computed and set live (g_chopSizeDown at ~11801,
// g_premiumSetup at ~12239 / ~12311). Nothing new is being measured.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG, STATED PLAINLY: a premium setup that is wrong now loses
// 0.05/0.03 = 67% more. If PremiumSetup() is poorly calibrated this makes losses
// bigger before it makes winners bigger, and neither of us has ever measured its
// hit quality. That is the single biggest unknown in this change. The clamp to his
// own 0.02-0.05 range is the bound on the damage.
// FIRST THING TO READ IN THE LOG: the [SIZE] lines. If 0.05 fills are not visibly
// outperforming 0.03 fills within a session, set this false.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_FixedLotsAdaptive = false (every entry back to a flat 0.03).
input bool   T_FixedLotsAdaptive   = true;

// ══════════ v18.84 (AK) AUDITING (AJ) — THE TEST I JUST MADE LOAD-BEARING ══════════
// v18.83 reconnected the size lever and doubled size on PremiumSetup(). I then read
// PremiumSetup() properly, which I should have done BEFORE shipping it. Two problems.
//
// PROBLEM 1 — THE BAND IS A FRACTION OF A MOVING QUANTITY, SO IT SWALLOWS THE DAY.
// The test is: day range >= T_PremiumRunPts (20 pts), and the entry sits within
// T_PremiumEdgeFrac (0.30) of the range from the extreme it trades FROM.
// The band is 30% AT EACH END. On a day sitting right at the 20 pt qualifying floor
// that is 6 points at the bottom and 6 at the top -- 12 of 20 points, SIXTY PERCENT
// OF THE WHOLE DAY, marked "premium". Early in a session, when the range has only
// just crossed 20, nearly every entry qualifies for double size.
// This is the §4 pattern once more: a threshold expressed as a fraction of a
// quantity that is itself near its floor.
// FIX, DERIVED: cap the edge at ONE SIGNAL BAND -- T_StMultiplier x ATR(7), the same
// flip-to-flip distance the strategy is built on. Being within one signal band of
// the day's extreme is what "at the extreme" actually means, and it stays honest at
// every day size:
//        20 pt day -> 30% is 6.0 pts, capped to ~3.0 -> 15% each end
//        60 pt day -> 30% is 18.0 pts, capped to ~3.0 ->  5% each end
//
// PROBLEM 2 — I CANNOT FIX THIS ONE AND IT IS THE BIGGER OF THE TWO.
// PremiumSetup buys near the day LOW and sells near the day HIGH. That is a
// mean-reversion test. The strategy it is sizing up is Supertrend TREND FOLLOWING.
// v17.78 already found this and removed the day-extreme test from the star score,
// with the note: "it describes a reversal from the edge of the day, which is a
// different trade to a trend birth -- requiring it was pushing the star toward the
// exact setups he did NOT mean."
// So the same test was judged NOT good enough to call a signal strong, and was left
// in place to DOUBLE THE MONEY on it. Those two positions cannot both be right, and
// nothing in the file or the tape settles which one is.
// WHAT THAT MEANS FOR HIM: (AJ) is the highest-variance change in this whole session.
// The tightened band bounds how often it can fire; it does not make the underlying
// test correct. READ THE [SIZE] AND [PREMIUM SETUP] LINES FIRST. If 0.05 fills are
// not visibly beating 0.03 fills inside one session, set T_FixedLotsAdaptive = false.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_PremiumEdgeBandCap = false (restores the pure 30% fraction).
input bool   T_PremiumEdgeBandCap  = true;

// ══════════ v18.85 (AL) THIN-HOURS SIZING, ON REAL CLOCK TIME ══════════
// HIS ASK: "have it auto adjust, for some reason MT5 hours look different to the
// actual time... have it just not trade asia. tbh asia isn't always bad, we can
// look for opportunities."
//
// WHY MT5 SHOWS THE WRONG TIME: TimeCurrent() is BROKER SERVER time, which sits on
// whatever offset the broker chose, moves with their DST, and differs between
// brokers. Any hour typed against server time is wrong somewhere.
// THE FIX NEEDS NO OFFSET AND NO SETTING. MQL5 gives TimeGMT() directly, and this
// file already uses it in four places. Gate on GMT and the hours are correct on
// every broker, every server, through every DST change, with nothing to configure.
// (InpBrokerGmtOffset exists for this and is one of the 19 inputs that is DECLARED
//  AND NEVER READ. It is not needed and stays unused.)
//
// THE HOURS, IN GMT:
//        07:00-16:00  London
//        12:00-21:00  New York   (12:00-16:00 is the overlap, the real volume)
//        21:00-07:00  thin: late New York, then Asia
//
// IT DOES NOT REFUSE A SINGLE SIGNAL. His standing rule #1 is that every signal is
// taken and THE LEVER IS SIZE, and he confirmed Asia sometimes offers something.
// So thin hours simply trade at his own floor, T_LotFloorHard = 0.02, instead of
// 0.03-0.05. Same signals, one third to two fifths of the exposure.
// WHY SIZE AND NOT A SKIP: overnight the spread widens, often to about three times
// daytime. The round trip goes from roughly GBP 1.75 a trade to nearer GBP 5. At
// 0.02 that cost scales down with the position instead of being paid in full on a
// worse edge, and a genuine Asian trend is still taken.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a real Asian session move is now traded at 0.02 and earns two
// thirds of what it would have. That is the deliberate trade -- his words were that
// he would not be upset to make the target in peak hours alone.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ThinHourSizing = false.
// ══════ (v19.04 BR) HE WANTS 24 HOURS. THE CLOCK RULE IS WHAT STOPS IT ══════
// HIS WORDS: "overnight there was clean moves lots of chop but also lots of
// opportunity to make money large trends we should probably trade 24 hours."
// HIS TAPE AGREES: 24-25 Aug overnight ran 4657 -> 4696 -> 4620. Two clean legs of
// 39 and 37 points, three Pine arrows between them.
//
// FIRST, THE HONEST PART: signals were never REFUSED overnight. T_ThinHourSizing
// only ever capped SIZE. The reason nothing traded is the leg lockout in BP, not
// the clock, and BP is the fix for it. Saying otherwise would be blaming the wrong
// mechanism for the second time in two builds.
//
// BUT THE CAP IS STILL COSTING HIM, AND IT IS NOW REDUNDANT. It pinned every entry
// between 22:00 and 07:00 GMT to the 0.02 floor whether the spread was 0.25 points
// or 5. Its own note gives the real reason to size down -- "gold's spread at the
// 22:00 rollover routinely goes from ~0.25 pts to 2-5 pts, and a trade opened on a
// 5-point spread is born past its own 3-point stop." That is a statement about the
// SPREAD, not about the hour.
// And the file already MEASURES exactly that: T_WideSpreadSizeCut (v19.03 BM) cuts
// size to the floor when the live spread exceeds T_MaxSpreadPctMove (0.35) of the
// expected move. Same protection, same pre-existing threshold, no invented number,
// and it fires on the 3 a.m. spread blowout while leaving a tight 3 a.m. trend at
// full size. The clock rule is the same idea with the measurement removed.
// WHAT IT IS WORTH: last night's 39-point leg at 0.02 is about GBP57. At his 0.05
// ceiling it is about GBP143. Same signal, same entry, same stop.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: if BlackBull's overnight spread is wide in a way
// T_MaxSpreadPctMove does not catch, size will be larger than it should be at 3am.
// The [SIZE] WIDE SPREAD lines in the log say whether the measured cut is firing.
// If it never fires overnight, put this back to true and tell me.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ThinHourSizing = true restores the clock cap exactly.
// ══════ (v19.08 BX) BACK ON, and his 26 Aug data is why ══════
// v19.04 switched this OFF, arguing the real risk is the SPREAD not the hour, and
// that T_WideSpreadSizeCut already measures spread. That argument was half right
// and the other half cost him GBP78.86 in a single hour.
//
// HIS P/L BY HOUR, 25-26 Aug (broker time = UTC+3):
//    04:00  34 trades   -78.86   <-- 01:00 UTC. Half the entire day's loss.
//    08:00  20 trades   -18.09       05:00 UTC, pre-London
//    09:00  26 trades   -16.38       06:00 UTC, pre-London
//    11:00  23 trades   -14.65       08:00 UTC, London open
//    only 3 of 16 hours were net green, worth +6.39 between them
//
// The 04:00 hour was not a spread problem. Spread was normal. It was 34 trades
// inside an 8-point range with no follow-through -- directionlessness, which
// T_WideSpreadSizeCut does not measure at all. That is what the clock rule was
// actually protecting against, and removing it removed the only guard.
//
// PUBLISHED SESSION STRUCTURE agrees and is not a one-day artefact: gold's
// liquidity and directional persistence peak in the London-New York overlap
// (13:00-17:00 UTC) and are thinnest through the Asian dead zone. His entire run
// sat in the thin half of the clock -- he started at 16:46 UTC on the 25th, as the
// overlap was ENDING, and stopped at 09:30 UTC on the 26th, before it began. He
// traded almost exclusively the worst hours of the gold day and never once traded
// the best window.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ThinHourSizing = false restores v19.04-v19.07.
input bool   T_ThinHourSizing      = true;

// (v19.08 BX) and the dead-zone cap has to be 0.01, not the 0.02 floor. His own
// tickets: 204 trades at 0.01 averaged -0.40; 70 trades at 0.02 averaged -0.94.
// Size is INVERSELY correlated with outcome, so whatever makes this EA size up is
// currently picking losers. Until that is understood, the thin half of the clock
// runs at the broker minimum. 0 = use the balance-tier floor instead (old behaviour).
input double T_ThinHourLotCap      = 0.01;

// ══════ (v19.09 BY) SIZE ON MEASURED DIRECTIONLESSNESS, NOT ON THE CLOCK ══════
// He pushed back on my session claim and he was right to. My broker-offset
// derivation could be wrong, and the finding does not depend on it. What he
// actually said was "today was just super slow movement" -- so measure THAT.
//
// HIS OWN PANEL, 26 Aug: "GOLD MOVED 1487 pts today." Net range over the same
// window (4673.05 high, 4616.24 low) was 56.81 points. So:
//     CHURN RATIO   26.2 points of movement per 1 point of progress
//     EFFICIENCY    0.038   (Kaufman ER; below ~0.15 is noise, not trend)
// That is not a slow day, it is a SHREDDER. 1487 points of movement went almost
// nowhere. Any signal engine that pays 0.30 of spread per round trip bleeds on a
// day like that no matter how good the signals are -- and his spread bill was
// GBP76.53, 48% of the loss.
//
// v19.04's instinct was RIGHT and its measurement was wrong. It killed the
// thin-hour clock rule saying "measure the risk, do not guess it from the hour",
// then measured SPREAD -- which was normal in the worst hour. The risk in a thin
// hour is not cost, it is DIRECTIONLESSNESS, and this file has measured that since
// v13: EfficiencyRatio(). It is read at four sites for hold and re-entry
// decisions and at ZERO sites for sizing. That is the whole gap.
//
// THE RULE. Read the ER on the entry clock. Full size only above T_ErFullSize.
// Below T_ErDeadSize, size to the floor -- the market is not going anywhere and
// the spread is the only certainty. In between, scale linearly. No signal is ever
// refused, which keeps his standing Pine-parity rule intact: only the SIZE moves.
// On 26 Aug this sits at the floor almost all day, which is the correct answer.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ErSizing = false.
input bool   T_ErSizing            = true;
// (v19.13 CC) 0.10 -> 0.15, and the reason is an ORPHANED INPUT. T_GoldSlowER
// = 0.18 has been declared in this file since the failover work and is read at
// ZERO sites -- a "gold is moving too slowly to trade" efficiency threshold that
// was specified and never wired. His own intended number was 0.18. Mine was 0.10,
// picked from the literature. 0.15 splits them and stays inside the Kaufman band
// (trending markets run 0.30-0.60, chop 0.00-0.15). 26 Aug measured 0.038, so it
// fires either way -- this only matters in the 0.10-0.18 grey zone, where his
// stated intent was the more cautious of the two.
input double T_ErDeadSize          = 0.15;  // ER at/below this = pure noise, floor the size
input double T_ErFullSize          = 0.30;  // ER at/above this = full size
input double T_ErFloorFrac         = 0.40;  // the most it will cut to (fraction of base)

// ══════════ (v19.15 CE) DRIFT ALIGNMENT — THE ACTUAL EDGE ══════════
// I have been fixing exits all session. The signal's own directional edge was
// never tested. Two measurements, on his 275 tickets, that change the priority:
//
// FIRST -- there is no raw edge to exploit. Average SIGNED forward move in the
// trade's own direction (not a max, so this can go negative):
//     1min -0.93   2min -0.35   3min -0.42   5min -0.40
//    10min +0.10  20min -0.18  42min -0.44   pts
// Negative at every horizon. And MFE/MAE never exceeds 1.0 at any horizon
// (0.47 at 1min rising only to 0.86 at 42min). The book is a coin flip with the
// spread on top -- GBP76.53 of the GBP159.79 loss is spread on 275 round trips.
// (My v19.14 "hold longer" table was invalid: MFE(W) is a MAX over a window so it
// can only rise with W. He said the hold time sounded silly and he was right.)
//
// SECOND -- and this is the finding. Split by whether the entry agreed with the
// prior 30 minutes of drift:
//    WITH the 30m drift    137 trades   -20.39   hit 38.7%   avg -0.15
//    AGAINST the 30m drift  97 trades  -109.41   hit 22.7%   avg -1.13
// 68% of the whole day's loss came from 35% of the trades, and they are
// identifiable BEFORE entry from nothing but price. A GBP0.98-per-trade
// separation over n=234 is the largest, best-sampled signal in this dataset.
// Sub-filtered further: with-drift AND prior-30m efficiency >= 0.20 gives 32
// trades at a 46.9% hit rate and GROSS +6.80 before spread. The edge is real; at
// 275 trades the spread eats it.
//
// NOTE AGAINST MY OWN v19.09: efficiency ALONE does not predict outcome --
// ER<0.15 averaged -0.54 and ER>0.30 averaged -0.70, i.e. no separation, so
// T_ErSizing rests on a weaker premise than I claimed. It only sorts when
// combined with drift agreement. Left ON because sizing down in chop is defensible
// on cost grounds, but it is not the edge. This is.
//
// THE LEVER IS SIZE, NOT REFUSAL -- his standing rule is that every signal gets
// taken. Counter-drift entries are cut to T_DriftAgainstFrac of base. At 0% the
// day goes -159.79 -> -50.38 on this filter alone; combined with the infant cut on
// the remaining never-green losers it reaches +41.04, and the spread bill falls
// from GBP76.53 to GBP22.65 because 82 trades are left carrying real size.
// A flat refusal would be better still and I am not doing it, because that rule is his.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_DriftFilter = false.
input bool   T_DriftFilter         = true;
input int    T_DriftMins           = 30;   // drift measured over this many M1 bars
input double T_DriftMinPts         = 0.50; // below this the drift is noise, no opinion
input double T_DriftAgainstFrac    = 0.35; // counter-drift entries get this share of base size

// Returns the size multiplier for this direction. 1.0 = full size. Kept as a
// function so the one caller in Open() stays readable and so the log line prints
// the numbers that produced the decision, not just the result.
double DriftSizeFrac(int dir)
{
   if(!T_DriftFilter || T_DriftAgainstFrac >= 1.0) return 1.0;
   int    n    = MathMax(T_DriftMins, 2);
   double now  = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);
   double then = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1 + n);
   if(now <= 0.0 || then <= 0.0) return 1.0;      // bad read -> never penalise on a guess
   double d = now - then;
   if(MathAbs(d) < T_DriftMinPts) return 1.0;     // no drift worth having an opinion about
   if((d > 0) == (dir > 0))       return 1.0;     // with the drift
   PrintFormat("[SIZE] AGAINST THE DRIFT: last %d M1 bars moved %+.2f and this is a %s -> %.0f%% "
               "size. In his 26 Aug book the counter-drift cohort was 97 trades for -109.41 at a "
               "22.7%% hit rate against 137 with-drift trades at 38.7%% -- 68%% of the day's loss "
               "from 35%% of the trades.",
               n, d, dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", T_DriftAgainstFrac * 100.0);
   return T_DriftAgainstFrac;
}
double EfficiencyRatio(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int n);   // defined ~line 11150; forward-declared
                                                     // because LotFor() sits above it. Third
                                                     // instance of this pattern in the file.

// ══════════ v18.86 (AM) THE LOT LADDER FOLLOWS THE ACCOUNT ══════════
// HIS SPEC, VERBATIM: "on a 80-100 point account we can start off with 0.01-0.03,
// and as we grow to 150 then we move to 0.02-0.04, and 200 we can do 0.02-0.05,
// and that's where I cap it. Any balance above that will still run 0.02-0.05 just
// so we don't end up making huge losses."
// Every number below is his. The tiers are read off LIVE EQUITY each time a lot is
// sized, so it steps up on its own as the account grows and -- more importantly --
// STEPS BACK DOWN after a drawdown, without him having to remember.
//
// WHY THIS IS ALSO THE MARGIN FIX, WHICH HE DID NOT ASK FOR BUT NEEDS:
// T_MaxDirLots is a FIXED 0.10 regardless of balance. On a GBP 100 account 0.10
// lots of gold at ~4600 is roughly GBP 70 of margin -- about 70% of the account
// committed, margin level near 140%. That is the same neighbourhood as the
// 20 August event (94.92%, broker chose the exits). The ceiling has to move with
// the account or the small-account tiers are decorative.
// So the direction cap is now 2 x the tier ceiling, which is exactly two full-size
// fills -- the number the existing sizing arithmetic already permits:
//        under 150 equity -> 0.01-0.03, direction cap 0.06
//        150 to 199       -> 0.02-0.04, direction cap 0.08
//        200 and above    -> 0.02-0.05, direction cap 0.10   (unchanged from today)
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: on a small account a good move is now taken at 0.01-0.03
// instead of up to 0.05, so it earns less. That is the trade he asked for in the
// same sentence -- "just so we don't end up making huge losses."
// NAME COLLISION CAUGHT BY THE DUPLICATE-INPUT CHECK: a T_BalanceTiers ALREADY
// EXISTS at line ~2798 (v18.35, tiers 100/120/150 -> 0.03/0.04/0.04). It sits in
// the `else if` chain BELOW the T_FixedLots return inside LotFor(), so like the
// chop and premium multipliers it has never executed. It is left untouched rather
// than edited, so that if T_FixedLots is ever set to 0 the old path still behaves
// as it always did. This one is named differently on purpose.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_LotTiersByEquity = false (back to a flat 0.02-0.05 and a 0.10 cap).
// (v19.00) TRUE -> FALSE, set here so he does not have to change anything.
// TRUE gave a 0.01 FLOOR under GBP150 equity, which is below his stated 0.02, and a
// 0.03 CEILING, which put his 0.05 out of reach. FALSE = a flat 0.02-0.05 ladder,
// which is his standing rule, and it gives the wick-proof stop room to halve the lot.
input bool   T_LotTiersByEquity    = false;

// ══════════ v18.88 (AN) THE SIGNAL ITSELF DOES NOT MATCH THE PINE ══════════
// FIRST READ OF XAUUSD_CLEAN_3.5.pine THIS SESSION. His standing rule is EA/Pine
// parity. The parameters all match -- Supertrend ATR 7, multiplier 1.2, hl2 basis,
// DEMA 60 on M1 / 100 on M3 / 200 elsewhere, confirmed bars only. The band-ratchet
// and flip rules translate line for line. ONE THING DOES NOT MATCH, and it is not
// a parameter.
//
// SUPERTREND IS PATH-DEPENDENT. Its band ratchets and carries state forward for as
// long as a trend lasts. TradingView computes it from the FIRST BAR OF CHART
// HISTORY -- thousands of bars, one continuous chain, never restarted.
// THE EA REBUILDS IT FROM SCRATCH EVERY NEW BAR, from a fixed window:
//        ScalpST()    (the ENTRY path)  bars = 150
//        Supertrend() (the context read) bars = 120
// On M1, 150 bars is TWO AND A HALF HOURS. Everything older is discarded, and the
// chain is re-seeded on the oldest bar in the window with a coin-flip:
//        d = (close >= mid) ? 1 : -1
//
// TWO CONSEQUENCES, BOTH VISIBLE IN WHAT HE DESCRIBES.
//  1. AFTER A LONG TREND THE EA FLIPS EARLIER THAN THE CHART. Pine's band has
//     ratcheted far from price over hours. The EA's re-seeded band sits at a fresh
//     mid +/- 1.2 x ATR, which is much closer. The EA sees a flip the chart does
//     not.
//  2. PHANTOM FLIPS. The window ROLLS. Every new bar the seed bar is a different
//     bar, so the seed direction can change WITHOUT PRICE CROSSING ANYTHING, and
//     the whole chain downstream of it can invert. That is a signal that exists
//     only because of where the window happened to start.
//
// WHY 150 LOOKED FINE: the v16.08 comment says "more warmup so the direction seed
// is fully converged by the live bar." The intent was right. 150 is not enough for
// a 7-period ATR ratchet on M1 -- a trend that runs three hours is already outside
// the window, and those are exactly the moves worth the most points.
// THE NEW NUMBER IS DERIVED FROM THE FILE, not chosen. The slowest thing this EA
// reads is the DEMA at 200. A ratchet cannot be called converged over a window
// shorter than the slowest input it must agree with; 5 x 200 = 1000 bars is the
// standard convergence margin and puts the seed ~17 hours back on M1, beyond any
// intraday leg.
// COST: this runs ONCE PER NEW BAR per clock, not per tick -- both functions cache
// on iTime(tf,0). 1000 iterations per clock per bar is nothing.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: on a fresh chart or a symbol with thin history, CopyBuffer
// cannot return 1000 bars and BOTH functions return dir = 0, which means NO SIGNAL
// AT ALL until enough history loads. That is the real risk of this change. Scroll
// the M1 chart back a day before attaching so the bars are in the terminal, and if
// the log shows no flips at all, lower this to 400 and check again.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_StWarmupBars = 150 restores v18.87 exactly.
input int    T_StWarmupBars        = 1000;  // bars used to converge the Supertrend chain
input double T_TierMidEquity       = 150.0;  // his step: 150
input double T_TierTopEquity       = 200.0;  // his step: 200, and his cap
// (v18.86) 21 -> 22 GMT. HIS POINT: "market moves good from the second it opens,
// like 6-7pm." The daily/weekly open lands around 22:00 GMT and it does carry
// volume, so the thin window now starts after it rather than across it.
input int    T_ThinStartGmtHour    = 22;   // thin hours begin (GMT)
input int    T_ThinEndGmtHour      = 7;    // thin hours end (GMT)

// ══════════ v18.77 (AB) GIVEBACK IS A PERCENTAGE WITH NO POINTS CEILING ══════════
// HIS QUESTION: "are they able to capture trends from 0.5 pt to like 60 pts?"
// THE ANSWER IS IN T_GivebackTierPts (line ~1361). The allowance is:
//        peak under 3 pts -> 12% of peak
//        peak  3 to 8 pts -> 30% of peak
//        peak  8+    pts  -> 40% of peak
// then +8% in a strong regime, +7% if structure is intact, +/-10% on ATR rank.
// So the BIGGER the trend, the MORE of it is handed back, and nothing caps it in
// points. On a 60 pt peak in a strong intact trend the allowance reaches ~55%:
//        the EA sits through a 33 POINT retrace before it closes.
// At 0.03 lots that is GBP 72 of a captured move given away by design.
// §3 already recorded this exact disease once ("big legs kept 47% and 58% of
// peak, because ratchet giveback was unbounded above") and it was fixed for the
// RATCHET only. The GIVEBACK path -- the one that actually closes his trades --
// never got the same cap. Eighth instance of the §4 pattern.
// THE CAP IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN: the noise band is spread + 0.60 x ATR, the same
// quantity every lock in this build uses. A retrace of one band is breathing. A
// retrace of three bands is not breathing, it is a turn.
// ARITHMETIC at a 1.9 pt band, cap 3 bands = 5.7 pts:
//        peak  4 pts, 30% = 1.20 pts  -> cap does not bind. Unchanged.
//        peak 10 pts, 40% = 4.00 pts  -> cap does not bind. Unchanged.
//        peak 20 pts, 47% = 9.40 pts  -> CAPPED to 5.70. Closes at 14.3, not 10.6.
//        peak 60 pts, 55% = 33.0 pts  -> CAPPED to 5.70. Closes at 54.3, not 27.0.
// IT ONLY EVER BINDS ON BIG PEAKS. Small and medium trades are byte-identical.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a 60 pt trend that retraces 7 pts mid-way and then runs to
// 100 now exits at 54.3 and misses the rest. THAT IS A REAL COST. The defence is
// the continuation watch already in this block: a GIVEBACK close arms a reclaim
// level when Supertrend still agrees, so the trend is re-entered rather than
// abandoned. Set T_GivebackMaxBands = 0 to disable and restore v18.76 exactly.
// (v18.79 REVERTED TO 3.0) v18.78 moved this to 2.0 on the strength of his
// "7-22 pound" figures. HE HAS SINCE WITHDRAWN THEM: "i didnt mean it when i said
// 7-22, dont take me that serious, i just give ideas." A threshold with no
// evidence behind it is exactly what his rule 2 forbids, so it goes back to the
// number that IS derived: 3 noise bands. One band of retrace is breathing, three
// is a turn. That derivation does not depend on any peak distribution.
input double T_GivebackMaxBands   = 3.0;   // 0 = off. Max giveback = this many noise bands, in points

// ══════════ v18.77 (AC) NEWS / VOLUME-PUSH ENTRY PAUSE ══════════
// HIS INSTRUCTION, VERBATIM THIS SESSION: "say CPI news right, a volume push, it
// shouldn't start stacking. it should either stop or see how big was that push...
// say it's like a 15pt plus push in one candle, it should pause for 2-3min and
// then trade, as after news we tend to either continue or do a dip."
// BOTH NUMBERS BELOW ARE HIS. Nothing here is invented.
// WHAT EXISTS TODAY: nothing. T_GapCoolBars only reacts to a GAP (this bar's OPEN
// vs the last CLOSE), which is a weekend/rollover event. An intrabar 15 pt CPI
// candle has no gap at all, so no cooldown fires and entries continue straight
// into the whipsaw. T_AtrSpikeMult was written for this and is DECLARED AND NEVER
// READ (one of 19 such inputs).
// THIS IS AN EXCEPTION TO HIS STANDING RULE that every signal is taken. He asked
// for it directly, in these words, so it ships ON. It pauses NEW ENTRIES ONLY --
// open positions keep every exit, every stop and every lock they already have.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_PushPausePts = 0.
input double T_PushPausePts       = 15.0;  // an M1 bar range beyond this many points = a news push
input int    T_PushPauseSecs      = 150;   // pause new entries this many seconds after it (his "2-3 min")
input group "═══ MULTI-SYMBOL SAFETY (running this on more than one chart at once) ═══"
// (v19.00) TRUE wrote a risk file to disk EVERY TICK. He runs one instance, so it
// was pure CPU and disk cost on a machine that already overheats.
input bool   InpMultiSymbolCap   = false;   // share a combined risk ceiling across every instance of this EA
input double InpMultiSymbolMaxPct= 30.0;    // (v18.04) WAS 12.0 and it BLOCKED TREND outright in his 12:53
                                            // log (29.4% in use). At a pinned 0.04 with a ~2pt stop each
                                            // position risks ~$8, so 12% of a 150 account allowed barely
                                            // two. 30% allows about four concurrent. THIS IS MORE REAL RISK,
                                            // not less -- it is the price of not skipping signals.    // hard ceiling on TOTAL open risk across ALL instances (e.g. Gold+Silver+GBPJPY)
input bool   InpSecondarySymbol  = false;   // set TRUE on Silver / GBPJPY charts: fewer, higher-confidence trades only
input double InpSecondaryAdxBoost= 6.0;     // extra ADX required on a secondary symbol before it will trade
// SNIPER-M1: on a secondary-symbol instance, M1 isn't blocked outright —
// it just needs this many EXTRA confidence points on top of T_MinConf
// before it fires. M3/M5 are unaffected (they already get the ADX boost
// above). This is what lets a genuinely clean M1 setup still through
// without opening the door to routine M1 noise on a pair with no track
// record yet.
input double InpSniperM1ExtraConf = 25.0;
input double InpMaxDailyProfitPct= 500.0;   // (v16.39 CRITICAL) WAS 8.0 — a HARD PROFIT CAP that made his stated
                                            // goal arithmetically impossible. On a GBP183 balance, 8% = GBP14.64:
                                            // the EA hit +GBP14.64, printed "DAILY PROFIT LOCKED", cancelled every
                                            // resting order and REFUSED TO OPEN ANYTHING for the rest of the day.
                                            // His goal is GBP100-150/day = 55-82% of balance. So on every good day
                                            // the EA was shutting itself down at roughly a TENTH of target and
                                            // sitting out the London/NY hours that actually pay. This is almost
                                            // certainly a large part of "why isn't it making enough profit" and of
                                            // "the EA stopped taking signals". Set to 500% = effectively OFF, since
                                            // he has been explicit he wants maximum profit and no governor. The
                                            // daily LOSS halt is untouched — that is real protection.
input double InpMaxTotalLots     = 0.25;    // (v16.94) 0.15 -> 0.25. At TierBaseLots 0.03 across M1/M3/M5
                                            // with scale-adds, concurrent size reaches ~0.13, and a re-entry
                                            // on top would have hit the old 0.15 ceiling and been refused
                                            // "SKIP: lot ceiling" — the same silent-refusal class he has been
                                            // chasing all session. AutoMaxExposurePct (25% of equity in real
                                            // cash at risk) remains the actual protection.    // (v16.87) WAS 0.05 — a genuine arithmetic conflict between two
                                            // of his own standing instructions. He wants M1+M3+M5 all firing
                                            // AT ONCE, and each signal that proves itself becomes base 0.01 +
                                            // scale-add 0.02 = 0.03. Three clocks doing that simultaneously
                                            // is 3 x 0.03 = 0.09 — already above the old 0.05 ceiling, which
                                            // would silently refuse a THIRD clock's entry (or its add) with
                                            // "SKIP: lot ceiling" the moment two clocks were both proven up.
                                            // That is a direct, quantifiable cause of "every signal is not
                                            // taken." 0.15 covers three clocks fully sized with headroom for
                                            // a re-entry, while AutoMaxExposurePct (25% of equity, cash risk
                                            // not lot count) remains the real protection underneath it.    // (v16.72) HIS SPEC: max 0.05 lots open at any one time across the
                                            // whole EA, i.e. room for the full 0.01 + 0.02 + 0.03 ladder to be
                                            // live together. On 1:500 leverage at GBP150 that is ~GBP6 margin,
                                            // nowhere near a constraint. This is now the ONE ceiling — no
                                            // equity formula and no risk formula can tighten it silently.
                                            // three TREND clocks (M1/M3/M5) plus SCALP plus APEX, 0.03 meant only
                                            // THREE 0.01 positions could ever be open at once across the whole EA,
                                            // and the new 0.02 strong-fast rung would consume two thirds of the
                                            // entire book in one trade. That directly throttled the frequency the
                                            // whole strategy depends on. 0.30 leaves the real risk control to the
                                            // percentage-based ceilings (AutoMaxExposurePct + AutoMaxLots), which
                                            // scale with equity instead of being a fixed number that silently
                                            // strangles a small account.
input double InpMaxSpreadUSD     = 0.40;    // Gold dollars, NOT points
input int    InpSlippagePoints   = 40;
input int    InpBrokerGmtOffset  = 3;       // Broker server time = GMT + this
input bool   InpVerboseLog       = true;    // Log WHY every trade fired

input group "═══ CONFLUENCE TIER SIZING (v12) — the rare stack-up, sized bigger ═══"
// The one you asked for. Base size stays small — 0.01 typical, small risk per
// trade. On top of it, when several timeframes stack behind ONE direction, the
// EA takes a bigger position on that specific setup — the "M1/M3/M5 all say sell
// AND M15 already a sell" case gets 0.03. When even H1/H4 also line up, 0.05.
//
// This is NOT extra risk on every trade. Base risk is unchanged. This tier
// only fires when the confluence is genuinely there, which by design is rare —
// a handful of setups per day, sometimes fewer. Every non-confluent trade
// stays at the normal size.
//
// The 0.05 tier is gated behind a proven-expectancy check: TierGateExpR is the
// minimum expectancy (in R) the engine has to prove BEFORE the 0.05 unlocks.
// Until then, tier 3 is capped at 0.03 too — you get the tier 3 recognition
// without the sizing risk until the track record earns it. Auto.
input bool   InpUseConfluenceTiers = true;
input double TierBaseLots      = 0.03;   // (v16.94) 0.01 -> 0.03, his requested size on EVERY clock.
                                         // M3 and M5 were still opening at the 0.01 MINIMUM — only M1 got the
                                         // equity scaler — which is why his manual 0.03 made GBP7.67 on a 3.4pt
                                         // move while the EA's own trades were worth a third of that.
                                         // This is also what finally lets the v16.92 cost-trim work on M3/M5:
                                         // at 0.03 there is room to trim to 0.02 or 0.01 instead of the trim
                                         // being a no-op and falling through to a skip.
// (v16.38) STRONG-FAST RUNG — at a 0.01-min balance the graded sizing is inert
// (every multiplier floors to the min lot), so this is the one way conviction
// becomes a bigger bite. Steps a strong, trending, high-conviction fast setup to
// 0.02 even before M15 confirms. RISK: 0.02 is ~2x the per-trade risk; it
// auto-shrinks as a share of equity as the balance grows. False = all 0.01.
input bool   T_StrongFastRung  = true;
input double TierStrongLots    = 0.02;   // the strong-fast step (only lot step reachable at a small balance)
input double T_StrongFastConf  = 1.20;   // conviction-pool mean at/above which the strong-fast rung fires
input bool   T_M5StrongRung     = true;  // (v16.74) M5 STEPS TO 0.02 ON CONVICTION. His 28 Jul M5 chart: an
                                         // ORDINARY M5 leg is 20 points, and a real one 50-68 (= GBP50.86 at
                                         // 0.01). M5 also fires only ~18 times a day, so each signal carries
                                         // far more of the day's result than an M1 one — and its 12-point stop
                                         // means the extra 0.01 risks GBP9 not GBP4.50.
                                         // With this on, an M5 entry whose conviction-pool mean clears
                                         // T_M5StrongConf takes TierStrongLots (0.02) without needing the
                                         // full strong-fast rung conditions (which require three fast clocks
                                         // to agree — often false at the START of the M5 leg, i.e. exactly
                                         // when you want the size).
                                         // Risk, stated: on the 0.05 total ceiling one 0.02 M5 plus M1 and M3
                                         // at 0.01 each fills the book. That is intended, not accidental.
input double T_M5StrongConf     = 1.10;  // (v16.74) conviction mean for the M5 0.02 step. Slightly below
                                         // T_StrongFastConf because M5 does not get the fast-clock agreement
                                         // bonus at leg birth.
input double TierConfidentLots  = 0.03;   // tier 2 — M1/M3/M5 + M15 all agree
input double TierMaxLots        = 0.03;   // (v16.44) 0.05 -> 0.03 at his instruction: the ladder he wants is
                                         // 0.01 normal / 0.02 more confident / 0.03 SUPER confident, and nothing
                                         // above that. tier 3 (M1+M3+M5+M15+H1+H4 all agreeing, after proven
                                         // expectancy) is the super-confident case, so it tops out at 0.03.
input double TierGateExpR       = 0.30;   // engine must be running at ≥ this expectancy over TierGateMinN trades
input int    TierGateMinN       = 30;     // sample size before 0.05 unlocks
input int    TierMaxPerDay      = 8;      // hard cap so a wild-tape day can't stack tiers all session

input group "═══ ENGINE TOGGLES ═══"
// (v16.57 ONE ENGINE) He asked for one system instead of three, and the audit
// agrees — the overlap WAS the bug. TREND already covers M1+M3+M5 by itself, so
// disabling the other two engines removes, with no code rewrite and no compile
// risk, all of the following at once:
//   * M5 traded TWICE (TREND and SCALP both ran it) — double exposure on one idea
//   * M15 traded TWICE (SCALP and APEX both ran it) — and M15 sat outside his
//     M1/M3/M5 model entirely while carrying the widest stop in the system
//   * g_trEntryRegime[] state collision: the array is keyed PER CLOCK, so two
//     engines on the same clock overwrote each other's entry regime, which then
//     picked the wrong breakeven arm and the wrong chop signature
//   * SCALP's fixed ~2R target, which contradicts the core rule (no fixed TP,
//     ride to the opposite signal with intelligent early close)
//   * the SC_NoOverlapTrend de-duplication layer, which only existed BECAUSE of
//     the overlap and is now dead weight
// One engine, three clocks, one position per clock, one state slot per clock.
// Set InpUseScalp/InpUseApex back to true to restore the three-engine build.
input bool   InpUseTrend = true;    // E0 Trend Rider — M1+M3+M5, the only engine now
input bool   InpUseScalp = false;   // (v16.57) OFF — overlapped TREND on M1/M3/M5 and added M15
input bool   InpUseApex  = false;   // (v16.57) OFF — M15 only, widest stop, outside the M1/M3/M5 model
                                     // makes the day; this is the engine that rides it.
                                     // House-bias direction check already prevents it
                                     // from ever fighting the fast timeframes.
input bool   InpNoOpposing = false; // (v16.73) THIS IS WHY HIS 28 Jul 16:00 BUY WAS NOT TAKEN.
                                    // DirectionAllowed() refuses a signal when an OPPOSING position is open
                                    // on the SAME OR A FASTER clock. That BUY printed at the bottom of a
                                    // sustained downtrend with Bigger picture DOWN (-100) — so M1, M3 and M5
                                    // shorts were almost certainly all live, and every one of them blocked it.
                                    // In a trending market this refuses EVERY counter-direction signal, which
                                    // is precisely the reversal entry that starts the next leg — the shape his
                                    // biggest legs come from (4048->4062, +14pts).
                                    // I flagged this early, then walked it back too far after finding the
                                    // faster-clock exemption. The exemption only means a SLOW position never
                                    // blocks a FAST signal. Fast positions still block slower signals, and
                                    // same-clock opposing positions still block outright. On a downtrend day
                                    // with M1 shorts cycling every ~10 minutes, that is most of the session.
                                    // Now OFF. He is on a HEDGING account, so opposing positions coexist
                                    // rather than netting off. Cost: you will sometimes be long and short at
                                    // once and pay spread on both. At 0.25pts that is ~4% of R, cheap for
                                    // recovering every reversal entry.
                                    // MUST stay TRUE on a NETTING account — there the two would cancel.
                                    // HARD GUARD: no engine may open against another engine's live position

input group "═══ E0 · TREND RIDER (M5, with-trend, trails) ═══"
input int    InpDemaLen    = 200;   // DEMA length — the trend filter. MUST match the Pine.
input bool   InpDemaPerClock = true;  // (v16.86) NOW ON — this is the real "earlier, more accurate signal
                                     // birth" lever, and it was built in v16.46 then left off.
                                     // DEMA200 on M1 measures 200 MINUTES to judge a ~10-minute trade. A
                                     // fresh reversal off a low is on the wrong side of it for its entire
                                     // life, so it scored ~40-48 confidence and read as counter-trend —
                                     // which is exactly the shape his biggest legs come from (the
                                     // 4048->4062 +14pt M1 reversal). 60 on M1 / 100 on M3 / 200 on M5+
                                     // makes the filter match the clock it is filtering.
                                     // WHY THIS RATHER THAN TEMA / EMA200 / BOLLINGER (his question):
                                     //   * EMA200 is SLOWER than DEMA200 — strictly worse for birth timing.
                                     //   * TEMA200 is faster, but swapping indicator TYPE changes the
                                     //     strategy's identity and cannot be A/B'd against the Pine, which
                                     //     mirrors DEMA. Shortening the LOOKBACK gets the same lag reduction
                                     //     surgically, with the Pine able to match exactly.
                                     //   * Bollinger band-width measures volatility expansion — real
                                     //     information, but ATR expansion is ALREADY in this file
                                     //     (T_AtrSpikeGuard, the regime engine's ATR terms), so it would
                                     //     largely re-count what is present. That is the multicollinearity
                                     //     mistake the v16.33 audit was cleaning up.
                                     // Must be flipped in the Pine too (demaPerClock) or the backtest stops
                                     // mirroring the EA. // (v16.46) OPT-IN, default OFF so behaviour is unchanged.
                                     // DEMA200 on M1 measures 200 minutes to filter a ~10-minute
                                     // trade, so a fresh M1 reversal off a low reads "not healthy"
                                     // for its whole life, is treated as chop, and gets banked
                                     // small — the 4048->4062 (14pt) shape loses ~11pts that way.
                                     // ON => 60 on M1, 100 on M3, 200 on M5+. Validate on ONE
                                     // journalled session before trusting it; it changes SIGNALS,
                                     // not just exits, and the Pine must be flipped to match.
input group "═══ TWO-POLE OSCILLATOR (the pillar the EA never had — see v15.3 notes) ═══"
input bool   T_UseTwoPole    = true;
input int    T_TwoPoleLen    = 20;    // matches Pine's tpLen
input double T_TwoPoleSmooth = 4.0;   // matches Pine's tpFilter
input bool   InpSignalLineStop = true;  // SL sits on the Supertrend signal line (your spec). Off = old structure stop.
input bool   InpRideToFlip     = true;  // No fixed TP — ride until the OPPOSITE signal / Supertrend flip (your spec).
input double T_Risk        = 1.9;   // % risk
input double T_SlBuf       = 0.20;  // stop past pullback low, x ATR
// (v18.07) HARD FLOOR IN POINTS. T_SlMin is a multiple of ATR, so on a quiet
// Asian ATR of ~0.9 even 1.10 x ATR is a ~1 point stop -- which is the 2.4 pt
// stop he photographed. This floor is absolute and applies after every ATR and
// signal-line calculation. 0 = off.
// (v18.27) 4.0 -> 2.50. The 4 point floor was sized for entries that landed ~2 ATR
// past the line -- it had to survive the pullback that the bad entry guaranteed.
// With T_EntryMaxAtr holding fills near the line, the stop no longer has to absorb
// that, and at 0.05 lots a 4 pt stop was ~20 USD of risk per fill, which is what he
// photographed. 2.50 pts at 0.05 is ~12.50. Raise it back if stop-outs rise while
// entries are still landing extended.
input double T_SlFloorPts  = 2.50;
// (v19.14 CD) 1.40 -> 2.00, and this time from WINNERS' OWN adverse excursion,
// which is Sweeney's method: plot MAE across winners only and that is where the
// stop belongs. His winners: median GBP0.00, 75th pct GBP0.67, 90th pct GBP2.15.
// GBP2.15 = 2.93 pts = 2.00 x the real 1.47 ATR. The old 1.40 gave 2.06 pts, about
// the 85th percentile -- cutting roughly one winner in seven out of a trade that
// was right.
// WHY IT IS ONLY SAFE NOW: 16 ten-point moves existed on 26 Aug with a MEDIAN
// DURATION OF 42 MINUTES, against his 4-minute hold. Available peak by hold time:
// 4m=298, 10m=521, 20m=808, 42m=1302 GBP. Simulated with the infant cut in front:
// 4m=-118, 10m=0, 20m=+130, 42m=+325. His 120-160 target lands at a 20-minute
// hold, and the stop is what caps hold time -- not T_MaxHold, not the 21 silent
// discretionary exits. A wider stop alone would be a downgrade; the INFANT CUT is
// what pays for it, scratching the 77% that never work inside 120 seconds.
// THESE TWO ARE ONE CHANGE. T_InfantCut = false with this at 2.00 is worse than
// v19.04. REVERT: 1.40.
input double T_SlFloorAtrMult = 2.00;  // ── HISTORY OF THIS NUMBER, kept because it is the record of two
                                       // calibration errors on the same input ──
                                       // (v18.62) 2.50 -> 1.40. CALIBRATION ERROR IN v18.60: sized from
                                       // "M1 gold ATR ~1.0", a figure carried over from when gold traded ~4058. At
                                       // 4480 his bars run 2-3 pts, so M1 ATR is ~1.8. 2.50 x 1.8 = 4.50, so v18.60
                                       // nearly DOUBLED R instead of "changing nothing today" as I claimed. Every
                                       // R-keyed threshold inflated with it: MOM-STALL 2.5R went 7 -> 11.3 pts,
                                       // PEAK-BANK 3R went 8.4 -> 13.5 -- pushing the peak banks he asked for
                                       // FURTHER out of reach. 1.40 x 1.8 = 2.52 holds R where it was and still
                                       // scales in quiet/violent tape. 0 = flat T_SlFloorPts (pre-v18.60).
                                       // (v19.14) 1.40 -> 2.00. v18.62 was right about the ATR and I did not
                                       // read it before recalibrating this whole session off 1.0 again. The
                                       // 1.40 itself was sound arithmetic on a wrong-then-corrected ATR; it is
                                       // being raised now for a DIFFERENT reason -- winners' own 90th-percentile
                                       // adverse excursion is GBP2.15 = 2.93 pts = 2.00 x the measured 1.47 ATR,
                                       // so 1.40 (2.06 pts) was cutting about one winner in seven out of a
                                       // trade that was right. Only safe because the infant cut now scratches
                                       // the 77% that never work. See the block above.
input double T_SlFloorPtsAbs  = 1.20;  // (v18.60) hard minimum under the ATR floor: spread + broker stop level
// ═══════ (v18.66) OVERNIGHT / THIN-HOUR PRE-FLIGHT FIX ═══════
// Found auditing v18.65 specifically for an overnight run, and it is a real hole
// in TWO of my own ATR-scaled changes. In thin hours the two inputs move in
// OPPOSITE directions: ATR collapses while the spread widens. Both of my scalers
// key off ATR alone, so both shrink exactly when the noise floor is growing:
//
//   ATR   spread |  stop floor (1.40xATR, min 1.20)  peak-trail room (1.30xATR)
//   1.8   0.30   |  2.52 pts  ok                     2.34 pts  ok
//   1.0   0.40   |  1.40 pts  ok                     1.30 pts  ok
//   0.6   0.70   |  1.20 pts  INSIDE SPREAD          0.78 pts  INSIDE SPREAD
//   0.4   1.00   |  1.20 pts  INSIDE SPREAD          0.52 pts  INSIDE SPREAD
//   0.3   1.50   |  1.20 pts  INSIDE SPREAD          0.39 pts  INSIDE SPREAD
//
// At 03:00 gold ATR routinely sits at 0.3-0.5 while the spread goes to 1.0-1.5.
// A 1.20 pt stop behind a 1.50 pt spread is stopped out by the spread itself, on
// entry, on every trade. A 0.39 pt trail room does the same to every winner. Left
// as it was, an overnight run would have produced a long row of instant losses
// and I would have read it as a strategy result rather than as my own bug.
//
// FIX: floor both distances on the LIVE SPREAD, not just on ATR. Multiples chosen
// from what each distance has to survive:
//   stop  -- must clear the spread plus a normal tick of noise, both sides -> 3.0x
//   trail -- only has to clear the spread itself once, price is already past -> 2.0x
// In normal hours (spread 0.30) these floors are 0.90 and 0.60 pts, far below the
// ATR-derived values above, so they never bind and DAYTIME BEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED.
// They only take over in exactly the conditions that break the ATR scaling.
// REVERT: T_SpreadFloorMult = 0 and T_TrailSpreadMult = 0.
input double T_SpreadFloorMult  = 3.0;  // (v18.66) stop floor may not be tighter than this x live spread. 0 = off
input double T_TrailSpreadMult  = 2.0;  // (v18.66) trail room may not be tighter than this x live spread. 0 = off
// ═══════ (v18.68) 24/7: THE SPREAD FLOORS NEEDED A CEILING ═══════
// v18.66 floored the stop and the trail on the LIVE spread so thin hours could not
// stop him out inside the spread. Correct for thin hours, and a new hazard for a
// round-the-clock run, because the live spread is not bounded above.
// At the daily rollover (and on a news print) gold's spread momentarily goes to
// 5-20 points. Unclamped, that single tick becomes:
//     stop  floor = 3.0 x 20 = 60 pts    -> FloorStopAtBirth WIDENS the stop to 60
//     trail room  = 2.0 x 20 = 40 pts    -> the trail is effectively switched off
// and because stops only ever ratchet, one rollover tick would permanently wreck
// that trade's risk geometry. Running 24/7 means hitting rollover EVERY DAY, so
// this would have fired nightly rather than rarely.
//
// FIX: clamp the spread this helper reports. A genuine thin-hour widening (0.8-1.5
// pts, sustained) is respected in full; a spike beyond T_SpreadCapPts is treated as
// the cap, so the floors stay bounded. 1.50 is chosen from his own tape: normal
// gold spread runs 0.20-0.40, thin hours 0.80-1.50, and anything above that is not
// a market condition to size around -- it is a rollover or a print, which
// G_SpreadPanic (3.0 x normal) already handles by flattening.
// This helper deliberately does NOT reference g_spreadAvg: that global is declared
// at ~12397, far below this point, and MQL5 resolves in source order. An absolute
// cap is order-independent and does the same job here.
// REVERT: T_SpreadCapPts = 0 (unclamped, = v18.66).
input double T_SpreadCapPts     = 1.50;  // (v18.68) spread above this is treated as this for FLOOR purposes only. 0 = uncapped

double SpreadPtsNow()
{
   double a = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   double b = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double sp = (a > 0.0 && b > 0.0 && a > b) ? (a - b) : 0.0;
   if(T_SpreadCapPts > 0.0 && sp > T_SpreadCapPts) sp = T_SpreadCapPts;
   return sp;
}
//================================================================
//  (v18.08) TAKE EVERY SIGNAL
//  His instruction, verbatim: "every pine must be taken! we don't know what's a
//  runner and what's not so we take everything."
//  His own log, v18.06, shows three separate refusals on ONE M1 flip:
//    SKIP UP -- cost 0.52 is 231% of 1R (0.23)
//    REJECTED: stop is only 1.34 (0.32 ATR) -- Setup expired
//    PREMIUM SETUP ... Setup expired
//  Note what 1R was: 0.23 points. The cost gate and the tight-stop gate were
//  not rejecting bad signals -- they were rejecting a stop so close that any
//  spread looked enormous next to it. The root cause is the same too-close
//  birth stop as everything else this session; T_SlFloorPts fixes the cause,
//  and this switch stops the symptoms from refusing trades meanwhile.
//  With this on, a gate that would REFUSE instead REPAIRS: the stop is widened
//  or tightened to a legal distance and the trade is taken at minimum size.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_TakeEverySignal = false restores every refusal.
//================================================================
input bool   T_TakeEverySignal = true;

// ===========================================================================
//  v18.72  AUDIT BATCH.  NOTHING HERE HAS BEEN COMPILED OR TESTED.
//
//  RULE FOR THIS BATCH: no threshold is a number I picked. Every one is derived
//  from his own tickets or his own existing settings, and the derivation is
//  written next to it so it can be checked. Everything I could not derive ships
//  DISABLED. Five earlier ideas of mine were withdrawn for failing that test:
//    v18.70 (H) M5 flip confirmation -- WITHDRAWN. He caught it: M5 is a lagging
//       AGGREGATE of M1, so it cannot confirm anything M1 has not already
//       finished. It would have delayed every exit and given back MORE peak.
//    v18.71 (I) trail giveback cap -- WITHDRAWN as redundant. The hard lock
//       (T_HardLockArmPts) fires first and freezes the stop, so the peak trail
//       never got to move it. (I) would have done nothing on his real trades.
//    v18.71 (J) bar-run trim -- WITHDRAWN. It measured the CURRENT BAR. His
//       4500.64 loser came off a 14 pt leg whose entry bar moved 2.4, so it
//       would have missed it, and it trimmed a winner.
// ===========================================================================
input group  "=== v18.73  EXECUTION FIXES ==="
// (K) Cap the ratchet giveback at the noise band instead of letting it scale as
//     a fraction of the peak. Derived from his 20 Aug tape: 47-58% of peak kept
//     on legs of 13-17 pts. Cannot affect small peaks -- see the note at the site.
input bool   T_RatchetCapGiveback = true;
// (L) Give SCALP the sub-band breakeven+spread that only TREND had. Arm level is
//     the existing spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr*ATR. Never closes a trade.
input bool   T_ScalpScratch       = false;  // (AUDIT) OFF. Arms on PEAK, parks the stop at entry+spread -> a leg whose first pullback returns to entry is capped at breakeven. Would have risked his 12pt 17:22 runner. Unmeasured.
// (M) NET corner box adds floating P/L. It was closed-positions-only, so it did
//     not move while a trade ran -- his "net and profit box are slow".
// (v18.98 BG) TRUE = the corner NET box is fed by the running day ledger instead of
// the history rebuild that has been reported frozen five times. See the note at the
// call site in QSP_Recompute().
input bool   T_NetBoxFromLedger  = true;
input bool   T_NetBoxLive         = true;
// (N) M5 is no longer an entry clock (SC_Slow = false). Instead, when M5's
//     Supertrend agrees with M1's, the entry gets this much extra size. His
//     number (+0.01), still clamped to his 0.02/0.05 rule. 0 = off.
input double T_M5ConfluenceLots   = 0.0;   // (AUDIT) OFF. M1 and M5 agree most strongly MID-trend, i.e. furthest from the leg start -- so this adds size at exactly the late entries. Directly fights (T).
// (O) SCALP gets the band-capped peak ratchet it never had. My own §4 defect:
//     (K) patched the ratchet inside the E_TREND block, so it never reached his
//     SCALP fills. Proven by his 21 Aug 16:54:52 fills closing at exactly +4.00.
input bool   T_ScalpRatchet       = true;
// (P) The hard lock's 2pt staircase returns exactly +4.00 for every gain from
//     6.00 to 7.99. Giveback now capped at the noise band, whichever is higher.
input bool   T_HardLockBandCap    = true;
// (Q) SHIPPED DISABLED -- COULD NOT BE DERIVED. Arms the SCALP breakeven on peaks
//     BELOW the noise band. His nine losers on 21 Aug 16:51-16:57 all closed at
//     -3.04 to -3.14 pts, which is SC_SlAtr(0.9)*ATR floored by T_SlFloorPts(2.50)
//     plus spread. Several had gone green first, but by less than the ~2.0pt band,
//     and a stop parked 1pt from price inside a 2pt band is a guaranteed noise
//     stop-out that would also cut every runner at breakeven. Whether that trade
//     is worth making is UNMEASURED -- his tape does not contain the retrace depth
//     of the winners, so I cannot price it. Set to e.g. 1.00 to test.
input double T_ScalpSubBandArmPts = 0.0;
// (R) BASKET GIVEBACK. Protects the STACK's total floating money, which no
//     per-position arm can see. Closes the basket when it gives back more than
//     the noise band priced at the current basket size. 2+ fills only.
input bool   T_BasketGiveback     = false; // (AUDIT) OFF. Tested against his WINNERS: his 17:22 (+GBP18) and 17:38:50 (+GBP19) stacks would have closed on the first GBP4.51 dip. Built from the 21:58 disaster only.
input double T_BasketMinPeakCcy   = 3.00;   // ignore baskets whose peak never cleared this much money
// (S) Arm the SCALP partial at the noise band instead of 1.0R. 1.0R = ~3.08 pts on
//     his tape, which is ABOVE the median measured peak (~2.7). A partial banks
//     with zero giveback, so it is the right tool for the small-peak trades that
//     no trailing stop can ever pay.
input bool   T_PartialAtBand      = false; // (AUDIT) OFF. Banks a third at ~2pts. On his 12pt runner that third makes 2pts instead of 12 -- I sold this as a gain and it is a cost on exactly the trades he cares about.
// (T) LATE-IN-LEG SIZE TRIM. Not a refusal -- every Pine signal is still taken.
//     Thresholds are the measured separation in his own tickets: winners entered
//     at 6% / 24% / 50% of the leg, losers at 81% / 81% / 100%+.
input int    T_LegFracBars        = 20;    // bars the leg is measured over (NOT the current bar - v18.71 J failed that way)
input double T_LegLateFrac        = 0.70;  // past this fraction of the leg, trim
input double T_LegLateTrimFrac    = 0.0;   // (AUDIT) OFF. His best trade (17:22, entry 4590.86) sits at 89% of its 20-bar leg -- because it was breaking OUT of a range, not late in a leg. The measure cannot tell a range ceiling from a breakout. Sample was 3 winners vs 3 losers.
input double T_LegVeryLateFrac    = 0.90;  // past this, floor size — this is where the 9-fill stack landed
// (U) A counter-flip may only close a WINNING scalp if price has crossed the DEMA
//     on THAT TRADE'S OWN clock. Losers still close on a bare flip. This is the
//     rule TREND has had since v18.23; SCALP never got it. NOT the v18.70 (H)
//     mistake -- no higher timeframe is involved, so nothing is delayed.
input bool   T_ScalpFlipNeedDema  = true;
// (V) HIS RULE: a big candle whose wick AGAINST us is bigger than its body means the
//     other side won that candle. Trim size. Not a refusal.
input bool   T_WickSideTrim       = false; // (AUDIT) OFF. His suggestion, never verified against his tape. A breakout candle out of a range often carries a wick, so this can trim the entries that matter most.
// (X) Refuse an ADD that is chasing within one noise band of the recent extreme.
//     First entries are never blocked. Derived from his 18:25:36 stack.
input bool   T_NoChaseAdds        = true;
input int    T_ChaseLookback      = 20;
// (Y) Refuse an ADD when the direction is already underwater. First entries are
//     never blocked. -1 = tolerance is the round-trip spread cost (derived).
input bool   T_NoAddIntoLoss      = true;
input double T_AddIntoLossCcy     = -1.0;
// (Z) Print the panel NET numbers to the Experts log every refresh, so a frozen
//     box can be diagnosed as a DRAWING problem or a COMPUTATION problem.
// ══════ (v19.04 BQ) THIS INSTRUMENT HAS ANSWERED ITS QUESTION — TURN IT OFF ══════
// HIS REPORT: "in expert box it keeps spamming panel closed bla bla".
// That is this line, printed EVERY panel refresh -- InpPanelRefreshS = 4, so about
// 900 lines an hour, all night. It was written to diagnose the frozen NET box and
// it succeeded: the box was the 7-day HistorySelect() rebuild in QSP_Recompute()
// finding nothing, while the ledger and the corner box were correct all along.
// The diagnosis is closed, so the probe is now pure cost, and the cost is real:
//   1. it BURIES the evidence. [LEG GUARD] and [BASKET LOCK] lines are the only
//      proof of what the EA is deciding, and they are one line each against
//      hundreds of these. He cannot see them, and neither can I.
//   2. his machine overheats -- his words, recorded in the v19.02 handover -- and
//      this is a Print plus a ChartRedraw on a 4-second timer, forever.
// TO BE CLEAR: this print does NOT stop the bot trading. The two things are
// unrelated and the not-trading cause is the leg lockout in BP below. Turning this
// off is what makes BP's evidence readable next session.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_LogPanelNet = true brings it straight back if a box freezes again.
input bool   T_LogPanelNet        = false;

// (v18.91 AR) see the note in LiveMarginWatch(). TRUE = only losing positions
// count toward the next adverse band, which is what the code always claimed.
input bool   T_MarginWatchLosersOnly = true;

// ══════════════════ (v18.91 AQ) THE DAY LEDGER ══════════════════
// TWO JOBS, and it is deliberately built to share NOTHING with the panel's
// history scan.
//
// 1. THE NET BOX. He has now reported it dead five times. Every previous fix
//    patched a PRINTER. The numbers those printers read come from
//    QSP_Recompute(), which rebuilds the whole day from a 7-day HistorySelect()
//    on every refresh -- and that function returns silently, drawing nothing
//    new, if HistorySelect() fails. A silent return leaves the last drawn
//    objects on the chart untouched. That is indistinguishable, on screen,
//    from a frozen box. Rather than guess a sixth time, this counts the money
//    as it is realised: every closing deal adds itself once, in
//    OnTradeTransaction, to a running total. No history scan, no day filter,
//    no array cap, nothing to fail silently.
//    If the LEDGER row moves and the NET row does not, the history scan is the
//    fault and we will have proved it in one session instead of arguing about
//    a screenshot.
//
// 2. POINTS NOT CAPTURED, which he asked for by name. For every closed trade:
//       offered = the best this trade was ever worth (peak, already tracked
//                 tick-by-tick by QSP_TrackLive)
//       took    = what it actually closed for
//       missed  = offered - took
//    Summed over the day, plus WHY: the exit reason on the single worst give-
//    back so far. No new measurement is invented -- both quantities already
//    existed in the file and were never subtracted from each other.
//
// Note what this CANNOT see: points the move made AFTER the trade was closed.
// That needs a post-exit tracker which does not exist yet. So "missed" here
// means "handed back before the exit", not "left on the table afterwards".
// OFF-SWITCH: T_DayLedger = false.
input bool   T_DayLedger          = true;

// ══════════ (v18.97 BE) THE BASKET LOCK — HIS RULE, HIS NUMBERS ══════════
// He has now said this five times and he is right, so it is built exactly as he
// described it: "whenever the total profit of a trade hits 10.2 or something lets
// move all sl so the total profit is 10."
//
// WHAT HE MEASURED, in his words: six baskets went above GBP5 inside twenty
// minutes -- GBP30 on the table -- and every one closed red. One went from +GBP5
// to -GBP6. Another reached GBP15 and closed at GBP9 with the stop never moving.
//
// WHY NOTHING IN THE FILE COULD CATCH IT. Every peak, ratchet, giveback and trail
// in 18,000 lines is PER POSITION. Four fills each showing 2.4 pts is four numbers
// too small to trigger anything -- and one basket of GBP7. Nothing anywhere was
// looking at the total. That is the whole gap.
//
// THE RULE. Once total floating profit reaches T_BasketArmCash, a floor is set at
// the peak rounded DOWN to the next T_BasketStepCash. The floor only ever rises.
// If the total falls back to it, EVERY open position is closed at market.
//   peak 5.40 -> floor 5     peak 10.20 -> floor 10     peak 15.30 -> floor 15
// This is a CLOSE, not a stop order, so it cannot be wicked out -- it is evaluated
// on the total, every tick.
//
// THE COST, plainly, because it is real. A GBP1 step on a 0.09-lot basket is about
// 0.15 points. That is far inside the noise band, so a runner will be closed by an
// ordinary wiggle: his +GBP56 buy would likely have been banked in single figures.
// He has chosen this deliberately -- "M1 trends don't last that long, we need to go
// in quick easy hard" -- and on his tape today the chop cost far more than the one
// runner made. If the runners start mattering more than the chop, either raise
// T_BasketStepCash or set T_BasketUseNoiseFloor = true, which widens the step to at
// least one noise band priced at the basket's own size.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_BasketLock = false.
input bool   T_BasketLock         = true;
input double T_BasketArmCash      = 5.0;   // arm once the TOTAL reaches this
input double T_BasketStepCash     = 1.0;   // guarantee in steps of this
input bool   T_BasketUseNoiseFloor = false;

// ══════ (v19.04 BN) THE GIVE-BACK NOW SCALES WITH THE BASKET — HIS TAPE ══════
// The note above said the GBP1 step "will close a runner by an ordinary wiggle"
// and he accepted that trade deliberately. On 24 Aug the tape priced it:
//   22:42:12  a six-fill BUY basket banked around 4649.4-4649.7
//   22:42:12+ price ran on to 4654.82 -- 5.1 further points
//   0.12 lots x GBP0.7345/pt/0.01 = GBP8.81 per point -> about GBP45 not taken
// That single basket is more than five times the whole day's give-back on 24 Aug.
// The chop argument was right when it was made. It is no longer the bigger number.
//
// WHY A FLAT CASH STEP CANNOT WORK. The step is fixed in POUNDS, the basket is not.
//   0.02 lots  GBP1 = 1.36 points   (wider than the noise band -- sane)
//   0.12 lots  GBP1 = 0.11 points   (a tenth of the noise band -- meaningless)
// So the LARGER the basket the TIGHTER the exit in points. Baskets get large in
// exactly one situation: a strong leg firing signal after signal. The mechanism is
// therefore tightest precisely when the move is best. That is backwards.
//
// WHAT REPLACES IT, derived, nothing invented:
//   band  = NoiseFloorPts() = spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr(0.60) x ATR(M1) -- the same
//           helper the margin watch and every ratchet already use. ~1.3-1.9 pts.
//   bandGBP = that band priced at THIS basket's own lots, via LossPerLot().
//   give-back = T_BasketGiveBackBands (1.0) x bandGBP. The file's own physics rule
//           is "a trail can never lock closer than one noise band". One band is the
//           floor that rule allows, so one band is what is used. Not 0.5, not 2.
//   arm     = T_BasketArmBands (2.0) x bandGBP, and never less than T_BasketArmCash.
//           Two bands is arithmetic, not taste: to guarantee one band of profit
//           after giving one band back, the peak must first reach two.
// Worked at 0.12 lots with a 1.5 pt band: bandGBP = GBP13.2, so it arms at GBP26.4
// and guarantees GBP13.2. Ten such baskets is GBP132 -- his GBP100 minimum, from
// the ledger, without a single new entry.
// Small baskets are barely touched: at 0.02 lots bandGBP = GBP2.20, arm GBP5 (the
// cash floor still binds), so chop behaves almost exactly as it does now.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_BasketGiveBackBands = 0 restores the v19.03 GBP1 ladder exactly.
input double T_BasketGiveBackBands = 1.0;  // 0 = off, use the flat T_BasketStepCash ladder
input double T_BasketArmBands      = 2.0;  // peak must reach this many bands before arming
input bool   T_BasketReopensLeg    = true; // (v19.04 BO) a banked basket releases the leg guard

// ══════ (v18.92 AT) THE WICK-PROOF STOP, ON THE ENGINE THAT ACTUALLY RUNS ══════
// EVIDENCE, 24 Aug 10:53-11:10, twelve consecutive closes:
//   -3.13 -3.59 -3.20 -3.23 -3.12 -3.06 -3.01 -2.98 -3.10 -3.49 -3.23 -3.09 pts
// Twelve trades, twelve full stops, not one discretionary exit. That is not a
// tuning problem, it is a race that the broker wins every single time:
//   - the entry stop is placed just beyond the Supertrend line
//   - the Supertrend FLIPS on a BAR CLOSE
//   - the broker stop TRIGGERS ON ANY TICK
// So any tick that pokes through the line takes the money before FLIP-CLOSE,
// GIVEBACK, SMALL-MOVE or anything else on this list can even be evaluated.
// Every exit in ManagePosition is downstream of a stop that fires first.
// v18.90 contained a fix for this (AP) written into Engine_Scalp(), which
// returns immediately because InpUseScalp is false. It has never executed.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES: pushes the broker stop ONE NOISE BAND beyond the signal line
// (spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr x ATR -- the same band every other lock in this
// build uses, not a new number), and shrinks the lot by exactly the same ratio
// so the POUNDS AT RISK ARE UNCHANGED.
//   0.02 lots x 3.08 pts  =  0.01 lots x 6.16 pts
// The trade now dies when the SIGNAL says it is wrong, not when a wick says so.
//
// HONEST COST, stated plainly: half the size means half of every winner too.
// The 24 Aug +8.12 pt sell would have paid half. This is only worth it if the
// stop is genuinely being hit by noise rather than by being wrong -- which is
// what the twelve stop-outs above suggest and what the ledger will confirm.
//
// FLOOR WARNING: the shrink cannot go below the broker minimum of 0.01. If the
// base size is already 0.01 there is nothing to shrink, so the wider stop would
// raise real risk instead of holding it flat. In that case this stands down and
// says so in the log. Run it with a 0.02 base (T_LotTiersByEquity = false).
// OFF-SWITCH: T_WickProofTrend = false.
// ══════ (v19.01 BH) THE BIG-VOLUME CANDLE — HIS 17:08 EXAMPLE ══════
// One M1 bar at 17:08 ran from 4663.6 to 4671.1. Seven and a half points in sixty
// seconds. The Supertrend flipped on that bar's CLOSE, so the entry price was the
// TOP of it: three buys at 4670.80, all stopped at 4667.80 for -GBP11.
// THE ARITHMETIC THAT SETTLES IT: the stop is about 3.0 points. The trigger bar was
// 7.5. A trade born at the close of a bar wider than its own stop is already
// stretched further from the signal line than the stop can tolerate -- it must
// retrace less than half of ONE candle to die. That is not an opinion, it is the
// geometry, and it uses no number that is not already in the file.
// HIS RULE IS KEPT: the signal is still TAKEN. Only the SIZE drops to the tier
// floor, which is the lever he has always said to use.
// His winners at 17:15, 17:16 and 17:18 were all on ordinary bars and are untouched.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_SpikeBarSizeCut = false.
// ══════ (v19.03 BM) THE OVERNIGHT SPREAD PROBLEM — HE IS RUNNING ASIA TONIGHT ══════
// There IS a spread gate at line ~12139. It is dead: `if(T_SpreadMoveGate &&
// !isReclaim && !T_TakeEveryPine)` and T_TakeEveryPine is TRUE, which is his rule.
// So right now spread is not consulted on any entry at all.
// WHY THIS MATTERS TONIGHT AND NOT IN LONDON: gold's spread at the 22:00 GMT
// rollover and through Sydney routinely goes from ~0.25 pts to 2-5 pts. The birth
// stop is ~3.0. A trade opened on a 5-point spread is born past its own stop --
// it cannot win, whatever price does.
// HIS RULE IS KEPT: the signal is still TAKEN. Only the SIZE drops to the tier
// floor, which is the lever he has always specified.
// THE NUMBER IS NOT INVENTED: T_MaxSpreadPctMove (0.35) already exists in this
// file as the line at which the dead gate judged a signal "negative expectancy,
// not worth taking". The same threshold now cuts size instead of refusing.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_WideSpreadSizeCut = false.
input bool   T_WideSpreadSizeCut = true;
input bool   T_SpikeBarSizeCut   = true;
input bool   T_WickProofTrend    = true;
input double T_WickProofBands    = 1.0;   // how many noise bands beyond the line
input double T_WickTrimFrac       = 0.50;   // 0 = off
input bool   T_WickProofRespectsFloor = true;  // (v19.04 BS) his 0.02 floor outranks the wick-proof shrink
input bool   T_HardFloorAfterMults   = true;  // (v19.04 BT) re-apply his 0.02/0.05 AFTER every multiplier

input group  "=== v18.72  AUDIT FIXES ==="
// (A) THE DOUBLE/QUAD FILL. His 20 Aug tickets: 17:37:44 filled 4500.64 THREE
//     times plus 4500.92; 17:40:21 filled 4498.30 three times; 18:01:31 shows
//     4527.66 x2, 4527.79 x2, 4528.22 x2. Sizes differ inside a cluster (0.01
//     AND 0.02 at the same second, same price), which proves it is TREND and
//     SCALP both taking ONE Supertrend flip, not a clock problem -- turning M3
//     off cannot fix it. Root cause, SCALP overlap guard:
//         if(SC_NoOverlapTrend && TrendHoldsTf(k)) continue;
//     That asks whether TREND ALREADY HOLDS. Both engines evaluate in the SAME
//     OnTick, before any position exists, so it is false and both fire.
//     THE FIX USES HIS OWN SETTING, NOT A NEW NUMBER. T_DupClusterMax = 2 is
//     already in this file and already says "at most 2 same-direction fills in
//     the dup window". It was never enforced, because DuplicateFill() computes
//     the answer and then returns without acting when T_DupGuardRefuse = false
//     -- it does neither of its two jobs. Now it honours the limit he set.
//     WHY IT IS THE BIGGEST LINE ITEM: every fill pays the spread, and on a
//     negative distribution the duplication amplifies the loss. Collapsing his
//     17:20-17:45 clusters to one fill each turns -33.50 GBP into about -9.
//     OFF-SWITCH: T_DupClusterMax = 0 (unlimited, pre-v18.72 behaviour).
input bool   T_EnforceDupCluster = true;
// (B) THE PEAK TRACKER WAS WIRED TO THE DISPLAY. QSP_TrackLive() is the only
//     writer of g_qspPeak and it sat inside PanelTick() behind
//     `if(!InpStatsPanel) return;`, which is the LAST call in OnTick(). So with
//     the panel off every peak-relative gate read ZERO, and even with it on they
//     read the PREVIOUS tick's peak -- hardest exactly when a trade is youngest.
//     Now sampled at the TOP of OnTick(), unconditionally.
//     CONFIRMED WORKING: his 20 Aug panel moved kept% from -43% to -14%.
//     OFF-SWITCH: T_TrackPeaksAlways = false.
input bool   T_TrackPeaksAlways = true;
// (C) 77% OF EXITS WERE INVISIBLE. Broker-side stop closes execute server-side,
//     never touch CloseTagged(), and were recorded nowhere. On 20 Aug that was
//     ~149 of 194 exits. Every session before this was diagnosed from under a
//     quarter of the evidence, which is why fix after fix landed on the minority
//     path and changed nothing measurable.
//     CONFIRMED WORKING: "SL-HIT 12 +17.2 pts lost -30.6" now prints. That row
//     had never existed, and it says the stops are banking winners BADLY --
//     net positive, but leaving 30.6 pts behind. That is the capture problem,
//     measured for the first time instead of inferred.
//     OFF-SWITCH: T_AttributeSlExits = false.
input bool   T_AttributeSlExits = true;
// (D) MARGIN, NOT THE TRAIL, DECIDED HIS EXITS. THIS IS THE BIG ONE.
//     His 20 Aug 17:31-18:03 leg ran 4488 -> 4535, about 47 points, and nearly
//     every entry went green. Then look at the exit timestamps: ~25 WINNERS all
//     closed 18:01:27-18:01:30 and 11 LOSERS closed 18:01:31. Winners and losers
//     together inside four seconds. NO EXIT RULE DOES THAT.
//     His terminal at the time: Margin 132.43, Free Margin -7.79, MARGIN LEVEL
//     94.92% -- under 100%. At ~25 positions x 0.02 = 0.50 LOTS on 124 GBP of
//     equity, the stack was margin-bound and the broker chose the exit, not the
//     EA. Peak 4535, forced exit ~4527: eight points x 0.50 lots, about 58 GBP
//     handed back. THAT is "it went up 200 and closed at 80".
//     It is also the honest answer to his own question, "what if the trend does
//     not continue". At 0.50 lots on a 100 GBP account a reversal does not cost
//     profit, it ends the account.
//     THE CEILING IS DERIVED FROM HIS MARGIN NUMBERS, NOT PICKED. 132.43 GBP of
//     margin for ~0.50 lots = 2.65 GBP per 0.01 lot. Holding margin under 30% of
//     a 100 GBP floor account: 30 / 2.65 = 0.11 lots. Rounded DOWN to 0.10.
//     This is now ON, and it replaces the old %-of-equity cap, which silently
//     changed meaning every time he topped the test account up (at 610 equity it
//     permitted ~4 doubled signals, at 100 about 1 -- no two sessions were ever
//     comparable). A lot ceiling means the same thing at every balance.
//     OFF-SWITCH: T_MaxDirLots = 0 restores the %-of-equity ceiling.
// (v19.18 CH) 0.10 -> 0.30. PROVE-PRESS targets 0.05 per pressed trade and the
// arithmetic that reaches GBP500/week needs about 12 of them a day: the proven
// cohort in his 26 Aug book won 82.6% and averaged +2.17 at 0.014 lots, which is
// +7.75 at 0.05, so 12 x 7.75 = GBP93/day = GBP465/week. That needs up to 0.60
// lots of one-directional capacity. At 0.10 the third press of the day is refused
// and the plan silently caps itself at two positions. 0.30 allows six concurrent.
// This raises the ceiling; T_MaxDirRiskPct (25% of equity) is still the real
// governor and is unchanged.
input double T_MaxDirLots       = 0.30;
// (E) NOTHING WAS EVER PROTECTED, BECAUSE THE LOCK ARMS OUT OF REACH.
//     T_HardLockArmPts = 6.0 arms the lock at +6.0 points and sets the stop
//     T_HardLockGivePts = 2.0 behind it. VERIFIED TO THE PENNY on his live
//     trade: entry 4499.65, peak 4506.06 = +6.41, lock armed at +6.0, stop
//     placed at entry + 4.00 = 4503.65 -- exactly the SL on his chart. And his
//     three winners that session closed at EXACTLY +4.00, +4.00, +4.00.
//     THE PROBLEM: his average trade offers 2.72 pts (panel: +117.1 offered over
//     43 trades). The lock arms at 6.0. It is set ABOVE THE REACH OF MOST
//     TRADES, so on most trades nothing is ever protected -- his open book
//     showed four trades sitting +4.11 with stops still 3.02 BELOW entry.
//     HIS OWN OBJECTION, HONOURED: a breakeven stop is a trap if it arms early,
//     because trades that linger red then shoot up get killed at flat. So this
//     arms on PROOF, not on time. T_BeArmPts = 3.0 is above his 2.72 average
//     offered -- the trade has already beaten its own average before anything
//     moves -- and the stop goes to entry + T_BeLockPts (0.50), which covers the
//     round-trip spread rather than sitting exactly at flat. A trade that is
//     lingering red has never been +3.0, so it is never touched by this.
//     IT ONLY EVER RATCHETS, and it never moves a stop backwards or tighter than
//     an existing lock, so it cannot pull a stop in on a runner.
//     OFF-SWITCH: T_BeArmPts = 0.
input double T_BeArmPts         = 3.0;
input double T_BeLockPts        = 0.50;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PRESENT BUT DISABLED -- I could not derive these from his data, so they do
// not ship on. Each has its arithmetic above or in the handover. Turn ONE on
// per session and read the panel.
input int    T_MinFillGapSecs   = 0;      // fill-gap lockout in seconds. (A) supersedes it.
input bool   T_ContinuousSize   = false;  // continuous risk-anchored lot sizing
input double T_RiskPctPerTrade  = 2.0;
input double T_LotFloorHard     = 0.02;   // his standing minimum
input double T_LotCeilHard      = 0.05;   // his standing maximum
// (v19.05 BU) MULTI-SYMBOL SIZING. His ask: "fix this ea to auto adjust to every
// pair I put it on in terms of lot size". The existing SymbolLotScale() holds
// "money per 1.00 price unit" constant, which is the wrong invariant: 1.00 of
// price is one dollar of gold but ten thousand pips of EURUSD, so it clamps to
// its 0.1x floor and sizes the position down to the broker minimum. And his
// standing 0.02/0.05 rule is itself a GOLD rule -- 0.02 lots of EURUSD carries a
// completely different risk. So on a non-gold symbol this switch hands sizing to
// the risk-anchored path, which reads the symbol's OWN tick value and the trade's
// OWN stop distance and is therefore correct on any instrument by construction.
// GOLD IS UNTOUCHED by this switch, always, whatever it is set to.
// DEFAULT OFF: he must turn it on when he puts the EA on a second pair, so a
// gold-only account can never have its sizing changed by an edit it did not ask
// for. Requires T_RiskPctPerTrade > 0.
input bool   T_SymAgnosticSize   = false;
input double T_TrailGivebackFrac = 0.0;   // withdrawn as redundant, see note above
input double T_SlMin       = 1.10;  // (v18.05) 0.70->1.10. Birth stop too close: 11 Aug losers ran
                                    // -3.2/-4.9/-6.7 pts. Revert to 0.70 to undo.
input double T_SlMax       = 2.00;  // (v18.05) 1.40->2.00 so the wider floor cannot trip the SKIP branch.
input double T_Tp1R        = 1.5;
input bool   T_GivebackFloorOn   = true;  // (v18.42) OFF-SWITCH: false = v18.42 exactly
input double T_GivebackPeakCap    = 0.50;  // (v18.59) no floor may demand a retrace bigger than this fraction of the peak. 0 = off
input double T_GivebackAtrFloor  = 1.00;  // (v18.42) giveback allowance may never be tighter than this many ATR
// (v18.52) Point-keyed giveback tiers — his manual staged plan, in points, because
// the R-keyed tiers above were unreachable. See the note in the giveback block.
// (v18.56) Once a trade has a real peak it is no longer "fresh", so the hold votes
// stop vetoing the giveback bank. See the note at the giveback close.
input bool   T_GivebackIgnoreHold = true;
input bool   T_GivebackTierPts = true;
input double T_GivebackMidPts  = 3.0;   // peak >= this many points ...
input double T_GivebackMidPct  = 30.0;  // ... allow this % of peak (his stage 2)
input double T_GivebackBigPts  = 8.0;   // peak >= this many points ...
input double T_GivebackBigPct  = 40.0;  // ... allow this % (his stage 3: proven moves get MORE room)
input double T_GivebackPct = 22.0;   // (v12b) Tightened from 35 — was letting over a third of peak
                                      // profit evaporate before closing. Direct fix for "price ran well
                                      // into profit and the EA never locked it in before rolling over."
                                      // independent of the Supertrend line, which is too slow to catch
                                      // a fast peak-then-reversal on its own. Only engages once
                                      // T_GivebackMinR is on the table, so it never affects a small trade.
input double T_GivebackMinR = 0.3;   // (v16.35) 0.4 -> 0.3 to CLOSE A PROTECTION GAP: the micro-lock arms at
                                      // 0.30R (breakeven only) but the giveback guard needed a 0.40R peak, so
                                      // between those two a trade was protected against a LOSS but not against
                                      // handing back everything it had made. Now the two meet with no gap.
                                      // (v12b) Lowered from 0.8 — was only protecting large moves. Now
                                      // arms on smaller confirmed profit too, so a modest winner that
                                      // starts rolling over gets banked instead of round-tripping.
// (v16.14) CHOP-STALL SMALL BANK — extract the small choppy profit that
// fades with NO reversal candle. Only inside a recognised chop batch
// (so clean runners are exempt) with small profit stalled for N bars.
// (v16.70) HIS SPEC, STATED PLAINLY: hold until the market FLIPS. No set goals,
// because the market cannot be predicted. The stop is a SAFETY NET for a random
// volatility spike — it is not the exit. The EA's exits should be its own
// decision, and the profit floor should follow price up without sitting so close
// it gets knocked out by noise. Every discretionary profit-taker below therefore
// goes OFF: each one is a FIXED GOAL by another name, and each was closing trades
// before the flip that justified them was gone.
// WHAT STILL PROTECTS A TRADE with all five off:
//   * peak ratchet — floor follows profit up, 35% of peak (v16.66)
//   * chandelier trail from 1.5R (pre-existing)
//   * giveback guard — a real reversal, 22-37% off peak
//   * structure-break early cut — the entry swing is gone (v16.66)
//   * gap-against close and the hard SL — the spike safety he described
//   * opposite flip — THE primary exit, as originally designed
// This removes machinery rather than adding it, and returns the EA to its own
// founding rule: no fixed TP, ride to the opposite signal with intelligent
// early-close. Set any one back to true to re-enable it individually.
input bool   T_ChopStallBank = false;  // (v16.70) OFF — a fixed goal at 0.55R
input int    T_ChopStallBars = 4;    // (v16.21) cooled 3->4 — one bar more patience before banking a stalled
                                     // choppy leg, so closing isn't quite so eager; still fires on a real stall
// (v17.57) 0.5 -> 0.8 for BOTH price guards. His log shows them firing on nearly
// every signal ("6.55 x ATR past the line", "0.93x ATR past the line" — both
// rejected at a 0.5 cap). A guard that blocks the majority of entries is worse
// than the wick fills it prevents: no trade at all captures 0 points. 0.8 x ATR
// on M1 gold is ~1.5pts of slack, which still refuses the 3pt wick tips he
// circled. Set either to 0 to switch that guard off entirely.
// (v17.80, his instruction: "I only want REALLY BAD trades not being taken,
// everything else taken") All four entry-price gates loosened to roughly double.
// The 17.7x numbers were tuned against a ~1.0 ATR session and this week gold has
// been running 2-6 ATR, so they were rejecting ordinary fills (his own log shows
// refusals at 0.82-0.90x against a 0.80 cap - near-misses, not bad entries).
// At these numbers a gate fires only when the fill is genuinely poor: 1.5x ATR
// past the level, or 2x ATR stretched from the mean, or into a 2x ATR opposing
// candle. Everything short of that trades. The deferred-chase still improves the
// price where it can, so loosening the gate costs fill quality only in the cases
// where waiting would not have helped anyway.
// (v18.15 OVERLAP AUDIT) THREE MECHANISMS GUARD THE SAME QUANTITY.
// Distance-from-signal-line at entry is now checked by three separate gates:
//     T_MaxWickAtr       1.50   early-flip path only
//     T_MaxExtensionAtr  1.50   two call sites (3115, 8476)
//     T_AnchorMaxAtr     1.20   ALL paths, at the single entry funnel (v18.06)
// Only the TIGHTEST can ever bind, so at 1.20 the anchor guard makes both 1.50
// gates unreachable -- they burn CPU and log lines and can never refuse anything
// the anchor guard has not already refused. That is the overlap class: three
// numbers for one decision, two of them dead, and a future edit to either 1.50
// would look like it changed behaviour while changing nothing.
// The anchor guard is kept as the single authority because it is the only one
// that covers every entry path -- the reason the peak entries got through.
// These two are left at 1.50 and documented as SUBORDINATE rather than deleted:
// removing call sites is a behaviour risk on an untested build, and if
// T_AnchorMaxAtr is ever raised above 1.50 these become live again as a backstop.
// If you set T_AnchorGuardAll = false, THESE become the active limit again.
input double T_MaxExtensionAtr = 1.5;  // (v17.54) 1.6 -> 0.5, aligned with T_MaxWickAtr so the two entry-price
                                      // guards agree instead of one allowing 3x what the other blocks. At an
                                      // M1 ATR of ~2pts this caps the fill at ~1pt past the line instead of ~3.2. // (v16.07) Set to 1.6 — the middle ground the trader asked for: looser than the original 1.2 (takes the mildly-extended M3 flips that were being over-skipped = more frequency) but tighter than 2.0 so it does NOT add the extra risk of near-chasing entries. Still firmly refuses genuine 3+ ATR chases. Skip a flip entry if price is already this many ATR past
                                      // the signal line — that's chasing an extended move, the actual
                                      // "bad entry" pattern that goes straight into drawdown. Does not
                                      // apply to a continuation reclaim (that price is deliberately re-tested).
input bool   T_EarlyBE       = true;  // (v12d) lock stop to entry+spread once T_EarlyBE_R is showing —
                                      // from that moment the trade can only finish at zero-or-better
input double T_EarlyBE_R     = 1.0;  // (v17.84) 0.6->1.0: his ask, BE too aggressive, needs meaningful move first   // (v12d) profit (in R) that arms the early breakeven lock
// ════ (v18.50) true -> false. THIS IS WHERE THE 0.02 TICKETS COME FROM. ════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE, and it answers his question directly.
// He asked why some trades are 0.02 when he set 0.03-0.05. This is why: with
// T_FixedLots = 0.03, this banks a third (0.01) at 1R and the remaining 0.02 rides.
// The 0.02 in his History is not an entry size at all -- it is the REMAINDER after
// a partial. That also explains the 0.04-with-0.02 pattern on 17 Aug.
// THE STRUCTURAL REASON TO TURN IT OFF, which is worse than the confusion:
// the partial only ever fires in PROFIT. So full size is exposed to every loss and
// two-thirds size to every win. That makes the average trade ARITHMETICALLY worse
// than the average price move, no matter how good the exits get. It is the one leak
// in this file that cannot be tuned away, because it is the shape of the mechanism
// rather than a number inside it. Winners and losers must get opposite treatment,
// and this gives losers the better treatment.
// HONEST COST: a trade that peaks near 1R and then fails now gives back the whole
// position instead of two-thirds of it. On a 2.8 pt R that is worth ~0.9 pts on
// those trades. Against that, every runner now runs at full size.
// REVERT: T_BankPartial = true.
input bool   T_BankPartial   = false; // (v18.50) off — see above   // (v12d) bank a third of the position at 1R when size allows
                                      // (>= 2x min lot) — cash constantly flows home, the rest rides
// (v16.03) T_StagnantExit / T_StagnantBars removed — near-dead code, see
// the removal note in the TREND management block. Two going-nowhere timers
// remain (stall at 12 bars, chop-cluster at 9) and both earn their keep.
input double T_FlipBodyAtr   = 0.0;   // (v16.70) was 0.12. He asked explicitly for even the horrible
                                      // signals to be taken. 0 = no body requirement, so a doji flip is
                                      // taken like any other. This is the last SIGNAL filter; only the
                                      // spread gate remains, and that is execution cost, not signal
                                      // quality. Restore 0.12 if the journal shows doji flips lose.  // (v12d) the flip bar itself must have a real directional body
                                      // (close-open in the flip's direction, >= this x ATR). A flip
                                      // printed by a doji in chop is the classic false start — this is
                                      // the cheapest "more accurate signals" filter there is.
input bool   T_SessionAdx    = false; // (v16.70) OFF. This required a HIGHER ADX in poor sessions, which
                                      // re-introduced the ADX gate through the back door after T_MinAdx was
                                      // set to 0 in v16.67. Leaving it on would have kept refusing the
                                      // Asian-session signals he wants taken.  // (v12d) in poor-quality sessions (dead Asian chop) require a
                                      // stronger ADX before entering — chop hours are where the losses
                                      // that aren't trend-start scratches actually come from
// (v17.56, his live ask: "entries should always be 0.03") FIXED ENTRY SIZE.
// Why he kept seeing 0.01: the risk model sizes lots = (equity x risk%) / loss-
// per-lot. At ~GBP148 equity with a 5.7pt stop, 0.01 lots already risks GBP4.26,
// so any sane risk% budget buys less than one minimum lot and the code falls
// through to g_lotMin. Nothing was broken — the account is simply small relative
// to a gold stop. Set to 0 to go back to risk-model sizing.
// SAY IT PLAINLY: 0.03 with a 5.7pt stop = GBP12.79 at risk, ~8.6% of a GBP148
// balance per trade. That is 3x what the risk model would choose. It is his
// call and it is the only way to hit his per-trade pound targets on this
// balance, but it is real risk, not a free upgrade.
// Pyramid top-ups and the exact-size requests (reversal 0.01 / re-entry 0.02)
// are deliberately NOT overridden — those sizes are the point of those paths.
// (v17.66, his spec) AUTO LOT LADDER — the account sizes itself, and the risk
// PERCENTAGE stays roughly flat as it grows instead of shrinking. His ladder:
//   under 100 -> 0.02      100-149 -> 0.03      150-199 -> 0.04
//   then +0.01 for every further 50 of equity.
// Floor is 50: below that one minimum gold lot with a 4-dollar stop is over 8%
// of the account and there is no size that makes the maths work, so it trades
// 0.01 and says so rather than sizing into an account that cannot carry it.
// T_FixedLots is now the OVERRIDE: leave it at 0 to use the ladder, or set a
// number to pin every entry to that size regardless of balance.
input double T_BigLotFloor      = 0.10;  // (v17.87) minimum lot once equity clears the line below
input double T_BigLotFloorMinEq = 400.0; // equity required before the 0.10 floor applies
input bool   T_AutoLotLadder  = true;
// (v18.05 COMPILE FIX) DUPLICATE REMOVED -- this input was re-declared in the
// v18.04 block below, which is MetaEditor's "variable already defined" at 1834.
// The v18.04 declaration (= 0.04) is the live one and is now the only one.
// input double T_FixedLots     = 0.0;   // (v17.62) his call. 0.04 in, T_BankPartial takes 0.01 (a third, floored) at 1R, 0.03 rides.
input bool   T_DemaSlopeHold  = true; // (v17.59) let a still-rising/falling DEMA stand the discretionary exits down
input double T_TrailMatureR    = 2.50;  // (v17.91) below this peak-R the ST line does not trail; the wider trend stop rules
// (v18.23) see the note at the flip-close vote tally.
input bool   T_FlipProfitIsVote = false;  // false = being in profit is NOT a reason to close on a counter-flip
input bool   T_FlipCloseNeedDema = true;  // a profitable trade only closes on a counter-flip if price crossed the DEMA
input int    T_FlipCloseVotesLosing = 1;  // (v18.30) votes needed to close a LOSING trade on an opposite flip
input int    T_FlipCloseVotes  = 2;     // (v18.06) agreeing votes needed to act on a bare Supertrend flip. 1 = old behaviour
input double T_FlipCloseMinR   = 0.80;  // (v17.90) a bare Supertrend flip needs this much profit, or a 2nd confirmation, to close
input bool   T_HoldNeedStrong  = true; // (v17.92) the quorum must include cross-clock agreement or an unfilled FVG
// (v18.15 CORRELATION AUDIT) THE VOTES ARE NOT INDEPENDENT.
// The hold quorum counts five votes: otherClockAgrees, demaHold, fvgHold,
// roomHold, rsiHold. But otherClockAgrees is another Supertrend's direction and
// demaHold is price-vs-DEMA -- in a trend those two agree almost by
// construction, because the signal that opened the trade already required
// Supertrend and DEMA to point the same way. So a "2 of 5" quorum is routinely
// satisfied by two readings of the SAME fact, which is false confidence, not
// confirmation. fvgHold, roomHold and rsiHold carry genuinely different
// information (imbalance, distance to structure, momentum).
// NOT changed tonight: raising the requirement would make trades exit EARLIER,
// which is the opposite of what this session is testing. The honest fix is to
// count the correlated pair as ONE vote, and that needs the arbiter's log first
// to show whether the quorum is even the binding constraint.
input int    T_HoldVotesNeeded = 2;    // (v17.89) votes required to defer exits AFTER the fresh-trade grace
input bool   T_RsiHold        = true;   // (v17.86) 8th HOLD-ONLY vote, never gates entries
input double T_RsiHoldLevel   = 55.0;    // RSI must still be at/beyond this (or 100-this for sells) to hold
input bool   T_RoomToRun      = true;  // (v17.64) hold while there is still space to the next opposing swing level
input int    T_RoomLookback    = 30;    // bars scanned for that prior swing high / low
input double T_RoomMinAtr      = 0.80;  // "space" = at least this x ATR of clear air to the level
input bool   T_FvgHold        = true;  // (v17.62) an unfilled fair-value gap behind the trade = displacement, hold
input int    T_FvgLookback    = 6;     // scan this many bars back for the imbalance
input double T_FvgMinAtr      = 0.60;  // the gap must be this x ATR to count as displacement, not a tick of noise
// (v17.77) DEFAULTED BACK TO OFF on his instruction, and his reasoning is sound:
// reversals are common and a system that only trades the day direction cannot
// catch them. The mechanism stays in the file because his 5 Aug charts really do
// show every counter-direction sell losing on a +163 day -- if a future session
// shows the same, mode 1 is one input away. It is his decision, not mine.
input int    T_DayBiasMode     = 0;     // 0 = off, 1 = halve counter-day-direction trades, 2 = skip them
input double T_DayBiasMinPts   = 15.0;  // the daily candle must have travelled this far from its open to count as committed
input double T_DayBiasCounterMult = 0.50; // size multiplier applied to a counter-direction trade in mode 1
input bool   T_StrongBirth     = true;  // (v17.75) size up on a strong signal birth (3 of 4 tests)
input double T_StrongBirthLots = 0.05;  // exact lot used on a STRONG birth (his number)
input int    T_StrongMinScore  = 2;     // (v17.78) how many of the 3 birth tests must pass, after both vetoes
input double T_StrongAtrExpand = 1.15;  // ATR now must be this x ATR of 20 bars ago
input double T_StrongRangeAtr  = 4.00;  // (v17.77) last 20 bars must span this x ATR — kills sideways stars
input double T_StrongBodyAtr   = 1.00;  // birth bar range must be this x ATR, closing our way
input bool   T_PremiumSetup    = true;  // (v17.71) size up when entering from the day extreme after a big run
input double T_PremiumRunPts   = 20.0;  // the day must have travelled at least this many points to qualify
input double T_PremiumEdgeFrac = 0.30;  // and the entry must sit within this fraction of the day range from the extreme
input double T_PremiumLotMult  = 2.00;  // lot multiplier on a premium setup (2.00 = double the ladder size)
input bool   T_EntryMeanGate   = true;  // (v17.66) refuse entries taken far from the DEMA
input double T_MaxDemaDistAtr  = 2.00;  // "far" = this x ATR of stretch from the DEMA
input bool   T_EntryCandleConfirm = true; // (v17.61) refuse an entry into a big candle running the other way
input double T_EntryOpposeAtr     = 2.00; // "big" = the opposing closed bar's range is this x ATR or more
input double T_DemaSlopeMinAtr = 0.10;  // (v17.84) lower so a genuine reversal is recognised sooner, not held past it // slope must be at least this x ATR over 3 bars to count as real
input bool   T_ConfSizing    = false; // (v17.45 — his log, his exact ask: "let 1,3,5 trade freely.")
                                       // The log showed SEVEN independent multipliers all shrinking the
                                       // same number in a chain: COST-TRIM, M15-counter-lean, efficiency
                                       // ratio, against-DEMA risk, trend-maturity, and the conviction
                                       // pool — one signal logged conviction 0.788 collapsing to a
                                       // size mult of 0.063 after they all compounded. The confluence
                                       // TIER then overrides back to 0.03 lots regardless, so the FINAL
                                       // size was fine — but every discretionary judgement upstream of
                                       // that override was computing against near-zero conviction, which
                                       // is not "trading freely", it is seven opinions fighting each
                                       // other before one flat rule ignores all of them. OFF now: none
                                       // of the seven multipliers apply, size comes straight from the
                                       // ladder/tier, exactly matching his instruction. (v12e) FREQUENCY WITHOUT THE RISK: instead of blocking a
                                      // borderline signal (which would cost a trend), take it at
                                      // REDUCED size. A conf-60 signal trades at T_ConfMinMult x risk,
                                      // scaling linearly up to full size at conf-100. Weak signals stay
                                      // caught — they just cost less when they're wrong. Strong signals
                                      // pay full. This is the "frequent but super secure" mechanism.
input double T_ConfMinMult   = 0.60;  // (v12e) risk multiplier at the minimum confidence floor
input bool   T_Pyramid       = true;  // (v12e) SUPER-CONFIDENT TOP-UP: once a TREND trade is ≥
                                      // T_PyramidMinR in profit (breakeven lock already on — the
                                      // original trade is now RISK-FREE) and the confluence stack still
                                      // agrees, add a small extra position. The add is funded entirely
                                      // by the open profit cushion — low risk, extra reward on the
                                      // exact trades that are already proving themselves.
// ═══ (v18.55) 3 -> 1. HIS EXPLICIT INSTRUCTION, AND HIS OWN EVIDENCE. ═══
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE, on request: "for the stacking we need a limit as
// we were up 5 pound and added another 0.02 and that closed in -4 pound and the
// other two didn't make much just pennies and it kept stacking after like 1-2 more."
// One add per position. The stack still exists -- a leg can still be doubled -- but
// it cannot become a ladder, and the third and fourth adds that arrive latest in a
// leg (and therefore closest to its end) are gone.
// ANSWERING HIS WORRY DIRECTLY -- "it's not certain market will move in direction,
// I'm not sure what this is based on": correct, it is not certain, and nothing can
// make it certain. What an add is based on is three measured conditions, not a
// forecast: the position is already in profit (T_PyramidMinR), the leg is still
// printing extremes (LegStillExtending), and the trade's own clock still agrees
// (ScalpST). v18.53 added two more: not on an oversized bar, and price still on the
// trade's side of the DEMA. So it is evidence that the leg is alive, never a
// prediction that it continues. The protection against being wrong is SIZE -- adds
// are forced to 0.01 (0.02 on full agreement) against a 0.03 base -- plus this cap.
// REVERT: T_PyramidMaxPerPos = 3.
input int    T_PyramidMaxPerPos = 1;   // (v18.55) was 3   // (v18.22) adds allowed per position — was hard-coded to 1
// (v18.33) 0.6 -> 0.30. His model: build the position early in the leg so the
// 3-5 point target is reached on a LARGER position, then shed 0.01 at a time.
// Adding at 0.6R on a 2.5 pt stop meant waiting 1.5 points before the first add,
// by which point most M1 legs are half over.
input double T_PyramidMinR   = 0.30;    // (v18.22) 1.2 -> 0.6: add while the leg runs, not after it   // (v12e) open profit (R) before a top-up is even considered
input double T_PyramidLots   = 0.01;  // (v12e) normal top-up size
input double T_PyramidT3Lots = 0.02;  // (v12e) top-up when the FULL stack (tier 3: +H1+H4) agrees
input int    T_PyramidMaxDay = 4;   // (v18.55) 6 -> 4, same reasoning as the per-position cap     // (v12e) daily cap on top-ups — rare by design

input group "═══ v13 — REGIME AWARENESS (research-backed) ═══"
// The most-validated improvement for a Supertrend flip system: don't add more
// signals, add awareness of WHEN the flip is trustworthy. Efficiency Ratio
// measures whether price is actually going somewhere or just thrashing — the
// exact difference between a real trend and a whipsaw. All of this SCALES SIZE,
// never blocks: frequency (your core goal) is fully preserved.
input bool   T_UseEfficiency   = true;  // (v13) chop-aware size scaling via Kaufman Efficiency Ratio
input int    T_EfficiencyLen   = 10;    // (v13) lookback bars for ER (Kaufman's classic ~10)
input double T_EfficiencyHi    = 0.45;  // (v13) ER at/above this = clean trend, full size (gold ~0.45 per research)
input double T_EfficiencyLo    = 0.20;  // (v13) ER at/below this = chop, minimum size
input double T_EfficiencyMinMult = 0.45;// (v13) size multiplier in full chop — still trades, just small
input bool   T_UseDayContext   = true;  // (v13) tilt size with how the day is going (H1 progress + range position)
input double T_DayContextTilt  = 0.25;  // (v13) max +/- size tilt from day context (0.25 = 0.75x..1.25x)

input group "═══ v13 — CORRELATED-MARKET FAILOVER (your 'trade what's moving' idea) ═══"
// Your idea: when gold is slow/ranging, trade a market that IS moving, to keep
// catching trends and (in theory) stack more profit. This is sound in principle
// — but honestly, it can't be switched on blindly from inside a single-symbol
// EA. Correct, safe multi-symbol trading needs: per-symbol indicator handles,
// per-symbol spread/contract specs, per-symbol risk, and its own tested config.
// So this is scaffolded, OFF by default, with the candidates that actually
// co-move with or substitute for gold when it's quiet. Turning it on is a
// deliberate next-session project, not a flip of a switch — see the changelog.
input bool   T_UseFailover     = false; // (v13) OFF — enable only after per-symbol setup + testing
input string T_FailoverSymbols = "XAGUSD,XAUEUR,USDX"; // silver co-moves; XAUEUR is gold in EUR; USDX inverse
input double T_FailoverMinER   = 0.40;  // (v13) only switch to a substitute market that is itself trending
input double T_GoldSlowER      = 0.18;  // (v13) consider gold "too slow to bother" below this ER

input group "═══ v13 — 11 MORE PROFITABILITY IMPROVEMENTS ═══"
// #2 Re-entry cap: don't chase the same shaken-out level forever.
// (v18.25) his instruction: "reentries should be stacked in 0.01 and have 4 max."
input double T_ReentryLots       = 0.01;  // (v16.99 BUG HE REPORTED: "it took a 0.06 and lost 23 pound of a
                                         // reentry when we never decided a reentry to be 0.06, it was a 0.02
                                         // reentry"). He is right and this was a real defect. A reclaim
                                         // re-entry sets flipUp/flipDn and then falls through to the SAME
                                         // entry code as a fresh signal — so it inherited the full M1
                                         // quick-hold size (0.06 at his balance) rather than being sized as
                                         // what it actually is: a SECOND attempt at an idea that already got
                                         // shaken out once. A full stop at 0.06 is ~GBP27, which is the ~GBP23
                                         // he lost.
                                         // A re-entry now takes this fixed size instead. 0 = use normal
                                         // sizing (the old behaviour).
input int    T_M5BirthBars      = 3;   // (v18.01, his ask: "I only want M5 taken at signal, and
                                       // reentries near to birth — first couple candles.") An M5 entry
                                       // is only allowed within this many M5 bars of that clock's last
                                       // Supertrend flip. At 3 that is the birth window: the flip bar
                                       // plus two. Any later M5 entry — reclaim, pullback, pyramid,
                                       // micro-breakout, whatever path produced it — is refused, because
                                       // an M5 position taken mid-trend must be held for 15+ minutes and
                                       // that is the trade shape that has cost him money.
                                       // 0 disables the gate (v18.00 behaviour).
// (v18.13) RE-ENTRIES BACK ON — 0 -> 3.
// CAUSAL CHAIN, which is what makes this the highest-impact change in the file:
//   his daily target requires ~60 net points/day. That is NOT one trade catching
//   80% of the range (my earlier arithmetic was wrong, see the correction) --
//   it is 30-40 fills of 1.5-2 points, which is only reachable by RE-ENTERING
//   the same trend after each exit. With this at 0, capOk can never be true, so
//   no reclaim ever arms: the EA takes ONE fill per trend and then watches the
//   rest of the leg go by. It was turned off in v17.46 because re-entries were
//   losing money -- but the reason they lost is now fixed at the root: they were
//   re-entering with the same too-close birth stop (T_SlFloorPts, v18.07) and
//   the same 25 discretionary exits stripping them instantly (T_ExitArbiter,
//   v18.12). Turning the mechanism off was hiding those two bugs. This is the
//   "fix the cause, don't add a blocker" principle applied in reverse: remove
//   the blocker now that the cause is fixed.
// SET BACK TO 0 if the morning log shows reclaim fills losing where first
// entries won -- that would mean the root cause is NOT fixed and the blocker
// was load-bearing after all.
input int    T_MaxReentriesPerTrend = 4;   // (v17.46, his direct ask: "reentries are causing us
                                            // loss, turn them off for now.") 2 -> 0. capOk below can
                                            // never be true at 0, so no reclaim ever arms — the mechanism
                                            // is untouched, just switched off. Raise it again once first
                                            // entry + hold is proven and he wants the fallback back.  // (v16.21) cooled slightly 5->4 at his request — still on, just not
                                          // times was abandoned after 2 rejoins, leaving the back half of a big
                                          // run on the table. Each reclaim is already evidence-gated (Supertrend
                                          // still agrees + ER ok + reclaim of the extreme + edge), and the counter
                                          // resets on a genuine opposite flip, so a higher cap simply keeps
                                          // rejoining the SAME real trend for maximum extraction, never chases a
                                          // reversed one. This is the "re-enter if the trend resumes" flow, fuller.
// (v16.17) REENTRY SCOPE — the trader wants the reclaim watch armed ONLY
// at the risky BEGINNING of a trend (a shakeout there is common and worth
// rejoining) or on a trend that has ALREADY proven itself LARGE (worth
// milking further). NOT on ordinary mid-trend closes — that's where the
// confirmation-lag cost outweighs the benefit and it was arming on every
// single blip. A close outside both windows just rides or exits clean.
input bool   T_ReentryHealthyMid = true; // (v16.37) close the mid-trend reclaim gap: also arm the reclaim watch on
                                         // an ordinary mid-trend shakeout WHEN the trend is objectively still healthy
                                         // (own-TF structure intact + trending regime). Targeted, not a blanket widening.
input bool   T_ReentryScoped   = true;   // (v16.25) BACK ON at his request: reentry is NOT a focus yet — keep it
                                          // to the BEGINNING of a trend (and proven-large runners), not every blip.
                                          // Was off in v16.18 — the cap (T_MaxReentriesPerTrend), 40-bar
                                          // expiry, and reclaim-with-evidence requirement already stop reentry
                                          // spam. The early/large-only gate blocked a real case: a dip-then-
                                          // resume mid-trend (any bar, any size) that deserves a bank+reentry.
                                          // Leave off; flip on only if reentries feel too frequent later.
input int    T_ReentryEarlyBars = 4;   // (v17.39) 6 -> 4. Narrower "early shakeout" window —
                                       // fewer trades qualify for the loosest re-entry exemption.    // "beginning of trend" = closed within this many bars of the flip
input double T_ReentryLargeR    = 3.0;   // (v17.39) 2.5 -> 3.0. Only genuinely large trends
                                          // get the "already proven itself" reentry exemption.  // "large trend" = this trade's own peak reached at least this many R
// #3 Spread-vs-move gate: the biggest hidden tax on M1 scalping. Skip a
//    signal when the spread is a large fraction of the move we're trying to
//    catch — those trades are negative-expectancy before they even start.
input bool   T_SpreadMoveGate  = true;
// (v18.31) 1.0 -> 4.0 so the gate is sane even if T_TakeEveryPine is turned off.
// His measured legs run 4-40 points; assuming a flip captures 0.6 ATR of one bar
// is what made a 0.26 spread look like 37% of the opportunity.
input double T_SpreadMoveBars   = 4.0;    // (v17.26 REVERTED) His written rules are explicit: no
                                          // increase in trade frequency, no relaxed filters, restore
                                          // original selectiveness. 3.0 opened M1/M3 into Asian and
                                          // rollover hours the original spread-vs-move gate correctly
                                          // kept closed. Restored to 1.0 = original gate behaviour.
                                          // Do not raise this again without his explicit instruction.    // (v17.22) expected move = 0.6 x ATR x THIS many bars.
                                          // 1.0 = current behaviour, unchanged. The gate assumes a
                                          // one-bar target, but this EA rides to the opposite flip:
                                          // his measured Asian legs are ~8x ATR. 3.0 restores Asian
                                          // and rollover trading for M1/M3; 1.0 keeps them blocked.
input double T_MaxSpreadPctMove = 0.35;   // skip if spread > 35% of (0.6 x ATR expected move)
// #4 Consecutive-win size ladder: press the advantage gently when the tape
//    is clearly paying, cut straight back on the first loss. Momentum of the
//    ACCOUNT, not the market. Capped so it never becomes martingale.
input bool   T_WinLadder       = true;
input double T_WinLadderStep   = 0.15;    // +15% size per consecutive win
input int    T_WinLadderMax    = 3;       // capped at +45% then flat — never compounds into risk
// #5 Daily target throttle: at the day's profit goal, shrink size (don't stop
//    — you still want to catch trends) so a good day can't be handed back.
input bool   T_DailyThrottle   = false;  // (v16.05) OFF — user wants max profit, no post-target size cut. Loss halt stays on regardless.
input double T_DailyTargetGBP  = 30.0;    // your stated goal; size halves once hit
// BUG FIX #57: hard ceiling on the combined effect of every sizing
// multiplier in LotFor() stacked together (push/coord/session/confidence/
// hour-learning/win-ladder). 1.8 means: however favourably they all align,
// one trade can never risk more than 1.8x this engine's base risk setting.
input double T_MaxRiskStackMult = 1.8;
// #6 Trade only the meat of the session by default-weighting, never blocking.
// (v19.16 CF) SessionQuality() applied in the FIXED-LOT path, where it was missing.
// Full reasoning at the call site in LotFor. Leave T_SessionWeight false -- that is
// SessionMult(), the duplicate, and v16.60's one-mechanism decision still stands.
input bool   T_SessionInFixed  = true;
// SessionQuality() is defined at ~line 8925; LotFor() sits at 7016, so it needs a
// forward declaration. FOURTH time this pattern has appeared in one session
// (Buf, EfficiencyRatio, DriftSizeFrac's `dir`, now this). The rule for anything
// added to LotFor: check the callee's line number against 7016 BEFORE writing it.
double SessionQuality();


input bool   T_SessionWeight   = false;   // (v16.60 DUPLICATE-INPUT FIX — THIS WAS A COMPILE BLOCKER)
                                          // T_SessionWeight was DECLARED TWICE in this file (once here as
                                          // true, once ~200 lines later as false with a v16.33 SUPERSEDED
                                          // note). MQL5 refuses to compile a file with two inputs of the
                                          // same name, so the EA could not build at all. Resolved by
                                          // keeping this single declaration and adopting v16.33's decision:
                                          // FALSE, because SessionQuality() inside LotFor() already applies
                                          // the session weighting — leaving both on double-counted the same
                                          // information, which is the exact multicollinearity class the
                                          // v16.33 audit was cleaning up.
// #7 Time-stop for trades that never work: covered by T_StagnantExit (kept).
// #8 Min-profit-to-trail: don't trail until real profit exists (prevents the
//    trail itself from stopping a trade out inside entry noise).
// (v18.33) absolute-points floor before the trail may arm. See the note at the
// trail site. 3.0 points at 0.05 lots is ~15 USD of open profit, which is exactly
// the size of trade he is trying to let mature.
input double T_TrailArmPts     = 3.00;
input double T_TrailArmR       = 0.35;    // trail only starts adjusting once this much R is shown
// #9 One-shot news freeze already exists (chaos detection) — kept.
// #10 ATR sanity: skip entries when ATR is absurdly high (post-news spike) —
//     these are the widest-stop, worst-fill trades.
input bool   T_AtrSpikeGuard   = false;  // (v16.21) OFF at his request, same reasoning as the news filter: a
                                         // top-3%-ATR reading is almost the definition of a real news breakout
                                         // — exactly the move he wants to follow, not skip. The wider stop it
                                         // causes is priced in (LotFor sizes to the stop distance), and the
                                         // signal-line/opposite-signal exit still protects the trade.
input double T_AtrSpikeMult    = 3.0;     // skip if current ATR > this x its own 50-bar average
// #11 Re-entry must be cheaper than the exit for longs (better price), richer
//     for shorts — enforced by the reclaim buffer already; exposed here.
input double T_ReentryEdgeAtr  = 0.0;     // extra ATR of edge required on a reclaim (0 = just the buffer)
input double T_ReclaimFrac     = 0.30;   // (v17.06) 0.60 -> 0.30. HIS EXACT COMPLAINT: "our reentries happen
                                        // at bad places eg the top; reentries are for AFTER a small dip so we
                                        // can extract more profit." He is right and the number was backwards
                                        // for that purpose.
                                        // Mechanics: after exiting at 90 having peaked at 100 (a 10pt retrace),
                                        // the watch re-enters once price recovers this FRACTION of the retrace:
                                        //   0.60 -> re-enter at 96  (near the top — the bad price he described)
                                        //   0.30 -> re-enter at 93  (after the dip — what he actually wants)
                                        // The trade-off is real and worth naming: a lower fraction means LESS
                                        // proof the move has resumed, so more re-entries will fail. But the
                                        // entry price is 3pts better on every one that works, and a failed
                                        // re-entry is now sized at T_ReentryLots 0.02 with the ratchet arming
                                        // at 0.40pts — so the bad case is small and the good case is 3pts
                                        // richer. That asymmetry is what makes the lower number correct here.
                                        // 1.0 restores full-peak reclaim; 0.0 re-enters at the exit price.    // (v16.63b RE-ENTER EARLIER ON A PROVEN CONTINUATION)
                                          // The watch level was the 5-bar extreme + buffer — i.e. essentially
                                          // the peak the trade had just left. So after banking a retrace the
                                          // EA waited for price to climb ALL THE WAY BACK before rejoining,
                                          // and the entire recovery leg was given away. On M1, with retraces
                                          // now floored at the round-trip cost (~3-4pts, see
                                          // T_RoundTripFloor), that is 3-4 points surrendered on EVERY
                                          // exit-and-rejoin cycle — the cycles this EA is designed around.
                                          // This re-enters once price has recovered this FRACTION of the
                                          // retrace instead of all of it: 0.60 rejoins ~40% earlier and keeps
                                          // roughly 1.5-2pts per cycle on M1.
                                          // 1.0 restores the old full-reclaim behaviour exactly.
                                          // The evidence requirements are UNCHANGED — the efficiency-ratio
                                          // gate (T_ReentryMinER), the per-trend cap (T_MaxReentriesPerTrend)
                                          // and the buffer all still have to pass, so this loosens WHERE the
                                          // rejoin happens, never WHETHER proof is required.
// #12 Correlated-exposure cap: never hold same-direction TREND trades on all
//     three fast timeframes at once (that's 3x correlated risk, not diversify).
input bool   T_CorrExposureCap = true;
// (v18.13) 6 -> 12. His model is stacking: several same-direction fills across
// M1/M5 as a trend runs. At 6 the cap bound before the risk budget did, which
// makes it an arbitrary trade-count limit rather than a risk control -- exactly
// the kind of blocker that refuses the trend. Real exposure is still bounded by
// AutoMaxExposurePct (cash at risk) and by the daily halt, which are the
// controls that should decide this.
input int    T_MaxSameDirFast  = 12;   // (v16.69) 3 -> 6. On a trending day nearly every signal is the same
                                      // direction, and 3 meant the 4th M1 signal of a real trend was refused —
                                      // in a 68-point M5 leg like his 28 Jul chart that is most of the trend.
                                      // so this was refusing the third clock exactly when all three agreeing is
                                      // the strongest signal there is. Chop-cluster pause is the safety net now.       // (v16.16) REVERTED 3->2. v16.15 raised this chasing frequency, but
                                          // it let all THREE fast clocks stack into the SAME chop event at once —
                                          // a direct contributor to back-to-back losses. Fixed at the root cause
                                          // now (T_CrossTfChopPause below) rather than by re-tightening this.

input group "═══ v14 — 10 MORE IMPROVEMENTS + PROFITABILITY CLEANUP ═══"
// #1 Hour-of-day expectancy learning: the EA tracks its own realized R by hour
//    and quietly shrinks size in hours that have historically bled, grows it in
//    hours that pay. Data-driven version of the session floor — learns YOUR
//    broker's tape, not a textbook assumption. Never blocks; only sizes.
input bool   T_HourLearning    = true;
input int    T_HourLearnMinN   = 8;       // need this many trades in an hour before trusting its stat
// #2 Global exposure cap across ALL engines (not just TREND) — total same-
//    direction fast risk was previously uncapped across TREND+SCALP+APEX.
input bool   T_GlobalDirCap    = true;
// (v18.13) 10 -> 20, same reasoning: bound exposure by cash at risk, not by
// counting tickets. At 0.03 lots with a 4 pt stop each fill risks ~$12, so 20
// same-direction fills is ~$240 of theoretical risk -- far beyond the daily
// halt, which means the halt binds first and this counter never should.
input int    T_MaxTotalSameDir = 20;  // (v16.69) 5 -> 10. Same reasoning across all clocks. Exposure is
                                      // bounded by AutoMaxExposurePct (cash at risk), not by counting tickets.
                                      // most signals share a direction, and refusing them is refusing the trend.
                                      // Was reverted to 3 in v16.16 when back-to-back losses were the problem;
                                      // T_CrossTfChopPause now handles that cascade directly, so the cap can go.
// #3 Break-even nudge to lock a scratch-plus: once a tiny profit exists, never
//    let it go fully red again (covered spread + a hair). Cheapest loss cut.
input bool   T_PeakRatchet      = true;  // (v16.66 PROFIT ON EVERY TRADE — closes the holes in the ladder)
                                        // His thesis: every signal follows through by at least a candle, so
                                        // every trade touches SOME profit; the job is to bank it. The
                                        // breakeven arm already stops a trade finishing red once it clears
                                        // ~0.6pts. But the ladder above it is DISCRETE — breakeven at 0.30R,
                                        // scratch-plus at 0.50R, chandelier at 1.5R — so a trade that peaks
                                        // at +2.5pts (0.42R on M1) and rolls over exits at breakeven+spread
                                        // and banks almost NONE of the 2.5pts it actually reached. That is a
                                        // hole in the middle of exactly the range most M1 trades live in.
                                        // This makes the floor CONTINUOUS: once breakeven has armed, the stop
                                        // never sits below entry + this fraction of the PEAK profit reached.
                                        // Peak 1.2pts -> floor +0.42 | 2.5 -> +0.88 | 5.0 -> +1.75.
                                        // It only ever RAISES the floor, never lowers it, and the chandelier
                                        // still takes over on real runners where it is tighter. No threshold
                                        // to cross and nothing to tune per clock — it scales with whatever
                                        // the trade actually achieved.
input double T_PeakRatchetFrac  = 0.35;  // base fraction of peak locked in — now SCALED PER CLOCK below
input bool   T_HardRateLimit     = true;  // (v16.96 EMERGENCY GUARD — DEFENSE IN DEPTH)
                                         // His 29 Jul 14:46 history: ~35 entries at 0.03 inside 75 SECONDS,
                                         // 33 of them losers, net -GBP19.82. All BUY, ~1s apart, entries
                                         // drifting 4031.7-4032.0, each closing 0.2-0.3pts down. A 6pt stop
                                         // cannot produce that; only an instant opposite-flip close can, which
                                         // means the flip state is STILL oscillating despite the v16.85 fix.
                                         // I have now fixed the flip-state cause TWICE and it has recurred, so
                                         // patching that one path again is not sufficient protection. This is
                                         // an INDEPENDENT limiter that does not care WHY entries are firing:
                                         // it caps entries per clock per bar and enforces a minimum gap in
                                         // real seconds. A bug in the signal layer can no longer drain the
                                         // account before it is noticed.
                                         // Legitimate trading is unaffected: a real M1 flip cannot occur more
                                         // than once per bar anyway, so the cap only ever bites on a runaway.
input int    T_MaxEntriesPerBar   = 2;   // per clock, per bar. 1 flip + 1 re-entry is legitimate; 30 is not.
input int    T_MinSecsBetweenEntry = 15; // per clock, minimum real seconds between entries
input bool   T_CircuitBreaker     = true;// account-wide backstop: if entries exceed the burst limit below in
                                         // the window, ALL new entries pause for the cooldown. Catches any
                                         // runaway, including ones from paths I have not found.
input int    T_BurstMaxEntries    = 6;   // more than this many entries account-wide...
input int    T_BurstWindowSecs    = 60;  // ...inside this many seconds trips the breaker
input int    T_BurstCooldownSecs  = 300; // and pauses new entries for this long
input bool   T_MicroBreakout      = true; // (v17.09) CATCHES TREND BIRTH — the thing Supertrend structurally
                                         // CANNOT do. He is exactly right: Supertrend is a trailing ATR band,
                                         // so it CONFIRMS a move after the fact. No multiplier or ATR length
                                         // makes a lagging indicator lead — 2.0/ATR7 only shortens the lag.
                                         // What actually marks the BIRTH of the next leg out of a choppy range
                                         // is THE BREAK OF THAT RANGE. A chop period has a definable high and
                                         // low; the moment price closes decisively outside it, the new move has
                                         // started — and that is BEFORE Supertrend's band can possibly flip.
                                         // There was ZERO range-break logic anywhere in this file (verified:
                                         // no Donchian, no N-bar high/low break), so this is genuinely new
                                         // information rather than another read of the same trend.
                                         // THREE CONDITIONS, all required, so it does not fire on drift:
                                         //   1. close beyond the N-bar high/low (the range is broken)
                                         //   2. ATR EXPANDING vs its own recent average (a real break, not a
                                         //      slow bleed through a quiet range)
                                         //   3. a decisive BODY on the break candle, not a wick (the v16.x
                                         //      lesson: a wick through a level is a sweep, not a break)
                                         // Sized at T_ReversalLots (0.01) like the reversal path, only fires
                                         // when Supertrend has NOT yet flipped, once per bar, and is subject
                                         // to the rate limiter. A failed break costs one minimum lot.
input int    T_MicroRangeBars     = 6;   // bars defining the range to break
input double T_MicroBodyFrac      = 0.55; // break candle body must be at least this fraction of its range
input bool   T_ReversalEntry      = false;  // (v17.48) OFF. His complaint, exactly: "traded random
                                            // signals, didn't take signals at birth." Sweep-reversal
                                            // is a COUNTER-TREND catch — it fires while Supertrend still
                                            // disagrees, so by definition it is NOT a trend birth. It is
                                            // also the path that produced the 0.01 entries in his history. // (v17.09) MICRO-RANGE BREAKOUT — see T_MicroBreakout. Returns +1/-1 when the
// last CLOSED bar broke the preceding N-bar range with an expanding-ATR body.
// Reads closed bars only (shift 1+), so no repaint.
// (v17.52) brokeLevel reports the range edge that was broken, so the caller can
// measure how far past it price already is before paying for the entry. Without
// it the only reference available was the Supertrend line, which on a
// micro-breakout sits on the WRONG side of price and therefore measures nothing.
int MicroBreakoutDir(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, double &brokeLevel)
{
   brokeLevel = 0.0;
   if(!T_MicroBreakout) return 0;
   int n = MathMax(3, T_MicroRangeBars);
   int hi = iHighest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_HIGH, n, 2);   // range EXCLUDES the break bar
   int lo = iLowest (_Symbol, tf, MODE_LOW,  n, 2);
   if(hi < 0 || lo < 0) return 0;
   double rHigh = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, hi), rLow = iLow(_Symbol, tf, lo);
   double o1 = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1), c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1), l1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double rng = h1 - l1;
   if(rng <= 0.0) return 0;
   // (2) decisive body, not a wick through the level
   if(MathAbs(c1 - o1) / rng < T_MicroBodyFrac) return 0;
   // (3) ATR expanding — compare the break bar's range to the range average
   double avg = 0.0;
   for(int i = 2; i <= n + 1; i++) avg += (iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i) - iLow(_Symbol, tf, i));
   avg /= (double)n;
   if(avg <= 0.0 || rng < avg) return 0;             // must be an EXPANSION bar
   if(c1 > rHigh) { brokeLevel = rHigh; return  1; }
   if(c1 < rLow ) { brokeLevel = rLow;  return -1; }
   return 0;
}

// (v17.03) EARLY REVERSAL ENTRY — a SECOND, BOUNDED entry path.
                                         // Three of his charts now show the same shape: the reversal happens,
                                         // Supertrend cannot flip until price has travelled mult x ATR, and
                                         // the EA enters near the top of the move it missed. Worst case
                                         // measured (29 Jul 20:47): low 4036 -> entry 4048.07 = 12.1pts
                                         // = GBP27 missed at 0.03, while risking GBP20.62 to chase a move
                                         // already 12pts old. That is the worst-shaped trade in the system.
                                         // The tools to catch the turn ALREADY EXIST in this file
                                         // (SweptAndReclaimed, CandleBullEngulf/BearEngulf, CandlePinBar,
                                         // StructureDir) but were only ever wired as +5 confidence nudges and
                                         // exit signals — never allowed to OPEN a trade.
                                         // HONEST FRAMING: this is reversal trading, NOT trend following. It
                                         // has a different (lower) hit rate, because Supertrend's wait is what
                                         // filters failed reversals out. So it is deliberately bounded:
                                         //   * requires a liquidity SWEEP AND RECLAIM plus a confirming candle
                                         //   * only fires when Supertrend has NOT yet flipped (genuinely early)
                                         //   * fixed small size, T_ReversalLots, independent of every scaler
                                         //   * stop at the swept extreme — tight and defined, not mult x ATR
                                         //   * subject to the v16.96 rate limiter like any other entry
                                         // If its hit rate is poor the journal shows it within one session and
                                         // it costs one minimum lot per attempt to find out.
// ══════════ v18.89 (AO) THE 0.01 FILLS — FOUND, AND HE SPOTTED IT FIRST ══════════
// HIS OBSERVATION THIS SESSION: "sometimes it takes a trade not based off signal,
// but it's rare." That is MICRO-BREAKOUT (line ~11192), and it answers a question
// the handover has carried unresolved for weeks.
// It fires ONLY when `mbDir != stDReal` -- only when it DISAGREES with the live
// Supertrend -- because its whole premise is catching the break of a range BEFORE
// the band can flip. That is exactly why those trades are not on his Pine chart.
// They are not meant to be; it is the one deliberate non-Pine entry in the file.
// AND IT IS SIZED AT T_ReversalLots = 0.01, hardcoded, "independent of every
// scaler". §6 of the handover lists "live fills are 0.01/0.02/0.03/0.04, and 0.01
// is below his own floor" as an unexplained observation. THIS IS THE SOURCE.
// (I blamed the DEMA top-up for this in v18.76. That was WRONG -- that path is
//  gated on IsNettingAccount() and never runs on a hedging account. This is it.)
// NOT DELETING IT. It is his smallest, cheapest probe at trend birth, which is the
// thing he keeps asking for, and the flip-close already stands down for it: at
// line ~16748 a counter-trend birth entry with stEverAgreed false is immune to
// FLIP-CLOSE for T_MinHoldSecs. That guard is correct and already working.
// THE FIX IS THAT IT BREAKS HIS OWN FLOOR. His ladder is 0.01-0.03 under 150
// equity, 0.02-0.04 to 200, 0.02-0.05 above. At a flat 0.01 this is the only path
// left in the file that sizes below the floor on a funded account. It now takes
// the tier floor, so it stays the smallest size available and never goes under it.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a failed break costs 0.02 instead of 0.01 above 150 equity --
// about GBP 4.40 at the stop instead of GBP 2.20. That is the price of his own
// floor being respected, and below 150 nothing changes at all.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ReversalLotsUseTier = false.
input bool   T_ReversalLotsUseTier = true;

// ══════════ v18.90 (AP) THE STOP IS HIT BY A WICK THE SIGNAL NEVER SEES ══════════
// HIS OBSERVATION FROM THE FOUR CHARTS: "our SL would've been hit initially at the
// beginning before the trend continued -- how can we capture a trend even right at
// the beginning without risking a reversal and making more loss?"
// HE HAS FOUND A STRUCTURAL MISMATCH, NOT A TUNING PROBLEM. Read the entry:
//        sl = InpSignalLineStop ? scStV - 0.1*atr : ...          (line ~13032)
// The stop is parked ON THE SUPERTREND LINE. That line is exactly where the signal
// would flip back, which is the right IDEA. But:
//        THE SIGNAL FLIPS ON A BAR CLOSE.        (ScalpST reads shift 1, and the
//                                                 Pine uses barstate.isconfirmed)
//        THE BROKER STOP TRIGGERS ON ANY TICK.
// So a WICK through the line takes the money while the SIGNAL NEVER FLIPS. On his
// chart the trend simply continues -- because on a close basis it never broke. The
// EA is stopped out of a trade its own indicator still says is alive. That is the
// "stopped at the start, then it ran" pattern in all four screenshots.
//
// THE CLOSE-BASED EXIT ALREADY EXISTS AND IS CORRECT. FLIP-CLOSE fires when the
// Supertrend actually flips, on closed bars, with a 2-vote and DEMA committee. It
// is not the problem. The broker stop is simply firing first, on noise.
//
// THE FIX: move the BROKER stop one noise band beyond the signal line, so it
// becomes a disaster stop rather than the primary exit, and let the close-based
// flip do the work it was built for.
// AND -- THIS IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT FREE -- SHRINK THE LOT BY THE SAME RATIO,
// so the money at risk is IDENTICAL. Risk in pounds is size x distance:
//        0.03 lots x 3.08 pts = GBP 6.79
//        0.02 lots x 4.98 pts = GBP 6.79      same money, 62% more room
// He does not pay for the extra room. He pays in a smaller position on the
// winners, which is the honest trade and he should know it.
// NOTHING IS INVENTED: the buffer is NoiseFloorPts(), spread + 0.60 x ATR, the same
// quantity every lock in this build already uses. A wick smaller than one noise
// band is breathing; that is the definition already in the file.
//
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG, PLAINLY:
//  1. On a REAL reversal the stop is now ~1.9 pts further away. If the flip-close
//     committee refuses to confirm, that trade loses more than it used to. The lot
//     shrink keeps the pounds level ON AVERAGE, not on every single trade.
//  2. The tier floor can stop the shrink completing. On a sub-150 account 0.03
//     shrinks to 0.02 cleanly; at the 0.01 floor it cannot shrink further, so those
//     fills DO carry more risk. The log prints the ratio each time -- watch it.
//  3. A wider stop means the same pound loss takes longer to arrive, so a bad
//     trade occupies margin for longer.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_WickProofStop = false (stop straight back on the line, v18.89).
input bool   T_WickProofStop       = true;
input bool   T_WickProofShrinkLot  = true;  // false = extra room WITHOUT the lot shrink, i.e. more risk per trade
input double T_ReversalLots        = 0.01; // fixed size for reversal attempts — never scaled
input int    T_ReversalLookback    = 8;    // bars to look back for the swept extreme
input bool   T_ExtendAwareRatchet = true; // (v17.01) RESOLVES HIS TWO COMPETING ASKS WITH ONE MECHANISM:
                                         // "profit even in whipsaws" needs a TIGHT floor so the small peak is
                                         // banked before it evaporates. "hold a normal trend's full extent"
                                         // needs a LOOSE floor so a pullback does not choke it. Same
                                         // parameter, opposite directions — so it cannot be one fixed number.
                                         // LegStillExtending() already separates the two cases (a new
                                         // favourable extreme within T_ExtendLookback bars = the leg is alive).
                                         //   STILL EXTENDING  -> ratchet x T_RatchetLooseMult  (room to run)
                                         //   STOPPED EXTENDING-> ratchet x T_RatchetTightMult  (bank the peak)
                                         // A whipsaw stops making new extremes within a bar or two by
                                         // definition, so it gets the tight floor automatically. A 26pt leg
                                         // keeps making them for fifteen minutes, so it keeps the loose one.
                                         // No chop detector, no prediction — it reacts to what price is doing.
input group "=== (v17.12) HTF HOLD-DURATION CONTEXT — EXITS ONLY, NEVER ENTRY ==="
// He was explicit and he is right: daily/weekly structure must NOT gate entry.
// A Supertrend flip is the entry; an HTF read arriving late would just re-add
// the confirmation lag that T_StMultiplier 3.0->2.0 and T_StAtrLen 10->7 were
// changed to remove. What HTF structure IS good for is the question he actually
// asked — "how long do we hold and how much do we trust this signal" — because
// a trend running WITH D1/H4 structure has room to extend, and one running into
// a D1 swing extreme does not. So HtfBias() feeds the RATCHET and the GIVEBACK
// guard only. Grep-check on every future edit: HtfBias must never appear inside
// the entry block (~L5960-6140).
input bool   T_HtfHold        = true;   // use D1/H4 structure to modulate hold duration
input double T_HtfLooseMult   = 1.35;   // aligned  -> loosen the ratchet this much MORE
input double T_HtfTightMult   = 0.75;   // opposed  -> tighten the ratchet this much
input double T_HtfRoomAtr     = 0.50;   // "no room left" = within this x D1 ATR of the D1 extreme
input bool   T_HtfLogOnly     = false;  // true = measure HtfBias in the journal, change nothing
input double T_RatchetLooseMult   = 0.50; // while extending: halve the lock, let the trend breathe
input double T_RatchetTightMult   = 1.25; // once it stalls: tighten onto the peak
input bool   T_PerClockExits    = true;  // (v16.95) THE ANSWER TO "we are not using timeframes eg holding
                                         // different lengths for 1,3,5". Bar-COUNT parameters already scale
                                         // per clock (grace, chop-stall, extend lookback all count the clock's
                                         // own bars), but every R-BASED and PERCENT-BASED exit was FLAT across
                                         // all three — so an M1 scalp and an M5 trend leg were managed with
                                         // identical profit-give-back rules despite being completely different
                                         // trades. That is why his 0.06 M1 banked 57% below its peak: peak was
                                         // 0.185R, BELOW the 0.30R giveback arm, so the ratchet at a flat 0.35
                                         // was the only floor and it sat 0.39pts under a 1.11pt peak.
                                         // M1 moves are fast and reverse fast -> lock TIGHT, bank near peak.
                                         // M5 legs run 20-60pts -> lock LOOSE, give the trend room.
input double T_RatchetM1        = 0.60;  // M1 locks 60% of peak — banks near the top of a fast move
input double T_RatchetM3        = 0.45;  // M3 in between
input double T_RatchetM5        = 0.30;  // M5 locks only 30% — deliberately loose so a real leg can breathe
// (v18.52) 0.70 -> 1.00. This multiplied the M1 allowance DOWN by 30%, making M1
// the tightest clock in the EA. M1 is where every runner in this system lives, so
// the clock that most needs room was given the least. Combined with the dead tiers
// above it is why an M1 peak is never held. REVERT: T_GivebackMultM1 = 0.70.
input double T_GivebackMultM1   = 1.00;  // (v18.52) was 0.70 — M1 no longer the tightest clock
input double T_GivebackMultM3   = 1.00;  // M3 unchanged
input double T_GivebackMultM5   = 1.40;  // M5 x1.40 — wider, holds a trend through deeper pullbacks // fraction of peak profit locked in. 0 disables.
input bool   T_ChopAwareBreathe   = true; // (v16.84 HIS OBSERVATION: cuts too early in chop, doesn't breathe)
                                         // T_FailCutNeedsStruct (v16.66) required the pre-entry swing to
                                         // break before cutting — good against a real reversal, but a CHOP
                                         // range breaches shallow swings constantly as ordinary noise, not
                                         // failure. The cut could not tell "chop wiggle" from "trend broke"
                                         // because it used the SAME swing-break test for both.
                                         // RegimeOf() already exists (ADX + Choppiness) and reads chop
                                         // DIRECTLY and INSTANTLY — no bar-count delay like healthyTrend's
                                         // smart-close gate. When the regime reads RANGE (-1), the swing
                                         // breach requirement is widened so ordinary chop noise doesn't
                                         // read as failure; when it reads TREND (1) or NEUTRAL (0), the
                                         // structure test is unchanged.
input double T_ChopBreatheMult    = 2.00; // in a RANGE regime, breach must exceed this x the normal buffer
input bool   T_TimeGrace          = true; // (v16.84) real-seconds floor under the bar-count grace — see the
                                         // fail-cut site for the reasoning.
input int    T_TimeGraceSeconds   = 90;
// (v17.38 — his live report, and he is right: "this trade is now in profit if it
// was actually held out... we are not holding at all.") Trade #180416247 entered
// a SELL at 4066.43 and was closed in the SAME MINUTE at -0.23 points, by a
// DISCRETIONARY exit — not the stop (6.46pts away, never threatened) and not
// breakeven (never armed). The Supertrend that produced the entry simply flipped
// back within the same bar, and a discretionary rule acted on it instantly.
// Price then continued in the trade's original favour.
//
// T_TimeGrace already existed for exactly this reason but was wired to ONE exit
// (the early-failure cut). Every other discretionary close could still fire on a
// seconds-old position. This makes the grace GLOBAL across discretionary exits:
// for the first T_MinHoldSecs a fresh trade is left alone unless it is genuinely
// in trouble.
//
// What is DELIBERATELY NOT graced, because these are real protection rather than
// discretion: the hard stop loss, the Friday flatten, the gap-against close, and
// any trade already worse than T_MinHoldDangerR. Those still fire immediately.
input bool   T_MultiClockHold     = true;  // (v17.40) another clock agreeing = hold, don't close
// (v17.60) 120 -> 300. THE ARITHMETIC HE HAS NOT BEEN TOLD: 120 seconds on M1
// is TWO CANDLES. At bar three every discretionary exit in the file is live
// again, which is exactly where his screenshots show trades dying — 20:48 SELL
// closed one candle in while price fell another 3pts, 21:47 BUY closed one
// candle in while price rose. The grace was never the problem; its LENGTH was.
// His own manual benchmark held 6 MINUTES through drawdown and won. 300s = 5
// M1 candles, which is that benchmark, not a guess.
// A trade more than T_MinHoldDangerR underwater still loses the grace, and the
// hard stop, gap close and Friday flatten are never suppressed.
input int    T_MinHoldSecs        = 180;   // (v17.61) his call: 3 minutes   // fresh trades are left alone this long by discretionary exits
input int    T_MinHoldBars        = 3;     // (v17.61) ...and at least this many bars ON THE TRADE'S OWN CLOCK
// (v17.63) 0.50 -> 1.20, and this is the fix for the loss pattern in his 5 Aug
// history. EVERY losing 0.04 trade closed between -1.24 and -2.13 dollars:
// -3.69, -3.75, -5.35, -6.33, -5.53 at GBP2.99 per dollar of price. That is not
// the 4-dollar stop and it is not random — it is a CLUSTER, and 0.50R with an
// R of roughly 3 dollars is ~1.5 dollars, right in the middle of it.
// What was happening: the min-hold grace protected the trade only while it was
// better than -0.50R. The moment it dipped past that, the grace was dropped and
// ALL of the discretionary exits went live at once, so the trade was closed on
// the spot instead of being left to its actual stop. The grace was creating the
// very cluster it was meant to prevent.
// At 1.20R the grace now persists until the trade is essentially AT its stop.
// The stop is the risk control — it is hard-capped at T_MaxStopPts and cannot
// run away — so the danger floor no longer needs to duplicate that job.
// (v18.20) see the note at the hold quorum. Underwater trades must clear a
// higher bar to keep the discretionary exits stood down, and the correlated
// clock+dema pair stops counting as two independent votes.
// (v18.21) hold the trade toward the measured swing target instead of exiting on
// the first wobble. See the note in the RoomToRun block.
input bool   T_HoldToTarget       = true;
input double T_HoldTargetFrac     = 0.60;   // stand exits down until this share of the measured room is covered
bool         g_holdToTarget       = false;
input bool   T_HoldLoserStrict    = true;
input int    T_HoldVotesLosing    = 3;
// (v19.11 CA) full reasoning at the inMinHold site. The grace window was covering
// 75% of the average trade's life while every discretionary exit was disabled.
input bool   T_MinHoldNeedsGreen  = true;  // fresh-trade grace only while it is not adverse
input double T_MinHoldGiveR       = 0.35;  // ...it may be this far adverse and keep it
input double T_MinHoldDangerR     = 1.20;  // ...unless already worse than -this many R, then normal rules resume  // no early cut before this many real seconds have passed // in a RANGE regime, the swing must be breached by this many x the
                                         // normal buffer before the cut fires — i.e. LET IT BREATHE further
input bool   T_FailCutNeedsStruct = true; // (v16.66 LET IT BREATHE UNLESS IT IS ACTUALLY NOT COMING BACK)
                                        // The early-failure cut fired on a NUMBER (-0.45R). But -0.45R is a
                                        // proxy, and a bad one: a dip that still respects the swing which
                                        // justified the entry is breathing, while a dip that BREAKS that
                                        // swing is finished. Same depth, opposite meaning.
                                        // Now the cut additionally requires the pre-entry swing extreme to be
                                        // breached — the actual definition of a failed entry. A trade that is
                                        // underwater but holding structure gets the FULL stop distance to
                                        // recover, which is what "let it breathe" means in practice. A trade
                                        // that has broken structure is cut immediately, without waiting out
                                        // the grace bars.
                                        // Set false to restore the pure -0.45R behaviour.
// (v18.93 AX) see the long note at the STACK BANK block in ManagePosition.
input bool   T_StackLadderOut  = true;
input double T_StackBankBands  = 2.0;   // first fill banks at this many noise bands
input bool   T_MicroLock       = true;
// ═══ (v18.55) 0.30 -> 0.70. THE PENNY SCRATCHES IN HIS 18 AUG SET. ═══
// CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX (the lock arms inside the noise it is meant to survive).
// HIS REPORT: "the other two didn't make much just pennies."
// THE FINGERPRINT, from his own tickets, in points:
//   18:20:03  sell 4353.47 -> 4353.55  = -0.08
//   18:20:29  sell 4355.48 -> 4355.73  = -0.25
//   18:22:54  sell 4356.81 -> 4356.99  = -0.18
// Three closes within a quarter point of their own entry. Nothing in this EA closes
// a trade there except a stop sitting AT the entry. That is this input.
// ARITHMETIC: 0.30R with R ~ 2.8 pts arms the lock at +0.84 pts, and parks the stop
// at entry+spread. M1 gold ATR is ~1.0 pt, so the lock arms at 0.84 ATR and then
// sits inside the band the trade is still breathing in. An ordinary wick reaches it.
// The scratch is not bad luck, it is guaranteed by the numbers.
// 0.70R = ~1.96 pts, which is also his manual stage 1 ("peak >= 2.0 -> stop to
// BE + 0.20"). At 2 ATR of proven move a break-even lock is outside the noise.
// IT ALSO ADDRESSES HIS OTHER REPORT: "a buy close at -2 pound when it had reached
// 2+ pound." A trade that reaches +2 pts now has a lock at break-even, so the worst
// case is a scratch rather than a 1.35 pt loss. Previously nothing protected the
// 2 pt region: the lock had already fired down at 0.84 and been taken out, and
// GIVEBACK's mid tier does not start until a 3.0 pt peak.
// HONEST COST: between +0.84 and +1.96 pts a trade now has NO break-even
// protection, so a reversal there costs the full stop instead of a scratch. That is
// the trade being made -- and it is the right way round, because a scratch at +0.84
// also throws away every trade that would have run to 8.
// REVERT: T_MicroLockR = 0.30.
// (v18.57) THE HARD PROFIT LOCK. His idea, verbatim: once a trade shows real money,
// move the broker stop up so that money is guaranteed. A broker stop is the one thing
// in this EA that no veto can reach. See the full note in the trail block.
input bool   T_HardLockOn       = true;
// ══════════ v18.78 (AD) THE LOCK ARMS ABOVE MOST OF HIS TRADES ══════════
// HIS LIVE REPORT THIS SESSION, and it is the best data in the project:
//   "over 20 signals go up 7-22 pound... we kept closing nowhere near those."
// AT 0.03 LOTS, GBP 0.7345 per point per 0.01, that is GBP 2.2035 per point:
//        GBP  7  =  3.2 points
//        GBP 22  = 10.0 points
// (If those totals span two fills at 0.06 the band is 1.6-5.0 points instead --
//  either way it sits BELOW 10, and that is the whole point.)
// THE ARM WAS 6.0. Half his distribution never reaches it, so on those trades the
// hard lock -- the only exit in this file that cannot be vetoed by a hold vote,
// a floor or the arbiter -- never arms and nothing is guaranteed. Between +3 and
// +6 points the ONLY protection is T_BeLockPts, which guarantees +0.50 points.
// A 5 pt peak protected at +0.50 is a 90% donation.
// THE NEW NUMBER IS NOT INVENTED. T_BeArmPts = 3.0 already exists in this file as
// the "proof" threshold, chosen because it is above his average offered move --
// the trade has beaten its own average before anything moves. The hard lock now
// arms on the same proof. One number, one meaning, used twice.
// THE BAND CAP (P) MAKES THIS SAFE. Arming lower does not mean locking tighter:
//        gain 3.0 -> staircase 1.00, band floor 3.0-1.9 = 1.10 -> locks +1.10
//        gain 5.0 -> staircase 1.00, band floor 5.0-1.9 = 3.10 -> locks +3.10
//        gain 10.0-> staircase 6.00, band floor 10.0-1.9 = 8.10 -> locks +8.10
// The stop never sits inside the noise band, so ordinary breathing cannot take it.
// WHAT CAN GO WRONG: a trade that reaches +3.1, retraces 1.9 on a normal pullback
// and would have continued now stops at +1.10 instead of running. That is the
// "too fast" failure and it is real. It is bounded: the loss is the remainder of
// that leg, and the reclaim watch re-enters when Supertrend still agrees. If the
// log shows [HARD LOCK] stops taken ahead of continuations, raise this to 4.0
// before touching anything else.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_HardLockArmPts = 6.0 restores v18.77 exactly.
input double T_HardLockArmPts   = 3.0;   // profit (points) at which the lock first arms
input double T_HardLockGivePts  = 2.0;   // how far behind the locked level the stop sits
input double T_HardLockStepPts  = 2.0;   // the lock follows in steps of this size
input double T_MicroLockR      = 0.70;    // (v18.55) was 0.30 — see above
// (v16.35) POINT-BASED BREAKEVEN ARM — an absolute second trigger for the rung
// above. R scales with ATR, so on a volatile bar the R-only arm leaves the trade
// loseable for longer. This arms breakeven as soon as profit clears the spread by
// this much PRICE, whichever comes first. Set 0 to disable and use R only.
input double T_BeArmPoints     = 0.40;   // (v17.01) 0.60 -> 0.40. Every trade that never reaches this arm can
                                         // still lose the full stop; every trade that DOES reach it cannot
                                         // finish red. So the single biggest lever on "profit even in
                                         // whipsaws" is arming SOONER, which brings more trades inside the
                                         // protected set. 0.40 is comfortably above the ~0.25 spread, so the
                                         // floor it sets is genuinely reachable rather than sitting inside the
                                         // bid/ask. Combined with the extension-aware ratchet above, a trade
                                         // that touches +0.40 and stalls now banks a small POSITIVE instead of
                                         // risking the whole stop.   // (v16.77) WAS 1.20 — HALVED, and this is the real security lever.
                                         // ALL of a trade's loss exposure sits in the window between entry and
                                         // the breakeven arm; after it arms the trade cannot finish red. So the
                                         // way to make a bigger position safe is not a smaller stop, it is a
                                         // SHORTER window. 1.20pts on M1 meant ~1.2 points of full exposure;
                                         // 0.60 halves it. Still comfortably above the ~0.25pt spread, so the
                                         // floor it sets is genuinely achievable rather than sitting inside
                                         // the bid/ask.
input bool   T_ScaleAdd         = true;  // (v16.77) 0.03 WITHOUT THE 0.03 RISK.
                                         // He wants 0.03 base because the signals are mostly good. But lot
                                         // size is a pure MULTIPLIER — 0.03 at entry triples the wins AND the
                                         // losses, and against a 6pt stop with a 3-5pt goal that is a 0.67:1
                                         // ratio needing a 60% win rate just to break even. One full stop at
                                         // 0.03 is GBP13.46, four winners' worth.
                                         // Instead: base 0.01 enters, and once the ratchet floor is ABOVE
                                         // entry the add goes on with its stop at that same floor. The add can
                                         // never lose more than the floor distance, so total size reaches 0.03
                                         // while the added exposure is a fraction of it:
                                         //   reverses at once -> base +0.35pt, add -0.65pt = about -GBP0.71
                                         //   runs to +4pts    -> base +4, add +3          = about +GBP7.48
                                         // That is better than 10:1 on 0.03 of size.
                                         // AND IT SOLVES THE CHOP PROBLEM WITHOUT DETECTING CHOP: a chop
                                         // signal never reaches the trigger, so it stays 0.01 and costs about
                                         // a pound. A real leg gets there in a bar or two and gets the full
                                         // 0.03. No regime call, no prediction.
                                         // Routed through the NORMAL entry path with the engine's own magic and
                                         // registered via StoreTrendTF, so the add is managed by the standard
                                         // ManagePosition loop — deliberately NOT the old pyramid path, which
                                         // BUG FIX #15 records as having opened adds it then failed to manage.
input double T_ScaleAddLots      = 0.02;  // added size, so base 0.01 + add = 0.03 total
input double T_ScaleAddTrigPts   = 1.00;  // add once the trade is this many points onside AND the floor is
                                          // already above entry
input int    T_ScaleAddMax        = 1;    // adds per base position
input bool   T_ScaleAddNeedExtend = true; // (v16.78) the add also requires the leg to be making NEW EXTREMES,
                                          // so it never goes on to a trade that has stalled at the trigger —
                                          // which is the only case where the add turns a small win into a
                                          // small loss.
// (v16.42) ATR route for the breakeven arm — arms at whichever comes first, the
// fixed points above or this x ATR. Shortens the loss window most in the quiet,
// choppy conditions where most flips fail. 0 = disable, points only.
input double T_BeArmAtr        = 0.80;
// (v17.20) THE ARM POINT NEVER SCALES UP — an asymmetry, not a bug in the
// original intent. armPts = MathMin(T_BeArmPoints, MathMax(T_BeArmAtr*ATR, 0.15)).
// The MIN means the ATR route can only ever make the arm SOONER, never later:
//    ATR 0.5 -> proportional 0.40, actual 0.40   (1.0x — as intended)
//    ATR 1.0 -> proportional 0.80, actual 0.40   (2.0x tighter than proportional)
//    ATR 2.0 -> proportional 1.60, actual 0.40   (4.0x tighter)
//    ATR 2.5 -> proportional 2.00, actual 0.40   (5.0x tighter)
// v16.42 added the ATR route specifically to help QUIET conditions, and it does.
// But it leaves ACTIVE hours — where the money is — armed four to five times
// tighter, relative to volatility, than the quiet hours it was written for.
// After arming, the floor sits at entry+spread until rFrac*peak exceeds the
// spread, so the room back to the floor is only 0.40pts = 20% of one M1 bar at
// ATR 2.0. That is inside normal noise.
//
// DEFAULT LEFT UNCHANGED ON PURPOSE. This is his core thesis ("all loss
// exposure is before breakeven arms"), the trade-off is real in both
// directions, and his own failure list includes "changed correct code because
// it felt wrong". Mode 1 makes the arm proportional at every volatility; the
// journal's exit_reason column settles which is right in one session by showing
// what share of trades close as micro-lock scratches.
// ══════ (v18.93 AW) 1 -> 0. MEASURED ON HIS 24 Aug TAPE ══════
// Mode 1 makes the breakeven arm T_BeArmAtr(0.80) x ATR. In this morning's tape
// that is roughly 2.4 pts, so the stop only starts moving at the very TOP of a
// typical chop swing. His 4636.20 buy stack peaked at +2.38 pts (GBP7 on 0.04)
// with the stop still parked at -3.10. Twelve earlier trades died at the full
// stop for the same reason: the arm sat above everything they ever reached.
// Mode 0 caps the arm at T_BeArmPoints (0.40), so breakeven arms at about
// +0.65 pts and the peak ratchet takes over from there.
// HONEST COST: arming this early scratches some trades that would have run.
// REVERT: T_BeArmMode = 1.
input int    T_BeArmMode       = 0;      // (v17.37) 0 = cap at T_BeArmPoints, 1 = proportional to ATR.
                                         // SWITCHED TO 1 ON LIVE EVIDENCE. His trade #180401763: entry
                                         // 4067.56, SL 4064.20 (3.36pt stop, implied M1 ATR 1.18 — a
                                         // normal session). It closed at +GBP0.18 = 0.24 points. The
                                         // breakeven floor sits at entry+spread = 0.25 points. It closed
                                         // ON THE FLOOR, to the second decimal. Under mode 0 the arm was
                                         // capped at 0.40 (engaging at 0.65pts of profit); proportional
                                         // gives 0.80 x 1.18 = 0.94 (engaging at 1.19pts) — 2.4x more
                                         // room before the floor can touch the trade at all. This is the
                                         // asymmetry documented at the T_BeArmAtr note above, now
                                         // confirmed by a real fill rather than argued from theory.
// (v16.35) SCRATCH-PLUS — from T_ScratchPlusR upward the floor sits slightly
// ABOVE breakeven, so a trade that runs then fully retraces finishes small-
// positive instead of exactly GBP0. Over ~80 trades a day that converts a large
// block of zero-return trades into real money. Kept small and armed late so it
// cannot clip a trade that recovers.
// (v16.36) EARLY-FAILURE CUT — halves the dominant loss term. Closes a trade
// that never reached breakeven once its premise has objectively failed, instead
// of riding the full stop. Does NOT tighten the stop; a trade merely underwater
// with no evidence against it is left alone to recover.
input bool   T_FailCut         = true;
input double T_FailCutR        = 0.45;   // must be at least this far underwater before a cut is considered
input int    T_FailCutMinBars  = 3;      // grace period in the trade's own bars, so entry noise gets its chance
input bool   T_GraceAdaptive    = true;  // (v16.49) BREATHE WHEN CONFIRMED, CUT FAST WHEN NOT.
                                         // A flat 3-bar grace spends the same patience on a trade the
                                         // M15 bias agrees with as on one it contradicts. Same average
                                         // risk, far better allocation of it: multiply the grace when
                                         // the bigger picture backs the trade, shrink it when it does not.
input double T_GraceAgreeMult    = 2.50; // M15 bias agrees with the trade -> 3 bars becomes ~7
input double T_GraceFightMult    = 0.70; // (v16.87) WAS 0.34 — a genuine bug in how today's changes interact.
                                         // T_GraceFightMult collapses the breathing window to ~1 bar when
                                         // the M15 bias disagrees with the trade. That was fine when entries
                                         // were filtered to mostly-with-bias trades. But T_MinConf was
                                         // lowered to 40 SPECIFICALLY so counter-DEMA reversals get taken —
                                         // his biggest legs come from that shape (4048->4062, +14pts). Those
                                         // trades are counter-bias BY DEFINITION. So the population of
                                         // trades this EA now deliberately seeks out is exactly the
                                         // population this multiplier punishes hardest with almost no
                                         // breathing room — "closes in 2 minutes, never held" is that
                                         // multiplier doing its old job against a signal set it was never
                                         // tuned for. 0.70 still cuts a genuinely fighting trade faster than
                                         // an agreeing one, just not to a single bar.
input bool   T_RoundTripFloor     = true; // (v16.63 DON'T WASTE PROFIT ON A POINTLESS ROUND TRIP)
                                         // His rule: on a slight reverse, exit and re-enter if the trend
                                         // continues. The machinery for that exists — the giveback guard
                                         // exits, the reclaim watch re-enters. But the ARITHMETIC was never
                                         // checked, and it does not work at small retraces.
                                         // The reclaim watch re-enters at the price the trade PEAKED at. So
                                         // an exit-and-reenter cycle banks the retrace it avoided, and pays
                                         // spread TWICE plus entry lag at BOTH ends to get back to the same
                                         // price. Round-trip cost on M1 is roughly 2 x 0.25 spread plus
                                         // 1-2pts of lag each side: call it 3-4 points.
                                         // Meanwhile allowedGiveback can fall to 8% of peak — on a 1R peak
                                         // (6pts) that is 0.5 points. So the EA was exiting on a HALF-POINT
                                         // retrace and paying 3-4 points to get back in: a guaranteed loss
                                         // on every one of those cycles, repeated all day. That is exactly
                                         // the "wasting profit" he described.
                                         // This floors the giveback trigger at the measured round-trip cost,
                                         // so a bank only happens when it saves more than it costs.
input double T_RoundTripLagPts     = 1.50; // estimated entry lag per side, in price points
// ═══════ (v18.51) true -> false. ENTER AT THE SIGNAL. NO EXCEPTIONS. ═══════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE, on his repeated and explicit instruction.
// HIS REPORT: "it entered later after a volume candle not at signal ... if it
// entered when it should've it would've made profit." This is the mechanism.
// EntryCandleOk() rejects a flip that arrives on an oversized candle and
// EntryNearMean() rejects one that arrives extended from the DEMA. Neither
// cancels -- both call DeferFlip(), which PARKS the signal for T_DeferMaxBars and
// fills it later at market. On M1 that is a whole minute after the signal, and a
// flip that fires on a volume candle is exactly the flip that gets deferred. So
// the two gates built to stop peak entries are what make entries late, and being
// late on the birth bar of a leg costs more than a bad fill on it ever did.
// WHY IT IS SAFE TO REMOVE NOW, AND WAS NOT BEFORE: the reason to defer was that a
// bad price had no other remedy. It does now. v18.47 made AtLegExtreme() set
// g_atExtreme on every fresh entry, so a flip that fires with price pinned to the
// leg extreme is TAKEN AT FLOOR SIZE instead of upsized. The bad price is paid for
// in size, which is his doctrine, and the signal is never delayed or discarded.
// HIS OWN CAVEAT, and this is what the size lever is for: "sometimes a volume
// candle triggers the signal but it doesn't continue, not often but it does."
// Correct -- and that trade now opens at 0.03 rather than 0.05, and is cut on the
// points, not refused at the door.
// REVERT: T_DeferChase = true.
input bool   T_DeferChase          = false; // (v18.51) enter at the signal   // (v17.54) BACK ON, and this is the answer to "we bought the top of
                                          // the wick so we start in loss." With T_DeferChase AND T_ExtensionSkip
                                          // both false, an extended flip was taken AT MARKET with no distance
                                          // limit at all — T_MaxExtensionAtr was dead code. Deferring is NOT the
                                          // "every signal delayed" behaviour he rejected in v16.83: nothing is
                                          // skipped, the flip is parked and taken the moment price comes back
                                          // toward the line, or at market once T_DeferMaxBars expires. Worst
                                          // case is a 2-bar wait on precisely the entries that were starting
                                          // in drawdown.
                                            // Deferring waits up to T_DeferMaxBars for a pullback before
                                            // firing, which is a BETTER average price but is still a delay —
                                            // and the log spam he saw made that delay look like the EA doing
                                            // nothing. OFF means the extended flip is taken at MARKET the
                                            // instant it confirms, same as every other flip, no waiting.
                                            // Trade-off, stated once: entries taken deep into an extension
                                            // pay more of the move away before the trade opens. The ratchet,
                                            // giveback guard and runner lock still manage it exactly the same
                                            // once it's open.
input bool   T_DeferChaseOLD      = true; // (v16.63 BEST PLACE TO GO IN) The chase guard used to SKIP a flip
                                         // outright when price had already run past the signal line — the
                                         // trend was simply lost. Now the flip is REMEMBERED: if price pulls
                                         // back toward the line within a few bars the entry is taken there
                                         // (a better price than the flip itself), and if it never does, the
                                         // trade is taken at market anyway so the trend is never missed.
                                         // Strictly better than the old behaviour: worst case is a short
                                         // delay, instead of no trade at all.
input int    T_DeferOpenSecs      = 12;  // (v17.82) an expired defer fills only within this many seconds of a new bar open
// (v17.95, his instruction: "the second Pine shows a signal we need to take")
// 2 -> 1. A parked flip now waits at most ONE bar for a better fill, then takes
// the next bar open. On M1 that caps the worst-case delay at ~60s instead of
// ~120s. The deferral still exists because the alternative he rejected earlier was
// filling at spike tips -- but one bar is the shortest wait that can still improve
// the price, so this is the fastest setting that is not simply "chase every fill".
// (v18.25) Deadline for a parked flip. Past this many bars it is taken at market
// regardless of the bar-open window. See the note in DeferReady().
// (v18.27) Maximum extension, in ATR, at which a deadline-expired park may fill
// at market. Beyond this the park is HELD, not filled, and not lost.
// ══════ (v18.37) THE MEASURED CAUSE, from his 13 Aug tickets ══════
// 18 trades, 0.05 lots. 5 winners, 13 losers. Hit rate 28%.
//   avg win  1.40 pts     avg loss  2.46 pts
//   wins total +6.98 pts  losses total -31.99 pts   NET about -25 points
// The decisive detail: THIRTEEN OF EIGHTEEN TRADES NEVER REACHED +1 POINT.
// That is not an exit problem and no hold rule can fix it. Those trades were
// filled at a price the market immediately moved away from. It matches the ANCHOR
// measurements exactly (fills 1.9-2.4 x ATR past the signal line): entering after
// the candle has run means the ordinary retrace IS the 2-4 point loss, every time.
//
// The two things I have tried both failed, and they failed in opposite directions:
//   v18.27 refused extended fills  -> FIVE HOURS, ZERO TRADES
//   v18.28 took them at 40% size   -> the -25 point session above
// So a market fill at an extended price has no edge, and refusing outright takes
// no trades. The answer is neither: WAIT for the retrace, but wait for as long as
// the flip stays valid instead of two bars.
//   T_EntryMaxAtr 1.20 -> 1.60 : accept a normally-extended fill, refuse a spike.
//   T_DeferHardBars 2 -> 999   : the park no longer converts to a market fill at
//     the extreme. It stays live and the re-arm keeps re-offering it, so it fills
//     on the pullback or not at all. A signal that never retraces produces no
//     trade AND no loss, which is strictly better than a 2-4 point loss.
// This is the one lever the ticket arithmetic actually points at.
// TO REVERT: T_DeferHardBars = 2.
// (v18.38) 1.60 -> 99: never treat a fill as too extended again. With the band at
// 1.2 x ATR the extension problem is fixed at source, so nothing needs sizing down
// or holding back, and every Pine signal is taken at market on the spot.
input double T_EntryMaxAtr         = 99.0;   // beyond this an entry is "extended" — sized down, never skipped
input double T_ExtendedLotFrac     = 0.40;   // size multiplier for an extended fill (1.0 = no reduction)
double       g_entryExtScale       = 1.0;
input int    T_DeferHardBars       = 1;     // (v18.38) fill promptly — no more waiting for retraces   // (v18.37) no market fill at the extreme — see the note above
input int    T_DeferMaxBars        = 1;   // (v17.54) 3 -> 2. On M1 three bars is three minutes; by then the leg
                                         // has usually moved on and "take it at market" fills WORSE than the
                                         // entry we were avoiding. Two bars, then take it or the move is gone.
input bool   T_EarlyFlip          = true; // (v16.75 CATCH THE SIGNAL AT BIRTH — his repeated request)
                                         // Supertrend confirms on the CLOSE of the flipping bar, so on M1 the
                                         // entry is up to 60 seconds after the turn. On a 14-point leg the
                                         // first bar alone is often 2-4 points, and that is the difference
                                         // between his manual fills and the EA's.
                                         // This is NOT prediction. ScalpST already gives the Supertrend LEVEL
                                         // from the last closed bar; if live price has ALREADY crossed that
                                         // level, the flip is locked in unless price reverses before the bar
                                         // closes. So the trade is taken the moment the level is crossed
                                         // rather than waiting out the remainder of the bar.
                                         // BOUNDED RISK, stated honestly: if the bar closes back on the wrong
                                         // side, you hold a position whose signal never confirmed. Cost is one
                                         // early-failure cut, ~1 point, and his two live losses (-1.23, -1.50)
                                         // are the empirical size of that. It is also the ONLY change in this
                                         // file that acts on an unclosed bar, which makes it the one most
                                         // likely to behave unexpectedly. Set false to return to
                                         // close-confirmed entries only.
input double T_EarlyFlipAtr       = 0.15; // price must clear the line by this x ATR — stops a one-tick brush
                                         // against the level counting as a flip
input int    T_TwoPoleMode        = 1;    // (v17.98) 0 = v17.97 behaviour (REQUIRE the oscillator to have
                                          // already turned -- structurally late). 1 = veto only when it is
                                          // moving against the flip. Set 0 to revert.
input double T_TwoPoleVetoSlope   = 0.02; // how hard it must be moving against to count as evidence
input bool   T_EarlyFlipTwoPole   = true; // also require the Two-Pole Oscillator to agree with the new
                                         // direction (his suggestion, and it is the right one — Two-Pole
                                         // LEADS, so it is the natural confirmation for an early entry)
input bool   T_NeverSkipM5        = true; // (v16.72) HIS SPEC: "no 5min signal should be skipped at all".
                                         // M5 legs on his 28 Jul chart ran 10-25pts in chop and 50-68pts on a
                                         // real trend (68pts = GBP50.86 at 0.01), and there are only ~18 M5
                                         // signals in a day — so a single refused M5 signal costs more than
                                         // a whole session of refused M1 ones. With this on, M5 bypasses the
                                         // three SOFT gates: spread-vs-move, cost-aware-minimum-move, and the
                                         // chaos/stop-hunt wick check. It does NOT bypass the lot ceiling,
                                         // exposure cap or daily halt — those are accounting, and skipping
                                         // them would mean opening a position the account cannot carry.
                                         // M1 and M3 keep all three gates, which is the "only the insanely
                                         // bad ones" line he drew.
input bool   T_SmartClose         = true; // (v16.71) see the SMART CLOSE block in ManagePosition. Wide giveback
                                         // while the leg makes new extremes, tight once it stops. This is the
                                         // "close when smart" behaviour without a fixed goal.
input double T_SmartCloseTightPct = 12.0; // giveback allowance once the leg has gone quiet
input int    T_SmartCloseMinBars  = 8;   // (v16.76) the smart close cannot arm before this many bars in the
                                         // trade. A trade that has only just opened has not "gone quiet" —
                                         // it has not started. // giveback allowance once the leg has gone quiet
input bool   T_M1QuickHold        = true;  // (v16.88) HIS DESIGN, M1 ONLY: open bigger, bank most of it fast
                                          // on proven profit, let a small remainder ride. Fires at 0.05
                                          // total instead of the normal tier size; after T_M1QuickBars
                                          // candles, if in genuine profit, PARTIAL-CLOSES the banked share
                                          // (60%) home and leaves the rest (40%) running under every
                                          // existing rule (ratchet, gated giveback, runner lock, chandelier,
                                          // structure cut, opposite flip) completely unchanged.
                                          // IMPLEMENTATION NOTE: this reuses g_lastTierSizeReq, the EXISTING
                                          // confluence-tier sizing override (line ~2987) — not new sizing
                                          // logic. The exit uses PositionClosePartial on the SAME ticket, so
                                          // on a hedging account there is no second ticket to register,
                                          // no comment-tag parsing, and the remainder inherits its
                                          // management from the ordinary flow with zero new code there.
                                          // M1 ONLY, per his request — M3/M5 keep their existing behaviour
                                          // (base 0.01 + scale-add 0.02), and the two mechanisms are made
                                          // mutually exclusive on M1 below so a signal can't get both.
input bool   T_StepScaling         = true; // (v17.10) HIS SPEC: 0.03 at a GBP120 account, then +0.01 for every
                                          // GBP80 gained. That is a STEP function, not the proportional
                                          // per-GBP100 formula that was here — proportional scaling from
                                          // GBP120 would have reached 0.06 by GBP200, roughly double what he
                                          // asked for. A step is also safer as the account grows: risk per
                                          // trade as a PERCENTAGE falls with each step instead of staying flat.
                                          //   GBP120 -> 0.03 | GBP200 -> 0.04 | GBP280 -> 0.05 | GBP360 -> 0.06
                                          // Applied to ALL clocks so M1/M3/M5 stay consistent, and capped by
                                          // T_M1QuickMaxLots as a hard ceiling.
// (v18.21) 0.03 -> 0.05, his structure: "enter with 0.05, close our 0.01 early in
// profit, and the 0.04 continues closing with partials slowly off."
// So the position is now 1 quick-bank lot + 4 runner lots, and T_PartialStep
// (0.01) sheds the rest one step at a time.
// (v18.35) balance tiers — see the note in LotFor(). T_BalanceTiers = false uses
// T_FixedLots directly, as before.
input bool   T_BalanceTiers        = true;
input double T_TierBal1            = 100.0;
input double T_TierLots1           = 0.03;
input double T_TierBal2            = 120.0;
input double T_TierLots2           = 0.04;   // (v18.40) his call: 0.04 for now   // (v18.38) 0.04 at 120+   // (v18.37) 0.03 across the board for now
input double T_TierBal3            = 150.0;
input double T_TierLots3           = 0.04;   // (v18.40) 0.04 across the tiers for now   // (v18.38) his call: 0.05 at 150+   // (v18.37) his call: everything down to 0.03 while this is unproven
// (v18.47) 0.04 -> 0.03. HIS INSTRUCTION: every Pine signal taken, 0.03 minimum,
// 0.05 maximum. With T_StrongBirthLots = 0.05 already live, this input becomes the
// FLOOR and the strong-birth path becomes the CEILING, so the size range is now
// exactly the one he specified: 0.03 base, 0.05 on a strong birth off the extreme.
// ═══════════════ (v18.58) THE SIDEWAYS-RANGE SIZE TRIM. BUG FIX. ═══════════════
// WHAT WAS WRONG. LotFor() returns inside the T_FixedLots branch at the top of the
// function. Both downward trims -- DEAD-CHOP SIZE (x0.45) and RANGE REGIME SIZE
// (x0.60) -- live BELOW that return, so with T_FixedLots > 0 (always, since v18.04)
// neither has ever executed. v17.89's comment claims it fixed T_RegimeRangeSizeX
// being dead "applied directly here"; it was placed below the return, so that fix
// was dead on arrival too. §2's whole plan -- "the lever is SIZE, not refusal" --
// was never connected to anything.
// v18.04 put the return first so no later multiplier could RE-INFLATE size. That
// intent is correct and is preserved exactly: the trim below is MathMin only, so it
// can never raise size, only lower it. Implementation now matches intent.
//
// WHY A FIXED POINT BAND AND NOT THE EXISTING ATR ONE. SignalsClustered() bands the
// signal spread by T_BandAtrMult x ATR(10) and that self-defeats in both regimes:
//   chop   : ATR ~1.0 -> band 1.5 pts. Its own founding evidence was two signals
//            2.5 pts apart, so it cannot fire on the case it was written for.
//   trend  : ATR expands to ~2.5-3.0 -> band becomes 20+ pts, which is wider than
//            the 18-20 pt signal spacing measured on the 18 Aug 4405->4352 leg,
//            so it would call THE TREND a range. Threshold moves the wrong way
//            in both directions. Points, per the standing rule.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC, from his two live sets:
//   18 Aug chop hour, ~15 flips in a ~6 pt band -> 3 consecutive signals span 2-5 pts.
//   18 Aug down leg, 53 pts on six sells       -> 3 consecutive signals span 18-20 pts.
//   6.0 pts sits above the chop band and 3x below the trend. Clean separation.
//   Effect on the chop hour: ~15 trades x ~-2.4 pts = -36 pts. At 0.03 (GBP2.22/pt)
//   that is about -GBP80; at 0.01 (GBP0.75/pt) about -GBP27.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS AND CANNOT FIX. It needs T_BandLookback signals, so the FIRST 2-3
// trades of every range are still paid at full size -- roughly -GBP16 of that hour
// is unreachable by this change. It observes, it never forecasts.
// REVERT: set T_RangeClusterTrim = false.
input bool   T_RangeClusterTrim     = true;  // (v18.58) off = exactly v18.57 behaviour
input int    T_RangeClusterSigs      = 5;    // (v18.60b) signals in the window. Ratio floor is 1/(n-1), so 3 is too few
input double T_RangeClusterRatio     = 0.60;  // (v18.60) scale-free: signal spread / path travelled. 0 = use the fixed point band below
// (v19.08 BX) UN-SCALED, reversing my own v19.05 change. This is NOT a volatility
// threshold -- it asks "how spread out were my last N entries", which is a
// statement about MY behaviour, not the market's. Scaling it DOWN on a quiet day
// makes it FIRE LESS, which is backwards: his 26 Aug 04:00 hour took 34 trades
// across an 8-point range and lost GBP78.86. A 6-point band catches that cluster.
// The 2-4 point band my scaling produced does not. Same reasoning that kept
// T_DayBiasMinPts and T_PremiumRunPts off the scaler -- I applied it there and
// missed it here.
input double T_RangeClusterBandPts  = 6.0;   // (v18.58) last T_BandLookback signals inside this many POINTS = range
input double T_RangeClusterLots     = 0.02;  // (v18.62) HIS STANDING RULE WINS: 0.02 min / 0.05 max. He approved
                                             // 0.01 mid-thread, then restated the 0.02 floor after seeing 0.01x2
                                             // open live. The standing rule is the authority. Chop containment is
                                             // therefore 0.05 -> 0.02 (-60%), not 0.05 -> 0.01 (-80%).
input double T_FixedLots           = 0.03;   // (v18.40) 0.04   // (v18.38)   // (v18.37)  // (v18.11) 0.03 tonight, 0.04 in the morning -- his call  // (v18.04) >0 = EVERY entry is exactly this size. Overrides the
                                           // ladder, the tiers, and every confidence/chop multiplier.
                                           // WHY: his log read "AT RISK NOW 41.78 of 37.48 allowed" and
                                           // "TREND BLOCKED - MULTI-SYMBOL RISK CAP 29.4% (cap 12.0%)" --
                                           // the ladder had grown lots to 0.08 and the risk accountant was
                                           // then refusing signals. Pin the lot and the budget stops binding.
                                           // 0 = ladder behaviour (v18.03).
input double T_StepBaseLots        = 0.03; // lots at (and below) the base equity
input double T_StepBaseEquity      = 100.0;   // (v17.13) 120 -> 100 at his request: 0.03 from a
                                             // GBP100 start. Margin check below is what makes this
                                             // safe to do — see T_MarginFloorPct.// the equity that gets T_StepBaseLots
input double T_StepEvery           = 80.0; // add T_StepAddLots for every this much ABOVE the base
input double T_StepAddLots         = 0.01;
input double T_M1QuickPer100      = 0.03;  // legacy proportional path, used only when T_StepScaling = false  // (v16.89) AUTO-SCALING, replaces the three fixed lot numbers.
                                          // Lots per GBP100 of live equity. 0.01 gives him his stated targets
                                          // exactly: GBP500 -> 0.05 total, and GBP100 -> 0.01.
                                          // WHY A SCALER AND NOT FIXED 0.05: at 1:500, 0.05 lots of XAUUSD
                                          // needs GBP40.45 MARGIN. On a GBP15 account the broker simply
                                          // REJECTS the order — it is not a risk preference, the position
                                          // cannot be opened. And a 6pt M1 stop on 0.05 is GBP22.44 = 150% of
                                          // a GBP15 account. So "adapt to any balance above GBP15" can only
                                          // mean scaling the SIZE with the balance, which is what this does.
                                          // It scales up as well as down — GBP1000 gives 0.10 with no retune.
input bool   T_QuickBankOneStep   = true;   // (v18.21) the early bank is exactly T_PartialStep, never a fraction
input double T_M1QuickBankFrac    = 0.25;  // (v18.04) WAS 0.60 -- it banked 57% of the position two minutes
                                           // in. At a pinned 0.04 total, 0.25 = 0.01 per bank, so size comes
                                           // off one step at a time and the runner keeps three quarters.  // (v16.89) fraction banked home; 0.60 of 0.05 = 0.03 banked /
                                          // 0.02 held, exactly his split, and it holds that ratio at ANY
                                          // balance. Both sides are rounded to the broker lot step, and the
                                          // held side is guaranteed at least one minimum lot.
input double T_M1QuickMaxLots     = 0.20;  // (v17.10) 0.06 -> 0.20 hard ceiling so the step ladder has room to
                                           // climb as the account grows, without ever becoming unbounded.  // (v16.96) his two stated targets are 0.06 on M1 NOW (equity
                                           // ~GBP340) and 0.03 on a GBP100 account. Those are not on the same
                                           // straight line through the origin, so: per100 0.03 gives
                                           // GBP100 -> 0.03 exactly, and this CAP holds M1 at 0.06 rather
                                           // than letting GBP340 scale to 0.10. Both targets met.  // hard ceiling so a large balance cannot scale into absurd size
input int    T_StAtrLen           = 7;    // (v17.05) THE SECOND EARLINESS LEVER, and it was HARDCODED at 10
                                         // in THREE places (iATR(_Symbol, tf, T_StAtrLen)) where nobody could reach it.
                                         // Supertrend's line sits at mult x ATR(len). The MULTIPLIER is a flat
                                         // scale, but the ATR LENGTH decides WHICH volatility it measures — and
                                         // that is what makes this the lever that matters most in CHOP.
                                         // In a choppy stretch recent bars are SMALLER than the 10-bar average,
                                         // so ATR(10) over-states current volatility and parks the line too far
                                         // away. ATR(7) tracks the contraction, the line comes closer, and the
                                         // flip fires earlier — exactly where his choppy legs are being lost.
                                         // Worked example, chop where ATR(10)=2.0 but ATR(7)=1.5, mult 2.0,
                                         // on an 8pt leg:
                                         //   ATR(10): line 4.0pts out -> captures 4.0 of 8 = 50%
                                         //   ATR(7) : line 3.0pts out -> captures 5.0 of 8 = 62%
                                         // In a TREND recent ATR is larger, so ATR(7) widens the line slightly
                                         // and gives trends a touch more room — which is the right direction
                                         // for both cases from one change.
                                         // COST: a shorter ATR is noisier, so the line moves around more and
                                         // whipsaw count rises. 7 is deliberately a middle step from 10, not
                                         // the 5 that would be aggressive. Mirror in the Pine (stLen).
// (v18.06) 2.0 -> 1.6. At 2.0 the band sits ~3.9 pts off price on M1, and the
// early-flip path only fires once price CROSSES that line -- so "catch it at
// birth" could not fire until ~4 pts of the move was already gone. Revert to 2.0
// if signals-per-hour rises sharply (that would mean a looser trigger, not just
// a nearer anchor).
// (v18.10) BACK TO 2.0. I lowered this to 1.6 on a theory that entries were
// arriving late. His own v18.06 path-audit log falsified that theory the first
// time it ran: "0 confirmed / 2 early" -- every entry was already firing at
// birth, so there was no lateness for 1.6 to cure. A change whose justification
// has been disproved does not get to stay, and this one has a second cost: he is
// taking these signals BY HAND off the Pine, and the Pine is 2.0. Two different
// multipliers means the EA and the chart disagree about where a signal is. They
// must be the same number.
// (v18.12) HIS EVIDENCE vs MINE. He reports 18.09 backtested BETTER than 18.11,
// and the only signal-affecting difference between those builds is this number:
// 18.09 ran 1.6, 18.11 runs 2.0. That is a point for 1.6. Against it: his own
// evidence hierarchy rates backtest statistics as corrupted and not to be used,
// and he takes these signals BY HAND off a Pine chart that draws 2.0. I am not
// picking a side quietly. Left at 2.0 so the EA and the chart agree; to test the
// other way set THIS and the Pine "Supertrend multiplier" to 1.6 together.
// ══════ (v18.38) THE ANSWER: IT IS THE PEAK, AND THIS NUMBER CAUSES IT ══════
// It is NOT late signals. He confirmed himself that the flip prints 1-2 candles
// before Supertrend, and the path audit showed every fill arriving via the EARLY
// path. The signal is on time. The FILL PRICE is the problem, and the geometry is
// forced by this input:
//
//   A Supertrend flip is DETECTED when price crosses the band.
//   At multiplier 2.0 the band sits ~2 x ATR away from price.
//   So at the instant the signal exists, price has ALREADY travelled ~2 x ATR.
//
// That is not lateness that can be optimised away -- it is arithmetic. The ANCHOR
// log measured exactly this: fills at 1.93, 1.97, 2.06, 2.40 x ATR past the line.
// The "extension" I have been trying to filter, size down, and wait out for six
// versions is not a fill defect. It is where the multiplier PUTS the signal.
//
// Which means every attempt to fix it downstream was doomed:
//   refuse extended fills  -> refuses the signal itself -> zero trades (v18.27)
//   take them at 40% size  -> still buys 2 ATR into the move (v18.28)
//   wait for a pullback    -> waits for something the geometry does not promise
// The only place to fix it is here. At 1.2 the band sits ~1.2 x ATR out, so the
// crossing -- and therefore the fill -- happens far nearer the actual turn.
//
// HIS OWN SECOND DATA POINT: he reported v18.09 backtesting BETTER than v18.11,
// and the only signal-affecting difference between those builds was this number
// (18.09 ran 1.6, 18.11 ran 2.0). Both pieces of evidence point the same way.
//
// COST, stated honestly: a nearer band flips more often, so expect MORE signals
// and more whipsaw flips. That is the trade being made -- worse signal purity for
// materially better fill prices. If signals-per-hour more than doubles and the
// small losses multiply, 1.6 is the middle setting.
// THE PINE MUST MATCH: set "Supertrend multiplier" to 1.2 in CLEAN 1.4 or the
// chart he trades by hand will disagree with the EA about where a signal is.
// (v18.42) 1.2 -> 1.6. 1.2 gave better fill prices AND a flip every couple of
// candles, which is what let the loop above form. 1.6 is the middle setting I
// flagged when the trade-off was first made: band ~1.6 x ATR instead of 2.0, so
// still materially better fills than the original, without the flip storm.
// THE PINE MUST MATCH: set "Supertrend multiplier" to 1.6 in CLEAN.
// ═════════════ (v18.45) 1.6 -> 1.2. THE PINE IS THE MASTER. ═════════════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE. Reverted on his direct visual evidence, and
// this was MY mistake to correct: last session I moved the Pine from 1.2 up to
// 1.6 to match this line, on the strength of a code comment. He then put CLEAN
// 1.5 (1.2) and CLEAN 2.1 (1.6) in two panes on the same M1 chart and the 1.2
// pane printed visibly more flips and printed them AT THE BIRTH of each leg,
// which is the whole thing he has been asking for. I changed the master to match
// the copy. Wrong direction: the standing rule is that the EA takes every signal
// the Pine shows, so the Pine sets this number and the EA follows.
// GEOMETRY, not lag: the band sits stMult x ATR from price, so a flip cannot
// print until that much of the move is already gone. At 1.6 x ~1.0 ATR on M1
// that is ~1.6 pts of a 3 pt target surrendered before entry exists. At 1.2 it
// is ~1.2 pts. That difference IS the "entering a few candles late" complaint.
// THE OLD JUSTIFICATION WAS A MISATTRIBUTION. The comment above 1.6 read "1.2
// flipped too often and produced a churn loop in the EA." It did produce a churn
// loop -- but the loop was the EXECUTION layer: every fresh flip was cut at
// T_ReversalCutFrac x T_MaxLossPts = 1.20 pts inside a ~1.0 pt ATR, and every
// winner was released at T_MinWinnerExitPts = 3.00. More signals into that
// machine means more 1.2 pt losses. The signal was blamed for the exit stack's
// behaviour. Widening the band hid the churn by refusing trades, which is exactly
// what he has forbidden: bad prices are handled by SIZE, bad trades by an early
// points cut, never by declining the trade.
// COUPLING -- READ THIS BEFORE RUNNING IT: shipping 1.2 on its own predictably
// makes the day WORSE, because it raises entry frequency into a cut that fires at
// 1.2 ATR. That is why the T_ReversalCutFrac edit below ships in the same build.
// These two are one change, not two: an entry-rate increase and the loss-side
// clearance it requires. Do not run 1.2 with the cut at 0.40.
// REVERT: T_StMultiplier = 1.6 (and set the Pine back to 1.6 in the same move).
input double T_StMultiplier       = 1.2;   // (v18.45) 1.6 -> 1.2, see above   // (v16.90) THIS IS WHY ENTRIES ARE "A FEW CANDLES LATE", and it was
                                           // HARDCODED at 3.0 inside ScalpST() where no one could reach it.
                                           // Supertrend(10, mult) places its line mult x ATR away from price.
                                           // On M1 with ATR ~2pts, 3.0 puts the line ~6pts from price — so a
                                           // flip CANNOT fire until price has already travelled ~6pts from
                                           // the turn. That is geometry, not lag in the code: no amount of
                                           // early-flip logic can catch a turn the indicator has not signalled
                                           // yet, because T_EarlyFlip only fires once price crosses the LINE.
                                           // On his 29 Jul M1 chart the 07:00 low was 4011 and the BUY printed
                                           // near 4014-4017 — that gap IS this multiplier.
                                           // 2.0 puts the line ~4pts out: flips roughly 2pts earlier, which on
                                           // a 26pt leg is ~8% more captured, and on a 6pt chop leg is the
                                           // difference between profit and scratch. THE TRADE-OFF IS REAL:
                                           // a closer line is crossed by noise too, so whipsaw count rises.
                                           // This is the single highest-leverage number for his "catch the
                                           // real birth" goal, and it is now testable instead of buried.
                                           // MUST be mirrored in the Pine (stMult, line 122) or the chart
                                           // stops matching the EA.  // hard ceiling so a large balance cannot scale into absurd size
input int    T_M1QuickBars        = 2;     // bank after this many CLOSED M1 candles
input double T_M1QuickMinAtr      = 0.50;  // (v17.98) ATR-relative floor under the quick-bank trigger. M1: 0.98pts.
input double T_M1QuickMinPts      = 0.30;  // must be at least this many points in profit to count as
                                          // "a candlestick or two of profit" — above the ~0.25pt spread so
                                          // it can't fire on a scratch
input bool   T_GateGivebackOnExtend = true; // (v16.87) see the GATE THE GIVEBACK GUARD note at its call site.
input bool   T_LegStillExtending  = true; // (v16.61 RUNNER LOCK — a SECOND, INDEPENDENT guard)
                                         // Every discretionary profit-taker in this EA (range-scalp,
                                         // chop-stall, exhaustion, spike-peak, leg-target) stands down on
                                         // ONE flag: healthyTrend, which needs the REGIME ENGINE to read
                                         // "trending". That is a single point of failure protecting the
                                         // whole runner upside — and runners are where the money is (his
                                         // Gold Hunter ledger: 2 of 53 trades produced 51% of gross profit).
                                         // Worked example, his 27 Jul 17:00 M1 SELL: entry ~4101.5, ran to
                                         // 4087.4 = +14.1pts. Median leg that session was ~8.25pts, so the
                                         // leg target would have banked it at ~7.2pts — HALF the move —
                                         // with only healthyTrend standing in the way, while the panel read
                                         // "bigger picture mixed (-20)".
                                         // This adds evidence that needs no regime call at all: if the trade
                                         // has made a NEW FAVOURABLE EXTREME in the last few bars, the leg
                                         // is demonstrably still alive, so the discretionary banks stand
                                         // down. The giveback guard, gap-against close, hard stop and
                                         // opposite flip are NOT affected — real protection still works.
input bool   T_LegExtendNowOnly    = true; // (v18.59) runner lock reads the LAST closed bar only, not any of 6. false = pre-v18.59
input int    T_ExtendLookback     = 6;   // (v16.69) 3 -> 6 bars. Bar-counting already scales with the clock
                                        // (6 bars = 6 min on M1, 30 min on M5), but 3 was too tight for a
                                        // mid-trend pause: an M5 downtrend that shelves for 20 minutes released
                                        // the runner lock and every discretionary bank went live inside a leg
                                        // that had 40 more points to give.
input bool   T_MaxStopCash        = true;  // (v16.55) BOUND THE STOP IN CASH, NOT IN ATR.
                                         // There was NO maximum stop distance anywhere in this file. The
                                         // SL is placed at the Supertrend line plus buffers, and ST(10,3)
                                         // sits 3 x ATR(10) out — so the stop distance is whatever ATR
                                         // happens to be. At M1 ATR 2.0 that is ~6pts (GBP4.50, 1.2% of
                                         // GBP365). After a spike, ATR 7.0 gives ~21pts: GBP15.75, 4.3% of
                                         // the account on ONE M1 trade. Capping in ATR terms does not help,
                                         // because ATR is the thing that spiked. The cap has to be in CASH.
                                         // NOTE: when this bites, the stop is no longer at the signal line.
                                         // That is a deliberate trade-off — a tighter stop is hit more
                                         // often, but an uncapped one can cost a multiple of plan.
input double T_MaxStopRiskPct     = 8.00; // (v16.62) WAS 2.00, and 2.00 was WRONG — it fought the strategy
                                         // instead of protecting it. At GBP150 a 2% budget is GBP3.00 = a
                                         // 4-POINT stop, but the natural signal-line stops are 6 / 9 / 12
                                         // points on M1 / M3 / M5. So the cap was pulling every stop inside
                                         // normal noise and would have caused constant stop-outs on all
                                         // three clocks. Now aligned with the file's existing
                                         // SmallAcctMaxRisk of 8%, which the natural stops already sit under
                                         // at GBP150 (3.0% / 4.5% / 6.0%). At GBP150 the cap is a 16-point
                                         // stop; at GBP500 it is 53 points. It therefore NEVER bites in
                                         // normal operation at any balance from GBP150 up, and scales by
                                         // itself with equity — no retuning at any account size.
                                         // Its only remaining job is the ANOMALY: when ATR spikes to ~7 the
                                         // signal line sits ~21 points out, and that single trade would cost
                                         // GBP15.75 (10.5% of GBP150). That is the case it catches, and
                                         // nothing else. Set 0 to disable entirely.
                                         // never place a stop further out than this % of equity, at the
                                         // lot size actually being used
input bool   T_GapGuard          = true;  // (v16.54) GAP GUARD — the only path in this EA that can lose a
                                         // MULTIPLE of its intended risk. Found on his 27 Jul 02:00-05:00
                                         // M1 chart: a SELL at ~4055 met a ~27pt discontinuity and price
                                         // resumed at ~4084 with NO candles in between. A stop loss does
                                         // not protect against that — it becomes a market order at the
                                         // first available price, so a 6pt stop filled ~27pts away:
                                         // -GBP20 instead of -GBP4.50, 5.5% of a GBP365 account in ONE fill.
                                         // Nothing in the file detected or handled this. Three responses:
                                         // (1) detect the gap, (2) suppress new entries while post-gap
                                         // price discovery is chaotic, (3) close anything the gap went
                                         // against immediately rather than hoping it comes back.
input double T_GapAtrMult         = 3.00; // bar open vs previous close beyond this x ATR(10) = a gap
input int    T_GapCoolBars        = 5;    // suppress new entries for this many bars after a gap
input bool   T_GapCloseAgainst    = true; // close a position the gap moved against, at once
input bool   T_FlatBeforeClose    = true; // (v16.54) flat before the weekly close so nothing is held
input int    T_FlatBeforeCloseMin = 15;   // minutes before Friday 22:00 to go flat
input bool   T_LegTarget          = false; // (v16.70) OFF — the most explicit fixed goal of all // (v16.53) SELF-CALIBRATING LEG TARGET. His 27 Jul 8-hour M1
                                         // chart shows realised leg sizes of 3-24pts, median ~11 — the
                                         // distance between CONSECUTIVE SIGNAL PRICES *is* the realised
                                         // leg, and v16.51 already stores those prices. So the system can
                                         // measure its own leg distribution live instead of using a fixed
                                         // R threshold guessed by me. Once a trade has taken a set
                                         // fraction of the recent median leg AND the trend is not healthy,
                                         // bank: waiting for the opposite flip on a median-sized leg means
                                         // handing back the lag at both ends. Healthy trends are exempt,
                                         // so a leg that wants to run 24pts still runs.
input double T_LegTargetFrac      = 0.75; // bank at this fraction of the recent median leg
input double T_LegNoisePts        = 1.50; // (v16.56 RUNNER PROTECTION — fixes a flaw in v16.53) Legs
                                         // SMALLER than this are excluded from the median. His Gold
                                         // Hunter ledger (53 trades, 16-27 Jul) shows why: ~25 of 53
                                         // trades earned under GBP0.30 each and contributed ~GBP1.50 in
                                         // total, while TWO runners (+34.63 and +32.24) produced 51% of
                                         // all gross profit. Micro-churn like that drags the median down
                                         // to ~3pts, so a 0.75 target would have banked the +34.63 trade
                                         // at about +2.25. The median must describe REAL legs, not noise.
input double T_LegTargetMinR      = 1.20; // and never target below this R regardless of the median — a
                                         // hard floor so no measured median can ever clip a runner early // bank at this fraction of the recent median leg
input int    T_LegMedianMin        = 4;   // need at least this many recorded legs before trusting it
input bool   T_SpikePeakBank      = true;  // (v18.77 AA) ON. "the ratchet covers blow-offs" is contradicted by this branch's own comment ("a trail cannot follow this") and by the arithmetic: a trail gives back the whole noise band. Revert = false. // (v16.52) SPIKE-PEAK BANK. Measured on his 27 Jul 06:00-10:00
                                         // M1 chart: extraction was 98% on the leg that rolled over
                                         // gradually (21.5pt peak, 0.5pt given back) but only 52% on the
                                         // leg that SPIKED (13.5pt peak, 6.5pt given back). The loss is
                                         // not a constant trail lag and it is not worse on small legs —
                                         // it is concentrated entirely in VERTICAL peaks, which no trail
                                         // can follow. v16.49's exhaustion bank fires on CONTRACTION
                                         // (range < 0.60 x ATR) and therefore misses this case exactly.
                                         // This is the mirror: an EXPANSION bar that closes well off its
                                         // own extreme is a blow-off, so bank it on the spot.
input double T_SpikeAtrMult       = 1.80; // bar range must exceed this x ATR(10) to count as a spike
input double T_SpikeCloseFrac     = 0.40; // and must close in the worst this fraction of its own range
input double T_SpikeMinR          = 0.80; // only once the leg has actually paid something
input bool   T_SignalBandRange   = true; // (v16.51) SIGNAL-CLUSTER RANGE DETECTOR — from his 27 Jul M1
                                         // chart. When consecutive OPPOSITE signals print at almost the
                                         // SAME PRICE (that chart: BUY 4094 then SELL 4096.5, 2.5pts
                                         // apart), riding flip-to-flip cannot pay: the exit is pinned
                                         // near the entry no matter how far price travelled in between.
                                         // Measured on that chart: peak 2.5-3.5pts, captured 1.0-2.5pts,
                                         // i.e. 29-60% of the peak handed back to the lag.
                                         // ADX/Choppiness do NOT catch this — they read BAR structure,
                                         // while this reads where the SIGNALS themselves land. A tight
                                         // cluster of signal prices IS the definition of a range for a
                                         // flip-to-flip system, so this forces the fast-bank path
                                         // (range-scalp / chop-stall / exhaustion) instead of riding.
input int    T_BandLookback      = 3;    // how many recent signal prices to measure the spread of
input double T_BandAtrMult       = 1.50; // cluster if that spread is under this x ATR(10) of the clock
input bool   T_ExhaustBank       = false; // (v16.70) OFF — a fixed goal at 2.0R // (v16.49) PEAK EXIT. Every existing profit-taker fires on a
                                         // RETRACE (giveback / chop-stall / range-scalp) or on a LEVEL
                                         // (chandelier). None fire on EXHAUSTION, so a leg that stops
                                         // expanding is held until it gives back instead of banked at
                                         // the peak. This is the "take profit at a peak before the
                                         // counter-trend" behaviour, and it needs no retrace to trigger.
input double T_ExhaustMinR       = 2.00; // only consider once the leg has genuinely paid
input double T_ExhaustRangeFrac  = 0.60; // last bar's range below this x ATR(10) = expansion has stopped
input bool   T_ScratchPlus     = true;
input double T_ScratchPlusR    = 0.50;   // arm the above-breakeven floor from this R
input double T_ScratchPlusAtr  = 0.10;   // how far above breakeven to sit, x ATR
// #4 Adaptive giveback by volatility: a fixed 22% giveback is too tight in a
//    fast trend (normal pullbacks are bigger) and too loose in a slow one.
//    Scale the giveback allowance with ATR percentile.
input bool   T_AdaptiveGiveback = true;
// #5 Re-entry quality gate: only re-enter on a reclaim if efficiency is still
//    decent — don't keep re-entering into deteriorating chop.
// (v18.14) 0.25 -> 0.10. Kaufman ER on M1 gold sits far below the value this
// gate assumed: his log printed 0.03. ER is net displacement / summed
// bar-to-bar travel, so on a 1-minute clock — where almost all travel is
// two-sided — a reading of 0.25 means an unusually clean leg, not a normal one.
// Setting a trend-timeframe threshold on a noise-timeframe measurement is the
// mis-scaled-threshold class of bug: the number was never wrong in the abstract,
// it was wrong for the clock it is applied to. 0.10 still refuses genuinely dead
// tape while allowing the ordinary resumption this mechanism exists to catch.
input double T_ReentryMinER    = 0.10;  // (v16.21) cooled slightly 0.20->0.25 — a cleaner resumption required
                                          // before rejoining; still on, still catches genuine continuations
// #6 Cost-aware minimum move: never take a trade whose signal-line stop is so
//    tight the spread+commission alone is a big fraction of 1R.
input bool   T_CostAwareR      = true;
input double T_MaxCostPctR     = 0.30;    // skip if round-trip cost > 30% of 1R
input bool   T_CostSkipAtFloor = false;  // (v16.93) when the cost trim cannot bite (size already at the
                                         // broker minimum, i.e. every M3/M5 trade), FALSE = take it anyway.
                                         // TRUE restores the old skip.
input bool   T_ExtensionSkip   = false;  // (v16.93) FALSE = an extended flip is TAKEN AT MARKET rather than
                                         // refused. TRUE restores the old chasing-guard skip. See the
                                         // EXTENDED ENTRY note at its call site — this is the gate my
                                         // T_DeferChase=false change accidentally left switched ON.    // skip if round-trip cost > 30% of 1R
// #7 Friday-close flatten: don't carry positions into the weekend gap.
input bool   T_FridayFlat      = true;
input int    T_FridayFlatHour  = 20;      // GMT hour Friday to stop opening + flatten
// #8 Trend-maturity throttle: the later a trend is (bars since flip), the
//    smaller a fresh entry — late entries into old trends are the worst R.
input bool   T_MaturityThrottle = true;
input int    T_TrendYoungBars  = 10;      // full size within this many bars of the flip
// ═══ (v16.22) REGIME ENGINE — the master switch (research-backed) ═══
// Every credible gold-Supertrend source says the biggest single edge is
// detecting TRENDING vs RANGING and behaving differently. In a TREND the
// birth flip is a 90%+ winner to ride and hold; in a RANGE it's a near
// coin-flip that just pays spread. This does NOT block signals — it
// changes how each one is HANDLED (size, hold vs bank-fast). ADX reads
// directional strength; Choppiness Index reads range-boundedness; both
// together catch what either misses alone.
input bool   T_RegimeEngine    = true;
input int    T_ChopLen         = 14;    // Choppiness Index lookback
input double T_RegimeAdxTrend  = 25.0;  // ADX at/above this = trending (the universal gold threshold)
input double T_RegimeChopTrend = 45.0;  // Choppiness at/below this = trending
input double T_RegimeAdxRange  = 20.0;  // ADX below this = ranging
input double T_RegimeChopRange = 61.8;  // Choppiness at/above this = ranging (Dreiss' classic 61.8 line)
input double T_RegimeTrendSizeX = 1.25; // size multiplier for a birth flip in a confirmed TREND (ride harder)
input double T_RegimeRangeSizeX = 0.60; // size multiplier in a RANGE (still taken — smaller — banked fast by the range-scalp exit)
// ══════ (v18.54) 99.0 -> 0.80. THE SIDEWAYS DEMA RANGES. ══════
// CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX (a complete mechanism disabled by an out-of-reach number)
// + STRATEGY CHANGE (the number itself).
// HIS REPORT: "for sideways near dema that is common on some days so we must
// perform well on it ... theres like 15 sideways a day and easily 4-5 pound profit
// there if collected properly ... we close in loss holding out hoping for it to go
// back down but sideways ranges won't perform like that."
// He is right, and the code agrees with him: this EA already contains a complete
// range-scalp exit, correctly scoped, and it has been switched off by a number
// rather than by a flag. It was set to 99.0 in v16.70 with the comment "raised out
// of reach, same effect as off." Nothing was ever wrong with the logic.
// WHAT IT DOES, AND WHY IT IS SAFE: it fires ONLY on a trade whose ENTRY regime was
// measured as a range (g_trEntryRegime[trK] < 0), and the block immediately above it
// PROMOTES the trade to trend and releases this cap the moment RegimeOf() says the
// range has resolved into a trend. So a range-born trade that turns into a real leg
// is never clipped by this -- it is handed to the normal runner stack. This can only
// ever bank a trade that is still, measurably, in chop.
// ARITHMETIC: 0.80R with RangeScalpClockMult(M1) = 1.00 and R ~ 2.8 pts gives a bank
// at ~2.24 pts. An M1 gold range near the DEMA is typically 3-5 pts wide, so 2.24 is
// roughly 50-75% of the traversal -- which is exactly the "claim 75% of a sideways
// trend" he asked for. M3/M5/M15 keep their 1.75x/4.00x/5.00x multipliers, so slow
// clocks are untouched and their runners are not amputated.
// SECOND HALF OF THIS FIX -- WITHOUT IT THE NUMBER DOES NOTHING: RANGE-SCALP is not
// on the `truly` list in the T_MinWinnerExitPts guard, and that floor is now 6.00
// pts. A 2.24 pt range bank is below it, so the floor would veto every one. It is
// exempted there. The justification is the same as GIVEBACK's: the floor exists to
// stop an OPINION clipping a TREND winner early, and a range-born trade has no trend
// to clip -- the floor's premise does not apply to it, and the regime test is the
// evidence rather than an opinion.
// HONEST COST: a range that was about to break out in our favour now banks at ~2.24
// instead of riding. The promote-to-trend block above is what limits that, and it
// depends on RegimeOf() calling the resolution quickly enough -- which is UNKNOWN
// and is the thing to watch in the log. If [RANGE-SCALP BANK] lines appear right
// before big legs, raise this to 1.50 rather than switching it off.
// REVERT: T_RangeScalpR = 99.0.
input double T_RangeScalpR       = 0.80; // (v16.70) was 0.8 — raised out of reach, same effect as off  // range-born trade banks at this R (small, fast) unless the range resolves into a trend
input bool   T_VolBirthSizing    = true; // (v16.22 #9) size a fresh flip up on a volume surge, down on dead volume — never blocks
input bool   T_RegimeLearn       = true; // (v16.22 #10) EA leans size toward whichever regime is actually paying this session
input int    T_RegimeLearnMinN   = 6;    // trades needed in a regime before the self-monitoring lean acts on it
// ═══ (v16.24) 10-FEATURE PROFITABILITY PACK — all SIZE, none block ═══
input bool   T_HtfBackdrop     = true;   // #1 size up when M15+H1 regime backs the fast flip, down when it fights
input bool   T_RegimeStrength  = false;  // (v16.33) SUPERSEDED - ADX was already priced in 4 other places. This
                                         // input and T_StrengthMaxBoost no longer affect sizing. Left in place so
                                         // existing .set files still load; do not expect it to do anything.
input double T_StrengthMaxBoost = 0.30;  //    max extra size at ADX 45+
input bool   T_BreakoutBoost   = true;   // #3 boost a flip that IS a range breaking into a trend (birth of a runner)
input double T_BreakoutBoostX  = 1.15;   // (v16.33) 1.30->1.15: conf already pays +12 for squeezeRelease,
                                        //    which detects the same expansion-out-of-compression by another
                                        //    route. Both stay (ADX-transition vs ATR-compression are
                                        //    different reads) but the overlap is no longer paid twice over.
// (v16.60) The second T_SessionWeight declaration lived here and was removed —
// see the single declaration above. Its v16.33 SUPERSEDED reasoning was adopted.
                                         // doing session sizing and is the tested one. This input and the three
                                         // T_Session*X multipliers below no longer affect sizing. T_SessionEaseRegime
                                         // is a DIFFERENT thing and is still fully live.
input double T_SessionPrimeX   = 1.25;   //    12-16 GMT (London/NY overlap) — the richest directional window
input double T_SessionGoodX    = 1.10;   //    London morning / NY afternoon
input double T_SessionDeadX    = 0.70;   //    00-06 GMT (Asian range) — trim
input bool   T_SessionEaseRegime = true; // #5 during prime hours, require a touch less ADX to call it a trend
input bool   T_AccelBoost      = true;   // #6 boost a flip on expanding ranges (real thrust), trim a limp one
input bool   T_DxyConfluence   = true;   // #7 dollar-direction confirmation (gold moves inverse to DXY)
input string T_DxySymbol       = "USDX"; //    DXY proxy symbol on the broker — set to your broker's name, or "" to disable
input bool   T_WinPress        = false;  // (v16.33) SUPERSEDED by T_WinLadder in LotFor(), which already presses on
                                         // a win streak and is hard-capped against compounding. This input and
                                         // T_WinPressMax no longer affect sizing - tune T_WinLadderStep instead.
input double T_WinPressMax     = 1.25;   //    cap on the win-press multiplier
input bool   T_RegimeProfitLock = true;  // #9 hold looser in a strong trend, lock tighter in weak/range (see ManagePosition)
input bool   T_TradeJournal    = true;   // #10 write every closed trade to CSV with regime/session/ADX for real measurement
// ═══ (v16.25) MARKET STRUCTURE — the second, independent trend read ═══
// A trend is DEFINED by its swing sequence (higher highs + higher lows),
// not by an indicator. This reads price itself, so it adds genuinely new
// information instead of double-counting Supertrend/DEMA/ADX/Two-Pole.
input bool   T_StructureBirth  = true;   // grade a flip UP when market structure confirms the same birth (BOS)
input double T_StructBirthBoost = 1.30;  // size multiplier when Supertrend AND structure agree on the birth
input double T_StructAgainstX   = 0.75;  // size multiplier when structure DISAGREES with the flip (still taken, smaller)
input int    T_SwingSide       = 3;      // bars required on each side of a swing (research: 3 filters the noise)
input int    T_SwingLook       = 60;     // how far back to search for swings
input double T_BosBodyAtr      = 0.05;   // a BOS close must clear the swing by this x ATR (a wick through is a sweep, not a break)
input bool   T_StructureExit   = true;   // CHoCH exit: close when structure is objectively broken (the "line in the sand")
// (v17.65) THE BACKTEST FINALLY GIVES US THE REAL NUMBER, and it is not the
// win rate. 189 trades, 62.43% WON. That win rate is fine — it is better than
// most trend systems ever manage. The killer is the ratio:
//        average WIN   1.48
//        average LOSS  4.79     -> winners are 31% the size of losers
// At a 62% win rate the break-even requirement is avgWin >= 0.60 x avgLoss.
// He is at 0.31, which is why 62% winners still produce a 0.51 profit factor.
// Nothing about the SIGNALS causes that shape. Cutting winners does. The banks
// were firing at 0.30R-0.40R (~1.5 dollars) while losses ran to the stop.
// T_MinBankR is now the single floor every discretionary profit bank must clear.
// At R ~= 3 dollars, 1.00R is ~3 dollars, which puts the average win in the same
// league as the average loss. With his 62% that alone flips the expectancy:
//   118 x 3.0 - 71 x 4.79 = +GBP14 per 189 trades before any other improvement,
//   versus -165.90 as it stands. It is not the whole answer, it is the sign flip.
// (v17.67) 1.00 -> 0.60, and HE IS RIGHT ABOUT WHY. My v17.65 reasoning treated
// the 1.48 average win as the disease. It is the symptom. The cause is the entry:
// if a trade is opened stretched from the mean, it starts underwater, claws back
// to +0.3R and that IS its high-water mark — forcing it to hold for 1.00R does
// not turn it into a 3-dollar winner, it turns a 1.48 win into a 4.79 LOSS.
// A profit floor on top of bad entries makes the ratio worse, not better.
// So the floor drops to 0.60R: still above the 0.30R cluster that was clipping
// genuine runners, but low enough that it cannot force a badly-entered trade to
// give everything back. The real work is in the entry gates (v17.66), and once
// entries land near the mean this number can be re-tested upward ON DATA.
input bool   T_SmallMoveCeilFix   = true; // (v18.59) makes the SMALL-MOVE bank satisfiable again. false = pre-v18.59
input double T_MinBankR          = 0.60; // (v17.67) no discretionary bank below this many R
input double T_StructureExitMinR = 1.00; // (v17.65) 0.40 -> 1.00, same reason // only structure-exit once at least this much profit exists (else the stop handles it)
input bool   T_StructurePatience = true; // hold a trade with confidence while ITS OWN timeframe structure is intact
input int    T_PatienceBars    = 5;      // for this many bars of its own TF, an intact-structure trade ignores the fast banks
// ═══ (v16.26) SIGNAL AUDIT + SHADOW P&L ═══
// Counts EVERY flip, records which gate skipped it, and shadow-tracks
// what the skipped trade would have made (final R and peak R) so you can
// see exactly which gate is costing profit and which is saving losses.
// Writes quad_signals.csv in the MT5 Common\Files folder.
input bool   T_TrendRideGuard  = true;   // (v16.26) while regime is TRENDING and structure INTACT, the small fast
                                         // banks stand down so a live trend can run to its peak — the fix for
                                         // "it doesn't follow trends to the end". Stop/peak-lock/CHoCH still guard it.
// (v16.29) DEAD-CHOP FILTER — skips ONLY the narrow worst case: range regime
// AND extreme choppiness AND no structural backing. Mildly choppy legs are
// still taken (they pay pennies-to-pounds via the range-scalp / chop-stall /
// breakeven rescue). Set false to go back to taking literally every flip.
input double T_StallBankR      = 0.55;   // (v16.45) WAS 0.25, and 0.25 was NEGATIVE EV once the spread is
                                        // priced in. Derivation: with the BE lock removing the downside on the
                                        // ~83% of trades that arm it, EV of banking at level x is
                                        //   0.17*(-1R) + 0.83*(P*x + (1-P)*0.15R) - spread/R
                                        // (v16.46 CORRECTED) This derivation used R~2pts. R on M1 is 6pts, so the
                                        // spread is 4.2% of R, NOT 12.5%. At the true stop, banking at 0.25R
                                        // (1.5pts = ~GBP1.13) clears the ~GBP0.19 cost comfortably and was NEVER
                                        // negative EV. The +GBP39/day claim below is therefore UNSUPPORTED and the
                                        // 0.25->0.55 raise is unvalidated. Superseded text kept for audit:
                                        // On M1 (R~2pts) the spread is 12.5% OF R on EVERY trade, so banking at
                                        // 0.25R (0.5pts = ~£0.375) barely cleared the ~£0.19 cost: net -£0.14
                                        // per trade. Banking at 0.55R nets +£0.19. Across ~110 trades/day that
                                        // single number is worth roughly +£39/day at 0.01 and +£55 on the
                                        // 0.01/0.02/0.03 ladder — the largest single-parameter gain found yet.
                                        // TRADE-OFF, stated honestly: fewer trades get a small bank, and more
                                        // finish at the scratch-plus floor instead. That is the opposite of
                                        // "bank early" instinct, but the arithmetic is unambiguous — an early
                                        // bank that only covers the spread is worse than letting the BE lock
                                        // hold the trade for a real one. Kept below T_RangeScalpR (0.8R) so the
                                        // regime-based range bank still has its own distinct job.
input bool   T_RangeVeto        = true;  // (v18.03, his ask: "we need to know to chill during these
                                        // sideways ranges -- that is the only place I do not want to
                                        // trade.") Choppiness at/above T_DeadChopLevel means rotation,
                                        // not a trend. Until now that only TRIMMED size (T_DeadChopSizeX
                                        // 0.45). This refuses the entry outright. false = v18.02.
input bool   T_DeadChopFilter  = true;
// (v16.43) If the dead-chop size-down cannot physically change the lot (because
// the account is already at the broker minimum), taking the trade "smaller" means
// taking it at FULL risk. In that case skip instead. Reverts to sizing down on its
// own once the balance is big enough for the trim to bite. false = always take.
input bool   T_DeadChopSkipIfNoTrim = false;  // (v16.65) WAS true, and at his lot size it was a hidden SKIP.
                                        // The design intent of the dead-chop filter is "never refuse a trend,
                                        // just size it down" — T_DeadChopSizeX 0.45 takes it smaller. But
                                        // 0.01 IS the broker floor, so on a GBP150-GBP500 account the trim
                                        // cannot physically bite, and this flag then turned the size-down
                                        // into an outright REFUSAL. That silently removed a whole class of
                                        // signals at exactly the account sizes he trades, which is the
                                        // opposite of "take every trend". Now false: the trade is taken.
                                        // Trade-off, stated plainly: these are the chop-signature entries, so
                                        // the loss COUNT rises. It is bounded — same 6/9/12 stop, same 8%
                                        // cash cap, same early-failure cut — and the round-trip floor and
                                        // runner lock still govern the exit. Reverts to sizing down by itself
                                        // once the balance is large enough for 0.45x to change the lot.
input double T_NoiseFloorAtr    = 0.60;   // (v17.98 THE EXECUTION FIX) Every giveback/ratchet threshold in
                                          // this file is a FRACTION OF PEAK with no absolute floor. Arithmetic
                                          // on M1 (ATR 1.95, spread ~0.20): a 3.0pt peak allows 40% giveback
                                          // via the ratchet (rFrac 0.60) = 1.20pts, and the hard ceiling fires
                                          // at 65% = 1.95pts. Both are AT OR BELOW T_LegNoisePts = 1.50, i.e.
                                          // this file's own definition of noise. So a normal pullback closes
                                          // the trade before the move develops -- and because the exit caps the
                                          // peak, the peak never grows into the range where the same percentages
                                          // would be generous. Self-fulfilling: modest peak -> tight guard ->
                                          // exit -> stays modest. That is the 90%-went-green vs 65%-outcome gap.
                                          // No exit may now sit closer to the peak than spread + this x ATR
                                          // (M1: 0.20 + 0.60*1.95 = 1.37pts). Set 0 to restore v17.97 behaviour.
input double T_MaxGivebackPct   = 65.0;   // (v17.43) HARD ceiling, fires regardless of healthyTrend/
                                          // structIntact — a peak can never be given back more than this
                                          // fraction before the trade closes, full stop.
input bool   T_SkipM3          = true;
input group  "=== v18.06  ENTRY ANCHOR + PATH AUDIT ==="
// (v18.06) THE PEAK-ENTRY BUG. T_MaxWickAtr existed but was wired into the EARLY
// FLIP path ONLY (~7790). The CONFIRMED flip, the micro-breakout, the reversal
// entry, the pullback and the reclaim all reached the order with NO extension
// check at all -- so a big candle that crossed the line got filled at its far
// end. That is exactly the 13:56 sell (4387.4, the swing low, then +10 against)
// and the 15:14 buy (4396.2, the swing high). The guard now sits at the single
// funnel every path passes through, so no route can bypass it.
// (v18.16) MODE, NOT A SWITCH — and an admission.
// v18.06 added this guard as a REFUSAL. It is in direct tension with the entry
// path his log shows every fill uses: EARLY-FLIP fires BECAUSE price crossed the
// signal line, and this guard refuses BECAUSE price is past the signal line. The
// same tick that creates the signal is the tick that trips the guard. On a fast
// cross price is already >1.20 ATR beyond the line, so the guard refuses the
// very entries the system depends on. It was also the TIGHTEST of three
// overlapping distance gates (1.20 vs 1.50 vs 1.50), so it silently became the
// binding constraint on all entries -- against the standing instruction that
// every signal must be taken.
// Mode 1 (default) MEASURES instead of refusing: every entry logs how far past
// the line it filled, so the real distribution decides the threshold instead of
// a number I guessed. Mode 2 restores refusing once that data exists.
//   0 = off        1 = log only, never refuse (DEFAULT)   2 = refuse
input int    T_AnchorMode        = 1;
// (v18.18) see the SEVERE note at the flip gate. Lets a parked (deferred) flip
// be re-offered to the entry path so the defer can actually resolve.
// (v18.19) The EA may only take entries the Pine chart also shows. See the note
// at the entry funnel. Blocks MICRO-BREAKOUT / REVERSAL / PULLBACK paths.
//================================================================
//  (v18.29) EVERY PINE SIGNAL IS TAKEN — enforced as a rule, not a hope
//  I have now caused three separate no-trade or bad-trade sessions by adding or
//  unblocking one admission gate at a time. This makes the rule structural: with
//  T_TakeEveryPine on, a Supertrend flip (the ONLY thing his Pine draws) cannot be
//  refused for price, for exposure counting, or for cost. The remaining refusals
//  are the ones that are not opinions: no signal at all, a dead clock, Friday
//  flat, and the duplicate-fill guard (which prevents ONE signal becoming three
//  positions — it does not refuse the signal itself).
//  Bad prices are handled by SIZE (T_ExtendedLotFrac) and bad trades by an early
//  points cut (T_MaxLossPts), never by declining the trade.
//================================================================
input bool   T_TakeEveryPine     = true;
input bool   T_PineParity        = true;
int          g_parityBlocked     = 0;
input bool   T_DeferRearm        = true;
input bool   T_AnchorGuardAll   = true;  // false = restore v18.05 (early-flip path guarded only)
input double T_AnchorMaxAtr     = 1.20;  // refuse entry if price is further than this x ATR beyond the signal line
// PATH AUDIT: prints which route actually produced each fill, so "we enter late"
// stops being an inference. CONFIRMED = the slow route (waited for bar close).
input bool   T_PathAudit        = true;

int g_pathN[6];   // 0 CONFIRMED 1 EARLY 2 MICRO 3 REVERSAL 4 PULLBACK 5 RECLAIM
int g_anchorBlocked = 0;

string PathName(int i)
{
   switch(i)
   {
      case 1: return "EARLY-FLIP";
      case 2: return "MICRO-BREAKOUT";
      case 3: return "REVERSAL";
      case 4: return "PULLBACK";
      case 5: return "RECLAIM";
   }
   return "CONFIRMED";
}

input double T_MaxWickAtr      = 1.50;   // (v17.50) skip entry if price is >this x ATR beyond the signal line (buying the wick tip)    // (v17.47) 3-minute clock disabled entirely, his measured call
input bool   T_OtherPairStrict  = true;    // (v17.42, his ask: "for other pairs, only take average
                                            // and good, not pure chop — for XAU take every trend, chop,
                                            // average, or good.") ONLY affects symbols other than gold —
                                            // XAU keeps its current frequency untouched. Reuses the SAME
                                            // regime engine (ADX + choppiness), which is not gold-specific
                                            // maths — it reads whatever symbol the chart is attached to,
                                            // so it transfers to any pair with no extra work.
input bool   T_DeadChopWorthIt   = true;  // (v17.25) skip dead-chop entries with no room over
                                          // round-trip cost, regardless of size
input double T_DeadChopMinEdgeMult = 1.5; // expected capture must be this many x round-trip cost
input double T_DeadChopSizeX   = 0.45;   // (v17.29 RE-ENABLED, his condition: "if the dead chops can
                                          // bring in profit then I'll allow.") The difference from v16.32
                                          // is that dead chop is no longer taken on faith — T_DeadChopWorthIt
                                          // (v17.25) now gates it on real arithmetic: expected capture must
                                          // clear round-trip cost by T_DeadChopMinEdgeMult (1.5x) BEFORE this
                                          // size is ever applied. So this is not "take every dead chop
                                          // again" — it is "take the ones that have already proven they can
                                          // clear cost, at reduced size." Set 0.0 to go back to skipping all
                                          // of them if the journal later shows this still isn't paying.
                                        //          (set 0 to skip instead — he wants profit off every trend, so it trades)
input double T_DeadChopLevel   = 70.0;   // Choppiness at/above this = rotation, not a trend (61.8 is the normal range line)
input bool   T_RangeBreakeven = true;    // (v16.27) in a RANGE regime, arm breakeven early so a choppy trade
input double T_RangeBeR        = 0.15;   //          scratches instead of paying a full stop ("at least break even")
// (v19.00) TRUE looped the shadow list and wrote a CSV every tick. His standing rule
// is that every signal is taken, so there are almost no shadows to track.
input bool   T_SignalAudit     = false;
input int    T_ShadowMaxBars   = 60;     // give up shadow-tracking a skipped signal after this many of its own bars
// (v15.5) PULLBACK-TO-LINE CONTINUATION ENTRY — the research-documented
// highest-edge Supertrend signal: inside an established trend (15+ bars, no
// flip), price pulls back to the line, holds it with a rejection candle,
// and the next leg begins. Lets TREND board a trend whose flip was missed
// (cooldown, spread gate, restart) instead of forfeiting the whole move.
input bool   T_UsePullbackEntry = false; // (v17.48) OFF. A pullback-to-line entry is a MID-TREND
                                        // continuation, not a birth — it enters an already-running move,
                                        // which is exactly the "not at birth" behaviour he is describing.
input int    T_PullbackMinBars  = 10;   // (v16.05) bars of established trend before a pullback entry is allowed — lower = more frequency on real trends
// (v15.5) FLIP-FREQUENCY CHOP PENALTY — the documented rule: 3+ Supertrend
// flips inside ~20 bars = ranging market, every flip pays the whipsaw toll.
// Applied as a confidence penalty (frequency-first: reduce, never block).
input bool   T_FlipFreqPenalty  = true;
// #9 Consecutive-loss-by-timeframe cool: if ONE timeframe is bleeding, rest
//    just that timeframe, not the whole engine.
input bool   T_TfCool          = true;
input int    T_TfCoolLosses    = 3;       // this many real losses on one TF -> rest that TF a while
// #10 Profit-lock ratchet: bank the RUNNER's floor upward in steps as R grows,
//     so a big move can't round-trip past a step it already cleared.
input bool   T_PeakTrailOn     = true;  // (v18.61) price-anchored Chandelier trail. false = pre-v18.61 (nothing follows price)
// ══════ (v18.92 AV) TRAIL ROOM vs THE NOISE BAND — MEASURED ON HIS TAPE ══════
// 24 Aug 11:17, live: sell 4637.46, low 4632.19, stop parked at 4634.46.
// Room = 1.30 x ATR = 2.27 pts. But the NOISE BAND this build defines everywhere
// else is spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr(0.60) x ATR = 1.30 pts at that same ATR.
// So the trail hands back 1.75 NOISE BANDS while every other lock in the file
// treats ONE band as the required breathing room. On that trade the difference is
// stop 4634.46 (locks +3.00) versus 4633.49 (locks +3.97) -- about a point per
// winner. Same reasoning as the v18.73 (P) hard-lock band cap, which is already
// accepted in this build.
// SHIPPED OFF, and deliberately. v17.84 raised this 0.90 -> 1.30 BECAUSE the
// trail was clipping normal pullbacks, and that was a live finding too. Tighter
// room takes a point more from every winner that runs cleanly and takes the whole
// trade from every winner that pulls back. Only his tape can settle which is
// bigger, and one session of the ledger will show it.
// TURN ON: T_TrailRoomBandCap = true.
input bool   T_TrailRoomBandCap = false;
input double T_TrailRoomAtr    = 1.30;  // (v17.84) 0.90->1.30: trail was hitting normal pullbacks  // (v17.75) minimum trail standoff while the leg is still making new extremes
input double T_LockRoomAtr     = 0.90;  // (v17.84) 0.60->0.90: room to breathe in first candles  // (v17.72) an early locked stop must keep this x ATR of air from live price
input double T_LockRoomUntilR  = 1.60;  // (v17.84) 1.20->1.60: protect only after real, not early, profit  // (v17.72) ...until peak profit passes this many R, after which normal locking resumes
input bool   T_StepLock        = true;
// ═══ IMPROVEMENT #4 — CONTINUOUS PEAK-LOCK (fills the 1R..2R gap) ═══
// Between micro-lock (0.3R -> breakeven) and step-lock (only starts at 2R,
// 1R granularity) there was a gap: a move that peaked at, say, 1.7R and
// rolled over gave back everything down to breakeven before any ratchet
// caught it. On your tight signal-line stops a 6-16 point move can peak
// in that exact 1-2R band, so this gap was directly costing the "banked
// at the tip" behaviour you want. This locks a rising fraction of PEAK
// profit continuously from +1R upward: at peak +1R it holds ~+0.5R, at
// +2R ~+1.2R, scaling toward the graduated fraction of peak. Only ratchets
// forward, never loosens — the runner is always protected near its high.
input bool   T_PeakLock        = true;
input double T_PeakLockArmR    = 1.0;    // start peak-locking once this much R has been reached
// BUG FIX #64 (conflict between two v15 additions, caught in audit): the
// flat 0.65 lock from +1R allowed only a 35% pullback from peak — but the
// clean-run giveback hold explicitly allows up to 60% giveback on
// efficient trends. The hard SL fired first, making the clean-run hold
// DEAD CODE past 1R peak, and a 35% allowance that early is tight enough
// to recreate the exact "closed on a small dip, trend continued" pattern
// on gold's fast clocks. Now GRADUATED: loose early (a young 1R trade may
// pull back ~55% and still be a healthy trend — ride it), tight late (a
// 5R runner holds 75% — bank near the tip). This makes the peak-lock and
// the clean-run hold coherent instead of fighting: early on, the softer
// giveback guard (with its efficiency read) leads; deep in profit, the
// hard lock leads.
input double T_PeakLockFracLo  = 0.45;   // fraction of peak held at the arm point (young trade — room to breathe)
input double T_PeakLockFracHi  = 0.75;   // fraction of peak held by 5R+ (mature runner — bank the tip)
// (v16.14 RESEARCH #3) CHANDELIER VOLATILITY TIP-LOCK. An EXTRA stop
// floor at (highest-high-since-entry - mult x ATR) for a long, mirror
// for a short (LeBeau's classic Chandelier Exit). Taken as the tighter
// of this and every existing floor, so it can only ever bank MORE,
// never loosen anything. Its advantage over the R-from-entry peak-lock:
// it anchors to the REAL price extreme and tightens automatically as
// ATR dries up near a top — exactly where a parabolic gold move rolls
// over — so it banks closer to the tip on the big runs the peak-lock
// leaves a little on the table. Kept loose (3x ATR) and armed only from
// decent profit so it protects the tip without clipping a healthy ride.
input bool   T_ChandelierLock = true;
input double T_ChandelierArmR  = 1.5;   // only arm once the trade has reached this peak R
input double T_ChandelierMult  = 3.0;   // ATR multiple below the highest-high-since-entry
input bool   T_M15Lockdown  = true;  // (v16.15) when a SUPER-confident M15 signal appears, a fast flip that
                                      // FIGHTS it is no longer SKIPPED — it is TAKEN at reduced size (your locked
                                      // rule: catch both directions on fast TFs, never hard-block, lean instead).
input double T_M15CounterRisk = 0.5; // (v16.15) size multiplier for a fast flip that fights a super-confident M15
input double T_M15LockConf  = 85.0;  // M15 confidence must be this high (0-100) to trigger the lockdown —
                                      // deliberately very high so it only happens on a genuinely strong M15 trend
input double T_Tp2R        = 3.0;
input double T_Tp3R        = 4.5;
input double T_PullDepth   = 0.4;   // pullback must be this deep, x ATR
input int    T_PullMaxBars = 8;
input double T_MinAdx      = 0.0;   // (v16.67) WAS 18.0. This was the gate refusing ~80% of his signals.
                                   // LIVE EVIDENCE, 28 Jul: the Pine printed ~10 signals between 06:00 and
                                   // 11:30 on a chart that ranged 4035-4050 for five hours. The EA took
                                   // TWO. In a range ADX sits around 10-15, so an 18 floor rejected almost
                                   // everything — and the two it did take were the only ones with enough
                                   // directional push to clear it, both of which whipsawed anyway
                                   // (-1.23pts and -1.50pts, about a pound each).
                                   // WHY THE OLD COMMENT ("REVERTED 14->18, lowering it took MORE chop")
                                   // CANNOT BE TRUSTED: that revert was made in v16.16, and the EA has not
                                   // compiled since v16.33 — but more importantly the observation behind it
                                   // was never separated from the then-broken exit stack. Chop entries are
                                   // only harmful if chop exits are bad, and the exit side has since been
                                   // rebuilt: peak ratchet, round-trip floor, runner lock, structure-gated
                                   // cut. His two live losses are the proof — chop now costs ~£1, not ~£4.
                                   // 0.0 = take every confirmed flip regardless of ADX, which is his
                                   // explicit instruction and the whole point of the design: many small
                                   // controlled losses funded by the legs that run.
                                   // THIS IS THE SINGLE HIGHEST-VARIANCE CHANGE IN THE FILE. It will
                                   // multiply trade count several times over. quad_signals.csv shadow P&L
                                   // on the ADX 0-18 band is what decides whether it stays. If that band's
                                   // aggregate net is negative, set it back to 12-15, not 18.
                                     // trades — exactly backwards for "reduce back-to-back losses without
                                     // touching profit potential." 18 is the tuned floor; frequency now comes
                                     // from taking every REAL trending flip, not from lowering the trend bar.
input double T_MinConf     = 0.0;   // (v16.93) 40 -> 0. I had told him a bare confirmed flip scores exactly
                                    // 40, so 40 was the floor at which nothing gets refused. HIS LOG DISPROVES
                                    // THAT: "SKIP DOWN on PERIOD_M5 — confidence 30 below floor 40." A flip
                                    // that is BOTH against DEMA and against the M15 bias carries PENALTIES
                                    // that take it below the +40 the flip itself contributes, so the floor was
                                    // still rejecting exactly the counter-trend reversals he wants most.
                                    // 0 = no signal is ever refused on confidence. Confidence still SIZES the
                                    // trade (g_lastConfMult, and the M15 counter-lean x0.50 visible in his
                                    // log), so weak signals go on smaller rather than not at all — which is
                                    // his own stated principle.  // (v16.69) 45 -> 40. A bare confirmed flip scores exactly 40, so 40 is
                                    // the floor at which EVERY confirmed flip passes. Nothing below 40 exists.
                                    // "Really really bad" is still filtered by the doji-flip skip (a flip bar
                                    // with no directional body) and the spread gate — those are execution
                                    // quality, not signal quality.  // (v16.59) WAS 60. A bare confirmed flip scores 40 (line ~5361) and
                                    // DEMA agreement alone adds 25, so 60 quietly meant "be on the DEMA
                                    // side, or find 20 points elsewhere". DEMA200 on M1 measures 200
                                    // minutes, so a fresh reversal off a low is against it for its entire
                                    // life — which filtered precisely the shape his biggest legs come from
                                    // (the 4048->4062 +14pt M1 reversal).
                                    // THE EVIDENCE FOR LOWERING IT: minGrade = 0, so the Pine PLOTS every
                                    // flip INCLUDING the ones this gate refuses. His live screenshots have
                                    // therefore been showing the REJECTED population all along, and he
                                    // reports it is mostly good — direct observation on the exact question
                                    // this number decides. Panels on winners read 50/100 and 74/100.
                                    // THE COUNTER-EVIDENCE, stated honestly: quad_losses.csv showed losers
                                    // clustering as against-DEMA / low-confidence. This WILL raise the loss
                                    // COUNT; the bet is the extra winners more than pay for it.
                                    // Also note 45 changes conviction SCALING at line ~5535, since that
                                    // maps (conf - T_MinConf) onto 0-1 — sizing becomes slightly more
                                    // generous. Inert at the 0.01 minimum lot, live above ~GBP500.
                                    // Set 40 to take literally every confirmed flip.
                                    // lightweight confidence floor (0-100) — Supertrend+DEMA agreeing
                                     // alone already scores 65, so that combination (the core rule)
                                     // can never be blocked by this; it only filters flips where DEMA
                                     // disagrees AND momentum/structure are also weak. Never delays
                                     // entry — built entirely from data already on hand, no extra fetch.
// (v16.14 BUG FIX) TREND AGAINST-DEMA DOWNSIZING. The loss forensics
// fingerprint counter-DEMA flips as the losing cluster. SCALP already
// downsizes them explicitly (SC_AgainstDemaRisk). TREND did NOT — it
// leaned only on the confidence score, which a momentum-strong counter-
// DEMA flip could overcome and still take near-full size while the log
// claimed "reduced size." This is TREND's explicit equivalent: the flip
// is still TAKEN (frequency preserved, no trend missed) but sized down
// materially when it fights the 200-DEMA. Set 1.0 to disable.
input bool   T_AgainstDemaBlock = true;  // (v18.02) HIS 11 Aug 09:22/09:27 LOSSES, from the tickets:
                                         // sell 0.02 @4370.651 -> 4372.787 (-4.27) and sell 0.02
                                         // @4370.960 -> 4372.798 (-3.68). Both SHORTS taken while price
                                         // was ABOVE the DEMA, both stopped at the initial signal-line
                                         // stop within minutes. The v16.14 design deliberately TOOK
                                         // counter-DEMA flips at half size ("frequency preserved") --
                                         // T_AgainstDemaRisk below. That was a choice, not a bug, and it
                                         // is the choice that produced both of today's losses.
                                         // He has now named this twice with money attached: if price is
                                         // on the wrong side of the DEMA, do not take the trade at all.
                                         // true = refuse the flip. false = v18.01 behaviour (take at
                                         // T_AgainstDemaRisk size). NOTE THE TRADE-OFF: this REDUCES
                                         // signal count, which pulls against "take every signal". It
                                         // wins because the refused ones are the measured losers.
input double T_AgainstDemaRisk = 0.5;
// (v16.14 RESEARCH #1) LIQUIDITY-SWEEP CONFIRMATION — grade a flip UP
// when its bar first swept a recent swing extreme and closed back inside
// (stop-run-and-reversal). Confidence nudge only, never a block.
input bool   T_UseLiquiditySweep = true;
input int    T_SweepLookback     = 10;   // bars of prior swing structure the sweep must pierce
input double T_SweepConfBonus    = 12.0; // confidence points added when a sweep+reclaim confirms the flip
// (v16.14 RESEARCH #2) SQUEEZE-RELEASE BOOST — grade a flip UP when it
// is the first expansion out of a genuine volatility compression, and
// exempt that flip from the low-ATR / isolation penalties that would
// otherwise dampen a trend START. Confidence nudge only, never a block.
input bool   T_UseSqueezeBoost = true;
input double T_SqueezeRatio    = 0.70;  // ATR a few bars back below this x its ~50-bar avg = compressed
input double T_SqueezeConfBonus = 12.0; // confidence points added on a confirmed squeeze release
// (v17.59, his live report: "we made a GBP15 loss and there is no risk
// management when a trade goes into loss") HARD STOP-DISTANCE CAP, in points.
// Why a GBP15 loss was possible with a "3pt" stop: the stop is the Supertrend
// line minus (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) x ATR, and the line itself sits
// T_StMultiplier x ATR from price — so the whole distance is ~3 x ATR. At the
// M1 ATR of ~1.0pt he logged that is ~3pts (GBP6.7 at 0.03), but when ATR
// expands to 2.2pts it becomes ~6.7pts = GBP15. Nothing capped it. This does:
// if the computed stop is further than T_MaxStopPts, the stop is placed at the
// cap instead. Set 0 to disable.
// THE TRADE-OFF, said plainly: a capped stop is a TIGHTER stop, so in fast
// volatility more trades will be stopped out that the wider stop would have
// survived. He is choosing a known GBP8.9 worst case over an uncapped one.
// (v17.85, his BTC ask) T_MaxStopPts was an ABSOLUTE dollar cap, tuned to gold
// (ATR ~1-5). BTC M1 ATR routinely runs $30-150+, so a fixed $4 cap would clamp
// every BTC stop far inside normal noise -> guaranteed near-instant stop-outs
// regardless of signal quality. That is the real "why doesn't it trade BTC
// properly" cause. Every OTHER gate in this file (wick/extension/mean/opposing
// candle/FVG/room) is already ATR-relative and needs no change.
// Fix: the cap now scales with the instrument's own ATR instead of a fixed
// number, so it means the same thing (in ATR terms) on gold and BTC alike.
input double T_MaxStopAtrMult = 3.0;   // stop cap = this x current ATR (was a fixed 4.0 pts)
input double T_MaxStopPts  = 4.0;      // (legacy) fixed-pt fallback, used only if T_MaxStopAtrMult <= 0
input double T_SlNoiseBuf  = 0.25;  // BUG FIX #46: the initial stop used to sit only 0.1x ATR past the
                                     // signal line — essentially zero room for ordinary noise. A single
                                     // normal wick poking back past entry (not a real reversal — Supertrend
                                     // and the broader trend read still agreeing) could clip the stop
                                     // before the trade ever had a chance to develop. This is the room
                                     // given specifically for that: still a tight, signal-line-anchored
                                     // stop, just enough wider that ordinary chop doesn't kill a
                                     // correct trade for a reason that has nothing to do with being wrong.
input double T_SlEntryBuf  = 0.60;  // (v12) ENTRY BREATHING ROOM. This is the fix for "opens, dips a
                                     // few pips, hits SL, then the trend continues" — losing winners at
                                     // the moment of entry. The stop at the actual moment of entry sits
                                     // at (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) x ATR beyond the signal line,
                                     // WIDER than the steady-state trail. This extra room ratchets away
                                     // over the first several bars as the trade proves itself.
                                     // Cash risk is UNCHANGED — R is measured from the wider stop, so
                                     // LotFor sizes the position proportionally smaller. Not looser
                                     // risk, smarter placement.
input int    T_SlEntryBufBars = 6;  // (v12) bars over which the extra room ratchets back to normal.
                                     // Also collapses immediately once openR >= 0.8, so a trade that
                                     // works out fast never keeps a wider stop than it needs to.
// (v18.17) HOLD HORIZON BY CLOCK — his instruction: "when a 15min gets a signal
// we take it and hold it out for the day, same with 5min, we really need to hold
// them out. M1 is our quick profits, max 30-50 min."
// T_MaxHold was ONE number in bars applied to every clock, which is 60 bars =
// 60 min on M1 but 60 bars = 5 HOURS on M5 and 15 hours on M15. The unit hid the
// fact that the three clocks were never given different horizons; they were
// given wildly different ones by accident, in the wrong direction relative to
// what he wants. These are explicit MINUTES per clock instead.
input int    T_HoldMinsM1  = 50;    // M1: quick trend capture
input int    T_HoldMinsM5  = 360;   // M5: hold it out
input int    T_HoldMinsM15 = 900;   // M15+: hold for the session
input int    T_MaxHold     = 60;    // bars — legacy, now only a fallback when the clock is unrecognised
input int    T_Stall       = 12;    // bars with no new extreme -> cut
// (v17.95) 2 -> 1 bar. He wants every birth signal taken; a 2-bar lockout after an
// exit could skip a genuine fresh flip on the bar right after a trade closed.
// One bar still blocks re-boarding the same leg on the same bar, which was the
// back-to-back pattern he photographed and disliked. Genuine flip-closes still
// set fast-rearm, which overrides this entirely.
input int    T_Cooldown    = 1;   // (v17.54) 0 -> 2 bars, per clock. With this at 0 AND T_CrossTfChopPause
                                  // false, NOTHING throttled a same-clock re-entry: the EA could bank a trade
                                  // and re-board the same M1 leg on the next tick. That is the back-to-back
                                  // entry pattern he photographed, and his own read is right — an M1 leg
                                  // rarely has a second push in it. A genuine Supertrend flip-close still
                                  // sets fast-rearm, which overrides this to 1 bar, so a real reversal is
                                  // never made to sit out the full wait.
                                  // (v16.28 note kept for history) A 3-bar cooldown on M1 is 3 minutes blind after every
                                  // exit — and it bites hardest exactly when a live trend re-signals.
                                  // The dangerous case it was guarding (rapid re-entry into the same
                                  // chop) is now covered properly by T_CrossTfChopPause, which pauses
                                  // on 2 real losses in 10 min instead of blocking every re-entry.
input bool   T_CrossTfChopPause = false; // (v16.83) OFF. Paused NEW entries for 5 minutes after 2+ TREND
                                         // losses in 10 minutes — a THROTTLE, and every throttle left in
                                         // this file is something that can make the EA look like it "isn't
                                         // trading" during exactly the busy, choppy stretches he keeps
                                         // sending screenshots of. Constant point-on-point trading needs
                                         // this off.
input int    T_ChopPauseTrigger = 2;    // this many TREND losses inside the window trips the pause
input int    T_ChopPauseWindowSec = 600; // window to count losses in (seconds) — 10 minutes
input int    T_ChopPauseSec       = 300; // how long new entries pause once tripped (seconds) — 5 minutes
input bool   T_TrailOnST   = true;  // Supertrend IS the trail
input bool   T_DynamicTP   = true;  // extend TP3 while momentum agrees

input group "=== E7 - MULTI-TF SCALP (M1 + M3 + M5 + M15, all at once) ==="
// THE ENGINE YOU DESCRIBED.
//
// It scans FOUR timeframes simultaneously — M1, M3, M5, M15 — and takes a scalp
// on any of them that shows an early, confirmed trend push. Not at birth (too
// many fakes), not late (no R left). EARLY-BUT-CONFIRMED: the trend has proven
// itself for a few bars, price has pulled back and resumed, and momentum is
// expanding again.
//
// Short. Sweet. Out before the tables turn.
//
// THE EXIT IS THE WHOLE POINT. A scalp does not sit through a reversal waiting
// for its stop. It leaves the moment momentum fades — you keep the small profit
// instead of watching it become a small loss. Three ways out, whichever comes first:
//    1. Target hit (2.0R)
//    2. Momentum fades (the push is over — take what is there)
//    3. Stop (rare, because #2 usually fires first)
//
// STACKING: on a STRONG, confirmed push it takes a bigger position — not more
// tickets, a bigger one, which is the same thing without the mess. Size scales
// with confidence, and the total is still capped by the auto risk ceiling.
input bool   SC_Fast       = true;   // M1  — the mini scalps
input bool   SC_Mid        = false;  // (v18.69) M3 OFF -- his instruction. T_SkipM3 already blocked TREND on M3; this closes the SCALP half.  // M3
input bool   SC_Slow       = false;  // (v18.73 N) M5 OFF -- his instruction: "m5 entries are being an issue currently turn them off they should act as say a signal to put an extra 0.01 on times when they match with m1". M5 now votes on SIZE only, via T_M5ConfluenceLots.  // M5
input int    SC_MaxOpen    = 4;      // how many scalps may be open at once
input int    SC_DedupeBars = 2;      // same-direction signals within N bars = the same idea
input int    SC_ConfirmTFs = 2;      // how many HIGHER timeframes must agree
input bool   SC_NoOverlapTrend = true;  // (v16.41) SCALP will not open on a timeframe TREND already holds —
                                        // stops two engines taking the SAME signal on the SAME clock (double
                                        // exposure on one idea). false = restore the old doubled behaviour.
input double SC_Risk       = 0.9;   // base risk %
input double SC_AgainstDemaRisk = 0.5;  // risk multiplier for flips AGAINST the DEMA (still taken, sized down)
input double SC_SlAtr      = 0.9;   // tight
input double SC_TpR        = 2.0;
input double SC_MinAdx     = 22.0;  // the trend filter IS the edge
input int    SC_MinTrendBars = 4;   // trend must have EXISTED. Not born 1 bar ago.
input double SC_MaxExt     = 1.1;   // do not chase - max ATR from EMA21
input double SC_MinBody    = 0.50;
input double SC_MinVol     = 1.05;
input int    SC_Cooldown   = 1;

// PUSH STACKING - size up when the push is genuinely strong
input bool   SC_StackPush  = true;
input double SC_StackMax   = 2.5;   // max size multiplier on a perfect push
input int    SC_StrongConf = 80;    // confidence above this = a strong push

// EXIT BEFORE THE TABLES TURN
input bool   SC_BankAt1R   = true;   // take half off at 1R, rest rides free from breakeven
input bool   SC_FadeExit   = true;  // leave when momentum dies, do not wait for the stop
input double SC_FadeMinR   = 0.5;   // only fade-exit once at least this much is on the table
input int    SC_MaxHold    = 25;    // bars, on that signal's own timeframe

input group "=== E6 - APEX TREND RIDER (M15, NO TAKE PROFIT, pyramids) ==="
// The mirror of the TradingView APEX. Completely different job from every other
// engine here: the others take many small profits. This one takes FEW, HUGE ones.
//
// THERE IS NO TAKE PROFIT. On purpose. The only exit is the trail or the death
// of the trend. If gold runs 20R, this engine collects 20R. A fixed target would
// cap the one trade that pays for everything.
//
// THE TRAIL WIDENS AS PROFIT GROWS. Wider, not tighter. Tightening a trail on a
// winner is the single most expensive habit in trend-following.
input double A_Risk        = 1.6;
input double A_TrailEarly  = 2.0;   // 0-2R  : protect the entry
input double A_TrailMid    = 2.8;   // 2-5R  : give it room
input double A_TrailLate   = 3.8;   // 5R+   : it has earned the right to breathe
input double A_BeAtR       = 1.2;
input int    A_MaxAdds     = 2;     // pyramid the pullbacks
input double A_AddSize     = 0.6;   // x first entry
input double A_PullDepth   = 0.35;
input int    A_HealthMin   = 30;    // exit when the trend is dying, not when the stop is hit
input double A_MinAdx      = 20.0;
input bool   A_UsePulse    = true;  // higher-frequency continuation entries, not just the birth

input group "=== ADAPTIVE SL/TP + AUTO RISK (per engine) ==="
// AUTO-ADJUSTING STOPS AND TARGETS.
// Gold's ATR at $4,000 is nothing like at $1,800, and at 13:30 nothing like at
// 03:00. A fixed "x ATR" stop adapts to PRICE but not to REGIME. These do both.
input bool   InpAdaptSLTP    = true;
input int    InpAtrRankBars  = 200;
input double T_AtrFloor      = 30.0; // BUG FIX: min ATR percentile for TREND's confidence score — matches
                                      // Pine's atrFloor. A flip during genuinely low, directionless
                                      // volatility (bottom 30% of the last 200 bars) is exactly the
                                      // "small dip mistaken for a real move" case — this was previously
                                      // not checked at all in the confidence score, only ADX was.
input double InpHiVolSL      = 1.30; // top 25% volatility: widen stops 30%
input double InpHiVolTP      = 1.35; // ...and reach further, the range supports it
input double InpLoVolSL      = 0.85; // bottom 25%: tighten, less noise to survive
input double InpLoVolTP      = 0.80; // ...and don't ask for a move the tape can't give

// AUTO RISK SCALING, PER ENGINE, ON ITS OWN EXPECTANCY.
// An engine proving itself gets more size. One bleeding gets less, long before
// the hard auto-disable fires. This is the per-engine monitoring layer.
input bool   InpAutoRisk     = true;
input int    InpAutoRiskMinN = 20;
input double InpRiskUpExpR   = 0.30;
input double InpRiskDnExpR   = 0.05;
input double InpRiskUpMult   = 1.25;
input double InpRiskDnMult   = 0.60;

input group "=== ADAPTIVE DISABLE (expectancy, not win rate) ==="

input bool   InpAutoDisable    = false; // (v16.82) OFF, on his explicit instruction to take every signal.
                                        // This is very likely WHY IT STOPPED TAKING TRADES TODAY. After
                                        // InpMinTradesJudge (60) trades, if net expectancy falls below
                                        // InpMinExpectancyR (-0.25R) the engine sets Stat[e].disabled = true
                                        // and refuses EVERY SIGNAL until the EA is manually restarted — not
                                        // next session, not next day, until you stop and re-attach it.
                                        // Every filter that used to keep expectancy up (T_MinAdx, the old
                                        // T_MinConf 60, the doji skip) was removed today specifically because
                                        // he wants every signal taken, so trade count is high and some are the
                                        // against-DEMA chop trades quad_losses.csv already showed lose more
                                        // often. 60 trades at a high volume session is very reachable in a
                                        // few hours, so this is a strong candidate for silent, total stoppage.
                                        // Cost of turning it off: no automatic protection against a truly
                                        // broken session — that job now falls entirely to the daily halt
                                        // (20% from peak) and the per-trade risk caps, which is the correct
                                        // place for it given his "take every signal" instruction.
input int    InpMinTradesJudge = 60;   // (v16.34) WAS 15. An engine judged over 15 trades then PERMANENTLY
                                       // disabled until EA restart is judged on statistical noise: for a
                                       // 40-45% win-rate system a perfectly ordinary losing streak inside 15
                                       // trades kills a good engine for the rest of the run, silently. That is
                                       // a strong candidate for "the EA stopped taking signals". 60 is the
                                       // smallest sample where expectancy means anything here.
input double InpMinExpectancyR = -0.25;// (v16.34) WAS -0.15, which is inside normal variance for this system.
                                       // -0.25R over 60+ trades is genuine, persistent bleeding rather than noise.
input int    InpMaxConsecLoss  = 8;    // was 4 — kept at 4 that number pauses the engine 45min
                                        // after a normal run of four small signal-line stop-outs at
                                        // the start of four different trend attempts, which on this
                                        // system is NORMAL, not a signal to sit out. Raised, and
                                        // fast/tiny scratches (< 0.5R held under ~5 bars) no longer
                                        // count toward the streak — they're normal trend-start
                                        // stops, not evidence anything is wrong. Only meaningful
                                        // losses now accumulate the streak.
input int    InpPauseMinutes   = 45;

input group "═══ MANUAL TRADE MONITOR ═══"
// (v19.00) TRUE scanned every open position every tick to guess why a HUMAN opened
// it. He is not trading alongside the bot during a demo session.
input bool   InpWatchManual    = false; // infer WHY you took it, and log it
input bool   InpManageManual   = true;  // also apply breakeven+trail protection to YOUR manual trades (never touches a trade with no SL set)
input double InpManualTrailAtr = 2.0;
input double InpManualEmgAtr    = 2.5;  // emergency stop distance (x ATR) auto-attached to a manual trade that has no SL

input group "═══ TRADINGVIEW SIGNAL FILE (optional) ═══"
input bool   InpReadPineFile   = false;
input string InpPineFileName   = "pine_signals.csv";
// Format, one line per signal:
//   TIMESTAMP,SIDE,ENTRY,SL,TP,REASON
//   2026-07-14 13:35,BUY,4102.50,4099.80,4114.20,Supertrend+pullback+ADX24

//==================== STATE ====================
struct EngCfg
{
   bool            on;
   int             magic;
   string          name;
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf;
   double          risk;
   int             maxHold;
   int             cooldown;
   bool            trails;
};

struct EngStat
{
   int      trades, wins;
   double   netR, netCash;
   int      consec;
   datetime pauseUntil;
   bool     disabled;
   datetime lastExit;
};

EngCfg  Cfg[ENG_COUNT];
EngStat Stat[ENG_COUNT];

// per-engine indicator handles
// E7 SCALP: its own handle set per timeframe. Four timeframes, scanned every bar.
// IMPROVEMENT 1: ELEVEN TIMEFRAMES, not four.
// MT5 gives you M1 M2 M3 M4 M5 M6 M10 M12 M15 M20 M30. A trend that is clean on
// M4 may be invisible on M5 — different bar boundaries, different noise. Scanning
// all of them catches trends the others miss, and the cost is only CPU.
#define SC_TFN 3
ENUM_TIMEFRAMES g_scTF[SC_TFN] = {PERIOD_M1, PERIOD_M3, PERIOD_M5};
// Narrowed from the original 11-timeframe spread (M1-M30) down to just M1/M3/M5.
// The reason: M30 could say SELL while M1 says BUY, putting the account into a
// real, visible drawdown from two engines fighting each other. M1/M3/M5 sit
// close enough together that a disagreement between them is small and short-
// lived — Scalp's own risk-per-trade is already small, so even when M1 and M5
// briefly disagree, neither side is big enough to hurt while the market sorts
// itself out and both timeframes end up agreeing again.
int  g_scAtr[SC_TFN], g_scAdx[SC_TFN], g_scE21[SC_TFN], g_scE50[SC_TFN], g_scSt[SC_TFN], g_scRsi[SC_TFN];

//================================================================
//  (v19.05 BU)  THE FIXED RULER — one root cause, both of his asks
//
//  HIS WORDS, 25 Aug: "on super slow days like today these 0.01s make 50p to
//  3 quid so we need to be able to claim that profit" — and separately, "fix
//  this ea to auto adjust to every pair I put it on".
//  Those are the SAME BUG. This file already diagnosed it at v18.60, in the
//  note inside FloorStopAtBirth: "THE REASON NOTHING SCALES: R IS A CONSTANT."
//  That note fixed exactly ONE threshold (the stop floor) out of thirty-one.
//  Every other point threshold is still a fixed number tuned at M1 gold
//  ATR ~= 1.0.
//
//  THE ARITHMETIC, AT HIS OWN SIZE (0.03 lots, so 1 point = $3):
//     T_BeArmPts        3.00 pts  = $9.00   breakeven never arms
//     T_TrailArmPts     3.00 pts  = $9.00   trail never arms
//     T_GivebackMidPts  3.00 pts  = $9.00   giveback tier never arms
//     T_PartialMinPts   2.00 pts  = $6.00   no partial ever taken
//     T_MinWinnerExitPts 6.00 pts = $18.00  never
//  On a day where the whole move is worth 50p to £3, EVERY ONE of those is
//  out of reach. So on a slow day this EA has NO profit protection at all:
//  every peak rides the whole way back to the stop. That is not a tuning
//  problem, and it is a complete explanation of "peaks summed £110, handed
//  back £73.43 on 43 trades" — £1.71 per trade, on a day where £1.71 was
//  most of what each trade was ever worth.
//
//  THE FIX: measure the thresholds in ATR, not in gold dollars. One scale
//  factor, applied at the point of consumption, so nothing else moves.
//     PtsScale() = live M1 ATR / T_PtsRefAtr
//  Calibrated to CHANGE NOTHING at normal gold volatility: T_PtsRefAtr = 1.00
//  is the M1 gold ATR these numbers were tuned at, so scale = 1.0 and today's
//  behaviour at ATR 1.0 is byte-identical to v19.04.
//     ATR 0.35 (this morning) -> scale 0.35 -> BeArm 1.05 pts = £3.15  REACHABLE
//     ATR 1.00 (normal)       -> scale 1.00 -> unchanged
//     ATR 3.00 (news)         -> scale 3.00 -> BeArm 9.00 pts, not banked at 8%
//
//  AND THIS IS WHY IT SOLVES THE MULTI-PAIR ASK FOR FREE: ATR is quoted in the
//  SYMBOL'S OWN price units. On EURUSD M1 the ATR is about 0.00012, so
//  T_BeArmPts 3.00 becomes 0.00036 — about 3.6 pips. No per-symbol table, no
//  number for him to remember, no conversion to get wrong. The thresholds stop
//  meaning "dollars of gold" and start meaning "multiples of what this
//  instrument actually moves", which is what they were always trying to say.
//
//  WHAT I DELIBERATELY DID NOT SCALE, and why — these are NOT volatility:
//   · T_SlFloorPts / T_SlFloorPtsAbs — already has its own T_SlFloorAtrMult
//     since v18.60. Wrapping it would scale it twice.
//   · T_SpreadCapPts, T_RoundTripLagPts — execution COST. Spread does not
//     halve because the market is quiet; if anything it widens.
//   · T_DayBiasMinPts, T_PremiumRunPts — these two switch aggression ON. On a
//     dead day a scaled version would declare a "premium run" out of a 10-point
//     day. Gates that increase risk stay on the fixed ruler on purpose.
//   · T_M1QuickMinPts — already paired with T_M1QuickMinAtr at its one site.
//   · InpPanelGreenPts / InpPanelGoalPts — those are HIS targets, not
//     thresholds. His goal does not shrink because the market got quiet.
//
//  REVERT: T_PtsAutoScale = false restores v19.04 exactly, everywhere.
//================================================================
input bool   T_PtsAutoScale = true;    // scale point thresholds by live volatility
// ══════════ (v19.14 CD) I CALIBRATED THIS SESSION OFF A WRONG ATR ══════════
// v18.62 already recorded the right figure and I did not read it before building:
// "at 4480 his bars run 2-3 pts, so M1 ATR is ~1.8" -- not the 1.0 I assumed.
// Derived independently from his own 26 Aug panel: 1487 pts of travel over 1012
// M1 bars = 1.47 pts average bar range. So M1 ATR was ~1.47, not the 0.35 I told
// him. One noise band is 0.30 + 0.6 x 1.47 = 1.18 pts = GBP0.87 at 0.01 lots,
// not GBP0.37. Everything I priced in bands this session was out by 2.3x:
//    PtsScale at T_PtsRefAtr=1.00  -> x1.47, INFLATING every threshold 47%,
//                                     the exact opposite of the intended cut
//    TRADE-LOCK arm at 3.0 bands   -> GBP2.60 against a GBP1.62 average peak,
//                                     so it would have NEVER ARMED
//    INFANT CUT at 1.2 bands       -> GBP1.04 against a simulated optimum of 0.44
// Two of the three mechanisms I built today were dead on arrival and the third
// pointed backwards. Corrected below, each against a measured number.
input double T_PtsRefAtr    = 1.80;    // v18.62 measured this at gold ~4480. PtsScale on 26 Aug becomes x0.82 -- a mild cut, as intended.
input double T_PtsScaleMin  = 0.30;    // never shrink a threshold below this multiple
input double T_PtsScaleMax  = 3.50;    // never inflate a threshold above this multiple

// Buf() is defined further down the file (~line 6004). Forward declaration so
// this helper can sit above its first consumer at ~line 4541. Same pattern the
// file already uses for ScalpST() in LotFor().
double Buf(int h, int b, int s);

double PtsScale()
{
   if(!T_PtsAutoScale || T_PtsRefAtr <= 0.0) return 1.0;
   int fk = -1;
   for(int i = 0; i < SC_TFN; i++) if(g_scTF[i] == PERIOD_M1) { fk = i; break; }
   if(fk < 0) return 1.0;
   double a = Buf(g_scAtr[fk], 0, 1);
   // A bad or not-yet-ready ATR read must size the threshold as it was, never
   // to zero -- a zero threshold would arm every mechanism on every tick.
   if(a <= 0.0) return 1.0;
   double s = a / T_PtsRefAtr;
   return MathMax(T_PtsScaleMin, MathMin(T_PtsScaleMax, s));
}
// PS = "point, scaled". Wrapper kept deliberately short because it appears at
// ~40 call sites and long names there would hurt readability of the comparisons.
double PS(double pts) { return pts * PtsScale(); }

// (v19.06 BV) THE PER-TRADE LOCK -- inputs and per-ticket peak registry. The two
// functions that use them (TradePeakCash, TradeLockCheck) sit just above
// ManagePosition, because they need CloseTagged() and NoiseFloorPts(). Full
// reasoning is in the block comment there.
// (v19.07 BW) HARM AUDIT OF MY OWN v19.06, DONE BEFORE HE TESTED IT.
// A FLAT one-band give-back would have cost more than it saved. Measured on his
// 26 Aug tickets: 34 trades closed at GBP2 or better, worth +119.44 together.
// EVERY ONE of them passed up through the GBP0.75 arm level on its way. One band
// is 0.50 points -- and a 0.5pt retrace inside a 5pt M1 gold run is completely
// ordinary. So most of those 34 would have banked about GBP0.60 instead:
//     +20.40 instead of +119.44  =  harm -99.04
// against a +88.20 gain on the SL-HIT pool. NET NEGATIVE. I shipped that.
//
// THE FIX: the give-back allowance is a FRACTION OF THE PEAK, floored at one
// band. So the floor rises with the peak instead of strangling the runner:
//     peak 1.10 -> banks 0.66      peak 4.00 -> banks 2.40
//     peak 1.62 -> banks 0.97      peak 7.54 -> banks 4.52
//     peak 2.50 -> banks 1.50      peak 19.10 -> banks 11.46
// Worst case if ALL 34 big trades retrace the full 40%: harm -47.78. Realistic
// (a third of them): about -15.93, against +63.57 on the SL-HIT pool. NET +47.64
// and the big trades survive, which is the thing he actually asked for.
//
// NOTE WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a hold vote. There is no opinion here about
// whether the trade "deserves" more room -- the floor is monotone arithmetic on
// the peak. Adding a vote is what let the peaks go in the first place.
input bool   T_TradeLock          = true;
input double T_TradeLockArmBands  = 1.0;   // (v19.14 CD) 3.0 -> 1.0. At the real band of GBP0.87, 3 bands = GBP2.60 and the average peak is GBP1.62, so it could never arm. 1 band = GBP0.87 and does.
input double T_TradeLockGivePct   = 0.40;  // give back this share of the peak...
input double T_TradeLockGiveBands = 1.0;   // ...but never less than this many bands
input double T_TradeLockMinCash   = 0.50;  // and never arm below this much real money

// ══════════════ (v19.10 BZ) THE INFANT CUT ══════════════
// I HAD THE PRIORITY BACKWARDS ALL SESSION. I built TRADE-LOCK to recover peak
// profit, on the assumption that the losers had been in profit and gave it back.
// Then I reconstructed a price path from his own 275 tickets, calibrated the
// look-forward window until my MFE total matched the EA's own GBP293.26 figure
// (it matched at FOUR MINUTES -- that is the real hold time), and measured each
// trade's excursion. The split:
//
//   153 of 188 losers NEVER got even GBP0.30 into profit   -252.27   peak pool GBP2.02
//    11 losers reached 0.30-0.75                            -20.69   peak pool GBP6.36
//     6 losers reached 0.75-1.50                            -10.07   peak pool GBP7.20
//    18 losers reached 1.50+                                -41.95   peak pool GBP79.56
//
// 77% of the loss is trades that were WRONG IMMEDIATELY. There is no peak to
// recover on them. TRADE-LOCK addresses the bottom row and the winners' give-back;
// it cannot touch the top row, which is four times bigger.
//
// AND THE TOP ROW HAS AN ALMOST FREE FIX, because the two populations separate
// harder than anything else in this data. Adverse excursion, own-exit prices
// excluded so the outcome cannot leak into the measurement:
//        first  60s : winners median GBP0.00   losers median GBP1.12
//        first 120s : winners median GBP0.00   losers median GBP1.47
//        first 300s : winners median GBP0.00   losers median GBP2.00
// A winner in this system does not go against you. AT ALL. The median winner's
// worst moment is zero at every window tested.
//
// SIMULATED ON HIS TICKETS, cutting at GBP0.44 adverse inside 120s:
//        winner profit destroyed  -1.05
//        loser damage prevented  +74.62
//        ratio 71 : 1, and the day moves from -159.79 to -86.22 on the 124
//        trades there was enough price evidence to judge.
//
// THIS IS SWEENEY'S MAE FRAMEWORK (Maximum Adverse Excursion, Wiley 1996): plot
// each trade's worst excursion against its outcome, and the level where the winner
// and loser clouds separate IS the stop. Standard caveat from that literature and
// I will repeat it rather than bury it: under ~50 trades the threshold is not
// meaningful, and it must be recalculated as conditions change. I have 124 judged
// trades from ONE day. That is enough to act on and not enough to trust forever.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT JUST A TIGHTER STOP LOSS. It is time-boxed. It only applies in
// the first T_InfantSecs, and only while the trade has NEVER been meaningfully
// green. Once a trade works, it stops being an infant and TRADE-LOCK owns it --
// so a trade that goes +GBP1 and then dips is NOT cut here. A permanently tight
// stop would kill the runners; this cannot, because a runner is never an infant
// for long. The floor of T_InfantSpreadMult spreads exists so the cut can never
// be triggered by the bid/ask itself, which is what makes a small stop a lottery.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_InfantCut = false.
input bool   T_InfantCut          = true;
input int    T_InfantSecs         = 120;   // only during the trade's first N seconds
input double T_InfantBands        = 0.5;   // (v19.14 CD) 1.2 -> 0.5. At the real band this is GBP0.43, which is the simulated optimum (0.44) and matches the 2-spread floor from the other side. 1.2 bands was GBP1.04 -- 2.4x too wide.
input double T_InfantSpreadMult   = 2.0;   // ...but never tighter than this many spreads
input double T_InfantGreenFrac    = 0.50;  // peak above this share of the cut level = "it worked"

// ══════════════ (v19.17 CG) PROVE-THEN-PRESS ══════════════
// HIS PROPOSAL, TESTED, AND WHAT IT TURNED INTO.
// He asked to enter 0.05 on a high-quality setup -- far from the DEMA, or a
// liquidity sweep that is not a fakeout. I measured both against his 275 tickets.
//
// "FAR FROM THE MEAN" AS A SIZE-UP TRIGGER LOSES, and loses worst exactly where he
// wants to press. Distance of entry from the prior hour's mean, in ATR:
//   BETTING ON REVERSION (sell above the mean / buy below -- his idea):
//     <0.5 ATR  n=14  -0.61/trade      1.5-3.0 ATR  n=16  -2.41/trade  hit 12.5%
//     0.5-1.5   n=26  -0.58/trade      >3.0 ATR     n=24  -0.86/trade
//   BETTING ON CONTINUATION (the mirror):
//     <0.5 ATR  n=14  -0.01/trade  hit 57.1%   <-- the best hit rate in the book
//     >3.0 ATR  n=86  -0.18/trade  hit 36.0%
// His exact proposal -- far from the mean, reverting, at 0.05 lots -- is 32 trades
// for -151.31. It would have almost doubled the day's loss from a tenth of the
// trades. Every bucket of the reversion side loses; the continuation side is the
// one that holds up. Fading distance from the mean is not the edge.
//
// AND "NOT A FAKEOUT" CANNOT BE KNOWN AT ENTRY. It can be known SIXTY SECONDS
// LATER, and the separation is the largest single number in this entire dataset:
//     went adverse 0.60+ in the first 60s : 48 trades  -86.49   hit  6.2%
//     did NOT go adverse in the first 60s : 23 trades  +49.92   hit 82.6%
// 82.6% against 6.2%. That is his fakeout filter. It just does not live at entry.
//
// SO THE MECHANISM IS HIS IDEA MOVED IN TIME. Enter every signal SMALL. Sixty
// seconds later the market has told you which kind of trade it is. Press the ones
// that never went against you up to full size; the infant cut scratches the rest
// for about GBP0.44. Rebuilt on his own tickets:
//     press proven trades to 0.02  ->  -74.39
//     press proven trades to 0.03  ->  -32.98
//     press proven trades to 0.05  ->  +49.84
// (199 of 275 had no price sample inside 60s to judge, and are left exactly as they
// happened, contributing -122.24. On live ticks every trade is judgeable, so the
// real figure should be better than +49.84 -- but I am not going to claim that
// number, because I cannot measure it from 2-minute-resolution ticket data.)
//
// WHY THIS BEATS SIZING AT ENTRY. Every entry filter I have tested on this book --
// distance from the mean, extremity of range, efficiency, session -- separates
// weakly or not at all. The one thing that separates violently is what price does
// in the first minute. Entry selection is guesswork here; the first minute is
// evidence. Risk stays small on the guess and goes large on the evidence.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ProvePress = false.
input bool   T_ProvePress         = true;
input int    T_ProveSecs          = 60;    // judge the trade at this age
input double T_ProveMaxAdverse    = 0.30;  // it must never have been this much down (account ccy, at entry size)
input double T_ProveTargetLots    = 0.05;  // press a proven trade up to this
input int    T_ProveMaxPerDay     = 12;    // ...at most this many times a day

// (v19.18 CH) MARGIN REALITY CHECK. The single most likely reason PROVE-PRESS
// never fires is that the account cannot afford the position, and MarginOkFor()
// would refuse it silently in the middle of a session. Compute it at startup and
// say it plainly, once, before any money is at risk.
void ProvePressCapacityWarning()
{
   if(!T_ProvePress) return;
   double bal = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_BALANCE);
   double need = 0.0;
   if(!OrderCalcMargin(ORDER_TYPE_BUY, _Symbol, T_ProveTargetLots,
                       SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK), need) || need <= 0.0)
      return;
   int canHold = (int)MathFloor(bal / need);
   int wantHold = (int)MathFloor(T_MaxDirLots / MathMax(T_ProveTargetLots, 0.01));
   PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS CAPACITY] balance %.2f, margin per %.2f lot = %.2f, leverage 1:%d. "
               "You can hold %d pressed position(s) at once; the direction cap allows %d.",
               bal, T_ProveTargetLots, need, (int)AccountInfoInteger(ACCOUNT_LEVERAGE),
               canHold, wantHold);
   if(canHold < 1)
      PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS CAPACITY] *** IT CANNOT FIRE AT ALL. *** One %.2f lot position needs "
                  "%.2f and the balance is %.2f. Every press will be refused on margin. Either lower "
                  "T_ProveTargetLots to something the account can carry, or fund it. This is "
                  "arithmetic, not a setting.", T_ProveTargetLots, need, bal);
   else if(canHold < 6)
      PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS CAPACITY] The GBP500/week arithmetic assumes about 12 presses a day at "
                  "%.2f lots. At %d concurrent the realistic ceiling is nearer GBP%.0f/day, not GBP93. "
                  "Margin is the binding constraint here, not the code.",
                  T_ProveTargetLots, canHold, canHold * 7.75 * 2.0);
}

ulong  g_tlTk[128];
double g_tlPk[128];
int    g_tlN = 0;


int  g_scMacd[SC_TFN];   // per-timeframe MACD handle — fix #49, so TREND's exit logic can read
                          // momentum on the position's OWN timeframe instead of always M5's.
int  g_scDema[SC_TFN];   // per-scalp-timeframe DEMA handle (trend filter, matches Pine)
datetime g_scBar[SC_TFN];
int      g_scLastDir[SC_TFN];   // last Supertrend direction per TF, to detect a flip
double   g_scStV[SC_TFN];
int      g_scStD[SC_TFN];
datetime g_scStBar[SC_TFN];

int g_hH1Ema    = INVALID_HANDLE;   // H1 EMA50 — the house bias reference
int g_hH1Adx    = INVALID_HANDLE;   // (v16.24) H1 ADX14 — HTF regime backdrop for the fast flips
int hST[ENG_COUNT], hAtr[ENG_COUNT], hAdx[ENG_COUNT], hRsi[ENG_COUNT];
int hEma21[ENG_COUNT], hEma50[ENG_COUNT], hEma200[ENG_COUNT], hMacd[ENG_COUNT];
// DEMA — the trend filter. A signal only counts on the right side of it. Added to
// match the live TradingView script exactly: buy needs price ABOVE the DEMA, sell
// BELOW. This one line is what keeps both systems out of counter-trend flips.
int hDema[ENG_COUNT];

double g_minStop = 0, g_tickVal = 0, g_tickSz = 0, g_lotStep = 0, g_lotMin = 0;
double g_dayStart = 0, g_dayPnl = 0;
int    g_consecWin[ENG_COUNT];   // (v13 #4) consecutive-win counter per engine — win ladder sizing
double g_hourR[24];              // (v14 #1) realized R by GMT hour
int    g_hourN[24];              // (v14 #1) trade count by GMT hour
int    g_tfLossStreak[SC_TFN];   // (v14 #9) per-timeframe consecutive real losses
datetime g_tfRestUntil[SC_TFN];  // (v14 #9) rest this timeframe until this time
int    g_lastDay = -1;
bool   g_halt = false, g_locked = false;

double g_scPushMult = 1.0;   // set by Engine_Scalp just before Open(), read by LotFor
int    g_lastPush = 0;       // conditions of the signal currently being opened
int    g_lastConfirms = 0;   // — so the autopsy can freeze them
// BUG FIX #55: SnapshotEntry() (called from Open()) used to read the
// extension-at-entry from the FIXED engine handles (hAtr[E_TREND]/
// hEma21[E_TREND]/Cfg[E_TREND].tf — all pinned to M5), even for a TREND
// entry actually firing on M1 or M3. That fed the autopsy's "wasChasing"
// pattern-learning wrong data for two of TREND's three timeframes. Same
// channel pattern as g_lastPush/g_lastConfirms above: set immediately
// before the Open() call, consumed inside it. -1 = not a TREND entry,
// use the engine's fixed handle as before.
int    g_lastEntryK = -1;

// ERROR FIX: g_scTFofPos was a SINGLE global holding "which timeframe the scalp
// came from". With SC_MaxOpen > 1 there are now up to three scalps live at once,
// each from a DIFFERENT timeframe — and they were all being managed as if they
// came from whichever one opened last. Every fade-exit and every trail decision
// on the older scalps was reading the wrong timeframe's data. Per-ticket now.
int    g_scTFofPos = 4;
ulong  g_scTfTk[64];
int    g_scTfK[64];
int    g_scTfN = 0;

// (v17.53 REAL BUG, all seven registries) Each registry used
// PositionSelectByTicket() as its "is this slot's ticket still open?" liveness
// test and NONE of them put the selection back. MQL5 keeps one globally
// selected position, so after the scan the caller's next PositionGetDouble /
// PositionGetInteger call reads WHATEVER TICKET THE SCAN LANDED ON — not the
// ticket being managed. It only runs when a ticket is NEW to that table, i.e.
// on the FIRST management tick of every trade, which is exactly the tick that
// decides entry-side state (peak R, clock, partial/pyramid flags). One shared
// helper now does the test and always restores the caller's selection.
bool SlotDead(ulong slotTk, ulong keepTk)
{
   bool dead = (slotTk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(slotTk));
   PositionSelectByTicket(keepTk);
   return dead;
}

void StoreScalpTF(ulong tk, int k)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++) if(g_scTfTk[i] == tk) { g_scTfK[i] = k; return; }
   // (v17.18) Identical to the TREND registry bug v16.47 fixed. SCALP is
   // disabled today so this is inert, but it is a live trap if it is ever
   // re-enabled — fixed now rather than left for a future session to rediscover.
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_scTfTk[i], tk))
      { g_scTfTk[i] = tk; g_scTfK[i] = k; return; }
}
int ScalpTFOf(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++) if(g_scTfTk[i] == tk) return g_scTfK[i];
   return SC_TFN - 1;   // sane default: the slowest of the 3 (M5)
}

// TREND now scans the SAME three timeframes as SCALP (M1/M3/M5) instead of only
// M5, so it needs the same per-ticket timeframe memory — a long taken on the M1
// flip and a short taken on the M5 flip are DIFFERENT trades on different clocks
// and must be managed with their own timeframe's Supertrend/ATR, not both forced
// through M5's. Same mechanism as ScalpTFOf, separate namespace so a TREND
// ticket and a SCALP ticket sharing a number (they never do, but to be safe)
// never collide.
ulong  g_trTfTk[64];
int    g_trTfK[64];
int    g_trTfN = 0;
// (v16.47 RING-BUFFER EVICTION FIX — SEVERE) The old body wrapped g_trTfN back
// to 0 once 64 tickets had been registered and overwrote slot 0 blindly. At
// ~130 trades/day the ring wraps about twice a day, so a LONG-LIVED position
// (an M15 APEX trade can be open 2.5 days) loses its registration while still
// open. TrendTFOf() then falls through to its M5 default and the trade is
// managed on the WRONG CLOCK for the rest of its life: M5 bar counts, M5
// grace, and — since v16.46 — the wrong RangeScalpClockMult. Now a slot is
// only reused if its ticket is no longer an open position, so open trades can
// never be evicted. If all 64 slots hold live positions the registration is
// refused and logged rather than silently corrupting an existing one.
void StoreTrendTF(ulong tk, int k)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++) if(g_trTfTk[i] == tk) { g_trTfK[i] = k; return; }
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++)
   {
      if(SlotDead(g_trTfTk[i], tk))
      {
         g_trTfTk[i] = tk; g_trTfK[i] = k;
         return;
      }
   }
   PrintFormat("[TREND] WARNING: clock registry full (64 live positions) — ticket %I64u will be managed on the default clock. Investigate.", tk);
}
// (v16.49) Cached ATR(10) handles for the exhaustion bank. v16.32 fixed a handle
// LEAK where per-timeframe handles were created and never released — exhausted
// handles look exactly like "the EA stopped taking signals", so the exhaustion
// check must never call iATR() on a tick path. One handle per clock, created once.
ENUM_TIMEFRAMES g_exTf[8];
int             g_exH[8];
int             g_exN = 0;
int ExhaustAtrHandle(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_exN; i++) if(g_exTf[i] == tf) return g_exH[i];
   if(g_exN >= 8) return INVALID_HANDLE;
   int h = iATR(_Symbol, tf, T_StAtrLen);
   if(h == INVALID_HANDLE) return INVALID_HANDLE;
   g_exTf[g_exN] = tf; g_exH[g_exN] = h; g_exN++;
   return h;
}

// (v16.51) Recent SIGNAL PRICES per clock, for the signal-cluster range detector.
// Deliberately separate from the bar-based regime engine: this measures where the
// system's own entries land, which is what determines flip-to-flip yield.
#define SIG_BAND_MAX 8
double g_sigPx[SC_TFN][SIG_BAND_MAX];
int    g_sigN[SC_TFN];
// (v16.55) Wipe a clock's signal ring at the start of a new session day so a
// cluster detected late last night cannot influence this morning's decisions.
datetime g_sigDay[SC_TFN];
void ResetSignalRingIfNewDay(int k)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return;
   MqlDateTime dt; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), dt);
   datetime day = StringToTime(StringFormat("%04d.%02d.%02d", dt.year, dt.mon, dt.day));
   if(g_sigDay[k] == day) return;
   g_sigDay[k] = day;
   for(int i = 0; i < SIG_BAND_MAX; i++) g_sigPx[k][i] = 0.0;
   g_sigN[k] = 0;
}

void RecordSignalPrice(int k, double px)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN || px <= 0.0) return;
   ResetSignalRingIfNewDay(k);
   for(int i = SIG_BAND_MAX-1; i > 0; i--) g_sigPx[k][i] = g_sigPx[k][i-1];
   g_sigPx[k][0] = px;
   if(g_sigN[k] < SIG_BAND_MAX) g_sigN[k]++;
}
// True when the last T_BandLookback signal prices sit inside a band narrower than
// T_BandAtrMult x ATR(10). Needs a full lookback before it will claim anything.
bool SignalsClustered(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_SignalBandRange || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return false;
   int n = MathMin(T_BandLookback, g_sigN[k]);
   if(n < T_BandLookback || n < 2) return false;
   double hi = g_sigPx[k][0], lo = g_sigPx[k][0];
   for(int i = 1; i < n; i++)
   {
      if(g_sigPx[k][i] <= 0.0) return false;
      if(g_sigPx[k][i] > hi) hi = g_sigPx[k][i];
      if(g_sigPx[k][i] < lo) lo = g_sigPx[k][i];
   }
   double atrB[1];
   int hB = ExhaustAtrHandle(tf);
   if(hB == INVALID_HANDLE || CopyBuffer(hB, 0, 1, 1, atrB) != 1 || atrB[0] <= 0.0) return false;
   return ((hi - lo) < atrB[0] * T_BandAtrMult);
}

// (v18.58) The same read as SignalsClustered() but banded in POINTS, and asked of
// EVERY clock rather than one. Any clock whose recent signals are landing on top of
// each other means flip-to-flip cannot pay on this tape, whichever clock fired.
// Declared here, above LotFor(), because MQL5 resolves in source order.
// Returns false until a clock has a full T_BandLookback ring, so it never guesses
// from one or two signals.
bool ClusterRangeNow()
{
   // ═══ (v18.60) HIS CORRECTION, AND HE IS RIGHT ═══
   // v18.58 banded this in FIXED POINTS (6.0) and I argued against ATR because ATR
   // expands in a trend. Both are wrong for the same reason: his trades run from
   // 0.1 points to 40 points, so ANY absolute band is calibrated to one regime.
   //   quiet Asia, ATR ~0.4 : a real 6-pt leg gets called a range -> sized to 0.01
   //   news, ATR ~4.0       : a genuine 8-pt chop box is never caught
   // THE SCALE-FREE READ. Compare the SPREAD of the recent signal prices with the
   // PATH price travelled between them. Both are in points, so the units cancel
   // and the ratio is identical at 0.1 points and at 40 points:
   //     trend : signals march one way, spread == path        -> ratio ~1.0
   //     chop  : signals double back, spread << path          -> ratio small
   // VERIFIED against his own two live sets, with no tuning:
   //   18 Aug chop hour, signals ~4371/4370/4366.5/4363.5/4368/4362.8
   //          spread 8.2, path 17.2 -> ratio 0.48  -> RANGE   ✓
   //   18 Aug down leg, six sells marching 4405 -> 4352
   //          spread 53, path ~53   -> ratio ~1.00 -> TREND    ✓
   // A 0.60 threshold sits midway with a wide margin on both sides, and it needs
   // no ATR, no point value and no recalibration when volatility changes.
   // REVERT: T_RangeClusterRatio = 0 falls back to the v18.58 fixed-point band.
   // ═══ (v18.60b) DEFECT IN MY OWN v18.60 EDIT, CAUGHT BY THE PRE-SHIP CHECK ═══
   // The ratio has a HARD MATHEMATICAL FLOOR of 1/(n-1). With T_BandLookback = 3
   // that floor is 0.500, so the whole discriminator was squeezed into [0.50, 1.00]
   // and a 0.60 threshold had almost no room -- his chop hour computed to EXACTLY
   // 0.600 on a 3-signal window, which is not < 0.600, so it would have failed on
   // the very tape it was built from. Same "threshold arithmetically out of reach"
   // class I have been cataloguing, this time authored by me two edits ago.
   // FIX: this detector gets its own lookback of 5, so the floor drops to 0.250 and
   // 0.60 sits mid-range with margin on both sides. Recomputed on his live sets:
   //     chop hour, 5 signals : spread 7.2 / path 16.2 = 0.444 -> RANGE ✓
   //     down leg,  5 sells   : spread == path          = 1.000 -> TREND ✓
   // HONEST COST: it now needs 5 signals rather than 3 before it may act, so the
   // first ~5 trades of a range are still paid at full size instead of the first 3.
   // On his 15-flip chop hour that is 10 trimmed instead of 12.
   // T_BandLookback is left alone because SignalsClustered still reads it.
   if(!T_RangeClusterTrim) return false;
   int want = MathMax(3, T_RangeClusterSigs);
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      int n = MathMin(want, g_sigN[k]);
      if(n < want || n < 2) continue;
      // (v18.67) index 0 was never validated -- only i>=1 was. A zeroed newest
      // slot (fresh session, ring reset mid-pass) would seed hi=lo=0 and produce a
      // meaningless ratio that could read as a range and trim size for no reason.
      if(g_sigPx[k][0] <= 0.0) continue;
      double hi = g_sigPx[k][0], lo = g_sigPx[k][0], path = 0.0;
      bool   ok = true;
      for(int i = 1; i < n; i++)
      {
         if(g_sigPx[k][i] <= 0.0) { ok = false; break; }
         if(g_sigPx[k][i] > hi) hi = g_sigPx[k][i];
         if(g_sigPx[k][i] < lo) lo = g_sigPx[k][i];
         path += MathAbs(g_sigPx[k][i-1] - g_sigPx[k][i]);   // index 0 is newest, so i and i-1 are adjacent in time
      }
      if(!ok) continue;
      if(T_RangeClusterRatio > 0.0)
      {
         if(path > 0.0 && ((hi - lo) / path) < T_RangeClusterRatio) return true;
      }
      else if(T_RangeClusterBandPts > 0.0 && (hi - lo) < T_RangeClusterBandPts)
         return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// (v16.53) Median of the recent realised legs on this clock, in PRICE points,
// taken from the signal-price ring recorded in v16.51. Returns 0.0 when there is
// not enough history to be honest about it, and every caller must treat 0.0 as
// "no opinion" rather than as a target.
double MedianLegPoints(int k)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return 0.0;
   // (v16.55 STALENESS FIX) v16.53 averaged the last 8 legs regardless of AGE, so
   // a median could mix 3pt Asian swings with 14pt London ones and be useful for
   // neither. Legs older than the window below are ignored.
   int n = g_sigN[k];
   if(n < T_LegMedianMin + 1) return 0.0;
   double legs[SIG_BAND_MAX];
   int m = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < n-1 && m < SIG_BAND_MAX; i++)
   {
      if(g_sigPx[k][i] <= 0.0 || g_sigPx[k][i+1] <= 0.0) break;
      double lg = MathAbs(g_sigPx[k][i] - g_sigPx[k][i+1]);
      if(lg < PS(T_LegNoisePts)) continue;          // (v16.56) noise, not a leg — see T_LegNoisePts
      legs[m++] = lg;
   }
   if(m < T_LegMedianMin) return 0.0;
   for(int a = 0; a < m-1; a++)             // small array, simple sort
      for(int b = a+1; b < m; b++)
         if(legs[b] < legs[a]) { double t = legs[a]; legs[a] = legs[b]; legs[b] = t; }
   return (m % 2 == 1) ? legs[m/2] : (legs[m/2 - 1] + legs[m/2]) * 0.5;
}

// (v16.54) GAP DETECTION. Compares the last closed bar's open against the prior
// bar's close on the given clock. Returns the signed gap in price terms, 0.0 when
// there is none worth acting on. Deliberately uses ATR so it scales with the
// instrument's own volatility instead of a hardcoded point count.
double GapPoints(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_GapGuard) return 0.0;
   double atrG[1];
   int hG = ExhaustAtrHandle(tf);
   if(hG == INVALID_HANDLE || CopyBuffer(hG, 0, 1, 1, atrG) != 1 || atrG[0] <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double op1 = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double cl2 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 2);
   if(op1 <= 0.0 || cl2 <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double g = op1 - cl2;
   if(MathAbs(g) < atrG[0] * T_GapAtrMult) return 0.0;
   return g;
}

// True while a clock is still inside its post-gap cooldown. Post-gap price
// discovery is the worst moment to open anything: spreads widen, the Supertrend
// is computed off a discontinuity, and the first flip after a gap is noise.
datetime g_gapUntil[SC_TFN];
bool GapCooling(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_GapGuard || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return false;
   double g = GapPoints(tf);
   if(g != 0.0)
   {
      datetime until = TimeCurrent() + (datetime)(T_GapCoolBars * PeriodSeconds(tf));
      if(until > g_gapUntil[k])
      {
         g_gapUntil[k] = until;
         PrintFormat("[GAP] %s gapped %.2f pts (open %.2f vs prior close %.2f). Suppressing new entries for %d bars — a stop cannot protect against a gap and the first flip after one is noise.",
                     EnumToString(tf), g, iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1), iClose(_Symbol, tf, 2), T_GapCoolBars);
      }
   }
   return (TimeCurrent() < g_gapUntil[k]);
}

// (v16.61) Is this leg still making new ground in the trade's direction? Pure
// price evidence — no ADX, no regime, no indicator that can lag or misread.
bool LegStillExtending(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   if(!T_LegStillExtending) return false;
   // ═══ (v18.59) WHY THIS FUNCTION DISABLED EVERY PEAK BANK ═══
   // HIS LIVE REPORT: "we closed far off the peak." This is the cause.
   // The old body looped i = 1..T_ExtendLookback (6) and returned true if ANY of
   // six consecutive bar-pairs made a new extreme our way. On roughly coin-flip
   // bar-to-bar odds that is 1 - 0.5^6 = 98.4% TRUE, so the negation that every
   // peak bank is gated on -- !LegStillExtending() -- was true about 1.6% of the
   // time. PEAK-BANK, MOM-STALL and RANGE-SCALP could not fire. That also
   // explains why eighteen versions of threshold tuning "moved nothing": the
   // thresholds sat behind a gate that was almost never open, so their values
   // never mattered. Only the 8.0 pt target and the hard lock were banking, which
   // is why his winners cluster on a fixed number instead of around peaks.
   //
   // THE HANDOVER SAID FIX THIS LAST because it "wakes six exits simultaneously".
   // That was wrong and it is checkable: T_LegTarget, T_SpikePeakBank,
   // T_ExhaustBank and T_ChopStallBank are all false by default, so four of the
   // six are off at their own input. This wakes THREE, two of which are the peak
   // banks he is asking for.
   //
   // INTENT PRESERVED. v16.61 added this to stop an opinion clipping a LIVE
   // runner. That intent is about NOW, not about the last six bars: a leg that is
   // still extending made a new extreme on its LAST closed bar. A leg that has
   // rolled over has not, and the lock should release immediately -- that is the
   // whole point of a peak bank. Bar 1 only. ~50% on a random walk, higher in a
   // real trend (so runners keep their protection), false at a stall (so the peak
   // banks can finally act).
   // ARITHMETIC: at 0.03 and R ~2.8 pts, MOM-STALL fires from 2.5R = 7 pts. A leg
   // that peaks at 10 and gives back to the 8.0 target donates 2 pts = GBP4.44
   // each time; on his six winners yesterday that is the difference between a
   // fixed goal and an actual peak.
   // REVERT: T_LegExtendNowOnly = false restores the 6-bar behaviour exactly.
   int n = T_LegExtendNowOnly ? 1 : MathMax(1, T_ExtendLookback);
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      if(dir > 0 && iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i) > iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i + 1)) return true;
      if(dir < 0 && iLow(_Symbol, tf, i)  < iLow(_Symbol, tf, i + 1))  return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// (v16.63) DEFERRED CHASE. A flip that arrives with price already extended past
// the signal line is no longer thrown away — it is parked here and re-checked.
datetime g_earlyFlipBar[SC_TFN];   // (v16.85) once-per-bar debounce, see the fix note above
int      g_defDir[SC_TFN];
double   g_defStV[SC_TFN];
datetime g_defAt[SC_TFN];
// (v17.79) THIS IS WHY EVERYTHING IS BEING SKIPPED, and it is a bug I introduced
// in v17.74. The clock stamp g_defAt is what makes a parked flip EXPIRE and get
// taken at market after T_DeferMaxBars. In v17.74 I made the entry gates call
// DeferFlip() on every failed tick -- and this function re-stamped g_defAt each
// time. So the countdown restarted on every single tick: the flip stayed parked
// forever, the expiry never arrived, and the signal was never taken. From the
// outside that looks exactly like what he is seeing -- "it is skipping
// everything" -- with the log cheerfully saying DEFERRED, not rejected each time.
// FIX: the stamp is only set when a flip is FIRST parked. Re-arming the same
// direction on the same clock refreshes the line level but leaves the original
// clock running, so the 2-bar expiry actually fires and the trade gets taken.
void DeferFlip(int k, int dir, double stV)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return;
   bool alreadyParked = (g_defDir[k] == dir && g_defAt[k] > 0);
   g_defDir[k] = dir;
   g_defStV[k] = stV;
   if(!alreadyParked) g_defAt[k] = TimeCurrent();   // start the clock ONCE
}
void ClearDefer(int k) { if(k >= 0 && k < SC_TFN) { g_defDir[k] = 0; g_defStV[k] = 0.0; g_defAt[k] = 0; } }
// Returns true when a parked flip should now be taken: either price has pulled
// back within the band, or the wait has expired (take it anyway — never miss the
// trend). Returns false while still waiting.
bool DeferReady(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, double stV, double atr, double px, bool &expired)
{
   expired = false;
   if(!T_DeferChase || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return false;
   if(g_defDir[k] != dir) return false;
   int bars = (int)((TimeCurrent() - g_defAt[k]) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(tf), 1));
   // (v17.82, his idea and it is a good one: "could we not open the second a new
   // candle fires, so there is more profit in it?") EXPIRY NOW FILLS AT A BAR OPEN.
   // Before this, an expired defer was taken at market on whatever tick happened
   // to be next -- which on a spike is the top of the spike, the exact fill he
   // keeps photographing. A fill in the first seconds of a NEW bar is, by
   // definition, near that bar open: the previous candle has finished extending
   // and the new one has not yet run. Same trade, same bar count of patience,
   // materially better price on precisely the fast candles where it matters.
   // If the first-seconds window is missed, it simply waits for the next bar open
   // rather than chasing mid-candle, so it can never fill at a spike tip again.
   if(bars >= T_DeferMaxBars)
   {
      // ════════ (v18.25) SEVERE — THE DEFER COULD NEVER EXPIRE ════════
      // HIS LOG, repeating for an hour with no entry:
      //   DEFER RE-ARM on PERIOD_M1 — a parked DOWN flip is still live...
      //   ANCHOR (measure only) SELL on PERIOD_M1 — filling 3.17 pts past the line
      //   DEFER RE-ARM ... 3.24 ... DEFER RE-ARM ... 3.37 ... (forever)
      // v18.18 made the parked flip reachable again. It still never RESOLVED,
      // because expiry additionally demanded that the evaluating tick land within
      // T_DeferOpenSecs (12s) of a new bar open. The TREND loop does not sample
      // that window reliably -- so bars >= T_DeferMaxBars was true, and the
      // function returned false anyway, every time, for as long as the flip lived.
      // Net effect: the entry was postponed indefinitely while the leg ran out.
      // This is the whole of "we enter after it already happened" and it is also
      // why the NEXT reversal was missed: the stale park kept re-arming instead of
      // letting a fresh flip through.
      // FIX: the bar-open window is now a PREFERENCE with a deadline. Past
      // T_DeferHardBars the trade is taken at market on the next evaluation, no
      // window required. A defer can no longer outlive its own purpose.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_DeferHardBars = 999 restores the old wait-forever behaviour.
      datetime bt = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
      long into = (bt > 0) ? (long)(TimeCurrent() - bt) : 0;
      if(bt > 0 && into <= (long)T_DeferOpenSecs) { expired = true; return true; }
      // ══════ (v18.27) THE HARD EXPIRY WAS FILLING AT THE PEAK ══════
      // His measured fills, from the ANCHOR log: 1.93, 1.97, 2.06, 2.40 x ATR
      // past the signal line. Those are the peak entries, and v18.25's hard
      // expiry is what produced them: it took the trade AT MARKET the moment the
      // deadline passed, wherever price happened to be -- which after a big candle
      // is the top of that candle. Then a 4 pt stop sits under a price that was
      // already 2 ATR extended, so a normal pullback takes the whole stop.
      // That is "we keep buying at peaks and selling at lows and the SL takes 20
      // quid" in one mechanism.
      //
      // The resolution of "take every signal" vs "never enter at a peak" is that
      // they are not actually in conflict: the signal stays PARKED and live, it is
      // the PRICE that is refused. The deadline now only converts to a market fill
      // when extension has decayed to something tradeable (T_EntryMaxAtr). While
      // price is still stretched, the park is held and the re-arm keeps re-offering
      // it, so the fill happens on the pullback instead of the peak. If price never
      // comes back, we declined a chase -- which is the correct outcome, not a lost
      // signal, because the entry that would have been taken there is precisely the
      // one that loses 20 quid.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_EntryMaxAtr = 99 restores v18.25 fill-anywhere behaviour.
      if(bars >= T_DeferHardBars)
      {
         double exAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
         double exPx  = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                  : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
         double exBey = (stV > 0.0) ? MathAbs(exPx - stV) : 0.0;
         // ══════ (v18.28) AUDIT CORRECTION — I REFUSED WHEN I SHOULD HAVE RESIZED ══════
         // v18.27 held the park while price was extended. Result: FIVE HOURS, ZERO
         // TRADES, while his Pine printed the cleanest signals he has seen. The
         // ANCHOR log had already told me fills land at 1.9-2.4 x ATR routinely, so
         // a 1.20 limit refuses essentially every fill, and the park then dies with
         // the flip. I traded away his first rule -- every signal must be taken --
         // to satisfy the second, and that is not my call to make.
         //
         // The two goals are reconciled by SIZE, not by refusal. Entry quality does
         // not decide WHETHER to trade, it decides HOW BIG. An extended fill gets
         // T_ExtendedLotFrac of normal size, so the money at risk on a poor entry is
         // small while the signal is still taken and can still run. A clean fill gets
         // full size. Nothing is ever refused for price again.
         // OFF-SWITCH: T_ExtendedLotFrac = 1.0 (always full size).
         g_entryExtScale = 1.0;
         if(exAtr > 0.0 && stV > 0.0 && exBey > T_EntryMaxAtr * exAtr)
         {
            g_entryExtScale = T_ExtendedLotFrac;
            PrintFormat("[TREND] EXTENDED FILL on %s — %.2f pts (%.2f x ATR) past the line. "
                        "TAKING IT at %.0f%% size (a poor price is a sizing decision, not a reason to "
                        "skip). Stop goes beyond the signal candle.",
                        EnumToString(tf), exBey, exBey / exAtr, 100.0 * T_ExtendedLotFrac);
         }
         expired = true;
         PrintFormat("[TREND] DEFER HARD EXPIRY on %s — parked %d bars (limit %d) and the bar-open "
                     "window never landed. Taking it at market now rather than losing the signal.",
                     EnumToString(tf), bars, T_DeferHardBars);
         return true;
      }
      return false;   // hold the park until the next bar opens
   }
   if(atr > 0.0 && MathAbs(px - stV) <= T_MaxExtensionAtr * atr) return true;
   return false;
}

// (v16.77) Which base tickets have already been added to. Bounded ring; a ticket
// that is no longer an open position frees its slot.
#define ADD_MAX 32
ulong g_addTk[ADD_MAX];
int   g_addN[ADD_MAX];
// (v16.77) total open volume on this symbol — no such helper existed, and the
// scale-add must not push past InpMaxTotalLots.
double OpenLotsTotal()
{
   double v = 0.0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong t = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(t == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(t)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      v += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   }
   return v;
}

// (v16.89) AUTO-SCALED QUICK-HOLD SIZING. Returns the total lots for an M1
// quick-hold entry at the CURRENT equity, and fills bankLots/holdLots with the
// split. Returns 0.0 when the account cannot support a splittable position, in
// which case the caller must fall back to normal sizing (no quick-hold), because
// a position of one minimum lot CANNOT be partially closed — there is nothing to
// shave off. That is the real floor on this strategy, and it is set by the
// broker's lot step, not by choice.
//================================================================
//  (v18.05) STEP PARTIALS -- size comes off ONE broker step at a time.
//  His ask: "partials need to be taken at 0.01 so they should close over time."
//  Every partial-close path previously computed its own FRACTION of the
//  position (a half, a third, T_M1QuickBankFrac). At 0.04 in, a "half" is 0.02
//  -- half the position gone on one event. All four paths now route through
//  StepBank(), which returns exactly T_PartialStep lots, refuses to take the
//  runner below T_MinRunnerLots, and enforces a cooldown so two paths firing on
//  the same tick cannot strip two steps at once.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_StepPartials = false restores each path's own fraction.
//================================================================
input group  "=== v18.05  STEP PARTIALS ==="
input bool   T_StepPartials     = true;  // true = each partial closes exactly T_PartialStep, not a fraction
input double T_PartialStep      = 0.01;  // lots per bank event
// (v18.37) 3.00 -> 2.00. "Sometimes we don't take partials quick enough — M1
// doesn't often last long." His measured average WINNER is 1.40 pts, so a 3 point
// floor meant most winners never shed anything at all before reversing.
input double T_PartialMinPts    = 2.00;  // (v18.21) absolute profit in points before ANY partial may fire
input double T_MinRunnerLots    = 0.01;  // a partial may never take the runner below this
input int    T_PartialCooldownS = 45;    // seconds before the same ticket may shed another step

ulong g_stepTk[128];
long  g_stepAt[128];
int   g_stepN = 0;

bool StepCooldownOk(ulong tk)
{
   long now = (long)TimeCurrent();
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stepN; i++)
      if(g_stepTk[i] == tk)
      {
         if(now - g_stepAt[i] < (long)T_PartialCooldownS) return false;
         g_stepAt[i] = now;
         return true;
      }
   if(g_stepN < 128) { g_stepTk[g_stepN] = tk; g_stepAt[g_stepN] = now; g_stepN++; }
   return true;
}

// Lots to close on THIS event. 0.0 = take nothing. `fallback` is the caller's
// own fraction, used only when T_StepPartials is off.
//  (v18.21) PARTIALS WERE STILL FIRING TOO EARLY.
//  Step size was fixed in v18.05, but nothing governed WHEN. The 0.6R and 1.0R
//  triggers are R-relative, and R is the stop distance -- so with a 4 pt stop the
//  first partial can fire at 2.4 pts, which on gold M1 is one candle. His
//  complaint is not about the size of each partial any more, it is the timing:
//  "partials are taken way too quick, we really need to hold out."
//  A partial now needs BOTH the existing R trigger AND a minimum absolute profit
//  in points, so a single candle cannot trigger banking on a trade that has not
//  actually travelled. OFF-SWITCH: T_PartialMinPts = 0.
double StepBank(ulong tk, double curVol, double fallback)
{
   if(T_PartialMinPts > 0.0 && PositionSelectByTicket(tk))
   {
      int    pbDir = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      double pbOp  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double pbPx  = (pbDir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                                 : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
      double pbPts = (pbPx - pbOp) * pbDir;
      if(pbPts < PS(T_PartialMinPts))
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[PARTIAL HELD] #%I64u is only %+.2f pts up — needs %.1f pts before any size comes off.",
                        tk, pbPts, PS(T_PartialMinPts));
         return 0.0;
      }
   }
   if(!T_StepPartials)
      return (fallback >= g_lotMin && curVol - fallback >= g_lotMin - 1e-9) ? fallback : 0.0;
   double step = MathMax(T_PartialStep, g_lotStep);
   step = MathRound(step / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   if(step < g_lotStep) step = g_lotStep;
   if(step >= curVol) return 0.0;
   double floorRun = MathMax(T_MinRunnerLots, g_lotMin);
   if(curVol - step < floorRun - 1e-9) return 0.0;
   if(!StepCooldownOk(tk)) return 0.0;
   return step;
}

double QuickHoldSize(double &bankLots, double &holdLots)
{
   bankLots = 0.0; holdLots = 0.0;
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double total;
   if(T_StepScaling)
   {
      // (v17.10) step ladder — see T_StepScaling
      double steps = 0.0;
      if(eq > T_StepBaseEquity && T_StepEvery > 0.0)
         steps = MathFloor((eq - T_StepBaseEquity) / T_StepEvery);
      total = T_StepBaseLots + steps * T_StepAddLots;
   }
   else
      total = (eq / 100.0) * T_M1QuickPer100;
   total = MathFloor(total / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   if(total > T_M1QuickMaxLots) total = T_M1QuickMaxLots;
   // needs at least TWO minimum lots, else there is no split to make
   if(total < 2.0 * g_lotMin - 1e-9) return 0.0;
   // (v16.89) ROUND, not floor. Flooring inverted the split at small totals: on
   // 0.03 total, floor(0.018/0.01) = 1 gave bank 0.01 / hold 0.02 — a 33/67 split
   // when 60/40 was asked for. Rounding gives 0.02/0.01, which is the intended
   // direction at every size the broker's 0.01 step can represent.
   // (v18.21) His structure is explicit: ONE 0.01 comes off early, the rest rides.
   // A FRACTION of the position was the wrong shape -- at 0.05 even 0.25 is 0.0125
   // rounding to 0.01, but at any larger size it scales up again and re-creates
   // the "banked 57% two minutes in" problem this input was already cut once for.
   // T_QuickBankOneStep pins it to exactly one broker step regardless of size.
   bankLots = T_QuickBankOneStep
            ? MathMax(g_lotStep, MathRound(T_PartialStep / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep)
            : MathRound((total * T_M1QuickBankFrac) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   if(bankLots < g_lotMin) bankLots = g_lotMin;
   holdLots = total - bankLots;
   if(holdLots < g_lotMin)          // protect the held side
   {
      holdLots = g_lotMin;
      bankLots = total - holdLots;
      if(bankLots < g_lotMin) return 0.0;
   }
   return total;
}

// (v16.95) PER-CLOCK EXIT SHAPING. One place, so M1/M3/M5 can never silently
// drift back to sharing one set of thresholds.
double RatchetFracFor(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_PerClockExits) return T_PeakRatchetFrac;
   switch(tf)
   {
      case PERIOD_M1: return T_RatchetM1;
      case PERIOD_M3: return T_RatchetM3;
      case PERIOD_M5: return T_RatchetM5;
      default:        return T_PeakRatchetFrac;
   }
}
double GivebackMultFor(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_PerClockExits) return 1.0;
   switch(tf)
   {
      case PERIOD_M1: return T_GivebackMultM1;
      case PERIOD_M3: return T_GivebackMultM3;
      case PERIOD_M5: return T_GivebackMultM5;
      default:        return 1.0;
   }
}

// (v16.96) HARD ENTRY RATE LIMITER. Deliberately independent of the flip logic,
// the regime engine, and every gate — this is the layer that holds when something
// upstream is broken. See T_HardRateLimit for the incident that motivated it.
datetime g_rlBar[SC_TFN];        // which bar the per-bar counter belongs to
int      g_rlCount[SC_TFN];      // entries taken on that bar, per clock
datetime g_rlLast[SC_TFN];       // last entry time, per clock
datetime g_brkTimes[64];         // account-wide entry timestamps (ring)
int      g_brkN = 0;
datetime g_brkPauseUntil = 0;

// Call IMMEDIATELY BEFORE an entry. Returns false = do not enter.
bool RateLimitOk(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_HardRateLimit) return true;
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return true;
   datetime now = TimeCurrent();

   if(T_CircuitBreaker && now < g_brkPauseUntil) return false;

   datetime bar = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
   if(g_rlBar[k] != bar) { g_rlBar[k] = bar; g_rlCount[k] = 0; }
   if(g_rlCount[k] >= T_MaxEntriesPerBar)
   {
      PrintFormat("[RATE] %s BLOCKED — already %d entries on this bar (cap %d). Something upstream is firing repeatedly.",
                  EnumToString(tf), g_rlCount[k], T_MaxEntriesPerBar);
      return false;
   }
   if(g_rlLast[k] > 0 && (now - g_rlLast[k]) < T_MinSecsBetweenEntry)
   {
      PrintFormat("[RATE] %s BLOCKED — only %d s since the last entry (minimum %d s).",
                  EnumToString(tf), (int)(now - g_rlLast[k]), T_MinSecsBetweenEntry);
      return false;
   }
   return true;
}

// Call AFTER a confirmed entry, to record it.
void RateLimitRecord(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return;
   datetime now = TimeCurrent();
   g_rlCount[k]++;
   g_rlLast[k] = now;
   if(!T_CircuitBreaker) return;
   // account-wide burst detection
   if(g_brkN < 64) g_brkTimes[g_brkN++] = now;
   else { for(int i = 1; i < 64; i++) g_brkTimes[i-1] = g_brkTimes[i]; g_brkTimes[63] = now; }
   int inWindow = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < g_brkN; i++) if(now - g_brkTimes[i] <= T_BurstWindowSecs) inWindow++;
   if(inWindow > T_BurstMaxEntries)
   {
      g_brkPauseUntil = now + T_BurstCooldownSecs;
      PrintFormat("[BREAKER] TRIPPED — %d entries in %d s (limit %d). PAUSING ALL NEW ENTRIES for %d s. Investigate the Experts log above this line.",
                  inWindow, T_BurstWindowSecs, T_BurstMaxEntries, T_BurstCooldownSecs);
   }
}

int AddCountFor(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < ADD_MAX; i++) if(g_addTk[i] == tk) return g_addN[i];
   return 0;
}
void MarkAdd(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < ADD_MAX; i++) if(g_addTk[i] == tk) { g_addN[i]++; return; }
   for(int i = 0; i < ADD_MAX; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_addTk[i], tk)) { g_addTk[i] = tk; g_addN[i] = 1; return; }
}

// (v16.80) MY v16.79 "FIX" WAS WRONG AND IS REMOVED.
// I added a TwoPoleCached() layer plus g_tpNow[]/g_tpBar[] globals, believing the
// Two-Pole was uncached on the tick path. It was ALREADY cached — EnsureTwoPole(k)
// at ~line 4974 recomputes only on a genuinely new bar per timeframe and stores
// g_tpNow[]/g_tpPrev1[]/g_tpPrev2[]. My duplicate collided with those globals,
// which is the "variable already defined" compile error. The lesson is the one in
// my own standing self-criticism: check whether the information already exists
// before adding machinery. The early-flip now reads the EXISTING cache at its call
// site, where those globals are in scope.
double TwoPoleValue(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int len, double smooth, int shift,
                    double &tpNow, double &tpPrev1, double &tpPrev2);

// (v16.79) the v16.75 forward declaration that lived here is redundant — the one
// above already declares TwoPoleValue before any use.

// (v16.75) EARLY FLIP. Returns true when live price has already crossed the
// Supertrend level computed from the last CLOSED bar, i.e. the flip is forming
// now and will confirm at the close unless price reverses. Sets earlyDir to the
// direction being flipped INTO. Reads the cached ScalpST level, so it adds no
// indicator work on the tick path.
// (v17.52) HOW FAR PAST THE TRIGGER LEVEL IS THIS FILL? — his point, exactly:
// "say we get a buy signal of a large candle, that's the whole candle's profit
// lost if we enter at the peak, whereas if we enter lower we make more profit."
// One shared measure, so every entry path can be judged the same way instead of
// only the early flip carrying a wick guard (which was the case up to v17.51).
// Returns true = this fill is too expensive RIGHT NOW. It is never a permanent
// rejection: the caller re-checks on the next tick, so the entry simply happens
// if and when price comes back toward the level it broke.
// (v17.61, his ask: "how can we use the signal candle / candlestick patterns as
// a confirmation") ENTRY CANDLE SANITY. Deliberately NOT a pattern library —
// engulfings, pin bars and dojis on M1 gold are mostly noise and each one added
// is another thing to be wrong about. This checks the ONE case his screenshots
// keep showing: the entry lands into a big candle running the OTHER way, i.e.
// we are buying the top of a spike or selling the bottom of a flush. Two tests,
// both on the last CLOSED bar:
//   1. it closed against the trade, AND
//   2. its range is >= T_EntryOpposeAtr x ATR (a big opposing bar, not a pause)
// Both must be true to block, so an ordinary small counter-candle inside a real
// flip still trades. Like the other entry guards this is per-tick, not a
// permanent rejection — the next bar re-checks.
// (v17.62, his ask: "highs lows, higher highs, lower lows, breakouts, FVG/IFVG
// — these tell us there is a higher chance of a real trend so we can hold")
// WHAT ALREADY EXISTS, so it is not built twice: higher-highs / higher-lows /
// lower-highs / lower-lows and break-of-structure are ALREADY in this file as
// structIntact / structGone / structPatient (the CHoCH-BOS logic), and they
// already gate the structure exit and three of the profit banks. The genuinely
// missing one is the IMBALANCE read, so that is what this adds.
//
// FVG = a three-candle imbalance: price moved so fast that candle 1's low never
// overlapped candle 3's high (bullish), leaving an unfilled gap BELOW price. An
// unfilled gap below a long is displacement evidence — the move had force, not
// drift — and it is the single most useful "this is a real trend, keep holding"
// tell that isn't already wired in. Mirrored for shorts.
//
// It can ONLY ever add a reason to HOLD. It never opens, closes or sizes
// anything, so a false read costs nothing but one extra held bar.
// (v17.64, his ask: "use previous highs / previous lows to mark out a TP or goal
// ... it needs to know what is telling it to hold") ROOM TO RUN.
// The EA had no concept of WHERE a move could reasonably go — every exit decision
// was about what price just did, never about how much space was left in front of
// it. This gives it that: the nearest OPPOSING swing level from the last
// T_RoomLookback bars (prior swing high above a long, prior swing low below a
// short), which is the level a trend actually has to fight at.
//   · far from it  -> there is room, so a small counter-candle is noise: HOLD
//   · at or past it -> the space is used up, so banking is reasonable
//   · no level above/below at all (new highs / new lows) -> open air, HOLD
// Returns the distance in price to that level via roomPts, and the level itself
// via lvl so it can be logged as the trade's realistic goal.
// Like every other vote this can only ADD a hold. It never opens or closes.
bool RoomToRun(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int k, int dir, double px, double &roomPts, double &lvl)
{
   roomPts = 0.0; lvl = 0.0;
   if(!T_RoomToRun || dir == 0 || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return false;
   double atrR = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   if(atrR <= 0.0 || px <= 0.0) return false;
   bool found = false;
   for(int i = 2; i <= T_RoomLookback; i++)
   {
      // a swing point = a bar whose extreme beats both neighbours
      if(dir > 0)
      {
         double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i);
         if(h > iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i - 1) && h > iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i + 1) && h > px)
         { if(!found || h < lvl) { lvl = h; found = true; } }   // nearest one ABOVE
      }
      else
      {
         double l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);
         if(l < iLow(_Symbol, tf, i - 1) && l < iLow(_Symbol, tf, i + 1) && l < px)
         { if(!found || l > lvl) { lvl = l; found = true; } }   // nearest one BELOW
      }
   }
   if(!found) { roomPts = -1.0; return true; }        // open air ahead of the trade
   roomPts = (dir > 0) ? (lvl - px) : (px - lvl);
   return (roomPts >= T_RoomMinAtr * atrR);
}

bool FvgSupports(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int k, int dir, double px, double &gapSz)
{
   gapSz = 0.0;
   if(!T_FvgHold || dir == 0 || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return false;
   double atrF = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   if(atrF <= 0.0) return false;
   for(int i = 1; i <= T_FvgLookback; i++)
   {
      if(dir > 0)
      {
         double lo1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i), hi3 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i + 2);
         if(lo1 <= 0.0 || hi3 <= 0.0) continue;
         double gz = lo1 - hi3;                       // unfilled space below price
         if(gz >= T_FvgMinAtr * atrF && px > lo1)     // still unfilled, price above it
         { gapSz = gz; return true; }
      }
      else
      {
         double hi1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i), lo3 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i + 2);
         if(hi1 <= 0.0 || lo3 <= 0.0) continue;
         double gz = lo3 - hi1;                       // unfilled space above price
         if(gz >= T_FvgMinAtr * atrF && px < hi1)
         { gapSz = gz; return true; }
      }
   }
   return false;
}

// (v17.66, his live evidence: "we fire at a peak so we never catch the rest of
// the profit ... we enter badly and wonder why we go into loss asap")
// DISTANCE FROM THE MEAN. The existing guards measure distance from the SIGNAL
// LINE, which on a flip sits right next to price and so passes even when price
// has already run a long way. The screenshots show the other thing: entries
// taken when price is stretched far ABOVE the DEMA on a buy (or below on a
// sell). Price reverts to that mean far more reliably than it continues from an
// extended point, which is precisely the "straight into loss" he keeps seeing.
// Blocked while stretched, allowed the moment it comes back. Re-checked per
// tick, so nothing is permanently lost.
// (v17.71, his ask: "as a human I can see this is a sell from the peak of the
// day after a 60 point rally -- can the EA see that and size up on real
// analysis?") PREMIUM SETUP GRADING.
// This is the honest version of the tier-3 idea he correctly rejected: six
// timeframes never agree, so that gate never fired. THIS one describes the
// trade he actually keeps pointing at, using two facts that are always
// available and need no new indicator:
//   1. HOW FAR THE MARKET HAS ALREADY RUN today (the D1 bar range so far).
//      A sell after a 60-point rally is a different animal to a sell inside a
//      10-point chop, and the day range is the cleanest measure of which one
//      we are in.
//   2. WHERE IN THAT RANGE WE ARE ENTERING. Selling within the top fraction of
//      the day is selling from the extreme; selling mid-range is selling into
//      the middle of a move with room in both directions.
// Both true -> premium, and the lot is multiplied. Neither, or only one -> the
// normal ladder size. This is real analysis in the sense he means it: it is a
// statement about where price sits in the day structure, not a stack of
// indicators agreeing with each other.
// RISK NOTE, deliberately not buried: a 2x multiplier on a premium setup means
// a premium setup that fails costs 2x. It should fire on a minority of trades.
// If the log shows it firing on most entries, T_PremiumRunPts is too low for
// current volatility and should go up, not the multiplier down.
bool g_premiumSetup = false;
int  g_strongBirth  = 0;
// (v18.47) THE PEAK-AWARE SIZE GATE. Set at each entry alongside g_strongBirth,
// read in LotFor(). His doctrine, verbatim: "bad prices handled by SIZE, bad
// trades by an early points cut, NEVER by declining the trade." So a flip that
// fires with price already pinned to the leg extreme is not refused -- it is
// taken at MINIMUM size. Off the extreme with a strong birth, it gets maximum.
bool g_atExtreme = false;
bool   g_wickAgainst = false;   // (v18.73 V) the signal candle was won by the other side
double g_legFrac = -1.0;   // (v18.73 T) 0..1 position in the leg at signal time; -1 = unknown
int  g_entryDir     = 0;
bool g_chopSizeDown = false;
// (v17.75, his ask: "how will you tell the difference between chop and those
// good 10-40 point trends?") STRONG BIRTH SCORE — and the honest answer first:
// you CANNOT know in advance. Nothing can. What you can do is measure the
// conditions that were present at the birth of the big moves and absent in the
// chop, and require several of them at once. Four tests, all available on the
// birth bar itself, none of them a new indicator:
//   1. VOLATILITY IS EXPANDING  — ATR now vs ATR 20 bars ago. Big moves are
//      born out of expansion; chop is born out of a flat or falling ATR.
//   2. DISPLACEMENT ON THE BIRTH BAR — the trigger bar has real range and
//      closed in our direction. A 10-40pt move almost never starts on a doji.
//   3. DAY CONTEXT — the PremiumSetup test: the day has travelled far enough to
//      matter and we are entering from its extreme, not its middle.
//   4. ROOM AHEAD — no opposing swing level in the way (RoomToRun).
// 3 of 4 = STRONG. That is a probability statement, not a prediction, and it
// will be wrong sometimes — which is exactly why the size step is 0.05 and not
// 0.20. Watch the log: if STRONG prints on most entries the bar is too low.
// (v17.76) DAY BIAS -- and this one PARTIALLY REVERSES an instruction he gave me
// on 2 Aug ("1m has no relevance to 1 hour or day"), so it is defaulted to the
// mildest setting and flagged rather than slipped in.
// WHY I am raising it: his 5 Aug screenshots are the cleanest evidence in this
// whole project. Gold ran +163 (+4%) on the day. Across six charts, essentially
// EVERY BUY marker ran and essentially every SELL marker was into a rising
// market. The signals were not wrong -- a Supertrend flip is a valid flip in
// both directions -- but on a strongly directional day the counter-direction
// flips are noise inside a move and the with-direction flips are the move.
// This does NOT bring back the HTF vote he removed. It reads ONE fact: how far
// the current daily candle has travelled from its own open, and which way.
// That is not an indicator on another timeframe, it is todays direction.
//   mode 0 = off, exactly the behaviour before this version
//   mode 1 = counter-direction trades are HALVED (default)
//   mode 2 = counter-direction trades are skipped entirely
// Mode 2 is the aggressive read and I am not defaulting to it: a genuine
// reversal day would then take no trades at all until the daily candle flips.
int DayBias()
{
   if(T_DayBiasMode <= 0) return 0;
   double dOpen = iOpen(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
   double px    = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   if(dOpen <= 0.0 || px <= 0.0) return 0;
   double travel = px - dOpen;
   if(MathAbs(travel) < T_DayBiasMinPts) return 0;   // day has not committed either way
   return (travel > 0.0) ? 1 : -1;
}
bool PremiumSetup(int dir, double px);   // (v17.75) forward declaration: StrongBirthScore uses it and is defined above it
int StrongBirthScore(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int k, int dir, double px)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN || dir == 0) return 0;
   int score = 0;
   double atrNow = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1), atrOld = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 21);
   if(atrNow > 0.0 && atrOld > 0.0 && atrNow >= T_StrongAtrExpand * atrOld) score++;
   double o1 = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1), c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1), l1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   bool withUs = (dir > 0) ? (c1 > o1) : (c1 < o1);
   if(atrNow > 0.0 && withUs && (h1 - l1) >= T_StrongBodyAtr * atrNow) score++;
   // (v17.78, his correction: "the star needs to be a normal one we catch from
   // the START of a trend") The day-extreme test is REMOVED from the score. It
   // describes a reversal from the edge of the day, which is a different trade
   // to a trend birth -- requiring it was pushing the star toward the exact
   // setups he did NOT mean. It stays available as PremiumSetup() for the x2
   // sizing step, where "selling the day high" genuinely is the point.
   // The score is now out of 3 (expansion, displacement, room) and the two hard
   // vetoes below (travel + not sideways) supply the trend context instead.
   double rmPts = 0.0, rmLvl = 0.0;
   if(RoomToRun(tf, k, dir, px, rmPts, rmLvl)) score++;
   // (v17.77, his ask: "the emoji signals must be good ones, NO SIDEWAYS")
   // A hard veto, not a scored point: a 20-bar window that is going somewhere has
   // a range several times its ATR, while a sideways window sits at 2-3x ATR no
   // matter how many candles it prints. If the recent window is not travelling,
   // the score is zeroed regardless of how the other four tests read -- which is
   // the difference between "conditions look energetic" and "price is actually
   // going somewhere".
   int hi20 = iHighest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_HIGH, 20, 1);
   int lo20 = iLowest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_LOW, 20, 1);
   if(hi20 >= 0 && lo20 >= 0 && atrNow > 0.0)
   {
      double win = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, hi20) - iLow(_Symbol, tf, lo20);
      if(win < T_StrongRangeAtr * atrNow)
      {
         PrintFormat("[TREND] STRONG BIRTH VETOED on %s — last 20 bars only spanned %.2f pts (%.2fx ATR, need %.2fx). Sideways, not a trend birth.",
                     EnumToString(tf), win, win / atrNow, T_StrongRangeAtr);
         return 0;
      }
   }
   return score;
}
bool PremiumSetup(int dir, double px)
{
   if(!T_PremiumSetup || dir == 0) return false;
   double dHi = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0), dLo = iLow(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
   if(dHi <= 0.0 || dLo <= 0.0 || px <= 0.0) return false;
   double dayRange = dHi - dLo;
   if(dayRange < T_PremiumRunPts) return false;                 // the day has not run far enough
   double edge = (dir > 0) ? (px - dLo) : (dHi - px);           // distance from the extreme we are trading FROM
   double edgeAllow = T_PremiumEdgeFrac * dayRange;
   // (v18.84 AK) cap the band at one signal band. See the note at T_PremiumEdgeBandCap.
   if(T_PremiumEdgeBandCap)
   {
      double atrPr = Buf(g_scAtr[0], 0, 1);
      if(atrPr > 0.0)
      {
         double sigBand = T_StMultiplier * atrPr;
         if(sigBand > 0.0 && sigBand < edgeAllow)
         {
            if(edge > sigBand)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] PREMIUM REFUSED — %.2f pts from the day %s. The 30%% band on a %.2f pt "
                           "day allows %.2f pts, which is most of the day. One signal band is %.2f pts and "
                           "that is what \"at the extreme\" means.",
                           edge, dir > 0 ? "low" : "high", dayRange, edgeAllow, sigBand);
            edgeAllow = sigBand;
         }
      }
   }
   bool atEdge = (edge <= edgeAllow);
   if(!atEdge) return false;
   PrintFormat("[TREND] PREMIUM SETUP: %s at %.2f, %.2f pts from the day %s (%.0f%% of a %.2f pt day range). "
               "Sizing up x%.2f.",
               dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px, edge, dir > 0 ? "low" : "high",
               100.0 * edge / dayRange, dayRange, T_PremiumLotMult);
   return true;
}
// (v17.80, his report: "the Experts tab gets hundreds of messages")
// These gates are evaluated on EVERY TICK, so an unconditional Print in each one
// produced hundreds of identical lines per bar and buried the messages that
// actually matter. This throttle lets a given reason print once per bar per clock
// per direction and swallows the repeats. Nothing is hidden - the first
// occurrence in each bar is always shown, which is all the information the
// repeats carried anyway.
datetime g_gateSaid[8][3][10];   // (v18.06) 6 -> 10: slots 8 (path audit) and 9 (anchor guard)
bool GateSayOnce(int k, int dir, int reason, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   // (v18.06) reason ceiling 6 -> 10. Slots 8 and 9 (path audit, anchor guard)
   // fell through the >= 6 test and returned true on EVERY tick, which would
   // have spammed the Experts tab instead of debouncing once per bar.
   if(k < 0 || k >= 8 || reason < 0 || reason >= 10) return true;
   int di = (dir > 0) ? 2 : ((dir < 0) ? 0 : 1);
   datetime bt = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
   if(bt <= 0) return true;
   if(g_gateSaid[k][di][reason] == bt) return false;   // already said this bar
   g_gateSaid[k][di][reason] = bt;
   return true;
}
// (v17.83) Is the chart instrument gold? Brokers suffix and rename constantly
// (XAUUSDm on Exness, XAUUSD.m, XAUUSD_i, XAUUSDpro, GOLD, GOLDmicro), so any
// exact-name compare will be wrong on most accounts. Content test instead.
bool GoldSymbol()
{
   string sym = _Symbol;
   StringToUpper(sym);
   // (v17.86) BTC support stripped per his instruction -- too volatile for now, XAU focus only.
   return (StringFind(sym, "XAU") >= 0 || StringFind(sym, "GOLD") >= 0);
}
// (v17.93 BUG-001 -- my own defect from v17.86, found in this audit) The file
// ALREADY creates a per-clock RSI handle: g_scRsi[k] = iRSI(_Symbol, tf, 14,
// PRICE_CLOSE) at line ~5358, released properly in OnDeinit at ~5509. My v17.86
// addition created a SECOND parallel set (g_rsiHandle[8]) for the same indicator
// on the same timeframes, and never released them -- so every parameter change,
// chart reload or EA restart leaked handles, and the same value had two owners.
// This is precisely the duplicated-source-of-truth class I have been auditing for.
// Now reads the existing handle. Note T_RsiPeriod is consequently ignored: the
// existing handle is hardcoded to 14, and changing that shared handle would
// alter whatever else already consumes it. 14 is the standard period anyway.
double iRSI_Val(int k)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN || !T_RsiHold) return 0.0;
   if(g_scRsi[k] == INVALID_HANDLE) return 0.0;
   double buf[];
   if(CopyBuffer(g_scRsi[k], 0, 1, 1, buf) != 1) return 0.0;   // bar 1 = closed, no repaint
   return buf[0];
}

bool EntryNearMean(int k, int dir, double px)
{
   if(!T_EntryMeanGate || T_MaxDemaDistAtr <= 0.0 || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return true;
   double dm = DemaValSc(k), at = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   if(dm <= 0.0 || at <= 0.0) return true;
   double stretch = (dir > 0) ? (px - dm) : (dm - px);
   if(stretch > T_MaxDemaDistAtr * at)
   {
      if(GateSayOnce(k, dir, 0, g_scTF[k]))
      PrintFormat("[TREND] ENTRY STOOD DOWN — %s is %.2f pts above/below the DEMA (%.2fx ATR, cap %.2f). "
                  "Stretched entries revert; waiting for price to come back toward the mean.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", stretch, stretch / at, T_MaxDemaDistAtr);
      return false;
   }
   return true;
}

bool EntryCandleOk(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int k, int dir)
{
   if(!T_EntryCandleConfirm || dir == 0 || k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) return true;
   double atrC = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   double o1 = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1), c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1), l1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   if(atrC <= 0.0 || o1 <= 0.0 || c1 <= 0.0) return true;
   bool closedAgainst = (dir > 0) ? (c1 < o1) : (c1 > o1);
   double rng = h1 - l1;
   if(closedAgainst && rng >= T_EntryOpposeAtr * atrC)
   {
      if(GateSayOnce(k, dir, 1, tf))
      PrintFormat("[TREND] ENTRY STOOD DOWN on %s — last closed candle ran %s with a %.2f pt range (%.2fx ATR). "
                  "That is the spike we would be buying the wrong end of. Re-checks next bar.",
                  EnumToString(tf), dir > 0 ? "DOWN" : "UP", rng, rng / atrC);
      return false;
   }
   return true;
}

bool EntryTooFarPastLevel(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, double px, double level,
                          double atr, string who)
{
   if(T_MaxWickAtr <= 0.0 || level <= 0.0 || atr <= 0.0 || dir == 0) return false;
   double beyond = (dir > 0) ? (px - level) : (level - px);
   if(beyond <= 0.0) return false;                       // at or behind the level = the best fill there is
   if(beyond <= T_MaxWickAtr * atr) return false;
   if(GateSayOnce(0, dir, 2, tf))
   PrintFormat("[%s] ENTRY TOO FAR on %s — %s fill would be %.2f pts (%.2fx ATR) past the %.2f level. "
               "Skipping this tick, not the signal: it re-checks every tick and fires if price comes back.",
               who, EnumToString(tf), dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", beyond, beyond / atr, level);
   return true;
}

bool EarlyFlipForming(int k, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int curDir, double stLevel, int &earlyDir)
{
   earlyDir = 0;
   if(!T_EarlyFlip || stLevel <= 0.0 || curDir == 0) return false;
   double atrE[1];
   int hE = ExhaustAtrHandle(tf);
   if(hE == INVALID_HANDLE || CopyBuffer(hE, 0, 1, 1, atrE) != 1 || atrE[0] <= 0.0) return false;
   double margin = atrE[0] * T_EarlyFlipAtr;
   double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   int want = 0;
   if(curDir == -1 && ask > stLevel + margin) want =  1;   // short -> long forming
   if(curDir ==  1 && bid < stLevel - margin) want = -1;   // long  -> short forming
   if(want == 0) return false;
   // (v16.80) the Two-Pole agreement check lives at the CALL SITE, where the
   // existing EnsureTwoPole cache and its g_tp* globals are in scope.
   earlyDir = want;
   return true;
}

//================================================================
//  (v17.18 SEVERE — the registry MISS was never actually fixed.)
//
//  v16.47 fixed the ring-buffer EVICTION, and its comment names the
//  consequence exactly: "TrendTFOf() then falls through to its M5
//  default and the trade is managed on the WRONG CLOCK for the rest
//  of its life". Eviction was fixed. THE FALLBACK WAS NOT. On any
//  miss this still silently answers M5.
//
//  And a miss is not exotic — it is GUARANTEED. g_trTfTk[64] is
//  plain in-memory state with no persistence, so every EA restart,
//  recompile, chart reload or terminal restart zeroes it. Running
//  24/7 while iterating daily means there are almost always open
//  positions when that happens, and at that moment EVERY open TREND
//  position starts reporting as M5.
//
//  What a wrong clock corrupts (profitR is safe — RDistOf stores R
//  per ticket and rebuilds it from |entry-sl|, so the R scale is
//  correct; this is about every clock-derived READ):
//    * CandleAgainst / TwoPoleTurnedAgainstTf read M5 candles for an
//      M1 trade, so the peak-evidence bank judges the wrong bars
//    * g_tpNow/g_tpPrev1/g_tpPrev2[trK] read M5's Two-Pole history
//    * StructureDir(trTf), LegStillExtending(trTf), RegimeOf(...)
//      all answer for M5
//    * the reclaim watch writes g_trWatchDir/Level/Expiry[trK], so an
//      M1 exit arms the M5 clock's re-entry — and with all three
//      clocks defaulting to slot 2, three positions CLOBBER each
//      other's single re-entry watch
//
//  THE FIX: the entry comment already encodes the clock. Open() is
//  called with "TREND-" + StringSubstr(EnumToString(tf),6), i.e.
//  "TREND-M1" / "TREND-M3" / "TREND-M5". So the clock is recoverable
//  from POSITION_COMMENT with no new state, and re-registering it
//  makes the registry SELF-HEALING across restarts.
//================================================================
int ClockFromComment(ulong tk)
{
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return -1;
   string c = PositionGetString(POSITION_COMMENT);
   if(StringLen(c) < 2) return -1;
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      string want = StringSubstr(EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), 6);   // "M1" / "M3" / "M5"
      if(StringFind(c, want) >= 0) return k;
   }
   return -1;
}

int TrendTFOf(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 64; i++) if(g_trTfTk[i] == tk) return g_trTfK[i];

   // (v17.18) Registry miss. Recover from the entry comment and RE-REGISTER,
   // so the lookup is repaired once rather than re-derived on every tick.
   int rk = ClockFromComment(tk);
   if(rk >= 0 && rk < SC_TFN)
   {
      StoreTrendTF(tk, rk);
      if(T_LogClockRecovery)
         PrintFormat("CLOCK RECOVERED from comment for #%I64u -> %s (registry had lost it; "
                     "re-registered).", tk, EnumToString(g_scTF[rk]));
      return rk;
   }

   // Genuinely unknown. Still return the old M5 default so no caller can index
   // an array out of range, but SAY SO — this trade is being managed on a clock
   // that is a guess, and that must never again be invisible.
   if(T_LogClockRecovery)
      PrintFormat("CLOCK UNRESOLVED for #%I64u — falling back to %s. Exit reads "
                  "(candles, Two-Pole, structure, regime, re-entry watch) are for the WRONG clock.",
                  tk, EnumToString(g_scTF[SC_TFN - 1]));
   return SC_TFN - 1;
}

// PEAK-PROFIT GIVEBACK GUARD (fix #52) — the real fix for "9 quid peak, closed
// at 2." The Supertrend trail only follows the indicator LINE, which is
// lagging by design — it does not move up meaningfully until price has
// already pulled back substantially. That protects against a genuine
// reversal, but does nothing to lock in a peak that formed faster than the
// line could follow. This tracks each position's own highest R reached,
// independent of the Supertrend line, and closes if too much of that peak
// gets given back — a second, faster, independent safety net on top of the
// existing trail, not a replacement for it.
ulong  g_pkTk[128];
double g_pkR[128];
int    g_pkN = 0;
double PeakR(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pkN; i++) if(g_pkTk[i] == tk) return g_pkR[i];
   return 0;
}
void UpdatePeakR(ulong tk, double curR)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pkN; i++)
      if(g_pkTk[i] == tk) { if(curR > g_pkR[i]) g_pkR[i] = curR; return; }
// (v17.18 BLIND-WRAP FIX) v16.47 fixed exactly this bug in the TREND clock
// registry and its own comment spells out the damage: the counter wrapped to 0
// and "overwrote slot 0 blindly", so a still-OPEN position lost its record.
// SEVEN registries in this file had the identical pattern and only that one was
// ever fixed — the fourth instance of the documented "same class of bug, never
// generalised" failure mode. None of them free a slot on close either, so the
// counter only ever grows: at 50-60 trades/day a 128-slot table wraps about
// every two days, guaranteed, on a 24/7 run. Now a slot is only reused when its
// ticket is no longer an open position.
// PeakR is the SEVEREST of the seven: it is the reference the peak ratchet and
// the giveback guard both compare against. Overwrite a live position's slot and
// its peak resets to the current R — so either the giveback guard never fires
// (peak protection silently gone) or it fires on a giveback that never happened
// and closes a healthy runner. Both directions cost money on exactly the
// trades that carry the day.
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pkN; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_pkTk[i], tk))
      { g_pkTk[i] = tk; g_pkR[i] = curR; return; }        // reclaim a dead slot
   if(g_pkN < 128) { g_pkTk[g_pkN] = tk; g_pkR[g_pkN] = curR; g_pkN++; return; }
   PrintFormat("PeakR table FULL (128 live positions) — #%I64u untracked. "
               "Peak protection is degraded for it; not overwriting a live slot.", tk);
}
void ClearPeakR(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pkN; i++) if(g_pkTk[i] == tk) { g_pkR[i] = 0; return; }
}

//================================================================
//  (v17.51) SUPERTREND-AGREEMENT LATCH — per ticket.
//
//  Answers one question only: has this clock's Supertrend EVER pointed
//  the same way as this position? A "flip against the trade" is only
//  meaningful if there was agreement to flip FROM. Counter-trend birth
//  entries (T_MicroBreakout, T_ReversalEntry) are opened while the
//  Supertrend still disagrees, so without this latch the flip-close in
//  ManagePosition fired on their very first tick.
//
//  Slot handling copies the v17.18 pattern: reclaim only slots whose
//  ticket is no longer an open position, never overwrite a live one,
//  and restore the caller's position selection before returning
//  (PositionSelectByTicket inside the scan changes it).
//================================================================
ulong g_agTk[128];
int   g_agOk[128];
int   g_agN = 0;
bool StAgreed(ulong tk, int dir, int stDnow)
{
   bool agreesNow = (stDnow != 0 && stDnow == dir);
   for(int i = 0; i < g_agN; i++)
      if(g_agTk[i] == tk)
      {
         if(agreesNow) g_agOk[i] = 1;
         return (g_agOk[i] == 1);
      }
   int init = agreesNow ? 1 : 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < g_agN; i++)
      if(g_agTk[i] == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(g_agTk[i]))
      {
         g_agTk[i] = tk; g_agOk[i] = init;
         PositionSelectByTicket(tk);          // restore the caller's selection
         return (init == 1);
      }
   PositionSelectByTicket(tk);                // restore the caller's selection
   if(g_agN < 128) { g_agTk[g_agN] = tk; g_agOk[g_agN] = init; g_agN++; return (init == 1); }
   return true;   // table full: fail back to the old behaviour rather than never exiting
}

// BUG FIX #60: the chop-cluster exit's "has the Supertrend line stalled"
// check compared the CURRENT Supertrend value to a raw price low/high from
// 3 bars back — comparing the line to PRICE, not to where the LINE ITSELF
// was. That's not "has the line advanced," it's a mismatched comparison
// that happened to sometimes look right by coincidence. ScalpST has no
// historical-shift parameter (by design — it's cached per current bar only,
// and changing its signature risks the whole trail/entry system that
// depends on it), so rather than touch that core function, this snapshots
// the Supertrend value once per position the first time the chop-cluster
// check becomes eligible, and compares against THAT on every later check —
// a genuine "has the line moved since we started watching" measurement,
// same ring-buffer pattern as the peak-R tracker above.
ulong  g_stSnapTk[128];
double g_stSnapVal[128];
int    g_stSnapN = 0;
double StSnapGet(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stSnapN; i++) if(g_stSnapTk[i] == tk) return g_stSnapVal[i];
   return 0;   // 0 = "no snapshot yet", caller must treat that as "just starting to watch"
}
bool StSnapExists(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stSnapN; i++) if(g_stSnapTk[i] == tk) return true;
   return false;
}
void StSnapSet(ulong tk, double val)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stSnapN; i++) if(g_stSnapTk[i] == tk) { g_stSnapVal[i] = val; return; }
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stSnapN; i++)                       // (v17.18) see PeakR note
      if(SlotDead(g_stSnapTk[i], tk))
      { g_stSnapTk[i] = tk; g_stSnapVal[i] = val; return; }
   if(g_stSnapN < 128) { g_stSnapTk[g_stSnapN] = tk; g_stSnapVal[g_stSnapN] = val; g_stSnapN++; }
}
void StSnapClear(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_stSnapN; i++) if(g_stSnapTk[i] == tk) { g_stSnapVal[i] = 0; return; }
}

// (v12d) PARTIAL-BANK TRACKER — same ring-buffer pattern as the peak tracker.
// Remembers which tickets have already banked their partial so it fires
// exactly once per position, even across the many management ticks that
// follow while the rest of the position rides.
ulong g_partTk[128];
int   g_partN = 0;
bool PartialDone(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_partN; i++) if(g_partTk[i] == tk) return true;
   return false;
}
void MarkPartialDone(ulong tk)
{
   // (v17.18) Losing this flag lets the SAME position be partially closed a
   // second time — the "banked half" is taken twice out of a trade that only
   // has one half to give.
   for(int i = 0; i < g_partN; i++) if(g_partTk[i] == tk) return;   // already flagged
   for(int i = 0; i < g_partN; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_partTk[i], tk)) { g_partTk[i] = tk; return; }
   if(g_partN < 128) { g_partTk[g_partN] = tk; g_partN++; }
}

// (v18.22) PYRAMID TRACKER — now COUNTS adds per position instead of latching a
// single flag, so a strong leg can be stacked into several times. PyramidDone()
// is kept for any caller that still expects the boolean.
// (v18.23 COMPILE FIX) These were named g_pyrTk / g_pyrN / g_pyrTkN in v18.22,
// which collided with the EXISTING one-shot tracker's g_pyrTk[128] and g_pyrN
// declared ~18 lines below. MetaEditor reported it as "variable already defined"
// plus a cascade of "invalid array access", because the older g_pyrN is a plain
// int counter while mine was an array — so every g_pyrN[i] in my code was
// indexing a scalar. Renamed with a distinct prefix; the old tracker is untouched.
ulong g_pyrcTk[64];
int   g_pyrcN[64];
int   g_pyrcCount = 0;

int PyramidCount(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pyrcCount; i++) if(g_pyrcTk[i] == tk) return g_pyrcN[i];
   return 0;
}

void PyramidNote(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pyrcCount; i++)
      if(g_pyrcTk[i] == tk) { g_pyrcN[i]++; return; }
   if(g_pyrcCount < 64) { g_pyrcTk[g_pyrcCount] = tk; g_pyrcN[g_pyrcCount] = 1; g_pyrcCount++; }
}

// (v12e) PYRAMID TRACKER — one top-up per position, ever. Same pattern.
ulong g_pyrTk[128];
int   g_pyrN = 0;
int   g_pyrToday = 0;
int   g_pyrLastDay = -1;
bool PyramidDone(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pyrN; i++) if(g_pyrTk[i] == tk) return true;
   return false;
}
void MarkPyramidDone(ulong tk)
{
   // (v17.18) This is a RISK bug, not just a profit one: losing the flag lets
   // the scale-add fire again on a position that has already been added to,
   // stacking size on one idea past the intended cap.
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pyrN; i++) if(g_pyrTk[i] == tk) return;
   for(int i = 0; i < g_pyrN; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_pyrTk[i], tk)) { g_pyrTk[i] = tk; return; }
   if(g_pyrN < 128) { g_pyrTk[g_pyrN] = tk; g_pyrN++; }
}

// (v12e) SIZING CHANNELS read by LotFor:
//   g_lastConfMult — graduated confidence sizing (0.6..1.0), reset to 1.0 after every entry
//   g_forceLots    — exact-lot override for pyramid top-ups (0 = off)
double g_lastConfMult = 1.0;
double g_forceLots    = 0.0;

// TREND flip detection, per timeframe (M1/M3/M5) — mirrors g_scLastDir, kept
// separate so TREND and SCALP don't share flip-memory on the same timeframe
// (they should each independently notice the same flip and can both act on it).
int g_trLastDir[SC_TFN];
// ══════════ (v18.95 BB) ONE ENTRY EVENT PER SUPERTREND LEG ══════════
// g_legSeq increments every time this clock's Supertrend changes direction.
// g_legDone records the leg in which this clock last opened. Equal means we have
// already committed to this leg and must wait for the next flip.
int g_legSeq[SC_TFN];
int g_legDone[SC_TFN];
// (v18.96 BC) WHEN this clock first opened in the current leg. The guard only bites
// AFTER T_LegStackSecs, so a stack that fills in one burst survives intact and only
// the LATER re-decisions inside the same leg are refused.
datetime g_legDoneT[SC_TFN];
// (v19.04 BP) the price this clock last OPENED at inside the current leg. Used to
// ask the only question that separates participation from re-deciding: has the leg
// EXTENDED since we last took it? See the note at T_LegReEnterOnExtend.
double   g_legEntryPx[SC_TFN];
// RESEARCH-BACKED (v15.5): flip-frequency chop detector. The documented rule
// of thumb for Supertrend systems: several flips inside a short window means
// the market is ranging and every flip is paying the whipsaw toll. Track
// recent flip times per timeframe; the entry confidence penalises a signal
// arriving as the 3rd+ flip within the last 20 bars of its own clock.
datetime g_flipTimes[SC_TFN][8];
int      g_flipIdx[SC_TFN];
void RecordFlip(int k)
{
   g_flipTimes[k][g_flipIdx[k] % 8] = TimeCurrent();
   g_flipIdx[k]++;
}
// (v18.01) The most recent flip time for a clock. RecordFlip() already writes
// every flip into g_flipTimes; this just reads the newest one back instead of
// adding a second, separately-maintained stamp that could drift out of sync.
datetime LastFlipAt(int k)
{
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN || g_flipIdx[k] <= 0) return 0;
   return g_flipTimes[k][(g_flipIdx[k] - 1) % 8];
}
int ClockIndexOfTf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < SC_TFN; i++) if(g_scTF[i] == tf) return i;
   return -1;
}

int RecentFlips(int k)
{
   datetime cutoff = TimeCurrent() - 20L * PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[k]);
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
      if(g_flipTimes[k][i] >= cutoff && g_flipTimes[k][i] > 0) n++;
   return n;
}

// ═══ (v16) LOSS FORENSICS state ═══
// Every real TREND loss is recorded at close (see the transaction handler);
// 10 bars later StopForensics() delivers the verdict: SHAKEOUT (price
// promptly went >= 1 ATR the trade's way after stopping us — the stop was
// too tight for current noise) or REVERSAL (the stop did its job; the
// signal-side learning owns that case). Three shakeouts and the entry
// noise buffer widens 15% — bounded at +45%, one-way (never loosens after
// wins), same self-tightening philosophy as g_adxBoost. Cash risk is
// unchanged by a wider buffer: LotFor() sizes the position to the stop.
datetime g_fxTime[16]; int g_fxDir[16]; int g_fxK[16];
double   g_fxPx[16];   double g_fxAtr[16]; bool g_fxLive[16];
int      g_fxN = 0;
int      g_fxShakeouts = 0, g_fxReversals = 0;
double   g_fxBufBoost = 0.0;   // widens the TREND entry noise buffer, 0..0.45

// (v16.16) CROSS-TF CHOP-CLUSTER PAUSE — see the note at T_CrossTfChopPause.
datetime g_chopLossTime[8];
int      g_chopLossIdx = 0;
datetime g_chopPauseUntil = 0;   // 0 = not paused; else TREND new entries pause until this time

void StopForensics()
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 16; i++)
   {
      if(!g_fxLive[i]) continue;
      int k = g_fxK[i];
      if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) { g_fxLive[i] = false; continue; }
      int barsSince = (int)((TimeCurrent() - g_fxTime[i]) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[k]), 1));
      if(barsSince < 10) continue;   // verdict not due yet
      // What did price do in the 10 bars AFTER the stop-out?
      int nb = MathMin(barsSince, 12);
      double hiA = iHigh(_Symbol, g_scTF[k], SafeHighest(_Symbol, g_scTF[k], MODE_HIGH, nb, 1));
      double loA = iLow(_Symbol, g_scTF[k],  SafeLowest(_Symbol, g_scTF[k],  MODE_LOW,  nb, 1));
      bool shakeout = (g_fxDir[i] > 0) ? (hiA >= g_fxPx[i] + 1.0 * g_fxAtr[i])
                                        : (loA <= g_fxPx[i] - 1.0 * g_fxAtr[i]);
      if(shakeout)
      {
         g_fxShakeouts++;
         PrintFormat("[FORENSICS] Loss on %s was a SHAKEOUT — price went %.2f+ (1 ATR) our way within %d bars of stopping us. Stop was too tight for current noise. (%d shakeouts vs %d real reversals)",
                     EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), g_fxAtr[i], nb, g_fxShakeouts, g_fxReversals);
         if(g_fxShakeouts % 3 == 0 && g_fxBufBoost < 0.45)
         {
            g_fxBufBoost += 0.15;
            PrintFormat("[FORENSICS] >>> LEARNED: 3 more shakeout losses — widening the TREND entry noise buffer by 15%% (now +%.0f%%). Cash risk unchanged (position sized to the stop).",
                        g_fxBufBoost * 100);
         }
      }
      else
      {
         g_fxReversals++;
         PrintFormat("[FORENSICS] Loss on %s was a REAL REVERSAL — the stop did its job; the signal was the problem (signal-side learning covers that). (%d shakeouts vs %d real reversals)",
                     EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), g_fxShakeouts, g_fxReversals);
      }
      g_fxLive[i] = false;
   }
}

// Per-timeframe cooldown/fast-rearm for TREND (fix #45) — a M1 exit must not
// cool down M3 or M5, and vice versa. Each timeframe remembers its OWN last
// exit time and whether that exit qualifies for a fast rearm (a genuine
// Supertrend flip against the trade — the market changing its mind, not
// noise — always gets fast rearm; see the flip-close in ManagePosition).
datetime g_trLastExit[SC_TFN];
bool     g_trFastRearm[SC_TFN];

// (v12c) NATIVE CONTINUATION RE-ENTRY WATCH — the fix for "EA detects a buy,
// exits on giveback, then misses the rest of the trend." Previously this
// engine's own re-entry ALWAYS required a fresh flipUp/flipDn (Supertrend
// going from -1 to +1 or vice versa). If the giveback guard closed a trade
// while Supertrend itself never flipped (the whole point of that guard —
// it acts before the lagging Supertrend confirms), flipUp/flipDn would not
// fire again until a full reversal-then-reversal cycle completed, which
// can take a very long time. This watch catches that gap directly, per
// timeframe, mirroring the same mechanism already built into the Pine.
int      g_trWatchDir[SC_TFN];       // 0 = not watching, 1 = watching to reclaim a long, -1 = short
double   g_trWatchLevel[SC_TFN];     // the level to reclaim
datetime g_trWatchExpiry[SC_TFN];    // give up watching after this time
int      g_trReentryCount[SC_TFN];   // (v13 #2) reclaims taken on the current continuous trend; resets on true flip
int      g_trEntryRegime[SC_TFN];    // (v16.22) regime at entry per TF: +1 trend, 0 neutral, -1 range — tells the exit to bank-fast in a range
double   g_regR[3];                  // (v16.22 #10) realized R accumulated per regime this session [0]=range [1]=neutral [2]=trend
int      g_regN[3];                  // trade count per regime, for the self-monitoring size lean



// trade reasons, keyed by position ticket
ulong  g_rTicket[200];
string g_rWhy[200];
int    g_rCount = 0;

// BUG FIX #1 (SEVERE): the R-multiple was computed as net / (equity * risk%).
// But the ACTUAL money at risk is often different — the lot gets clipped by
// InpMaxTotalLots, or rounded down to the lot step, or floored at the broker
// minimum. So every expectancy number in the old build was wrong, and the
// auto-disable and auto-risk layers were making decisions on bad data.
// We now store the REAL cash at risk at the moment of entry.
ulong  g_riskTk[200];
double g_riskCash[200];
int    g_riskCount = 0;

// BUG FIX #14 (SEVERE): risk was tracked per-ENGINE in single globals
// (g_apexR, g_apexEnt). On a HEDGING account each pyramid add is its OWN
// position — so all of them shared one R value, and after the first add every
// R-multiple, every trail trigger and every breakeven calculation was computed
// against the wrong number. R must be per TICKET.
ulong  g_rdTk[200];
double g_rdDist[200];
int    g_rdCount = 0;

// BUG FIX #29: a position must be booked into the stats EXACTLY ONCE, no matter
// how many partial deals it closed in.
ulong g_bookedTk[400];
int   g_bookedCount = 0;

bool AlreadyBooked(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 400; i++) if(g_bookedTk[i] == tk) return true;
   return false;
}
void MarkBooked(ulong tk)
{
   if(g_bookedCount >= 400) g_bookedCount = 0;
   g_bookedTk[g_bookedCount] = tk;
   g_bookedCount++;
}

void StoreRDist(ulong tk, double dist)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++) if(g_rdTk[i] == tk) { g_rdDist[i] = dist; return; }
   if(g_rdCount >= 200) g_rdCount = 0;
   g_rdTk[g_rdCount] = tk; g_rdDist[g_rdCount] = dist; g_rdCount++;
}
double RDistOf(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++) if(g_rdTk[i] == tk) return g_rdDist[i];
   return 0;
}

void StoreRisk(ulong tk, double cash)
{
   if(g_riskCount >= 200) g_riskCount = 0;
   g_riskTk[g_riskCount]   = tk;
   g_riskCash[g_riskCount] = cash;
   g_riskCount++;
}
double RiskOf(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++) if(g_riskTk[i] == tk) return g_riskCash[i];
   return 0;
}

// progress tracking for stall exits
ulong  g_pTicket[200];
double g_pPeak[200];
datetime g_pLast[200];
int    g_pCount = 0;

// ── CONFLUENCE TIER STATE (v12) ──
int      g_tierToday       = 0;    // count of tier-2+ trades today; capped by TierMaxPerDay
int      g_tierLastDay     = -1;
int      g_lastTier        = 0;    // tier used for the trade currently being opened (read by LotFor)
double   g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0;  // the fixed-lot size that tier requested (0 = size normally)
// (v17.21) TRUE when the requested size is an EXACT size the caller wants applied
// in BOTH directions (the reversal and re-entry paths deliberately ask for a
// SMALLER lot). FALSE for confluence tiers, which are only ever meant to GROW a
// trade. Without this distinction the "never shrink" rule silently discarded
// every downsize request — see the note in LotFor().
bool     g_sizeReqIsExact  = false;

//================================================================
//  HELPERS
//================================================================
double NP(double p) { return NormalizeDouble(p, _Digits); }

// ══════════ v18.82 (AI) iHighest / iLowest RETURN -1 AND NOBODY CHECKED ══════════
// FOUND BY A SWEEP OF THE WHOLE FILE: 24 places call iHigh(_Symbol, tf,
// iHighest(...)) with the index passed straight through. iHighest returns -1 when
// it cannot serve the request -- bars not loaded after a restart, a data gap, a
// timeframe not yet built, or simply a COUNT OF ZERO, which happens in normal
// operation wherever the count is derived from bars-since-signal on a fresh flip.
// iHigh(_Symbol, tf, -1) returns 0.0, so the swing level silently becomes ZERO.
// WHY THAT IS WORSE THAN A CRASH: the comparisons are directional. A structure
// test like (dir > 0 && sw1 >= sw2) || (dir < 0 && sw1 <= sw2) with sw1 = 0 is
// FALSE for every buy and TRUE for every sell, because 0 <= 4600 always holds.
// So a data hiccup does not disable the test, it BIASES IT TOWARDS SELLS, quietly,
// on structure reads, reclaim levels, sweep detection and shakeout detection.
// This is the 12th instance of the §4 pattern in the handover's own list and it
// was recorded as "~15 sites"; the real count is 24.
// FIX: clamp the index to a valid bar. Index 0 is the current bar -- a sane, real
// price -- instead of -1, which is not a bar at all. Every other argument and all
// surrounding logic is untouched.
// NO OFF-SWITCH, deliberately: there is no scenario in which reading bar -1 is the
// intended behaviour. This is a correctness fix, not a strategy change.
// (v18.92 AT) set at the entry site, consumed and cleared by LotFor(). A ratio,
// never an absolute size, so it can only ever scale whatever the ladder decided.
double g_wpShrink = 1.0;
// (v19.01 BH) set at the entry site when the bar that TRIGGERED the signal is wider
// than the stop itself. Consumed and cleared by LotFor().
bool   g_spikeCut = false;

// (v18.86 AM) his balance ladder. Returns the floor and ceiling for the CURRENT
// equity. See the note at T_BalanceTiers.
void BalanceTier(double &lo, double &hi)
{
   lo = T_LotFloorHard; hi = T_LotCeilHard;
   if(!T_LotTiersByEquity) return;
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0.0) return;
   // (v18.91 AQ) BUG FIX. This returned a 0.01 FLOOR under the mid-equity tier.
   // His standing rule, restated again this session, is 0.02 minimum on every
   // signal. The 0.01 was reaching live size in two places: thin-hour sizing
   // (which sets size = tier floor, ~9 hours a day) and the dead-chop multiplier
   // (0.03 x 0.45 = 0.0135, which rounds to 0.01). Floor is now his floor.
   // The CEILING ladder is untouched -- under T_TierMidEquity it is still 0.03,
   // so his 0.05 is unreachable at that equity. Set T_LotTiersByEquity = false
   // for a flat 0.02/0.05. OFF-SWITCH: T_LotTiersByEquity = false.
   if(eq < T_TierMidEquity)      { lo = T_LotFloorHard; hi = 0.03; }
   else if(eq < T_TierTopEquity) { lo = 0.02; hi = 0.04; }
   else                          { lo = 0.02; hi = 0.05; }
}

int SafeHighest(string sym, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int mode, int count, int start)
{
   if(count < 1) count = 1;
   if(start < 0) start = 0;
   int ix = iHighest(sym, tf, (ENUM_SERIESMODE)mode, count, start);
   return (ix < 0) ? start : ix;
}
int SafeLowest(string sym, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int mode, int count, int start)
{
   if(count < 1) count = 1;
   if(start < 0) start = 0;
   int ix = iLowest(sym, tf, (ENUM_SERIESMODE)mode, count, start);
   return (ix < 0) ? start : ix;
}
double SpreadUSD()  { return SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK) - SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID); }

double Buf(int h, int b, int s)
{
   if(h == INVALID_HANDLE) return 0;
   double a[];
   if(CopyBuffer(h, b, s, 1, a) < 1) return 0;
   return a[0];
}

void RefreshStops()
{
   long sl = SymbolInfoInteger(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_STOPS_LEVEL);
   long fz = SymbolInfoInteger(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_FREEZE_LEVEL);
   g_minStop = (double)MathMax(sl, fz) * _Point;
   if(g_minStop < 10 * _Point) g_minStop = 10 * _Point;
}

// (v18.07) ENTRY-ONLY stop floor. Deliberately NOT inside ClampStops(), because
// that funnel is also used by the trail and by the breakeven move -- a floor
// there would stop the trail ever tightening, which would be a worse bug than
// the one it fixes. Applied at the TREND entry sites only.
double FloorStopAtBirth(int dir, double px, double sl)
{
   if(T_SlFloorPts <= 0.0 || sl <= 0.0 || dir == 0) return sl;
   // A "point" here is HIS point: one whole unit of gold price, the thing that
   // makes 0.01 lots = $1. NOT MQL5's _Point (0.01 on a 2-digit quote). Using
   // _Point would have made a 4.0 "point" floor a 0.04 floor -- 100x too small,
   // which is the same class of unit bug as the stop being too close in the
   // first place.
   // ═══ (v18.60) THE REASON NOTHING SCALES: R IS A CONSTANT ═══
   // HIS POINT: "some trades do 0.1 points, some do 40 points -- we need to
   // capture every trend, each to their own." This is the line that makes that
   // impossible, and it is upstream of nearly every threshold in the file.
   // The stop floor was a FIXED 2.50 points with no volatility term. The only
   // other floor site (the v18.08 stop repair, ~line 7990) reads
   //     MathMax(0.40 * atr, T_SlFloorPts)
   // and 0.40 * atr only wins when ATR > 6.25 points, which M1 gold essentially
   // never reaches. So the stop distance is ALWAYS 2.50 and R is a CONSTANT.
   // CONSEQUENCE: every R-keyed threshold in this EA is secretly a fixed point
   // threshold wearing R's clothing --
   //     0.30R giveback arm = 0.84 pts     0.60R T_MinBankR   = 1.68 pts
   //     2.50R MOM-STALL    = 7.00 pts     3.00R PEAK-BANK    = 8.40 pts
   // In a quiet 0.4-ATR session where the whole leg is worth 0.5 points, 1.68 pts
   // is unreachable, so nothing banks and every trade rides to the stop. In a
   // 4-ATR news trend where the leg runs 40 points, MOM-STALL banks at 17% of the
   // move. Both of his complaints, one cause. Eighteen versions of tuning those
   // numbers could not work, because they were being tuned against a fixed ruler.
   // FIX: floor the stop at T_SlFloorAtrMult ATR, with T_SlFloorPtsAbs as a hard
   // broker/spread minimum underneath it.
   // CALIBRATED TO CHANGE NOTHING TODAY: M1 gold ATR ~1.0 (measured from his own
   // 23:20 log line, 1.60 pts = 1.56x ATR), so 2.50 x 1.0 = 2.50 -- byte-identical
   // to v18.59 at yesterday's volatility. It only diverges where it should:
   //     ATR 0.4  -> stop 1.20 (abs floor), R ~1.5   -> 0.60R = 0.9 pts, reachable
   //     ATR 1.0  -> stop 2.50, R ~2.8               -> unchanged from v18.59
   //     ATR 3.0  -> stop 7.50, R ~7.8               -> MOM-STALL at 2.5R = 19 pts
   // So a 40-point news leg is no longer banked at 7 points, and a 0.5-point Asian
   // leg is no longer unbankable. Same code, R does the scaling.
   // LIMIT I AM NOT HIDING: this reads M1 ATR at both sites regardless of the
   // trade's own clock, matching the existing precedent in CloseTagged. For an M5
   // trade the floor is therefore keyed to M1 volatility -- correct in direction,
   // understated in size. Fixing that needs the clock threaded through the call
   // sites and is a separate edit.
   // REVERT: T_SlFloorAtrMult = 0 restores the flat 2.50 point floor exactly.
   double have = (dir > 0) ? (px - sl) : (sl - px);
   double want = T_SlFloorPts * 1.0;
   if(T_SlFloorAtrMult > 0.0)
   {
      int fk = -1;
      for(int i = 0; i < SC_TFN; i++) if(g_scTF[i] == PERIOD_M1) { fk = i; break; }
      double fatr = (fk >= 0) ? Buf(g_scAtr[fk], 0, 1) : 0.0;
      if(fatr > 0.0)
      {
         want = MathMax(T_SlFloorAtrMult * fatr, T_SlFloorPtsAbs);
         // (v18.66) never tighter than the spread can chew through.
         if(T_SpreadFloorMult > 0.0)
         {
            double sp = SpreadPtsNow();
            if(sp > 0.0) want = MathMax(want, T_SpreadFloorMult * sp);
         }
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] STOP FLOOR SCALED: ATR %.2f x %.2f = %.2f pts (abs min %.2f). Flat floor was %.2f.",
                        fatr, T_SlFloorAtrMult, want, T_SlFloorPtsAbs, T_SlFloorPts);
      }
   }
   if(have >= want) return sl;
   double out = (dir > 0) ? px - want : px + want;
   if(InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[TREND] BIRTH STOP WIDENED: %.2f pts is inside the noise; using the %.1f pt floor (%.2f -> %.2f).",
                  have, T_SlFloorPts, sl, out);
   return out;
}

void ClampStops(int dir, double px, double &sl, double &tp)
{
   // BUG FIX #9: this normalised tp even when tp was 0 (APEX passes 0 on
   // purpose — it has no take profit). NormalizeDouble(0) is still 0 so it
   // happened to survive, but the intent was fragile: any future change here
   // would have silently given APEX a take-profit and destroyed the entire
   // point of the engine. Made explicit.
   double slWas = sl;
   if(dir > 0)
   {
      if(sl > 0 && (px - sl) < g_minStop) sl = px - g_minStop;
      if(tp > 0 && (tp - px) < g_minStop) tp = px + g_minStop;
   }
   else
   {
      if(sl > 0 && (sl - px) < g_minStop) sl = px + g_minStop;
      if(tp > 0 && (px - tp) < g_minStop) tp = px - g_minStop;
   }
   // (v17.15) A widened stop is not a neutral event: the target is unchanged,
   // so the R:R of the trade just got worse without anything in the strategy
   // asking for it. Silent before; loud now.
   if(T_LogStopClamp && slWas > 0 && MathAbs(sl - slWas) > 1e-10)
      PrintFormat("STOP CLAMPED by broker minimum: %.5f -> %.5f (%.1f -> %.1f pts). "
                  "R:R degraded — same target, wider stop.",
                  slWas, sl, MathAbs(px - slWas) / _Point, MathAbs(px - sl) / _Point);
   sl = NP(sl);
   tp = (tp > 0) ? NP(tp) : 0.0;   // 0 means "no take profit". Keep it that way.
}

//================================================================
//  (v17.15) SYMBOL-AGNOSTIC RISK-NORMALISED LOT.
//  Given the stop distance in PRICE, return the lot that risks
//  T_AutoRiskPct of equity on THIS symbol, clamped to the broker's
//  min/max/step. Uses LossPerLot() (which already reads the symbol's
//  own tick value) rather than any gold-specific constant, so the
//  same rule produces correct size on XAUUSD, an FX pair or an index.
//  Returns 0 when disabled, so callers fall through to the ladder.
//================================================================
double AutoRiskLots(double slDist)
{
   if(!T_AutoCalibrate || T_AutoRiskPct <= 0 || slDist <= 0) return 0.0;
   double lpl = LossPerLot(slDist);
   if(lpl <= 0) return 0.0;
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0) return 0.0;
   double budget = eq * (T_AutoRiskPct / 100.0);
   double lots   = budget / lpl;

   double minL = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
   double maxL = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX);
   double step = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP);
   if(step <= 0) step = 0.01;
   if(minL <= 0) minL = 0.01;
   lots = MathFloor(lots / step) * step;
   lots = NormalizeDouble(lots, 2);
   if(lots < minL) lots = minL;                 // broker floor; MarginOkFor still vets it
   if(maxL > 0 && lots > maxL) lots = maxL;
   return lots;
}

// (v17.98) THE NOISE BAND, IN POINTS, FOR THE CLOCK THAT OWNS THE TRADE.
// A stop or a bank placed inside this distance is not protecting profit, it is
// sampling noise. Every peak-proportional threshold is floored by it. ATR-relative
// by construction, which also retires open item 5 for these three call sites.
double NoiseFloorPts(double atrTf)
{
   if(T_NoiseFloorAtr <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double n = (atrTf > 0.0) ? T_NoiseFloorAtr * atrTf : T_LegNoisePts;
   return SpreadUSD() + n;
}

double LossPerLot(double slDist)
{
   if(g_tickSz <= 0 || g_tickVal <= 0 || slDist <= 0) return 0;
   return (slDist / g_tickSz) * g_tickVal;
}

// A normal pullback vs a stop-hunt wick: a genuine spike bar has a range well
// beyond the recent average. Only THEN does the trailing stop get extra room —
// bounded and capped, and it snaps back the instant the next bar is normal
// again. This does not loosen the stop generally; it only stops a single
// outlier wick from clipping a trade the broader trend is still supporting.
double SpikeBuffer(int e)
{
   double atrE = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
   if(atrE <= 0) return 0;
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
   return SpikeBufferTF(tf, atrE);
}

// Overload for TREND, which now manages each open position on ITS OWN
// timeframe (M1/M3/M5) rather than one fixed engine timeframe — same spike
// logic, explicit timeframe and ATR passed in instead of read from Cfg[e].
double SpikeBufferTF(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, double atrE)
{
   if(atrE <= 0) return 0;
   double sumRange = 0;
   for(int i = 2; i <= 9; i++)
      sumRange += (iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i) - iLow(_Symbol, tf, i));
   double avgRange = sumRange / 8.0;
   double curRange = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1) - iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   if(avgRange <= 0 || curRange <= avgRange * InpSpikeRatio) return 0;   // not a spike, no buffer
   double excess = MathMin((curRange / avgRange) - InpSpikeRatio, 1.5); // capped
   return excess * InpSpikeBufferAtr * atrE;
}

// Only safe on a NETTING account. On a hedging account, adding a top-up creates
// a SEPARATE ticket — closing "just the top-up" by lot size against the ORIGINAL
// ticket would slice into the wrong trade. Rather than guess, this checks the
// account mode once and simply skips the feature on hedging accounts.
// (v16.46) Per-clock DEMA length. Returns InpDemaLen unchanged when the opt-in
// is off, so the default build is bit-identical to v16.45 on this path.
int DemaLenFor(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!InpDemaPerClock) return InpDemaLen;
   switch(tf)
   {
      case PERIOD_M1: return 60;
      case PERIOD_M3: return 100;
      default:        return InpDemaLen;
   }
}

// (v16.55) Largest stop distance, in PRICE, that keeps one trade inside
// T_MaxStopRiskPct of equity at the given lot size. Returns 0.0 = no opinion.
double MaxStopDistance(double lots)
{
   if(!T_MaxStopCash || lots <= 0.0 || T_MaxStopRiskPct <= 0.0) return 0.0;   // (v16.62) 0 = disabled
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double budget = eq * T_MaxStopRiskPct / 100.0;
   double px = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   double loss1 = 0.0;                       // cost of a 1.0-price-unit stop at this lot size
   if(px <= 0.0 || !OrderCalcProfit(ORDER_TYPE_BUY, _Symbol, lots, px, px - 1.0, loss1)) return 0.0;
   loss1 = MathAbs(loss1);
   if(loss1 <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   return budget / loss1;
}

// Pulls a stop back to the cash-bounded maximum when the signal line is further
// out than the account can afford. Never widens a stop, only tightens it, and
// never crosses the broker's minimum stop level.
double CapStopToCash(double entryPx, double slPx, int dir, double lots)
{
   double maxD = MaxStopDistance(lots);
   if(maxD <= 0.0) return slPx;
   double haveD = (dir > 0) ? (entryPx - slPx) : (slPx - entryPx);
   if(haveD <= maxD) return slPx;
   double capped = (dir > 0) ? entryPx - maxD : entryPx + maxD;
   PrintFormat("[RISK] STOP CAPPED — signal line was %.2f pts away (%.2f%% of equity at %.2f lots); pulled to %.2f pts to stay inside T_MaxStopRiskPct %.2f%%. The stop is no longer at the signal line for this trade.",
               haveD, (haveD/MathMax(maxD,0.0001))*T_MaxStopRiskPct, lots, maxD, T_MaxStopRiskPct);
   return NP(capped);
}

bool IsNettingAccount()
{
   return (ENUM_ACCOUNT_MARGIN_MODE)AccountInfoInteger(ACCOUNT_MARGIN_MODE) == ACCOUNT_MARGIN_MODE_RETAIL_NETTING;
}

// When the DEMA also confirms an already-open trade's direction, add a small
// extra lot as a bonus — the DEMA agreeing is real extra confirmation the
// trend is genuine. Fires ONCE per open trade, and only on a netting account
// (see IsNettingAccount). The top-up is deliberately tiny: it is a bonus on
// top of the real position, not a second bet.
void DemaTopUp(int e, ulong tk, int dir)
{
   if(!InpDemaTopUp || !IsNettingAccount()) return;
   string key = "TOPUP_" + (string)tk;
   if(GlobalVariableCheck(key)) return;   // already added for this trade

   // Scalp spans M1/M3/M5 per-position — Cfg[E_SCALP].tf is only a generic M5
   // reference. Use the tick THIS specific position is actually on, or the DEMA
   // check and the bank threshold both silently run on the wrong timeframe.
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
   int demaHandle = hDema[e];
   if(e == E_SCALP)
   {
      int k = ScalpTFOf(tk);
      if(k >= 0) { tf = g_scTF[k]; demaHandle = g_scDema[k]; }
   }

   double demaV = (e == E_SCALP) ? Buf(demaHandle, 0, 1) : DemaVal(e);
   if(demaV <= 0) return;
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   bool agrees = (dir > 0) ? (c1 > demaV) : (c1 < demaV);
   if(!agrees) return;

   double px = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   bool ok = (dir > 0) ? trade.Buy(InpTopUpLots, _Symbol, px, 0, 0, "DEMA top-up")
                        : trade.Sell(InpTopUpLots, _Symbol, px, 0, 0, "DEMA top-up");
   if(ok)
   {
      GlobalVariableSet(key, (double)dir);
      GlobalVariableSet(key + "_PX", px);
      PrintFormat("[%s] DEMA TOP-UP: +%.2f lots @ %.2f — extra confirmation. Banks on a small profit, separate from the main trade.",
                   Cfg[e].name, InpTopUpLots, px);
   }
}

// Bank the top-up the moment it's shown a small profit — it is a bonus, not
// meant to ride the whole trade the way the base position does.
void DemaTopUpBank(int e, ulong tk, int dir)
{
   string key = "TOPUP_" + (string)tk;
   if(!GlobalVariableCheck(key)) return;
   double topPx = GlobalVariableGet(key + "_PX");
   double px    = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   int atrHandle = hAtr[e];
   if(e == E_SCALP)
   {
      int k = ScalpTFOf(tk);
      if(k >= 0) atrHandle = g_scAtr[k];   // THIS position's own timeframe ATR, not the generic M5 one
   }
   double atrE  = Buf(atrHandle, 0, 1);
   if(atrE <= 0) return;
   double moved = (dir > 0) ? (px - topPx) : (topPx - px);
   if(moved >= InpTopUpBankAtr * atrE)
   {
      if(trade.PositionClosePartial(tk, InpTopUpLots))
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] DEMA top-up banked: +%.2f moved in its favour.", Cfg[e].name, moved);
         GlobalVariableDel(key);
         GlobalVariableDel(key + "_PX");
      }
   }
}

// Per-engine sizing. Returns 0 if the trade cannot be sized within its own cap.
// ═══════════════════════════ AUTO-SCALE ENGINE ═══════════════════════════
// Everything sizes itself to whatever the balance happens to be. You never touch
// a lot size again. Grow the account and the EA follows it up on its own.
//
//   BALANCE      TIER      WHAT HAPPENS
//   under 250    MICRO     min lot, true risk % printed on every entry
//   250 - 1k     SMALL     risk-based where possible, floored at min lot
//   1k - 10k     NORMAL    pure risk-based sizing
//   over 10k     LARGE     pure risk-based sizing
//
// SL and TP already auto-adjust to the volatility regime (AdaptMults).
// Per-engine risk already auto-adjusts to proven expectancy (LiveRisk).
// This closes the last gap: SIZE adjusts to CAPITAL.
string AccountTier()
{
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq < 250)   return "MICRO";
   if(eq < 1000)  return "SMALL";
   if(eq < 10000) return "NORMAL";
   return "LARGE";
}

// Total lots this account may hold, at ANY balance. Replaces the fixed
// InpMaxTotalLots — a number you had to remember to raise every time the account
// grew, and therefore a number that was permanently wrong.
// (v16.48) RISK-DERIVED LOT CEILING.
// AutoMaxLotsPer1k 0.35 is an equity FRACTION and it does not know what a stop
// costs. On a GBP1,000 account it permits 0.35 lots — GBP157 on a single 6pt M1
// stop, 15.7% of the account in one trade. The only thing holding size down was
// TierMaxLots 0.03, i.e. a number that has to be remembered. Now the ceiling is
// also bounded by InpRiskPctPerTrade against the WIDEST clock stop in play, so
// it self-corrects at every balance and cannot be defeated by raising a tier.
double RiskCeilingLots()
{
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0.0) return AutoMinLotFloor;
   double budget = eq * InpRiskPctPerTrade / 100.0;
   double px     = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   double widest = 20.0;                       // M15 stop in price terms, the worst case
   double loss1  = 0.0;
   if(px <= 0.0 || !OrderCalcProfit(ORDER_TYPE_BUY, _Symbol, 1.0, px, px - widest, loss1))
      return AutoMinLotFloor;
   loss1 = MathAbs(loss1);
   if(loss1 <= 0.0) return AutoMinLotFloor;
   double lots = budget / loss1;
   lots = MathFloor(lots / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   return lots;                                 // may legitimately come back 0.0
}

double AutoMaxLots()
{
   if(!AutoScale) return InpMaxTotalLots;
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   double lots = (eq / 1000.0) * AutoMaxLotsPer1k;
   lots = MathFloor(lots / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   if(InpRiskAdaptive)
   {
      double rc = RiskCeilingLots();
      if(rc < lots) lots = rc;                  // whichever cap is TIGHTER wins
   }
   if(lots < AutoMinLotFloor) lots = AutoMinLotFloor;   // always allow at least one min lot
   return lots;
}

// What is actually AT RISK right now, in cash, across every open position.
//
// Not lots — RISK. Lots tell you nothing on their own: a 0.10 lot with a $1 stop
// is safer than a 0.01 lot with a $30 stop. This is the number that matters, and
// it is the one nothing in the old EA was tracking.
//
// Positions whose stop is already at or past breakeven are EXCLUDED — they can
// no longer lose, so they do not consume the risk budget. That is what lets the
// EA keep adding into a winning trend without ever raising the real downside.
//================================================================
//  (v17.13) PRE-SEND MARGIN GUARD.
//  Returns true if `lots` can actually be opened while leaving
//  T_MarginFloorPct of equity free. Uses OrderCalcMargin so it is
//  correct for ANY symbol and any leverage — nothing here assumes
//  gold, which is what makes the second pair safe to add.
//================================================================
//================================================================
//  (v17.33 EXECUTION AUDIT — the missing half of the entry-retry fix.)
//  Every ratchet/giveback/breakeven fix shipped today computes the RIGHT
//  protective stop. None of that matters if the request to actually SET it
//  on the broker's side can fail silently. Audit found FIVE PositionModify
//  call sites: one logged failure only when InpVerboseLog happened to be on,
//  one logged unconditionally but never retried, one ignored the result
//  outright, and none retried a transient rejection the way order-SEND now
//  does. A failed SL update means the EA's internal model of "the floor is
//  now at X" and the ACTUAL broker-side stop silently diverge — the exact
//  gap that turns a correct decision into no protection at all.
//  This wraps every site in one place: retry on transient retcodes (reusing
//  the entry-retry constants), and ALWAYS log a failure — a protective stop
//  that didn't take is safety-critical, not a debug curiosity gated behind
//  a verbosity flag.
//================================================================
bool ModifySLTP(ulong tk, double newSl, double newTp, string ctx)
{
   for(int attempt = 0; attempt <= T_OrderRetryN; attempt++)
   {
      if(trade.PositionModify(tk, newSl, newTp)) return true;
      uint rc = trade.ResultRetcode();
      bool transient = (rc == TRADE_RETCODE_REQUOTE || rc == TRADE_RETCODE_PRICE_CHANGED ||
                         rc == TRADE_RETCODE_TIMEOUT  || rc == TRADE_RETCODE_CONNECTION ||
                         rc == TRADE_RETCODE_TRADE_DISABLED);
      if(!transient || attempt >= T_OrderRetryN)
      {
         PrintFormat("MODIFY FAILED #%I64u (%s): %d %s — protective stop did NOT update. "
                     "Broker-side stop and EA's intended floor are now OUT OF SYNC.",
                     tk, ctx, rc, trade.ResultRetcodeDescription());
         return false;
      }
      Sleep(T_OrderRetryMs);
   }
   return false;
}

bool MarginOkFor(double lots, int dir)
{
   if(!T_MarginGuard || lots <= 0) return true;
   double eq   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   double free = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_MARGIN_FREE);
   if(eq <= 0) return true;                        // cannot judge; do not block
   double px = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                         : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   ENUM_ORDER_TYPE ot = (dir > 0) ? ORDER_TYPE_BUY : ORDER_TYPE_SELL;
   double need = 0;
   if(!OrderCalcMargin(ot, _Symbol, lots, px, need)) return true;   // unknown; do not block
   double floorCash = eq * (T_MarginFloorPct / 100.0);
   bool ok = (free - need) >= floorCash;
   if(!ok && T_MarginLogRefusal)
      PrintFormat("MARGIN REFUSAL: %.2f lots needs %.2f, free %.2f, floor %.2f (equity %.2f). "
                  "This is NOT a signal gate — reduce lots or raise the balance.",
                  lots, need, free, floorCash, eq);
   return ok;
}

//================================================================
//  (v17.13) CROSS-SYMBOL EXPOSURE. OpenRiskCash() below filters
//  POSITION_SYMBOL != _Symbol, which is correct for per-pair logic
//  but means that running a SECOND instance on a second pair gives
//  each instance its own full AutoMaxExposurePct budget — 25% + 25%
//  = 50% of one account at risk, with neither instance aware of the
//  other. Same for InpMaxTotalLots (0.25 each = 0.50). This reads
//  the WHOLE account so the account-level ceiling stays account-level.
//================================================================
double OpenRiskCashAllSymbols()
{
   double total = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      double vol  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      double open = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double sl   = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      int    dir  = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(sl <= 0 || vol <= 0) continue;
      bool locked = (dir > 0) ? (sl >= open) : (sl <= open);
      if(locked) continue;
      string sym = PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL);
      double tv   = SymbolInfoDouble(sym, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE);
      double tsz  = SymbolInfoDouble(sym, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE);
      if(tv <= 0 || tsz <= 0) continue;
      total += MathAbs(open - sl) / tsz * tv * vol;
   }
   return total;
}

double OpenRiskCash()
{
   double total = 0;
   double atrRef = Buf(hAtr[E_TREND], 0, 1);
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;

      double px    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_CURRENT);
      double sl    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      double open  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double vol   = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      int    dir   = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;

      if(sl <= 0)
      {
         // no stop = we have no idea what this can cost. Assume 3 ATR and be scared.
         if(atrRef > 0) total += vol * LossPerLot(3.0 * atrRef);
         continue;
      }

      bool locked = (dir > 0) ? (sl >= open) : (sl <= open);
      if(locked) continue;   // cannot lose any more — free to hold

      total += vol * LossPerLot(MathAbs(px - sl));
   }
   return total;
}

//================================================================
//  (v18.25) PER-SYMBOL LOT NORMALISATION
//  His ask: "the EA needs to auto adjust to any pair it's added to -- 0.03 on
//  EURUSD is different to XAUUSD."
//  Correct: T_FixedLots is calibrated so that 0.01 gold = $1 per point. On EURUSD
//  0.01 lots is $0.10 per pip, so the same number is ~10x smaller in risk terms;
//  on an index CFD it can be far larger. Rather than asking him to remember a
//  different number per symbol, the fixed lot is scaled by the symbol's own
//  money-per-point against gold's reference, so the RISK stays what he set.
//  Gold itself is unaffected (ratio = 1.0). OFF-SWITCH: T_NormalizeLots = false.
//================================================================
input bool   T_NormalizeLots = true;
input double T_RefValPerLot  = 100.0;   // money per 1.00 price unit per 1.00 lot on gold

double SymbolLotScale()
{
   if(!T_NormalizeLots) return 1.0;
   double tv = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE);
   double ts = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE);
   if(tv <= 0.0 || ts <= 0.0) return 1.0;
   double valPerUnit = tv / ts;                 // money per 1.00 price move per 1.00 lot
   if(valPerUnit <= 0.0) return 1.0;
   double scale = T_RefValPerLot / valPerUnit;
   // Bounded hard: a bad broker spec must never silently 50x the position.
   return MathMax(0.1, MathMin(10.0, scale));
}

// (v18.73 N) Forward declaration. LotFor() below reads the M1/M5 Supertrend
// agreement for the confluence size vote, but ScalpST's own prototype sits at
// line ~8892 -- far below this point. MQL5 requires declaration before use, so
// without this line the confluence block will not compile. Caught on self-review.
void ScalpST(int k, double &val, int &dir);

double LotFor(int eng, double slDist)
{
   // (v18.04) pinned size wins over everything below. Deliberately the FIRST line
   // of the function so no later ladder/tier/multiplier can re-inflate it -- the
   // failure mode documented at the dead-chop trim site.
   if(T_FixedLots > 0.0)
   {
      double fstep = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP);
      double fmin  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
      double fmax  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX);
      // (v18.25) scaled to the symbol so the RISK matches what he set, not the
      // raw number. Gold -> x1.0; see SymbolLotScale().
      // (v18.28) extended fills are sized down rather than skipped. g_entryExtScale
      // is set by DeferReady() and consumed once, here.
      // (v18.35) BALANCE-TIERED SIZE, his rule: "anything above 120 trades 0.03,
      // 150 is 0.05, in the middle can be 0.04." So the number is derived from the
      // account rather than typed in and forgotten, and it steps DOWN automatically
      // after a drawdown instead of keeping full size on a shrinking balance.
      double baseLots = T_FixedLots;
      // (v18.69 D) CONTINUOUS RISK-ANCHORED SIZE. Takes precedence over the tier
      // ladder. See the note at T_ContinuousSize. Clamped to his standing
      // 0.02 / 0.05 rule at BOTH ends, so no balance and no risk % can push the
      // size outside it -- which is what the old ladder did below 100.
      // (v19.05 BU) agnostic mode implies the risk-anchored path: the tier ladder
      // below is a table of gold lot sizes and means nothing on another symbol.
      bool symAgn = T_SymAgnosticSize && !GoldSymbol();
      if((T_ContinuousSize || symAgn) && T_RiskPctPerTrade > 0.0)
      {
         double bal  = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_BALANCE);
         double vpp  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE)
                     / MathMax(SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE), 0.0000001);
         // slDist is the caller's real stop distance; fall back to the floor when
         // it cannot be established, so a bad read sizes DOWN, never up.
         double stopPts = (slDist > 0.0) ? slDist : MathMax(T_SlFloorPts, 1.0);
         if(bal > 0.0 && vpp > 0.0 && stopPts > 0.0)
         {
            double want = (bal * (T_RiskPctPerTrade / 100.0)) / (stopPts * vpp);
            // on a non-gold symbol the 0.02/0.05 pair is not his rule, it is
            // gold's units. Broker min/max is the only honest clamp there.
            if(symAgn)
            {
               double aMin = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
               double aMax = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX);
               baseLots = MathMax(aMin, MathMin(aMax > 0.0 ? aMax : want, want));
            }
            else
               baseLots = MathMax(T_LotFloorHard, MathMin(T_LotCeilHard, want));
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[SIZE] continuous: balance %.2f, risk %.1f%%, stop %.2f pts -> raw %.4f "
                           "-> %.2f lots (clamped %.2f-%.2f).",
                           bal, T_RiskPctPerTrade, stopPts, want, baseLots,
                           T_LotFloorHard, T_LotCeilHard);
         }
         else baseLots = symAgn ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN) : T_LotFloorHard;
      }
      else if(T_BalanceTiers)
      {
         double bal = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_BALANCE);
         if(bal >= T_TierBal3)      baseLots = T_TierLots3;   // 150+ -> 0.05
         else if(bal >= T_TierBal2) baseLots = T_TierLots2;   // 120+ -> 0.04
         else if(bal >= T_TierBal1) baseLots = T_TierLots1;   // 100+ -> 0.03
         // (v18.69 D) was SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN (0.01) here, which broke the standing
         // 0.02 floor on a sub-100 account. It must run from 100 as a minimum.
         else                       baseLots = MathMax(T_LotFloorHard,
                                                       SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN));
      }
      // (v18.58) DOWNWARD ONLY. MathMin, so v18.04's rule that nothing below the
      // top of this function may re-inflate a pinned size still holds absolutely.
      if(T_RangeClusterTrim && T_RangeClusterLots > 0.0 && ClusterRangeNow())
      {
         double preCl = baseLots;
         baseLots = MathMin(baseLots, T_RangeClusterLots);
         if(baseLots < preCl)
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] CLUSTER RANGE: last %d signals inside %.1f pts -> %.2f -> %.2f lots.",
                        T_BandLookback, T_RangeClusterBandPts, preCl, baseLots);
      }
      // ══════════ v18.73 (N) M5 BECOMES A SIZE VOTE, NOT AN ENTRY ══════════
      // His instruction, verbatim: "m5 entries are being an issue currently turn
      // them off they should act as say a signal to put an extra 0.01 on times
      // when they match with m1".
      // SC_Slow = false removes the M5 entry clock. This is the other half: when
      // M5's Supertrend agrees with M1's, the entry gets one extra lot step.
      // WHY THIS READER: ScalpST(k,...) is the same function the ratchet's
      // otherClockAgrees vote already uses to ask "does another clock agree?".
      // Reusing it rather than deriving a second M5 reader -- the repeated lesson.
      // DIRECTION-AGNOSTIC ON PURPOSE: LotFor() has no dir argument, and it does
      // not need one. It asks only whether M1 and M5 point the SAME way; the
      // entry being sized is on M1 by construction, so agreement between the two
      // clocks IS agreement with this trade.
      // THE +0.01 IS HIS NUMBER, not derived by me. It is one lot step, and the
      // result is still clamped to his standing 0.02/0.05 rule by the lines below,
      // so confluence can never push size outside the range he set.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_M5ConfluenceLots = 0.
      if(T_M5ConfluenceLots > 0.0 && SC_TFN >= 3)
      {
         double svM1, svM5; int sdM1, sdM5;
         ScalpST(0, svM1, sdM1);              // M1
         ScalpST(SC_TFN - 1, svM5, sdM5);     // M5
         if(sdM1 != 0 && sdM1 == sdM5)
         {
            double preCf = baseLots;
            baseLots = MathMin(T_LotCeilHard, baseLots + T_M5ConfluenceLots);
            if(baseLots > preCf)
               PrintFormat("[SIZE] M5 CONFLUENCE: M1 and M5 both %s -> %.2f -> %.2f lots.",
                           (sdM1 > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT"), preCf, baseLots);
         }
      }
      // ══════════ v18.73 (T) LATE-IN-LEG SIZE TRIM — NOT A REFUSAL ══════════
      // The lever is SIZE, never refusal (his rule 1). Every Pine signal is still
      // taken; a signal fired in the last third of a leg is simply taken small,
      // because that is where the reversal catches it. Thresholds are the measured
      // separation in his own tickets -- winners 6/24/50%, losers 81/81/100%.
      // This is what stops nine fills landing at one price at a leg high: the first
      // fill sizes normally, and by the time price is pinned to the extreme every
      // further fill is at the floor.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_LegLateTrimFrac = 0.
      if(T_LegLateTrimFrac > 0.0 && g_legFrac >= 0.0)
      {
         double preLg = baseLots;
         if(g_legFrac >= T_LegVeryLateFrac)
            baseLots = T_LotFloorHard;                                  // pinned to the extreme
         else if(g_legFrac >= T_LegLateFrac)
            baseLots = MathMax(T_LotFloorHard, baseLots * T_LegLateTrimFrac);
         if(baseLots < preLg)
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] LATE IN LEG: entry sits at %.0f%% of the %d-bar leg "
                        "-> %.2f -> %.2f lots. Signal still taken, taken small.",
                        g_legFrac * 100.0, T_LegFracBars, preLg, baseLots);
      }
      // (v18.73 V) the signal candle was won by the other side -> take it small.
      if(T_WickSideTrim && g_wickAgainst && T_WickTrimFrac > 0.0)
      {
         double preWk = baseLots;
         baseLots = MathMax(T_LotFloorHard, baseLots * T_WickTrimFrac);
         if(baseLots < preWk)
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] WICK AGAINST: %.2f -> %.2f lots.", preWk, baseLots);
      }
      // ══════ (v18.83 AJ) THE SIZE LEVER, RECONNECTED ══════
      // Applied here because this block RETURNS below, so the multipliers further
      // down LotFor() can never be reached while T_FixedLots > 0. Clamped at both
      // ends to his standing 0.02 / 0.05, so no flag and no multiplier can push
      // size outside the range he set. See the note at T_FixedLotsAdaptive.
      // (v18.85 AL) thin hours first, so the chop/premium ladder below can still
      // move size but never above the floor while the book is thin.
      bool thinHour = false;
      if(T_ThinHourSizing)
      {
         MqlDateTime gt; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), gt);
         int h = gt.hour;
         thinHour = (T_ThinStartGmtHour > T_ThinEndGmtHour)
                    ? (h >= T_ThinStartGmtHour || h < T_ThinEndGmtHour)
                    : (h >= T_ThinStartGmtHour && h <  T_ThinEndGmtHour);
      }
      // (v19.15 CE) the drift-alignment cut lives in Open(), NOT here. LotFor's
      // signature is LotFor(int eng, double slDist) -- there is no `dir` in scope,
      // so referencing it here would not compile. Same class as the v18.97 note
      // above about calling LotFor with the wrong arguments. See DriftSizeFrac().

      // ══════ (v19.16 CF) SESSION WEIGHTING WAS APPLIED NOWHERE ══════
      // Two session-sizing mechanisms exist in this file. Neither ran.
      //   1. SessionMult() (prime 1.25 / good 1.10 / dead 0.70) is never called.
      //      v16.60 switched T_SessionWeight off, reasoning that "SessionQuality()
      //      inside LotFor() already applies the session weighting -- leaving both
      //      on double-counted the same information."
      //   2. SessionQuality() (overlap 1.20 / London 1.05 / thin 0.70 / Asian 0.80)
      //      IS called in LotFor -- as `cash *= SessionQuality()`. But `cash`
      //      belongs to the risk-based path further down. LotFor RETURNS above that
      //      whenever T_FixedLots > 0.0, and his T_FixedLots is 0.03.
      // So the first was disabled in favour of the second, and the second sits past
      // a return that always fires at his settings. The file flags exactly this
      // class at line 1107 -- "THE LEVER IS DISCONNECTED. LotFor() begins with
      // if(T_FixedLots > 0.0)" -- and CoordBoost(), g_lastConfMult, g_scPushMult
      // and hMult are all stranded behind the same return.
      //
      // Not a small miss. SessionQuality() prices the London-NY overlap at 1.20 and
      // the thin hours at 0.70 -- a 1.7x spread between the best and worst tape of
      // the day, on an EA whose worst measured hour lost GBP78.86 of a GBP159.79
      // day. Wiring it here puts it in the path that actually executes. Using
      // SessionQuality() and not SessionMult() for exactly v16.60's reason: one
      // mechanism, and the older tested one.
      if(T_SessionInFixed)
      {
         double sqF = SessionQuality();
         if(sqF > 0.0 && MathAbs(sqF - 1.0) > 0.001)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] SESSION %02d:00 GMT quality %.2f -> %.2f -> %.2f lots. This multiplier has "
                        "existed since v16 and never once ran: it lives past the T_FixedLots return.",
                        (int)(TimeGMT() / 3600 % 24), sqF, baseLots, baseLots * sqF);
            baseLots = baseLots * sqF;
         }
      }

      // (v19.09 BY) MEASURED directionlessness first -- see the note at T_ErSizing.
      if(T_ErSizing && T_ErFullSize > T_ErDeadSize)
      {
         double erNow = EfficiencyRatio(PERIOD_M1, T_EfficiencyLen);
         double frac  = 1.0;
         if(erNow <= T_ErDeadSize)      frac = T_ErFloorFrac;
         else if(erNow < T_ErFullSize)  frac = T_ErFloorFrac
                    + (1.0 - T_ErFloorFrac) * (erNow - T_ErDeadSize) / (T_ErFullSize - T_ErDeadSize);
         if(frac < 1.0)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] CHOP MEASURED: efficiency %.3f over %d M1 bars -> %.0f%% size "
                        "(%.2f -> %.2f lots). His 26 Aug ran at 0.038 -- 26 points of movement "
                        "per point of progress. On that the spread is the only certainty.",
                        erNow, T_EfficiencyLen, frac * 100.0, baseLots, baseLots * frac);
            baseLots = baseLots * frac;
         }
      }

      // (v19.08 BX) apply the dead-zone cap before any ladder can move size.
      if(thinHour && T_ThinHourLotCap > 0.0 && baseLots > T_ThinHourLotCap)
      {
         PrintFormat("[SIZE] DEAD ZONE: %.2f -> %.2f lots. %02d:00 GMT is inside the thin "
                     "window (%02d-%02d). His 26 Aug 01:00 UTC hour was 34 trades and "
                     "-78.86 GBP; 0.01 averaged -0.40 there against 0.02's -0.94.",
                     baseLots, T_ThinHourLotCap, (int)(TimeGMT() / 3600 % 24),
                     T_ThinStartGmtHour, T_ThinEndGmtHour);
         baseLots = T_ThinHourLotCap;
      }
      if(T_FixedLotsAdaptive)
      {
         double preAd = baseLots;
         if(g_chopSizeDown && T_DeadChopSizeX > 0.0 && T_DeadChopSizeX < 1.0)
            baseLots = baseLots * T_DeadChopSizeX;
         else if(g_premiumSetup && T_PremiumLotMult > 1.0)
            baseLots = baseLots * T_PremiumLotMult;
         // (v18.85 AL) thin hours cap at his floor. Applied before the clamps so the
         // ceiling below cannot re-inflate it. Signals are still all taken.
         double tierLo, tierHi; BalanceTier(tierLo, tierHi);
         if(thinHour && tierLo > 0.0 && baseLots > tierLo)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] THIN HOURS (GMT %02d:00) — %.2f -> %.2f lots. Spread is widest and ranges "
                        "smallest between %02d:00 and %02d:00 GMT, so the signal is still taken, at his floor.",
                        (int)((TimeGMT() / 3600) % 24), baseLots, tierLo,
                        T_ThinStartGmtHour, T_ThinEndGmtHour);
            baseLots = tierLo;
         }
         // (v18.86 AM) clamp to the tier for the CURRENT equity, not a fixed pair.
         if(tierLo > 0.0 && baseLots < tierLo) baseLots = tierLo;
         if(tierHi > 0.0 && baseLots > tierHi) baseLots = tierHi;
         if(MathAbs(baseLots - preAd) > 1e-9)
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] %s: %.2f -> %.2f lots. Same signal, different conviction — "
                        "his rule is that the lever is size, not refusal.",
                        g_chopSizeDown ? "DEAD CHOP" : "PREMIUM SETUP", preAd, baseLots);
      }
      // (v19.05 BU) SymbolLotScale() must not stack on top of a size that was
      // already derived from this symbol's real tick value and real stop
      // distance -- that double-counts the conversion. Gold is unaffected either
      // way, because SymbolLotScale() returns exactly 1.0 on gold.
      double symScl = (symAgn || (T_ContinuousSize && T_RiskPctPerTrade > 0.0))
                      ? 1.0 : SymbolLotScale();
      double fl    = baseLots * symScl * MathMax(0.1, g_entryExtScale);
      // ══════ (v18.92 AT) WICK-PROOF SHRINK — see the note at T_WickProofTrend ══════
      // Applied HERE, above the return, because everything below this return is
      // unreachable while T_FixedLots > 0 -- the same trap that hid the size lever
      // in v18.83 and the tier size request to this day. Consume and clear, so a
      // failed Open() can never leave a stale ratio on the next entry.
      // (v19.01 BH) born on a bar wider than its own stop -> tier floor. See the note.
      if(g_spikeCut)
      {
         double sfLo, sfHi; BalanceTier(sfLo, sfHi);
         if(sfLo > 0.0 && fl > sfLo)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] SPIKE BAR: the candle that fired this signal is wider than the "
                        "stop, so the entry is already at the far edge of a move that has "
                        "happened. Signal still taken, size cut %.2f -> %.2f.", fl, sfLo);
            fl = sfLo;
         }
      }
      g_spikeCut = false;
      if(g_wpShrink > 0.0 && g_wpShrink < 1.0)
      {
         double preWp = fl;
         fl = fl * g_wpShrink;
         PrintFormat("[SIZE] WICK-PROOF: stop widened, so lots %.2f -> %.2f (x%.2f). "
                     "Pounds at risk are unchanged; the stop now sits outside the noise "
                     "instead of on the line where any tick reaches it.",
                     preWp, fl, g_wpShrink);
      }
      g_wpShrink = 1.0;
      // ══════ (v19.04 BT) THE SECOND ROUTE UNDER HIS FLOOR — CLOSED ══════
      // v19.04 BS fixed the wick-proof route and 0.01 fills CONTINUED. His tape,
      // 25 Aug: 0.01 at 12:04, 12:14, 12:33, 19:49, 19:51, 19:54, 19:58, 20:00,
      // 20:08, 20:15. Ten of them. So there was a second route and BS only closed
      // one. I said the clamp above was the guarantee; it was not. Here it is.
      // THE SECOND ROUTE: g_entryExtScale. An "extended fill" -- an entry far from
      // the Supertrend line -- is sized down by T_ExtendedLotFrac instead of being
      // refused, which is right and is his own rule that the lever is size. But the
      // number is 0.40, and 0.40 x his 0.02 floor is 0.008, which rounds to 0.01
      // and then only meets the BROKER minimum. His floor is never consulted again
      // after the clamp 30 lines up, whose comment claims "no flag and no
      // multiplier can push size outside the range he set."
      // WHY A FLAT CLAMP IS CORRECT HERE AND WAS NOT IN BS. The wick-proof shrink
      // PAYS for a wider stop -- clamping it back up would have doubled the cash at
      // risk, so BS instead refuses the wider stop. g_entryExtScale pays for
      // nothing: it is a conviction discount on an unchanged stop. Clamping it to
      // 0.02 raises the risk on those fills from ~£1 to ~£2, which is the cost of
      // his own standing rule, stated plainly rather than quietly ignored.
      // WHAT IT IS WORTH ON HIS TAPE: those ten fills netted about -£4.4 at 0.01.
      // At 0.02 that is about -£8.8, so on TODAY this change LOSES about £4.
      // I am shipping it because it is his standing rule, corrected four times, and
      // because sizing is not the edge -- but he should know it costs money on a
      // day like today and can revert it in one switch if he would rather have the
      // smaller fills. That is his call, not mine.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_HardFloorAfterMults = false.
      // (v19.05 BU) his BT re-clamp is a GOLD rule. Forcing EURUSD back up to
      // 0.02 lots after the risk maths deliberately sized it lower would undo
      // the whole point of agnostic mode. Broker min/max below still applies.
      if(T_HardFloorAfterMults && !symAgn)
      {
         double hfLo, hfHi; BalanceTier(hfLo, hfHi);
         if(hfLo > 0.0 && fl < hfLo)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SIZE] FLOOR RESTORED: %.3f -> %.2f lots. A multiplier took the size "
                        "under his %.2f minimum; the broker floor is 0.01 and is not his rule.",
                        fl, hfLo, hfLo);
            fl = hfLo;
         }
         if(hfHi > 0.0 && fl > hfHi) fl = hfHi;
      }
      if(fstep > 0.0) fl = MathRound(fl / fstep) * fstep;
      if(fmin  > 0.0 && fl < fmin) fl = fmin;
      if(fmax  > 0.0 && fl > fmax) fl = fmax;
      return fl;
   }
   double eq   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   double cash = eq * LiveRisk(eng) / 100.0;
   // E7 sizes UP on a strong, confirmed push — capped by the auto risk ceiling below.
   if(eng == E_SCALP) cash *= g_scPushMult;
   cash *= CoordBoost(eng);      // APEX riding + house agrees -> the fast engines push harder
   cash *= SessionQuality();     // size follows the quality of the tape   // scaled by this engine's proven expectancy
   if(T_ConfSizing && eng == E_TREND) cash *= g_lastConfMult;   // (v12e) graduated confidence sizing

   // (v14 #1) HOUR-OF-DAY SIZING — once an hour has enough history, tilt size
   // by how that hour has actually performed (in R). Winning hours size up
   // modestly, bleeding hours size down. Bounded so a small sample can't
   // swing it wildly. Learns the real tape rather than assuming.
   if(T_HourLearning && eng == E_TREND)
   {
      MqlDateTime htm; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), htm);
      int hh = htm.hour;
      if(hh >= 0 && hh < 24 && g_hourN[hh] >= T_HourLearnMinN)
      {
         double avgR = g_hourR[hh] / (double)g_hourN[hh];
         double hMult = 1.0 + MathMax(-0.5, MathMin(0.5, avgR));  // avgR of +0.5 -> 1.5x, -0.5 -> 0.5x
         cash *= hMult;
      }
   }

   // (v13 #4) CONSECUTIVE-WIN LADDER — press gently when the tape is clearly
   // paying, cut straight back on the first real loss (handled in the stat
   // update). Capped hard so it can never compound into martingale territory.
   if(T_WinLadder && eng == E_TREND && g_consecWin[eng] > 0)
   {
      int steps = MathMin(g_consecWin[eng], T_WinLadderMax);
      cash *= (1.0 + T_WinLadderStep * steps);
   }

   // (v13 #5) DAILY-TARGET THROTTLE — DEFAULT OFF as of v16.05 at user's
   // direction: he wants maximum profit, not a governor that halves size
   // once the daily target is hit. Kept as a toggle (T_DailyThrottle, now
   // default false at the input) rather than deleted, so it's one flag to
   // restore if a day ever runs away. The daily LOSS halt is untouched —
   // that's real protection, not a profit cap, and stays fully active.
   if(T_DailyThrottle && g_dayPnl >= T_DailyTargetGBP)
      cash *= 0.5;

   // BUG FIX #57 (real risk gap, not cosmetic): everything above this line —
   // push mult, coord-boost, session-quality, confidence sizing, hour-
   // learning, win-ladder — is individually bounded, but nothing capped
   // their COMBINED effect. On a rare alignment where several land
   // favourably on the same trade at once, the aggregate could multiply
   // the intended per-trade risk well past what Cfg[eng].risk actually
   // specifies, with no ceiling catching it before position size is
   // computed. This clamps the total effective risk to at most
   // T_MaxRiskStackMult x the base risk for this engine — generous enough
   // that every individual feature above still does its job on ordinary
   // days, but no combination of favourable multipliers can silently turn
   // one trade into 2-3x its intended risk.
   double baseCash = eq * LiveRisk(eng) / 100.0;
   double cashCeiling = baseCash * T_MaxRiskStackMult;
   if(cash > cashCeiling) cash = cashCeiling;

   double lpl  = LossPerLot(slDist);
   if(lpl <= 0 || cash <= 0) return 0;

   double lots = MathFloor((cash / lpl) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
   lots = NormalizeDouble(lots, 3);

   // (v12e) EXACT-LOT OVERRIDE — pyramid top-ups request a precise small size
   // (0.01/0.02) funded by the open profit cushion, not the risk model. Still
   // passes through every ceiling below — never bypasses the hard risk caps.
   if(g_forceLots > 0)
   {
      lots = MathFloor(g_forceLots / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
      if(lots < g_lotMin) lots = g_lotMin;
   }

   // (v12) CONFLUENCE TIER OVERRIDE — when the entry sequence flagged this as
   // a tier 2 or tier 3 stack-up, override with the requested fixed lot size
   // (e.g. 0.03 for tier 2, 0.05 for tier 3). This is NOT extra risk on every
   // trade — the tier only fires when the timeframe stack genuinely agrees,
   // which is rare by design. Every non-tier trade still sizes normally.
   // The tier size still passes through the exposure/risk ceilings below —
   // never exceeds the account's hard risk cap even on a confluence.
   if(InpUseConfluenceTiers && g_lastTierSizeReq > 0)
   {
      double tLots = MathFloor(g_lastTierSizeReq / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
      if(tLots < g_lotMin) tLots = g_lotMin;
      // (v17.21 SILENT NO-OP — both deliberate downsizes were being discarded.)
      // "never SHRINK a normal-risk trade with a tier" is right for a CONFLUENCE
      // tier, which exists to grow a trade. But the same variable is reused by two
      // paths that deliberately ask for a SMALLER lot:
      //    T_ReversalLots 0.01 — "a reversal attempt takes its own fixed small size"
      //    T_ReentryLots  0.02 — "a re-entry is NOT a fresh signal, size it as a
      //                           second attempt"
      // With the ladder sizing at 0.03, both requests are below `lots`, so
      // `if(tLots > lots)` threw them away. The sweep-reversal path — explicitly
      // the lowest-conviction entry in the system — has been running at 300% of
      // its intended risk, and every re-entry at 150%. Full stop at 5.7pts:
      // 0.01 = GBP4.26, 0.02 = GBP8.53, 0.03 = GBP12.79.
      // This is the "a switch did the reverse of what I was told" failure mode,
      // and both values are quoted as live settings in the handover document.
      if(g_sizeReqIsExact)
      {
         if(tLots != lots)
            PrintFormat("[%s] EXACT SIZE REQUEST honoured: %.2f lots (risk model wanted %.2f)",
                        Cfg[eng].name, tLots, lots);
         lots = tLots;                       // apply in BOTH directions
      }
      else if(tLots > lots) lots = tLots;    // confluence tier: grow only, unchanged
      PrintFormat("[TREND] CONFLUENCE TIER %d — sizing %.2f lots (stack-up setup, base risk model would size %.2f)",
                   g_lastTier, tLots, MathFloor((cash / lpl) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep);
   }

   // (v17.56) FIXED ENTRY SIZE — applied after the tier/exact-size logic so it
   // never overrides a path whose whole purpose is a specific smaller size, and
   // BEFORE the exposure/margin ceilings below so it can still be cut down by a
   // genuine hard cap rather than bypassing it.
   double ladderLots = 0.0;
   if(T_AutoLotLadder && T_FixedLots <= 0.0)
   {
      if(eq <  50.0)      ladderLots = g_lotMin;
      else if(eq < 100.0) ladderLots = 0.02;
      else if(eq < 150.0) ladderLots = 0.03;
      else                ladderLots = 0.04 + MathFloor((eq - 150.0) / 50.0) * 0.01;
   // (v17.87, his ask: "hit near 0.1, go big") FLOOR raised, not the whole curve
   // replaced -- his existing scaling by equity band still applies, this just
   // sets a higher minimum once the account can actually carry it. The margin
   // and exposure ceilings below are UNCHANGED, so this cannot exceed what the
   // account can structurally support -- it can only raise the ask up to that
   // limit.
   if(T_BigLotFloor > 0.0 && eq >= T_BigLotFloorMinEq) ladderLots = MathMax(ladderLots, T_BigLotFloor);
   }
   double sizeReq = (T_FixedLots > 0.0) ? T_FixedLots : ladderLots;
   // (v17.68, his live evidence: on a 150 account it took 0.01 when the ladder
   // says 0.04) THAT WAS THIS CONDITION. Reversal and re-entry entries set
   // g_sizeReqIsExact with T_ReversalLots / T_ReentryLots = 0.01-0.02, and the
   // ladder was deliberately stepping aside for them. On a 150 account that
   // means the trade he watched -- a first-sell that then ran far enough to be
   // worth about 8 pounds at ladder size -- was taken at a QUARTER of the size
   // the account can carry. Those small fixed sizes made sense when they were
   // probe trades on a 100 account; they do not survive a ladder whose entire
   // purpose is that size follows equity.
   // The ladder now governs EVERY base entry. The only thing still exempt is a
   // pyramid top-up (g_forceLots), where the small size IS the point: it is an
   // add onto a position that already exists, not a position in its own right.
   if(sizeReq > 0.0 && g_forceLots <= 0.0)
   {
      // (v17.73) THE PREMIUM MULTIPLIER NOW FAILS SAFE. As written in v17.71 it
      // doubled the size and left the result to the risk ceiling further down —
      // and that ceiling SKIPS the trade rather than trimming it. So on a small
      // account a premium setup (0.04 x 2 = 0.08, roughly 16% of a 150 balance at
      // a 4pt stop, against a 25% all-engine cap already partly used) could be
      // rejected outright. The best setups would be the ones silently not taken —
      // the exact opposite of the intent, and a candidate for the sell he says
      // never appeared.
      // Now the doubled size is only used if it actually fits the remaining risk
      // budget. If it does not, the trade is taken at the NORMAL ladder size
      // instead of being skipped. A premium setup at standard size still beats no
      // trade at all.
      // (v17.76) day-bias adjustment, applied BEFORE the premium / strong-birth
      // steps so a counter-day-direction trade cannot be sized UP by them.
      int dayB = DayBias();
      if(dayB != 0 && g_entryDir != 0 && dayB != g_entryDir)
      {
         if(T_DayBiasMode >= 2)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: trade is against a committed day direction (%s day, %s trade). T_DayBiasMode=2.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, dayB > 0 ? "UP" : "DOWN", g_entryDir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL");
            return 0.0;
         }
         double before = sizeReq;
         sizeReq *= T_DayBiasCounterMult;
         PrintFormat("[%s] AGAINST THE DAY (%s day, %s trade): size %.2f -> %.2f.",
                     Cfg[eng].name, dayB > 0 ? "UP" : "DOWN", g_entryDir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", before, sizeReq);
      }
      // (v17.81) dead-chop now expresses itself as size, not refusal.
      if(g_chopSizeDown && T_DeadChopSizeX > 0.0 && T_DeadChopSizeX < 1.0)
      {
         double preChop = sizeReq;
         sizeReq = MathMax(g_lotMin, sizeReq * T_DeadChopSizeX);
         PrintFormat("[%s] DEAD-CHOP SIZE: %.2f -> %.2f lots.", Cfg[eng].name, preChop, sizeReq);
      }
      // (v17.89 AUDIT FIX B) T_RegimeRangeSizeX was DEAD: it only ever fed the cf[]
      // conviction pool, and that pool is short-circuited to 1.0 while
      // T_ConfSizing is false (correctly false -- it used to compound 7 factors
      // down to 0.063x). So the EA has not actually been sizing down in a range at
      // all. That was tolerable at 0.04. It is NOT tolerable next to the new 0.10
      // floor, which would otherwise take full size in exactly the regime that
      // pays least. Applied directly here, same pattern as the dead-chop trim, so
      // the broken cascade stays disabled.
      if(eng == E_TREND && T_RegimeEngine && T_RegimeRangeSizeX > 0.0 && T_RegimeRangeSizeX < 1.0)
      {
         int rgNow = RegimeOf(g_scAdx[SC_TFN - 1], g_scTF[SC_TFN - 1]);
         if(rgNow < 0)
         {
            double preRg = sizeReq;
            sizeReq = MathMax(g_lotMin, sizeReq * T_RegimeRangeSizeX);
            PrintFormat("[%s] RANGE REGIME SIZE: %.2f -> %.2f lots.", Cfg[eng].name, preRg, sizeReq);
         }
      }
      double sizeReqAdj = sizeReq;
      // (v17.75) STRONG BIRTH takes precedence over the ladder: this is the trade
      // he would take manually with size, so it gets his number exactly.
      // (v18.47) SNIPER SIZING. Was: strong birth -> max size, unconditionally.
      // Now: max size requires the birth AND that price is not pinned to the leg
      // extreme. AtLegExtreme() was only ever consulted on pyramid adds (14080)
      // and the dead reclaim path (9276) -- NEVER on a fresh flip. So the guard
      // was applied to the one entry type he WANTS (stacking) and absent from the
      // two he complains about (peak entries and post-exit re-entries, which
      // arrive through this same fresh-flip funnel and were fully unguarded).
      // This puts it where it belongs, as a SIZE decision, never a refusal.
      if(T_StrongBirth && g_strongBirth >= T_StrongMinScore && T_StrongBirthLots > 0.0
         && !g_atExtreme)
      {
         sizeReqAdj = MathMax(sizeReq, T_StrongBirthLots);
         PrintFormat("[%s] STRONG BIRTH %d/4, off the extreme — sizing at %.2f (base %.2f).",
                     Cfg[eng].name, g_strongBirth, sizeReqAdj, sizeReq);
      }
      else if(g_atExtreme)
         PrintFormat("[%s] AT THE EXTREME — taken at base %.2f, not upsized. "
                     "Signal is never declined; the bad price is paid for in size.",
                     Cfg[eng].name, sizeReq);
      if(g_premiumSetup && T_PremiumLotMult > 1.0)
      {
         double want     = sizeReq * T_PremiumLotMult;
         double riskWant = want * lpl;
         double riskRoom = eq * AutoMaxExposurePct / 100.0 - OpenRiskCash();
         if(riskWant <= riskRoom)
            sizeReqAdj = want;
         else
            PrintFormat("[%s] PREMIUM SETUP kept at ladder size %.2f — x%.2f would risk %.2f but only %.2f of budget is free.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, sizeReq, T_PremiumLotMult, riskWant, riskRoom);
      }
      double fx = MathFloor(sizeReqAdj / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
      if(fx >= g_lotMin && MathAbs(fx - lots) > 1e-8)
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] SIZE %.2f lots at %.2f equity (%s — risk model wanted %.2f).",
                     Cfg[eng].name, fx, eq,
                     (T_FixedLots > 0.0) ? "T_FixedLots override" : "auto ladder", lots);
         lots = fx;
      }
   }

   if(lots < g_lotMin)
   {
      double minRisk = g_lotMin * lpl;
      double trueRiskPct = 100.0 * minRisk / eq;

      if(!SmallAccountMode)
      {
         // (v17.65, his live report: "I have a 150 account and it refuses to
         // trade") This veto is why. cash = equity x risk%, so on 150 USD at
         // ~1.5% the budget is ~2.25, while ONE minimum 0.01 gold lot with a
         // 4-dollar stop risks ~4.35. minRisk > cash * 1.5 is therefore true on
         // every tick and the entry is skipped silently unless SmallAccountMode
         // is on. Setting T_FixedLots is an EXPLICIT instruction about size, so
         // it now counts as the same consent SmallAccountMode gives — the veto
         // is skipped and the trade is taken at the size he asked for.
         // The exposure and margin ceilings BELOW still apply, so this cannot
         // open a position the account cannot actually carry.
         if(minRisk > cash * 1.5 && T_FixedLots <= 0.0 && !T_AutoLotLadder)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: min lot risks %.2f (%.1f%% of equity), budget is %.2f (%.1f%%). "
                        "Turn on SmallAccountMode, or get nano lots from your broker.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, minRisk, trueRiskPct, cash, Cfg[eng].risk);
            return 0;
         }
         lots = g_lotMin;
      }
      else
      {
         // SMALL ACCOUNT MODE: take the trade at min lot, but never past the hard ceiling,
         // and say out loud what it actually costs.
         if(trueRiskPct > SmallAcctMaxRisk)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: even one min lot risks %.1f%% of equity — over your %.1f%% ceiling. "
                        "The stop (%.2f) is too wide for this account.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, trueRiskPct, SmallAcctMaxRisk, slDist);
            return 0;
         }
         lots = g_lotMin;
         PrintFormat("[%s] MIN-LOT TRADE. Intended risk %.1f%%, ACTUAL RISK %.1f%% (%.2f of %.2f equity). "
                     "This is what a 0.01 minimum costs you on this balance.",
                     Cfg[eng].name, Cfg[eng].risk, trueRiskPct, minRisk, eq);
      }
   }

   // ═══════════ AUTO-SCALED EXPOSURE CAP ═══════════
   // TWO ceilings, and a trade must pass both.
   //
   //   1. LOT CEILING — scales with the balance. No fixed number to forget.
   //   2. RISK CEILING — the real one. Total cash at risk across every open
   //      position may never exceed AutoMaxExposurePct of equity.
   //
   // The risk ceiling is what stops the thing you did manually: opening twelve
   // 0.01 lots because leverage allowed it. Leverage decides what you can OPEN.
   // The stop decides what you can LOSE. Only the second one is survivable.
   // Positions whose stop is already at breakeven are excluded — they cannot
   // lose any more, so they do not consume the budget.
   double maxLots = AutoMaxLots();
   double openLots = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      openLots += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   }
   if(openLots + lots > maxLots)
   {
      lots = maxLots - openLots;
      lots = MathFloor(lots / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
      if(lots < g_lotMin)
      {
         if(InpDiagnostics)
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: lot ceiling. %.2f open, %.2f allowed at %.2f equity.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, openLots, maxLots, eq);
         return 0;
      }
   }

   double riskNow  = OpenRiskCash();
   double riskAdd  = lots * lpl;
   double riskCeil = eq * AutoMaxExposurePct / 100.0;
   if(riskNow + riskAdd > riskCeil)
   {
      double room = riskCeil - riskNow;
      if(room <= 0)
      {
         if(InpDiagnostics)
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: already risking %.2f of %.2f allowed (%.0f%% of equity). Book is full.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, riskNow, riskCeil, AutoMaxExposurePct);
         return 0;
      }
      lots = MathFloor((room / lpl) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
      if(lots < g_lotMin)
      {
         if(InpDiagnostics)
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: only %.2f of risk budget left — not enough for one min lot.",
                        Cfg[eng].name, room);
         return 0;
      }
      PrintFormat("[%s] TRIMMED to %.3f lots — the risk ceiling (%.0f%% of equity) is close.",
                  Cfg[eng].name, lots, AutoMaxExposurePct);
   }

   return lots;
}

int EngineOf(long magic)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < ENG_COUNT; i++)
      if(Cfg[i].magic == magic) return i;
   return -1;
}

// Net direction across every engine.
int NetDirection()
{
   int net = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      net += (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   }
   return net;
}

// HARD GUARD. The old EA could hold 7 positions in BOTH directions at once,
// paying the spread on both sides of a hedge it did not know it had. Never again.
//
// BUG FIX #63 (THE REVERSAL DEADLOCK — a root cause of "misses the new
// trend / enters late / never rides a full trend"): this gate blocked ANY
// entry against the NET of open positions. Play out a normal reversal:
// M1 flips DOWN, the M1 long is closed instantly (fix #41/#53) and M1
// fast-rearms — but M3/M5 longs are still open because their slower
// Supertrends haven't flipped yet (that's not disagreement, that's just
// how slower clocks work). The fresh M1 SHORT — the exact signal the
// whole system is built to catch — was then BLOCKED here, every time,
// until the slower positions unwound. By then the new M1 trend is half
// over and the entry chases. This directly contradicted the engine's own
// stated design ("a long on the M1 flip and a short on the M5 flip are
// different trades on different clocks and both deserve to be held") AND
// the locked philosophy that slower clocks are context, never blockers,
// for faster ones.
//
// The fix honors BOTH intentions: a fresh TREND signal may open against
// the net ONLY when every opposing position lives on a strictly SLOWER
// clock (their lag is expected, not a contradiction). Any opposing
// position on the same or a faster clock still blocks — that's a real
// disagreement, and the guard's original job (no blind self-hedging in
// chop) stays fully intact. Non-TREND entries (g_lastEntryK == -1) keep
// the strict original behaviour.
bool DirectionAllowed(int dir, int sigK = -1)
{
   if(!InpNoOpposing) return true;
   int net = NetDirection();
   if(net == 0) return true;
   if((dir > 0) == (net > 0)) return true;   // with the net — always fine

   // Opposing the net: allowed only for a TREND signal whose clock is
   // strictly faster than EVERY open opposing position's clock.
   if(sigK < 0 || sigK >= SC_TFN) return false;
   int sigSec = PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[sigK]);

   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      int pe = EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC));
      if(pe < 0) continue;
      int pDir = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pDir == dir) continue;               // same way — not in conflict
      int posSec;
      if(pe == E_TREND)      posSec = PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)]);
      else if(pe == E_SCALP) posSec = PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[ScalpTFOf(tk)]);
      else                   posSec = PeriodSeconds(Cfg[pe].tf);   // APEX = M15
      if(posSec <= sigSec) return false;      // opposing on same/faster clock = real conflict, block
   }
   return true;   // every opposing position is on a slower clock — its lag is expected, the fast signal leads
}

// ═══════════ MULTI-SYMBOL SHARED RISK (Gold + Silver + GBPJPY at once) ═══════════
// Each running instance writes ITS OWN open risk (as % of ITS OWN equity) to a
// global terminal variable keyed by symbol. Any instance can then sum every
// instance's contribution to see the TRUE combined exposure across the whole
// account — because on one $150 account, three correlated instruments each
// separately "within limits" can still add up to an account-blowing total.
string SharedRiskKey() { return "QUADRISK_" + _Symbol; }

double MyOpenRiskPct()
{
   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0) return 0;
   double riskCash = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      double px  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double sl  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      double lot = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      if(sl <= 0) continue;   // no stop = can't measure its risk, skip rather than guess
      double tv  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE);
      double ts  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE);
      if(tv <= 0 || ts <= 0) continue;
      riskCash += MathAbs(px - sl) / ts * tv * lot;
   }
   return 100.0 * riskCash / eq;
}

void PublishMyRisk()
{
   GlobalVariableSet(SharedRiskKey(), MyOpenRiskPct());
   GlobalVariableSet(SharedRiskKey() + "_T", (double)TimeCurrent());
}

double CombinedRiskPct()
{
   double total = 0;
   int n = GlobalVariablesTotal();
   for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
   {
      string name = GlobalVariableName(i);
      if(StringFind(name, "QUADRISK_") != 0) continue;
      if(StringFind(name, "_T", StringLen(name) - 2) == StringLen(name) - 2) continue;  // skip timestamp companions
      // stale entries (a chart that was closed) shouldn't hold the cap hostage
      // forever — ignore anything not touched in the last hour.
      if(TimeCurrent() - (datetime)GlobalVariableGet(name + "_T") > 3600) continue;
      total += GlobalVariableGet(name);
   }
   return total;
}


// This is the answer to "why is my EA not trading?" — no more guessing.
string WhyNotReady(int e)
{
   if(!Cfg[e].on)                     return "switched off in inputs";
   if(InNewsWindow())                 return "NEWS WINDOW — no edge survives a 40-pip spread";
   if(IsChaos(e))                     return "CHAOS — volatility spike with no direction. Stop hunt.";
   if(Stat[e].disabled)               return "AUTO-DISABLED (expectancy below floor)";
   if(TimeGMT() < Stat[e].pauseUntil) return "paused after consecutive losses";
   if(g_halt)                         return "DAILY LOSS LIMIT hit";
   if(g_locked)                       return "daily profit locked";
   if(InpMultiSymbolCap && CombinedRiskPct() >= InpMultiSymbolMaxPct)
      return StringFormat("MULTI-SYMBOL RISK CAP: %.1f%% already in use across all instances (cap %.1f%%)",
                            CombinedRiskPct(), InpMultiSymbolMaxPct);
   double sp = SpreadUSD();
   if(sp > InpMaxSpreadUSD)
      return StringFormat("SPREAD too wide: %.2f vs cap %.2f", sp, InpMaxSpreadUSD);
   int barSec = PeriodSeconds(Cfg[e].tf);
   long since = TimeCurrent() - Stat[e].lastExit;
   int neededCooldown = g_fastRearm[e] ? 1 : Cfg[e].cooldown;   // early breakeven scratch: 1 bar, not the full wait
   // BUG FIX #45: this cooldown was ENGINE-WIDE (one shared Stat[e].lastExit,
   // one shared g_fastRearm[e]), so a M1 exit put M3 and M5 into cooldown too,
   // and vice versa — even though they are independent trades on independent
   // clocks. Worst case: a fast reversal (a position stopped/flip-closed on
   // one timeframe, with the opposite signal ready to fire right after, on
   // the SAME or a DIFFERENT timeframe) could sit blocked for up to the full
   // cooldown window for no reason connected to that specific trade. TREND
   // now tracks cooldown per timeframe inside Engine_Trend() itself; this
   // engine-wide version no longer applies to it.
   if(e != E_TREND && since < (long)neededCooldown * barSec)
      return StringFormat("cooling down (%d more bars)", (int)((neededCooldown*barSec - since)/barSec) + 1);
   // TREND can legitimately hold up to 3 positions at once now — one per
   // timeframe (M1/M3/M5), since a long taken on the M1 flip and a short
   // taken on the M5 flip are different trades on different clocks. The
   // per-timeframe "already holding one here" check and the overall cap
   // both live inside Engine_Trend() itself, so this blanket check is
   // skipped for TREND specifically — applying it here would silence the
   // whole engine after its very first trade on any single timeframe.
   if(e != E_TREND && PosOf(e) != 0) return "already in a position";
   return "";
}

bool EngineReady(int e)
{
   return WhyNotReady(e) == "";
}

// ══════════════════════ THE COORDINATION LAYER ══════════════════════
// Three engines, one direction, three speeds. They are not three strategies —
// they are ONE strategy sampled at three horizons. This is what makes them stack
// instead of cancel.
//
//   SCALP  M1-M15  the small pushes inside the move   many/day   2R
//   TREND  M5      the pullback                       few/day    4.5R
//   APEX   M15     the whole move                     few/week   no limit
//
// HOUSE BIAS: the direction the system as a whole believes in, decided by the
// M15 Supertrend alone (v16.12 removed H1 — see the function body). Every engine must agree with it.
//
// Why: the old EA had engines trading against each other — one long, one short,
// both paying spread, net exposure zero, net cost real. That is not
// diversification, it is an expensive way to hold cash.
int HouseBias()
{
   // (v16.12) H1 REMOVED from the bias. H1 is too slow to be relevant to a
   // 1-3-5min entry — a fast flip rarely correlates with where H1's EMA sits,
   // so gating on it was killing real fast trends (the trader's correct
   // complaint, and the research agrees the HTF check for scalps should be
   // 15/30min, not H1). Now the "bigger picture" is M15 ALONE via APEX's
   // Supertrend: fast enough to matter to the fast engines, slow enough to
   // filter genuine chop. This catches more trends while keeping one honest
   // higher-timeframe sanity check (which every credible gold-scalp source
   // says you need). M15 flat/uncomputable -> no bias, everything may trade.
   double svM; int sdM; Supertrend(E_APEX, svM, sdM);      // M15
   return (svM > 0) ? sdM : 0;
}

// APEX is the anchor. When it is riding a trend, the faster engines get a boost:
// they know the big picture agrees with them, so they can be a little braver.
// When APEX is flat, they stay conservative. The slow engine informs the fast ones.
double CoordBoost(int e)
{
   ulong ap = PosOf(E_APEX);
   if(ap == 0) return 1.0;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(ap)) return 1.0;
   int apexDir = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   int bias = HouseBias();
   if(bias != 0 && bias == apexDir && e != E_APEX)
      return 1.3;   // APEX is riding and the house agrees. Push harder.
   return 1.0;
}

// Every N bars, print a full status board. If the EA is not trading, this tells
// you exactly which gate is closed — engine by engine, in plain English.
datetime g_lastDiag = 0;
void Diagnostics()
{
   if(!InpDiagnostics) return;
   if(TimeCurrent() - g_lastDiag < 60) return;   // (v16.22) 300->60s — surfaces the BLOCKED reason faster for diagnosis
   g_lastDiag = TimeCurrent();

   double eqD   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   double riskD = OpenRiskCash();
   double ceilD = eqD * AutoMaxExposurePct / 100.0;

   Print("┌──────────── STATUS ────────────");
   int hb = HouseBias();
   PrintFormat("│ %s TIER | equity %.2f | spread %.2f", AccountTier(), eqD, SpreadUSD());
   if(T_SignalAudit)
      PrintFormat("│ SIGNAL AUDIT: %d flips seen | %d TAKEN | %d skipped (%.0f%% taken) — see quad_signals.csv for what the skips would have made",
                  g_sigSeen, g_sigTaken, g_sigSkipped,
                  g_sigSeen > 0 ? 100.0 * g_sigTaken / g_sigSeen : 0.0);

   // (v16.34) SHOW THE KILL-SWITCHES BEFORE THEY FIRE. Both the daily halt and
   // the per-engine auto-disable stop the EA SILENTLY — from the outside it just
   // looks like "the EA isn't taking signals any more", which is exactly the
   // complaint that has been hardest to diagnose. Now the headroom is printed
   // every minute so an approaching shutdown is visible rather than mysterious.
   if(InpDailyHaltEnable)
   {
      double eqNow   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
      double haltAt  = g_dayStart * (1.0 - InpMaxDailyLossPct / 100.0);
      double room    = eqNow - haltAt;
      PrintFormat("│ DAILY HALT: %s | equity %.2f, halts at %.2f (%.1f%% of %.2f) — headroom %.2f",
                  g_halt ? "*** ALREADY HALTED — NO NEW ENTRIES TODAY ***" : "active",
                  eqNow, haltAt, InpMaxDailyLossPct, g_dayStart, room);
   }
   for(int de = 0; de < ENG_COUNT; de++)
   {
      if(!Cfg[de].on) continue;
      if(Stat[de].disabled)
         PrintFormat("│ *** %s IS AUTO-DISABLED *** expectancy %.2fR over %d trades was below %.2fR. It will NOT trade again until you restart the EA.",
                     Cfg[de].name, Stat[de].trades > 0 ? Stat[de].netR/Stat[de].trades : 0.0,
                     Stat[de].trades, InpMinExpectancyR);
      else if(Stat[de].trades > 0 && Stat[de].trades >= InpMinTradesJudge / 2)
         PrintFormat("│ %s judge-watch: %d/%d trades toward review, expectancy %.2fR (disable floor %.2fR)",
                     Cfg[de].name, Stat[de].trades, InpMinTradesJudge,
                     Stat[de].netR/Stat[de].trades, InpMinExpectancyR);
   }
   PrintFormat("│ HOUSE BIAS: %s  (M15 only, H1 removed v16.12)",
               hb > 0 ? "LONG" : hb < 0 ? "SHORT" : "NONE — every engine may trade freely");
   PrintFormat("│ lot ceiling %.3f (auto) | AT RISK NOW %.2f of %.2f allowed (%.0f%% of equity)",
               AutoMaxLots(), riskD, ceilD, AutoMaxExposurePct);
   if(riskD > ceilD * 0.8)
      Print("│ *** risk budget nearly full — new trades will be trimmed or skipped ***");

   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
   {
      if(!Cfg[e].on) continue;
      string why = WhyNotReady(e);
      double atrE = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
      double adxE = Buf(hAdx[e], 0, 1);

      if(why != "")
      {
         PrintFormat("│ %-6s BLOCKED — %s", Cfg[e].name, why);
         continue;
      }

      // ready, but is the market giving it a setup?
      double slGuess = ((e==E_TREND) ? 1.0 : (e==E_SCALP) ? 0.9 : 1.3) * atrE;
      double minRisk = g_lotMin * LossPerLot(slGuess);
      double eqNow   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
      double truePct = (eqNow > 0) ? 100.0 * minRisk / eqNow : 0;

      PrintFormat("│ %-6s READY — ADX %.1f, ATR %.2f | a min-lot trade here = %.1f%% risk %s",
                  Cfg[e].name, adxE, atrE, truePct,
                  truePct > SmallAcctMaxRisk ? "*** OVER YOUR CEILING, WILL SKIP ***" : "");
   }
   Print("└───────────────────────────────────────────────");
}

// one position per engine
ulong PosOf(int e)
{
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[e].magic) return tk;
   }
   return 0;
}

//---- reason store -------------------------------------------------
void StoreReason(ulong tk, string why)
{
   if(g_rCount >= 200) g_rCount = 0;
   g_rTicket[g_rCount] = tk;
   g_rWhy[g_rCount] = why;
   g_rCount++;
   if(InpVerboseLog)
   {
      PrintFormat("WHY #%I64u : %s", tk, why);
      int fh = FileOpen("quad_reasons.csv", FILE_READ|FILE_WRITE|FILE_CSV|FILE_ANSI);
      if(fh != INVALID_HANDLE)
      {
         FileSeek(fh, 0, SEEK_END);
         FileWrite(fh, TimeToString(TimeCurrent()), (string)tk, why);
         FileClose(fh);
      }
   }
}

string ReasonFor(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++)
      if(g_rTicket[i] == tk) return g_rWhy[i];
   return "";
}

//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//|  (v17.99) TAGGED CLOSE — THE ONE THING MISSING FOR THREE MONTHS  |
//|                                                                  |
//|  This file has TWENTY-FIVE call sites for trade.PositionClose().  |
//|  Nineteen of them can fire on a live, winning TREND position.     |
//|  They do not cooperate: the trade dies on whichever fires FIRST,   |
//|  so the hold time is an argmin over nineteen independent draws.    |
//|  That is why three months of correct single-mechanism fixes did    |
//|  not move the outcome — repairing the binding one just promotes    |
//|  the next-tightest, and the observable barely changes.             |
//|                                                                    |
//|  Only THREE of the twenty-five recorded a reason. So the journal    |
//|  could never name the killer, and every diagnosis had to be an     |
//|  inference from the code. That is the actual blocker.              |
//|                                                                    |
//|  Every close now routes through here, tagged, counted, with the    |
//|  points it took and the peak it gave up. One session and the panel |
//|  names which of the nineteen is eating the moves. No more guessing.|
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
input group "=== (v17.99) EXIT FAMILY SWITCHES ==="
input bool   T_BanksOff  = true;   // suppress the eight small-profit BANK closes (LEG TARGET, SPIKE-PEAK,
                                   // EXHAUSTION, RANGE-SCALP, SMALL-MOVE, CHOP-STALL, PEAK BANK,
                                   // MOMENTUM-STALL). Each one exists to take a small profit NOW, which is
                                   // the disease, and each is redundant against the giveback guard, the
                                   // ratchet floor and the hard stop — all of which stay live.
                                   // Set false to restore v17.98 behaviour exactly.
input bool   T_FadesOff  = false;  // suppress the three "the move looks over" closes (MOMENTUM FADE,
                                   // M1 MICRO-CHOP, CHOP CLUSTER). Left ON until measured.

#define QSP_TAGS 40
string   g_xTag[QSP_TAGS];
int      g_xN[QSP_TAGS];
double   g_xPts[QSP_TAGS], g_xPeak[QSP_TAGS];
int      g_xUsed = 0;
datetime g_xDay  = 0;

#define QSP_LIVE_MAX 128
ulong  g_qspTk[QSP_LIVE_MAX];
double g_qspPeak[QSP_LIVE_MAX];

// ═══════════ (v18.63) EVERY EXIT GATE BECOMES PEAK-RELATIVE ═══════════
// HIS QUESTION: "some trades are 0.5pt wins and some 20 -- how can we claim the
// most of each." The answer is that no ABSOLUTE number can, and every gate in
// front of a profitable exit in this file is absolute. Measured against his own
// panel (16 trades, +58.1 pts offered, 7.53 kept = 13%, average peak 3.63 pts):
//
//   GATE                          VALUE      0.5pt win   3.63pt win   20pt win
//   T_TrailArmPts                 3.00 pts   blocked     passes       passes
//   T_GivebackAtrFloor 1.0xATR    ~1.5 pts   blocked     passes       passes
//   T_ExitMinR 1.00R              ~2.5 pts   blocked     passes       passes
//   T_MinWinnerExitPts            6.00 pts   BLOCKED     BLOCKED      passes
//
// A 20-point trade clears everything. The AVERAGE trade clears almost nothing.
// Each threshold was tuned on the trades that ran, so the exit system is
// calibrated to the top of his distribution and is structurally forbidden from
// banking the other 80%. That is the 13%, and it is not the exits choosing badly
// -- for most trades no exit is PERMITTED to choose at all.
//
// FIX: one idea, applied to every gate instead of one at a time (the mistake in
// v18.59, where I capped the giveback allowance and left this floor standing in
// front of it). Each gate becomes min(fixed, fraction x peak achieved), so a
// 0.5pt trade is judged against 0.5 and a 20pt trade against 20. Big winners are
// untouched: at a 20pt peak, 0.60 x 20 = 12 exceeds every fixed value, so every
// gate keeps its original number. The cap only binds where the fixed value is
// ABOVE the peak -- i.e. exactly the unsatisfiable cases.
// This is the same bug class as v18.59's giveback cap and v16.68's round-trip
// floor: a threshold set above the peak forces the trade back through zero.
input group  "=== v18.63  PEAK-RELATIVE EXIT GATES ==="
input double T_PeakGateFrac    = 0.35;  // (v18.65) 0.60 -> 0.35. See the interaction note. 0 = off (pre-v18.63)
// ═══ (v18.65) BUG IN MY OWN v18.63, FOUND AUDITING TODAY'S CHANGES ═══
// v18.59 and v18.63 were fighting each other, and v18.63 was winning.
//   v18.59 capped the GIVEBACK allowance at 0.50 x peak, so in the worst case a
//     giveback fires when the trade still RETAINS 0.50 x peak.
//   v18.63 set the winner floor to 0.60 x peak, i.e. no discretionary exit may
//     act unless the trade RETAINS 0.60 x peak.
//   0.50 < 0.60, so the floor I shipped an hour later blocked the giveback I had
//   just unblocked, by ten percentage points. Net effect on the case he actually
//   complains about -- a winner decaying back through zero -- would have been NIL.
// This is the same failure mode as the whole file's history: two locally correct
// fixes, never checked against each other. It is also why I keep insisting on one
// change per session and then shipping four.
//
// WHY 0.35 IS THE RIGHT NUMBER, not a guess. It has to sit BELOW the tightest
// retention at which any bank can fire, so it never becomes the binding gate:
//   giveback, worst case (allowance capped at 0.50 x peak) -> fires at 0.50 retained
//   giveback, T_GivebackPct 22                             -> fires at 0.78 retained
//   giveback, big tier 40 + 15 bonus                       -> fires at 0.45 retained
//   peak trail, 1.30 ATR behind a 3.63 pt peak (ATR ~1.8)  -> ~0.36 retained
// The tightest of those is ~0.36, so 0.35 clears every one of them with no margin
// wasted. Above 0.36 this gate silently vetoes the peak trail as well -- which is
// exactly the bug being fixed here, one mechanism further along.
// It also still does its original job: a 0.5 pt trade may bank at 0.18 pts instead
// of being forbidden to bank at all, and at a 20 pt peak 0.35 x 20 = 7.0 still
// exceeds the 6.00 fixed floor, so big winners keep the v18.44 number unchanged.
input bool   T_PeakGateWinner  = true;  // apply to T_MinWinnerExitPts
input bool   T_PeakGateArbiter = true;  // apply to T_ExitMinR
input bool   T_PeakGateTrail   = true;  // apply to T_TrailArmPts
// Peak profit in POINTS for a live ticket, from the panel's own running max.
// Returns 0 when unknown, and every caller treats 0 as "do not relax the gate",
// so a missing peak can never loosen anything.
double PeakPtsOf(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < QSP_LIVE_MAX; i++)
      if(g_qspDir[i] != 0 && g_qspTk[i] == tk) return g_qspPeak[i];
   return 0.0;
}
int    g_qspDir[QSP_LIVE_MAX];          // 0 == free slot

bool QSP_IsBank(string t)
{
   return (t=="LEG-TARGET" || t=="SPIKE-PEAK" || t=="EXHAUSTION" || t=="RANGE-SCALP"
        || t=="SMALL-MOVE" || t=="CHOP-STALL" || t=="PEAK-BANK"  || t=="MOM-STALL");
}
bool QSP_IsFade(string t)
{
   return (t=="MOM-FADE" || t=="M1-MICROCHOP" || t=="CHOP-CLUSTER");
}

void QSP_TagRecord(string tag, double pts, double peak)
{
   MqlDateTime dt; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), dt);
   dt.hour=0; dt.min=0; dt.sec=0;
   datetime d0 = StructToTime(dt);
   if(d0 != g_xDay) { g_xDay = d0; g_xUsed = 0; }
   for(int i = 0; i < g_xUsed; i++)
      if(g_xTag[i] == tag)
      { g_xN[i]++; g_xPts[i] += pts; g_xPeak[i] += peak; return; }
   if(g_xUsed >= QSP_TAGS) return;            // no blind wrap
   g_xTag[g_xUsed]=tag; g_xN[g_xUsed]=1; g_xPts[g_xUsed]=pts; g_xPeak[g_xUsed]=peak;
   g_xUsed++;
}

// The single door every close goes through. Returns false if the family is
// suppressed — NOTE the caller then continues as if the close failed, which is
// the intended behaviour for a suppressed exit, but it does mean any state the
// caller sets AFTER this call (rearm stamps, cooldowns) is still set. That is a
// known imperfection of doing this in one place instead of nineteen.
//================================================================
//  (v18.12) TWO VETOES AT THE ONE CHOKE POINT
//
//  Every exit in this EA funnels through CloseTagged(), so both rules live here
//  once instead of being chased through 25 call sites.
//
//  VETO 1 -- A HIGHER CLOCK MAY NOT CLOSE A LOWER-CLOCK TRADE.
//  His instruction: "if an M1 is a sell and M15 turns to buy, or M5 turns to
//  buy, my M1 shouldn't close -- we only trade M1 and M5."
//
//  VETO 2 -- THE EXIT ARBITER, which is the structural finding of this audit.
//  This EA has ONE way to open a trade and TWENTY-FIVE ways to close one:
//    THESIS-DEAD  STRUCT-BREAK  STALL  SPREAD-PANIC  SPIKE-PEAK  SMALL-MOVE
//    SCALP-ST-FLIP  RANGE-SCALP  PEAK-BANK  NEWS-FLAT  MOM-STALL  MOM-FADE
//    MAX-HOLD  M1-MICROCHOP  LEG-TARGET  H1-FLIP  GIVEBACK  GAP-AGAINST
//    FRI-FLAT  FLIP-CLOSE  EXHAUSTION  EARLY-FAIL  CHOP-STALL  CHOP-CLUSTER
//    APEX-HEALTH
//  Each is individually sensible. Collectively they are the problem, and the
//  arithmetic is not subtle: a trade satisfies ONE condition to be born, then
//  must survive TWENTY-FIVE independent kill switches for its whole life.
//  Whichever has the shortest fuse decides every trade. That is why signals land
//  in moves running 4-40 points while exits deliver ~2 -- the strategy actually
//  being traded is not "Supertrend + DEMA", it is "whatever the fastest of 25
//  exits does". T_BanksOff already silenced 8 of them; this is the same
//  discovery, made once instead of one exit at a time.
//
//  The arbiter splits exits in two:
//    SAFETY / OWNERSHIP -- the stop, the trade's OWN Supertrend flip, news,
//      weekend, spread panic, gap, max-hold. Always allowed: disaster brake, or
//      the trade's own thesis revoked by its own clock.
//    DISCRETIONARY -- stall, fade, giveback, exhaustion, chop, small-move,
//      peak-bank, thesis-score. These are OPINIONS about a live trade, and they
//      may not act until it has earned T_ExitMinR. Below that they are guessing,
//      and the guess costs the whole move.
//  Vetoes are recorded via QSP_TagRecord("~"+tag) so the panel shows what was
//  suppressed and what it would have banked.
//  OFF-SWITCHES: T_HtfNeverCloses = false, T_ExitArbiter = false.
//================================================================
input group  "=== v18.12  EXIT VETOES ==="
input bool   T_HtfNeverCloses = true;   // a higher timeframe can never close a trade
input bool   T_ExitArbiter    = true;   // discretionary exits must wait for T_ExitMinR
// (v19.02 BI) the basket exits are arithmetic on the total, not opinions about one
// ticket, and they are all-or-nothing. See the long note in CloseTagged.
input bool   T_BasketExemptFloor = true;
// (v18.34) Absolute floor, in points, below which NO exit may close a winning
// trade (bar a true reversal, stop, news or weekend). See the note in CloseTagged.
// ═════════ (v18.44) 3.00 -> 6.00, AND THE REVERSAL ESCAPE TIGHTENED ═════════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE (the number) + BUG FIX (the escape below).
// LIVE EVIDENCE, 17 Aug 07:29, his own ticket: BUY 0.02 at 4399.450 closed at
// 4402.781 = +3.33 pts. The M1 leg it was in ran 4396.06 -> 4406.08, ten points.
// It banked 3.33 of 10 and 6.6 pts were left on the table.
// +3.33 is NOT the 4.0 target and NOT a 1.2pt cut. It is this floor RELEASING.
// This guard refuses every non-safety exit while profit < T_MinWinnerExitPts --
// so the instant profit crosses 3.00 the ENTIRE clipper stack unlocks at once and
// the first of ten exits in line order closes the trade. The floor was built as
// protection and behaves as a starting gun.
// That is also the exact shape of the 14 Aug distribution: winners 0.05-1.70,
// then NOTHING between 1.70 and 3.14, then a dense cluster 3.14-4.87. The empty
// gap is this floor holding; the cluster starting at 3.14 is it letting go.
// So v18.43 (target 4->8) on its own would have changed almost nothing: a trade
// released at 3.1 never survives to reach 8.0. This is the binding constraint.
// 6.00 puts the release above the 3-6 pt zone where every clipper was banking,
// and inside the 6-15 pt runner band he trades by hand.
// HONEST COST: a winner that genuinely tops out at 4-5 pts can no longer be
// banked by an opinion. It exits on the stop, the ratchet, the giveback or a
// genuine DEMA reversal instead, so it gives back the allowance (~1 pt) first.
// On 14 Aug that is ~8 trades x ~1 pt = ~-8 pts. It LOSES money if the 3-6 pt
// exits were mostly correct tops rather than pullbacks.
// REVERT: T_MinWinnerExitPts = 3.00. Off entirely = 0.
input double T_MinWinnerExitPts = 6.00;
// (v18.44) The escape hatch in this guard tested the LIVE TICK against the M1
// DEMA. On M1 gold with DEMA 60 a tick crosses that line constantly, so any exit
// could clear the floor on a wick and close a small winner -- that, not
// FLIP-CLOSE's membership of IsSafetyExit(), is the 0.05-1.70 pt band. A reversal
// now needs a CLOSED bar on the far side by this much ATR, not a tick touching it.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_RevBufAtr = 0 restores the hairline test (the tick is still gone).
input double T_RevBufAtr = 0.25;
input double T_ExitMinR       = 1.00;   // profit in R before an opinion may close a trade
// (v18.17) The arbiter only guards the WINNING band. At or below this R a
// discretionary exit may act freely, so a trade that fails immediately can still
// be cut instead of riding to the full stop. 0.0 = break-even.
input double T_ExitLoserFloorR = 0.00;

bool IsHtfCloseTag(string tag)
{
   string t = tag; StringToUpper(t);
   return (StringFind(t, "H1-FLIP")  >= 0 || StringFind(t, "HTF") >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "H1")       >= 0 || StringFind(t, "H4")  >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "M15")      >= 0 || StringFind(t, "LOCKDOWN") >= 0);
}

bool IsSafetyExit(string tag)
{
   string t = tag; StringToUpper(t);
   return (StringFind(t, "FLIP-CLOSE")    >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "SCALP-ST-FLIP") >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "NEWS-FLAT")     >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "FRI-FLAT")      >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "SPREAD-PANIC")  >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "GAP-AGAINST")   >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "MARGIN")        >= 0     // (v18.76) a margin action is never an "opinion" either
        || StringFind(t, "MAX-LOSS")      >= 0     // (v18.29) the loss cap is never an "opinion"
        || StringFind(t, "MAX-HOLD")      >= 0
        || StringFind(t, "MANUAL")        >= 0);
}

// Profit in R for a live ticket, using its own entry-to-stop distance as R.
// ok = false when R cannot be established, which makes the arbiter PERMISSIVE
// rather than trapping a trade it cannot measure.
double TicketProfitR(ulong tk, bool &ok)
{
   ok = false;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return 0.0;
   int    dr = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   double op = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
   double sl = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
   double px = (dr > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                        : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   if(sl <= 0.0) return 0.0;
   double R = MathAbs(op - sl);
   if(R <= _Point) return 0.0;
   ok = true;
   return ((px - op) * dr) / R;
}

//================================================================
//  (v18.29) MAX LOSS PER TRADE, CLOSED ON OUR OWN ACCORD
//  His instruction: "put a cap on max loss one trade can make -- maybe 4 point
//  loss max -- however we don't rely on SL, we close of our own accord."
//  This runs on every management pass, ahead of the stop. Two triggers:
//    1. HARD: the trade is T_MaxLossPts against us -> close at market now. The
//       broker stop stays where it is as the disaster brake only.
//    2. REVERSAL: at half that distance, if the trade is ALSO on the wrong side of
//       the DEMA, the thesis is gone, not just the price -- cut it early rather
//       than paying the remaining points to find out. This is his own read: "all
//       our losers are clear reversals."
//  Sized for lots: at 0.05 a 4 point cut is ~20 USD, at 0.01 it is ~4.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_MaxLossPts = 0.
//================================================================
// (v18.37) 5.0 -> 4.0. Of 18 trades, ONE reached +3.22 pts. A 5 point target was
// unreachable for this distribution; 4.0 is still above the measured average win
// and the partials take size off before it.
// ═════════════ (v18.43) 4.0 -> 8.0. THIS IS THE WALL. ═════════════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE. The implementation matched its intent exactly;
// the intent was what capped the system. Do not read this as a bug fix.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC, from the 14 Aug set of 76 tickets. Realised winners in points:
//   0.05 0.59 0.61 0.65 0.70 1.06 1.12 1.26 1.52 1.67 1.70
//   3.14 3.29 3.62 3.78 4.00 4.18 4.18 4.19 4.24 4.34 4.36 4.87
// EIGHT of the twelve winners above 3 pts land in the band 4.00-4.87, i.e. within
// 0.9 pts of this input, and NOTHING in the day's history exceeds 4.87. That is
// not a distribution, it is a ceiling with a number on it.
//
// WHY NOTHING ELSE COULD BE THE CEILING — traced, not assumed:
//   · This test lives in MaxLossCheck(), which ManagePosition() calls at its very
//     FIRST line (~11988), before the ratchet, the partials, the hold quorum,
//     HOLD-TO-TARGET (~12147), the arbiter and all 28 tagged exits.
//   · It closes via CloseTagged("MAX-LOSS"). "MAX-LOSS" is in the `truly` list in
//     the T_MinWinnerExitPts guard (~6032) AND in IsSafetyExit(), so both the
//     3-point winner floor and T_ExitMinR exempt it. Its own log line says so out
//     loud: "Tagged MAX-LOSS so no hold rule or arbiter can veto a deliberate
//     target."
//   · There is no broker TP to blame: InpRideToFlip = true, so tp = 0.
//   So on the profit side this single line preempts the entire tuned exit stack,
//   on every tick. T_HoldToTarget cannot "lose" to it — it is evaluated 159 lines
//   after the trade has already been closed.
//
// AND IT MADE ITS OWN TUNING UNREACHABLE. givebackPct is tiered:
//   peakR >= 5 -> 37% : peakR >= 2.5 -> 22% : 12%.
// With R ~2.8 pts, 2.5R is ~7 pts and 5R is ~14 pts. A 4.0 pt cap is 1.43R, so
// the 22% and 37% branches could never execute and the ": 12" branch was the only
// live one. The giveback percentages were tuned for peaks this input forbade.
//
// EXPECTED EFFECT, from the measured continuation table (7 legs, 14 Aug):
// after banking ~4 pts, price continued +11.81 +9.76 +10.42 +6.54 +2.91 +1.42
// +2.33. Peaks would therefore have reached roughly 16.0 14.1 14.8 11.4 6.7 5.8
// 6.5 — five of seven clear 8.0 and bank outright; the other two ride to a
// giveback allowance that has by then widened to 22% (~1.3-1.5 pts). Estimated
// 49.6 pts against the 29.06 pts actually taken on those same seven legs. It also
// removes the reason those legs were re-entered at all, and those re-entries netted
// -66 USD.  CONFIDENCE: LIKELY, not confirmed — the continuation figures are
// price extremes, and I cannot see from ticket data whether the path to them
// dipped far enough en route to trip the ~1 ATR allowance first.
//
// HONEST COST, and the condition under which this LOSES money: if a leg tops out
// between 4.0 and 8.0 pts, the outright bank no longer fires and the trade exits
// on the giveback instead, handing back the allowance — currently max(0.6 ATR +
// spread, 1.0 ATR) ~= 1.0 pt. So each such trade banks ~1 pt less. On 14 Aug that
// is eight trades, ~-8 pts, ~-32 USD at 0.04 lots. It loses overall if fewer than
// about a fifth of the continuation measured above is actually capturable.
// WORSE FAILURE MODE, stated plainly: if the soft giveback is stood down by
// holdGrace (see the v17.88 note at ~13138 — six OR-ed hold votes are true most
// of the time), the only remaining ceiling is hardGivebackCap at
// T_MaxGivebackPct = 65% of peak. On an 8 pt peak that banks ~2.8 pts, which is
// WORSE than the 4.0 it replaced. That is the single thing to watch in the log.
//
// ONE-LINE REVERT: set T_TargetPts back to 4.0. T_TargetPts = 0 disables the
// outright target entirely — do NOT use 0 as the revert, that is a third
// behaviour, not the old one.
input double T_TargetPts      = 8.0;    // (v18.43) 4.0 -> 8.0, see above   // (v18.36) bank the position outright at this many points
input double T_MaxLossPts     = 3.0;    // (v18.36) 4.0 -> 3.0: at 0.05 a 4 pt loss is ~20 USD, which he
                                        // has said twice is more than the account can carry per trade.    // close at market once a trade is this many points down
input bool   T_ReversalCut    = true;
// ═════════ (v18.45) 0.40 -> 0.70. THE LOSS SIDE, FROM HIS TICKETS. ═════════
// CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE. Required precondition for 1.2 above.
// Realised losses in points, 17 Aug, from his two History screenshots:
//   07:09 -1.29 | 07:13 -1.13 | 07:27 -0.78 | 07:55 -1.28 | 07:57 -1.31
//   08:12 -0.96 | 08:25 -0.25 | 08:39 -1.33 | 08:40 -1.45
// and 14 Aug: median 1.28, mean 1.40, with 34 of 53 losers between 1.0 and 1.5.
// That is not a distribution. It is one mechanism firing at a fixed distance.
// THE ARITHMETIC: 0.40 x T_MaxLossPts (3.0) = 1.20 pts, measured from ENTRY,
// inside an M1 ATR of ~1.0. So the cut sits at 1.2 ATR -- close to a coin flip on
// noise alone. Meanwhile T_SlFloorPts = 2.50 is the real stop, so this fires at
// 48% of the distance to the risk that was actually budgeted. The broker stop has
// effectively never been reached. This is a stop-loss in disguise at half size.
// THE PROOF THAT IT CARRIES NO INFORMATION: 17 Aug 07:55 and 07:57, two SELLS at
// 4404.309 and 4404.599, both cut at -1.28 and -1.31. Price then fell to 4392.5
// -- twelve points, in their favour. In the same hour the 08:39 and 08:40 BUYS at
// 4401.989 and 4400.738 were cut at -1.33 and -1.45, and they were wrong. The
// mechanism killed the right trades and the wrong trades at the same distance, to
// within 0.2 pts. A rule that cannot separate a 12 pt winner from a genuine loser
// is not risk control; it is a random 1.3 pt tax on every entry.
// 0.70 puts the cut at 2.10 pts = ~2.1 ATR: outside the noise band, still inside
// the 2.50 stop floor so the mechanism survives as an early exit and the broker
// stop remains the backstop.
// HONEST COST, stated plainly: losers roughly double in size, ~1.3 -> ~2.1 pts.
// On 14 Aug's 53 losers that is about -42 pts, roughly -170 USD at 0.04 lots.
// It only pays if enough of the cut trades were right-direction. On the 17 Aug
// sample 3 of 9 look recoverable -- n=9, one day, one direction, so LIKELY, not
// CONFIRMED. THIS IS THE SINGLE RISKIEST NUMBER IN THE BUILD. If one session
// shows losses widening without the runners appearing, revert this first.
// (v18.46 SELF-AUDIT — I had this number wrong.) 0.70 x 3.0 = 2.10 pts, and
// T_SlFloorPts = 2.50. That leaves 0.40 pts of separation, and after ~0.30 of
// spread the real clearance is ~0.10 pts. So at 0.70 the reversal cut and the
// broker stop fire essentially TOGETHER and the mechanism stops being "an early
// exit with the stop as backstop" -- it becomes a redundant duplicate of the
// stop, which is the same duplication class as shipping a fourth peak tracker.
// My justification last build claimed the separation existed. It did not.
// 0.60 x 3.0 = 1.80 pts: still 1.8 ATR, still clear of the measured loss cluster
// (0.96 to 1.45 pts), and now with 0.70 pts of genuine room before the stop.
// REVERT: T_ReversalCutFrac = 0.40.
input double T_ReversalCutFrac = 0.60;
// (v18.48) Age gate for the reversal cut. Defaults to T_MinHoldSecs' value (180),
// stated as its own input so the cut can be aged independently of the discretionary
// stack. 0 = off. Declared here so it precedes MaxLossCheck() in source order.
input int    T_ReversalCutMinSecs = 180;
// (v18.49) Let the peak-anchored GIVEBACK through the winner floor. See the note in
// CloseTagged(). Declared here to precede first use in source order.
input bool   T_GivebackExemptFloor = true;
// (v18.51) A genuine opposite Pine signal must be able to close a winner. See the
// note in CloseTagged(). Declared here to precede first use in source order.
input bool   T_FlipExemptFloor     = true;
// (v18.54) Let the range-scalp bank through the winner floor. See both notes.
input bool   T_RangeExemptFloor    = true;
// (v18.51) false = the DEMA-cross escape no longer releases the entry-anchored
// clippers. true restores the pre-18.51 blanket release (the 50p bug).
input bool   T_RevEscapeAllTags    = false;   // (v18.30) share of T_MaxLossPts at which a wrong-side-of-DEMA trade is cut   // cut at half that distance if price is also across the DEMA

void MaxLossCheck(int e, ulong tk)
{
   if(T_MaxLossPts <= 0.0) return;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;
   if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) return;
   int    dr = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   double op = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
   double px = (dr > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                        : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   double pts = (px - op) * dr;
   if(pts >= 0.0)
   {
      // (v18.36) the profit side is handled first — see the target block below.
      if(T_TargetPts > 0.0 && pts >= PS(T_TargetPts))
      {
         PrintFormat("[TARGET] #%I64u reached %+.2f pts (target %.1f). Banking it — in and out; the goal "
                     "is the hourly number, not the maximum possible. Tagged MAX-LOSS so no hold rule "
                     "or arbiter can veto a deliberate target.", tk, pts, PS(T_TargetPts));
         CloseTagged("MAX-LOSS", tk);
      }
      return;
   }
   double down = -pts;

   // (v18.36) NOTE: the profit target is handled in the pts >= 0 branch above, not
   // here. This point in the function is only reached when the trade is DOWN, so a
   // target test here would be dead code. Flagging it rather than leaving a
   // plausible-looking block that can never run.
   if(down >= PS(T_MaxLossPts))
   {
      PrintFormat("[MAX LOSS] #%I64u is %.2f pts down (cap %.1f). Closing at market on our own accord — "
                  "the stop is the disaster brake, not the exit.", tk, down, PS(T_MaxLossPts));
      CloseTagged("MAX-LOSS", tk);
      return;
   }
   // (v18.30) 0.5 -> 0.4 of the cap. His screenshot sat at 1.93 pts down with the
   // trigger at 2.00 -- it missed by 7 cents while the reversal ran.
   // ════════ (v18.48) BUG FIX — THE 3-MINUTE GRACE WAS BEING BYPASSED ════════
   // CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX. Implementation != intent, and the intent is his own.
   // v17.61 set T_MinHoldSecs = 180 on his explicit call ("3 minutes"), so that a
   // fresh trade is left alone by discretionary exits while the entry breathes.
   // But holdGrace is computed inside ManagePosition, and ManagePosition calls
   // MaxLossCheck() on its FIRST line (~11988) -- before holdGrace exists. So this
   // cut, which closes the large majority of all losers, never saw the grace at
   // all. It could fire on a trade seconds old. That is the RULE 2 failure mode
   // exactly: a guard sitting above the tuned stack, returning first.
   // HIS EVIDENCE, 17 Aug: the SELL at 4404.309 (closed 07:55:35) and the SELL at
   // 4404.599 (closed 07:57:34) were the two best entries of the morning -- price
   // fell from there to 4392.5, thirteen points. Both were cut at -1.28 and -1.31
   // roughly one to two minutes after opening. The grace he asked for would have
   // held both, and the down leg started at ~08:00.
   // WHAT IS *NOT* AGED, deliberately: the hard T_MaxLossPts cap above, and the
   // broker stop at T_SlFloorPts = 2.50. Risk stays bounded at all times -- a
   // fresh trade that runs 2.5 pts against us inside the grace still stops out at
   // the broker. This only stops the DISCRETIONARY cut from pre-empting the grace.
   // HONEST COST: a trade whose thesis dies in the first 180 seconds now pays up to
   // the 2.50 stop instead of 1.80 -- about 0.70 pts more on those. Against that,
   // every correct entry that needed two minutes to work now gets them.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_ReversalCutMinSecs = 0 restores the old, ungated behaviour.
   long   rcAge  = (long)(TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME));
   bool   rcAged = (T_ReversalCutMinSecs <= 0 || rcAge >= T_ReversalCutMinSecs);
   if(!rcAged && T_ReversalCut && down >= T_ReversalCutFrac * PS(T_MaxLossPts))
      PrintFormat("[REVERSAL CUT HELD] #%I64u is %.2f pts down but only %I64d s old "
                  "(grace %d s). The entry has not had room to work yet; the broker stop "
                  "at %.2f pts is still the backstop.",
                  tk, down, rcAge, T_ReversalCutMinSecs, T_SlFloorPts);
   if(rcAged && T_ReversalCut && down >= T_ReversalCutFrac * PS(T_MaxLossPts))
   {
      // inline slot lookup — TwoPoleKOf() does the same job but is declared further
      // down the file, and MQL5 resolves in source order.
      int mk = -1;
      for(int i = 0; i < SC_TFN; i++) if(g_scTF[i] == Cfg[e].tf) { mk = i; break; }
      double dm = (mk >= 0) ? Buf(g_scDema[mk], 0, 1) : 0.0;
      if(dm > 0.0 && ((dr > 0 && px < dm) || (dr < 0 && px > dm)))
      {
         PrintFormat("[REVERSAL CUT] #%I64u is %.2f pts down AND on the wrong side of the DEMA (%.2f vs %.2f). "
                     "The thesis is gone, not just the price — cutting at half the cap instead of paying "
                     "the rest to find out.", tk, down, px, dm);
         CloseTagged("MAX-LOSS", tk);
      }
   }
}

bool CloseTagged(string tag, ulong t)
{
   if(T_HtfNeverCloses && IsHtfCloseTag(tag))
   {
      PrintFormat("[VETO] %s wanted to close #%I64u because a higher clock disagrees. Refused — "
                  "this trade is managed by its own clock only.", tag, t);
      QSP_TagRecord("~" + tag, 0.0, 0.0);
      return false;
   }
   // (v18.17) THE ARBITER WAS SYMMETRIC AND SHOULD NOT HAVE BEEN.
   // v18.12 blocked every discretionary exit below T_ExitMinR. That range
   // includes NEGATIVE R -- so a trade that went straight against us could no
   // longer be cut by EARLY-FAIL, THESIS-DEAD or STRUCT-BREAK, and its only
   // remaining exit was the full stop. Combined with the v18.07 4-point stop
   // floor, I turned every immediate loser into a maximum-size loser. That is
   // exactly the behaviour he identified from the 07:18 ticket
   // (4405.06 -> 4398.86, -6.2 pts, ran to the stop): "when we go into loss at
   // the beginning and the market reverses we don't close, we just wait for it
   // to hit SL -- that's kinda wrong." He is right, and I built it.
   // The arbiter's purpose was only ever to stop opinions cutting WINNERS short.
   // It now applies solely in the band 0 < R < T_ExitMinR. At or below break-even
   // the failure-cut logic is free again, which is where it belongs: a thesis
   // that is wrong immediately is the one case where an early exit is cheap.
   // ══════ (v18.34) THE GAP IN THE ARBITER: SAFETY EXITS WERE EXEMPT ══════
   // HIS CHART: BUY at ~4385.6, the leg ran to 4392.5 -- about 7 points. Three
   // entry/exit pairs inside that leg, each banking roughly ONE point.
   // The arbiter (v18.12) was supposed to stop this, but it exempts every tag in
   // IsSafetyExit() -- and FLIP-CLOSE is in that list. On M1 with a 2.0 multiplier
   // the Supertrend flips back and forth several times inside a single clean leg,
   // so FLIP-CLOSE fires repeatedly, and being "safety" it bypassed every hold
   // mechanism I built: the arbiter, the hold quorum, hold-to-target, all of it.
   // Exempting it was right for LOSING trades (a revoked thesis must be actionable)
   // and wrong for winners.
   // THE RULE: no exit may close a PROFITABLE trade for less than T_MinWinnerExitPts
   // unless it is a genuine reversal (price across the DEMA), a hard stop, news, or
   // the weekend. Losing trades are untouched -- they still cut fast. This is the
   // asymmetry that has caught me out four times now: hold winners hard, cut losers
   // fast, and never write one rule for both.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_MinWinnerExitPts = 0.
   if(T_MinWinnerExitPts > 0.0 && PositionSelectByTicket(t))
   {
      string ut = tag; StringToUpper(ut);
      // ═══ (v18.49) BUG FIX OF MY OWN v18.44 REGRESSION — READ THIS ═══
      // v18.44 raised T_MinWinnerExitPts from 3.00 to 6.00. This guard vetoes every
      // exit not on the `truly` list while profit sits below that figure. GIVEBACK
      // is not on the list. GIVEBACK is the ONLY exit that banks a retracing winner.
      // So from v18.44 until now, a trade that peaked anywhere under 6 points could
      // not be banked by anything: the floor vetoed the giveback, the target had been
      // moved to 8.0, and the clippers were vetoed too. The position simply rode back
      // through its peak, through break-even, and down to the 2.50 broker stop.
      // WORST CASE I CREATED: peak +5.9, exit -2.50. An 8.4 point round trip on a
      // trade that was 5.9 points in profit. That is the exact thing he asked about:
      // how does the EA lose money on a trade that HAD profit in it.
      // WHY GIVEBACK BELONGS HERE AND THE CLIPPERS DO NOT: this guard exists to stop
      // an OPINION clipping a winner early. GIVEBACK is not an opinion and cannot
      // fire early -- it is peak-anchored by construction, so it only exists after a
      // peak has formed and been given back. The clippers (SMALL-MOVE, CHOP-STALL,
      // MOM-FADE, STALL, FLIP-CLOSE...) are entry-anchored opinions and stay vetoed.
      // That is the doctrine: winners and losers measured from OPPOSITE anchors.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_GivebackExemptFloor = false restores v18.44 behaviour.
      bool truly = (StringFind(ut, "NEWS")   >= 0 || StringFind(ut, "FRI")   >= 0
                 || StringFind(ut, "GAP")    >= 0 || StringFind(ut, "SPREAD-PANIC") >= 0
                 || StringFind(ut, "MAX-LOSS") >= 0 || StringFind(ut, "MANUAL") >= 0
      // ══════════ (v19.02 BI) HIS OWN PANEL FOUND THIS. READ IT. ══════════
      //   BASKET-LOCK    24 fired   +46.7 pts   lost -2.0
      //  ~BASKET-LOCK   157 SUPPRESSED
      // The basket lock tried to fire 181 times and this floor vetoed 157 of them.
      // The 24 that got through were the best mechanism in the build: +46.7 points
      // for GBP2 of give-back. The 157 that did not are the whole loss.
      //
      // AND IT EXPLAINS THE THING HE DESCRIBED EXACTLY: "it went up 5 pound profit,
      // somehow closed 3 positions in loss, held 3 and they also closed in loss."
      // BASKET-LOCK closes every open position in one pass. This floor is applied
      // PER POSITION. So on a six-fill basket it let through the three already past
      // the per-ticket floor and vetoed the three that were not -- splitting one
      // basket decision down the middle, which is the one thing it must never do.
      // A basket exit is not an opinion about a single trade. It is arithmetic on
      // the total, already gated by its own arm level, and it is all-or-nothing by
      // construction. STACK-BANK is a fixed target for the same reason.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_BasketExemptFloor = false.
                 || (T_BasketExemptFloor && (StringFind(ut, "BASKET-LOCK") >= 0
                                          || StringFind(ut, "STACK-BANK")  >= 0))
      // (v19.06 BV) TRADE-LOCK is the per-ticket form of BASKET-LOCK and is
      // exempt for the identical reason: it is arithmetic on a peak already
      // gated by its own arm level, not an opinion about the trade. Leaving it
      // subject to T_MinWinnerExitPts (6.00 pts = GBP4.40) would veto it on
      // every trade whose peak was under that -- which on 26 Aug was 109 of
      // them, worth GBP176.41. That veto IS the bug being fixed.
                 || (T_TradeLock && StringFind(ut, "TRADE-LOCK") >= 0)
      // (v19.10 BZ) and the infant cut. It closes a LOSER so the winner floor
      // should not reach it anyway -- listed explicitly so a future change to that
      // condition cannot silently start vetoing 77% of the day's fix.
                 || (T_InfantCut && StringFind(ut, "INFANT-CUT") >= 0)
                 || (T_GivebackExemptFloor && StringFind(ut, "GIVEBACK") >= 0)
      // ═══ (v18.51) THE SELL THAT WOULD NOT CLOSE ON A BUY SIGNAL ═══
      // He watched a profitable SELL stay open while an opposite BUY signal printed,
      // and the BUY never fired either -- "we lost both profits". This is the cause
      // of the first half. A genuine opposite Pine signal closes through FLIP-CLOSE
      // (or SCALP-ST-FLIP). Neither tag is on this list, so from v18.44 -- when I
      // raised T_MinWinnerExitPts to 6.00 -- a reversal could not close a winner
      // holding less than 6 points. The position simply sat there against the signal.
      // A FULL REVERSAL IS NOT A CLIPPER. The clippers this floor exists to stop
      // (SMALL-MOVE, CHOP-STALL, MOM-FADE, STALL) are entry-anchored opinions about
      // a trade that is still in its own direction. A Supertrend flip on the trade's
      // own clock is the signal itself changing its mind, and the standing rule is
      // that every Pine signal is taken -- which is impossible while the opposite
      // position is still open and the EA is hedging.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_FlipExemptFloor = false.
                 || (T_FlipExemptFloor && (StringFind(ut, "FLIP-CLOSE") >= 0
                                        || StringFind(ut, "SCALP-ST-FLIP") >= 0))
      // (v18.54) A range-born trade is exempt too. The floor stops an opinion from
      // clipping a TREND winner early; a trade the regime engine measured as
      // range-born has no trend to clip, and RANGE-SCALP only fires while the range
      // is still, measurably, unresolved. Without this the floor vetoes every range
      // bank and T_RangeScalpR = 0.80 would have no effect at all.
                 || (T_RangeExemptFloor && StringFind(ut, "RANGE-SCALP") >= 0));
      if(!truly)
      {
         int    wd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         double wo = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
         double wp = (wd > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                              : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
         double wpts = (wp - wo) * wd;
         // (v18.63) peak-relative: the floor may not exceed T_PeakGateFrac of what
         // this trade actually offered. At a 3.63 pt peak the 6.00 floor becomes
         // 2.18, so a bank is possible instead of impossible. At a 20 pt peak
         // 0.60 x 20 = 12 > 6.00, so the floor stays 6.00 exactly as v18.44 set it.
         double wFloor = PS(T_MinWinnerExitPts);
         if(T_PeakGateWinner && T_PeakGateFrac > 0.0)
         {
            double wpk = PeakPtsOf(t);
            if(wpk > 0.0) wFloor = MathMin(wFloor, T_PeakGateFrac * wpk);
         }
         if(wpts > 0.0 && wpts < wFloor)
         {
            // a real reversal still gets through: price on the wrong side of the DEMA
            int wk = -1;
            for(int i = 0; i < SC_TFN; i++) if(g_scTF[i] == PERIOD_M1) { wk = i; break; }
            double wdm  = (wk >= 0) ? Buf(g_scDema[wk], 0, 1) : 0.0;
            double watr = (wk >= 0) ? Buf(g_scAtr[wk],  0, 1) : 0.0;
            double wcl  = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);   // (v18.44) CLOSED bar, not the live tick
            double wbuf = T_RevBufAtr * watr;
            // ═══ (v18.51) THE 50p BUG — "went into a pound profit and closed at 50p" ═══
            // At 0.03 lots GBP 1.00 is about 0.42 pts and 50p is about 0.21 pts, so
            // something banked roughly a fifth of a point. Nothing on the `truly` list
            // can do that. This escape can: it opens the floor to EVERY clipper the
            // moment a closed M1 bar sits across the M1 DEMA, and on M1 with a 60-length
            // DEMA that happens constantly. Once open, the first of ten clippers in file
            // order takes the trade at whatever it is holding -- 0.2 pts included.
            // That is the 0.05-1.70 pt winner band, and it is the real cause of it.
            // FIXED: the escape now only releases the DIRECTIONAL exits. If the signal
            // has genuinely reversed, FLIP-CLOSE and SCALP-ST-FLIP are already exempt
            // above and do not need this. What this escape was actually doing was
            // letting entry-anchored opinions clip a winner on a DEMA wobble.
            // OFF-SWITCH: T_RevEscapeAllTags = true restores the blanket behaviour.
            bool reversed = (T_RevEscapeAllTags && wdm > 0.0 && wcl > 0.0 &&
                             ((wd > 0 && wcl < wdm - wbuf) || (wd < 0 && wcl > wdm + wbuf)));
            if(!reversed)
            {
               PrintFormat("[WINNER HELD] %s tried to close #%I64u at only %+.2f pts (floor %.1f) and price "
                           "is still our side of the DEMA. Refused — this is the one-point bank inside a "
                           "seven-point leg.", tag, t, wpts, wFloor);
               QSP_TagRecord("~" + tag, 0.0, 0.0);
               return false;
            }
         }
      }
   }
   if(T_ExitArbiter && !IsSafetyExit(tag))
   {
      bool rOk = false;
      double pr = TicketProfitR(t, rOk);
      // (v18.63) same peak-relative treatment, in R rather than points.
      double aMin = T_ExitMinR;
      if(T_PeakGateArbiter && T_PeakGateFrac > 0.0)
      {
         double apk = PeakR(t);
         if(apk > 0.0) aMin = MathMin(aMin, T_PeakGateFrac * apk);
      }
      if(rOk && pr > T_ExitLoserFloorR && pr < aMin)
      {
         PrintFormat("[ARBITER] %s HELD OFF #%I64u at %+.2fR — a discretionary exit may not act below "
                     "%.2fR. The stop and this trade's own flip are still live.", tag, t, pr, aMin);
         QSP_TagRecord("~" + tag, 0.0, 0.0);
         return false;
      }
   }
   // (v18.77 AA) the two TOP DETECTORS are exempt from the blanket. See the note
   // at T_BanksOffExemptPeak. The other six BANK tags are unchanged and stay off.
   bool bankSuppressed = T_BanksOff && QSP_IsBank(tag);
   if(bankSuppressed && T_BanksOffExemptPeak && (tag == "PEAK-BANK" || tag == "SPIKE-PEAK"))
      bankSuppressed = false;
   // (v18.79 AE) the sub-2.31 pt band. See the note at T_BanksOffExemptSmall.
   if(bankSuppressed && T_BanksOffExemptSmall && tag == "SMALL-MOVE")
      bankSuppressed = false;
   if(bankSuppressed || (T_FadesOff && QSP_IsFade(tag)))
   {
      QSP_TagRecord("~" + tag, 0.0, 0.0);     // counted as SUPPRESSED, so the cost is visible
      return false;
   }
   double pts = 0.0, peak = 0.0;
   if(PositionSelectByTicket(t))
   {
      int    dr = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      double op = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double px = (dr > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                           : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
      pts = (px - op) * dr;
   }
   for(int i = 0; i < QSP_LIVE_MAX; i++)
      if(g_qspDir[i] != 0 && g_qspTk[i] == t) { peak = g_qspPeak[i]; break; }
   bool ok = trade.PositionClose(t);
   PrintFormat("[EXIT %s] #%I64u took %+.2f pts of a %+.2f pt peak (%.0f%% of peak given up)%s",
               tag, t, pts, peak,
               (peak > 0.0) ? 100.0 * (peak - pts) / peak : 0.0,
               ok ? "" : " — REJECTED, retrying");
   if(ok) QSP_TagRecord(tag, pts, peak);
   return ok;
}

//---- progress tracking -------------------------------------------
// BUG FIX #2: the old version filled the array to 200 and then SILENTLY
// stopped registering new tickets — so after 200 trades the stall-exit stopped
// working entirely and nobody would ever have noticed. Now it recycles.
void TrackProgress(ulong tk, int dir, double px)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++)
   {
      if(g_pTicket[i] == tk)
      {
         bool adv = (dir > 0) ? (px > g_pPeak[i]) : (px < g_pPeak[i]);
         if(adv) { g_pPeak[i] = px; g_pLast[i] = TimeCurrent(); }
         return;
      }
   }
   if(g_pCount >= 200) g_pCount = 0;   // recycle the oldest slot
   g_pTicket[g_pCount] = tk;
   g_pPeak[g_pCount]   = px;
   g_pLast[g_pCount]   = TimeCurrent();
   g_pCount++;
}

bool Stalled(ulong tk, int barsAllowed, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++)
      if(g_pTicket[i] == tk)
         return (TimeCurrent() - g_pLast[i]) > (long)barsAllowed * PeriodSeconds(tf);
   return false;
}

//---- DEMA value on a given engine's timeframe (closed bar) ---------
// The trend filter. A long is only valid ABOVE this, a short only BELOW it.
double DemaVal(int e)      { return Buf(hDema[e], 0, 1); }
double DemaValSc(int k)    { return Buf(g_scDema[k], 0, 1); }

//---- Supertrend (cached per bar, per TF) --------------------------
double g_stVal[ENG_COUNT];
int    g_stDir[ENG_COUNT];
datetime g_stBar[ENG_COUNT];

void Supertrend(int e, double &val, int &dir)
{
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
   if(g_stBar[e] == b0 && b0 > 0) { val = g_stVal[e]; dir = g_stDir[e]; return; }

   // (v18.88 AN) was a fixed 120, and 30 bars SHORTER than the entry path's window,
   // so the context read and the signal read could disagree purely on warmup.
   // Both now use the same number. See T_StWarmupBars.
   int bars = MathMax(50, T_StWarmupBars);
   double mult = T_StMultiplier;   // (v16.90) was hardcoded 3.0 here too. Both Supertrend
                                   // implementations now read the SAME input, so the M15/HouseBias
                                   // context read can never silently drift from the M1/M3/M5
                                   // signal read — which would make the bias filter judge trades
                                   // against a differently-shaped trend than the one that fired.
   double a[]; ArraySetAsSeries(a, false);
   if(CopyBuffer(hST[e], 0, 1, bars, a) < bars) { val = 0; dir = 0; return; }

   double up = 0, dn = 0, prevUp = 0, prevDn = 0;
   int d = 1;
   for(int i = bars - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      int sh = i + 1;
      double hh = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, sh), lo = iLow(_Symbol, tf, sh), cl = iClose(_Symbol, tf, sh);
      double pc = iClose(_Symbol, tf, sh + 1);
      double atr = a[bars - 1 - i];
      double mid = (hh + lo) / 2.0;
      double bUp = mid - mult * atr, bDn = mid + mult * atr;

      if(i == bars - 1) { up = bUp; dn = bDn; d = (cl >= mid) ? 1 : -1; }   // (v16.08 fix #68) seed from basis, not upper band
      else
      {
         up = (pc > prevUp) ? MathMax(bUp, prevUp) : bUp;
         dn = (pc < prevDn) ? MathMin(bDn, prevDn) : bDn;
         if(d == 1 && cl < up) d = -1;
         else if(d == -1 && cl > dn) d = 1;
      }
      prevUp = up; prevDn = dn;
   }
   val = (d == 1) ? up : dn;
   dir = d;
   g_stBar[e] = b0; g_stVal[e] = val; g_stDir[e] = dir;
}

//---- session VWAP + std dev --------------------------------------
double g_vwap = 0, g_vwapSd = 0;
int    g_vwapBars = 0;   // how much session has actually developed
datetime g_vwapBar = 0;

void CalcVWAP()
{
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, 0);
   if(g_vwapBar == b0 && b0 > 0) return;
   g_vwapBar = b0;

   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), t);
   double pv = 0, vv = 0;
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = 1; i <= 300; i++)
   {
      datetime bt = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, i);
      if(bt == 0) break;
      MqlDateTime b; TimeToStruct(bt, b);
      if(b.day != t.day) break;
      double tp = (iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M5,i) + iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M5,i) + iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M5,i)) / 3.0;
      double v  = (double)iTickVolume(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, i);   // real volume is 0 on CFDs
      pv += tp * v; vv += v; n++;
   }
   g_vwap = (vv > 0) ? pv / vv : iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, 1);

   double s = 0;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      double d = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, i) - g_vwap;
      s += d * d;
   }

   // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #24 (SEVERE) ══════════════════
   // The old code only required n > 1. So twenty minutes into a session, with
   // FOUR M5 bars, it computed a "standard deviation" from four samples — which
   // is not a standard deviation, it is noise. That tiny sd went into
   //     dev = (close - vwap) / sd
   // and produced enormous deviation readings. The VWAP fade engine would fire
   // 2.2-sigma "extremes" that were nothing of the sort, every single morning,
   // in the first hour — the worst possible time to fade anything on gold.
   //
   // A session VWAP band means nothing until the session has actually developed.
   // 24 M5 bars = two hours. Below that, this engine stays out.
   g_vwapBars = n;
   g_vwapSd = (n >= 24) ? MathSqrt(s / n) : 0;
}

double RelVol(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int shift)
{
   double sum = 0;
   for(int i = shift + 1; i <= shift + 20; i++) sum += (double)iTickVolume(_Symbol, tf, i);
   double avg = sum / 20.0;
   if(avg <= 0) return 1.0;
   return (double)iTickVolume(_Symbol, tf, shift) / avg;
}

//---- VOLATILITY REGIME + ADAPTIVE SIZING ------------------------
// Percentile of the CURRENT ATR against its own history. A percentile, not an
// absolute, because absolutes silently break the moment gold re-rates.
// BUG FIX #25 (PERFORMANCE): AtrRank() ran a 200-element CopyBuffer and a
// 200-iteration loop on EVERY call. AdaptMults() calls it, and AdaptMults() is
// called from every engine's entry check AND from every trailing-stop update,
// which run on EVERY TICK for EVERY open position. On seven engines that is
// thousands of buffer copies per second on a fast gold feed. The strategy
// tester would crawl and the live EA would lag the market it is trying to trade.
// Cached per bar, per engine.
double   g_arVal[ENG_COUNT];
datetime g_arBar[ENG_COUNT];

double AtrRank(int e)
{
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, Cfg[e].tf, 0);
   if(g_arBar[e] == b0 && b0 > 0) return g_arVal[e];

   double now = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
   if(now <= 0) return 50.0;
   double a[];
   if(CopyBuffer(hAtr[e], 0, 1, InpAtrRankBars, a) < InpAtrRankBars) return 50.0;
   int below = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < InpAtrRankBars; i++) if(a[i] < now) below++;

   double r = 100.0 * below / (double)InpAtrRankBars;
   g_arBar[e] = b0; g_arVal[e] = r;
   return r;
}

// Overload for TREND's per-timeframe reads (M1/M3/M5), same pattern as
// SpikeBufferTF — explicit ATR handle instead of reading it via Cfg[e].tf.
double AtrRankHandle(int handle, double curAtr)
{
   if(curAtr <= 0) return 50.0;
   double a[];
   if(CopyBuffer(handle, 0, 1, InpAtrRankBars, a) < InpAtrRankBars) return 50.0;
   int below = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < InpAtrRankBars; i++) if(a[i] < curAtr) below++;
   return 100.0 * below / (double)InpAtrRankBars;
}

void AdaptMults(int e, double &slM, double &tpM)
{
   slM = 1.0; tpM = 1.0;
   if(!InpAdaptSLTP) return;
   double r = AtrRank(e);
   if(r >= 75)      { slM = InpHiVolSL; tpM = InpHiVolTP; }
   else if(r <= 25) { slM = InpLoVolSL; tpM = InpLoVolTP; }
}

// The engine's LIVE risk %, scaled by its own proven expectancy.
// IMPROVEMENT 9: SESSION QUALITY.
// Gold does not trend at 03:00. The London-NY overlap (12:00-16:00 GMT) is where
// the real moves live — deepest liquidity, tightest spread, cleanest trends. The
// Asian session is chop with a wide spread. Size should follow the quality of the
// tape, not pretend every hour is the same hour.
double SessionQuality()
{
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), t);
   int h = t.hour;
   if(h >= 12 && h < 16) return 1.20;   // London-NY overlap. The best tape of the day.
   if(h >= 8  && h < 12) return 1.05;   // London.
   if(h >= 16 && h < 20) return 1.00;   // NY afternoon.
   if(h >= 20 || h < 2)  return 0.70;   // late NY / Sydney. Thin.
   return 0.80;                          // Asian. Chop with a wide spread.
}

double LiveRisk(int e)
{
   double r = Cfg[e].risk;
   if(!InpAutoRisk || Stat[e].trades < InpAutoRiskMinN) return r;
   double ex = Stat[e].netR / Stat[e].trades;
   if(ex >= InpRiskUpExpR)     r *= InpRiskUpMult;
   else if(ex < InpRiskDnExpR) r *= InpRiskDnMult;
   return r;
}

// A live confidence read per engine, 0-100. Not entry confidence — this asks
// "how much do I trust THIS ENGINE right now, on its own track record?"
// It weights proven expectancy by SAMPLE SIZE, so a +2R engine with 3 trades is
// not treated like a +0.4R engine with 200. Small samples get pulled toward 50,
// which is the honest answer when you do not know yet.
int EngineConfidence(int e)
{
   if(Stat[e].disabled) return 0;
   if(Stat[e].trades < 5) return 50;
   double ex = Stat[e].netR / Stat[e].trades;
   double w  = MathMin(1.0, MathSqrt((double)Stat[e].trades / 100.0));
   double raw = 50.0 + ex * 60.0;
   raw = 50.0 + (raw - 50.0) * w;
   if(Stat[e].consec >= 3) raw -= 10.0;
   return (int)MathMax(0.0, MathMin(100.0, raw));
}

int GmtHour()
{
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), t);
   return t.hour;
}

//================================================================
//  INIT
//================================================================
int OnInit()
{
   // (v18.73 W) unmissable build banner. He must be able to confirm which code is
   // running without asking me, and I must never again reason about his tape
   // without knowing which build produced it.
   PrintFormat("=============== XAUUSD QUAD %s ===============", EA_BUILD);
   PrintFormat("[BUILD] %s | ScalpFlipNeedDema=%s ScalpRatchet=%s HardLockBandCap=%s "
               "NetBoxLive=%s | OFF-by-audit: Scratch=%s Basket=%s PartialAtBand=%s "
               "LegTrim=%.2f WickTrim=%s M5Conf=%.2f",
               EA_BUILD,
               T_ScalpFlipNeedDema ? "on" : "off", T_ScalpRatchet ? "on" : "off",
               T_HardLockBandCap ? "on" : "off", T_NetBoxLive ? "on" : "off",
               T_ScalpScratch ? "ON" : "off", T_BasketGiveback ? "ON" : "off",
               T_PartialAtBand ? "ON" : "off", T_LegLateTrimFrac,
               T_WickSideTrim ? "ON" : "off", T_M5ConfluenceLots);
   // (v14 #1b) RESTORE HOUR-LEARNING — pick the lessons back up from the last
   // run. See OnDeinit for the save.
   for(int hp = 0; hp < 24; hp++)
   {
      string kR = "QUAD_hR_" + IntegerToString(hp), kN = "QUAD_hN_" + IntegerToString(hp);
      if(GlobalVariableCheck(kR)) g_hourR[hp] = GlobalVariableGet(kR);
      if(GlobalVariableCheck(kN)) g_hourN[hp] = (int)GlobalVariableGet(kN);
   }

   // ── PERMISSION CHECK ──
   // The single most common reason an EA "compiles fine, logs fine, never
   // trades": AutoTrading is off, or algo trading is not permitted on this
   // account/symbol. OnTick still runs and still prints the Diagnostics board —
   // it just silently refuses every trade.Buy()/trade.Sell() call. Loud warning
   // instead of a silent one.
   if(!MQLInfoInteger(MQL_TRADE_ALLOWED))
      Print("⚠ ALGO TRADING IS OFF FOR THIS EA. Click 'AutoTrading' in the toolbar, "
            "and check 'Allow Algo Trading' in this EA's Properties > Common tab. "
            "Nothing will trade until this is on, even though the log keeps printing.");
   if(!TerminalInfoInteger(TERMINAL_TRADE_ALLOWED))
      Print("⚠ TRADING IS DISABLED AT THE TERMINAL LEVEL. Check the AutoTrading button top toolbar.");
   if(!AccountInfoInteger(ACCOUNT_TRADE_ALLOWED))
      Print("⚠ THIS ACCOUNT DOES NOT ALLOW TRADING right now — check with your broker "
            "(demo account expired, or trading restricted).");

   // Running this on Silver/GBPJPY as a secondary instrument: fewer, stronger
   // trades only. Folds straight into the ADX floor every engine already checks.
   if(InpSecondarySymbol)
   {
      g_adxBoost += InpSecondaryAdxBoost;
      PrintFormat("Secondary-symbol mode: ADX floor raised by %.1f on %s — highest-confidence trades only.",
                   InpSecondaryAdxBoost, _Symbol);
   }

   //---- engine config table. Every field is deliberately different. ----
   Cfg[E_TREND].on=InpUseTrend; Cfg[E_TREND].magic=770100; Cfg[E_TREND].name="TREND";
   Cfg[E_TREND].tf=PERIOD_M5;  Cfg[E_TREND].risk=T_Risk; Cfg[E_TREND].maxHold=T_MaxHold;
   Cfg[E_TREND].cooldown=T_Cooldown; Cfg[E_TREND].trails=true;






   Cfg[E_APEX].on=InpUseApex;  Cfg[E_APEX].magic=770700; Cfg[E_APEX].name="APEX";
   Cfg[E_APEX].tf=PERIOD_M15;  Cfg[E_APEX].risk=A_Risk; Cfg[E_APEX].maxHold=9999;  // NO time limit. It rides.
   Cfg[E_APEX].cooldown=4;     Cfg[E_APEX].trails=true;

   Cfg[E_SCALP].on=InpUseScalp; Cfg[E_SCALP].magic=770800; Cfg[E_SCALP].name="SCALP";
   Cfg[E_SCALP].tf=PERIOD_M5;   Cfg[E_SCALP].risk=SC_Risk; Cfg[E_SCALP].maxHold=SC_MaxHold;
   Cfg[E_SCALP].cooldown=SC_Cooldown; Cfg[E_SCALP].trails=true;

   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
   {
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
      hST[e]     = iATR(_Symbol, tf, T_StAtrLen);
      hAtr[e]    = iATR(_Symbol, tf, 14);
      hAdx[e]    = iADX(_Symbol, tf, 14);
      hRsi[e]    = iRSI(_Symbol, tf, 14, PRICE_CLOSE);
      hEma21[e]  = iMA(_Symbol, tf, 21, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
      hEma50[e]  = iMA(_Symbol, tf, 50, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
      hEma200[e] = iMA(_Symbol, tf, 200, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
      hDema[e]   = iDEMA(_Symbol, tf, DemaLenFor(tf), 0, PRICE_CLOSE);
      hMacd[e]   = iMACD(_Symbol, tf, 12, 26, 9, PRICE_CLOSE);
      // (v16 audit) FAIL LOUD, NOT SILENT: only 2 of these 9 handles were
      // validated. If any unvalidated one failed to create (broker resource
      // limits, VPS restart before history loads), Buf() silently returns 0
      // and the engine trades on garbage — price always "above" a zero DEMA,
      // EMA structure checks meaningless. Every handle is now checked.
      if(hST[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hAtr[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hAdx[e] == INVALID_HANDLE ||
         hRsi[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hEma21[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hEma50[e] == INVALID_HANDLE ||
         hEma200[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hDema[e] == INVALID_HANDLE || hMacd[e] == INVALID_HANDLE)
      { Print("FATAL: indicator handle creation failed on engine ", Cfg[e].name); return INIT_FAILED; }

      Stat[e].trades=0; Stat[e].wins=0; Stat[e].netR=0; Stat[e].netCash=0;
      Stat[e].consec=0; Stat[e].pauseUntil=0; Stat[e].disabled=false; Stat[e].lastExit=0;
      g_stBar[e]=0;
   }

   // (the old MICRO reference handle is gone with the MICRO engine — SCALP builds
   //  its own reference from the timeframe ladder, which is strictly better)
   g_hH1Ema = iMA(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 50, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
   if(g_hH1Ema == INVALID_HANDLE) return INIT_FAILED;
   g_hH1Adx = iADX(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 14);
   if(g_hH1Adx == INVALID_HANDLE) return INIT_FAILED;

   // E7 SCALP handles — one full set per timeframe
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];
      g_scAtr[k] = iATR(_Symbol, tf, 14);
      g_scAdx[k] = iADX(_Symbol, tf, 14);
      g_scE21[k] = iMA(_Symbol, tf, 21, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
      g_scE50[k] = iMA(_Symbol, tf, 50, 0, MODE_EMA, PRICE_CLOSE);
      g_scSt[k]  = iATR(_Symbol, tf, T_StAtrLen);
      g_scRsi[k] = iRSI(_Symbol, tf, 14, PRICE_CLOSE);
      g_scDema[k] = iDEMA(_Symbol, tf, DemaLenFor(tf), 0, PRICE_CLOSE);
      g_scMacd[k] = iMACD(_Symbol, tf, 12, 26, 9, PRICE_CLOSE);
      g_scBar[k] = 0; g_scStBar[k] = 0; g_scLastDir[k] = 0;
      // (v16 audit) same fail-loud rule as the engine handles above.
      if(g_scAtr[k] == INVALID_HANDLE || g_scAdx[k] == INVALID_HANDLE ||
         g_scE21[k] == INVALID_HANDLE || g_scE50[k] == INVALID_HANDLE ||
         g_scSt[k]  == INVALID_HANDLE || g_scRsi[k] == INVALID_HANDLE ||
         g_scDema[k] == INVALID_HANDLE || g_scMacd[k] == INVALID_HANDLE)
      { Print("FATAL: per-timeframe handle creation failed on ", EnumToString(g_scTF[k])); return INIT_FAILED; }
   }

   RefreshStops();
   g_tickVal = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE);
   g_tickSz  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE);
   g_lotStep = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP);
   g_lotMin  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);

   trade.SetDeviationInPoints(InpSlippagePoints);
   long fm = SymbolInfoInteger(_Symbol, SYMBOL_FILLING_MODE);
   if((fm & SYMBOL_FILLING_FOK) != 0)      trade.SetTypeFilling(ORDER_FILLING_FOK);
   else if((fm & SYMBOL_FILLING_IOC) != 0) trade.SetTypeFilling(ORDER_FILLING_IOC);
   else                                    trade.SetTypeFilling(ORDER_FILLING_RETURN);

   g_dayStart = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_BALANCE);   // BUG FIX #21

   Print("=== QUAD v3 ===");
   Print("E0 TREND  M5  with-trend  trails  ride to opposite signal  ", T_Risk, "%");
   Print("E6 APEX   M15 TREND RIDE  NO TP     open  ", A_Risk, "%");
   Print("E7 SCALP  M1+M3+M5+M15 multi-TF  fade-exit  2.0R  ", SC_Risk, "%");
   Print("Tight stop, ride to the opposite signal — wins scale with the trend, losses stay small. Watch expectancy, not win rate.");

   // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #30 — THE PRACTICAL ONE ══════════════════
   // LotFor() refuses to trade when the broker's MINIMUM lot would risk more
   // than 1.5x the engine's budget. That is correct behaviour. But nobody ever
   // told you what it MEANS on a small account:
   //
   //   TREND: 1.9% of £100 = £1.90 budget. A $6 stop at 0.01 lot risks $6 = £4.50.
   //   £4.50 > £1.90 x 1.5.  ->  SKIPPED. Every time. Silently.
   //
   // On a £100 account with 0.01 minimum lots, MOST OF THESE ENGINES WILL SIMPLY
   // NEVER FIRE. You would have attached this, watched it do nothing for a day,
   // and had no idea why. Now it tells you, loudly, at startup.
   PrintFormat("AUTO-SCALE: %s tier at %.2f equity. Lot ceiling %.3f. Risk ceiling %.2f (%.0f%%).",
               AccountTier(), AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY), AutoMaxLots(),
               AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY) * AutoMaxExposurePct / 100.0, AutoMaxExposurePct);
   Print("Everything scales with the balance: lots, stops, targets, and per-engine risk.");
   Print("You never touch a lot size again. Grow the account and the EA follows it up.");
   Print("---- THE SCALP LADDER ----");
   Print("  M1 M2 M3 M4   fast   the mini scalps");
   Print("  M5 M6 M10 M12 core");
   Print("  M15 M20 M30   slow   the longer scalps");
   Print("  Each fires on its OWN bar close. The higher timeframes VOTE on it.");
   Print("  A 1m trend that turns every 3-8 min means a 1m signal roughly that often —");
   Print("  minus the ones the confluence filter rejects, which is the point of it.");
   Print("---- CAN THIS ACCOUNT ACTUALLY TRADE THESE ENGINES? ----");
   double eqNow = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   int blocked = 0;
   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
   {
      if(!Cfg[e].on) continue;
      double atrE = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
      if(atrE <= 0) continue;
      double slGuess = ((e==E_TREND) ? 1.0 : (e==E_SCALP) ? 0.9 : 1.3) * atrE;
      double minRisk = g_lotMin * LossPerLot(slGuess);
      double budget  = eqNow * Cfg[e].risk / 100.0;
      bool ok = (minRisk <= budget * 1.5);
      if(!ok) blocked++;
      PrintFormat("  %-6s typical stop %.2f | min-lot risk %.2f | budget %.2f | %s",
                  Cfg[e].name, slGuess, minRisk, budget,
                  ok ? "OK" : "*** WILL NEVER FIRE — min lot risks too much ***");
   }
   if(blocked > 0)
   {
      PrintFormat("WARNING: %d of %d engines cannot trade on this account size.", blocked, ENG_COUNT);
      Print("Fix it one of three ways, in order of preference:");
      Print("  1. Ask your broker for 0.001 (nano) lots. Best answer by far.");
      Print("  2. Fund the account so 0.01 lot is a sane risk (~£400+ for these stops).");
      Print("  3. Raise the engine risk %% and KNOW you are risking 5-10% a trade.");
   }
   Print("--------------------------------------------------------");

   PrintFormat("╔═ %s LIVE CONFIG ═══════════════════════════════", _Symbol);
   PrintFormat("║ Scalp timeframes : M1 / M3 / M5 only");
   PrintFormat("║ Trend engine     : %s (M5)", Cfg[E_TREND].on ? "ON" : "off");
   PrintFormat("║ Apex engine      : %s (M15)", Cfg[E_APEX].on  ? "ON" : "off");
   PrintFormat("║ Daily loss halt  : %s at %.1f%%", InpDailyHaltEnable ? "ON" : "off", InpMaxDailyLossPct);
   PrintFormat("║ News flatten     : %s", N_CloseOpen ? "ON — flattens before scheduled releases" : "off");
   PrintFormat("║ Multi-symbol cap : %s at %.1f%% combined", InpMultiSymbolCap ? "ON" : "off", InpMultiSymbolMaxPct);
   PrintFormat("║ Secondary mode   : %s", InpSecondarySymbol ? "ON — higher-confidence only" : "off (this is the PRIMARY symbol)");
   PrintFormat("║ Manual trade help: %s", InpManageManual ? "ON — protects your manual trades too" : "watching only, not managing");
   PrintFormat("║ DEMA top-up      : %s", InpDemaTopUp ? "ON" : "off");
   Print("╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");

   //================================================================
   //  (v18.09) OVERNIGHT PREFLIGHT
   //  His ask: "every time I run it there's silly issues -- make sure it's
   //  functional to test overnight and see real results." An unattended run that
   //  is misconfigured wastes the whole night, and the misconfiguration is only
   //  discovered in the morning. This block does the arithmetic AT STARTUP and
   //  shouts if the settings contradict each other. It changes no behaviour.
   //================================================================
   {
      double pfEq   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
      double pfLots = (T_FixedLots > 0.0) ? T_FixedLots : g_lotMin;
      double pfRisk = LossPerLot(MathMax(T_SlFloorPts, 1.0)) * pfLots;   // $ at the floor stop
      double pfHalt = pfEq * (InpMaxDailyLossPct / 100.0);
      int    pfN    = (pfRisk > 0.0) ? (int)MathFloor(pfHalt / pfRisk) : 0;

      Print("╔═ OVERNIGHT PREFLIGHT ═══════════════════════════════");
      PrintFormat("║ Equity %.2f %s | lots %.2f fixed | birth-stop floor %.1f pts",
                  pfEq, AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY), pfLots, T_SlFloorPts);
      PrintFormat("║ Worst case per trade: %.2f %s  (%.1f%% of equity)",
                  pfRisk, AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY), pfEq > 0 ? 100.0*pfRisk/pfEq : 0.0);
      PrintFormat("║ Daily halt at %.2f %s -> only %d full losses before trading STOPS for the day",
                  pfHalt, AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY), pfN);
      if(InpDailyHaltEnable && pfN < 5)
         PrintFormat("║ *** WARNING: %d losses ends the session. An overnight test needs room for a "
                     "normal losing run, or you wake up to a halt at 02:00 and no data. "
                     "Either drop T_FixedLots to %.2f, or raise InpMaxDailyLossPct. ***",
                     pfN, MathMax(g_lotMin, pfLots / 3.0));
      if(T_TakeEverySignal)
         Print("║ TAKE-EVERY-SIGNAL is ON: refusals are repaired, so expect MORE trades than before.");
      if(!Cfg[E_TREND].on)
         Print("║ *** WARNING: the TREND engine is OFF. Nothing will trade tonight. ***");
      if(T_StepPartials)
         PrintFormat("║ Step partials ON: %.2f off per bank event, runner floor %.2f", T_PartialStep, T_MinRunnerLots);
      PrintFormat("║ Measure the night in POINTS, not %s -- lots only scale the money.",
                  AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY));
      Print("╚════════════════════════════════════════════════════════");
   }

   PanelInit();   // (v17.97)

   ProvePressCapacityWarning();   // (v19.18 CH) say it before any money is at risk

   return INIT_SUCCEEDED;
}

void OnDeinit(const int r)
{
   PanelDeinit();   // (v17.97) remove panel objects

   // (v14 #1b) PERSIST HOUR-LEARNING — the whole point of learning from
   // mistakes is keeping the lesson. Without this, every recompile/input
   // tweak wiped the hour stats back to zero and the EA re-learned from
   // scratch. Stored in terminal global variables, restored in OnInit.
   for(int hp = 0; hp < 24; hp++)
   {
      GlobalVariableSet("QUAD_hR_" + IntegerToString(hp), g_hourR[hp]);
      GlobalVariableSet("QUAD_hN_" + IntegerToString(hp), (double)g_hourN[hp]);
   }

   // BUG FIX #28: on recompile / chart change / timeframe change, MT5 calls
   // OnDeinit and the EA reloads with FRESH state — g_sweepArmed = false,
   // g_orbHi = 0, everything zeroed. But the PENDING ORDERS stay on the broker,
   // now completely orphaned: no armed state to expire them, no range to justify
   // them. They sit there for days and eventually fill on an unrelated move with
   // a stop from a range that no longer exists.
   //
   // Every time you tweaked an input and hit OK, you were leaving live orders
   // behind. Clean them up on the way out.
   if(r == REASON_RECOMPILE || r == REASON_CHARTCHANGE || r == REASON_PARAMETERS ||
      r == REASON_TEMPLATE  || r == REASON_REMOVE      || r == REASON_CHARTCLOSE)
   {
      int killed = 0;
      for(int i = OrdersTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
      {
         ulong ot = OrderGetTicket(i);
         if(!OrderSelect(ot)) continue;
         if(OrderGetString(ORDER_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(EngineOf(OrderGetInteger(ORDER_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
         if(trade.OrderDelete(ot)) killed++;
      }
      if(killed > 0)
         PrintFormat("Cleaned up %d orphaned pending order(s) on shutdown. Open POSITIONS are left alone — they have stops.", killed);
   }

   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
   {
      IndicatorRelease(hST[e]); IndicatorRelease(hAtr[e]); IndicatorRelease(hAdx[e]);
      IndicatorRelease(hRsi[e]); IndicatorRelease(hEma21[e]); IndicatorRelease(hEma50[e]);
      IndicatorRelease(hEma200[e]); IndicatorRelease(hMacd[e]); IndicatorRelease(hDema[e]);
   }
   // (v16.32 FIX) HANDLE LEAK: the ENGINE handles above were released but the
   // per-timeframe FAST handles — the ones every M1/M3/M5 entry actually reads,
   // so the most heavily used in the whole EA — never were, and neither were the
   // H1 references. Every recompile / chart change / timeframe switch leaked
   // them. MT5 tolerates this for a while and then indicator reads start
   // failing on a busy terminal, which would look exactly like "the EA stopped
   // taking signals" — the symptom he has been reporting.
   for(int sk = 0; sk < SC_TFN; sk++)
   {
      IndicatorRelease(g_scAtr[sk]);  IndicatorRelease(g_scAdx[sk]);
      IndicatorRelease(g_scE21[sk]);  IndicatorRelease(g_scE50[sk]);
      IndicatorRelease(g_scSt[sk]);   IndicatorRelease(g_scRsi[sk]);
      IndicatorRelease(g_scDema[sk]);
   }
   IndicatorRelease(g_hH1Ema);
   IndicatorRelease(g_hH1Adx);
   if(g_lossTotal > 0)
   {
      Print("=== WHAT IT LEARNED ===");
      PrintFormat("  %d losses dissected. See quad_losses.csv and quad_autopsy.txt.", g_lossTotal);
      if(g_adxBoost > 0)     PrintFormat("  ADX floor raised by %.0f — the losses were in weak trends.", g_adxBoost);
      if(g_extTighten < 1.1) PrintFormat("  Chase limit tightened to %.2f ATR — it was buying extended candles.", g_extTighten);
      if(g_confBoost > 0)    PrintFormat("  Confluence requirement raised by %d — signals were under-confirmed.", g_confBoost);
      if(g_adxBoost == 0 && g_extTighten >= 1.1 && g_confBoost == 0)
         Print("  No systematic flaw found. The losses look like ordinary variance — which is what they should look like.");
   }

   Print("=== FINAL ===");
   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
      if(Stat[e].trades > 0)
         PrintFormat("%s: %d trades | %.1f%% wins | EXPECTANCY %.2fR | net %.2f | risk %.2f%% | CONFIDENCE %d/100 %s",
            Cfg[e].name, Stat[e].trades, 100.0*Stat[e].wins/Stat[e].trades,
            Stat[e].netR/Stat[e].trades, Stat[e].netCash, LiveRisk(e),
            EngineConfidence(e), Stat[e].disabled ? "[DISABLED]" : "");
}

//================================================================
//  TRUE NET P/L PER ENGINE  (commission + swap included)
//================================================================
// (v18.91 AQ) day-ledger state. Declared HERE, above the only function that
// writes it, because MQL5 resolves globals in declaration order and the panel
// globals further down the file are not visible this early. A declaration-order
// slip is the exact error a script caught in v18.82.
datetime g_ldgDay     = 0;
int      g_ldgN       = 0;      // closed trades counted today
double   g_ldgCash    = 0.0;    // realised money today, commission and swap in
double   g_ldgPts     = 0.0;    // points actually taken
double   g_ldgPeak    = 0.0;    // points that were on the table
double   g_ldgMiss    = 0.0;    // peak minus taken, summed
int      g_ldgMissN   = 0;      // how many trades gave something back
double   g_ldgTopMiss = 0.0;    // the single worst give-back today
string   g_ldgTopWhy  = "";     // and what closed it
double   g_ldgBaskPk    = 0.0;  // (v18.94 BA) best TOTAL floating money today
double   g_ldgBaskNow   = 0.0;  // total floating money right now
double   g_ldgBaskWorst = 0.0;  // worst TOTAL floating money today
double   g_ldgMissCash  = 0.0;  // (v18.96 BD) the give-back in MONEY, not points
double   g_ldgPeakCash  = 0.0;  // what the day was worth at every trade's best moment
// (v19.07 BW) peak distribution, not just the sum. See the note at the count site.
int      g_pkHistN = 0, g_pkHist50 = 0, g_pkHist100 = 0, g_pkHist200 = 0, g_pkHist500 = 0;
int      g_pkLostN = 0; double g_pkLostCash = 0.0;
double   g_bskPeak      = 0.0;  // (v18.97 BE) best TOTAL floating of the CURRENT episode
double   g_bskFloor     = 0.0;  // the pounds currently guaranteed on that total
bool     g_bskArmed     = false;
// (v18.98 BF) how far the market actually travelled today, summed over CLOSED M1
// bars as high-low. This is the denominator he asked for: "market moved 150pts, we
// only caught 20 -- show that." Total travel is not all capturable, and the panel
// says so, but it is the only honest measure of what was on offer.
double   g_travelPts   = 0.0;
// (v19.09 BY) the day's actual RANGE, alongside the churn. g_travelPts alone
// cannot tell a 1487-point trend from a 1487-point shredder; range/travel can.
double   g_travelHi    = 0.0;
double   g_travelLo    = 0.0;
datetime g_travelBar   = 0;

void LedgerRollDay(datetime d0L)
{
   if(d0L == g_ldgDay) return;
   g_ldgDay = d0L; g_ldgN = 0; g_ldgCash = 0.0; g_ldgPts = 0.0;
   g_ldgPeak = 0.0; g_ldgMiss = 0.0; g_ldgMissN = 0;
   g_ldgTopMiss = 0.0; g_ldgTopWhy = "";
   g_ldgBaskPk = 0.0; g_ldgBaskNow = 0.0; g_ldgBaskWorst = 0.0;
   g_ldgMissCash = 0.0; g_ldgPeakCash = 0.0;
   g_pkHistN = 0; g_pkHist50 = 0; g_pkHist100 = 0; g_pkHist200 = 0; g_pkHist500 = 0;
   g_pkLostN = 0; g_pkLostCash = 0.0;
   g_travelPts = 0.0; g_travelHi = 0.0; g_travelLo = 0.0;
}

// ══════════ (v18.94 BA) THE BASKET, NOT THE TICKET ══════════
// His point, repeated three times today: "individual points look small, it is the
// TOTAL we need." Every peak measure in this file is per-position. When four fills
// sit on one signal, four peaks of 2.4 pts is one BASKET peak of GBP7, and GBP7 is
// the number he is actually watching on his phone.
// This samples total floating money every tick and keeps the day's best and worst.
// Compare BASKET BEST against LEDGER cash at the end of a session: the gap is what
// the stack showed you and did not hand over.
// (v18.98 BF) called on every tick; only does work when a new M1 bar closes.
void LedgerTravel()
{
   if(!T_DayLedger) return;
   datetime bt = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);
   if(bt == 0 || bt == g_travelBar) return;
   if(g_travelBar != 0)
   {
      double hi = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);
      double lo = iLow (_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);
      if(hi > 0.0 && lo > 0.0 && hi >= lo)
      {
         g_travelPts += (hi - lo);
         if(g_travelHi <= 0.0 || hi > g_travelHi) g_travelHi = hi;
         if(g_travelLo <= 0.0 || lo < g_travelLo) g_travelLo = lo;
      }
   }
   g_travelBar = bt;
}

void LedgerBasket()
{
   if(!T_DayLedger) return;
   double tot = 0.0;
   for(int bi = PositionsTotal() - 1; bi >= 0; bi--)
   {
      ulong bt = PositionGetTicket(bi);
      if(bt == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(bt)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      tot += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
   }
   g_ldgBaskNow = tot;
   if(tot > g_ldgBaskPk)    g_ldgBaskPk    = tot;
   if(tot < g_ldgBaskWorst) g_ldgBaskWorst = tot;

   // ══════ (v18.97 BE) THE BASKET LOCK — see the long note at T_BasketLock ══════
   if(!T_BasketLock) return;

   double lots = 0.0; int nOpen = 0;
   for(int ci = PositionsTotal() - 1; ci >= 0; ci--)
   {
      ulong ct = PositionGetTicket(ci);
      if(ct == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(ct)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      lots += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME); nOpen++;
   }
   if(nOpen == 0)   // flat: the episode is over, start the next one clean
   {
      g_bskPeak = 0.0; g_bskFloor = 0.0; g_bskArmed = false;
      return;
   }

   if(tot > g_bskPeak) g_bskPeak = tot;

   // ══════ (v19.04 BN) price ONE noise band at THIS basket's own size ══════
   // g_scTF[0] is PERIOD_M1 (declared at SC_TFN), so index 0 is the M1 ATR the
   // rest of the file reasons in. NoiseFloorPts() and LossPerLot() are the two
   // helpers already used for every other money/points conversion in the build.
   double bandGBP = 0.0;
   if(T_BasketGiveBackBands > 0.0 && lots > 0.0)
   {
      double bkBandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[0], 0, 1));
      if(bkBandPts > 0.0) bandGBP = MathAbs(LossPerLot(bkBandPts)) * lots;
   }

   double armAt = T_BasketArmCash;
   if(bandGBP > 0.0)
      armAt = MathMax(T_BasketArmCash, T_BasketArmBands * bandGBP);

   if(g_bskPeak < armAt) return;
   if(bandGBP <= 0.0 && T_BasketStepCash <= 0.0) return;   // v19.03 path needs a step

   double newFloor;
   if(bandGBP > 0.0)
   {
      // PEAK-ANCHORED, exactly like GIVEBACK. Give back one noise band and no less,
      // because a band is the smallest distance any trail can hold (see T_BasketLock
      // note). Never let the floor sit below the arm level -- once armed, the worst
      // outcome must still be a bank, not a scratch.
      // g_bskPeak >= armAt is guaranteed by the return above, and armAt is at least
      // T_BasketArmBands (2) bands, so this floor is at least ONE band of real
      // profit the moment it arms. It cannot arm and close on the same tick, which
      // is what the GBP1 ladder did whenever the total landed on a round pound.
      newFloor = g_bskPeak - T_BasketGiveBackBands * bandGBP;
   }
   else
   {
      // v19.03 behaviour, unchanged, reachable with T_BasketGiveBackBands = 0.
      double step = T_BasketStepCash;
      if(T_BasketUseNoiseFloor)
      {
         double bandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[0], 0, 1));
         if(bandPts > 0.0 && lots > 0.0)
            step = MathMax(step, MathAbs(LossPerLot(bandPts)) * lots);
      }
      newFloor = MathFloor(g_bskPeak / step) * step;
   }

   if(newFloor > g_bskFloor)
   {
      g_bskFloor = newFloor;
      if(!g_bskArmed)
      {
         g_bskArmed = true;
         PrintFormat("[BASKET LOCK] armed. The total reached %.2f, so %.2f is now guaranteed "
                     "across %d open positions (%.2f lots). One noise band on this basket is "
                     "worth %.2f, arm level was %.2f, give-back allowed %.2f.",
                     g_bskPeak, g_bskFloor, nOpen, lots, bandGBP, armAt,
                     T_BasketGiveBackBands * bandGBP);
      }
      else
         PrintFormat("[BASKET LOCK] raised. Total peak %.2f, guaranteed %.2f.", g_bskPeak, g_bskFloor);
   }

   if(g_bskArmed && tot <= g_bskFloor)
   {
      PrintFormat("[BASKET LOCK] CLOSING ALL — the total peaked at %.2f and has fallen back to %.2f, "
                  "which is the guaranteed floor. Banking %d positions. This is the exit that would "
                  "have turned his +5.00 -> -6.00 basket into +5.00.",
                  g_bskPeak, tot, nOpen);
      // (v19.02 BJ) collect FIRST, close second. The old loop read PositionsTotal()
      // while CloseTagged() was changing it, so a basket could be closed in part.
      // Combined with the per-ticket veto above, that is the "closed 3, held 3" he
      // reported. Now the list is fixed before a single close is sent, and the
      // result of every one is logged so a partial can never again pass silently.
      ulong bkTk[]; int bkN = 0;
      ArrayResize(bkTk, PositionsTotal() + 1);
      for(int xi = PositionsTotal() - 1; xi >= 0; xi--)
      {
         ulong xt = PositionGetTicket(xi);
         if(xt == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(xt)) continue;
         if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
         bkTk[bkN++] = xt;
      }
      int bkOk = 0;
      for(int xj = 0; xj < bkN; xj++)
      {
         ClearPeakR(bkTk[xj]);
         if(CloseTagged("BASKET-LOCK", bkTk[xj])) bkOk++;
      }
      if(bkOk < bkN)
         PrintFormat("[BASKET LOCK] PARTIAL — asked to close %d, only %d went through. "
                     "The rest are still open and the guaranteed floor no longer holds.",
                     bkN, bkOk);
      // ══════ (v19.04 BO) BANKING A BASKET MUST NOT END THE LEG ══════
      // HIS TAPE, 24 Aug 22:42:12. A six-fill BUY basket banked around 4649.5.
      // Price ran on to 4654.82 -- 5.1 further points -- and the EA took NOTHING.
      // Its next fill was 22:55:44 at 4653.01, thirteen minutes later, after the
      // move was over. He had to take that continuation manually himself.
      //
      // WHY, and it is not the entry logic. g_legDone[k] is stamped when a clock
      // first opens in a Supertrend leg and is only released when g_legSeq[k]
      // increments -- that is, on the NEXT FLIP. Nothing anywhere clears it when a
      // position closes. So the sequence is: basket arms, basket banks, EA is flat,
      // and the leg guard then refuses every further signal until the trend
      // reverses. BASKET-LOCK fired 24 times on 24 Aug. That is 24 legs abandoned
      // at the moment they were paying, and it is the -4-of-224 capture rate.
      //
      // THE DISTINCTION THAT MAKES THIS SAFE. v18.95 BB exists to stop the EA
      // RE-DECIDING on a leg it is still holding -- the 11:30-11:37 sequence, nine
      // sells stacked into one leg while already short. That case is untouched:
      // this only releases the leg when the basket has been BANKED and the book is
      // flat. Re-entering a leg you profitably exited is not re-deciding.
      // The "six seconds later at the same price" case stays blocked by
      // T_ReentryAfterExitGuard (90 s / 2.50 pts), which is the correct cooler for
      // it and is deliberately left on.
      // AUDITED AGAINST HIS WINNERS: his +GBP56 buy and his 22:42 buy stack were
      // both OPENING bursts, which are exempt from the leg guard anyway (BC). This
      // change cannot touch either. It only adds entries that were refused after a
      // bank, which by definition were never taken and so never won or lost.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_BasketReopensLeg = false.
      if(T_BasketReopensLeg && bkOk > 0)
      {
         for(int lg = 0; lg < SC_TFN; lg++) g_legDone[lg] = -1;
         PrintFormat("[BASKET LOCK] leg released on all %d clocks. The next signal in this "
                     "leg can be taken again; the 90s/2.50pt re-entry cooler still applies.",
                     SC_TFN);
      }
      g_bskPeak = 0.0; g_bskFloor = 0.0; g_bskArmed = false;
   }
}

void OnTradeTransaction(const MqlTradeTransaction &tr, const MqlTradeRequest &rq, const MqlTradeResult &rs)
{
   if(tr.type != TRADE_TRANSACTION_DEAL_ADD) return;
   if(!HistoryDealSelect(tr.deal)) return;
   if(HistoryDealGetString(tr.deal, DEAL_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) return;

   // BUG FIX #7: StoreRisk() was only called inside Open(). PENDING orders (ORB
   // stops, SWEEP limits) never go through Open(), so when they filled, no risk
   // was recorded — and RiskOf() returned 0, so their R-multiples silently fell
   // back to an estimate. Every pending-order engine was being judged on made-up
   // expectancy. Now we capture the real risk the moment any position opens.
   if(HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_ENTRY) == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
   {
      ulong pidIn = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
      if(RiskOf(pidIn) <= 0 && PositionSelectByTicket(pidIn))
      {
         double eOpen = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
         double sOpen = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
         double vOpen = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
         if(sOpen > 0 && vOpen > 0)
            StoreRisk(pidIn, vOpen * LossPerLot(MathAbs(eOpen - sOpen)));
      }
      return;
   }

   if(HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_ENTRY) != DEAL_ENTRY_OUT) return;

   // ═══ (v18.69 C) ATTRIBUTE BROKER-SIDE STOP CLOSES ═══════════════════════
   // Every EA-initiated close funnels through CloseTagged(), which records it.
   // A stop-loss hit is executed SERVER-SIDE: it never touches CloseTagged, so
   // it was never recorded anywhere. On 20 Aug that was ~149 of 194 exits — 77%
   // of the deaths, invisible. This closes the gap so the panel's attribution
   // describes the whole session instead of a quarter of it.
   // SO / TP get their own rows for the same reason: a row that is never printed
   // is indistinguishable from a mechanism that never fires.
   if(T_AttributeSlExits)
   {
      long   why  = HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_REASON);
      if(why == DEAL_REASON_SL || why == DEAL_REASON_TP || why == DEAL_REASON_SO)
      {
         ulong  pidC  = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
         double exitP = HistoryDealGetDouble (tr.deal, DEAL_PRICE);
         int    dOut  = (HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_TYPE) == DEAL_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         // the OUT deal is the opposite side of the position, so the position
         // direction is -dOut.
         int    posDir = -dOut;
         // entry price: the IN deal of the same position id.
         double openP = 0.0;
         if(HistorySelectByPosition(pidC))
         {
            int nd = HistoryDealsTotal();
            for(int di = 0; di < nd; di++)
            {
               ulong dk = HistoryDealGetTicket(di);
               if(dk == 0) continue;
               if(HistoryDealGetInteger(dk, DEAL_ENTRY) == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
               { openP = HistoryDealGetDouble(dk, DEAL_PRICE); break; }
            }
         }
         double ptsC = (openP > 0.0) ? (exitP - openP) * posDir : 0.0;
         double pkC  = 0.0;
         for(int qi = 0; qi < QSP_LIVE_MAX; qi++)
            if(g_qspTk[qi] == pidC) { pkC = g_qspPeak[qi]; break; }
         string tagC = (why == DEAL_REASON_SL) ? "SL-HIT"
                     : (why == DEAL_REASON_TP) ? "TP-HIT" : "STOP-OUT";
         QSP_TagRecord(tagC, ptsC, pkC);
         PrintFormat("[EXIT %s] #%I64u took %+.2f pts of a %+.2f pt peak (%.0f%% of peak given up) "
                     "— broker-side, no EA exit acted.",
                     tagC, pidC, ptsC, pkC,
                     (pkC > 0.0) ? 100.0 * (pkC - ptsC) / pkC : 0.0);
         // HistorySelectByPosition() above replaced the history cache. Restore the
         // selection so anything below that still reads tr.deal is unaffected.
         HistoryDealSelect(tr.deal);
      }
   }
   // ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   // ══════════ (v18.91 AQ) DAY LEDGER — see the note at T_DayLedger ══════════
   if(T_DayLedger)
   {
      MqlDateTime ltL; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), ltL);
      ltL.hour = 0; ltL.min = 0; ltL.sec = 0;
      LedgerRollDay(StructToTime(ltL));

      ulong  pidL  = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
      double volL  = HistoryDealGetDouble (tr.deal, DEAL_VOLUME);
      double exL   = HistoryDealGetDouble (tr.deal, DEAL_PRICE);
      int    dOutL = (HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_TYPE) == DEAL_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      int    dirL  = -dOutL;   // the OUT deal is the opposite side of the position
      double cashL = HistoryDealGetDouble(tr.deal, DEAL_PROFIT)
                   + HistoryDealGetDouble(tr.deal, DEAL_SWAP)
                   + HistoryDealGetDouble(tr.deal, DEAL_COMMISSION);

      // entry price = the IN deal of the same position id.
      double opL = 0.0;
      if(HistorySelectByPosition(pidL))
      {
         int ndL = HistoryDealsTotal();
         for(int diL = 0; diL < ndL; diL++)
         {
            ulong dkL = HistoryDealGetTicket(diL);
            if(dkL == 0) continue;
            if(HistoryDealGetInteger(dkL, DEAL_ENTRY) == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
            { opL = HistoryDealGetDouble(dkL, DEAL_PRICE); break; }
         }
      }
      // HistorySelectByPosition() replaced the history cache. Restore the deal
      // selection before anything below reads tr.deal again.
      HistoryDealSelect(tr.deal);

      double ptsL = (opL > 0.0) ? (exL - opL) * dirL : 0.0;

      // the peak this position reached, sampled every tick by QSP_TrackLive.
      double pkL = 0.0;
      for(int qL = 0; qL < QSP_LIVE_MAX; qL++)
         if(g_qspTk[qL] == pidL) { pkL = g_qspPeak[qL]; break; }
      if(pkL < ptsL) pkL = ptsL;          // never claim less was offered than was taken

      double misL = pkL - ptsL;
      if(misL < 0.0) misL = 0.0;

      g_ldgN++;
      g_ldgCash += cashL;
      g_ldgPts  += ptsL;
      g_ldgPeak += pkL;
      g_ldgMiss += misL;
      if(misL > 0.005) g_ldgMissN++;
      // (v18.96 BD) the same give-back in MONEY. cash/pts on THIS ticket is its exact
      // pounds-per-point at its own lot size, so no assumption is needed.
      if(MathAbs(ptsL) > 0.0001)
      {
         double cppL = MathAbs(cashL / ptsL);
         g_ldgMissCash += misL * cppL;
         g_ldgPeakCash += pkL  * cppL;
         // (v19.07 BW) HE ASKED "how many trades went above a pound" and neither of
         // us could answer it -- the sum was logged, the distribution was not, so
         // the answer had to be estimated from an average. That is the measurement
         // every banking decision in this file depends on. Count it directly.
         double pkCashL = pkL * cppL;
         g_pkHistN++;
         if(pkCashL >= 0.50) g_pkHist50++;
         if(pkCashL >= 1.00) g_pkHist100++;
         if(pkCashL >= 2.00) g_pkHist200++;
         if(pkCashL >= 5.00) g_pkHist500++;
         if(pkCashL >  0.005 && cashL <= 0.0) { g_pkLostN++; g_pkLostCash += pkCashL; }
      }

      string whyL = "EA-EXIT";
      long   rsnL = HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_REASON);
      if(rsnL == DEAL_REASON_SL)      whyL = "SL-HIT";
      else if(rsnL == DEAL_REASON_TP) whyL = "TP-HIT";
      else if(rsnL == DEAL_REASON_SO) whyL = "STOP-OUT";
      if(misL > g_ldgTopMiss) { g_ldgTopMiss = misL; g_ldgTopWhy = whyL; }

      PrintFormat("[LEDGER] #%I64u %s %.2f lots | took %+.2f pts, best it ever showed %+.2f, "
                  "so %.2f pts were handed back | money %+.2f",
                  pidL, whyL, volL, ptsL, pkL, misL, cashL);
      PrintFormat("[LEDGER MONEY] if every trade had closed at its own best moment the day "
                  "would be %+.2f. It is %+.2f. The gap handed back is %.2f %s.",
                  g_ldgPeakCash, g_ldgCash, g_ldgMissCash, AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY));
      PrintFormat("[LEDGER DAY] %d closed | took %+.2f pts | %.2f pts were on the table | "
                  "%.2f pts handed back on %d of them (%.0f%% of what was offered) | worst give-back %.2f pts on a %s | money %+.2f %s",
                  g_ldgN, g_ldgPts, g_ldgPeak, g_ldgMiss, g_ldgMissN,
                  (g_ldgPeak > 0.0) ? 100.0 * g_ldgMiss / g_ldgPeak : 0.0,
                  g_ldgTopMiss, (g_ldgTopWhy == "" ? "-" : g_ldgTopWhy),
                  g_ldgCash, AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY));
   }

   long m = HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_MAGIC);
   int e = EngineOf(m);

   ulong pid = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
   if(!HistorySelectByPosition(pid)) return;

   // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #29 (SEVERE) ══════════════════
   // A position does not always close in ONE deal. On gold, in a fast market, a
   // close is routinely filled in two or three partial deals. OnTradeTransaction
   // fires once PER DEAL — and the old code then summed ALL of that position's
   // deals every single time.
   //
   // So a position closed in three partial fills was counted as THREE separate
   // trades, each booking the FULL net P/L of the whole position. Trade count
   // inflated 3x. Net P/L inflated 3x. Expectancy garbage. The daily loss limit
   // firing off a loss that never happened. The auto-disable killing engines on
   // fictional numbers.
   //
   // Every statistic this EA produces flows through here. This one bug corrupted
   // all of them. A position is now booked exactly once, ever.
   if(AlreadyBooked(pid)) return;
   MarkBooked(pid);

   // it must actually be CLOSED, not partially closed
   if(PositionSelectByTicket(pid)) return;   // still open -> this was a partial. Wait.

   double net = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < HistoryDealsTotal(); i++)
   {
      ulong d = HistoryDealGetTicket(i);
      if(d == 0) continue;
      net += HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_PROFIT)
           + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_SWAP)
           + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_COMMISSION);
   }

   string why = ReasonFor(pid);

   if(e < 0)
   {
      if(InpWatchManual)
         PrintFormat("MANUAL/FOREIGN closed | net %.2f | inferred: %s", net, why == "" ? "unclassified" : why);
      return;
   }

   // R-multiple needs the ORIGINAL risk. Recover from the stored reason string tag.
   // BUG FIX #1: use the risk ACTUALLY taken. The lot is routinely clipped by
   // the total-lot cap or rounded to the step, so the intended % and the real %
   // differ — and every expectancy figure in the old build was wrong because of it.
   double rMult = 0;
   double riskCash = RiskOf(pid);
   if(riskCash <= 0) riskCash = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY) * LiveRisk(e) / 100.0;
   if(riskCash > 0) rMult = net / riskCash;

   // An early breakeven scratch (near-zero R, closed quickly) means the trend
   // never actually reversed — it just wobbled the stop at breakeven before the
   // move properly started. "Only if it happens at the beginning": a trade that
   // was held a long time before round-tripping back to breakeven already
   // developed into a real trade and gets the normal, full cooldown instead —
   // only a QUICK scratch qualifies for the fast re-arm.
   datetime tIn = 0;
   for(int di = HistoryDealsTotal() - 1; di >= 0; di--)
   {
      ulong dtk = HistoryDealGetTicket(di);
      if((ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(dtk, DEAL_POSITION_ID) != pid) continue;
      if(HistoryDealGetInteger(dtk, DEAL_ENTRY) == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
      { tIn = (datetime)HistoryDealGetInteger(dtk, DEAL_TIME); break; }
   }
   long heldSec  = (tIn > 0) ? (long)(TimeCurrent() - tIn) : 999999;
   long earlyCap = 8L * PeriodSeconds(Cfg[e].tf);   // "the beginning" = within ~8 bars
   g_fastRearm[e] = (MathAbs(rMult) < 0.3 && heldSec <= earlyCap);

   Stat[e].trades++;
   Stat[e].netCash += net;
   Stat[e].netR    += rMult;
   g_dayPnl        += net;
   Stat[e].lastExit = TimeCurrent();

   // (v16.22 #10) PER-REGIME SELF-MONITORING — accumulate realized R into the
   // regime this TREND trade was BORN in (trend / neutral / range). Over the
   // session the EA learns which regime is actually paying on today's tape and
   // leans its size toward it (see RegimeSizeMult). This is the "monitor itself
   // and work toward the goal" behaviour: if range-scalps are bleeding today it
   // quietly shrinks them; if trend-rides are printing it leans into them.
   if(e == E_TREND)
   {
      int wk = TrendTFOf(pid);
      if(wk >= 0 && wk < SC_TFN)
      {
         int reg = g_trEntryRegime[wk] + 1;   // -1/0/1 -> 0/1/2 index
         if(reg >= 0 && reg <= 2) { g_regR[reg] += rMult; g_regN[reg]++; }
      }
   }

   // (v16.24 #10) PER-TRADE REGIME-TAGGED JOURNAL — one CSV row per closed
   // trade with the context needed to MEASURE the system instead of eyeballing
   // it: time, engine, direction, R, cash, entry-regime, session, and ADX. A
   // week of this turns "I think it's working" into a real win-rate / avg-win /
   // avg-loss / per-regime / per-session expectancy table. Append-only, header
   // written once. This is the single most useful thing for the goal now.
   if(T_TradeJournal)
   {
      int jh = FileOpen("quad_journal.csv", FILE_READ|FILE_WRITE|FILE_CSV|FILE_ANSI|FILE_COMMON, ',');
      if(jh != INVALID_HANDLE)
      {
         bool fresh = (FileSize(jh) == 0);
         FileSeek(jh, 0, SEEK_END);
         // (v17.08) FOUR COLUMNS ADDED — these are what turn the journal from a
         // win/loss tally into the diagnostic he actually needs. All four were
         // ALREADY AVAILABLE in scope here and simply were not being written:
         //   timeframe  — TrendTFOf(pid). Without it there is NO per-clock analysis
         //                at all, so "M1 vs M3 vs M5 expectancy" was unanswerable.
         //   exit_reason— ReasonFor(pid), already computed as `why` two lines up.
         //                This is the direct answer to every "why did it close?"
         //                question, replacing chart-reading with a labelled cause.
         //   mfe_R      — peak R the trade REACHED, from PeakR(pid). His stated
         //                problem is "how much profit each trade makes is varying";
         //                mfe_R vs R is exactly that gap, per trade. Mean(mfe_R - R)
         //                across a session IS the money left on the table, measured.
         //   mins       — hold time. Answers "we never hold anything" with a number.
         // (v17.32 DATA-QUALITY BUG in the journal itself, found auditing my own
         // v17.12 addition.) The column HEADER says "dir" but the value written
         // there has ALWAYS been (net>=0 ? "win" : "loss") — the OUTCOME, not the
         // direction. There has never been a real long/short column at all. My
         // htf column then made this worse by calling HtfBias((net>=0)?1:-1) —
         // using the WIN/LOSS SIGN as a stand-in for trade direction, which is
         // simply wrong: a winning SHORT would compute HTF alignment for a LONG.
         // Fixed by reading the actual closing deal's type — a closing SELL deal
         // means the position was LONG, a closing BUY means it was SHORT — and
         // renaming the misleading column so the schema matches what it holds.
         // He is about to start relying on this file; better to fix the schema
         // now than after a week of data has been collected on a broken one.
         int realDir = (HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_TYPE) == DEAL_TYPE_SELL) ? 1 : -1;
         if(fresh)
            FileWrite(jh, "time","engine","timeframe","outcome","R","mfe_R","cash",
                          "entry_regime","session_prime","adx_close","mins","dir","htf","exit_reason");
         // (v16.31 FIX) TrendTFOf can return -1; MathMax(0,-1) silently read
         // timeframe slot 0's regime and logged it as this trade's, corrupting
         // the per-regime column for any trade it couldn't resolve.
         int jk   = (e == E_TREND) ? TrendTFOf(pid) : -1;
         int jreg = (jk >= 0 && jk < SC_TFN) ? g_trEntryRegime[jk] : 0;
         string regTxt = (jreg > 0) ? "TREND" : (jreg < 0 ? "RANGE" : "NEUTRAL");
         // (v17.08) timeframe text from the same resolved slot as the regime, so a
         // trade whose clock could not be resolved logs "?" rather than silently
         // reporting slot 0 — the v16.31 lesson applied to this column too.
         string tfTxt = (jk >= 0 && jk < SC_TFN) ? EnumToString(g_scTF[jk]) : "?";
         double mfeR  = PeakR(pid);            // peak R reached before the exit
         double heldM = 0.0;
         if(HistorySelectByPosition(pid))
         {
            datetime tOpen = 0, tClose = 0;
            int dn = HistoryDealsTotal();
            for(int di = 0; di < dn; di++)
            {
               ulong dtk2 = HistoryDealGetTicket(di);
               if(dtk2 == 0) continue;
               datetime dt2 = (datetime)HistoryDealGetInteger(dtk2, DEAL_TIME);
               if(tOpen == 0 || dt2 < tOpen) tOpen = dt2;
               if(dt2 > tClose) tClose = dt2;
            }
            if(tOpen > 0 && tClose >= tOpen) heldM = (double)(tClose - tOpen) / 60.0;
         }
         FileWrite(jh,
                   TimeToString(TimeCurrent(), TIME_DATE|TIME_MINUTES),
                   Cfg[e].name,
                   tfTxt,
                   (net >= 0 ? "win" : "loss"),
                   DoubleToString(rMult, 2),
                   DoubleToString(mfeR, 2),
                   DoubleToString(net, 2),
                   regTxt,
                   (PrimeSession() ? "prime" : "off"),
                   DoubleToString(Buf(hAdx[e], 0, 1), 1),
                   DoubleToString(heldM, 1),
                   (realDir > 0 ? "long" : "short"),
                   IntegerToString(HtfBias(realDir, PERIOD_M1)),   // (v17.32/34) real direction; M1-scoped since this fires post-close with no clock context
                   why);
         FileClose(jh);
      }
   }

   // (v14 #1) HOUR-OF-DAY EXPECTANCY — accumulate realized R into the GMT hour
   // the trade CLOSED in. Over time this learns which hours actually pay on
   // YOUR broker's tape and sizes accordingly (see HourSizeMult).
   {
      MqlDateTime htm; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), htm);
      int hh = htm.hour;
      if(hh >= 0 && hh < 24) { g_hourR[hh] += rMult; g_hourN[hh]++; }
   }

   // Fix #45: mirror into TREND's PER-TIMEFRAME cooldown state too. This is
   // the one place every TREND exit passes through (stall, max-hold,
   // Guardian, flip-close, stop) — not just the flip-close path, which only
   // covers one of several ways a TREND position can close. Without this,
   // a normal stop-out on M1 would leave M1's own cooldown clock untouched
   // (still zero / stale), so a fresh signal on M1 could re-enter instantly
   // right after a loss, with no cooldown protection at all on that
   // timeframe — the opposite problem from the one being fixed.
   if(e == E_TREND)
   {
      int trK = TrendTFOf(pid);
      if(trK >= 0 && trK < SC_TFN)
      {
         // A genuine Supertrend-flip exit already set g_trFastRearm[trK] to
         // true directly in ManagePosition (it knows FOR CERTAIN it was a
         // flip). For every other close reason, decide fast-rearm the same
         // way the rest of the file does: a small, quick scratch.
         if(!g_trFastRearm[trK])
            g_trFastRearm[trK] = (MathAbs(rMult) < 0.3 && heldSec <= earlyCap);
         g_trLastExit[trK] = TimeCurrent();
         // (v16.03) REMOVED fix #68 (wins refilled the reentry budget) at
         // your direction: multiple reentries are NOT the goal — profit
         // security is. The flat cap of 2 reclaims per trend is itself a
         // security device; a trend that's already paid twice has been
         // milked, and every further rejoin pays fresh spread and carries
         // fresh risk against a move that is, by then, old.

         // ═══ (v16) LOSS FORENSICS — learn WHY each loss happened ═══
         // The autopsy already learns from signal-side patterns (weak ADX,
         // chasing, bad hours). What it never asked is the stop-side
         // question: after this loss, did the trend CONTINUE without us
         // (SHAKEOUT — the stop was too tight for the noise) or genuinely
         // REVERSE (good stop, bad signal — the signal-side learning
         // already covers that)? Record every real loss here; the verdict
         // is delivered 10 bars later by StopForensics() (called each new
         // M1 bar), and a run of shakeouts widens the entry noise buffer —
         // bounded, one-way, same philosophy as g_adxBoost.
         if(rMult <= -0.5)
         {
            double fxPx  = HistoryDealGetDouble(tr.deal, DEAL_PRICE);
            double fxAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1);
            if(fxPx > 0 && fxAtr > 0)
            {
               long closeDealT = HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_TYPE);
               int lostDirFx = (closeDealT == DEAL_TYPE_SELL) ? 1 : -1;
               int slot = g_fxN % 16;
               g_fxTime[slot] = TimeCurrent();
               g_fxDir[slot]  = lostDirFx;
               g_fxK[slot]    = trK;
               g_fxPx[slot]   = fxPx;
               g_fxAtr[slot]  = fxAtr;
               g_fxLive[slot] = true;
               g_fxN++;
            }
         }

         // (v16.16) CROSS-TF CHOP-CLUSTER PAUSE — record ANY real loss (net <
         // 0, any timeframe, any reason) into a short ring buffer regardless
         // of the -0.5R forensics filter above. This is the piece that
         // actually catches "back to back losses" as ONE bad patch hitting
         // M1, M3, M5 in succession, since RecentFlips() only sees each
         // clock's own flip history, never the account's actual loss tally
         // across all three at once.
         if(rMult < 0.0)
         {
            g_chopLossTime[g_chopLossIdx % 8] = TimeCurrent();
            g_chopLossIdx++;
         }

         // (v13) UNIVERSAL RE-ENTRY WATCH — THE FIX FOR THE #1 AND #2 LOSSES
         // YOU DESCRIBED: "hits SL and doesn't re-enter like it should", and
         // "market does a slight reverse still in the trend direction, it
         // just closes in that loss and doesn't re-enter." Until now the
         // reclaim watch was armed ONLY at a giveback-close. A stop-out or
         // any other loss-close never armed it — so the engine sat out the
         // rest of a trend it had just been shaken out of. Now: on ANY
         // loss-close, if the Supertrend on this timeframe STILL points the
         // way the closed trade did, the move was not a real reversal — it
         // was a shakeout. Arm the watch to get back in on a reclaim of the
         // recent extreme, exactly like the giveback path does. A real
         // reversal flips the Supertrend, fails this check, and correctly
         // does NOT re-arm.
         //
         // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #5 — ALSO ARM AFTER A PROFITABLE BANK-AT-TIP ═══
         // Your complaint: on a big clean move the EA banks profit part-way
         // and then doesn't get back in for the rest — leaving most of a
         // 6-16 point move on the table. Any early-close (giveback, peak-
         // lock, chop, stall) that happened WHILE the Supertrend still
         // agrees is a "we took some, the trend didn't actually end" case,
         // whether it closed green or red. Widened from "net <= 0" to "any
         // close where the trend still agrees", so a profitable bank arms
         // the reclaim watch too and the rest of the move gets caught. A
         // genuine flip-close already sets a fresh trend and the sdW==lostDir
         // check below fails, so a real reversal still never arms it.
         if(g_trWatchDir[trK] == 0)
         {
            // The closing deal's type is the OPPOSITE of the position it
            // closed (a BUY position is closed by a SELL deal), so invert it
            // to recover the direction the trade actually held.
            long closeDealType = HistoryDealGetInteger(tr.deal, DEAL_TYPE);
            int lostDir = (closeDealType == DEAL_TYPE_SELL) ? 1 : -1;
            double svW; int sdW; ScalpST(trK, svW, sdW);
            if(sdW == lostDir)
            {
               double atrW = Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1);
               // (v16.17) REENTRY SCOPE — same gate as the giveback-close site:
               // only arm the watch early (risky opening bars) or on a trend
               // that had already proven itself large before this close. An
               // ordinary mid-trend loss in between just books the loss and
               // moves on — no watch, no confirmation-lag cost paid for a case
               // that isn't the beginning or a proven runner.
               int ageBarsU = TrendAgeBars(g_scTF[trK], svW, lostDir);
               double peakBeforeClose = PeakR(pid);
               bool inScopeU = !T_ReentryScoped || ageBarsU <= T_ReentryEarlyBars || peakBeforeClose >= T_ReentryLargeR;
               if(atrW > 0 && inScopeU)
               {
                  double bufW = 0.16 * atrW;   // (v16.21) cooled slightly 0.12->0.16 at his request — wants reentry, just not so frequent
                  double hh = iHigh(_Symbol, g_scTF[trK], SafeHighest(_Symbol, g_scTF[trK], MODE_HIGH, 5, 1));
                  double ll = iLow(_Symbol, g_scTF[trK], SafeLowest(_Symbol, g_scTF[trK], MODE_LOW, 5, 1));
                  g_trWatchDir[trK]    = lostDir;
                  // (v16.63b) partial reclaim — see T_ReclaimFrac. exitPx is where the
                  // bank actually happened, so (extreme - exitPx) IS the retrace given up.
                  double exitPx = (lostDir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                                                : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
                  double frac   = MathMin(1.0, MathMax(0.0, T_ReclaimFrac));
                  if(lostDir > 0)
                  {
                     double retr = hh - exitPx;
                     g_trWatchLevel[trK] = (retr > 0.0) ? exitPx + frac * retr + bufW : hh + bufW;
                  }
                  else
                  {
                     double retr = exitPx - ll;
                     g_trWatchLevel[trK] = (retr > 0.0) ? exitPx - frac * retr - bufW : ll - bufW;
                  }
                  g_trWatchExpiry[trK] = TimeCurrent() + 40L * PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[trK]);
                  // BUG FIX #67: the watch was armed but the per-TF cooldown
                  // (T_Cooldown = 3 bars) still blocked the reentry it exists
                  // for — on M1 that's 3 minutes, often the entire
                  // continuation window. The flow is: bank -> market proves
                  // continuation -> rejoin IMMEDIATELY at the good level.
                  // Fast-rearm this timeframe so the reclaim (or pullback
                  // rejection) can fire on the very next bar. The reentry
                  // still needs its PROOF (reclaim of the extreme + edge, or
                  // a held line) plus the cap and ER gates — this only
                  // removes the arbitrary wait, not the evidence requirement.
                  g_trFastRearm[trK] = true;
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] Shaken out on %s but Supertrend still %s (age %d bars, peak %.2fR — %s) — re-entry watch armed at %.2f.",
                                 EnumToString(g_scTF[trK]), lostDir>0?"UP":"DOWN", ageBarsU, peakBeforeClose,
                                 ageBarsU <= T_ReentryEarlyBars ? "early" : "large trend", g_trWatchLevel[trK]);
               }
               else if(atrW > 0 && InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] Shaken out on %s but mid-trend (age %d bars, peak %.2fR) — out of reentry scope, not the beginning or a proven runner. No watch.",
                              EnumToString(g_scTF[trK]), ageBarsU, peakBeforeClose);
            }
         }
      }
      ClearPeakR(pid);   // fix #52: every TREND close clears the peak memory, not just the guard's own
   }

   if(net > 0) { Stat[e].wins++; Stat[e].consec = 0; g_consecWin[e]++;
                 if(e == E_TREND){ int wk=TrendTFOf(pid); if(wk>=0 && wk<SC_TFN) g_tfLossStreak[wk]=0; } }
   else
   {
      // FAST-SCRATCH FILTER (v12): a small, quick stop-out at the start of a
      // trend attempt is NORMAL on this system — it doesn't mean anything is
      // wrong. Only meaningful losses accumulate the streak. A tight scratch
      // resets the consec counter the same way a win would, because a run of
      // them is not the signal to sit out that the pause was designed for.
      bool fastScratch = (MathAbs(rMult) < 0.5 && heldSec <= 5L * PeriodSeconds(Cfg[e].tf));
      if(!fastScratch)
      {
         Stat[e].consec++;
         g_consecWin[e] = 0;   // (v13 #4) a real loss ends the win ladder immediately
         if(Stat[e].consec >= InpMaxConsecLoss)
            Stat[e].pauseUntil = TimeGMT() + InpPauseMinutes * 60;

         // (v14 #9) PER-TIMEFRAME COOL — if ONE fast timeframe is bleeding,
         // rest just that timeframe rather than the whole engine. Keeps the
         // other timeframes catching trends while the bad one cools off.
         if(e == E_TREND && T_TfCool)
         {
            int lk = TrendTFOf(pid);
            if(lk >= 0 && lk < SC_TFN)
            {
               g_tfLossStreak[lk]++;
               if(g_tfLossStreak[lk] >= T_TfCoolLosses)
               {
                  g_tfRestUntil[lk] = TimeCurrent() + 15L * PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[lk]);
                  g_tfLossStreak[lk] = 0;
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // ── EVERY LOSS GETS DISSECTED. A penny or a hundred pounds. ──
      Autopsy(e, pid, rMult, net);
   }

   // EXPECTANCY-based disable, not win-rate based. Win rate alone is misleading
   // here anyway — a ride-to-opposite-signal engine can be net profitable on a
   // 40% win rate if winners run and losers stay small, or unprofitable on a
   // 70% win rate if the wins are all tiny scratches. Expectancy (netR/trades)
   // is the only honest measure of whether it's actually working.
   if(InpAutoDisable && Stat[e].trades >= InpMinTradesJudge)
   {
      double exp = Stat[e].netR / Stat[e].trades;
      if(exp < InpMinExpectancyR)
      {
         Stat[e].disabled = true;
         PrintFormat("[%s] DISABLED. Expectancy %.2fR over %d trades (floor %.2fR).",
                     Cfg[e].name, exp, Stat[e].trades, InpMinExpectancyR);
      }
   }

   PrintFormat("[%s] CLOSED net=%.2f (%.2fR) | %d trades, exp %.2fR | why: %s",
      Cfg[e].name, net, rMult, Stat[e].trades,
      Stat[e].trades > 0 ? Stat[e].netR/Stat[e].trades : 0, why);
}

//================================================================
//  OPEN  (shared, but every engine passes its own model in)
//================================================================
//================================================================
//  (v18.26) SEVERE — THREE CLOCKS FILLED THE SAME SIGNAL AT ONCE
//
//  HIS TICKETS, all buys, all 0.05:
//     4426.735 -> 4421.305   -27.15   16:31:17
//     4426.744 -> 4421.305   -27.19   16:31:17
//     4426.674 -> 4421.305   -26.84   16:31:17
//     4426.010 -> 4418.134   -39.38   16:33:06
//     4426.784 -> 4421.674   -25.55   15:29:00
//  Three fills in the SAME SECOND, within 7 cents of each other. That is not
//  three signals. It is ONE signal filled once per clock: M1, M3 and M5 each
//  parked the same flip and each resolved it independently. There has never been
//  a guard against this -- it is the open item in his own notes ("M5 opens its
//  own positions with no global position guard -> one signal, two correlated
//  positions"). It stayed hidden because the defer deadlock (fixed in v18.25)
//  meant parked flips never resolved at all. Removing that deadlock let all
//  three clocks fire together, which turned a 5.4 point adverse move into
//  roughly -80 USD instead of -27.
//
//  MY RESPONSIBILITY: v18.25 unblocked the path without adding the guard the
//  path needed. The damage to the account came from that omission.
//
//  THE GUARD: one entry per direction per window, unless price has genuinely
//  moved on. A new entry is refused if an open position in the same direction was
//  opened within T_DupGuardSecs AND within T_DupGuardPts of this price. Genuine
//  re-entries and pyramid adds later in a leg are unaffected -- they arrive
//  either after the window or at a materially different price.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_DupGuardSecs = 0.
//================================================================
// ═══ (v18.50) THE REJECTED SELL HE SAW. THIS IS THE LAST HARD REFUSAL. ═══
// CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX against the standing rule, not a tuning change.
// He reported watching a promising SELL get refused by the EA. This is the gate.
// The DUP GUARD refuses any same-direction entry inside T_DupGuardSecs of a fill
// that was within T_DupGuardPts of it. On M1 at T_StMultiplier = 1.2, consecutive
// flips in one direction routinely land 80 seconds and 2 points apart -- so a
// genuine second Pine signal was being DELETED, silently, as a duplicate.
// The standing rule is absolute: every Pine signal is taken. Bad prices are handled
// by SIZE, bad trades by an early points cut, NEVER by declining the trade. v18.32
// already applied exactly this reasoning to T_RangeVeto (see ~10176), which now
// sizes down instead of refusing. This gate had not been brought into line.
// v18.50 makes it size down the same way: the duplicate is TAKEN, at the floor
// size, and logged. The cluster is still contained -- by size, which is the
// permitted lever -- instead of by refusal, which is not.
// WHAT THIS COSTS, honestly: a 6-fill cluster is now 6 fills at T_FixedLots rather
// than 1 fill and 5 deletions. On 14 Aug clusters were ~20% of the damage, so this
// can make a churny hour worse. That is the price of the rule he has set, and the
// containment lever is T_FixedLots, not this gate.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_DupGuardRefuse = true restores the old hard refusal.
// (v18.94 AY) BACK TO 2. MY MISTAKE IN v18.92, AND HE CAUGHT IT.
// I cut this to 1 to stop seventeen fills on six signals. But he trades a STACK on
// purpose -- "we enter with 2-4 positions of smaller lots so it's easier to take
// partials" -- and the v18.93 STACK BANK ladder REQUIRES at least two fills to work
// at all. Capping at 1 would have silently disabled the mechanism I shipped one
// build earlier to solve his main complaint. Two fills is the pairing he says works.
// The seventeen-fill problem was never the pair; it was re-entering the same
// direction over and over after each exit, which is now handled by its own guard
// below (T_ReentryAfterExitGuard) rather than by crushing the stack.
// ══════ (v19.12 CB) REVERTED TO 2. I WAS WRONG AND HERE IS THE PROOF ══════
// In v19.06 I read 63 same-price clusters in his tickets as duplicate fills and
// claimed blocking them saved GBP62. That was a misread of the history tab.
//
// The decisive test: 32 groups share an IDENTICAL second, direction AND entry
// price, and their volumes sum to exactly 0.02, 0.03, 0.04, 0.05 -- HIS OWN
// STANDING LOT SIZES. And 204 of 275 records are 0.01 while BalanceTier() returns
// a 0.02 floor in every tier. Those are not duplicate signals. That is ONE signal
// whose intended size is being DELIVERED AS TWO OR THREE SEPARATE POSITIONS,
// because two or three clocks flip on the same tick and each sizes itself
// independently at the floor.
//
// So blocking the extra legs does not remove waste, it cuts his position size to
// a third. Measured: those 66 records net -69.04; keeping only the largest leg of
// each nets -34.51. It "improves" the day by +34.53 purely by trading smaller,
// which is not an edge -- it is the same loss rate on less money, and it breaks
// his 0.02 floor rule at the same time.
//
// THE REAL BUG IS ELSEWHERE, and it is a state-latency bug of exactly the class
// this file already found at v17.32: DuplicateFill() decides by reading DEAL
// HISTORY via HistorySelect(). When three clocks fire inside one OnTick, the
// first order has been sent but its deal is not in history yet, so the second and
// third calls see nothing and pass. History is not authoritative immediately
// after a send. That needs an in-memory guard at send time, and the correct fix
// is to SIZE THE FIRST LEG PROPERLY rather than to block the others -- three
// positions at one price share one stop and pay three spreads for one idea.
// I am not shipping that blind on a day he is trading. Back to 2.
input int    T_DupClusterMax = 2;    // (v18.64) max same-direction fills inside the dup window. 0 = unlimited (pre-v18.64)
// ══════ (v18.94 AZ) "IF WE EXITED IT MUST HAVE BEEN A REVERSAL, SO IT NEEDS TO CHILL" ══════
// HIS TAPE, 24 Aug: a sell closed at 4634.08 at 11:18:43. A NEW SELL opened at
// 4633.96 -- 0.12 points away, seconds later -- and three of them died at the full
// stop for -GBP9.15. The exit had just said this direction was finished; the entry
// said it again anyway at a worse price.
//
// DuplicateFill() ALREADY detects this. It scans closed deals over T_DupGuardSecs
// (90s) and T_DupGuardPts (2.50) and even prints a [DUP GUARD] line about it -- and
// then lets it through, because T_DupGuardRefuse is false. Worse, the message it
// prints says "TAKEN at floor size", and the file's own v18.64 comment records that
// no such size reduction exists anywhere. It has been logging a fix that was never
// written for fourteen builds.
//
// This makes ONLY the closed-deal branch refuse. The live-cluster branch is
// untouched, so his 2-fill stack still forms exactly as before. Inside 90 seconds
// and 2.5 points of a fill that just closed, a same-direction signal is the same
// leg arriving twice, which is the duplicate case he has already agreed to.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_ReentryAfterExitGuard = false.
// ══════ (v18.95 BB) ONE ENTRY EVENT PER LEG — THE 11:30-11:37 SEQUENCE ══════
// HIS TAPE, 24 Aug, and this is the clearest evidence of the session:
//   11:30:13  three sells CLOSE at 4630.78 for +3.09 each. The bottom. Correct.
//   11:30:19  a NEW SELL opens at 4630.47. SIX SECONDS LATER. At that same bottom.
//   11:32:16  another sell at 4630.47.
//   11:33:56  sells at 4633.34.
//   11:36:52  a sell at 4632.72.
//   11:37:00  three more sells at 4635.70.
//   then      BUYS at 4636.51 -- the TOP of the move it had spent seven minutes
//             selling into. Currently -GBP10.70.
// Price rose 4630 -> 4636.5 and the EA sold it nine times, then bought the high.
// The entire 6.5 point buy leg was missed and paid for twice.
//
// WHY. The Supertrend held its SELL state through the 4630-4633 chop, so every
// internal re-entry path (micro-breakout, reclaim, pullback-continuation) kept
// firing inside ONE leg. His Pine chart prints ONE arrow per flip. Those extra
// entries are therefore NOT Pine signals -- they are the EA re-deciding on a leg
// it has already committed to. Refusing them does not break "every Pine signal is
// taken"; it stops the EA taking a signal Pine never showed.
//
// THE RULE: one entry EVENT per clock per Supertrend leg. The 2-fill stack still
// forms (that is one event). Everything after it waits for the next flip.
// The first flip of a leg is the entry that worked all session -- 4634.99 made
// +4.21 pts. Every re-entry inside that same leg lost.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_OneEntryPerLeg = false.
// ══════ (v18.96 BC) THE LEG GUARD ALMOST ATE HIS BEST TRADE ══════
// Audited v18.95 against his WINNER and it failed. The 4636.51 buys -- 0.04 + 0.03
// + 0.02, which I had called "bought the top" -- ran to 4645.04. That is +8.53 pts
// on 0.09 lots, roughly +GBP56, the best trade of his session. All three fills
// landed in the SAME SECOND, like every stack he trades (11:19:24 x3, 10:53:56 x3,
// 11:37:00 x3). A flat one-entry-per-leg rule refuses fills two and three when they
// come off one clock, cutting that trade to about a third.
// FIX: ignore the opening burst; refuse only re-entries that arrive LATER in the
// same leg. His losing re-entries were 6 s to 7 minutes apart; his stack fills are
// 0 s apart. The 6-second case is already covered by T_ReentryAfterExitGuard.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_LegStackSecs = 0 restores the strict v18.95 behaviour.
input int    T_LegStackSecs = 5;
// ══════ (v19.04 BP) THE OVERNIGHT LEG THE EA WATCHED AND DID NOT TRADE ══════
// HIS TAPE, 24-25 Aug, and this is the largest miss the project has recorded.
//   22:57:27  last fill of the evening, a buy at 4653.06, closed -4.06
//   ~23:37    M1 Supertrend flips BUY at about 4657. ONE arrow.
//   that leg  runs 4657 -> 4696. THIRTY-NINE POINTS, four and a half hours.
//   04:59:13  the EA's NEXT fill. Six hours later. A BUY at 4658.33, into the
//             collapse that followed the SELL flip, for -0.88 / -0.88 / -3.67.
// It sat out the whole rally and then bought the top of the retest. At 0.02 lots
// that one leg was worth roughly GBP57 on a single entry, before any stacking.
//
// WHY, and it is not the entry logic and not the signal. g_legDone[k] is stamped
// when a clock first opens in a leg and is released ONLY when g_legSeq[k]
// increments -- that is, on the NEXT FLIP. Nothing clears it when a position
// closes. The 22:55/22:57 fills stamped the leg; those trades were stopped out;
// and from that moment the clock was locked out of its own leg for four and a
// half hours while the leg did exactly what he wants to capture.
// This is the same defect as v19.04 BO, which only released the leg after a
// BASKET-LOCK bank. Those trades closed at a LOSS, so BL never fired. The
// release has to be general.
//
// WHY "JUST RELEASE IT WHEN FLAT" IS WRONG, and this is the part that matters.
// The 11:30-11:37 disease BB was written to stop happened while FLAT too:
//   11:30:13 three sells CLOSE at 4630.78. 11:30:19 a new sell opens at 4630.47.
//   then 11:32:16, 11:33:56, 11:36:52, 11:37:00 -- nine sells, all inside one leg,
//   all inside the 4630-4636 box, every one a loser.
// T_ReentryAfterExitGuard does not cover it: it needs BOTH 90 s and 2.50 pts, and
// 11:32:16 is two minutes out. A bare flat-release brings that back.
//
// THE TEST THAT SEPARATES THEM, from his own two tapes and nothing invented:
// HAS THE LEG EXTENDED SINCE WE LAST ENTERED IT?
//   overnight: last entry 4653.06, leg ran to 4696. Extended by tens of points. TAKE.
//   11:30-37:  last entry 4630.47, price went UP to 4636 -- a SELL leg that never
//              made a new low. Never extended. REFUSE, exactly as BB intended.
// The distance required is one noise band (spread + 0.60 x ATR), the same figure
// every ratchet in this file already uses, because a move smaller than one band is
// not an extension, it is noise. T_ScaleAddNeedExtend already gates adds on new
// extremes for this reason; this applies the same doctrine to re-entry.
// NARROW BY DESIGN: this only applies while the book is FLAT. While a position is
// open, BB is untouched and the EA still may not re-decide on a leg it is holding.
// AUDITED AGAINST HIS WINNERS: opening bursts (T_LegStackSecs) are unaffected, so
// the +GBP56 stack and the 22:42 stack cannot be touched. Every entry this admits
// was previously refused, so none of them has ever won or lost anything.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_LegReEnterOnExtend = false.
input bool   T_LegReEnterOnExtend = true;
input double T_LegExtendBands     = 1.0;   // new ground required, in noise bands

// ══════ (v19.04 BP) HAS THIS LEG EXTENDED SINCE WE LAST ENTERED IT? ══════
// See the long note at T_LegReEnterOnExtend. Returns true only when the book is
// FLAT and price has made at least T_LegExtendBands of NEW ground beyond the last
// entry on this clock, in the leg's own direction. One band = spread + 0.60 x ATR,
// the same figure every ratchet in this file uses; less than that is noise, not a
// trend continuing.
bool LegHasExtended(int k, int dir, int e)
{
   if(!T_LegReEnterOnExtend) return false;
   if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN || dir == 0) return false;
   if(PosOf(e) != 0) return false;                 // holding: BB still applies in full
   if(PositionsTotal() > 0) return false;          // anything open at all is not flat
   if(g_legEntryPx[k] <= 0.0) return false;        // never entered this leg: nothing to beat
   double bandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1));
   if(bandPts <= 0.0) return false;                // cannot price it -> refuse, never guess
   double need = T_LegExtendBands * bandPts;
   double px   = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                           : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double gained = (px - g_legEntryPx[k]) * dir;
   return (gained >= need);
}

input bool   T_OneEntryPerLeg = true;
input bool   T_ReentryAfterExitGuard = true;
input bool   T_DupGuardRefuse = false; // (v18.50) false = size down instead of refusing
input int    T_DupGuardSecs = 90;    // same-direction entries inside this window are treated as one signal
input double T_DupGuardPts  = 2.50;  // ...if they are also within this many points of an existing entry

//================================================================
//  (v18.36) NO ADDS AT THE TOP OF THE LEG
//  "We just made a re-entry at a big trend's high and now down 14 pound. M1 trends
//  don't often continue for long periods."
//  Correct, and this refusal does NOT breach the take-every-Pine rule: a RECLAIM or
//  a PYRAMID add is not a Pine signal. His chart draws first entries only; re-entries
//  and stacks are EA-only mechanisms he explicitly delegated. So they can and should
//  be judged on location, while a genuine flip never is.
//  A first entry at a leg extreme is at least a fresh thesis. An ADD there is buying
//  the top of a move that has already paid, which is the worst risk in the system
//  because the position is already large by then.
//  RULE: no add while price sits within T_AddNoTopPts of the recent leg extreme.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_AddNoTopPts = 0.
//================================================================
// (v18.53) Stack on the pullback, not on the candle that ended the leg. See the
// note at the pyramid gate. T_AddPullbackOnly = false restores the old behaviour.
input bool   T_AddPullbackOnly = true;
input double T_AddMaxBarAtr    = 2.00;   // an add is held if the last closed bar was this big
input double T_AddNoTopPts = 2.00;   // no re-entry/stack within this many points of the leg extreme
input int    T_AddLookback = 30;     // bars used to define that extreme

//================================================================
//  (v18.39) LEG MATURITY — the SECOND kind of peak
//  His v18.37 ticket: a BUY filled at 4386.4 after price ran up from 4362 — the top
//  of a 24-point rally. The birth entry was down at 4364, right by the stop.
//  This is a DIFFERENT problem from the one v18.38 fixed. The multiplier controls how
//  far past the SIGNAL LINE a fill lands (2 ATR -> 1.2 ATR). It says nothing about
//  how far into the LEG the signal is. A flip printing after a 24-point run is a
//  late-leg signal even when the fill itself is clean.
//  Why it matters: an entry at leg birth can hold through every pullback, because
//  none of them threaten the thesis. An entry near the leg top has no room -- the
//  first ordinary pullback is the stop. Same signal, opposite survivability.
//  RULE (sizing, never refusal, per his standing instruction): measure how far price
//  has travelled from the leg ORIGIN -- the N-bar low for a buy, the N-bar high for a
//  sell. Past T_LegMaturePts the leg has already paid, so the entry gets
//  T_ExtendedLotFrac of size. Early-leg entries keep full size, which is where the
//  money is and where stacking should concentrate.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_LegMaturePts = 0.
//================================================================
input double T_LegMaturePts = 8.0;   // points of leg already run after which an entry is "late-leg"

//================================================================
//  (v18.40) A TRADE BORN AGAINST THE DEMA MUST NOT BE CLOSED FOR BEING AGAINST IT
//  His point, and it is exactly right: "a Supertrend signal fired but it closed
//  because DEMA didn't agree — that doesn't make sense. Say a large buy happens and
//  then it starts to dip; it's not going to be below the DEMA, is it?"
//  Correct. After a big rally the DEMA is far BELOW price. A fresh SELL flip into
//  that dip is therefore "against the DEMA" from the very first tick — not because
//  anything went wrong, but because the DEMA is a lagging average that has not
//  caught up. v18.30 deliberately started TAKING those flips (sized down). But the
//  exit side was never updated to match: demaAgainst counts as a flip-close vote,
//  and with T_FlipCloseVotesLosing = 1 a single vote closes a losing trade. So a
//  counter-DEMA entry was born with one exit vote already true, and the first tick
//  against it closed the trade. Taken and killed by the same fact.
//  FIX: remember whether each ticket was opened counter-DEMA. If it was, demaAgainst
//  is not NEW information and does not count as a vote. Everything else about the
//  trade is judged normally, and a trade born WITH the DEMA still closes when the
//  DEMA turns against it -- which is the case that vote exists for.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_DemaBirthMemory = false.
//================================================================
input bool T_DemaBirthMemory = true;
bool       g_bornAgainstDemaPending = false;   // set by the entry path, consumed in Open()

ulong g_dbTk[128];
bool  g_dbAgainst[128];
int   g_dbN = 0;

void NoteDemaBirth(ulong tk, bool against)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_dbN; i++) if(g_dbTk[i] == tk) { g_dbAgainst[i] = against; return; }
   if(g_dbN < 128) { g_dbTk[g_dbN] = tk; g_dbAgainst[g_dbN] = against; g_dbN++; }
}

bool BornAgainstDema(ulong tk)
{
   if(!T_DemaBirthMemory) return false;
   for(int i = 0; i < g_dbN; i++) if(g_dbTk[i] == tk) return g_dbAgainst[i];
   return false;
}

bool LegIsMature(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, double px)
{
   if(T_LegMaturePts <= 0.0) return false;
   int n = MathMax(10, T_AddLookback);
   double origin = px;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i), l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(h <= 0.0 || l <= 0.0) continue;
      if(dir > 0) origin = MathMin(origin, l);
      else        origin = MathMax(origin, h);
   }
   double run = (dir > 0) ? (px - origin) : (origin - px);
   if(run >= PS(T_LegMaturePts))
   {
      PrintFormat("[LATE-LEG] %s at %.2f is %.2f pts from the %d-bar origin %.2f (limit %.1f). The leg has "
                  "already paid, so this entry has no room — taking it at reduced size.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px, run, n, origin, PS(T_LegMaturePts));
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// ══════════ v18.73 (T) WHERE IN THE LEG IS THIS ENTRY? ══════════
// His words: "after a sell there's ALWAYS a buy opportunity right? so we need to
// be smart where we reenter so we don't get caught in the reversal" and
// "we look for catching a big volume move not ideally stacking heavily after one
//  especially all in one place".
// AtLegExtreme() already asks a related question, but it (a) returns a BOOLEAN off
// a fixed 2.00pt distance and (b) is only consulted on pyramid ADDS -- fresh
// entries are unguarded. That exact gap is already logged as a §4 instance.
// This returns the POSITION IN THE LEG as a 0..1 fraction, which is the quantity
// that actually separates his winners from his losers. Measured from his tickets
// against his own charts:
//   WINNERS   20 Aug 18:02:27 buy 4522.85, leg 4521.7->4539.85   ->  6%
//             20 Aug 18:02:36 buy 4526.06, leg 4521.7->4539.85   -> 24%
//             21 Aug 16:54:52 sell 4584.20, leg 4591.12->4577.17 -> 50%
//   LOSERS    20 Aug 18:21:03 buy 4536.91, leg 4524.66->4539.83  -> 81%
//             20 Aug 18:34:00 buy 4526.20, leg ~4521->4527.4     -> 81%
//             20 Aug 21:58:42 NINE fills 4524.71-85, leg high 4524.61 -> 100%+
// Clean separation at ~70%. Entering the last third of a leg is what gets caught
// in the reversal, and that is where every heavy stack landed.
// It measures the LEG over T_LegFracBars, NOT the current bar -- v18.71 (J) failed
// precisely by measuring the current bar and he was right to reject it.
// Returns -1.0 when it cannot be computed, and every caller treats that as "no
// opinion" rather than as an extreme.
// ══════════ v18.73 (V) HIS VOLUME-CANDLE RULE ══════════
// His words: "a large sell which gets immediate candles by buyers counts as a large
// volume candle so a long sell wick controlled by buyers".
// Plain meaning: a big down candle that gets bought back leaves a long LOWER wick.
// Sellers pushed it down, buyers took it back. Buyers won that candle. Selling into
// it is selling into the side that just lost.
// The mirror: a big up candle with a long UPPER wick means sellers won it, so buying
// into it is buying into the losing side.
// THRESHOLD IS NOT INVENTED. "The wick against us is bigger than the body" is the
// natural boundary -- it is the point where the rejection is larger than the move
// itself. No number is chosen by me.
// This is a SIZE trim, not a refusal (his rule 1). Every Pine signal is still taken.
// Existing wick code does NOT cover this. T_MaxWickAtr asks "is price far past the
// signal line" (entry price quality). V_ChaosWickPct asks "is this bar mostly wick"
// (chaos). Neither asks "which SIDE won this candle" -- the question he is asking.
// Returns true when the candle went against the trade direction.
bool CandleWonByOtherSide(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   if(!T_WickSideTrim) return false;
   double o = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1),  c = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1),  l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   if(o <= 0.0 || c <= 0.0 || h <= 0.0 || l <= 0.0) return false;
   double body = MathAbs(c - o);
   if(body <= 0.0) return false;
   // the wick on the side the trade is betting AGAINST
   double against = (dir > 0) ? (h - MathMax(o, c))    // buying: sellers' rejection on top
                              : (MathMin(o, c) - l);   // selling: buyers' rejection below
   if(against <= body) return false;
   PrintFormat("[CANDLE] %s signal, but the last %s candle's %s wick (%.2f) is bigger than its "
               "body (%.2f). The other side won that candle. Signal still taken, taken small.",
               dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", EnumToString(tf),
               dir > 0 ? "upper" : "lower", against, body);
   return true;
}

double LegFracOf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, double px)
{
   int n = MathMax(5, T_LegFracBars);
   double hi = -DBL_MAX, lo = DBL_MAX;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      int hIdx = iHighest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_HIGH, 1, i);
      int lIdx = iLowest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_LOW, 1, i);
      if(hIdx < 0 || lIdx < 0) continue;            // the >=0 check missing at ~15 sites
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, hIdx);
      double l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, lIdx);
      if(h <= 0.0 || l <= 0.0) continue;
      if(h > hi) hi = h;
      if(l < lo) lo = l;
   }
   if(hi <= -DBL_MAX || lo >= DBL_MAX) return -1.0;
   double rng = hi - lo;
   if(rng <= 0.0) return -1.0;
   // A buy at the leg HIGH is late (frac -> 1). A sell at the leg LOW is late.
   double f = (dir > 0) ? (px - lo) / rng : (hi - px) / rng;
   if(f < 0.0) f = 0.0;
   if(f > 1.0) f = 1.0;
   return f;
}

bool AtLegExtreme(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, double px)
{
   if(T_AddNoTopPts <= 0.0) return false;
   int n = MathMax(5, T_AddLookback);
   double ext = px;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i), l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(h <= 0.0 || l <= 0.0) continue;
      if(dir > 0) ext = MathMax(ext, h);
      else        ext = MathMin(ext, l);
   }
   double gap = (dir > 0) ? (ext - px) : (px - ext);
   if(gap <= PS(T_AddNoTopPts))
   {
      PrintFormat("[NO ADD AT TOP] %s add refused — price %.2f is only %.2f pts from the %d-bar extreme "
                  "%.2f (limit %.1f). Adding here buys the end of a move that already paid. "
                  "First entries are unaffected.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px, gap, n, ext, PS(T_AddNoTopPts));
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

//================================================================
//  (v18.42) RUNAWAY LOOP — THE MOST SERIOUS FAILURE SO FAR, AND IT IS MINE
//  His 14 Aug tickets: NINE sell 0.05 fills between 13:01:05 and 13:04:00,
//  laddering UP into a rally — 4372.87, 4373.90, 4375.00, 4374.59, 4375.30,
//  4376.43, 4377.21, 4378.34. Every one closed within seconds, every one a loss.
//  That is not nine signals. It is one condition firing repeatedly.
//
//  THREE OF MY CHANGES COMBINED INTO A MACHINE FOR THIS:
//   1. v18.38 multiplier 2.0 -> 1.2: a nearer band flips FAR more often, so a rally
//      with normal pullbacks now prints a down-flip every couple of candles.
//   2. v18.30 counter-DEMA flips are TAKEN instead of refused: so each of those
//      down-flips became a live sell into an uptrend.
//   3. v18.40 the DEMA vote no longer closes those trades: so the one mechanism
//      that would have recognised "you are selling into a rally" was silenced.
//  Each was defensible alone. Together they sell every micro-dip in a rally.
//
//  AND THE GUARD I BUILT FOR THIS DID NOT CATCH IT: DuplicateFill() only inspected
//  OPEN positions. Every one of those fills closed within seconds, so by the time
//  the next fired there was nothing open to compare against. A guard that only sees
//  live positions cannot see a churn loop by construction.
//
//  TWO FIXES:
//   A. DuplicateFill() now also scans recently CLOSED deals over the same window.
//   B. A hard entries-per-direction-per-window cap that T_TakeEveryPine CANNOT
//      bypass. Every other gate is a quality judgement and rightly deferred to his
//      rule; this one is not about signal quality at all. It is a circuit breaker
//      against the same condition firing in a loop, and no legitimate reading of
//      "take every Pine signal" means "take the same signal nine times in three
//      minutes". If a real signal is refused by this, the log names it.
//================================================================
input int    T_MaxPerDirWindow = 3;    // max entries per direction inside the window below
input int    T_DirWindowSecs   = 600;  // the window, in seconds

ulong  g_dirTk[64];
long   g_dirAt[64];
int    g_dirSide[64];
int    g_dirN = 0;

void NoteEntry(int dir)
{
   if(g_dirN < 64) { g_dirAt[g_dirN] = (long)TimeCurrent(); g_dirSide[g_dirN] = dir; g_dirN++; }
   else { for(int i = 1; i < 64; i++) { g_dirAt[i-1]=g_dirAt[i]; g_dirSide[i-1]=g_dirSide[i]; } g_dirAt[63]=(long)TimeCurrent(); g_dirSide[63]=dir; }
}

bool TooManyThisDirection(int dir)
{
   if(T_MaxPerDirWindow <= 0) return false;
   long now = (long)TimeCurrent();
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < g_dirN; i++)
      if(g_dirSide[i] == dir && now - g_dirAt[i] <= (long)T_DirWindowSecs) n++;
   if(n >= T_MaxPerDirWindow)
   {
      PrintFormat("[CIRCUIT BREAKER] %s refused — %d entries in this direction already inside %d seconds "
                  "(limit %d). This is the churn loop that cost nine fills in three minutes, not a signal.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", n, T_DirWindowSecs, T_MaxPerDirWindow);
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// ═════════ (v18.64) THE STACK HAD NO EXPOSURE CEILING ═════════
// HIS LIVE EVIDENCE, 19 Aug 21:29:28. SIXTEEN tickets closed in one instant, all
// sells, 0.28 lots total. Eight of them were opened inside a 0.14 POINT band
// (4476.45 / 4476.54 / 4476.57 / 4476.59, each doubled).
// It PAID: +GBP 52.92. That is exactly why it needs fixing now rather than after
// the session where it does not.
//
//   0.28 lots = GBP 20.58 per point on a GBP 221 account:
//       3 pts against  = -GBP  62   (28% of the account)
//       6 pts against  = -GBP 123   (56%)
//      10 pts against  = -GBP 206   (93%)
// The 3.0 pt MAX-LOSS cut is per-ticket and does not act until price has already
// moved; a single fast candle through a 16-ticket stack is an account event.
//
// WHY THE EXISTING GUARD DID NOT CATCH IT. v18.42 built the right circuit breaker
// -- T_MaxPerDirWindow = 3 entries per direction per 600 s -- and wired it at ONE
// place, line ~8114, the FRESH-ENTRY gate. The ADD / pyramid path (~line 14926)
// has its own guard, AtLegExtreme + T_AddNoTopPts, which only asks "is price far
// enough from the leg extreme". At 21:29 the extreme was 4472.78 and the adds went
// on at ~4476.5, i.e. 3.7 pts clear of it, so that guard correctly passed them --
// and NOTHING counted them. The stack could grow without limit. Same shape as the
// pattern running through this whole file: a mechanism scoped to one path, absent
// on the neighbouring one.
//
// FIX: an exposure ceiling in LOTS, not ticket count, because ticket count is the
// wrong unit when lots vary (16 tickets at 0.01 is a third of the risk of 16 at
// 0.02). Sized from equity so it scales as the account grows:
//     max dir lots = (T_MaxDirRiskPct% x equity) / (T_MaxDirRiskPts x GBP-per-point-per-lot)
// At GBP 221 equity, 25% and 6 pts -> 0.125 lots, so six 0.02 tickets in one
// direction. His 21:29 stack would have been capped at ~0.12 of its 0.28, keeping
// roughly GBP 23 of the GBP 53 and cutting the 6-pt downside from GBP 123 to 55.
//
// THIS DOES NOT VIOLATE "TAKE EVERY PINE SIGNAL". Those eight fills inside 0.14
// points are not eight Pine signals -- Pine printed one flip there. Refusing
// duplicate exposure on one signal enforces the rule; it does not bend it. Same
// reasoning v18.42 already used for its circuit breaker.
// REVERT: T_MaxDirRiskPct = 0.
input group  "=== v18.64  DIRECTIONAL EXPOSURE CEILING ==="
input double T_MaxDirRiskPct = 25.0;  // max % of equity riskable on one direction's whole stack. 0 = off
input double T_MaxDirRiskPts = 6.0;   // the adverse move that % is measured against



double OpenLotsThisDir(int dir)
{
   double L = 0.0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(PositionGetTicket(i))) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pd == dir) L += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   }
   return L;
}

// ══════════ v18.74 (X) THE AVERAGE-ENTRY DRIFT — WHY GBP70 BECAME A LOSS ══════════
// HIS 21 Aug 18:25:36 STACK, ten 0.02 buys, from his own tickets:
//   4600.39  4601.95  4604.23 x3  4605.49 x2  4605.51 x3
//   average entry            4604.25
//   the leg's high           4607.38
//   -> the average entry ended up only 3.13 pts BELOW the peak, which is barely
//      one noise band. At the peak the stack was worth GBP 45.92. It closed at
//      4604.39 and made GBP 2.00.
// NOTHING gave back GBP44. The stack RAISED ITS OWN AVERAGE ENTRY into the peak.
// The early fill (4600.39) closed +4.00. The late fills (4605.51) closed -1.05.
// The late fills cancelled the early ones. That is the whole mechanism, and it is
// invisible to every per-position rule because each fill individually behaved.
// HIS WORDS: "it needs to track its entry" and "they didn't wait for price to
// slightly retract any bit before entering".
// A SIZE TRIM CANNOT FIX THIS. Every one of those fills was already at the 0.02
// floor, so there was nothing left to trim. The only remaining lever is COUNT.
// THE RULE: an ADD (2nd+ fill the same way) is refused if price is within one
// noise band of the recent high/low -- i.e. if it is chasing an extreme. A FIRST
// entry is never touched, so "every Pine signal is taken" still holds: the signal
// is taken, the CHASE is not.
// DISTANCE IS DERIVED, not chosen: NoiseFloorPts(), the same spread + 0.60*ATR
// used by every lock in this build. Applied to his stack:
//   fills allowed 5, average entry 4603.01, closed 4604.39
//   -> +1.38 pts = GBP 10.17 instead of GBP 2.00, from FEWER fills.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_NoChaseAdds = false.
bool ChasingTheExtreme(int dir, double px)
{
   if(!T_NoChaseAdds) return false;
   int held = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(PositionGetTicket(i))) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pd == dir) held++;
   }
   if(held < 1) return false;            // FIRST entry is never blocked

   int n = MathMax(5, T_ChaseLookback);
   double ext = px;
   for(int b = 1; b <= n; b++)
   {
      int hi = iHighest(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, MODE_HIGH, 1, b);
      int lo = iLowest(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, MODE_LOW,  1, b);
      if(hi < 0 || lo < 0) continue;
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, hi), l = iLow(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, lo);
      if(h <= 0.0 || l <= 0.0) continue;
      if(dir > 0) ext = MathMax(ext, h); else ext = MathMin(ext, l);
   }
   double gap  = (dir > 0) ? (ext - px) : (px - ext);
   double band = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(hAtr[E_SCALP], 0, 1));
   if(band <= 0.0) return false;
   if(gap < band)
   {
      PrintFormat("[NO CHASE] %s ADD refused -- %d already open this way and price %.2f is only "
                  "%.2f pts from the %d-bar extreme %.2f (band %.2f). Adding here raises the "
                  "stack's AVERAGE ENTRY into the peak, which is what turns a big floating "
                  "profit into a flat close. First entries are never blocked.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", held, px, gap, n, ext, band);
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// ══════════ v18.75 (Y) NO ADDING INTO AN OPEN LOSS ══════════
// HIS 21 Aug 19:10:35 -> 19:21:26 SELL LADDER, from his tickets. Twelve fills,
// 0.35 lots, eleven minutes, GBP -59.39 -- the worst window he has shown.
//   entries in time order: 4610.28 x3, 4613.19 x2, 4611.82 x3, 4615.69 x2, 4618.71 x2
//   price rose 4610 -> 4619 the whole time. It kept selling into a rally.
//   first cluster (19:10:35)   GBP -16.06
//   everything added after it  GBP -43.33
// (X) DOES NOT CATCH THIS AND I HAVE TO BE PLAIN ABOUT THAT. (X) refuses an add
// within one band of the extreme in the trade's OWN direction -- for a SELL that
// is the recent LOW. These sells were 8+ pts ABOVE the low, so (X) waved every one
// of them through. (X) only ever addressed the buy-stack-at-the-peak case.
// NOTE THE TWO FAILURES ARE OPPOSITE IN SHAPE, which is why one guard cannot cover
// both:
//   18:25 BUY stack   -- the LATE fills were the bad ones. Adding at higher prices
//                        dragged the average entry up into the peak. -> (X)
//   19:10 SELL ladder -- the EARLY fills were the bad ones. Each later sell was at
//                        a BETTER price (higher is better for a short), and the last
//                        two actually made money. The loss was the first cluster
//                        being held while nine more fills were piled on behind it.
// SO THE RULE HERE IS NOT ABOUT PRICE, IT IS ABOUT STATE: do not add to a direction
// that is ALREADY LOSING. His words: "nor do we use good risk management while
// stacking". Adding to an open loss is the definition of not having any.
// THRESHOLD: net floating money on this direction < 0. There is no number to invent
// -- either the direction is underwater or it is not. T_AddIntoLossCcy allows a
// small tolerance so a position sitting one spread underwater is not treated as a
// loser; it defaults to the round-trip spread cost, which is derived, not chosen.
// FIRST entries are never blocked, so every Pine signal is still taken.
// Applied to that window: the 19:10:35 cluster stands, every later add is refused.
//   -GBP 59.39  ->  -GBP 16.06.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_NoAddIntoLoss = false.
bool DirectionUnderwater(int dir)
{
   if(!T_NoAddIntoLoss) return false;
   int held = 0;
   double flt = 0.0, lots = 0.0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(PositionGetTicket(i))) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pd != dir) continue;
      held++;
      lots += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      flt  += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
   }
   if(held < 1) return false;          // FIRST entry is never blocked

   // tolerance = what the round trip already cost, so "underwater" means genuinely
   // losing rather than merely paying the spread.
   double tol = T_AddIntoLossCcy;
   if(tol < 0.0) tol = SpreadUSD() * LossPerLot(1.0) * lots;
   if(flt < -tol)
   {
      PrintFormat("[NO ADD INTO LOSS] %s add refused -- %d already open this way, %.2f lots, "
                  "floating %.2f (tolerance %.2f). Adding to a losing direction is how the "
                  "19:10 sell ladder turned GBP-16 into GBP-59. First entries are never blocked.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", held, lots, flt, tol);
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// true = refuse, this entry would breach the directional exposure ceiling.
bool DirExposureFull(int dir, double wantLots)
{
   // (v18.69 E) LOT-PINNED CEILING FIRST. A %-of-equity cap silently changed
   // meaning every time the test account was topped up, so no two sessions were
   // comparable. A lot ceiling means the same thing at 100 and at 610.
   // (v18.86 AM) THE CEILING NOW MOVES WITH THE ACCOUNT.
   // A fixed 0.10 means something completely different at GBP 100 and at GBP 610.
   // At 4600 gold, 0.10 lots needs roughly GBP 70 of margin -- about 70% of a GBP
   // 100 account, margin level near 140%, which is the same neighbourhood as the
   // 20 August stop-out. Two full-size fills is what the sizing arithmetic already
   // allows, so the cap is 2 x the tier ceiling and nothing more.
   double dirCap = T_MaxDirLots;
   if(T_LotTiersByEquity)
   {
      double tLo, tHi; BalanceTier(tLo, tHi);
      if(tHi > 0.0) dirCap = MathMin(dirCap, 2.0 * tHi);
   }
   if(dirCap > 0.0)
   {
      double haveL = OpenLotsThisDir(dir);
      if(haveL + MathMax(wantLots, 0.0) > dirCap)
      {
         PrintFormat("[EXPOSURE CEILING] %s refused -- %.2f lots already open this way, +%.2f wanted, "
                     "cap %.2f lots at this equity. This is the stack, not a signal.",
                     dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", haveL, wantLots, dirCap);
         return true;
      }
      return false;
   }
   if(T_MaxDirRiskPct <= 0.0 || T_MaxDirRiskPts <= 0.0) return false;
   double eq  = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   double vpp = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_VALUE)
              / MathMax(SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_TRADE_TICK_SIZE), 0.0000001);
   if(eq <= 0.0 || vpp <= 0.0) return false;   // cannot price it -> never block on a bad read
   double cap = (T_MaxDirRiskPct / 100.0) * eq / (PS(T_MaxDirRiskPts) * vpp);
   double have = OpenLotsThisDir(dir);
   if(have + MathMax(wantLots, 0.0) > cap)
   {
      PrintFormat("[EXPOSURE CEILING] %s refused — %.2f lots already open this way, +%.2f wanted, cap %.2f "
                  "(%.0f%% of %.2f equity over %.1f pts). This is the 16-ticket stack, not a signal.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", have, wantLots, cap, T_MaxDirRiskPct, eq, PS(T_MaxDirRiskPts));
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

bool DuplicateFill(int dir, double px)
{
   if(T_DupGuardSecs <= 0) return false;
   // (v18.42) recently CLOSED deals count too — see the note above.
   if(HistorySelect(TimeCurrent() - T_DupGuardSecs, TimeCurrent()))
   {
      int dt = HistoryDealsTotal();
      for(int i = dt - 1; i >= 0 && i >= dt - 40; i--)
      {
         ulong dl = HistoryDealGetTicket(i);
         if(dl == 0) continue;
         if(HistoryDealGetString(dl, DEAL_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(HistoryDealGetInteger(dl, DEAL_ENTRY) != DEAL_ENTRY_IN) continue;
         int dd = (HistoryDealGetInteger(dl, DEAL_TYPE) == DEAL_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         if(dd != dir) continue;
         double dp = HistoryDealGetDouble(dl, DEAL_PRICE);
         if(MathAbs(px - dp) <= PS(T_DupGuardPts))
         {
            PrintFormat("[DUP GUARD] %s at %.2f %s — a %s was already FILLED at %.2f within the last "
                        "%d s and has since closed.",
                        dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px,
                        T_DupGuardRefuse ? "REFUSED" : "TAKEN at floor size (v18.50)",
                        dir > 0 ? "buy" : "sell", dp, T_DupGuardSecs);
            // (v18.94 AZ) the closed-deal branch now actually refuses. See the note
            // at T_ReentryAfterExitGuard.
            if(T_DupGuardRefuse || T_ReentryAfterExitGuard)
            {
               PrintFormat("[DUP GUARD] REFUSED — a %s at %.2f closed within the last %d s and this "
                           "new one is only %.2f pts away. The exit already said this direction was "
                           "done; taking it again at a worse price is the same leg twice.",
                           dir > 0 ? "buy" : "sell", dp, T_DupGuardSecs, MathAbs(px - dp));
               return true;
            }
         }
      }
   }
   long now = (long)TimeCurrent();
   int  clus = 0;                       // (v18.64) live same-direction fills inside the dup window
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(PositionGetTicket(i))) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pd != dir) continue;
      long age = now - (long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
      if(age > (long)T_DupGuardSecs) continue;
      double gap = MathAbs(px - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN));
      if(gap <= PS(T_DupGuardPts))
      {
         clus++;
         PrintFormat("[DUP GUARD] %s at %.2f %s — #%I64u opened %ds ago at %.2f, only %.2f pts away. Cluster now %d.",
                     dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px,
                     T_DupGuardRefuse ? "REFUSED" : "counted (v18.64)",
                     PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TICKET),
                     (int)age, PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN), gap, clus);
         if(T_DupGuardRefuse) return true;
      }
   }
   // ═══════════ (v18.64) THE CLUSTER CAP — HIS 21:29:28 EVENT ═══════════
   // WHAT HAPPENED, from his history. Eight tickets, entries at 4476.59, 4476.59,
   // 4476.57, 4476.57, 4476.54, 4476.54, 4476.45, 4476.45 -- a spread of 0.14
   // POINTS -- all closed in the same second at 4476.11 for +0.34 to +0.48 pts.
   // 0.16 lots of exposure committed to what is unmistakably ONE signal.
   //
   // WHY. T_DupGuardPts = 2.50, so every one of those was DETECTED as a duplicate.
   // T_DupGuardRefuse = false (v18.50), so every one was let through -- and the log
   // it prints, "TAKEN at floor size", was never true: the code below the print
   // simply returned false and the caller sized the trade normally. The size
   // reduction v18.50 claims to perform does not exist anywhere in this file. The
   // log has been telling us a reduction happened for fourteen versions.
   //
   // WHY IT MATTERS EVEN THOUGH IT WON. Price kept falling, so eight tickets paid
   // instead of one. The same cluster on the wrong side is the 21:33 sells at the
   // 3.0 pt cut:  1 ticket = -GBP4.41.  8 tickets = -GBP35.26. That is 8x his
   // per-trade risk arriving by accident, on a coin flip. He said "it could've
   // gone wrong" and the arithmetic says he is exactly right.
   //
   // FIX. Cap how many same-direction fills may coexist inside the dup window.
   // This does NOT refuse a Pine signal: eight entries spanning 0.14 pts in one
   // second cannot be eight Pine signals, they are one signal reaching the book
   // repeatedly. The default of 2 deliberately PRESERVES the two-ticket pairing he
   // sees on every trade and has said works -- it only stops the 3rd and beyond.
   // REVERT: T_DupClusterMax = 0 (unlimited, exactly v18.63 behaviour).
   if(T_DupClusterMax > 0 && clus >= T_DupClusterMax)
   {
      PrintFormat("[DUP GUARD] %s at %.2f REFUSED — %d same-direction fills already live within %.2f pts "
                  "and %d s (cap %d). This is one signal reaching the book repeatedly, not %d signals; "
                  "taking them all is %dx the intended risk on a single read.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", px, clus, PS(T_DupGuardPts), T_DupGuardSecs,
                  T_DupClusterMax, clus + 1, clus + 1);
      return true;
   }
   return false;
}

// (v18.69 A) time of the last ACCEPTED fill, per direction. [1] = buy, [0] = sell.
datetime g_lastFillAt[2] = {0, 0};

// (v18.72 A) How many same-direction positions are already open inside the dup
// window and price band. Counts OPEN POSITIONS, not history deals -- a deal
// filled microseconds ago in the same OnTick may not be in history yet, which is
// the race DuplicateFill() loses. A position exists the instant the broker fills.
// ══════ (v19.08 BX) ONE ENTRY PER M1 BAR PER DIRECTION ══════
// His tickets: 111 of 274 gaps between trades were under 60 seconds, median 98s.
// On an M1 chart two same-direction entries inside one bar are two reads of the
// SAME candle -- the second one buys no new information and pays the spread again.
// And the spread bill is the largest single line in the day: 275 trades at ~0.30
// points round trip is GBP76.53, which is 48% of the GBP159.79 loss. Not a bug in
// any one mechanism -- just trade count multiplied by cost.
// T_DupGuardPts only catches this when price has not moved 2.5 points, so a fast
// 3-point leg slips two fills through inside one candle. This is the bar-level
// backstop it needs.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_OneEntryPerBar = false.
// (v19.12 CB) DEFAULT OFF, same correction as T_DupClusterMax above. This does
// block the same-tick multi-clock fills -- in memory, before history, so it does
// fix the real latency bug. But its side effect is to collapse his intended
// 0.02-0.05 per signal down to the single first leg of 0.01, and changing
// effective position size is not something to do blind on a live day. Turn it on
// only alongside a sizing fix that puts the full size on the first leg.
input bool T_OneEntryPerBar = false;
datetime g_barLastBuy  = 0;
datetime g_barLastSell = 0;
bool BarAlreadyFilled(int dir)
{
   if(!T_OneEntryPerBar) return false;
   datetime b = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 0);
   if(b <= 0) return false;
   return (dir > 0) ? (g_barLastBuy == b) : (g_barLastSell == b);
}
void MarkBarFilled(int dir)
{
   datetime b = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 0);
   if(b <= 0) return;
   if(dir > 0) g_barLastBuy = b; else g_barLastSell = b;
}

int SameDirFillsInWindow(int dir, double px, int secs, double pts)
{
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(pd != dir) continue;
      datetime pt = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
      if(secs > 0 && (TimeCurrent() - pt) > (long)secs) continue;
      if(pts > 0.0 && MathAbs(PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN) - px) > pts) continue;
      n++;
   }
   return n;
}

// ══════════ (v18.77 AC) NEWS / VOLUME-PUSH DETECTOR ══════════
// His numbers, his words. See the note at T_PushPausePts.
// Reads CLOSED M1 bars only, so it cannot be re-triggered tick by tick by the
// same candle. Looks back far enough to cover the whole pause window.
// Returns the seconds remaining, or 0 if we are clear to trade.
int PushPauseRemaining()
{
   if(T_PushPausePts <= 0.0 || T_PushPauseSecs <= 0) return 0;
   int bars = (T_PushPauseSecs / 60) + 2;
   for(int i = 1; i <= bars; i++)
   {
      double hi = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, i);
      double lo = iLow (_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, i);
      if(hi <= 0.0 || lo <= 0.0) continue;
      if((hi - lo) < PS(T_PushPausePts)) continue;
      datetime bt   = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, i);
      long     over = (long)(TimeCurrent() - (bt + 60));   // seconds since that bar CLOSED
      if(over < 0) over = 0;
      if(over < (long)T_PushPauseSecs) return (int)(T_PushPauseSecs - over);
   }
   return 0;
}

bool Open(int e, int dir, double sl, double tp, string why, string tag = "")
{
   // (v18.77 AC) HIS INSTRUCTION: a 15 pt one-candle push is news, and the EA
   // must not stack into it. Open positions are untouched -- every exit, stop and
   // lock they have stays live. This refuses NEW fills only, for his 2-3 minutes.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_PushPausePts = 0.
   int pushWait = PushPauseRemaining();
   if(pushWait > 0)
   {
      PrintFormat("[PUSH PAUSE] refused a %s entry — an M1 bar in the last few minutes ranged more than "
                  "%.1f pts, which is a news push, not a signal. Waiting %d more seconds before taking "
                  "fills again. After a push price either continues or dips back, and both look identical "
                  "while the candle is still printing.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", PS(T_PushPausePts), pushWait);
      return false;
   }
   // (v17.59) STOP-DISTANCE CAP — applied before sizing so the lot maths sees
   // the real risk, and before the order is sent so the broker gets the capped
   // level, not a wide one we "meant" to tighten later.
   double stopCapPts = PS(T_MaxStopPts);
   if(T_MaxStopAtrMult > 0.0)
   {
      double capAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[SC_TFN - 1], 0, 1);   // slowest/most stable clock's ATR
      if(capAtr > 0.0) stopCapPts = T_MaxStopAtrMult * capAtr;
   }
   if(stopCapPts > 0.0 && sl > 0.0 && dir != 0)
   {
      double capRef  = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                 : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
      double capDist = MathAbs(capRef - sl);
      if(capRef > 0.0 && capDist > stopCapPts)
      {
         double capped = (dir > 0) ? capRef - stopCapPts : capRef + stopCapPts;
         PrintFormat("[%s] STOP CAPPED: signal-line stop was %.2f pts away (%.2f) — too wide for this account. "
                     "Placing at %.2f = %.2f pts. Widen T_MaxStopPts if this clips too many trades.",
                     Cfg[e].name, capDist, sl, capped, PS(T_MaxStopPts));
         sl = capped;
      }
   }
   // (fix #63a) Consume the TREND entry-timeframe channel FIRST, into a
   // local, and clear the global immediately — so an Open() that fails at
   // any early-return below can never leave a stale g_lastEntryK behind to
   // mislead DirectionAllowed or the entry snapshot on a later, unrelated
   // entry (e.g. a SCALP open moments after a blocked TREND one).
   int entryK = g_lastEntryK;
   g_lastEntryK = -1;

   // ── (v18.01) M5 BIRTH-WINDOW GATE ────────────────────────────────────────
   // Placed HERE, in the one shared door every entry goes through, rather than
   // in each entry path. That is deliberate: he reported an unwanted M5 re-entry
   // and I cannot yet name which path produced it, so gating the paths I suspect
   // would leave the others open. This gates the CLOCK, so every path is covered
   // whether or not I have identified it.
   if(T_M5BirthBars > 0 && e == E_TREND && entryK >= 0 && entryK < SC_TFN
      && g_scTF[entryK] == PERIOD_M5)
   {
      datetime fa5 = LastFlipAt(entryK);
      int bars5 = (fa5 > 0) ? (int)((TimeCurrent() - fa5) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(PERIOD_M5), 1))
                            : 9999;
      if(bars5 > T_M5BirthBars)
      {
         PrintFormat("[TREND] M5 SKIP: entry is %d M5 bars past the flip, birth window is %d. "
                     "Tag %s. M5 is taken at birth only.",
                     bars5, T_M5BirthBars, (tag != "" ? tag : "(none)"));
         return false;
      }
   }

   double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double px  = (dir > 0) ? ask : bid;
   // (v18.69 A) FILL-GAP LOCKOUT. See the note at T_MinFillGapSecs. This sits at
   // the one shared door every entry passes through, so two engines racing inside
   // a single OnTick cannot both get through it.
   if(T_MinFillGapSecs > 0 && dir != 0)
   {
      int gi = (dir > 0) ? 1 : 0;
      if(g_lastFillAt[gi] > 0 && (TimeCurrent() - g_lastFillAt[gi]) < (long)T_MinFillGapSecs)
      {
         PrintFormat("[FILL GAP] %s refused -- a %s already filled %d s ago (window %d s). "
                     "This is the SECOND fill of ONE signal, not a second signal.",
                     dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", dir > 0 ? "buy" : "sell",
                     (int)(TimeCurrent() - g_lastFillAt[gi]), T_MinFillGapSecs);
         return false;
      }
   }
   // (v18.72 A) ENFORCE THE CLUSTER LIMIT HE ALREADY SET. See note (A). This uses
   // T_DupClusterMax, already in this file, which was computed and then ignored.
   if(T_EnforceDupCluster && T_DupClusterMax > 0 && dir != 0)
   {
      if(BarAlreadyFilled(dir))
   {
      PrintFormat("[ONE PER BAR] %s refused -- this M1 candle already has a %s fill. "
                  "The second read of one candle carries no new information and pays "
                  "the spread twice. 111 of his 274 trade gaps were under 60 seconds.",
                  dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL");
      return false;
   }
   int nClust = SameDirFillsInWindow(dir, px, T_DupGuardSecs, PS(T_DupGuardPts));
      if(nClust >= T_DupClusterMax)
      {
         PrintFormat("[DUP CLUSTER] %s refused -- %d same-direction fills already inside %ds / %.2f pts, "
                     "limit T_DupClusterMax = %d. These are extra FILLS of one signal, not extra signals. "
                     "Each one pays the spread again.",
                     dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", nClust, T_DupGuardSecs, PS(T_DupGuardPts), T_DupClusterMax);
         return false;
      }
   }
   if(DuplicateFill(dir, px)) return false;      // (v18.26/18.42) one signal, one position
   if(TooManyThisDirection(dir)) return false;   // (v18.42) churn circuit breaker
   // (v18.67) was DirExposureFull(dir, 0.0), which only blocks once ALREADY at or
   // over the cap -- so one more full-size ticket always got through, and with the
   // double fill that is TWO. Pass the size this entry will actually be, so the
   // ceiling is tested against the post-fill total. T_FixedLots is the right
   // estimate because with T_FixedLots > 0 LotFor() returns exactly that (times
   // symbol scale) for every entry.
   if(DirExposureFull(dir, MathMax(T_FixedLots, 0.01) * 2.0)) return false;   // (v18.64/67) exposure ceiling, x2 for the double fill
   if(ChasingTheExtreme(dir, px)) return false;   // (v18.74 X) average-entry drift
   if(DirectionUnderwater(dir)) return false;     // (v18.75 Y) no adding into an open loss
   // (v18.39) late-leg entries are sized DOWN, never refused.
   if(LegIsMature(Cfg[e].tf, dir, px))
      g_entryExtScale = MathMin(g_entryExtScale, T_ExtendedLotFrac);
   double atrE = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);

   // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #31 (SEVERE) — AFFECTS EVERY ENGINE ══════════════════
   // Every engine computes its stop from bar[1] data, then Open() executes at the
   // LIVE price. On gold those can be seconds and several dollars apart. If price
   // ran hard in the trade's favour between the signal bar closing and this call,
   // the stop can end up on the WRONG SIDE of the entry.
   //
   // The old code did:  slD = MathAbs(px - sl)
   // MathAbs happily turned a wrong-sided stop into a large, plausible-looking
   // distance. LotFor() then sized the position for that large stop. Then
   // ClampStops() noticed the stop was invalid and "fixed" it to px - 10 points.
   //
   // Net result: a MASSIVELY OVERSIZED position with a $0.10 stop on gold.
   // Guaranteed instant stop-out, at many times the intended risk. Silently.
   // This is the single most dangerous line of code in the file.
   //
   // A stop on the wrong side is not a stop that needs fixing. It is a signal
   // that has expired. Reject it.
   bool wrongSide = (dir > 0) ? (sl >= px) : (sl <= px);
   if(wrongSide)
   {
      PrintFormat("[%s] REJECTED: price ran to %.2f, the stop is at %.2f — wrong side. Setup expired.",
                  Cfg[e].name, px, sl);
      return false;
   }

   // and a stop that is absurdly tight is the same problem in a milder form
   double slD = MathAbs(px - sl);
   if(atrE > 0 && slD < 0.35 * atrE)
   {
      if(T_TakeEverySignal)
      {
         // (v18.08) REPAIR, don't refuse. A stop this tight is a stop-placement
         // problem, not an expired signal -- widen it to the legal minimum and
         // take the trade. This is the line that killed the 4371.18 flip.
         double wantD = MathMax(0.40 * atrE, T_SlFloorPts);
         sl = (dir > 0) ? px - wantD : px + wantD;
         PrintFormat("[%s] STOP REPAIRED: was %.2f (%.2f ATR), too tight to be a stop. Widened to %.2f. TAKING the signal.",
                     Cfg[e].name, slD, slD / atrE, wantD);
         slD = wantD;
      }
      else
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] REJECTED: stop is only %.2f (%.2f ATR) — price has already run. Setup expired.",
                     Cfg[e].name, slD, slD / atrE);
         return false;
      }
   }
   if(atrE > 0 && slD > 4.0 * atrE)
   {
      if(T_TakeEverySignal)
      {
         double wantD = 4.0 * atrE;
         sl = (dir > 0) ? px - wantD : px + wantD;
         PrintFormat("[%s] STOP REPAIRED: was %.2f (%.2f ATR), too wide. Tightened to %.2f. TAKING the signal.",
                     Cfg[e].name, slD, slD / atrE, wantD);
         slD = wantD;
      }
      else
      {
      PrintFormat("[%s] REJECTED: stop is %.2f (%.2f ATR) — too wide, the R:R is gone.",
                     Cfg[e].name, slD, slD / atrE);
         return false;
      }
   }

   if(!DirectionAllowed(dir, entryK))
   {
      if(InpVerboseLog)
         PrintFormat("[%s] BLOCKED: would open %s against live %s exposure.",
                     Cfg[e].name, dir>0?"LONG":"SHORT", NetDirection()>0?"LONG":"SHORT");
      return false;
   }

   // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #32 (SEVERE) ══════════════════
   // LotFor() was called with the PRE-CLAMP stop distance, then ClampStops()
   // moved the stop afterwards. So the position was always sized for a stop that
   // was not the one actually placed — and StoreRisk() recorded the wrong number
   // too, poisoning the expectancy stats. Clamp FIRST. Size against reality.
   ClampStops(dir, px, sl, tp);
   slD = MathAbs(px - sl);
   if(slD <= 0) return false;

   double lots = LotFor(e, slD) * DriftSizeFrac(dir);   // (v19.15 CE)
   if(lots <= 0) return false;

   // (v17.15) Symbol-agnostic sizing. Take the SMALLER of the ladder lot and
   // the risk-normalised lot. Smaller, not larger, deliberately: on gold at his
   // balance the two are close, so behaviour barely changes — but on a symbol
   // where the ladder's absolute 0.03 would be a much bigger cash risk, the
   // risk rule caps it. This is what lets the EA be attached to anything
   // without the lot number silently meaning something different.
   if(T_AutoCalibrate && T_AutoRiskPct > 0)
   {
      double rl = AutoRiskLots(slD);
      if(rl > 0 && rl < lots)
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] auto-calibrate: ladder %.2f -> risk-normalised %.2f lots (%.1f%% of equity on this symbol)",
                     Cfg[e].name, lots, rl, T_AutoRiskPct);
         lots = rl;
      }
   }

   // (v17.15) ATR-relative slippage tolerance. An absolute 40 points is $0.40
   // on 2-digit gold = GBP0.90 per fill at 0.03, against a GBP3 choppy winner.
   // Proportionate to ATR it is right on every symbol and it tightens by itself
   // in quiet hours, where the winners are smallest and a fixed tolerance does
   // the most damage.
   if(T_SlipAtrFrac > 0)
   {
      double atrS = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
      if(atrS > 0 && _Point > 0)
      {
         int slipPts = (int)MathMax(1.0, MathRound(T_SlipAtrFrac * atrS / _Point));
         if(slipPts < InpSlippagePoints) trade.SetDeviationInPoints(slipPts);
      }
   }

   // (v17.13) MARGIN GUARD, wired in. A guard function that is never called is
   // worse than none — it reads as protection that isn't there. Rather than
   // refuse the signal outright (he is frequency-first: never hard-block, size
   // down), step the lot down toward the broker minimum until it fits. Only if
   // even ONE minimum lot cannot be opened do we refuse, and then we say so
   // loudly, because that refusal is a MARGIN wall and must never again be
   // mistaken for the EA "stopping taking signals".
   if(T_MarginGuard && !MarginOkFor(lots, dir))
   {
      double step = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP);
      double minL = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
      if(step <= 0) step = 0.01;
      if(minL <= 0) minL = 0.01;
      double tryL = lots;
      while(tryL - step >= minL - 1e-9)
      {
         tryL = NormalizeDouble(tryL - step, 2);
         if(MarginOkFor(tryL, dir)) break;
      }
      if(!MarginOkFor(tryL, dir))
      {
         if(T_MarginLogRefusal)
            PrintFormat("[%s] SKIP: MARGIN WALL — even %.2f lots will not fit. "
                        "Free margin %.2f, equity %.2f. This is a BALANCE limit, not a signal gate.",
                        Cfg[e].name, minL, AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_MARGIN_FREE),
                        AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY));
         return false;
      }
      if(T_MarginLogRefusal)
         PrintFormat("[%s] margin step-down %.2f -> %.2f lots (signal TAKEN, not skipped)",
                     Cfg[e].name, lots, tryL);
      lots = tryL;
   }

   trade.SetExpertMagicNumber(Cfg[e].magic);

   string cmt = (tag != "") ? tag : Cfg[e].name;
   // (v17.31 EXECUTION AUDIT — his ask: "make sure entry/exit/hold/reentry/
   // close is all good.") There was NO RETRY on order-send anywhere in this
   // file. A genuine, correctly-timed birth signal that hit a REQUOTE, a
   // TIMEOUT, or "trade context busy" — all transient, all common on retail
   // MT5 servers under load — was simply LOGGED and LOST. That directly
   // undermines "catch every trend from birth": the signal was right and the
   // entry still never happened. Retrying is ONLY safe for retcodes that mean
   // "try again", never for ones that mean "this request is wrong" (bad
   // stops, no money, invalid volume) — retrying those would just resend a
   // broken request. T_OrderRetryN x2 attempts, T_OrderRetryMs between them,
   // short enough not to meaningfully worsen entry lag on a birth signal.
   bool ok = false;
   for(int attempt = 0; attempt <= T_OrderRetryN; attempt++)
   {
      ok = (dir > 0) ? trade.Buy(lots, _Symbol, 0.0, sl, tp, cmt)
                     : trade.Sell(lots, _Symbol, 0.0, sl, tp, cmt);
      // (v18.69 A) stamp the fill the moment the broker accepts it, so the next
      // engine in this same tick sees it. Stamped on ACCEPTANCE, not on the later
      // position scan -- that lag is exactly what the old guard raced against.
      if(ok) g_lastFillAt[(dir > 0) ? 1 : 0] = TimeCurrent();
      if(ok) break;
      uint rc = trade.ResultRetcode();
      // (v17.32 SELF-AUDIT of the v17.31 fix I just shipped, before he tests it.)
      // TIMEOUT and CONNECTION are AMBIGUOUS: the client-side request can time
      // out while the broker's side actually filled it. Blindly retrying those,
      // as v17.31 did, risks sending a SECOND real order on top of a first one
      // that already went through — a duplicate live position, silently, which
      // is a strictly worse failure than the one this fix was trying to solve.
      // REQUOTE / PRICE_CHANGED / TRADE_DISABLED / no-quotes are UNAMBIGUOUS —
      // the broker explicitly says nothing was opened, so those are safe to
      // retry freely. Ambiguous ones now get ONE extra check first: if a
      // position with this engine's magic already opened in the last few
      // seconds, treat it as filled and stop, instead of risking a duplicate.
      bool unambiguousRetry = (rc == TRADE_RETCODE_REQUOTE || rc == TRADE_RETCODE_PRICE_CHANGED ||
                                rc == TRADE_RETCODE_TRADE_DISABLED || rc == 10021 /* no quotes */);
      bool ambiguousRetry   = (rc == TRADE_RETCODE_TIMEOUT || rc == TRADE_RETCODE_CONNECTION);
      if(ambiguousRetry)
      {
         for(int pi = PositionsTotal()-1; pi >= 0; pi--)
         {
            ulong ptk = PositionGetTicket(pi);
            if(ptk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(ptk)) continue;
            if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != (long)Cfg[e].magic) continue;
            if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
            if((long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME) >= TimeCurrent() - 5)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[%s] order retry SKIPPED after ambiguous retcode %d — a matching position "
                              "already opened %d seconds ago. Treating it as filled, not retrying.",
                              Cfg[e].name, rc, (int)(TimeCurrent() - (long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME)));
               return true;   // it filled; do not retry, do not report failure
            }
         }
      }
      bool transient = unambiguousRetry || ambiguousRetry;
      if(!transient || attempt >= T_OrderRetryN)
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] ORDER FAIL %d %s%s", Cfg[e].name, rc, trade.ResultRetcodeDescription(),
                     transient ? " (transient, retries exhausted)" : " (not retryable)");
         return false;
      }
      if(InpVerboseLog)
         PrintFormat("[%s] order retry %d/%d after retcode %d %s", Cfg[e].name, attempt+1, T_OrderRetryN, rc,
                     trade.ResultRetcodeDescription());
      Sleep(T_OrderRetryMs);
      // price may have moved during the pause — refresh the stop/target against
      // the current price rather than resend the exact same stale request
      double pxR = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
      ClampStops(dir, pxR, sl, tp);
   }
   g_fastRearm[e] = false;   // consumed — back to the normal cooldown after this one
   // (v19.08 BX) mark the bar HERE: this line is past every failure return and
   // ahead of every success return, including the "could not resolve the ticket"
   // path below. Marking it inside NoteEntry() would miss that one.
   MarkBarFilled(dir);
   // BUG FIX #16: trade.ResultDeal() returns 0 under asynchronous execution and
   // on some brokers. The old code then called StoreReason(0, ...) and
   // StoreRisk(0, ...), so the reason and the risk were filed under ticket 0 —
   // i.e. lost. Every trade on such a broker would have had no reason logged and
   // fallen back to estimated expectancy. Fall back to the order ticket.
   ulong pos = trade.ResultDeal();
   if(pos > 0 && HistoryDealSelect(pos))
      pos = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(pos, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
   else
      pos = trade.ResultOrder();
   if(pos == 0)
   {
      Print("WARNING: could not resolve the position ticket after entry. Reason and R will be estimated.");
      return true;
   }
   // (v18.40) remember whether this ticket was opened counter-DEMA, then clear the
   // pending flag so it cannot leak into the next entry.
   NoteEntry(dir);   // (v18.42) count it for the circuit breaker
   NoteDemaBirth(pos, g_bornAgainstDemaPending);
   if(g_bornAgainstDemaPending)
      PrintFormat("[TREND] #%I64u opened COUNTER-DEMA — the DEMA is lagging behind a move that already "
                  "happened, so it will not be counted as a reason to close this trade.", pos);
   g_bornAgainstDemaPending = false;

   StoreRisk(pos, lots * LossPerLot(slD));   // BUG FIX #1: the REAL cash at risk, not the intended %
   StoreRDist(pos, slD);                     // BUG FIX #14: this ticket's own R, in price

   // Freeze the conditions we entered on. When this trade dies, the autopsy needs
   // to compare what we BELIEVED against what actually happened — and you cannot
   // reconstruct that after the fact from a chart that has already moved on.
   {
      // BUG FIX #55: read the ENTRY'S OWN timeframe for TREND (M1/M3/M5 via
      // g_lastEntryK), not the fixed M5 engine handle — see the channel's
      // declaration above for why. Every other engine is unaffected;
      // entryK (captured and cleared at the top of Open()) is -1 unless
      // Engine_Trend() set the channel just before calling.
      bool  useOwnTf = (e == E_TREND && entryK >= 0 && entryK < SC_TFN);
      int   atrHE     = useOwnTf ? g_scAtr[entryK] : hAtr[e];
      int   e21HE      = useOwnTf ? g_scE21[entryK] : hEma21[e];
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tfE = useOwnTf ? g_scTF[entryK] : Cfg[e].tf;
      double atrE2 = Buf(atrHE, 0, 1);
      double e21E  = Buf(e21HE, 0, 1);
      double c1E   = iClose(_Symbol, tfE, 1);
      double extE  = (atrE2 > 0) ? MathAbs(c1E - e21E) / atrE2 : 0;
      SnapshotEntry(pos, e, g_lastPush, g_lastConfirms, extE);
      // (channel already consumed and cleared at the top of Open())
   }
   double slM, tpM; AdaptMults(e, slM, tpM);
   StoreReason(pos, StringFormat("%s %s | %s | SL %.2f TP %.2f | risk %.2f%% (base %.1f%%) | vol %.0f%% [SLx%.2f TPx%.2f]",
               Cfg[e].name, dir > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT", why, sl, tp,
               LiveRisk(e), Cfg[e].risk, AtrRank(e), slM, tpM));
   return true;
}

//================================================================
//  E0 · TREND RIDER  (M1 + M3 + M5 — the SAME raw Supertrend flip Pine takes,
//  DEMA grades it, does not block it. Stop on the signal line. No fixed
//  target — rides to the opposite signal, per timeframe.)
//================================================================
// Forward declaration: ScalpST is defined further down (with the rest of the
// SCALP engine, which correctly comes after it) but TREND now reuses it for
// its own per-timeframe Supertrend reads. This prototype satisfies MQL5's
// declare-before-use requirement without moving either block.
void ScalpST(int k, double &val, int &dir);

//================================================================
//  KAUFMAN EFFICIENCY RATIO (v13) — the research-backed whipsaw fix.
//  ER = |net move over N bars| / sum(|bar-to-bar moves| over N bars).
//  1.0 = perfectly clean directional move; ~0 = thrashing/chop.
//  A Supertrend flip in a high-ER environment is a real trend; the
//  same flip in a low-ER environment is a whipsaw waiting to happen.
//  Used to SCALE SIZE, never to block — a low-ER flip is still taken
//  (frequency preserved, your core goal), it just risks less. Reads
//  off closed bars only, so it adds ZERO entry lag — the hard
//  constraint you set. This is the profitable half of "mean reversion
//  awareness": shrink in chop, full size in clean trends.
//================================================================
double EfficiencyRatio(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int n)
{
   if(n < 2) n = 2;
   double netMove = MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1) - iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1 + n));
   double sumMove = 0.0;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
      sumMove += MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol, tf, i) - iClose(_Symbol, tf, i + 1));
   if(sumMove <= 0) return 0.0;
   return netMove / sumMove;
}

// (v16.13) VOLUME-SPIKE READER — research-backed pullback-vs-reversal tell.
// A pullback happens on FALLING volume (just profit-taking, ~20-30% below
// average); a real reversal happens on a volume SPIKE (~50%+ above average,
// real selling/buying pressure). Tick volume is the right proxy on gold CFDs
// (real volume is 0). Returns the last closed bar's volume as a multiple of
// the recent average: >1.5 = spike (likely real turn), <0.8 = drying up
// (likely just a pullback). Used to decide whether a counter-move against an
// open trade is worth banking on or holding through.
double VolMult(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   long v1 = iTickVolume(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   if(v1 <= 0) return 1.0;
   double sum = 0; int n = 0;
   for(int i = 2; i <= 11; i++) { long v = iTickVolume(_Symbol, tf, i); if(v > 0) { sum += (double)v; n++; } }
   if(n == 0 || sum <= 0) return 1.0;
   double avg = sum / n;
   return (avg > 0) ? (double)v1 / avg : 1.0;
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.22) CHOPPINESS INDEX + REGIME MODE — the master switch.
//  The research is unanimous on gold Supertrend systems: the single
//  biggest edge is knowing whether price is TRENDING or RANGING and
//  behaving differently, because gold "can appear to trend visually
//  while actually oscillating in a messy range" — exactly the chart the
//  trader kept screenshotting as a bad-signal example. Supertrend flips
//  in a range are near coin-flips that just pay spread; the same flips
//  in a trend are the 90%+ winners.
//
//  Choppiness Index (Dreiss): 100*log10(sum(ATR1,n)/(maxHigh-minLow)) /
//  log10(n). ~100 = pure chop/range, ~0 = strong directional move. It
//  reads a DIFFERENT thing than ADX (range-boundedness vs directional
//  strength), so the two together catch cases either misses alone —
//  which is why both are used, not one.
//================================================================
double ChoppinessIndex(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int n)
{
   if(n < 2) n = 14;
   double atrSum = 0.0;
   double hi = -DBL_MAX, lo = DBL_MAX;
   for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
   {
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i), l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i), pc = iClose(_Symbol, tf, i + 1);
      if(h <= 0 || l <= 0 || pc <= 0) return 50.0;   // not enough data -> neutral
      double tr = MathMax(h - l, MathMax(MathAbs(h - pc), MathAbs(l - pc)));
      atrSum += tr;
      if(h > hi) hi = h;
      if(l < lo) lo = l;
   }
   double rng = hi - lo;
   if(rng <= 0 || atrSum <= 0) return 50.0;
   double chop = 100.0 * MathLog10(atrSum / rng) / MathLog10((double)n);
   if(chop < 0) chop = 0; if(chop > 100) chop = 100;
   return chop;
}

// Regime of a given timeframe RIGHT NOW. Returns:
//   +1 = TRENDING (ride hard, hold, reenter — the £12-trade regime)
//    0 = NEUTRAL/TRANSITION (normal graded behaviour)
//   -1 = RANGING (scalp-and-bank-fast; do NOT hold for a trend that
//        isn't there — the bad-screenshot regime)
// ADX and CHOP must AGREE for a definite call; if they disagree it's a
// transition, treated as neutral. Reads closed bars only -> zero lag.
int RegimeOf(int adxHandle, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   double adx  = Buf(adxHandle, 0, 1);
   double chop = ChoppinessIndex(tf, T_ChopLen);
   if(adx <= 0) return 0;
   // (v16.24 #5) during prime directional hours (London open -> NY overlap),
   // trends are real and worth calling sooner, so ease the ADX bar a touch;
   // outside them keep the full requirement to stay out of dead-hour chop.
   double adxTrendReq = T_RegimeAdxTrend;
   if(T_SessionEaseRegime && PrimeSession()) adxTrendReq -= 3.0;
   bool trending = (adx >= adxTrendReq) && (chop <= T_RegimeChopTrend);
   bool ranging  = (adx <  T_RegimeAdxRange) || (chop >= T_RegimeChopRange);
   if(trending) return  1;
   if(ranging)  return -1;
   return 0;
}

// (v16.22 #10) SELF-MONITORING SIZE LEAN — after enough same-regime trades
// this session, lean size toward the regime that's actually paying and away
// from one that's bleeding. Bounded to [0.6, 1.4] so it nudges, never swings.
// Needs T_RegimeLearnMinN trades in that regime before it acts (no reacting to
// one or two results). This is the EA "monitoring itself" toward the goal.
double RegimeSizeMult(int regime)
{
   if(!T_RegimeLearn) return 1.0;
   int idx = regime + 1;
   if(idx < 0 || idx > 2) return 1.0;
   if(g_regN[idx] < T_RegimeLearnMinN) return 1.0;
   double exp = g_regR[idx] / g_regN[idx];   // avg R per trade in this regime today
   double m = 1.0 + 0.4 * MathMax(-1.0, MathMin(1.0, exp));   // +exp -> up, -exp -> down
   return MathMax(0.6, MathMin(1.4, m));
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.24) THE 10-FEATURE PROFITABILITY PACK — regime-awareness,
//  session weighting, and confidence grading. All SIZE the entry
//  (multiply g_lastConfMult); none block a signal. Reentry untouched.
//================================================================

// #1 HTF REGIME BACKDROP — does the bigger picture (M15 + H1) back this
// fast flip? A M1 long inside a trending-up M15/H1 is a far better bet
// than one fighting a trending-down backdrop. Returns a multiplier:
// >1 when the backdrop agrees & trends, <1 when it fights, 1 neutral.
double HtfBackdropMult(int dir)
{
   if(!T_HtfBackdrop) return 1.0;
   // M15 regime + direction
   double m15st; int m15sd; Supertrend(E_APEX, m15st, m15sd);   // APEX = M15
   double m15adx = Buf(hAdx[E_APEX], 0, 1);
   // H1 direction via EMA50 + H1 ADX
   double h1c = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 1), h1e = Buf(g_hH1Ema, 0, 1);
   int    h1sd = (h1e > 0) ? ((h1c > h1e) ? 1 : -1) : 0;
   double h1adx = Buf(g_hH1Adx, 0, 1);
   double mult = 1.0;
   // M15 agrees and is trending -> boost; disagrees and trending -> trim
   if(m15sd == dir && m15adx >= 22) mult *= 1.15;
   else if(m15sd == -dir && m15adx >= 22) mult *= 0.80;
   // H1 the same, lighter weight (it's context, never a gate — his rule)
   if(h1sd == dir && h1adx >= 20) mult *= 1.10;
   else if(h1sd == -dir && h1adx >= 20) mult *= 0.88;
   return mult;
}

// #2 REGIME STRENGTH — grade HOW trending, not just yes/no. ADX 25 and
// ADX 45 are both "trend" but the 45 deserves more size. Linear from
// 1.0 at the trend threshold up to +T_StrengthMaxBoost by ADX 45.
double RegimeStrengthMult(int adxHandle)
{
   if(!T_RegimeStrength) return 1.0;
   double adx = Buf(adxHandle, 0, 1);
   if(adx <= T_RegimeAdxTrend) return 1.0;
   double t = MathMin(1.0, (adx - T_RegimeAdxTrend) / MathMax(45.0 - T_RegimeAdxTrend, 1.0));
   return 1.0 + T_StrengthMaxBoost * t;
}

// #3 RANGE->TREND BREAKOUT — the highest-value moment: a flip on the bar
// a genuine range breaks into a trend. Detected as: the PRIOR few bars
// were ranging (high Choppiness) and NOW ADX has just jumped above the
// trend threshold. That flip gets a boost — it's the birth of a runner.
bool BreakoutBirth(int adxHandle, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_BreakoutBoost) return false;
   double adxNow = Buf(adxHandle, 0, 1), adxPast = Buf(adxHandle, 0, 4);
   double chopPast = ChoppinessIndex(tf, T_ChopLen);   // still elevated as the breakout is fresh
   // ADX was below threshold a few bars ago, now above, and it's rising hard
   return (adxPast < T_RegimeAdxTrend) && (adxNow >= T_RegimeAdxTrend) && (adxNow > adxPast + 3.0) && (chopPast >= 40.0);
}

// #4/#5 SESSION — gold's real directional moves cluster in the London
// open and the London/NY overlap; the Asian session is mostly range.
// Returns a size multiplier for the current GMT hour, and (separately)
// whether we're in a "prime" window so the regime thresholds can ease.
double SessionMult()
{
   if(!T_SessionWeight) return 1.0;
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), t);
   int h = t.hour;
   // London open burst 07-09, London/NY overlap 12-16 = prime directional hours
   if((h >= 12 && h < 16)) return T_SessionPrimeX;        // the richest window
   if((h >= 7  && h < 12)) return T_SessionGoodX;         // London morning
   if((h >= 16 && h < 20)) return T_SessionGoodX;         // NY afternoon
   if((h >= 0  && h < 6))  return T_SessionDeadX;         // Asian range — trim
   return 1.0;
}
bool PrimeSession()
{
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), t);
   int h = t.hour;
   return (h >= 7 && h < 16);   // London open through the overlap
}

// #6 MOMENTUM ACCELERATION — a flip where the last few ranges are
// EXPANDING (each bar bigger than the last) is a real thrust; a flip on
// contracting ranges is limp. Boost the thrust, trim the limp one.
double AccelMult(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   if(!T_AccelBoost) return 1.0;
   double r1 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1) - iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double r2 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,2) - iLow(_Symbol,tf,2);
   double r3 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,3) - iLow(_Symbol,tf,3);
   if(r1 <= 0 || r2 <= 0 || r3 <= 0) return 1.0;
   if(r1 > r2 && r2 > r3) return 1.12;   // expanding — real thrust
   if(r1 < r2 && r2 < r3) return 0.92;   // contracting — limp
   return 1.0;
}

// #7 DXY INVERSE CONFLUENCE — gold moves opposite the dollar. If a DXY
// proxy symbol is available, a gold long is stronger when the dollar is
// falling. Pure nudge; silently 1.0 if the symbol isn't on the broker.
double DxyConfluenceMult(int dir)
{
   if(!T_DxyConfluence) return 1.0;
   string sym = T_DxySymbol;
   if(sym == "" || !SymbolInfoInteger(sym, SYMBOL_SELECT)) return 1.0;
   double c1 = iClose(sym, PERIOD_M5, 1), c6 = iClose(sym, PERIOD_M5, 6);
   if(c1 <= 0 || c6 <= 0) return 1.0;
   int dxyDir = (c1 > c6) ? 1 : -1;              // dollar rising or falling
   // gold long (dir>0) wants dollar falling (dxyDir<0) -> agreement
   bool agree = (dir > 0 && dxyDir < 0) || (dir < 0 && dxyDir > 0);
   return agree ? 1.10 : 0.92;
}

// #8 CONSECUTIVE-WIN PRESS — after 2+ wins in the trending regime, lean
// size up a touch to press the hot hand; resets on any loss. Bounded.
double WinPressMult(int regime)
{
   if(!T_WinPress || regime <= 0) return 1.0;
   int w = g_consecWin[E_TREND];
   if(w < 2) return 1.0;
   return MathMin(1.0 + 0.08 * (w - 1), T_WinPressMax);
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.25) MARKET STRUCTURE — THE SECOND, INDEPENDENT WAY TO SEE A
//  TREND. Research is unanimous that a trend is DEFINED by the swing
//  sequence, not by an indicator: higher highs + higher lows = uptrend,
//  lower highs + lower lows = downtrend, sideways swings = range. This
//  reads PRICE ITSELF, so it adds real information rather than
//  double-counting Supertrend / DEMA / ADX / Two-Pole.
//
//  Three jobs, matching what the trader asked for:
//   1. StructureDir()   — what the swing sequence says the trend IS.
//   2. StructureBirth() — BOS: a decisive BODY CLOSE beyond the last
//      swing extreme = a confirmed new trend leg. Body-close matters:
//      a wick through the level is a liquidity sweep, NOT a structural
//      shift, and that distinction kills a lot of false births.
//   3. StructureBroken() — CHoCH: price closed beyond the most recent
//      protective swing = the trend is objectively damaged. This is the
//      "line in the sand" that replaces guessing whether a move against
//      us is a pullback or a real reversal. It is ALSO what lets a slow
//      timeframe trade be held with CONFIDENCE: while structure holds,
//      a fast wobble against it is noise BY DEFINITION.
//================================================================

// A swing high needs T_SwingSide bars strictly lower on BOTH sides.
// Returns the bar shift of the most recent confirmed swing, or -1.
int LastSwingHigh(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int maxLook)
{
   int s = T_SwingSide;
   for(int i = 1 + s; i <= maxLook; i++)
   {
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(h <= 0) break;
      bool ok = true;
      for(int j = 1; j <= s && ok; j++)
         if(iHigh(_Symbol,tf,i-j) >= h || iHigh(_Symbol,tf,i+j) >= h) ok = false;
      if(ok) return i;
   }
   return -1;
}
int LastSwingLow(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int maxLook)
{
   int s = T_SwingSide;
   for(int i = 1 + s; i <= maxLook; i++)
   {
      double l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(l <= 0) break;
      bool ok = true;
      for(int j = 1; j <= s && ok; j++)
         if(iLow(_Symbol,tf,i-j) <= l || iLow(_Symbol,tf,i+j) <= l) ok = false;
      if(ok) return i;
   }
   return -1;
}

// Second-most-recent swing, so we can compare (HH vs LH, HL vs LL).
int PrevSwingHigh(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int after, int maxLook)
{
   int s = T_SwingSide;
   for(int i = after + 1; i <= maxLook; i++)
   {
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(h <= 0) break;
      bool ok = true;
      for(int j = 1; j <= s && ok; j++)
         if(iHigh(_Symbol,tf,i-j) >= h || iHigh(_Symbol,tf,i+j) >= h) ok = false;
      if(ok) return i;
   }
   return -1;
}
int PrevSwingLow(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int after, int maxLook)
{
   int s = T_SwingSide;
   for(int i = after + 1; i <= maxLook; i++)
   {
      double l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(l <= 0) break;
      bool ok = true;
      for(int j = 1; j <= s && ok; j++)
         if(iLow(_Symbol,tf,i-j) <= l || iLow(_Symbol,tf,i+j) <= l) ok = false;
      if(ok) return i;
   }
   return -1;
}

// 1. What the swing sequence says the trend IS. +1 up, -1 down, 0 unclear/range.
int StructureDir(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   int look = T_SwingLook;
   int h1i = LastSwingHigh(tf, look);   if(h1i < 0) return 0;
   int l1i = LastSwingLow(tf, look);    if(l1i < 0) return 0;
   int h2i = PrevSwingHigh(tf, h1i, look); if(h2i < 0) return 0;
   int l2i = PrevSwingLow(tf, l1i, look);  if(l2i < 0) return 0;
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,h1i), h2 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,h2i);
   double l1 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,l1i),  l2 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,l2i);
   bool hh = h1 > h2, hl = l1 > l2;
   bool lh = h1 < h2, ll = l1 < l2;
   if(hh && hl) return  1;    // higher highs AND higher lows = uptrend
   if(lh && ll) return -1;    // lower highs AND lower lows  = downtrend
   return 0;                  // mixed = range / unclear
}

// 2. BOS — a confirmed structural trend birth in `dir`, this bar.
//    Requires a decisive BODY CLOSE beyond the last swing extreme (a mere
//    wick through is a liquidity sweep, not a structural break), and the
//    close must clear it by a real fraction of ATR so a 1-tick poke
//    doesn't count.
bool StructureBirth(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   if(!T_StructureBirth) return false;
   double atr = iATRproxy(tf);
   if(atr <= 0) return false;
   double need = T_BosBodyAtr * atr;
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1 = iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   if(dir > 0)
   {
      int hi = LastSwingHigh(tf, T_SwingLook);
      if(hi < 0) return false;
      double lvl = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,hi);
      // decisive bullish body closing above the swing high
      return (c1 > o1) && (c1 >= lvl + need);
   }
   else
   {
      int li = LastSwingLow(tf, T_SwingLook);
      if(li < 0) return false;
      double lvl = iLow(_Symbol,tf,li);
      return (c1 < o1) && (c1 <= lvl - need);
   }
}

// 3. CHoCH — the trend in `dir` is objectively DAMAGED: price has closed
//    beyond the most recent protective swing (the last higher low for a
//    long, the last lower high for a short). Body close again, so a wick
//    hunt doesn't eject us from a still-valid trend.
bool StructureBroken(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1);
   if(c1 <= 0) return false;
   if(dir > 0)
   {
      int li = LastSwingLow(tf, T_SwingLook);
      if(li < 0) return false;
      return c1 < iLow(_Symbol,tf,li);      // closed below the protective higher low
   }
   else
   {
      int hi = LastSwingHigh(tf, T_SwingLook);
      if(hi < 0) return false;
      return c1 > iHigh(_Symbol,tf,hi);     // closed above the protective lower high
   }
}

// Convenience: is the structure still backing this trade? Used to hold a
// slower-timeframe trade with confidence through faster-timeframe noise.
bool StructureIntact(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   return !StructureBroken(tf, dir);
}

//================================================================
//  (v17.12) HTF HOLD-DURATION CONTEXT — "how long do we trust this?"
//
//  Returns a conviction score in [-2, +2] for holding a trade in `dir`.
//  It is NOT a signal and NOT an entry gate. It answers one question:
//  does higher-timeframe structure give this move room to keep going?
//
//    +1  D1 structure (HH+HL / LH+LL) agrees with the trade
//    +1  H4 structure agrees
//    -1  D1 structure opposes
//    -1  H4 structure opposes
//    -1  price is already jammed into the D1 extreme it is running toward,
//        i.e. the room to extend is spent regardless of what structure says
//
//  Deliberately reuses StructureDir() rather than writing a second swing
//  reader. The standing lesson from the v16.33 multicollinearity audit is
//  that duplicating an existing read is how the same information gets
//  counted twice and how the saturation bug happened.
//================================================================
int HtfBias(int dir, ENUM_TIMEFRAMES callerTf)
{
   if(!T_HtfHold || dir == 0) return 0;
   int score = 0;

   // (v17.34 — his direct, correct challenge: "how's a 1min got to do with an
   // hour anyway?") HtfBias used to apply the SAME W1+D1+H4+H1 stack no matter
   // which clock called it. A weekly structure read is genuinely uninformative
   // about whether an M1 trade — typically resolved in minutes — should survive
   // a dip; by the time weekly structure could matter, the M1 trade is long
   // over. This scales which votes apply to the calling clock's own timescale,
   // so slower context is only consulted by clocks whose trades actually run
   // long enough for it to mean anything.
   // (v17.35 — his correction, more precise than my v17.34 attempt.) "1m has
   // no relevance to 1 hour or day, only 5min meets 1 and 4 hour." M1 and M3
   // get NOTHING — both resolve too fast for hourly-or-slower structure to be
   // informative before the trade is already over. Only M5 consults H1+H4,
   // both at equal weight (he gave neither priority over the other). Daily
   // and weekly are now unused by any active clock, since TREND only runs
   // M1/M3/M5 — kept available for M15+ only in case APEX is ever re-enabled.
   bool useW1 = false, useD1 = false, useH4 = false, useH1 = false;
   if(callerTf == PERIOD_M5)  { useH4 = true; useH1 = true; }
   if(callerTf >= PERIOD_M15) { useH4 = true; useD1 = true; useW1 = true; useH1 = true; }

   // (v17.13) He asked for W1 + D1 + H4 + H1. Weighted, NOT counted flat:
   // four equal votes would let two fast clocks outvote the weekly, and the
   // whole point of this read is that SLOWER structure tells you more about
   // how long a move can last. W1/D1 set the ceiling on hold duration; H4/H1
   // are the confirmation he described for "holding out long", so they carry
   // half a vote each. Deliberately still capped at +/-2 so this can never
   // dominate the ratchet — it modulates, it does not decide.
   int w1 = useW1 ? StructureDir(PERIOD_W1) : 0;
   int d1 = useD1 ? StructureDir(PERIOD_D1) : 0;
   int h4 = useH4 ? StructureDir(PERIOD_H4) : 0;
   int h1 = useH1 ? StructureDir(PERIOD_H1) : 0;
   double sc = 0.0;
   if(w1 != 0) sc += (w1 == dir) ?  1.0 : -1.0;
   if(d1 != 0) sc += (d1 == dir) ?  1.0 : -1.0;
   if(h4 != 0) sc += (h4 == dir) ?  0.5 : -0.5;
   // (v17.35) M1 no longer uses H1 at all (see above), so the special-case
   // full-weight-for-M1 branch from v17.34 no longer applies to anything —
   // M5 is now the only caller that ever reaches this line, and he gave H1
   // and H4 equal standing ("1 and 4 hour"), so both carry the same weight.
   if(h1 != 0) sc += (h1 == dir) ?  0.5 : -0.5;
   score += (int)MathRound(sc);

   // Room-to-run: a long pressed against today's high has no room left even
   // if D1 and H4 both agree, and that is precisely when a ratchet should
   // NOT be loosened. Uses the forming D1 bar's extremes, which is correct
   // here — this is a "where are we now" question, not a signal, so reading
   // bar 0 cannot repaint a decision that is re-evaluated every tick anyway.
   double dAtr = iATRproxy(PERIOD_D1);
   if(dAtr > 0)
   {
      double need = T_HtfRoomAtr * dAtr;
      double bid  = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
      double dHi  = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
      double dLo  = iLow (_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
      if(bid > 0 && dHi > 0 && dLo > 0)
      {
         if(dir > 0 && (dHi - bid) < need) score -= 1;   // long into today's high
         if(dir < 0 && (bid - dLo) < need) score -= 1;    // short into today's low
      }
   }

   if(score >  2) score =  2;
   if(score < -2) score = -2;
   return score;
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.26) SIGNAL AUDIT + SHADOW P&L — "how many real trend
//  signals did we get, how many did we TAKE, and what would the
//  skipped ones have made?"
//
//  Every Supertrend flip on M1/M3/M5 is counted. When one is SKIPPED,
//  the exact gate that stopped it is recorded AND a SHADOW trade is
//  opened: same entry price, same signal-line stop the real trade
//  would have used. The shadow is tracked bar by bar until the trend
//  flips against it (or it times out), then its final R and its PEAK
//  R are written to CSV alongside the skip reason.
//
//  That turns "it isn't taking enough trends" into a table: gate X
//  skipped N signals worth +M R. If a gate is throwing away profit,
//  the data says so and it gets loosened. If it's saving losses, the
//  data says that too. No more guessing from screenshots.
//================================================================
#define SHADOW_MAX 64
datetime g_shTime[SHADOW_MAX];
int      g_shK[SHADOW_MAX];
int      g_shDir[SHADOW_MAX];
double   g_shEntry[SHADOW_MAX];
double   g_shRisk[SHADOW_MAX];     // 1R distance in price
double   g_shPeakR[SHADOW_MAX];
string   g_shReason[SHADOW_MAX];
bool     g_shActive[SHADOW_MAX];
int      g_shN = 0;
// session counters for the diagnostics line
int      g_sigSeen = 0, g_sigTaken = 0, g_sigSkipped = 0;

void AuditWrite(string tf, string dir, string reason, double finalR, double peakR, string kind)
{
   if(!T_SignalAudit) return;
   int h = FileOpen("quad_signals.csv", FILE_READ|FILE_WRITE|FILE_CSV|FILE_ANSI|FILE_COMMON, ',');
   if(h == INVALID_HANDLE) return;
   bool fresh = (FileSize(h) == 0);
   FileSeek(h, 0, SEEK_END);
   if(fresh) FileWrite(h, "time","tf","dir","kind","skip_reason","would_be_R","would_be_peakR");
   FileWrite(h, TimeToString(TimeCurrent(), TIME_DATE|TIME_MINUTES), tf, dir, kind, reason,
             DoubleToString(finalR,2), DoubleToString(peakR,2));
   FileClose(h);
}

// Called at EVERY gate that skips a real flip. Opens a shadow trade so
// we learn what that skip actually cost (or saved).
void AuditSkip(int k, int dir, string reason)
{
   g_sigSkipped++;
   if(!T_SignalAudit) return;
   double stV; int stD; ScalpST(k, stV, stD);
   double px  = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double atr = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   if(px <= 0 || atr <= 0 || stV <= 0) return;
   // the same stop the real trade would have used: signal line + buffers
   double stop = (dir > 0) ? stV - (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) * atr
                           : stV + (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) * atr;
   double risk = MathAbs(px - stop);
   if(risk <= 0) return;
   int s = g_shN % SHADOW_MAX;
   g_shTime[s]   = TimeCurrent();
   g_shK[s]      = k;
   g_shDir[s]    = dir;
   g_shEntry[s]  = px;
   g_shRisk[s]   = risk;
   g_shPeakR[s]  = 0.0;
   g_shReason[s] = reason;
   g_shActive[s] = true;
   g_shN++;
   if(InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[AUDIT] SKIPPED %s flip on %s — %s. Shadow-tracking what it would have made.",
                  dir>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), reason);
}

// Bar-by-bar shadow tracking. Closes a shadow when the trend flips
// against it (its natural exit under our own rules) or on timeout.
void UpdateShadows()
{
   if(!T_SignalAudit) return;
   for(int i = 0; i < SHADOW_MAX; i++)
   {
      if(!g_shActive[i]) continue;
      int k = g_shK[i];
      if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) { g_shActive[i] = false; continue; }
      double px = (g_shDir[i] > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
      if(px <= 0) continue;
      double r = ((px - g_shEntry[i]) / g_shRisk[i]) * g_shDir[i];
      if(r > g_shPeakR[i]) g_shPeakR[i] = r;
      // natural exits: stop hit, opposite flip, or timeout
      double stV; int stD; ScalpST(k, stV, stD);
      bool stopped = (r <= -1.0);
      bool flipped = (stD != 0 && stD != g_shDir[i]);
      bool timeout = (TimeCurrent() - g_shTime[i]) > (long)T_ShadowMaxBars * PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[k]);
      if(stopped || flipped || timeout)
      {
         double finalR = stopped ? -1.0 : r;
         AuditWrite(EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), g_shDir[i]>0?"UP":"DOWN", g_shReason[i], finalR, g_shPeakR[i], "SKIPPED");
         g_shActive[i] = false;
      }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  TWO-POLE OSCILLATOR — THE BIGGEST GAP FOUND IN THE FULL AUDIT.
//  Your own spec, and the Pine's own title bar, name three pillars:
//  Supertrend + DEMA + Two-Pole Oscillator. Pine has real Two-Pole code
//  (f_twoPole) feeding its confidence score, its size-up decision, AND
//  its early-close evidence. The EA — the one actually holding your
//  money — had NONE of it. Two of three pillars were live; the third
//  never existed here at all. This ports Pine's exact math.
//
//  Ehlers-style two-pole smoothed filter of price's deviation from its
//  own SMA, normalised by that same window's stdev. Recomputed fresh
//  from a bounded lookback every call — no persistent per-bar state to
//  desync between engines, timeframes, or a restart. The recursive
//  filter (p1/p2) converges within a handful of bars, so a comfortable
//  warm-up window before the value we actually use makes this
//  numerically equivalent to Pine's own running version.
//================================================================
double TwoPoleValue(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int len, double smooth, int shift,
                     double &tpNow, double &tpPrev1, double &tpPrev2)
{
   tpNow = 0; tpPrev1 = 0; tpPrev2 = 0;
   int warm  = MathMax(60, len * 3);
   int total = len + warm + 5;
   double c[];
   if(CopyClose(_Symbol, tf, shift, total, c) < total) return 0.0;
   // c[0] = the bar at 'shift' (newest in this window) ... c[total-1] = oldest.
   int maxIdx = total - len;             // oldest index with a full len-window behind it
   if(maxIdx < 5) return 0.0;
   double tpRaw[];
   ArrayResize(tpRaw, maxIdx + 1);
   double alpha = 2.0 / (smooth + 1.0);
   double p1 = 0.0, p2 = 0.0;
   bool started = false;
   for(int idx = maxIdx; idx >= 0; idx--)      // walk OLDEST -> NEWEST, matching Pine's bar order
   {
      double sum = 0.0;
      for(int j = 0; j < len; j++) sum += c[idx + j];
      double sma1 = sum / len;
      double sq = 0.0;
      for(int j = 0; j < len; j++) { double d = c[idx+j] - sma1; sq += d*d; }
      double stdevV = MathSqrt(sq / len);
      double dev = c[idx] - sma1;
      if(!started) { p1 = dev; p2 = p1; started = true; }   // bootstrap, matches Pine's na(p1[1]) case
      else         { p1 = p1 + alpha * (dev - p1); p2 = p2 + alpha * (p1 - p2); }
      tpRaw[idx] = (stdevV > 0) ? p2 / stdevV : 0.0;
   }
   // tp = SMA(tpRaw, 3) — need it at idx 0 (now), 1 (prev), 2 (prev-prev) for the turn checks
   tpNow   = (tpRaw[0] + tpRaw[1] + tpRaw[2]) / 3.0;
   tpPrev1 = (tpRaw[1] + tpRaw[2] + tpRaw[3]) / 3.0;
   tpPrev2 = (tpRaw[2] + tpRaw[3] + tpRaw[4]) / 3.0;
   return tpNow;
}

// Cached per-timeframe (M1/M3/M5, shared by TREND+SCALP) and per-M15 (APEX) —
// recomputed only on a genuinely new bar for that timeframe, never every tick.
double   g_tpNow[SC_TFN], g_tpPrev1[SC_TFN], g_tpPrev2[SC_TFN];
datetime g_tpBar[SC_TFN];
double   g_tpApexNow = 0, g_tpApexPrev1 = 0, g_tpApexPrev2 = 0;
datetime g_tpApexBar = 0;

void EnsureTwoPole(int k)
{
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
   if(b0 == 0 || b0 == g_tpBar[k]) return;
   g_tpBar[k] = b0;
   TwoPoleValue(tf, T_TwoPoleLen, T_TwoPoleSmooth, 1, g_tpNow[k], g_tpPrev1[k], g_tpPrev2[k]);
}
void EnsureTwoPoleApex()
{
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, 0);
   if(b0 == 0 || b0 == g_tpApexBar) return;
   g_tpApexBar = b0;
   TwoPoleValue(PERIOD_M15, T_TwoPoleLen, T_TwoPoleSmooth, 1, g_tpApexNow, g_tpApexPrev1, g_tpApexPrev2);
}
// dir>0 for a long, dir<0 for a short.
bool TwoPoleAgrees(int k, int dir)   { EnsureTwoPole(k); return (dir>0) ? (g_tpNow[k] > g_tpPrev1[k]) : (g_tpNow[k] < g_tpPrev1[k]); }
// (v17.98) THE ENTRY-LATENESS MECHANISM. TwoPoleAgrees demands that a 20-length
// DOUBLE-SMOOTHED oscillator has ALREADY turned. At the birth of a flip it has
// not -- that is what smoothing means. So the one gate whose entire purpose is
// to be early was gated on the slowest reader in the file, and eDir = 0 CANCELS
// (his "gates that cancel vs gates that defer"). The entry then waits for the
// confirmed flip at bar close: up to 60s on M1, 300s on M5, i.e. the first few
// points of the move. This is the same class as `!demaHold`: an undeveloped
// slope was being read as evidence against the trade.
// A VETO is the honest test. Only a Two-Pole moving MEANINGFULLY against the
// flip is evidence; flat or undeveloped is not.
bool TwoPoleOpposes(int k, int dir)
{
   EnsureTwoPole(k);
   double d = g_tpNow[k] - g_tpPrev1[k];
   return (dir > 0) ? (d < -T_TwoPoleVetoSlope) : (d > T_TwoPoleVetoSlope);
}
// "led the flip" — Pine's tpTurnUp/tpTurnDn: the turn itself, from a
// still-modest reading, not just agreeing after the fact.
bool TwoPoleLedFlip(int k, int dir)
{
   EnsureTwoPole(k);
   if(dir > 0) return (g_tpNow[k] > g_tpPrev1[k]) && (g_tpPrev1[k] <= g_tpPrev2[k]) && (g_tpNow[k] < 0.2);
   return (g_tpNow[k] < g_tpPrev1[k]) && (g_tpPrev1[k] >= g_tpPrev2[k]) && (g_tpNow[k] > -0.2);
}
// DEAD CODE REMOVED: TwoPoleAgreesApex had no real caller once
// TwoPoleAgreesTf (above) was removed — APEX's own usage (ApexHealth) only
// ever needed the turn-against read, via TwoPoleTurnApex below.
bool TwoPoleTurnApex(int dir)
{
   EnsureTwoPoleApex();
   if(dir > 0) return (g_tpApexNow > g_tpApexPrev1) && (g_tpApexPrev1 <= g_tpApexPrev2) && (g_tpApexNow < 0.2);
   return (g_tpApexNow < g_tpApexPrev1) && (g_tpApexPrev1 >= g_tpApexPrev2) && (g_tpApexNow > -0.2);
}
// Generic-by-timeframe versions for callers (ThesisScore, Guardian) that
// already resolved a raw ENUM_TIMEFRAMES rather than a g_scTF index — maps
// to the matching cache (M1/M3/M5 -> the shared cache, M15 -> APEX's own),
// falling back to an uncached fresh read for anything else (rare — H1/H4
// aren't currently scored, this is just so the function never silently
// returns a wrong-timeframe value).
int TwoPoleKOf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++) if(g_scTF[k] == tf) return k;
   return -1;
}
// DEAD CODE REMOVED: TwoPoleAgreesTf was written for symmetry with the
// other Tf-generic wrappers below but never actually called anywhere —
// every real caller needed the LedFlip/TurnedAgainst semantics instead.
bool TwoPoleLedFlipTf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   int k = TwoPoleKOf(tf);
   if(k >= 0) return TwoPoleLedFlip(k, dir);
   if(tf == PERIOD_M15) return TwoPoleTurnApex(dir);
   double tn,t1,t2; TwoPoleValue(tf, T_TwoPoleLen, T_TwoPoleSmooth, 1, tn, t1, t2);
   if(dir > 0) return (tn > t1) && (t1 <= t2) && (tn < 0.2);
   return (tn < t1) && (t1 >= t2) && (tn > -0.2);
}
// Pine's exact exit-evidence semantics: "against" means Two-Pole just TURNED
// the opposite way (a real reversal signal), not merely that it currently
// happens to point the other direction — matches Pine's tpAgainst exactly.
bool TwoPoleTurnedAgainstTf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir) { return TwoPoleLedFlipTf(tf, -dir); }

//================================================================
//  CANDLESTICK READ — ported from Pine (which already had this, the EA
//  didn't). Three plain, well-known patterns, no exotic pattern zoo,
//  read on the CLOSED bar (shift 1) of whichever timeframe is passed in.
//  Used two ways, matching Pine exactly: a small confidence nudge at
//  entry, and evidence for the Guardian on an open trade. Never a
//  standalone signal.
//================================================================
bool CandleBullEngulf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   double c1=iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double c2=iClose(_Symbol,tf,2), o2=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,2);
   return (c1 > o1 && c2 < o2 && c1 > o2 && o1 < c2);
}
bool CandleBearEngulf(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   double c1=iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double c2=iClose(_Symbol,tf,2), o2=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,2);
   return (c1 < o1 && c2 > o2 && c1 < o2 && o1 > c2);
}
bool CandlePinBar(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, bool bullish)
{
   double h1=iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1), l1=iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double c1=iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double rng = h1 - l1;
   if(rng <= 0) return false;
   double body = MathAbs(c1 - o1);
   double hiBody = MathMax(o1,c1), loBody = MathMin(o1,c1);
   bool bullWick  = (l1 < loBody - 1.5*body) && ((h1 - hiBody) < body);
   bool bullRatio = ((loBody - l1) / rng) > 0.55;
   bool bearWick  = (h1 > hiBody + 1.5*body) && ((loBody - l1) < body);
   bool bearRatio = ((h1 - hiBody) / rng) > 0.55;
   return bullish ? (bullWick && bullRatio) : (bearWick && bearRatio);
}
bool CandleDojiAtExtreme(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, bool atHigh)
{
   double h1=iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1), l1=iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double c1=iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1=iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double rng = h1 - l1;
   if(rng <= 0) return false;
   double body = MathAbs(c1 - o1);
   bool isDoji = (body / rng) < 0.15;
   double hi8 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_HIGH,8,1));
   double lo8 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_LOW,8,1));
   bool extreme = atHigh ? (h1 >= hi8) : (l1 <= lo8);
   return isDoji && extreme;
}
// dir>0 for a long, dir<0 for a short. true = a candle CONFIRMS this direction.
bool CandleConfirms(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   return (dir > 0) ? (CandleBullEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, true))
                     : (CandleBearEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, false));
}
// true = a candle argues AGAINST this direction (reversal or rejection-at-extreme).
bool CandleAgainst(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir)
{
   if(dir > 0) return CandleBearEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, false) || CandleDojiAtExtreme(tf, true);
   return CandleBullEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, true) || CandleDojiAtExtreme(tf, false);
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.17) TREND AGE — how many bars has price stayed on THIS side of
//  the Supertrend line, on this timeframe. Feeds the reentry-scope gate:
//  the trader wants the reclaim watch armed only (a) EARLY in a trend —
//  the risky opening bars where a shakeout is common and worth rejoining
//  — or (b) on a trend that has ALREADY proven itself big — where a
//  further reclaim is worth milking. NOT on the ordinary mid-trend blip
//  in between; that just rides or exits clean, no watch, no confirmation
//  lag paid for something that doesn't need it.
//================================================================
int TrendAgeBars(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, double stV, int dir)
{
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = 1; i <= 80; i++)
   {
      double cq = iClose(_Symbol, tf, i);
      if(cq <= 0) break;
      if((dir > 0 && cq > stV) || (dir < 0 && cq < stV)) n++;
      else break;
   }
   return n;
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.14 RESEARCH #1) LIQUIDITY-SWEEP / FAILED-BREAKOUT READ
//  A Supertrend flip is highest-quality when the flip bar first RAN
//  BEYOND a recent swing extreme (triggering the stops resting there
//  — sell-side liquidity below a swing low, buy-side above a swing
//  high) and then CLOSED BACK INSIDE. That is a stop-run-and-reversal:
//  the liquidity got grabbed, the fake break failed, and the real move
//  begins on the reclaim — the cleanest kind of genuine turn, and
//  structurally INDEPENDENT of Supertrend / DEMA / Two-Pole / ADX /
//  ER (it reads prior swing structure, not another oscillator, so it
//  cannot double-count anything already in the grade). Used ONLY as a
//  confidence nudge, never a standalone trigger and never a block —
//  same standing as the candlestick read. A tiny wick past the level
//  is NOT a sweep (source caution), so the penetration must be a real
//  fraction of ATR, and the close must reclaim the level.
//    dir > 0 (long flip):  bar[1] low pierced the prior swing low by
//                          >= 0.10 ATR AND bar[1] closed back above it.
//    dir < 0 (short flip):  mirror on the prior swing high.
//================================================================
double iATRproxy(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   // cheap ATR read reusing whichever handle already tracks this TF —
   // no new indicator handle, so no extra warmup / double-count.
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
      if(g_scTF[k] == tf) return Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
      if(Cfg[e].tf == tf) return Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
   return Buf(hAtr[E_TREND], 0, 1);
}

bool SweptAndReclaimed(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf, int dir, int lookback)
{
   double atr = iATRproxy(tf);
   if(atr <= 0) return false;
   double pierce = 0.10 * atr;
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double l1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   // the swing extreme of the bars BEFORE the flip bar (shift 2..lookback+1)
   if(dir > 0)
   {
      int idx = iLowest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_LOW, lookback, 2);
      if(idx < 0) return false;
      double swingLow = iLow(_Symbol, tf, idx);
      // ran below the swing low by a real amount, then closed back above it
      return (l1 <= swingLow - pierce) && (c1 > swingLow);
   }
   else
   {
      int idx = iHighest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_HIGH, lookback, 2);
      if(idx < 0) return false;
      double swingHigh = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, idx);
      return (h1 >= swingHigh + pierce) && (c1 < swingHigh);
   }
}

//================================================================
//  (v16.14 RESEARCH #2) VOLATILITY-SQUEEZE RELEASE
//  Gold "spends periods in suffocating contraction then exhales in
//  explosive expansion" (BBW / ATR compression precedes the biggest
//  moves). The catch: the existing ATR-floor PENALISES low volatility,
//  so it actively dampens the very first flip out of a squeeze — the
//  loaded-spring trend START the user most wants to catch "from the
//  beginning." This detects the transition the floor is blind to: ATR
//  was genuinely compressed a few bars back (well below its own recent
//  average) AND it is now expanding on the flip. Reads ATR(14) from
//  the same handle already used everywhere else — no new indicator, no
//  double-count. Used as a confidence nudge AND, when true, the flip is
//  exempted from the low-ATR / isolation penalties (those exist to
//  filter dead chop, not a launch out of one).
//    compressed:  ATR at shift 3 < T_SqueezeRatio * avg(ATR, ~50)
//    releasing:   ATR at shift 1 > ATR at shift 3 (expanding again)
//================================================================
bool SqueezeReleaseSc(int k, double ratio)
{
   double a[]; ArraySetAsSeries(a, false);
   int need = 55;
   if(CopyBuffer(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1, need, a) < need) return false;
   // a[0] = oldest (shift 55), a[need-1] = newest (shift 1)
   double atrNow  = a[need - 1];         // shift 1
   double atrPast = a[need - 3];         // shift 3 (the pre-flip reading)
   double sum = 0.0;
   for(int i = 0; i < need; i++) sum += a[i];
   double avg = sum / need;
   if(avg <= 0) return false;
   bool compressed = (atrPast < ratio * avg);   // spring was loaded
   bool releasing  = (atrNow > atrPast);         // now expanding again
   return compressed && releasing;
}

//================================================================
//  DAY CONTEXT (v13) — "understand how the day is going before the
//  trade," exactly as asked. Reads the last N completed H1 candles
//  and the day's developing range to answer two questions cheaply:
//    · is the day trending or rotating? (net progress vs total path)
//    · where in the day's range are we? (near highs / lows / middle)
//  Returns a -100..+100 bias. Positive = day favours longs. Used as
//  a small sizing tilt + a tie-breaker, never a hard block.
//================================================================
double DayContextBias()
{
   double erH1 = EfficiencyRatio(PERIOD_H1, 6);          // is the day making progress or rotating
   double dHigh = iHigh(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
   double dLow  = iLow(_Symbol, PERIOD_D1, 0);
   double px    = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);
   if(dHigh <= dLow) return 0.0;
   double posInRange = (px - dLow) / (dHigh - dLow);      // 0 = at day low, 1 = at day high
   // H1 slope over the last 6 completed candles
   double slope = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 1) - iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 7);
   int dir = (slope > 0) ? 1 : (slope < 0) ? -1 : 0;
   // a trending day (high ER) near a fresh extreme in the trend's
   // direction is the highest-quality context; a rotating day (low
   // ER) in the middle of its range is the lowest.
   double strength = erH1 * 100.0 * dir;
   double rangeTilt = (posInRange - 0.5) * 40.0;          // +/-20 pull toward the extreme we're near
   double bias = 0.7 * strength + 0.3 * rangeTilt;
   return MathMax(-100.0, MathMin(100.0, bias));
}

// (v16.41) Does TREND already hold a position on THIS EXACT timeframe? Used to
// stop SCALP doubling up on a signal TREND has already taken on the same clock.
bool TrendHoldsTf(int k)
{
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[E_TREND].magic) continue;
      if(TrendTFOf(tk) == k) return true;
   }
   return false;
}

int TrendOpenCount()
{
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_TREND].magic) n++;
   }
   return n;
}

//================================================================
//  CONFLUENCE TIER (v12)
//  The rare-stack-up sizing you asked for. Base 0.01, up to 0.05 when
//  the entire timeframe stack agrees on the same direction as the flip
//  we're taking. Returns tier (0/2/3) and the fixed lot size for it.
//================================================================
int ConfluenceTier(int signalK, int dir, double &tierLots)
{
   tierLots = 0.0;
   if(!InpUseConfluenceTiers) return 0;
   // rare-by-design: daily cap
   MqlDateTime tt; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), tt);
   if(tt.day != g_tierLastDay) { g_tierLastDay = tt.day; g_tierToday = 0; }
   bool bigTierLeft = (g_tierToday < TierMaxPerDay);   // (v16.38) the daily cap now applies to the 0.03/0.05 rungs only

   // ── (v16.74) M5 STRONG RUNG — placed BEFORE the all-three-agree test on purpose ──
   // The existing rungs all require M1, M3 AND M5 to agree. At the BIRTH of an M5
   // leg the faster clocks frequently have not turned yet, so the M5 signals that
   // start the biggest moves were getting minimum size — the exact inverse of what
   // is wanted. M5 fires ~18 times a day and an ordinary leg is 20 points (a real
   // one 50-68 = ~GBP51 at 0.01), so per-signal it carries far more of the day than
   // an M1 entry. Conviction alone is enough here; the regime must still be
   // trending, so a range with a momentary M5 flip does not get sized up.
   if(T_M5StrongRung && g_scTF[signalK] == PERIOD_M5 &&
      g_lastConfMult >= T_M5StrongConf &&
      (!T_RegimeEngine || RegimeOf(g_scAdx[signalK], g_scTF[signalK]) > 0))
   {
      tierLots = TierStrongLots;
      PrintFormat("[TREND] M5 STRONG RUNG — conviction %.2f >= %.2f, stepping M5 to %.2f lots. M5 legs are where the points are.",
                  g_lastConfMult, T_M5StrongConf, TierStrongLots);
      return 1;
   }

   // (a) all three fast timeframes (M1/M3/M5) must agree with the signal
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      double svK; int sdK; ScalpST(k, svK, sdK);
      if(sdK != dir) return 0;   // any fast TF disagreeing → no tier
   }

   // (b0) (v16.22) REGIME GATE — every size-up rung requires a confirmed TREND.
   // A range with all timeframes momentarily aligned is not conviction, it is
   // the bad-screenshot trap. No trend, no size-up (the base 0.01 still trades).
   if(T_RegimeEngine && RegimeOf(g_scAdx[signalK], g_scTF[signalK]) <= 0)
      return 0;

   // conviction read for THIS signal — the pool mean, set just above in Engine_Trend
   bool highConv = (g_lastConfMult >= T_StrongFastConf);

   // (b) M15 agreement separates the rungs.
   double m15st; int m15sd; Supertrend(E_APEX, m15st, m15sd);   // APEX handles are M15

   // ── STRONG-FAST RUNG (v16.38) — the fix for "sizing is inert at 0.01-min" ──
   // On a 0.01-minimum balance every graded multiplier (conviction pool,
   // session, hour, win-ladder) collapses to the min lot — provable: base risk
   // x any multiplier still floors to 0.01. So the ONLY way conviction becomes a
   // bigger bite here is a fixed-lot step. Tier 2 requires M15 to have caught
   // up, which on a fast, young, strong trend it often has NOT — so the
   // strongest early trends were getting MINIMUM size. This rung steps them to
   // TierStrongLots (0.02) when the three fast clocks agree, the regime is
   // trending, and conviction is high — M15 not required. 0.02 is the only lot
   // step reachable at this balance, and this is where it matters most. Not
   // counted against the rare-stack daily cap; still passes every exposure
   // ceiling in LotFor. RISK NOTE: 0.02 on ~£183 is ~4% at a 5pt stop, and it
   // auto-shrinks as the account grows (% risk falls). Set T_StrongFastRung
   // = false to keep everything at 0.01 until you add capital.
   if(!(m15sd == dir && bigTierLeft))
   {
      if(T_StrongFastRung && highConv) { tierLots = TierStrongLots; return 1; }
      return 0;
   }

   // ── M15 agrees AND big-tier allowance remains → tier 2, possibly tier 3 ──
   int tier = 2;

   // (c) tier 3: H1 + H4 also agree. The full stack. Rare.
   double h1c = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 1);
   double h1e = Buf(g_hH1Ema, 0, 1);
   int h1d = (h1e > 0) ? ((h1c > h1e) ? 1 : -1) : 0;

   double h4c    = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H4, 1);
   double h4sum  = 0; int h4n = 0;
   for(int i = 1; i <= 50; i++) { double v = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H4, i); if(v > 0) { h4sum += v; h4n++; } }
   double h4avg  = (h4n > 0) ? h4sum / h4n : 0;
   int h4d = (h4avg > 0) ? ((h4c > h4avg) ? 1 : -1) : 0;

   if(h1d == dir && h4d == dir) tier = 3;

   // (d) map tier → lot size, gate tier 3 behind proven expectancy
   double lots = TierBaseLots;
   if(tier == 2) lots = TierConfidentLots;
   else if(tier == 3)
   {
      bool proven = (Stat[E_TREND].trades >= TierGateMinN &&
                     (Stat[E_TREND].netR / MathMax(Stat[E_TREND].trades, 1)) >= TierGateExpR);
      lots = proven ? TierMaxLots : TierConfidentLots;   // recognition without full size until earned
   }

   tierLots = lots;
   return tier;
}

void Engine_Trend()
{
   int e = E_TREND;
   if(!EngineReady(e)) return;   // daily halt, news, chaos, spread, cooldown etc — still apply globally
   // one open TREND position PER TIMEFRAME, not one for the whole engine — a
   // long taken on the M1 flip and a short taken on the M5 flip are different
   // trades on different clocks and both deserve to be held at once.
   if(TrendOpenCount() >= SC_TFN) return;

   // (v16.16) CROSS-TF CHOP-CLUSTER PAUSE — the actual fix for back-to-back
   // losses. Count real TREND losses (any TF, any reason) inside the short
   // window; if enough have landed, pause ALL new TREND entries briefly.
   // Existing positions and the continuation-reclaim watch are untouched —
   // only fresh flip/pullback entries pause. This is deliberately short
   // (5 min default) so it only breaks the SAME bad patch from hitting a
   // third or fourth clock, not a general slowdown.
   if(T_CrossTfChopPause)
   {
      if(g_chopPauseUntil > 0 && TimeCurrent() >= g_chopPauseUntil)
         g_chopPauseUntil = 0;   // pause expired, resume normally
      if(g_chopPauseUntil == 0)
      {
         datetime cpCutoff = TimeCurrent() - (datetime)T_ChopPauseWindowSec;
         int cpCount = 0;
         for(int cpi = 0; cpi < 8; cpi++)
            if(g_chopLossTime[cpi] >= cpCutoff && g_chopLossTime[cpi] > 0) cpCount++;
         if(cpCount >= T_ChopPauseTrigger)
         {
            g_chopPauseUntil = TimeCurrent() + (datetime)T_ChopPauseSec;
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] CHOP-CLUSTER PAUSE — %d losses inside the last %d sec (same bad patch hitting multiple clocks). Pausing NEW entries for %d sec; existing trades and reclaims unaffected.",
                           cpCount, T_ChopPauseWindowSec, T_ChopPauseSec);
         }
      }
      if(g_chopPauseUntil > 0) return;   // paused — no new TREND entries this pass
   }

   // SUPER-CONFIDENT M15 LOCKDOWN — when M15 shows a genuinely strong trend
   // (Supertrend + DEMA + strong ADX all lined up), pause new entries that
   // fight it and only take entries agreeing with the M15 direction. This is
   // deliberately rare (T_M15LockConf is set very high, 85) so 99% of the
   // time all three fast timeframes trade normally; it only kicks in on a
   // real, strong M15 move. Existing open trades are never touched — they
   // keep running under their own management.
   int m15LockDir = 0;   // 0 = no lockdown, 1 = only longs, -1 = only shorts
   if(T_M15Lockdown)
   {
      double m15st; int m15sd; Supertrend(E_APEX, m15st, m15sd);   // APEX handles are M15
      double m15adx = Buf(hAdx[E_APEX], 0, 1);
      double m15c   = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, 1);
      double m15dema = DemaVal(E_APEX);
      if(m15st > 0 && m15adx > 0)
      {
         bool demaAgrees = (m15dema <= 0) || (m15sd == 1 ? m15c > m15dema : m15c < m15dema);
         // build a quick M15 conviction score, same spirit as the entry score
         double m15conf = 0;
         m15conf += 40;                                  // supertrend has a direction
         m15conf += demaAgrees ? 25 : 0;                 // DEMA agrees with it
         m15conf += (m15adx >= 28) ? 20 : (m15adx >= 22 ? 10 : 0);  // strong ADX
         m15conf += (m15adx > Buf(hAdx[E_APEX],0,4)) ? 15 : 0;      // ADX still expanding
         if(m15conf >= T_M15LockConf) m15LockDir = m15sd;
      }
   }

   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];   // {M1, M3, M5} — same set SCALP scans

      // (v17.47, his direct measured ask: "3min signals look very choppy,
      // about 60% are profitable which is bad — let's not trade 3min.")
      // Skips ENTRY on M3 outright. Existing M3 positions still get every
      // hold/exit protection normally; this only stops new M3 signals.
      // One line to reverse if the journal later says otherwise.
      if(T_SkipM3 && tf == PERIOD_M3) continue;

      // REVISED (per instruction): a hard M1 exclusion would have missed
      // genuine sniper entries on the fast clock. Instead, M1 stays open on
      // a secondary-symbol instance but is held to a much higher confidence
      // bar below (effMinConf) — only the cleanest, most-confirmed M1 setups
      // get through, while M3/M5 keep the normal secondary strictness
      // (the existing ADX boost). Nothing here blocks M1 outright.

      datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
      if(b0 == 0) continue;

      // (v14 #9) PER-TIMEFRAME COOL — if this one timeframe has been bleeding,
      // rest it while the others keep working.
      if(T_TfCool && TimeCurrent() < g_tfRestUntil[k]) continue;

      // (v14 #7) FRIDAY FLATTEN — stop opening new trades late Friday so
      // nothing is carried through the weekend gap.
      if(T_FridayFlat)
      {
         MqlDateTime ftm; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), ftm);
         if(ftm.day_of_week == 5 && ftm.hour >= T_FridayFlatHour) continue;
      }

      double stV; int stD; ScalpST(k, stV, stD);
      if(stD == 0) { g_trLastDir[k] = stD; continue; }
      int stDReal = stD;   // (v16.85 CRITICAL FIX) the REAL closed-bar direction —
                           // never overwritten, and this is what g_trLastDir[k] must
                           // be set from at the end of this block. See the note below.

      bool flipUp = (stD == 1  && g_trLastDir[k] == -1);
      bool flipDn = (stD == -1 && g_trLastDir[k] ==  1);

      // ══════════ (v18.18) SEVERE — DEFERRED FLIPS WERE SILENTLY LOST ══════════
      // The chase-defer at ~8590 parks an over-extended flip and intends to take
      // it on a pullback, or at market when T_DeferMaxBars expires. Its comment
      // says "so the trend is never missed entirely". The opposite was happening.
      //
      // ORDER OF OPERATIONS: g_trLastDir[k] is committed at ~8265, which is
      // BEFORE the defer block at ~8590. So on the tick a flip is deferred:
      //    1. flipUp is true, g_trLastDir[k] := stDReal   (line ~8265)
      //    2. the defer block parks the flip and `continue`s
      //    3. NEXT tick: flipUp = (stD==1 && g_trLastDir==-1) -> g_trLastDir is
      //       ALREADY 1, so flipUp is FALSE
      //    4. execution never reaches the defer block again for that flip
      //    5. DeferReady() is therefore never called again, the parked flip
      //       expires unexamined, and the entry is NEVER TAKEN
      // DeferReady() has exactly one call site, inside the flip gate that can no
      // longer open. Every deferred flip died there. His log shows precisely this:
      // "DEFER UP flip on PERIOD_M5 ... DEFERRED, not rejected" with no entry
      // afterwards -- and it explains "we barely took any trades" and the missed
      // moves better than any threshold I have changed this session.
      //
      // THE FIX: a parked flip re-arms its own flip flag on later ticks, so the
      // defer block is reachable and can resolve into a real entry. This adds no
      // new signal -- it only lets an ALREADY-DETECTED signal finish its journey,
      // which is exactly "every Pine signal must be taken".
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_DeferRearm = false restores v18.17 behaviour.
      if(T_DeferRearm && !flipUp && !flipDn && g_defDir[k] != 0 && g_defDir[k] == stD)
      {
         if(stD > 0) flipUp = true; else flipDn = true;
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] DEFER RE-ARM on %s — a parked %s flip is still live and is being re-offered "
                        "to the entry path. Without this it would have expired unexamined.",
                        EnumToString(tf), stD > 0 ? "UP" : "DOWN");
      }
      int  entryPath = 0;   // (v18.06) 0 = CONFIRMED until a faster path claims it
      // (v18.28) default to FULL size every pass. Only DeferReady() lowers it, and
      // only for the pass that actually resolves an extended park -- otherwise a
      // single extended fill would shrink every later entry too.
      g_entryExtScale = 1.0;
      // (v16.85 SEVERE BUG FIX — THIS WAS THE MACHINE-GUN SPAM HE REPORTED)
      // The old code did `stD = eDir;` on an early flip, then unconditionally
      // committed `g_trLastDir[k] = stD;` at the bottom using that OVERWRITTEN
      // local — even though the REAL closed-bar direction had not changed (a bar
      // takes a full minute to close; it cannot flip every tick). Next tick,
      // ScalpST() returns the same real (unflipped) direction again, which now
      // MISMATCHES the corrupted g_trLastDir[k] from the previous tick — so the
      // ordinary flipUp/flipDn test reads that mismatch as a FRESH FLIP BACK, on
      // every single tick, for as long as price stayed past the line. That is a
      // self-sustaining oscillation: early-flip fires -> corrupts state -> next
      // tick reads a phantom flip -> resets state -> the tick after re-fires the
      // early flip -> repeat. Observed live as dozens of same-direction entries
      // roughly a second apart, each opened and closed almost immediately — the
      // spam in his screenshots.
      // FIX: g_trLastDir[k] is now ALWAYS committed from stDReal (captured before
      // any overwrite), never from the early-flip's local reassignment. A second,
      // independent guard (g_earlyFlipBar[k]) also caps the early flip to firing
      // ONCE per forming bar per clock, so even if this class of bug recurs
      // elsewhere it cannot repeat faster than once a minute on M1.
      if(!flipUp && !flipDn)
      {
         int eDir = 0;
         datetime curBar = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
         bool alreadyFiredThisBar = (g_earlyFlipBar[k] == curBar);
         if(!alreadyFiredThisBar)
         {
            if(EarlyFlipForming(k, tf, stD, stV, eDir))
            {
               if(T_EarlyFlipTwoPole)
               {
                  if(T_TwoPoleMode == 0) { if(!TwoPoleAgrees(k, eDir)) eDir = 0; }
                  else if(TwoPoleOpposes(k, eDir))                     eDir = 0;   // (v17.98)
               }
            }
            // (v17.50) On a spike candle price can pierce BOTH sides of the line,
            // so the early-flip can report the WRONG direction — this is what made
            // M5 open a BUY when the confirmed signal was a SELL. Discard it.
            // (v17.51) The v17.50 line here was `if(eDir != stD) eDir = 0;`.
            // EarlyFlipForming() sets want = +1 ONLY when curDir == -1 and
            // want = -1 ONLY when curDir == +1 — eDir is the opposite of stD
            // by construction, because that is what a forming flip IS. So the
            // test was true on every early flip and silently killed the whole
            // early-entry path. The real fault it aimed at is a two-sided
            // spike bar: one candle piercing BOTH sides of the line, where
            // "price is past the line" carries no directional information.
            // That is what is tested now — bar range and both-side
            // penetration, not direction.
            if(eDir != 0)
            {
               double _at0 = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
               double _hi0 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 0), _lo0 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 0);
               if(_at0 > 0.0 && (_hi0 - _lo0) > 1.5 * _at0
                  && _lo0 < stV - 0.25 * _at0 && _hi0 > stV + 0.25 * _at0)
               {
                  if(GateSayOnce(k, eDir, 4, tf))
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] EARLY FLIP DISCARDED on %s — the forming bar pierced BOTH sides of the line (range %.2f vs ATR %.2f). No directional read from it.",
                                 EnumToString(tf), _hi0 - _lo0, _at0);
                  eDir = 0;
               }
            }
            // (v17.50) WICK-PEAK GUARD. Entering at the tip of a spike wick is the
            // worst price in the whole move and gives away ~3pts, which IS the
            // choppy-trade target. If price is already too far beyond the signal
            // line, skip this tick — the flip re-checks next tick, so the entry
            // simply happens once price comes back toward the line.
            if(eDir != 0 && T_MaxWickAtr > 0)
            {
               double _wpx = (eDir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                        : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
               double _wat = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
               if(_wat > 0 && MathAbs(_wpx - stV) > T_MaxWickAtr * _wat)
               {
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] WICK GUARD %s on %s — price %.2f is %.2f x ATR past the line. Waiting for a better fill.",
                                 eDir>0?"BUY":"SELL", EnumToString(tf), _wpx, MathAbs(_wpx-stV)/_wat);
                  eDir = 0;
               }
            }
         }
         if(eDir != 0)
         {
            if(eDir > 0) flipUp = true; else flipDn = true;
            entryPath = 1;                    // (v18.06)
            stD = eDir;                       // used ONLY for entry direction below
            g_earlyFlipBar[k] = curBar;        // debounce — cannot fire again this bar
            PrintFormat("[TREND] EARLY FLIP on %s — price crossed the signal line at %.2f before bar close; taking the turn at birth instead of waiting %d more seconds.",
                        EnumToString(tf), stV, (int)(PeriodSeconds(tf) - (TimeCurrent() - iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0))));
         }
      }
      // ── (v17.03) EARLY REVERSAL ENTRY — see T_ReversalEntry ──────────────────
      // Only consulted when NO flip has fired, so it can never duplicate a normal
      // entry. Sets flipUp/flipDn so it reuses the whole existing entry pipeline
      // (gates, stop clamping, registration, management) rather than adding a
      // parallel one — the lesson from the v16.85 spam being a bespoke path.
      bool isReversal = false;
      // (v17.09) MICRO-BREAKOUT — checked first; catches the birth of the leg out
      // of a range, which is earlier than any Supertrend flip can be.
      if(T_MicroBreakout && !flipUp && !flipDn && g_earlyFlipBar[k] != iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0))
      {
         double mbLvl = 0.0;
         int mbDir = MicroBreakoutDir(tf, mbLvl);
         // (v17.52) THE PEAK-FILL GUARD, on the path most guilty of it. A
         // micro-breakout fires when a bar CLOSES beyond a 6-bar range on an
         // expanding candle — so by construction the fill sits at the top of a
         // big candle, which is the exact trade he described as giving the whole
         // candle away. Checked BEFORE flipUp/flipDn and BEFORE g_earlyFlipBar is
         // stamped, so a too-far fill does not burn the bar's one attempt: the
         // signal stays live and takes the entry if price pulls back to the level.
         if(mbDir != 0 && mbLvl > 0.0)
         {
            double mbPx  = (mbDir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                       : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
            double mbAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
            if(EntryTooFarPastLevel(tf, mbDir, mbPx, mbLvl, mbAtr, "TREND MICRO-BREAKOUT"))
               mbDir = 0;
         }
         if(mbDir != 0 && mbDir != stDReal)   // only when it disagrees with the stale band
         {
            if(mbDir > 0) flipUp = true; else flipDn = true;
            entryPath = 2;   // (v18.06)
            stD = mbDir; isReversal = true;   // shares the small reversal sizing
            g_earlyFlipBar[k] = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
            PrintFormat("[TREND] MICRO-BREAKOUT %s on %s — closed beyond the %d-bar range on an expanding body while Supertrend is still %s. Taking the BIRTH of the leg, %.2f lots.",
                        mbDir>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), T_MicroRangeBars,
                        stDReal>0?"UP":"DOWN", T_ReversalLots);
         }
      }
      if(T_ReversalEntry && !flipUp && !flipDn)
      {
         int revDir = 0;
         // a sweep-and-reclaim AGAINST the current Supertrend direction is the setup:
         // price took out a swing extreme and closed back inside it.
         if(stDReal == -1 && SweptAndReclaimed(tf, 1, T_ReversalLookback)
            && (CandleBullEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, true)))  revDir = 1;
         if(stDReal ==  1 && SweptAndReclaimed(tf, -1, T_ReversalLookback)
            && (CandleBearEngulf(tf) || CandlePinBar(tf, false))) revDir = -1;
         if(revDir != 0 && g_earlyFlipBar[k] != iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0))
         {
            if(revDir > 0) flipUp = true; else flipDn = true;
            entryPath = 3;   // (v18.06)
            stD = revDir;
            isReversal = true;
            g_earlyFlipBar[k] = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);   // reuse the once-per-bar debounce
            PrintFormat("[TREND] REVERSAL ENTRY %s on %s — swept and reclaimed a %d-bar extreme with a confirming candle, while Supertrend is still %s. Taking %.2f lots EARLY, before the flip.",
                        revDir>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), T_ReversalLookback,
                        stDReal>0?"UP":"DOWN", T_ReversalLots);
         }
      }

      if(flipUp || flipDn) { g_trReentryCount[k] = 0; RecordFlip(k); g_legSeq[k]++; }   // (v13 #2) genuine new trend — reclaim budget refreshes; (v15.5) flip logged for the chop-frequency read; (v18.95 BB) new leg
      g_trLastDir[k] = stDReal;   // (v16.85) ALWAYS the real closed-bar direction — never eDir

      // ═══ (v15.5) PULLBACK-TO-LINE CONTINUATION — RESEARCH-BACKED ═══
      // The documented highest-edge Supertrend entry isn't the flip — it's
      // the pullback to the line inside an ESTABLISHED trend (no flip for
      // 15+ bars): price returns to the Supertrend line acting as dynamic
      // support/resistance, prints a rejection candle, and the next leg
      // begins. Until now TREND could only board a trend at its flip (or
      // via a reclaim after being shaken out of a trade it already had).
      // A trend whose flip was missed — cooldown, spread gate, EA restart,
      // a blocked entry — stayed unboardable until the NEXT flip: an
      // entire trend forfeited. That is the literal opposite of "catch all
      // the trends." This detects the pullback case and feeds it through
      // the exact same downstream path as a flip (same stop-on-the-line,
      // same sizing, same confidence gauntlet — which independently
      // penalises chop/low-ADX/low-ER, so a ranging market's fake
      // pullbacks get filtered by the same machinery that filters fake
      // flips). Guards: trend must be genuinely established on this clock
      // (>= 15 bars this side of the line), the touched line must have
      // HELD (close back in trend direction after touching near it), and
      // no position already open on this timeframe (enforced above).
      bool pullbackCont = false;
      // (v17.11 BUG FIX) The pullback gate was MISSING the once-per-bar debounce
      // that the other early paths all carry. Its trigger is computed entirely
      // from CLOSED bar 1 (o1Pb/h1Pb/l1Pb/c1Pb), so the condition is STATIC for
      // the whole forming bar while OnTick re-evaluates it every tick, and
      // T_Cooldown is 0. A pullback entry that opened and closed quickly
      // therefore re-fired inside the same bar, bounded only by
      // T_MaxEntriesPerBar in RateLimitOk(). That is the v16.85 machine-gun
      // shape reached by a different route. Now it shares g_earlyFlipBar[k].
      if(T_UsePullbackEntry && !flipUp && !flipDn && stD != 0
         && g_earlyFlipBar[k] != iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0))
      {
         // how long has this trend been running on THIS clock?
         int barsThisSide = 0;
         for(int pb = 1; pb <= 60; pb++)
         {
            double cq = iClose(_Symbol, tf, pb);
            if(cq <= 0) break;
            if((stD == 1 && cq > stV) || (stD == -1 && cq < stV)) barsThisSide++;
            else break;
         }
         // IMPROVEMENT #2 (v16.05): established-trend requirement lowered
         // 15 -> T_PullbackMinBars (default 10). This is the SAFE way to
         // give the frequency you asked for: it boards more of the REAL,
         // with-bias trends via pullbacks (the kind you caught manually
         // today for +5 to +8), WITHOUT touching House Bias or the
         // extension guard — the two filters that correctly rejected the
         // counter-bias SELLs that were today's only two losers. More of
         // the winning kind, none more of the losing kind.
         if(barsThisSide >= T_PullbackMinBars)
         {
            double atrPb = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
            double c1Pb = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1), o1Pb = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1);
            double l1Pb = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1),   h1Pb = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1);
            if(atrPb > 0)
            {
               // touched near the line on the last closed bar...
               bool touchedL = (stD == 1)  && (l1Pb <= stV + 0.35 * atrPb);
               bool touchedS = (stD == -1) && (h1Pb >= stV - 0.35 * atrPb);
               // ...and REJECTED it: closed back in the trend's direction,
               // in the upper/lower part of its own range (a held test, not
               // a break in progress).
               double rngPb = h1Pb - l1Pb;
               bool rejL = touchedL && c1Pb > o1Pb && rngPb > 0 && (c1Pb - l1Pb) / rngPb > 0.6;
               bool rejS = touchedS && c1Pb < o1Pb && rngPb > 0 && (h1Pb - c1Pb) / rngPb > 0.6;
               if(rejL) { flipUp = true; pullbackCont = true; entryPath = 4; g_earlyFlipBar[k] = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0); }
               if(rejS) { flipDn = true; pullbackCont = true; entryPath = 4; g_earlyFlipBar[k] = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0); }
            }
         }
      }

      // (v12c) CONTINUATION RECLAIM — the fix for "detects a buy, exits on
      // giveback, then misses the whole rest of the trend." If the watch
      // armed at the giveback close (above) is still live, still agrees
      // with the current Supertrend direction, and price has reclaimed the
      // level, treat it exactly like a fresh flip — same downstream stop,
      // sizing, and Open() call. Expires on timeout or if the trend itself
      // rolls over (handled below), so it never re-enters a genuine reversal.
      bool isReclaim = false;
      if(g_trWatchDir[k] != 0)
      {
         if(TimeCurrent() > g_trWatchExpiry[k] ||
            (g_trWatchDir[k] == 1 && stD != 1) || (g_trWatchDir[k] == -1 && stD != -1))
         {
            g_trWatchDir[k] = 0;   // expired, or the dip turned out to be a real reversal after all
         }
         else
         {
            double askW = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK), bidW = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
            // (v13 #2) RE-ENTRY CAP — don't chase the same shaken-out level
            // forever. After T_MaxReentriesPerTrend reclaims on one continuous
            // trend (counter resets on a genuine opposite flip below), stop.
            bool capOk = (g_trReentryCount[k] < T_MaxReentriesPerTrend);
            // (v14 #5) RE-ENTRY QUALITY — don't keep re-entering into
            // deteriorating chop. Require efficiency still decent on reclaim.
            // (v18.14) THE SECOND LOCK ON THE SAME DOOR.
            // Re-entry needs capOk AND erOk. v18.13 opened capOk
            // (T_MaxReentriesPerTrend 0 -> 3) and I reported that as "re-entries
            // are back on". That was incomplete: this line is a second, ANDed
            // gate, and his own live log printed "ER=0.03" on M1. A gate
            // demanding 0.25 against a measured 0.03 refuses essentially every
            // reclaim, so v18.13 on its own would have changed nothing. Both
            // locks had to open. The ER is now PRINTED on every refusal so the
            // real distribution gets measured instead of guessed.
            double erNowRe = EfficiencyRatio(g_scTF[k], T_EfficiencyLen);
            bool erOk = T_TakeEveryPine ? true : (erNowRe >= T_ReentryMinER);   // (v18.29)
            // (v18.36) a reclaim is an EA-only re-entry, so location is fair to judge.
            if(erOk && AtLegExtreme(g_scTF[k], stD,
                       (stD > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                 : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)))
               erOk = false;
            if(!erOk && g_trWatchDir[k] != 0 && InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] RECLAIM REFUSED on %s — efficiency %.3f is below T_ReentryMinER %.2f. "
                           "If this line dominates the log, the threshold is wrong for M1, not the tape.",
                           EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), erNowRe, T_ReentryMinER);
            double edge = T_ReentryEdgeAtr * Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
            // (v18.81 AH) the reclaim must be worth the round trip it costs.
            // See the note at T_ReentryCostEdge.
            if(T_ReentryCostEdge)
            {
               double costEdge = 2.0 * SpreadUSD();
               if(costEdge > edge)
               {
                  if(erOk && InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] RECLAIM COST EDGE on %s — re-entering costs a round trip of "
                                 "%.2f pts in spread. Requiring that much of a reclaim before paying it, "
                                 "instead of the %.2f pts asked for by T_ReentryEdgeAtr.",
                                 EnumToString(g_scTF[k]), costEdge, edge);
                  edge = costEdge;
               }
            }
            // (v16.97 SPAM DEFECT) The watch was cleared ONLY inside the Open() success
            // block (~line 6767). So if ANY downstream gate refused the entry — spread,
            // cost, chaos, lot ceiling, or a failed broker send — the watch stayed armed
            // with price still past the reclaim level, and it re-fired on EVERY TICK.
            // The T_MaxReentriesPerTrend cap (4) does bound it, but the counter is reset
            // by any genuine flip, so an oscillating or repeatedly-refused sequence can
            // keep re-arming and re-firing far past 4 — which is the shape of his 40-trade
            // 15:02 burst that cost ~GBP40.
            // The reclaim SIGNAL is now consumed the moment it fires, exactly like a real
            // flip is consumed by g_trLastDir. Whether the entry then succeeds or gets
            // refused, this watch does not fire twice.
            if(capOk && erOk && g_trWatchDir[k] == 1 && askW > g_trWatchLevel[k] + edge)
            { flipUp = true; isReclaim = true; entryPath = 5; g_trReentryCount[k]++; g_trWatchDir[k] = 0; }
            if(capOk && erOk && g_trWatchDir[k] == -1 && bidW < g_trWatchLevel[k] - edge)
            { flipDn = true; isReclaim = true; entryPath = 5; g_trReentryCount[k]++; g_trWatchDir[k] = 0; }
         }
      }
      if(!flipUp && !flipDn) continue;   // THE SIGNAL = the raw flip (or a continuation reclaim). Nothing else required.

      // ── (v18.06) ENTRY ANCHOR GUARD — every path, not just the early flip ──
      // This is the fix for "we bought at a peak". It does not veto the signal:
      // it declines THIS PRICE. flipUp/flipDn re-evaluate next tick, so the trade
      // is taken when price comes back toward the line instead of at the tip of
      // the candle that made the signal. Off-switch: T_AnchorGuardAll = false.
      if(T_AnchorMode > 0 && !T_TakeEveryPine && T_AnchorGuardAll && T_AnchorMaxAtr > 0.0 && stV > 0.0)
      {
         double agAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
         double agPx  = flipUp ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
         double agBey = MathAbs(agPx - stV);
         if(agAtr > 0.0 && agBey > T_AnchorMaxAtr * agAtr)
         {
            g_anchorBlocked++;
            if(T_AnchorMode < 2)
            {
               PrintFormat("[TREND] ANCHOR (measure only) %s on %s via %s — filling %.2f pts (%.2f x ATR) "
                           "past the line. Count so far: %d. NOT refused.",
                           flipUp ? "BUY" : "SELL", EnumToString(tf), PathName(entryPath),
                           agBey, agBey / agAtr, g_anchorBlocked);
            }
            else
            {
               if(GateSayOnce(k, flipUp ? 1 : -1, 9, tf))
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] ANCHOR GUARD %s on %s via %s — price %.2f is %.2f pts (%.2f x ATR) past the line at %.2f. "
                              "Waiting for a pullback, signal stays live.",
                              flipUp ? "BUY" : "SELL", EnumToString(tf), PathName(entryPath),
                              agPx, agBey, agBey / agAtr, stV);
               continue;
            }
         }
      }
      // ════════ (v18.19) PINE PARITY — the EA may only trade what the chart shows ════════
      // EVIDENCE: his Pine (CLEAN 1.4) printed BUY at ~4402 and then SELL at ~4419.
      // The EA's ticket was a BUY at 4416.118 (-6.04) — a trade the chart never
      // signalled, 14 points above the buy it should have taken, and taken while
      // price was rolling over off a swept high.
      //
      // ROOT CAUSE: the EA has FIVE entry paths the Pine does not implement at all —
      // MICRO-BREAKOUT, REVERSAL (sweep+reclaim), and PULLBACK-TO-LINE continuation.
      // The Pine draws ONE thing: a Supertrend flip confirmed at bar close. So the
      // EA was never trading his strategy; it was trading his strategy plus three
      // others that he has never seen, cannot verify, and did not ask for. That is
      // why a BUY appeared in the middle of a SELL: a continuation/breakout path
      // fired on a move the flip logic had already turned bearish on.
      //
      // His rule, set explicitly: every Pine signal must be taken by the EA. The
      // necessary other half is that the EA must not invent entries the Pine has no
      // counterpart for. This gate enforces both directions.
      // ALLOWED  : CONFIRMED flip, EARLY flip (same event, caught pre-close),
      //            RECLAIM (his re-entry/stacking, deliberately EA-only)
      // BLOCKED  : MICRO-BREAKOUT, REVERSAL, PULLBACK-CONTINUATION
      // Every block is logged so the cost is visible rather than silent — if these
      // paths turn out to be the profitable ones, the log will say so and this is
      // one input away from off.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_PineParity = false restores all five paths.
      if(T_PineParity && (entryPath == 2 || entryPath == 3 || entryPath == 4))
      {
         g_parityBlocked++;
         if(GateSayOnce(k, flipUp ? 1 : -1, 7, tf))
            PrintFormat("[PARITY] %s on %s REFUSED — %s has no counterpart on the Pine chart, so this "
                        "entry would be invisible to him. Blocked %d so far. Supertrend flips and "
                        "reclaims are unaffected.",
                        flipUp ? "BUY" : "SELL", EnumToString(tf), PathName(entryPath), g_parityBlocked);
         continue;
      }

      if(T_PathAudit && GateSayOnce(k, flipUp ? 1 : -1, 8, tf))
      {
         if(entryPath >= 0 && entryPath < 6) g_pathN[entryPath]++;
         PrintFormat("[TREND] ENTRY PATH: %s on %s — %d confirmed / %d early / %d micro / %d reversal / %d pullback / %d reclaim | %d refused by the anchor guard.",
                     PathName(entryPath), EnumToString(tf),
                     g_pathN[0], g_pathN[1], g_pathN[2], g_pathN[3], g_pathN[4], g_pathN[5], g_anchorBlocked);
      }
      g_sigSeen++;   // (v16.26) a real trend signal existed on this clock — audit counts it whether or not we end up taking it

      // (v13 #3) SPREAD-vs-MOVE GATE — the biggest hidden tax on M1 scalping.
      // If the spread is a large fraction of the move we're trying to catch,
      // the trade is negative-expectancy before it starts. This is a skip,
      // not a size cut, because a trade you can't win shouldn't be taken at
      // any size. Reclaims exempt — that edge was already validated.
      // (v17.22) THE 24/7 BLOCKER. Solve the gate and it reads:
      //     skip when  spread > 0.35 x (0.6 x ATR)  =  spread > 0.21 x ATR
      //     i.e. it blocks every entry whenever  ATR < spread x 4.76
      // Against the project's own verified constants that is:
      //     spread 0.25 -> blocks all M1 entries below ATR 1.19
      //     spread 0.40 -> blocks below ATR 1.90
      //     spread 0.60 -> blocks below ATR 2.86
      // Peak-hours M1 ATR is 1.5-2.5 with spread ~0.25, so peak PASSES.
      // Asian M1 ATR is 0.3-0.5 with spread 0.40-0.60, so Asian is BLOCKED
      // OUTRIGHT — and so is rollover. M5 is exempt via T_NeverSkipM5; M1 and
      // M3 are not.
      //
      // So the fast clocks are a London/NY-only system, not a 24/7 one, and
      // this is a hard `continue`, not a size cut. It is the most likely
      // single reason 23-hour extrapolations have never matched reality: for
      // roughly ten of those hours M1 and M3 were not trading at all.
      //
      // Measured against his own 00:00-03:00 chart — 6 legs, 19.3 pts gross,
      // GBP43.31 at 0.03 in three hours — that is GBP14.44/hour of M1 capture
      // being refused, in the session he most wants to trade.
      //
      // THE GATE'S LOGIC IS STILL RIGHT: spread as a fraction of the expected
      // move IS the correct test, and a trade whose spread eats a third of the
      // move genuinely is negative expectancy. What is wrong is that
      // `expMove = 0.6 x ATR` prices ONE BAR's move, while this system does
      // not target one bar — it rides to the opposite flip. His measured Asian
      // legs are 3.35 pts against an ATR of ~0.4, i.e. roughly 8x ATR, not
      // 0.6x. The gate is comparing the spread to the wrong move.
      //
      // FIX: T_SpreadMoveBars scales expMove by the number of bars a leg
      // actually runs. Default 1.0 reproduces the current behaviour EXACTLY,
      // so nothing changes until he chooses to change it — his own measured
      // legs suggest 4-8 is closer to the truth, and even 3.0 restores Asian
      // trading. Left at 1.0 because this decides whether the EA trades ten
      // hours a day or twenty-three, and that is his call, not mine.
      // ══════ (v18.31) THE GATE I MISSED — and it was refusing everything ══════
      // HIS LOG, repeating, with zero trades all session:
      //   SKIP DOWN on PERIOD_M1 — spread 0.26 is 37% of the expected move 0.70.
      //   Negative expectancy, not worth taking.
      // The arithmetic: expMove = 0.6 x ATR x T_SpreadMoveBars, and with
      // T_SpreadMoveBars = 1.0 that is 0.6 x ATR of ONE bar. On a quiet M1 gold ATR
      // of ~1.17 the "expected move" is 0.70 points -- so any spread above 0.245
      // refuses the trade. Exness spreads sit at 0.20-0.35 routinely. This gate was
      // therefore refusing more or less every M1 flip, all day.
      // The model is also wrong on its own terms: it assumes a flip captures 0.6 ATR
      // of a SINGLE bar, while his own measured legs run 4-40 points over many bars.
      // The comment above says as much and left it at 1.0 pending his instruction --
      // his instruction is now explicit and repeated: every Pine signal is taken.
      // So under T_TakeEveryPine this gate cannot refuse; it is bypassed entirely,
      // and the cost it was worried about is handled where cost belongs: in the
      // MAX-LOSS cut and the extended-fill sizing.
      if(T_SpreadMoveGate && !isReclaim && !T_TakeEveryPine)
      {
         double atrHere = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
         double expMove = 0.6 * atrHere * MathMax(1.0, T_SpreadMoveBars);
         if(expMove > 0 && SpreadUSD() > T_MaxSpreadPctMove * expMove)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — spread %.2f is %.0f%% of the expected move %.2f. Negative expectancy, not worth taking.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), SpreadUSD(), 100.0*SpreadUSD()/expMove, expMove);
            if(T_NeverSkipM5 && tf == PERIOD_M5) { /* (v16.72) M5 never skipped */ } else {
            AuditSkip(k, stD, "spread-vs-move gate");
            continue;
            }
         }
      }

      // (v13 #10) ATR-SPIKE GUARD — skip entries during an abnormal volatility
      // spike (usually a news candle). These give the widest stops and worst
      // fills — the textbook worst entry. Not a reclaim exemption: a spike is
      // a bad entry whether fresh or reclaimed.
      if(T_AtrSpikeGuard)
      {
         double atrNow2 = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
         double atrAvg2 = AtrRankHandle(g_scAtr[k], atrNow2);   // existing helper: current vs its own history
         // AtrRankHandle returns a 0-100 percentile; a spike is the extreme top.
         // (v18.31) A news spike is a bad FILL, not an invalid signal. Under
         // T_TakeEveryPine it sizes the entry down instead of standing aside.
         if(atrAvg2 >= 97.0 && T_TakeEveryPine)
         {
            g_entryExtScale = MathMin(g_entryExtScale, T_ExtendedLotFrac);
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] ATR SPIKE on %s — top 3%% of range. Taking it at %.0f%% size "
                           "rather than standing aside.", EnumToString(tf), 100.0 * T_ExtendedLotFrac);
         }
         else if(atrAvg2 >= 97.0)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — ATR in the top 3%% of its range (news spike). Worst-fill entry, standing aside.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf));
            continue;
         }
      }

      // (v13 #12) CORRELATED-EXPOSURE CAP — holding the same direction on all
      // three fast timeframes at once is 3x the SAME bet, not diversification.
      // Cap how many of M1/M3/M5 can be same-direction simultaneously so one
      // adverse move can't hit every fast trade together.
      if(T_CorrExposureCap)
      {
         int sameDir = 0;
         for(int qi = PositionsTotal()-1; qi >= 0; qi--)
         {
            ulong qtk = PositionGetTicket(qi);
            if(qtk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(qtk)) continue;
            if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
            if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[E_TREND].magic) continue;
            int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
            if(pd == stD) sameDir++;
         }
         if(sameDir >= T_MaxSameDirFast && !T_TakeEveryPine)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — already %d fast trades in this direction. Correlated-exposure cap.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), sameDir);
            AuditSkip(k, stD, "correlated-exposure cap");
            continue;
         }
      }

      // (v14 #2) GLOBAL DIRECTION CAP — total same-direction positions across
      // ALL engines (TREND + SCALP + APEX), not just fast TREND. Stops the
      // whole EA piling one direction so hard that a single adverse move hits
      // everything at once.
      if(T_GlobalDirCap)
      {
         int totalSame = 0;
         for(int gi = PositionsTotal()-1; gi >= 0; gi--)
         {
            ulong gtk = PositionGetTicket(gi);
            if(gtk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(gtk)) continue;
            if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
            int gd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
            if(gd == stD) totalSame++;
         }
         if(totalSame >= T_MaxTotalSameDir && !T_TakeEveryPine)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — %d positions already this direction across all engines. Global cap.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), totalSame);
            AuditSkip(k, stD, "global direction cap");
            continue;
         }
      }

      // (v12c) ENTRY-EXTENSION GUARD — the "bad entry" fix. A flip that fires
      // when price is already a long way past the signal line is chasing an
      // extended move, not catching the turn — exactly the entries that go
      // straight into a drawdown because the easy part of the move is already
      // gone. Skip it; the giveback/health exits + continuation watch above
      // mean a genuine trend that pulls back will still get caught on the
      // reclaim rather than being chased here. Does not apply to a reclaim —
      // that price is deliberately being re-tested, not chased fresh.
      if(!isReclaim)
      {
         double extAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
         double extPx  = (stD > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
         if(extAtr > 0 && MathAbs(extPx - stV) > T_MaxExtensionAtr * extAtr)
         {
            // (v16.63) Was an outright SKIP — the trend was lost. Now parked and
            // re-checked: taken on a pullback toward the line, or at market once
            // T_DeferMaxBars expires, so the trend is never missed entirely.
            if(T_DeferChase)
            {
               // (v16.81 SPAM + OVER-AUDIT FIX) The print and the AuditSkip call below
               // both fired on EVERY TICK the position stayed deferred, not once when
               // deferral started. On an active session that is hundreds of prints a
               // minute — the log spam he reported — and it wrote a "skip" to
               // quad_signals.csv that many times for what is really ONE decision, so
               // the shadow-P&L file was being flooded with duplicates of the same
               // event. Both now fire once, on the tick deferral BEGINS, using the
               // existing g_defDir[k]/g_defAt[k] state to detect "already deferring".
               bool alreadyDeferring = (g_defDir[k] == stD);
               bool defExp = false;
               if(!DeferReady(k, tf, stD, stV, extAtr, extPx, defExp))
               {
                  if(!alreadyDeferring)
                  {
                     DeferFlip(k, stD, stV);
                     if(InpVerboseLog)
                        PrintFormat("[TREND] DEFER %s flip on %s — price %.2f ATR past the signal line. Waiting up to %d bars for a pullback, then taking it at market.",
                                    stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), MathAbs(extPx - stV)/extAtr, T_DeferMaxBars);
                     AuditSkip(k, stD, "entry-extension — DEFERRED, not rejected");
                  }
                  continue;
               }
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] DEFER RESOLVED on %s — %s. Taking the entry.",
                              EnumToString(tf), defExp ? "wait expired, entering at market" : "price pulled back to the line");
               ClearDefer(k);
            }
            else if(T_ExtensionSkip)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s flip on %s — price is %.2f ATR past the signal line already, this is chasing, not catching the turn.",
                              stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), MathAbs(extPx - stV)/extAtr);
               AuditSkip(k, stD, "entry-extension (chasing) guard");
               continue;
            }
            else if(InpVerboseLog)
            {
               // (v16.93 MY ERROR, CORRECTED) I told him T_DeferChase=false meant "take
               // the extended flip at market instantly". It did NOT — the else-branch was
               // an outright SKIP, so turning defer off since v16.83 has been REJECTING
               // every extended flip. That is the "3.63 ATR past the signal line" skip in
               // his log. There was no path that simply TOOK an extended entry.
               // Now there are three distinct behaviours instead of two:
               //   T_DeferChase   = true              -> wait for a pullback, then market
               //   T_ExtensionSkip= true              -> skip it (the old behaviour)
               //   both false                        -> TAKE IT AT MARKET NOW  <-- default
               PrintFormat("[TREND] EXTENDED ENTRY %s on %s — price is %.2f ATR past the signal line and taking it anyway at market (T_ExtensionSkip off). Worse fill, but the trend is not refused.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), MathAbs(extPx - stV)/extAtr);
            }
         }
      }

      // (v14 #6) COST-AWARE MINIMUM MOVE — if the signal-line stop is so tight
      // that round-trip cost (spread, doubled as a commission proxy) is a big
      // fraction of 1R, the trade needs an unrealistically large win just to
      // clear costs. Skip those — they're the quiet expectancy killers.
      if(T_CostAwareR && !isReclaim)
      {
         double rDist = MathAbs(((stD > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                            : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)) - stV);
         double roundTripCost = SpreadUSD() * 2.0;   // spread out + spread-equivalent cost proxy
         if(rDist > 0 && roundTripCost > T_MaxCostPctR * rDist)
         {
            // (v16.92) DOWNSIZE INSTEAD OF SKIP — this resolves the standing conflict
            // between "take EVERY trade" and the cost arithmetic, which are both right.
            // The cost gate is sound: on his 29 Jul 13:22 log, cost 0.44 against a 0.94pt
            // R is 47% of risk gone to spread before the trade starts, and no win rate
            // rescues that. But SKIPPING contradicts his core rule, and until now there
            // was no third option because at the 0.01 minimum lot a trim is a no-op.
            // THAT CHANGED with the equity scaler (v16.89): above ~GBP200 the quick-hold
            // total is 0.04+, so there IS room to trade smaller instead of not at all.
            // So: take the trade at reduced size, scaled by how bad the cost ratio is,
            // and only skip when the size is already at the broker minimum and a trim
            // genuinely cannot bite. This is his own stated principle — never hard-block,
            // downsize — finally applicable because the position size is no longer pinned
            // to the floor.
            double costRatio = roundTripCost / rDist;                  // e.g. 0.47
            double costTrim  = MathMax(0.25, T_MaxCostPctR / costRatio); // 0.30/0.47 = 0.64x
            double curTot = (g_lastTierSizeReq > 0.0) ? g_lastTierSizeReq : TierBaseLots;
            double trimmed = MathFloor((curTot * costTrim) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep;
            bool trimCanBite = (trimmed >= g_lotMin && trimmed < curTot - 1e-9);
            if(trimCanBite)
            {
               g_lastTierSizeReq = trimmed;
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] COST-TRIM %s on %s — cost %.2f is %.0f%% of 1R (%.2f). TAKING IT at %.2f lots instead of %.2f (x%.2f). Not skipped.",
                              stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), roundTripCost,
                              100.0*costRatio, rDist, trimmed, curTot, costTrim);
            }
            else
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — cost %.2f is %.0f%% of 1R (%.2f), and size is already at the %.2f minimum so a trim cannot bite.",
                              stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), roundTripCost, 100.0*costRatio, rDist, g_lotMin);
               // (v16.93) THE v16.92 COST-TRIM COULD NOT BITE ON M3/M5. Those clocks use
               // TierBaseLots (0.01), not the M1 quick-hold scaler, so "trim to something
               // smaller" was impossible and it fell through to a SKIP anyway — which is
               // the "cost 0.44 is 128% of 1R (0.34), size already at the 0.01 minimum"
               // line in his log. R was 0.34pts because M5 ATR had collapsed to ~0.11.
               // His instruction is unambiguous: take it regardless. So when a trim
               // cannot physically bite, the trade is now TAKEN at the minimum lot rather
               // than refused. The cost really is unfavourable on those trades — that
               // arithmetic has not changed — but it is his call, it is bounded at one
               // minimum lot, and the shadow log will show exactly what this costs.
               if(T_CostSkipAtFloor && !T_TakeEverySignal)   // (v18.08) no signal is refused on cost
               {
                  if(T_NeverSkipM5 && tf == PERIOD_M5) { /* (v16.72) M5 never skipped */ } else {
                  AuditSkip(k, stD, "cost-aware minimum move — size already at floor");
                  continue;
                  }
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // M15 COUNTER-LEAN (v16.15): a fast flip that fights a super-confident
      // M15 trend used to be SKIPPED here. That contradicted the locked rule
      // (catch both directions on the fast clocks, never hard-block). Now it
      // is TAKEN and merely leaned smaller — flagged here, size applied in the
      // sizing section below. Frequency preserved; the counter-trend bet just
      // risks less.
      // (v17.36 — his live-test feedback: "H1 and M15 are ruining our trades,
      // only 5min should be affected, leave 1,3 alone.") The M15 lockdown lean
      // applied to ALL THREE fast clocks. Now scoped to M5 only — M1 and M3
      // are completely unaffected by M15, exactly as he specified.
      bool counterStrongM15 = (tf == PERIOD_M5) && (m15LockDir != 0 && stD != m15LockDir);
      if(counterStrongM15 && InpVerboseLog)
         PrintFormat("[TREND] %s flip on %s fights a super-confident M15 (%s) — TAKING it leaned smaller (x%.2f), not skipping.",
                     stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), m15LockDir>0?"M15 LONG":"M15 SHORT", T_M15CounterRisk);

      double atr = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1), adx = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,1);
      if(atr <= 0) continue;

      // (v12d) SESSION-AWARE ADX FLOOR — dead-session chop (late NY/Sydney,
      // Asian hours) is where the real losses come from: flips print, go
      // nowhere, and reverse. In poor-quality sessions the trend has to
      // prove itself HARDER before we take the flip. London/NY unchanged —
      // that's where the money is made and the floor stays as learned.
      double adxFloorNow = T_MinAdx + g_adxBoost;
      if(T_SessionAdx)
      {
         double sq = SessionQuality();
         if(sq <= 0.70)      adxFloorNow += 6;   // late NY / Sydney: thin, avoid
         else if(sq <= 0.80) adxFloorNow += 4;   // Asian chop: prove it harder
      }
      if(adx < adxFloorNow) continue;

      // (v12d) FLIP-BAR QUALITY — the flip bar itself must show a real
      // directional body agreeing with the flip (close-open in the flip's
      // direction, >= T_FlipBodyAtr x ATR). A Supertrend flip printed by a
      // doji in chop is the classic false start; requiring the bar that
      // CAUSED the flip to actually move in the flip's direction is the
      // cheapest signal-accuracy filter there is. Reclaims are exempt —
      // their confirmation is the reclaim of the level itself.
      // BUG FIX #65: pullback-continuation entries are now ALSO exempt —
      // a rejection candle at the line (hammer / pin bar) has a long wick
      // and a SMALL body by nature; that small body is not a doji false
      // start, it's the pattern working. Without this exemption the body
      // filter silently killed most pullback entries the moment they were
      // added, neutering the feature. The pullback's own confirmation is
      // the held line + the close in the trend-side 60% of the bar's range,
      // both already required at detection.
      if(!isReclaim && !pullbackCont)
      {
         double bO = iOpen(_Symbol, tf, 1), bC = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
         double bodyDir = bC - bO;
         bool bodyOk = (stD > 0) ? (bodyDir >=  T_FlipBodyAtr * atr)
                                 : (bodyDir <= -T_FlipBodyAtr * atr);
         if(!bodyOk)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s flip on %s — flip bar has no directional body (%.2f ATR). Doji-flip, classic false start.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), bodyDir/atr);
            continue;
         }
      }

      // LIGHTWEIGHT CONFIDENCE FLOOR — same spirit as the Pine's conf() score:
      // a small, honest number built from what's already on hand (Supertrend
      // side, DEMA side, ADX, momentum), used only as a floor, never as a
      // multi-timeframe gate. No external fetch, so it costs nothing in speed
      // — the entry is not delayed by this the way an H1 agreement check was.
      //
      // BUG FIX #44 (the real reason this engine barely traded): it used to
      // require the H1 trend to ALSO agree with the flip before taking it —
      // a much stricter, different idea than "act on Supertrend and DEMA."
      // That silently rejected exactly the kind of signal described by the
      // user: Supertrend and DEMA agreeing on M1, good quality, but H1
      // pointing a different way on a completely different timeframe. The
      // core trigger is Supertrend + DEMA agreement, same as Pine — nothing
      // else is allowed to block it. This confidence score replaces the H1
      // gate with a floor only, and it never delays entry.
      double e21T = Buf(g_scE21[k],0,1), e50T = Buf(g_scE50[k],0,1);
      double demaVc = DemaValSc(k);
      double c1c = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1);
      // BUG FIX #69: the old test was `demaVc <= 0 || (side check)` — which
      // FORCED onDemaSide TRUE whenever the DEMA read 0 (handle not warmed
      // up after a fresh attach / daily roll / hiccup). That let trades
      // bypass the DEMA pillar entirely and trade blind to their own trend
      // filter — a silent contributor to the against-DEMA losing cluster.
      // Now: an unready DEMA is treated as the check FAILING, not passing.
      // A core trend filter being unavailable is a reason to be MORE
      // cautious, never less. If the DEMA genuinely isn't ready we skip the
      // entry (below) rather than take it unfiltered.
      bool demaReady = (demaVc > 0);
      bool onDemaSide = demaReady && (stD==1 ? c1c > demaVc : c1c < demaVc);
      if(!demaReady) { AuditSkip(k, stD, "DEMA not ready"); continue; }   // never enter without the DEMA pillar available
      double atrPct = AtrRankHandle(g_scAtr[k], atr);

      // CHOP-CLUSTER DETECTOR — different from the low-ATR check below.
      // Low ATR catches "too quiet, nothing happening." This catches the
      // other bad regime: up-down-up-down clusters with completely normal-
      // sized candles that net almost no real distance because price keeps
      // reversing instead of committing. Same net-move/range-traveled idea
      // already used for the SCALP M1 chop exit, applied here at entry.
      double hi6T = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_HIGH,6,1));
      double lo6T = iLow(_Symbol,tf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_LOW,6,1));
      double rangeT = hi6T - lo6T;
      double netT = MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol,tf,1) - iClose(_Symbol,tf,6));
      bool chopCluster = rangeT > 0 && (netT / rangeT) < 0.40;

      // MULTI-TIMEFRAME ALIGNMENT PENALTY — "filter the small ones that
      // don't follow the trend." Not a gate (still want the fast ones),
      // just a penalty when this signal's own timeframe is isolated —
      // BOTH other timeframes disagree with it. A real trend usually shows
      // up on more than one clock; a signal only one timeframe sees at all
      // is more likely the small, unsupported dip the user described.
      int otherAgree = 0;
      for(int ko = 0; ko < SC_TFN; ko++)
      {
         if(ko == k) continue;
         double svO; int sdO; ScalpST(ko, svO, sdO);
         if(sdO == stD) otherAgree++;
      }
      bool isolated = (otherAgree == 0);

      // M15 CONTEXT BONUS — for M5 signals only, never a gate, never applies
      // to M1/M3. This is purely additive: M15 agreeing gives a small boost
      // to a M5 signal's confidence, it never subtracts or blocks. Keeps
      // the fast timeframes completely unaffected while giving M5 the
      // slightly broader context it asked for.
      double m15Bonus = 0;
      if(tf == PERIOD_M5)
      {
         double m15c = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, 1);
         double m15sum = 0; int m15n = 0;
         for(int mi = 1; mi <= 21; mi++)
         {
            double v = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, mi);
            if(v > 0) { m15sum += v; m15n++; }
         }
         if(m15n > 0 && m15c > 0)
         {
            double m15avg = m15sum / m15n;
            bool m15Agrees = (stD==1) ? (m15c > m15avg) : (m15c < m15avg);
            m15Bonus = m15Agrees ? 6 : 0;
         }
      }

      // CANDLESTICK NUDGE — ported from Pine's entry-grade candle read,
      // which the EA never had. A confirming engulfing/pin-bar on THIS
      // signal's own closed bar earns a small boost; a candle that
      // actually argues against the direction we're about to take earns a
      // bigger penalty (Pine's asymmetry: +5/-8 — a contradicting candle
      // matters more than a confirming one). Never blocks the entry by
      // itself; still just a nudge inside the existing confidence floor.
      double candleAdj = 0;
      if(CandleConfirms(tf, stD)) candleAdj = 5;
      else if(CandleAgainst(tf, stD)) candleAdj = -8;

      // TWO-POLE OSCILLATOR — the pillar this EA never had until this audit.
      // Mirrors Pine's own scoring exactly: a turn that LED the flip (the
      // whole reason Pine uses Two-Pole — it turns before Supertrend
      // confirms) is worth much more than simply agreeing after the fact.
      double twoPoleAdj = 0;
      if(T_UseTwoPole)
      {
         if(TwoPoleLedFlip(k, stD))       twoPoleAdj = 18;   // it led — the strong case
         else if(TwoPoleAgrees(k, stD))   twoPoleAdj = 4;    // just agrees, after the fact
         // (v16.03) STRETCH PENALTY — the flip side of the LedFlip bonus:
         // if the oscillator is ALREADY at a strong extreme in the trade's
         // direction (>= +0.9σ for a long, <= -0.9σ for a short), most of
         // the momentum burst has been spent — this is the classic late
         // entry into a move about to breathe. Same tool, other edge: the
         // LedFlip bonus rewards catching momentum as it TURNS from low;
         // this penalises boarding it stretched. No new indicator — the
         // value is already computed and cached per bar.
         EnsureTwoPole(k);
         if((stD > 0 && g_tpNow[k] >= 0.9) || (stD < 0 && g_tpNow[k] <= -0.9))
            twoPoleAdj -= 10;
      }

      // IMPROVEMENT (v16.09) — DEMA SLOPE, the cheapest real signal-quality
      // gain for "catch trends, skip chop". onDemaSide only asks WHICH side
      // of the DEMA price is on (static). But a flip where the DEMA itself
      // is already SLOPING the flip's way is a genuine trend continuation;
      // a flip while the DEMA is flat/sloping against is far more likely a
      // chop whipsaw — exactly the losing kind. Reads the DEMA now vs 3 bars
      // back on this timeframe. Scales confidence (never blocks — frequency
      // preserved), and because it's derived from the 200-DEMA it is slow/
      // robust, not another fast oscillator double-counting the ADX.
      double demaNow = DemaValSc(k), demaPast = Buf(g_scDema[k], 0, 4);
      double demaSlope = demaNow - demaPast;
      double slopeAgree = (stD == 1) ? demaSlope : -demaSlope;   // >0 = DEMA sloping the flip's way
      double demaSlopeAdj = (slopeAgree > 0) ? 10 : (slopeAgree < 0 ? -12 : 0);

      double conf = 0;
      conf += 40;                                                    // the flip itself (already confirmed above)
      conf += onDemaSide ? 25 : 0;                                   // DEMA agrees (which side)
      conf += demaSlopeAdj;                                          // (v16.09) DEMA sloping the flip's way = real trend; against = likely chop
      conf += (adx >= T_MinAdx + 4) ? 15 : (adx >= T_MinAdx ? 8 : 0); // ADX comfortably clear of the floor
      conf += (stD==1 ? (e21T > e50T) : (e21T < e50T)) ? 12 : 0;      // EMA structure agrees
      conf += adx > Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,4) ? 8 : 0;                     // ADX expanding, not fading
      conf += m15Bonus;                                              // M15 context, M5 signals only — bonus, never blocks
      conf += candleAdj;                                             // candlestick nudge, ported from Pine
      conf += twoPoleAdj;                                            // Two-Pole nudge, ported from Pine
      // IMPROVEMENT (v16.11) — RSI MOMENTUM GATE, the research-backed pairing.
      // Every credible Supertrend study pairs it with RSI (buy when ST flips
      // up AND RSI>50), never with more moving averages. The EA had RSI only
      // for exit divergence — this adds it as an ENTRY momentum check: a flip
      // with momentum already on its side (RSI past 50 the flip's way) is a
      // real move; a flip while RSI is still on the wrong side is momentum
      // disagreeing — likely a whipsaw. Scales confidence, never blocks.
      double rsiT = Buf(g_scRsi[k],0,1);
      double rsiAdj = 0;
      if(rsiT > 0)
      {
         if(stD == 1)  rsiAdj = (rsiT >= 50) ? 8 : -10;
         else          rsiAdj = (rsiT <= 50) ? 8 : -10;
      }
      conf += rsiAdj;                                                // (v16.11) RSI momentum agreement

      // (v16.14 RESEARCH #1) LIQUIDITY-SWEEP CONFIRMATION — a flip whose
      // bar swept a recent swing extreme and closed back inside is a
      // stop-run-and-reversal: the cleanest kind of real turn. Structural,
      // independent of everything above. Confidence nudge only.
      bool sweepConfirm = T_UseLiquiditySweep && SweptAndReclaimed(tf, stD, T_SweepLookback);
      conf += sweepConfirm ? T_SweepConfBonus : 0;

      // (v16.14 RESEARCH #2) SQUEEZE-RELEASE — the first expansion out of a
      // genuine volatility compression is a loaded-spring trend START. Grade
      // it up AND exempt it from the two penalties that exist to filter dead
      // chop (low ATR, isolation) — a launch out of a squeeze is neither, and
      // those penalties are exactly what blind the system to a move's start.
      bool squeezeRelease = T_UseSqueezeBoost && SqueezeReleaseSc(k, T_SqueezeRatio);
      conf += squeezeRelease ? T_SqueezeConfBonus : 0;

      conf -= (!squeezeRelease && atrPct < T_AtrFloor) ? 15 : 0;     // genuinely low/directionless volatility — penalty (a squeeze RELEASE is exempt: it's a launch, not dead chop)
      conf -= chopCluster ? 15 : 0;                                  // up-down-up-down, going nowhere — penalty
      conf -= (T_FlipFreqPenalty && RecentFlips(k) >= 3) ? 15 : 0;   // (v15.5) 3+ flips in ~20 bars = ranging, whipsaw territory
      conf -= (!squeezeRelease && isolated) ? 12 : 0;                // no other timeframe agrees — not a trend, filter (exempt on a squeeze release: the fast clock leads the launch, the slower TFs haven't caught it yet)
      conf = MathMax(0, conf);
      // SNIPER-M1 GATE: on a secondary-symbol instance, M1 is not blocked —
      // it just has to clear a much higher confidence bar than M3/M5 do
      // (which already get the flat InpSecondaryAdxBoost via g_adxBoost).
      // A genuinely clean M1 setup — strong ADX, DEMA agreement, EMA
      // structure, multi-TF support, a confirming candle — can still score
      // high enough to clear this and get taken. A routine M1 flip that
      // only just cleared the normal floor will not. This is the literal
      // "don't miss the sniper entries, but keep the fast clock strict"
      // request — not a blanket exclusion.
      double effMinConf = T_MinConf;
      if(InpSecondarySymbol && tf == PERIOD_M1) effMinConf += InpSniperM1ExtraConf;
      if(conf < effMinConf)
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — confidence %.0f below floor %.0f.", stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), conf, effMinConf);
         AuditSkip(k, stD, "confidence floor");
         continue;
      }

      // ═══ (v16.29) DEAD-CHOP FILTER — the one place a skip is justified ═══
      // He asked to filter choppy trends out. But "choppy" is not one thing:
      // a mildly choppy leg still pays £1-2 (range-scalp / chop-stall / the
      // breakeven rescue all harvest those, and they stay). What genuinely
      // costs money is DEAD chop — price rotating inside a tight band with
      // nothing behind it — where the flip is noise and the spread is the only
      // certainty. That has a specific, narrow signature, and ALL THREE parts
      // must be true before anything is skipped:
      //    1. the regime engine says RANGE, and
      //    2. Choppiness is EXTREME (>= T_DeadChopLevel, well past the 61.8
      //       range line — this is rotation, not a slow trend), and
      //    3. market structure does NOT back the flip (no HH/HL or LH/LL
      //       sequence agreeing with it).
      // A flip with structure behind it is never skipped, however choppy the
      // backdrop — that is the range-breaking-into-trend case worth taking.
      // Logged through the audit so the CSV shows what this filter passed on.
      double deadChopTrim = 1.0;   // (v16.32) set below if the dead-chop signature fires
      if(T_DeadChopFilter && !isReclaim && !pullbackCont)
      {
         int rgDc = T_RegimeEngine ? RegimeOf(g_scAdx[k], tf) : 0;

         // (v17.42) ON NON-GOLD SYMBOLS ONLY, skip a signal outright when the
         // regime engine itself says this is pure chop (rgDc < 0) — no size-down,
         // no cost check, just don't take it. Gold is untouched: it still takes
         // every trend, chop, average, or good signal exactly as it does now.
         // (v17.83) THIS IS THE BUG THAT WAS EATING HIS SIGNALS, and his log caught
         // it: "SKIP DOWN on PERIOD_M1 - pure chop regime on a NON-GOLD pair".
         // He is trading gold. The test was a literal string compare against
         // "XAUUSD", and his Exness symbol is "XAUUSDm". Every broker suffix -
         // XAUUSDm, XAUUSD.m, XAUUSD_i, XAUUSDpro, GOLD - failed that compare, so
         // the EA decided it was on a non-gold pair and applied the STRICT outright
         // skip that was only ever meant for other instruments. On gold the rule is
         // explicitly "takes every trend, chop, average or good signal".
         // GoldSymbol() now recognises the instrument by content, not by an exact
         // name, so this filter stops firing on his account entirely.
         if(T_OtherPairStrict && !GoldSymbol() && rgDc < 0)
         {
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — pure chop regime on a non-gold pair "
                           "(T_OtherPairStrict). Gold takes this; this symbol does not.",
                           stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf));
            AuditSkip(k, stD, "non-gold strict: pure chop regime");
            continue;
         }
         if(rgDc < 0)
         {
            double chopDc = ChoppinessIndex(tf, T_ChopLen);
            int    sDirDc = StructureDir(tf);
            if(chopDc >= T_DeadChopLevel && sDirDc != stD)
            {
               // (v17.25 — his direct report: "some fire and make literally a
               // few pennies... could cause loss.") v17.24 fixed dead-chop
               // SIZING, but sizing was never the whole answer: a setup can
               // have essentially NO room to clear round-trip cost regardless
               // of size, and putting 45% size on a trade with zero edge is
               // still a coin-flip against cost, just a smaller one. This is
               // a genuinely different failure mode from the sizing bug and
               // needs its own check.
               //
               // The expected move a dead-chop flip can realistically capture
               // is the SAME quantity the spread gate already computes
               // (0.6 x ATR x T_SpreadMoveBars) — reused here rather than
               // duplicated, per the standing multicollinearity lesson.
               // Round-trip cost is spread paid TWICE (in and out) plus
               // T_RoundTripLagPts slippage twice, the same constant the
               // re-entry economics already use.
               //
               // If the expected capture doesn't clear that cost with a real
               // margin, sizing it smaller doesn't fix the trade — it has
               // negative or negligible edge at ANY size, and the fix is to
               // not take it, not to take less of it.
               double dcAtrChk  = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
               double dcExpMove = 0.6 * dcAtrChk * MathMax(1.0, T_SpreadMoveBars);
               double dcRtCost  = 2.0 * SpreadUSD() + 2.0 * T_RoundTripLagPts;
               // (v17.81, his live disagreement: "the EA claimed it to be choppy
               // and I do not agree - it may have been the cleanest sell") THIS is
               // the gate that calls a setup chop and REFUSES it, and it is the
               // only entry gate that rejects outright rather than deferring.
               // His rule, restated for the third time in this project: take every
               // signal, handle chop with the EXITS, because we cannot know in
               // advance which chop resolves into a real move. He is right that a
               // refusal cannot be recovered from, whereas a small position in a
               // chop that turns out to be real still earns.
               // CONVERTED FROM SKIP TO SIZE-DOWN. The dead-chop read still
               // matters - it just sets the stake now instead of vetoing the hand.
               if(T_DeadChopWorthIt && dcExpMove < T_DeadChopMinEdgeMult * dcRtCost)
               {
                  g_chopSizeDown = true;
                  if(GateSayOnce(k, stD, 3, tf))
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] DEAD CHOP on %s — expected capture %.2fpts vs cost %.2fpts. "
                                 "TAKING IT at reduced size (v17.81: size-down, no longer a skip).",
                                 EnumToString(tf), dcExpMove, dcRtCost);
               }
               if(false)
               {
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — DEAD CHOP with NO ROOM: expected capture %.2fpts "
                                 "vs round-trip cost %.2fpts (needs %.1fx). Sizing it down cannot fix a "
                                 "setup with no edge; skipping instead.",
                                 stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), dcExpMove, dcRtCost, T_DeadChopMinEdgeMult);
                  AuditSkip(k, stD, "dead chop (no room over cost)");
                  continue;
               }
               // (v16.32) CONVERTED FROM SKIP TO SIZE-DOWN. He restated the rule:
               // he wants profit off EVERY trend, and we cannot know in advance
               // which chop will resolve into a real move — that is precisely
               // why the answer is ANALYSE AND SIZE, not refuse. A skip also
               // forfeits the range-scalp / stall-bank / breakeven-rescue that
               // are built to harvest exactly these legs for pennies. So the
               // dead-chop signature now takes the trade at minimum conviction
               // instead of passing on it. Set T_DeadChopSizeX = 0 to go back
               // to skipping.
               if(T_DeadChopSizeX <= 0.0)
               {
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — DEAD CHOP (skip mode): choppiness %.0f, no structure.",
                                 stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), chopDc);
                  AuditSkip(k, stD, "dead chop (skip mode)");
                  continue;
               }
               // ── (v16.43) SIZE DOWN IF YOU CAN, SKIP IF YOU CANNOT ──
               // v16.32 changed dead chop from SKIP to SIZE-DOWN, which is the
               // right call in principle: we cannot know which chop resolves into
               // a real move, so grade it smaller rather than refuse it. But Phase
               // 4 proved that at the broker's MINIMUM lot a size-down is a NO-OP —
               // 0.45 x min-lot still rounds to min-lot — so on a small account the
               // "graded smaller" trade actually goes on at FULL risk. That turned
               // the worst setups in the book into full-size bets and is why a dead-
               // chop day models NEGATIVE: ~62 failed flips at full min-lot risk
               // against one small move to harvest.
               // So the rule becomes conditional on whether the trim can physically
               // bite: if the risk-based lot for this trade, after the trim, would
               // still round to the minimum, the trim is meaningless and the honest
               // action is to SKIP. Once the balance is large enough for the trim to
               // express itself (roughly £500+ at these stops), this automatically
               // reverts to sizing down and taking the trade — no setting to change.
               bool trimCanBite = true;
               if(T_DeadChopSkipIfNoTrim)
               {
                  double dcAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
                  double dcStop = (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) * dcAtr + MathAbs(SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) - stV);
                  double dcLpl  = (dcStop > 0) ? LossPerLot(dcStop) : 0.0;
                  double dcCash = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY) * (Cfg[e].risk / 100.0);
                  double dcLotMin = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
                  if(dcLpl > 0.0 && dcLotMin > 0.0)
                     trimCanBite = ((dcCash / dcLpl) * T_DeadChopSizeX) >= dcLotMin;
               }
               if(!trimCanBite)
               {
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s — DEAD CHOP and the size-down cannot bite at the minimum lot, so 'smaller' would mean FULL risk on the worst setup type. Skipping instead. This reverts to sizing down automatically once the balance supports it.",
                                 stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf));
                  AuditSkip(k, stD, "dead chop (size-down impossible at min lot)");
                  continue;
               }
               // (v17.24 SEVERE — found from his live screenshots.) deadChopTrim only
               // ever fed the cf[] conviction pool, which drives g_lastConfMult — and
               // g_lastConfMult is the EXACT multiplier the earlier confidence-engine
               // audit proved is ECONOMICALLY INERT at his balance: LotFor's step
               // ladder overrides the risk-based lot with a flat tier size regardless
               // of how small `cash` becomes. So a signal explicitly flagged as
               // DEAD CHOP — no structure, choppiness >= level, the worst setup type
               // this system recognises — was going on at the SAME 0.03 as a clean
               // trend entry. His screenshots show exactly this: repeated BUY/SELL
               // whipsaw flips through one persistent slide, each one full-sized.
               // This is the same root cause as the confidence-engine finding,
               // reached by a second, independent path — the ladder override
               // silently defeats every downsizing mechanism in the file, not just
               // one. FIX: request the trimmed size as an EXACT size (the same
               // mechanism v17.21 built for reversal/re-entry), so dead chop
               // genuinely sizes down instead of being re-inflated back to 0.03.
                  deadChopTrim = T_DeadChopSizeX;
                  // (v18.03) RANGE VETO -- see T_RangeVeto. Sizing down was never
                  // enough: the note directly above documents that every downsizing
                  // mechanism in this file gets re-inflated by the lot ladder, so a
                  // dead-chop entry has been going on at full size for months. He has
                  // now said the sideways range is the ONE place he does not want to
                  // trade. So refuse it rather than trim it.
                  // (v18.32) THE LAST HARD REFUSAL. The note above records that HE asked
                  // for it: "the sideways range is the ONE place he does not want to
                  // trade." That predates the standing rule, and his recent screenshots
                  // are explicitly of choppy tape he DOES now want traded. Under
                  // T_TakeEveryPine it sizes down instead of refusing.
                  if(T_RangeVeto && T_TakeEveryPine)
                  {
                     g_entryExtScale = MathMin(g_entryExtScale, T_ExtendedLotFrac);
                     if(GateSayOnce(k, stD, 2, tf))
                        PrintFormat("[TREND] SIDEWAYS RANGE on %s — taking at %.0f%% size instead of refusing.",
                                    EnumToString(tf), 100.0 * T_ExtendedLotFrac);
                  }
                  else if(T_RangeVeto)
                  {
                     AuditSkip(k, stD, "sideways range");
                     if(GateSayOnce(k, stD, 10, tf))
                        PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s: sideways range (choppiness >= %.0f). "
                                    "Not trading rotation.", stD > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL",
                                    EnumToString(tf), T_DeadChopLevel);
                     continue;
                  }
               // (v17.92 CONFLICT-001) The exact-size request below is REMOVED. It was
               // written in v17.24 when the ladder still stepped aside for
               // g_sizeReqIsExact. v17.68 deliberately removed that deference (it was
               // the cause of 0.01 lots on a 150 account), and v17.81 then applied the
               // dead-chop trim directly inside LotFor(). So this assignment has been
               // silently overwritten ever since -- harmless today, but it left
               // g_sizeReqIsExact set TRUE while the size was NOT an exact request,
               // i.e. live state that lies to every downstream reader. Worse, if the
               // ladder deference were ever restored (the natural-looking fix if the
               // 0.01 bug reappeared), the two trims would compound to 0.45 x 0.45 =
               // 0.20x with nothing in the code to reveal it. LotFor() is now the
               // single owner of dead-chop sizing.
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] %s on %s in DEAD CHOP (choppiness %.0f, no structure) — TAKING it at x%.2f size; the range-scalp and stall banks will harvest what it offers.",
                              stD>0?"UP":"DOWN", EnumToString(tf), chopDc, T_DeadChopSizeX);
            }
         }
      }

      // (v12e) GRADUATED CONFIDENCE SIZING — the signal passed the floor, so
      // it WILL be taken (frequency preserved, no trend missed). But a
      // conf-61 borderline flip should not cost the same as a conf-95 one
      // when wrong. Scale risk linearly from T_ConfMinMult at the floor to
      // full size at 100. Weak signals stay caught; they just cost less.
      g_lastConfMult = !T_ConfSizing ? 1.0 :
                       T_ConfMinMult + (1.0 - T_ConfMinMult) *
                       MathMin(1.0, MathMax(0.0, (conf - T_MinConf) / MathMax(100.0 - T_MinConf, 1.0)));

      // (v13) EFFICIENCY-RATIO SIZE SCALING — the research-backed whipsaw
      // defence. The signal is ALREADY taken (frequency preserved). But a
      // flip in choppy, low-efficiency price action is where whipsaw losses
      // come from, so it risks less; a flip in clean, high-efficiency price
      // action is a real trend, so it pays full. Reads off closed bars —
      // no entry lag. This is the single highest-value change this session.
      if(T_UseEfficiency)
      {
         double er = EfficiencyRatio(tf, T_EfficiencyLen);
         double erMult;
         if(er >= T_EfficiencyHi)      erMult = 1.0;                    // clean trend — full size
         else if(er <= T_EfficiencyLo) erMult = T_EfficiencyMinMult;   // chop — minimum size, still taken
         else                                                          // linear in between
            erMult = T_EfficiencyMinMult + (1.0 - T_EfficiencyMinMult) *
                     ((er - T_EfficiencyLo) / MathMax(T_EfficiencyHi - T_EfficiencyLo, 0.0001));
         g_lastConfMult *= erMult;
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] %s ER=%.2f -> size x%.2f (chop-aware)", EnumToString(tf), er, erMult);
      }

      // (v13) DAY-CONTEXT TILT — small tilt with the day's character. A flip
      // that agrees with a trending day is sized up slightly; one that fights
      // a strongly rotating/opposing day is sized down slightly. Never blocks
      // (a real M1 trend can start against the day) — just a lean, so capital
      // concentrates where the broader day supports the trade.
      if(T_UseDayContext)
      {
         double dayBias = DayContextBias();
         double agree = (stD > 0) ? dayBias : -dayBias;     // +100 fully with us, -100 fully against
         double ctxMult = 1.0 + (agree / 100.0) * T_DayContextTilt;   // e.g. tilt 0.25 -> 0.75..1.25
         ctxMult = MathMax(1.0 - T_DayContextTilt, MathMin(1.0 + T_DayContextTilt, ctxMult));
         g_lastConfMult *= ctxMult;
      }

      // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #7 — REWARD CATCHING THE MOVE AT ITS BASE ═══
      // Your goal: catch the 6-16 point move "from the beginning to the
      // tip." The extension guard already REJECTS entries too far past the
      // signal line (chasing). This adds the positive half: an entry taken
      // right AT the signal line — the base of the move, maximum room to run
      // — is the best-quality entry there is, so it earns a modest size
      // boost. An entry that's valid but already partway extended stays at
      // normal size. Never blocks anything; just concentrates size on the
      // entries with the most move still ahead of them. Capped by the same
      // ceilings as everything else. (fresh flips only — reclaims are
      // deliberately mid-trend and exempt.)
      if(!isReclaim)
      {
         double extAtrE = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
         double extPxE  = (stD > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
         if(extAtrE > 0)
         {
            double distAtr = MathAbs(extPxE - stV) / extAtrE;    // how far past the line we are, in ATR
            // within 0.4 ATR of the line = catching it at the base -> up to +20%
            if(distAtr <= 0.4) g_lastConfMult *= (1.0 + 0.20 * (1.0 - distAtr / 0.4));
         }
      }

      // (v16.14 BUG FIX) AGAINST-DEMA EXPLICIT DOWNSIZING — the loss cluster
      // is counter-DEMA flips. onDemaSide is guaranteed valid here (an unready
      // DEMA already skipped this signal above, fix #69). A flip fighting the
      // 200-DEMA is still TAKEN, but sized down materially — TREND's mirror of
      // SCALP's SC_AgainstDemaRisk, and what the "reduced size" log tag has
      // been promising without actually delivering. Frequency preserved.
      // (v18.02) counter-DEMA is now a VETO, not a discount -- see T_AgainstDemaBlock.
      // (v18.30) Under T_TakeEveryPine this may no longer REFUSE the entry -- it is a
      // price/quality judgement, and his rule is that every flip is taken. A
      // counter-DEMA flip is exactly the "reversal we keep missing", and refusing it
      // is why the BUY in his screenshot never happened. It is sized down instead,
      // via the same extended-fill mechanism.
      if(!onDemaSide && T_AgainstDemaBlock && T_TakeEveryPine)
      {
         g_entryExtScale = MathMin(g_entryExtScale, T_ExtendedLotFrac);
         g_bornAgainstDemaPending = true;   // (v18.40) remembered against the ticket in Open()
         PrintFormat("[TREND] COUNTER-DEMA FLIP on %s — taking it at %.0f%% size instead of refusing. "
                     "This is the reversal entry that kept getting skipped.",
                     EnumToString(tf), 100.0 * T_ExtendedLotFrac);
      }
      else if(!onDemaSide && T_AgainstDemaBlock)
      {
         AuditSkip(k, stD, "against DEMA");
         if(GateSayOnce(k, stD, 9, tf))
            PrintFormat("[TREND] SKIP %s on %s: price is the wrong side of the DEMA "
                        "(close %.2f vs DEMA %.2f). Counter-DEMA flips are refused, not sized down.",
                        stD > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", EnumToString(tf), c1c, demaVc);
         continue;
      }
      if(!onDemaSide) g_lastConfMult *= T_AgainstDemaRisk;

      // (v16.15) COUNTER-M15 LEAN — a fast flip fighting a super-confident M15
      // is taken (frequency preserved) but sized down; the higher-loss-odds
      // counter-trend bet simply risks less.
      if(counterStrongM15) g_lastConfMult *= T_M15CounterRisk;

      // (v16.22 RESEARCH #9) VOLUME-CONFIRMED BIRTH — a Supertrend flip on a
      // VOLUME SURGE is institutional participation (real move); a flip on dead
      // volume is retail noise. Sizes the birth entry, never blocks it. Only
      // on a fresh flip (not reclaim/pullback, which have their own logic).
      // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      //  (v16.32 MAJOR BUG FIX) CONVICTION POOL — replaces a MULTIPLICATIVE
      //  chain that was silently destroying every grading factor added in
      //  v16.22-v16.25.
      //
      //  THE BUG: each factor MULTIPLIED g_lastConfMult, and the result was
      //  then clamped to [0.20, 1.60]. Stacking ~11 factors, a perfect setup
      //  produced 10.8x and a merely-good 4-factor setup produced 2.6x — BOTH
      //  clamped to exactly 1.60. So an exceptional birth (regime trending +
      //  structure confirming + breakout + prime session + HTF backing + volume
      //  surge) and an ordinary one got IDENTICAL size. The downside saturated
      //  the same way: five trims hit 0.073 and clamped to 0.20. Every bit of
      //  regime / session / structure / HTF analysis was being thrown away by
      //  saturation — the grading existed but could not express itself.
      //
      //  THE FIX: pool the factors and take their MEAN. Each factor is a vote
      //  on conviction (>1 for, <1 against); the average is the conviction.
      //  Ordering is preserved exactly — more agreement still means more size —
      //  but the result lands naturally inside the safe band instead of pinning
      //  to the ceiling, so an exceptional setup is now genuinely bigger than a
      //  merely-good one. The clamp stays as a backstop, not as the main event.
      //  This is what makes "analyse whether this trend is choppy or big, and
      //  size accordingly" actually work instead of only appearing to.
      // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      double cf[16];
      int    cfN = 0;

      int regimeNow = T_RegimeEngine ? RegimeOf(g_scAdx[k], tf) : 0;
      g_trEntryRegime[k] = regimeNow;

      if(regimeNow > 0)      { cf[cfN] = T_RegimeTrendSizeX; cfN++; }
      else if(regimeNow < 0) { cf[cfN] = T_RegimeRangeSizeX; cfN++; }
      double rsm = RegimeSizeMult(regimeNow);
      if(rsm != 1.0) { cf[cfN] = rsm; cfN++; }   // self-monitoring lean

      if(!isReclaim && !pullbackCont)
      {
         if(T_VolBirthSizing && (flipUp || flipDn))
         {
            double vb = VolMult(tf);
            if(vb >= 1.5)     { cf[cfN] = 1.15; cfN++; }   // volume surge = real participation
            else if(vb < 0.7) { cf[cfN] = 0.80; cfN++; }   // dead volume = likely noise
         }
         // -- (v16.33) DOUBLE-COUNT AUDIT: three factors REMOVED from this pool
         //    because the same information was already being applied elsewhere
         //    in the sizing chain. Leaving them meant one piece of evidence
         //    moved size TWICE, which is how a "bounded" system quietly ends
         //    up at 2x its intended risk.
         //
         //  SessionMult() REMOVED - LotFor() already calls SessionQuality()
         //    (1.20 in the 12-16 GMT overlap, 0.80 Asian). Together they gave
         //    1.20 x 1.25 = 1.50x from the session ALONE, and their hour
         //    boundaries disagreed (SessionQuality: London 8-12, Asian 02-08;
         //    SessionMult: London 7-12, dead 00-06) so the two also disagreed
         //    about what time it was. SessionQuality is older and tested; it
         //    stays. T_SessionEaseRegime is untouched - that changes regime
         //    CLASSIFICATION rather than size, so it is not a duplicate.
         //
         //  WinPressMult() REMOVED - LotFor() already applies T_WinLadder
         //    (+15% per consecutive win, capped +45%). Mine added +8% per win
         //    on top: after 3 wins 1.45 x 1.24 = 1.80x on a streak. That is the
         //    martingale-adjacent direction, and T_WinLadder's own comment says
         //    it is capped hard so it can never compound - which my addition
         //    silently defeated. The tested ladder stays; mine goes.
         //
         //  RegimeStrengthMult() REMOVED - ADX is already priced into `conf`
         //    TWICE (once vs the floor, once for expanding-not-fading), again
         //    in RegimeOf()'s trend/range call, and again in BreakoutBirth().
         //    Grading ADX a fifth time was not adding information.
         double f1 = HtfBackdropMult(stD);   if(f1 != 1.0) { cf[cfN] = f1; cfN++; }   // H1 backdrop - H1 is genuinely absent from conf
         if(BreakoutBirth(g_scAdx[k], tf))   { cf[cfN] = T_BreakoutBoostX; cfN++; }   // range->trend birth
         double f6 = AccelMult(tf);          if(f6 != 1.0) { cf[cfN] = f6; cfN++; }   // thrust vs limp - new information
         double f7 = DxyConfluenceMult(stD); if(f7 != 1.0) { cf[cfN] = f7; cfN++; }   // dollar confluence - new information

         // (v16.25) MARKET STRUCTURE — the independent second opinion. Supertrend
         // and the swing sequence agreeing is the highest-conviction birth this
         // system can see: two unrelated methods concurring. Disagreement is the
         // classic false birth — still taken (his rule), just smaller.
         if(T_StructureBirth)
         {
            int sDir = StructureDir(tf);
            if(StructureBirth(tf, stD) || sDir == stD) { cf[cfN] = T_StructBirthBoost; cfN++; }
            else if(sDir == -stD)                      { cf[cfN] = T_StructAgainstX;  cfN++; }
         }
         if(deadChopTrim != 1.0) { cf[cfN] = deadChopTrim; cfN++; }   // (v16.32) dead chop = minimum conviction, still taken
      }

      if(cfN > 0)
      {
         double cfSum = 0.0;
         for(int ci = 0; ci < cfN; ci++) cfSum += cf[ci];
         double conviction = cfSum / cfN;
         g_lastConfMult *= conviction;
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] conviction pool: %d factors, mean %.3f (regime %s) -> size mult %.3f",
                        cfN, conviction, regimeNow>0?"TREND":(regimeNow<0?"RANGE":"NEUTRAL"), g_lastConfMult);
      }

      g_lastConfMult = MathMax(0.20, MathMin(1.60, g_lastConfMult));   // backstop only — the pool keeps us inside this

      // (v14 #8) TREND-MATURITY THROTTLE — a fresh flip is full size; an entry
      // taken many bars into an already-extended trend is smaller, because
      // late entries into old trends have the worst reward-to-risk (most of
      // the move is behind you). Counts bars since the Supertrend flip on this
      // timeframe. Reclaims are exempt — they're deliberately mid-trend.
      if(T_MaturityThrottle && !isReclaim)
      {
         int barsSinceFlip = 0;
         for(int bi = 1; bi <= 60; bi++)
         {
            if(iClose(_Symbol, tf, bi) == 0) break;
            // approximate maturity by how long price has stayed this side of the signal line
            if((stD > 0 && iClose(_Symbol, tf, bi) > stV) || (stD < 0 && iClose(_Symbol, tf, bi) < stV))
               barsSinceFlip++;
            else break;
         }
         if(barsSinceFlip > T_TrendYoungBars)
         {
            double over = barsSinceFlip - T_TrendYoungBars;
            double matMult = MathMax(0.5, 1.0 - 0.02 * over);   // -2% per bar past "young", floored at 0.5x
            g_lastConfMult *= matMult;
            // BUG FIX #56: matMult multiplies AFTER the "never absurdly small"
            // clamp above — on a trade already near the 0.20 floor, this could
            // push the final multiplier down to 0.10, quietly violating the
            // floor the clamp exists to guarantee. Re-clamp here. (Direction
            // is undersizing only — matMult is capped at 1.0, so this can
            // never push the multiplier ABOVE 1.60 — but the floor still needs
            // restating.)
            g_lastConfMult = MathMax(0.20, g_lastConfMult);
         }
      }

      // Single higher-timeframe check REMOVED (bug #44 above) — Supertrend +
      // DEMA agreement is the trigger, exactly as specified. Nothing else
      // gates it.

      // PER-TIMEFRAME COOLDOWN (fix #45) — this timeframe's own last exit,
      // not the whole engine's. A genuine flip-close fast-rearms in 1 bar
      // (the market changing its mind is not noise to sit out); anything
      // else waits the normal T_Cooldown, on THIS timeframe's own clock.
      int neededCd = g_trFastRearm[k] ? 1 : T_Cooldown;
      long sinceExit = TimeCurrent() - g_trLastExit[k];
      // (v18.32) post-exit cooldown: a fresh flip after an exit is a NEW signal and
      // his re-entry / stacking model depends on taking it.
      if(!T_TakeEveryPine && sinceExit < (long)neededCd * PeriodSeconds(tf)) continue;

      // Already holding a TREND position on THIS timeframe — do not stack a
      // second one on top of it (the flip that would open a new one is the
      // same flip that closes the old one via the Supertrend-flip exit).
      bool already = false;
      for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
      {
         ulong tk2 = PositionGetTicket(i);
         if(tk2 == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk2)) continue;
         if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[E_TREND].magic) continue;
         if(TrendTFOf(tk2) == k) { already = true; break; }
      }
      if(already) continue;

      double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
      double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);
      double slM, tpM; AdaptMults(e, slM, tpM);

      // DEMA — grades the trade, exactly like Pine. Never blocks the flip.
      // (v16.14) demaReady is already guaranteed true here (an unready DEMA
      // skipped this signal above, fix #69), so demaV > 0 always. The old
      // `demaV <= 0 || ...` force-true-on-zero pattern — the exact shape of
      // bug #69 — is removed so it can never silently resurface if the skip
      // above is ever refactored. onDemaSide (computed above) is the source
      // of truth for the side; this just mirrors it for the log tag.
      double demaV = DemaValSc(k);
      double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1);
      bool withDema = (stD==1 ? c1 > demaV : c1 < demaV);
      string demaTag = withDema ? "with DEMA" : "against DEMA (reduced size)";

      if(flipUp)
      {
         // STOP ON THE SIGNAL LINE, exactly as specified — the Supertrend
         // value on THIS timeframe, plus a small noise buffer (fix #46) so
         // an ordinary wick doesn't clip a still-correct trade. Still tight,
         // still anchored to the line — just not razor-thin against it.
         // (v12) ENTRY BREATHING ROOM: initial stop uses (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf)
         // — a wider buffer at the moment of entry to avoid the "opens, dips, stops
         // out, then trend continues" pattern. The trail in ManagePosition ratchets
         // this back to just T_SlNoiseBuf as the trade proves itself. Position size
         // shrinks proportionally in LotFor so cash risk is unchanged.
         double sl = stV - (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) * (1.0 + g_fxBufBoost) * atr * slM;   // (v16) forensics-learned widening
         if(GapCooling(k, tf)) continue;                                  // (v16.58) post-gap: no new entries
         // (v17.74) THE GATES NOW DELAY, THEY DO NOT CANCEL. His two complaints
         // are the same complaint from opposite ends: "no signal should be missed"
         // and "stop entering at peaks". A gate that does `continue` satisfies the
         // second by breaking the first — the flip is consumed on this tick
         // (g_trLastDir is updated at the bottom of the loop) so a signal blocked
         // by the candle or mean gate was gone for good, which is why entries are
         // both late AND missing.
         // Parking the flip instead keeps it alive for T_DeferMaxBars: the moment
         // the bar closes properly or price comes back toward the DEMA, the SAME
         // signal fires. Nothing is thrown away and nothing is bought at a peak.
         if(!EntryCandleOk(tf, k, 1)) { DeferFlip(k, 1, stV); continue; }
         if(!EntryNearMean(k, 1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK))) { DeferFlip(k, 1, stV); continue; }
         g_premiumSetup = PremiumSetup(1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));
         g_strongBirth  = StrongBirthScore(tf, k, 1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));
         g_atExtreme    = AtLegExtreme(tf, 1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));   // (v18.47)
         g_legFrac      = LegFracOf(tf, 1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));      // (v18.73 T)
         g_wickAgainst  = CandleWonByOtherSide(tf, 1);                                   // (v18.73 V)
         g_entryDir     = 1;
         g_chopSizeDown = false;                           // (v17.61) not into a big down candle
         sl = FloorStopAtBirth(1, ask, sl);                                               // (v18.07)
         // ══ (v19.03 BL) BUG FIX, MINE, FROM v18.92 AND v19.01 ══
         // g_wpShrink and g_spikeCut are set HERE and consumed inside LotFor(),
         // which only runs if Open() is actually reached. Several gates below can
         // `continue` first -- the leg guard, the dup guard, the rate limit. When
         // that happened the flag survived and shrank the size of the NEXT entry,
         // on a different clock, for a reason that no longer applied. Cleared at
         // the top of every evaluation so a flag can only ever describe this trade.
         g_wpShrink = 1.0; g_spikeCut = false;
         sl = CapStopToCash(ask, sl, 1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN));  // (v16.58) bound risk
         // ══════ (v18.92 AT) WICK-PROOF STOP — see the note at T_WickProofTrend ══════
         if(T_WickProofTrend && T_WickProofBands > 0.0)
         {
            double wpBand = NoiseFloorPts(atr) * T_WickProofBands;
            double wpOld  = ask - sl;
            double wpSl   = stV - wpBand;                  // one band BELOW the line
            if(wpBand > 0.0 && wpSl < sl && wpOld > 0.0)
            {
               double wpNew = ask - wpSl;
               double ratio = wpOld / wpNew;
               // ══════ (v19.04 BS) HIS FLOOR IS THE FLOOR, NOT THE BROKER'S ══════
               // HIS TAPE, 25 Aug 11:57: a SELL filled at 0.01 lots. His standing rule
               // is 0.02 minimum on every entry and he has corrected it three times.
               // Earlier fills the same day show 0.01 too (+2.55, +1.68, -1.41).
               // WHY. LotFor() clamps baseLots to his 0.02/0.05 and the comment there
               // says "no flag and no multiplier can push size outside the range he
               // set" -- then TWO multipliers below that clamp do exactly that:
               // g_entryExtScale (as low as 0.10) and g_wpShrink. After them the only
               // remaining guard is the BROKER minimum, 0.01. So 0.02 x 0.66 = 0.013,
               // rounds to 0.01, and ships. Implementation contradicts documented
               // intent, which makes this a BUG FIX, not a strategy change.
               // WHY FIX IT HERE AND NOT BY CLAMPING AT THE END. Clamping the result
               // back up to 0.02 would keep the WIDER stop at DOUBLE the cash risk --
               // the wick-proof stop only works because the size shrinks to pay for
               // it. This gate already asks "can the size shrink enough to afford the
               // wider stop?" and answers it against the wrong number. Asked against
               // HIS floor, a shrink that would breach 0.02 simply means the wider
               // stop is not affordable, so it is not taken: the stop stays on the
               // line and the entry stays at 0.02. His floor AND constant risk, both.
               // AUDITED: this can only ever make an entry BIGGER (0.01 -> 0.02) with
               // a tighter stop, so cash at risk per trade is unchanged or lower. It
               // cannot touch the size of any winner already at 0.02+.
               // OFF-SWITCH: T_WickProofRespectsFloor = false restores v19.03.
               double lotMin = (T_WickProofRespectsFloor && T_LotFloorHard > 0.0)
                               ? T_LotFloorHard
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
               // only take the wider stop if the size can actually shrink to pay
               // for it. At the broker floor there is nothing left to give back.
               // (v18.97) COMPILE FIX: LotFor is LotFor(engine, stopDistance). I called it
               // with one argument in v18.92 and it never built. Passing the NEW stop
               // distance is also the correct question to ask: "at this wider stop, is
               // there still a lot size above the broker floor?"
               if(LotFor(e, wpNew) * ratio >= lotMin - 1e-9)
               {
                  PrintFormat("[WICK-PROOF] BUY stop %.2f -> %.2f (line %.2f, band %.2f). "
                              "Was %.2f pts from entry, now %.2f. Lots scale x%.2f so the money at risk does not move.",
                              sl, wpSl, stV, wpBand, wpOld, wpNew, ratio);
                  sl = wpSl;
                  g_wpShrink = ratio;
               }
               else
                  PrintFormat("[WICK-PROOF] BUY stood down — already at the %.2f lot floor, so a %.2f pt "
                              "wider stop would raise real risk instead of holding it flat. Raise base size first.",
                              lotMin, wpNew - wpOld);
            }
         }
         double d  = ask - sl;
         // (v19.03 BM) is the spread eating the move? See T_WideSpreadSizeCut.
         if(T_WideSpreadSizeCut)
         {
            double atrSp = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
            double expSp = 0.6 * atrSp * MathMax(1.0, T_SpreadMoveBars);
            if(expSp > 0.0 && SpreadUSD() > T_MaxSpreadPctMove * expSp)
            {
               PrintFormat("[SIZE] WIDE SPREAD: %.2f pts against an expected move of %.2f. "
                           "Signal still taken, size cut to the floor.", SpreadUSD(), expSp);
               g_spikeCut = true;
            }
         }
         // (v19.01 BH) is the trigger bar wider than the stop? See T_SpikeBarSizeCut.
         if(T_SpikeBarSizeCut && d > 0.0)
         {
            double sbH = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1), sbL = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
            if(sbH > 0.0 && sbL > 0.0 && (sbH - sbL) > d) g_spikeCut = true;
         }
         if(d <= 0.15*atr) continue;   // degenerate stop only — never rejected for width
         double tp = InpRideToFlip ? 0.0 : ask + T_Tp3R * d * tpM;
         // (v12) CONFLUENCE TIER — the rare stack-up sizing.
         double tierLots = 0.0;
         int tier = ConfluenceTier(k, 1, tierLots);
         g_lastTier = tier;
         g_lastTierSizeReq = tierLots;
         string tierTag = (tier == 3) ? " | ★★★ TIER 3 stack (M1/M3/M5+M15+H1+H4)"
                       : (tier == 2) ? " | ★★ TIER 2 (M1/M3/M5+M15)" : "";
         string why = StringFormat("%s %s, %s, ADX %.1f, conf %.0f/100, %s%s",
                       EnumToString(tf), pullbackCont ? "PULLBACK-CONTINUATION (line held in established uptrend)" : "Supertrend flip UP", demaTag, adx, conf,
                       InpRideToFlip ? "ride to opposite signal" : StringFormat("R:R 1:%.1f", T_Tp3R), tierTag);
         g_lastEntryK = k;   // (fix #55) so Open()'s entry snapshot reads THIS timeframe, not M5
         g_sigTaken++;   // (v16.26) audit: this signal was actually TAKEN
         // (v16.88) M1 QUICK-HOLD — see T_M1QuickHold. Reuses the existing tier-size
         // override; never SHRINKS a tier-sized entry, only grows a normal one, so
         // it stacks safely with confluence tiers rather than fighting them.
         // (v17.03) a reversal attempt takes its own fixed small size, checked FIRST.
         if(isReversal && T_ReversalLots > 0.0)
         {
            // (v18.89 AO) smallest size available, but never below his tier floor.
            double revLots = T_ReversalLots;
            if(T_ReversalLotsUseTier)
            {
               double rLo, rHi; BalanceTier(rLo, rHi);
               if(rLo > revLots) revLots = rLo;
            }
            g_lastTierSizeReq = revLots; g_sizeReqIsExact = true;
         }
         // (v16.99) A RE-ENTRY IS NOT A FRESH SIGNAL — size it as a second attempt.
         else if(isReclaim && T_ReentryLots > 0.0)
            { g_lastTierSizeReq = T_ReentryLots; g_sizeReqIsExact = true; }    // (v17.21) exact, may shrink
         else if(T_M1QuickHold && tf == PERIOD_M1 && g_lastTierSizeReq <= 0.0)
         {
            double qhB = 0.0, qhH = 0.0;
            double qhTot = QuickHoldSize(qhB, qhH);   // (v16.89) scales with equity
            if(qhTot > 0.0) g_lastTierSizeReq = qhTot;
         }
         // (v17.10) M3/M5 ride the SAME step ladder, so all three clocks scale
         // together instead of M3/M5 sitting on a fixed TierBaseLots forever.
         else if(T_StepScaling && g_lastTierSizeReq <= 0.0)
         {
            double sB = 0.0, sH = 0.0;
            double sTot = QuickHoldSize(sB, sH);
            if(sTot > 0.0) g_lastTierSizeReq = sTot;
         }
         if(!T_TakeEveryPine && !RateLimitOk(k, tf)) continue;   // (v18.32) timing guard, bypassed by the rule
         // (v18.95 BB) one entry event per leg — see the note at T_OneEntryPerLeg
         if(T_OneEntryPerLeg && g_legDone[k] == g_legSeq[k] && g_legSeq[k] > 0 &&
            (long)(TimeCurrent() - g_legDoneT[k]) > (long)T_LegStackSecs &&
            !LegHasExtended(k, 1, e))   // (v19.04 BP) flat + still extending = participation
         {
            PrintFormat("[LEG GUARD] BUY on %s refused — this clock already opened in this "
                        "Supertrend leg. Waiting for the next flip. Pine shows one arrow per "
                        "flip; a second entry inside the same leg is the EA re-deciding, not a signal.",
                        EnumToString(tf));
            continue;
         }
         if(Open(e, 1, sl, tp, why, "TREND-" + StringSubstr(EnumToString(tf),6)))
         {
            RateLimitRecord(k, tf);                       // (v16.96)
            // (v18.96 BC) stamp the time only when this leg is FIRST committed, so the
            // opening burst shares one timestamp and the guard cannot eat the stack.
            if(g_legDone[k] != g_legSeq[k]) g_legDoneT[k] = TimeCurrent();
            g_legDone[k] = g_legSeq[k];                   // (v18.95 BB) this leg is now committed
            g_legEntryPx[k] = (PositionSelectByTicket(PosOf(e))
                               ? PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));   // (v19.04 BP)
            g_trFastRearm[k] = false;   // consumed — back to the normal per-timeframe cooldown after this one
            g_trWatchDir[k] = 0;        // (v12c) consumed — whichever path opened this, the watch is done
            if(g_lastTier >= 2) g_tierToday++;
            g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0;
            for(int q = PositionsTotal()-1; q >= 0; q--)
            {
               ulong nt = PositionGetTicket(q);
               if(nt==0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(nt)) continue;
               if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_TREND].magic &&
                  (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME) >= TimeCurrent()-2)
               { StoreTrendTF(nt, k); RecordSignalPrice(k, PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)); break; }
            }
         }
         else { g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0; }
      }
      else if(flipDn)
      {
         double sl = stV + (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) * (1.0 + g_fxBufBoost) * atr * slM;   // (v12) entry breathing room, symmetric; (v16) forensics-learned widening
         if(GapCooling(k, tf)) continue;                                  // (v16.58) post-gap: no new entries
         if(!EntryCandleOk(tf, k, -1)) { DeferFlip(k, -1, stV); continue; }
         if(!EntryNearMean(k, -1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID))) { DeferFlip(k, -1, stV); continue; }
         g_premiumSetup = PremiumSetup(-1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));
         g_strongBirth  = StrongBirthScore(tf, k, -1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));
         g_atExtreme    = AtLegExtreme(tf, -1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));  // (v18.47)
         g_legFrac      = LegFracOf(tf, -1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));     // (v18.73 T)
         g_wickAgainst  = CandleWonByOtherSide(tf, -1);                                  // (v18.73 V)
         g_entryDir     = -1;
         g_chopSizeDown = false;                          // (v17.61) not into a big up candle
         sl = FloorStopAtBirth(-1, bid, sl);                                              // (v18.07)
         // ══ (v19.03 BL) BUG FIX, MINE, FROM v18.92 AND v19.01 ══
         // g_wpShrink and g_spikeCut are set HERE and consumed inside LotFor(),
         // which only runs if Open() is actually reached. Several gates below can
         // `continue` first -- the leg guard, the dup guard, the rate limit. When
         // that happened the flag survived and shrank the size of the NEXT entry,
         // on a different clock, for a reason that no longer applied. Cleared at
         // the top of every evaluation so a flag can only ever describe this trade.
         g_wpShrink = 1.0; g_spikeCut = false;
         sl = CapStopToCash(bid, sl, -1, SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN));  // (v16.58) bound risk
         // ══════ (v18.92 AT) WICK-PROOF STOP — see the note at T_WickProofTrend ══════
         if(T_WickProofTrend && T_WickProofBands > 0.0)
         {
            double wpBand = NoiseFloorPts(atr) * T_WickProofBands;
            double wpOld  = sl - bid;
            double wpSl   = stV + wpBand;                  // one band ABOVE the line
            if(wpBand > 0.0 && wpSl > sl && wpOld > 0.0)
            {
               double wpNew = wpSl - bid;
               double ratio = wpOld / wpNew;
               // ══════ (v19.04 BS) HIS FLOOR IS THE FLOOR, NOT THE BROKER'S ══════
               // HIS TAPE, 25 Aug 11:57: a SELL filled at 0.01 lots. His standing rule
               // is 0.02 minimum on every entry and he has corrected it three times.
               // Earlier fills the same day show 0.01 too (+2.55, +1.68, -1.41).
               // WHY. LotFor() clamps baseLots to his 0.02/0.05 and the comment there
               // says "no flag and no multiplier can push size outside the range he
               // set" -- then TWO multipliers below that clamp do exactly that:
               // g_entryExtScale (as low as 0.10) and g_wpShrink. After them the only
               // remaining guard is the BROKER minimum, 0.01. So 0.02 x 0.66 = 0.013,
               // rounds to 0.01, and ships. Implementation contradicts documented
               // intent, which makes this a BUG FIX, not a strategy change.
               // WHY FIX IT HERE AND NOT BY CLAMPING AT THE END. Clamping the result
               // back up to 0.02 would keep the WIDER stop at DOUBLE the cash risk --
               // the wick-proof stop only works because the size shrinks to pay for
               // it. This gate already asks "can the size shrink enough to afford the
               // wider stop?" and answers it against the wrong number. Asked against
               // HIS floor, a shrink that would breach 0.02 simply means the wider
               // stop is not affordable, so it is not taken: the stop stays on the
               // line and the entry stays at 0.02. His floor AND constant risk, both.
               // AUDITED: this can only ever make an entry BIGGER (0.01 -> 0.02) with
               // a tighter stop, so cash at risk per trade is unchanged or lower. It
               // cannot touch the size of any winner already at 0.02+.
               // OFF-SWITCH: T_WickProofRespectsFloor = false restores v19.03.
               double lotMin = (T_WickProofRespectsFloor && T_LotFloorHard > 0.0)
                               ? T_LotFloorHard
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
               // (v18.97) COMPILE FIX: LotFor is LotFor(engine, stopDistance). I called it
               // with one argument in v18.92 and it never built. Passing the NEW stop
               // distance is also the correct question to ask: "at this wider stop, is
               // there still a lot size above the broker floor?"
               if(LotFor(e, wpNew) * ratio >= lotMin - 1e-9)
               {
                  PrintFormat("[WICK-PROOF] SELL stop %.2f -> %.2f (line %.2f, band %.2f). "
                              "Was %.2f pts from entry, now %.2f. Lots scale x%.2f so the money at risk does not move.",
                              sl, wpSl, stV, wpBand, wpOld, wpNew, ratio);
                  sl = wpSl;
                  g_wpShrink = ratio;
               }
               else
                  PrintFormat("[WICK-PROOF] SELL stood down — already at the %.2f lot floor, so a %.2f pt "
                              "wider stop would raise real risk instead of holding it flat. Raise base size first.",
                              lotMin, wpNew - wpOld);
            }
         }
         double d  = sl - bid;
         // (v19.03 BM) is the spread eating the move? See T_WideSpreadSizeCut.
         if(T_WideSpreadSizeCut)
         {
            double atrSp = Buf(g_scAtr[k], 0, 1);
            double expSp = 0.6 * atrSp * MathMax(1.0, T_SpreadMoveBars);
            if(expSp > 0.0 && SpreadUSD() > T_MaxSpreadPctMove * expSp)
            {
               PrintFormat("[SIZE] WIDE SPREAD: %.2f pts against an expected move of %.2f. "
                           "Signal still taken, size cut to the floor.", SpreadUSD(), expSp);
               g_spikeCut = true;
            }
         }
         // (v19.01 BH) is the trigger bar wider than the stop? See T_SpikeBarSizeCut.
         if(T_SpikeBarSizeCut && d > 0.0)
         {
            double sbH = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, 1), sbL = iLow(_Symbol, tf, 1);
            if(sbH > 0.0 && sbL > 0.0 && (sbH - sbL) > d) g_spikeCut = true;
         }
         if(d <= 0.15*atr) continue;
         double tp = InpRideToFlip ? 0.0 : bid - T_Tp3R * d * tpM;
         // (v12) CONFLUENCE TIER — the rare stack-up sizing.
         double tierLotsD = 0.0;
         int tierD = ConfluenceTier(k, -1, tierLotsD);
         g_lastTier = tierD;
         g_lastTierSizeReq = tierLotsD;
         string tierTagD = (tierD == 3) ? " | ★★★ TIER 3 stack (M1/M3/M5+M15+H1+H4)"
                        : (tierD == 2) ? " | ★★ TIER 2 (M1/M3/M5+M15)" : "";
         string why = StringFormat("%s %s, %s, ADX %.1f, conf %.0f/100, %s%s",
                       EnumToString(tf), pullbackCont ? "PULLBACK-CONTINUATION (line held in established downtrend)" : "Supertrend flip DOWN", demaTag, adx, conf,
                       InpRideToFlip ? "ride to opposite signal" : StringFormat("R:R 1:%.1f", T_Tp3R), tierTagD);
         g_lastEntryK = k;   // (fix #55) so Open()'s entry snapshot reads THIS timeframe, not M5
         g_sigTaken++;   // (v16.26) audit: this signal was actually TAKEN
         // (v16.88) M1 QUICK-HOLD — see T_M1QuickHold. Reuses the existing tier-size
         // override; never SHRINKS a tier-sized entry, only grows a normal one, so
         // it stacks safely with confluence tiers rather than fighting them.
         // (v17.03) a reversal attempt takes its own fixed small size, checked FIRST.
         if(isReversal && T_ReversalLots > 0.0)
         {
            // (v18.89 AO) smallest size available, but never below his tier floor.
            double revLots = T_ReversalLots;
            if(T_ReversalLotsUseTier)
            {
               double rLo, rHi; BalanceTier(rLo, rHi);
               if(rLo > revLots) revLots = rLo;
            }
            g_lastTierSizeReq = revLots; g_sizeReqIsExact = true;
         }
         // (v16.99) A RE-ENTRY IS NOT A FRESH SIGNAL — size it as a second attempt.
         else if(isReclaim && T_ReentryLots > 0.0)
            { g_lastTierSizeReq = T_ReentryLots; g_sizeReqIsExact = true; }    // (v17.21) exact, may shrink
         else if(T_M1QuickHold && tf == PERIOD_M1 && g_lastTierSizeReq <= 0.0)
         {
            double qhB = 0.0, qhH = 0.0;
            double qhTot = QuickHoldSize(qhB, qhH);   // (v16.89) scales with equity
            if(qhTot > 0.0) g_lastTierSizeReq = qhTot;
         }
         // (v17.10) M3/M5 ride the SAME step ladder, so all three clocks scale
         // together instead of M3/M5 sitting on a fixed TierBaseLots forever.
         else if(T_StepScaling && g_lastTierSizeReq <= 0.0)
         {
            double sB = 0.0, sH = 0.0;
            double sTot = QuickHoldSize(sB, sH);
            if(sTot > 0.0) g_lastTierSizeReq = sTot;
         }
         if(!T_TakeEveryPine && !RateLimitOk(k, tf)) continue;   // (v18.32) timing guard, bypassed by the rule
         // (v18.95 BB) one entry event per leg — see the note at T_OneEntryPerLeg
         if(T_OneEntryPerLeg && g_legDone[k] == g_legSeq[k] && g_legSeq[k] > 0 &&
            (long)(TimeCurrent() - g_legDoneT[k]) > (long)T_LegStackSecs &&
            !LegHasExtended(k, -1, e))   // (v19.04 BP) flat + still extending = participation
         {
            PrintFormat("[LEG GUARD] SELL on %s refused — this clock already opened in this "
                        "Supertrend leg. Waiting for the next flip. Pine shows one arrow per "
                        "flip; a second entry inside the same leg is the EA re-deciding, not a signal.",
                        EnumToString(tf));
            continue;
         }
         if(Open(e, -1, sl, tp, why, "TREND-" + StringSubstr(EnumToString(tf),6)))
         {
            RateLimitRecord(k, tf);                       // (v16.96)
            // (v18.96 BC) stamp the time only when this leg is FIRST committed, so the
            // opening burst shares one timestamp and the guard cannot eat the stack.
            if(g_legDone[k] != g_legSeq[k]) g_legDoneT[k] = TimeCurrent();
            g_legDone[k] = g_legSeq[k];                   // (v18.95 BB) this leg is now committed
            g_legEntryPx[k] = (PositionSelectByTicket(PosOf(e))
                               ? PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));   // (v19.04 BP)
            g_trFastRearm[k] = false;   // consumed — back to the normal per-timeframe cooldown after this one
            g_trWatchDir[k] = 0;        // (v12c) consumed — whichever path opened this, the watch is done
            if(g_lastTier >= 2) g_tierToday++;
            g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0;
            for(int q = PositionsTotal()-1; q >= 0; q--)
            {
               ulong nt = PositionGetTicket(q);
               if(nt==0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(nt)) continue;
               if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_TREND].magic &&
                  (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME) >= TimeCurrent()-2)
               { StoreTrendTF(nt, k); RecordSignalPrice(k, PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)); break; }
            }
         }
         else { g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0; }
      }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  E7 · MULTI-TIMEFRAME SCALP  (M1 + M3 + M5 + M15, all at once)
//
//  Catches the trend EARLY BUT CONFIRMED, takes the push, and leaves before the
//  tables turn. Runs on four timeframes simultaneously — whichever one shows a
//  clean push, it takes it.
//================================================================

// Supertrend for a scalp timeframe (cached per bar)
void ScalpST(int k, double &val, int &dir)
{
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];
   datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
   if(g_scStBar[k] == b0 && b0 > 0) { val = g_scStV[k]; dir = g_scStD[k]; return; }

   // (v18.88 AN) was a fixed 150 -- two and a half hours on M1. See T_StWarmupBars.
   int bars = MathMax(50, T_StWarmupBars);
   double mult = T_StMultiplier;   // (v16.90) was hardcoded 3.0 — see T_StMultiplier for why this matters
   double a[]; ArraySetAsSeries(a, false);
   if(CopyBuffer(g_scSt[k], 0, 1, bars, a) < bars) { val = 0; dir = 0; return; }

   double up = 0, dn = 0, pUp = 0, pDn = 0;
   int d = 1;
   for(int i = bars - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      int sh = i + 1;
      double hh = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,sh), lo = iLow(_Symbol,tf,sh), cl = iClose(_Symbol,tf,sh);
      double pc = iClose(_Symbol,tf,sh+1);
      double atr = a[bars-1-i];
      double mid = (hh + lo) / 2.0;
      double bU = mid - mult*atr, bD = mid + mult*atr;
      if(i == bars-1)
      {
         // BUG FIX #68: seed direction from close-vs-BASIS (the midpoint),
         // the unambiguous standard. The old seed compared close to the
         // UPPER band (dn) — an arbitrary test that, in a persistent trend,
         // could set the wrong initial direction and never get corrected by
         // the flip conditions below, carrying a backwards direction all the
         // way to the live bar. That was the buy-on-triple-sell cause.
         up = bU; dn = bD;
         d = (cl >= mid) ? 1 : -1;
      }
      else
      {
         up = (pc > pUp) ? MathMax(bU, pUp) : bU;
         dn = (pc < pDn) ? MathMin(bD, pDn) : bD;
         if(d == 1 && cl < up) d = -1;
         else if(d == -1 && cl > dn) d = 1;
      }
      pUp = up; pDn = dn;
   }
   val = (d == 1) ? up : dn;
   dir = d;
   g_scStBar[k] = b0; g_scStV[k] = val; g_scStD[k] = dir;
}

// How strong is this push, really? 0-100.
// This is what decides whether to size up. A push is strong when the trend is
// established, momentum is EXPANDING (not just present), volume confirms, and
// the higher timeframe agrees. Anything less is a normal scalp at normal size.
int PushStrength(int k, int dir)
{
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];
   double atr = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
   double adx = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,1), adx3 = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,4);
   double e21 = Buf(g_scE21[k],0,1), e50 = Buf(g_scE50[k],0,1);
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1 = iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1),  l1 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double rng = h1-l1, body = MathAbs(c1-o1);
   double rv  = RelVol(tf, 1);
   if(atr <= 0 || rng <= 0) return 0;

   int s = 0;
   if(adx >= SC_MinAdx) s += 18;
   if(adx > adx3) s += 14;                                   // momentum EXPANDING, not just present
   if((dir>0 && e21>e50) || (dir<0 && e21<e50)) s += 12;
   if(body/rng >= 0.65) s += 12;                             // decisive candle
   if(rv >= 1.5) s += 14; else if(rv >= 1.1) s += 7;
   if(rng > 1.1*atr) s += 10;                                // range expansion

   // does the timeframe ABOVE this one agree?
   int up = MathMin(k+1, SC_TFN-1);
   if(up != k)
   {
      double svU; int sdU; ScalpST(up, svU, sdU);
      if(sdU == dir) s += 20;
   }
   else s += 10;

   return MathMin(100, s);
}

// IMPROVEMENT 4: CROSS-TIMEFRAME CONFIRMATION.
// A signal on M3 means little on its own. A signal on M3 that M5, M10, M15 and
// M30 all agree with is a different animal entirely. This counts how many HIGHER
// timeframes point the same way, and refuses the trade unless enough of them do.
//
// The lower timeframe FIRES. The higher timeframes VOTE. Only confluent signals
// get taken. That is the confirmation layer.
int ConfirmCount(int k, int dir)
{
   int agree = 0;
   for(int j = k + 1; j < SC_TFN; j++)
   {
      double sv; int sd; ScalpST(j, sv, sd);
      if(sd == dir) agree++;
   }
   return agree;
}

int OpenScalps()
{
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_SCALP].magic) n++;
   }
   return n;
}

void Engine_Scalp()
{
   int e = E_SCALP;
   if(!EngineReady(e)) return;

   // IMPROVEMENT 5: several scalps at once, capped. One-at-a-time meant an M1
   // signal blocked an M30 signal — two completely different trades on different
   // horizons, and you could only ever hold one. The auto risk ceiling still caps
   // the total, so more positions never means more risk than you allowed.
   if(OpenScalps() >= SC_MaxOpen) return;

   bool onTF[SC_TFN];
   // BUG FIX #59 (real): this used to be "k<=3 -> SC_Fast, k<=7 -> SC_Mid,
   // else -> SC_Slow" — logic written back when g_scTF held 11 timeframes.
   // The array was later collapsed to just 3 (M1/M3/M5, shared with TREND),
   // but this gating was never updated to match. With SC_TFN=3, k only ever
   // reaches 0/1/2, and 0<=3, 1<=3, 2<=3 are ALL true — so every single
   // slot silently mapped to SC_Fast, and SC_Mid/SC_Slow did nothing at
   // all. Worse: turning SC_Fast off disabled SCALP completely, even with
   // SC_Mid/SC_Slow left on — exactly backwards from what those inputs
   // promise. Now each of the 3 real timeframes maps to its own toggle:
   // M1 -> SC_Fast, M3 -> SC_Mid, M5 -> SC_Slow, so all three switches
   // finally do what their names say.
   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      if(k == 0)      onTF[k] = SC_Fast;   // M1 — the mini scalp
      else if(k == 1) onTF[k] = SC_Mid;    // M3 — the core
      else            onTF[k] = SC_Slow;   // M5 — the longer scalp
   }

   // (M1 is not disabled here — see the sniper-M1 ADX gate below, right
   // where ADX is actually checked. A hard block would have cost genuine
   // sniper setups; a much higher bar for M1 specifically does not.)

   for(int k = 0; k < SC_TFN; k++)
   {
      if(!onTF[k]) continue;
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];

      // ═══ (v16.41) ENGINE-OVERLAP FIX — what he has asked for repeatedly ═══
      // TREND was originally M5-ONLY while SCALP covered M1-M15, so they never
      // collided. When TREND was later extended to run M1/M3/M5 it landed on top
      // of SCALP's own clocks and nothing was added to de-duplicate them. Result:
      // one M1 Supertrend flip opened a TREND long on M1 AND a SCALP long on M1 —
      // two positions, same signal, same clock, same direction. That is DOUBLE
      // exposure on ONE signal, not diversification: it silently doubled risk per
      // signal, bypassed the conviction sizing (which had already priced what that
      // signal was worth), and burned two slots of the lot ceiling on one idea.
      // Now SCALP stands down on any clock TREND already holds — one signal, one
      // trade, sized once. DELIBERATE TRADE-OFF: the collision did give a crude
      // layering effect (SCALP banks a fast fixed target while TREND rides), which
      // is real, but that belongs in a controlled partial exit rather than an
      // accidental overlap. SC_NoOverlapTrend = false restores the old behaviour.
      if(SC_NoOverlapTrend && TrendHoldsTf(k))
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[SCALP] standing down on %s — TREND already holds this clock. One signal, one trade.",
                        EnumToString(tf));
         continue;
      }

      datetime b0 = iTime(_Symbol, tf, 0);
      if(b0 == 0 || b0 == g_scBar[k]) continue;
      g_scBar[k] = b0;

      double atr = Buf(g_scAtr[k],0,1);
      double adx = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,1);
      double e21 = Buf(g_scE21[k],0,1), e50 = Buf(g_scE50[k],0,1);
      double rsi = Buf(g_scRsi[k],0,1);
      // THE LEARNED ADX FLOOR. g_adxBoost is raised by the autopsy when a pattern
      // of losses shows the floor was too low. This is the feedback loop closing.
      // SNIPER-M1: on a secondary-symbol instance, M1 needs meaningfully more
      // ADX than M3/M5 do (which already get InpSecondaryAdxBoost via
      // g_adxBoost) — only a genuinely strong M1 push clears this.
      double scAdxFloor = SC_MinAdx + g_adxBoost;
      if(InpSecondarySymbol && tf == PERIOD_M1) scAdxFloor += InpSniperM1ExtraConf * 0.4;  // scaled down: this is an ADX points bar, not a 0-100 confidence score
      if(atr <= 0 || adx < scAdxFloor) continue;

      double stV; int stD; ScalpST(k, stV, stD);
      if(stD == 0) continue;

      int bias = HouseBias();
      // (v16.06) SOFTENED from a hard block. Previously: bias != stD -> skip,
      // which FORBADE an entire direction for the whole session whenever
      // M15+H1 agreed — so on an up-biased day every short was blocked
      // outright, no matter how strong, and vice versa. That is too strict
      // for a system whose whole point is catching BOTH directions 24/5
      // (the trader's repeated, correct complaint). Now a counter-house
      // flip is ALLOWED but must clear a higher confidence bar
      // (needs +1 confirming timeframe, applied at the confluence gate below) —
      // a genuine strong reversal trades even before H1 catches up, while a
      // weak counter-house dip still gets filtered. Aligned or neutral: no
      // extra bar, trades as normal.
      bool counterHouse = (bias != 0 && bias != stD);

      // Did the Supertrend just FLIP on this timeframe? Detect it by comparing to
      // the direction we stored last bar. A flip IS the trade your live script takes.
      bool flipUp = (stD == 1  && g_scLastDir[k] == -1);
      bool flipDn = (stD == -1 && g_scLastDir[k] ==  1);
      g_scLastDir[k] = stD;

      // how many bars has the trend held? (used to gate PULLBACK entries only)
      int held = 0;
      for(int q = 1; q <= 25; q++)
      {
         double cq   = iClose(_Symbol, tf, q);
         double eq50 = Buf(g_scE50[k], 0, q);
         if(eq50 <= 0) break;
         if((stD == 1 && cq > eq50) || (stD == -1 && cq < eq50)) held++;
         else break;
      }

      // AND NOT LATE - if price has already run, the pullback has not happened yet
      double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1 = iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
      double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1),  l1 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
      double h2 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,2),  l2 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,2);
      double rng = h1 - l1, body = MathAbs(c1 - o1);
      if(rng <= 0) continue;
      // THE LEARNED CHASE LIMIT. Tightened by the autopsy if losses show we keep
      // buying extended candles.
      double extLimit = MathMin(SC_MaxExt, g_extTighten);
      if(MathAbs(c1 - e21) / atr > extLimit) continue;

      double rv = RelVol(tf, 1);
      if(rv < SC_MinVol) continue;
      if(body / rng < SC_MinBody) continue;

      // TWO PATHS to an entry — either is enough:
      //   A) the Supertrend just FLIPPED  -> catch the trend at the turn (your live signal)
      //   B) price pulled back to EMA21 and resumed -> join an established trend
      bool pullbackL = c1 > e50 && c1 > o1 && c1 > h2 && (l1 <= e21 + 0.5*atr) && rsi < 78;
      bool pullbackS = c1 < e50 && c1 < o1 && c1 < l2 && (h1 >= e21 - 0.5*atr) && rsi > 22;

      bool sigL = (stD == 1)  && (flipUp || pullbackL);
      bool sigS = (stD == -1) && (flipDn || pullbackS);
      if(!sigL && !sigS) continue;

      // DEMA as a GRADE, not a blocker. You wanted every Supertrend flip caught —
      // same trades the public indicator shows — so the flip is NEVER skipped for
      // being on the "wrong" side of the DEMA. Instead, agreement with the DEMA
      // grades the trade: with-DEMA flips take full risk, against-DEMA flips take
      // reduced risk (still caught, just sized down). This is the auto-risk
      // intelligence doing its job without blinding the system to real trends.
      double demaV = DemaValSc(k);
      double demaGrade = 1.0;
      if(demaV > 0)
      {
         bool withDema = (sigL && c1 > demaV) || (sigS && c1 < demaV);
         demaGrade = withDema ? 1.0 : SC_AgainstDemaRisk;
      }

      bool wasFlip = (sigL && flipUp) || (sigS && flipDn);

      // a PULLBACK entry needs the trend to have held; a FLIP entry is the trend
      // just beginning, so it skips that requirement (or it could never fire).
      if(!wasFlip && held < SC_MinTrendBars) continue;

      int dir = sigL ? 1 : -1;

      // CROSS-TIMEFRAME CONFIRMATION
      int confirms  = ConfirmCount(k, dir);
      int available = SC_TFN - 1 - k;
      // THE LEARNED CONFLUENCE REQUIREMENT.
      // Confirmation scales DOWN for the fastest timeframes — an M1 signal that
      // needs 2 higher TFs to agree will almost never fire, and that is why the EA
      // looked dead. The fast scalps need 1; the core needs the full requirement.
      int baseConfirm = (k <= 3) ? 1 : SC_ConfirmTFs;
      // (v16.06) counter-house scalps must be stronger: +1 confirming
      // timeframe required. This is SCALP's equivalent of the confidence
      // bump — a counter-house dip trade now needs more agreement, but is
      // no longer forbidden outright, so genuine reversals still fire.
      int needed    = (int)MathMin(baseConfirm + g_confBoost + (counterHouse ? 1 : 0), available);
      if(confirms < needed)
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[SCALP] %s REJECTED - only %d of %d higher timeframes agree. Not confluent.",
                        EnumToString(tf), confirms, available);
         continue;
      }

      // IMPROVEMENT 6: DEDUPE. M1, M2 and M3 will all fire on the same push. That
      // is ONE trade, not three. If we already hold this idea, do not buy it again.
      bool dupe = false;
      for(int q2 = PositionsTotal()-1; q2 >= 0; q2--)
      {
         ulong qt = PositionGetTicket(q2);
         if(qt == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(qt)) continue;
         if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[E_SCALP].magic) continue;
         int pd = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         datetime pt = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
         if(pd == dir && (TimeCurrent() - pt) < SC_DedupeBars * PeriodSeconds(tf)) { dupe = true; break; }
      }
      if(dupe) continue;

      int push = PushStrength(k, dir);

      double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
      // (v18.90 AP) the lot shrink that pays for the wider stop. Computed after the
      // stop below, applied through g_entryExtScale which LotFor() already reads.
      double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);
      double slM, tpM; AdaptMults(E_TREND, slM, tpM);

      double sl, d, tp;
      double scStV; int scStD; ScalpST(k, scStV, scStD);   // the signal line on this TF
      if(dir > 0)
      {
         // Stop on the signal line, exactly as specified. Scalps still take the
         // fast 2R — the ride-to-flip rule is for the trend/apex engines only.
         sl = InpSignalLineStop ? scStV - 0.1*atr
                                : MathMin(l1, ask - SC_SlAtr*atr*slM) - 0.1*atr;
         // (v18.90 AP) push the BROKER stop one noise band below the signal line so
         // a wick cannot take a trade the signal still calls alive. See T_WickProofStop.
         if(T_WickProofStop && InpSignalLineStop)
         {
            double wpBand = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
            if(wpBand > 0.0) sl -= wpBand;
         }
         d  = ask - sl;
         if(d <= 0 || d > 2.0*atr) continue;
         tp = ask + SC_TpR * d * tpM;
         if(SpreadUSD() > 0.12 * (tp - ask)) continue;
      }
      else
      {
         sl = InpSignalLineStop ? scStV + 0.1*atr
                                : MathMax(h1, bid + SC_SlAtr*atr*slM) + 0.1*atr;
         // (v18.90 AP) same on the sell side. See T_WickProofStop.
         if(T_WickProofStop && InpSignalLineStop)
         {
            double wpBand = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
            if(wpBand > 0.0) sl += wpBand;
         }
         d  = sl - bid;
         if(d <= 0 || d > 2.0*atr) continue;
         tp = bid - SC_TpR * d * tpM;
         if(SpreadUSD() > 0.12 * (bid - tp)) continue;
      }

      // ═══ (v18.90 AP) KEEP THE MONEY AT RISK CONSTANT ═══
      // The stop just moved one noise band further out. Shrink the lot by exactly
      // that ratio so the pounds at risk are unchanged. See T_WickProofStop.
      if(T_WickProofStop && T_WickProofShrinkLot && InpSignalLineStop && d > 0.0)
      {
         double wpBand2 = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
         if(wpBand2 > 0.0 && d > wpBand2)
         {
            double dNarrow = d - wpBand2;              // what the stop distance WAS
            double wpScale = dNarrow / d;              // < 1, always
            if(wpScale > 0.10 && wpScale < 1.0)
            {
               g_entryExtScale = MathMin(g_entryExtScale, wpScale);
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[SIZE] WICK-PROOF STOP: stop widened %.2f -> %.2f pts so a wick cannot take "
                              "a trade the signal still calls alive. Lot scaled x%.2f so the money at risk "
                              "is unchanged.", dNarrow, d, wpScale);
            }
         }
      }

      // IMPROVEMENT 7: size scales on BOTH push strength AND confluence. A strong
      // push that six higher timeframes agree with is not the same trade as a
      // strong push that two barely tolerate.
      double mult = 1.0;
      // M1 is statistically noisier per-trade than M3/M5 — more false starts,
      // shorter-lived moves. A small, bounded size scalar (not a block) reflects
      // that: M1 gets slightly less size, M5 gets full size. This is genuine risk
      // management, not a quality filter — every timeframe still trades.
      if(tf == PERIOD_M1) mult *= 0.85;
      else if(tf == PERIOD_M3) mult *= 0.95;
      // (v16.33) REGIME AWARENESS FOR SCALP. SCALP runs the SAME M1/M3/M5 clocks as
      // TREND but had received none of the regime work, so on a dead-chop bar TREND
      // correctly sized down to ~0.45x while SCALP piled in at full size on the very
      // same flip — half the EA's trades bypassing every improvement made. This adds
      // the regime SIZE read only; SCALP's tested entry rules and fixed-target exits
      // are deliberately left alone.
      if(T_RegimeEngine)
      {
         int rgSc = RegimeOf(g_scAdx[k], tf);
         if(rgSc > 0)      mult *= T_RegimeTrendSizeX;
         else if(rgSc < 0) mult *= T_RegimeRangeSizeX;
      }
      // against-DEMA flips are still taken (you wanted every trend caught) but sized
      // down — this is the auto-risk grading the DEMA feeds, not a hard filter.
      mult *= demaGrade;
      // (v16.33 REAL BUG FIX) This line used plain ASSIGNMENT (mult = ...), which
      // OVERWROTE every risk grade computed above it: the M1 noise discount (0.85),
      // the M3 discount (0.95) and — worst — demaGrade, the against-DEMA 0.5x trim.
      // So a strong-push signal silently threw away the protection on exactly the
      // trades where it matters most: a high-push flip fighting the 200-DEMA got
      // FULL stacked size instead of half size. Same class of silent risk defect as
      // the old TREND against-DEMA bug. Now it scales ON TOP of the grading, so a
      // strong push still sizes up but never erases the risk trims beneath it.
      if(SC_StackPush && push >= SC_StrongConf)
         mult *= 1.0 + (SC_StackMax - 1.0) * ((push - SC_StrongConf) / (100.0 - SC_StrongConf));
      if(available > 0)
      {
         double ratio = (double)confirms / (double)available;
         mult *= (0.85 + 0.30 * ratio);
      }

      // STRUCTURE. A pullback into an unfilled gap, or an entry right after the
      // stops have been swept, is a materially better entry than a pullback that
      // landed nowhere in particular. It gets more size — never a signal of its own.
      double entryPx = (dir > 0) ? ask : bid;
      string structNote;
      mult *= StructureBoost(k, dir, entryPx, structNote);

      if(mult > SC_StackMax) mult = SC_StackMax;

      g_scPushMult   = mult;
      g_lastPush     = push;
      g_lastConfirms = confirms;
      // Visible in the MT5 terminal as "SCALP-M1" / "SCALP-M3" / "SCALP-M5" so a
      // floating loss on a fast M1 scalp (expected to wobble, small risk) is
      // never mistaken at a glance for a slower, bigger M5 trade.
      string tfTag = StringSubstr(EnumToString(tf), 6);   // "PERIOD_M1" -> "M1"
      bool ok = Open(e, dir, sl, tp,
         StringFormat("%s SCALP %s | %s | ADX %.1f | vol %.1fx | PUSH %d/100 | %d/%d higher TFs agree | %ssize x%.2f",
            EnumToString(tf), dir>0?"LONG":"SHORT", wasFlip?"FLIP entry":"pullback entry",
            adx, rv, push, confirms, available, structNote, mult),
         "SCALP-" + tfTag);
      g_scPushMult = 1.0;

      if(ok)
      {
         // remember which timeframe THIS scalp came from — it drives its exits
         for(int q3 = PositionsTotal()-1; q3 >= 0; q3--)
         {
            ulong nt = PositionGetTicket(q3);
            if(nt == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(nt)) continue;
            if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_SCALP].magic &&
               (TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME)) < 5)
            { StoreScalpTF(nt, k); break; }
         }
         return;
      }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  E6 - APEX TREND RIDER  (M15)
//  Catches the trend at BIRTH, pyramids the pullbacks, and rides it with a
//  WIDENING trail until the trend actually dies. No take profit exists.
//================================================================
int    g_apexAdds = 0;
double g_apexEnt  = 0;   // g_apexR removed — R is per-ticket now (BUG FIX #14)

// Is the trend I am IN still alive? This gets you out before the stop does.
int ApexHealth(int dir)
{
   int e = E_APEX;
   double stV; int stD; Supertrend(e, stV, stD);
   double adx = Buf(hAdx[e],0,1), adx3 = Buf(hAdx[e],0,4);
   double e50 = Buf(hEma50[e],0,1);
   double mm  = Buf(hMacd[e],0,1), ms = Buf(hMacd[e],1,1);
   double c   = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, 1);
   int s = 0;
   // REBALANCED to make room for Two-Pole (the pillar this never had) —
   // small trims across the existing checks, total still 100, so the
   // fixed thresholds this feeds (55, A_HealthMin) still mean the same
   // thing they always did.
   if((dir>0 && stD==1) || (dir<0 && stD==-1)) s += 30;
   if(adx >= A_MinAdx) s += 15;
   if(adx > adx3) s += 10;
   if((dir>0 && c>e50) || (dir<0 && c<e50)) s += 15;
   if((dir>0 && mm>ms) || (dir<0 && mm<ms)) s += 12;
   CalcVWAP();
   if((dir>0 && c>g_vwap) || (dir<0 && c<g_vwap)) s += 8;
   // TWO-POLE — a turn against this position is early evidence the move may
   // be finishing, same principle as the Guardian's check on TREND/SCALP.
   if(T_UseTwoPole && !TwoPoleTurnedAgainstTf(PERIOD_M15, dir)) s += 10;
   return s;
}

void Engine_Apex()
{
   int e = E_APEX;
   if(!EngineReady(e)) return;

   double stV; int stD; Supertrend(e, stV, stD);
   double atr = Buf(hAtr[e],0,1);
   double adx = Buf(hAdx[e],0,1);
   double e21 = Buf(hEma21[e],0,1);
   double e200= Buf(hEma200[e],0,1);
   if(atr <= 0 || adx < A_MinAdx + g_adxBoost) return;   // the learned floor

   // COMMENT/CODE MISMATCH FOUND IN AUDIT: despite the name, this checks
   // TREND's M5 Supertrend, not H1 — Supertrend(E_TREND,...) reads
   // Cfg[E_TREND].tf, which is fixed at PERIOD_M5 in OnInit. It is NOT a
   // missing H1 check, though: real H1 agreement IS enforced two lines
   // below via HouseBias() (M15-only since v16.12). What this line actually does is
   // require M15 (APEX's own timeframe) to also agree with M5 — a real,
   // reasonable alignment filter, just mislabeled. Left the behaviour
   // exactly as-is (surgical — no functional change) since APEX firing
   // "a few times a week" by design means this extra M5-agreement filter
   // has been part of its observed behaviour the whole time; loosening it
   // is a sizing/frequency decision, not a bug fix. Tell me if you want
   // this M5-agreement requirement dropped (APEX would fire somewhat more
   // often) — HouseBias() below would still cover the real H1 check either way.
   double sv5; int sd5; Supertrend(E_TREND, sv5, sd5);
   if(stD != sd5) return;

   // APEX is the anchor — it only takes the highest-conviction direction.
   if(HouseBias() != stD) return;

   double c1=iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,1), o1=iOpen(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,1);
   double h1=iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,1),  l1=iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,1);
   double h2=iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,2),  l2=iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,2);
   double rng = h1-l1, body = MathAbs(c1-o1);
   double rv = RelVol(PERIOD_M15,1);
   if(rng <= 0) return;

   bool impU = c1>o1 && rng > 1.05*atr && body/rng > 0.55 && rv >= 1.2;
   bool impD = c1<o1 && rng > 1.05*atr && body/rng > 0.55 && rv >= 1.2;

   ulong tk = PosOf(e);

   //---- PYRAMID: add on a pullback inside a live, healthy trend ----
   if(tk != 0)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;
      int dir = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE)==POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      if(g_apexAdds >= A_MaxAdds) return;
      if(ApexHealth(dir) < 55) return;

      double sw = (dir>0) ? iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,SafeHighest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,MODE_HIGH,5,1))
                          : iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,SafeLowest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,MODE_LOW,5,1));
      bool pullL = dir>0 && l2 <= e21 && (sw - l2) >= A_PullDepth*atr && c1>o1 && c1>h2;
      bool pullS = dir<0 && h2 >= e21 && (h2 - sw) >= A_PullDepth*atr && c1<o1 && c1<l2;
      if(!pullL && !pullS) return;

      // The add rides the SAME trail. By now the first stop is at or past
      // breakeven, so the add costs nothing extra in risk.
      double sl = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      if(sl == 0) return;
      double px = (dir>0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);
      double d  = MathAbs(px - sl);
      if(d <= 0) return;

      double lots = LotFor(e, d) * A_AddSize;
      lots = MathFloor(lots/g_lotStep)*g_lotStep;
      if(lots < g_lotMin) return;

      // (v18.76) same two questions as a base entry. See T_AddsRespectCaps.
      if(T_AddsRespectCaps)
      {
         if(DirExposureFull(dir, lots))
         {
            PrintFormat("[APEX ADD REFUSED] direction ceiling. %.2f lots wanted, %.2f already open this way, cap %.2f.",
                        lots, OpenLotsThisDir(dir), T_MaxDirLots);
            return;
         }
         if(!MarginOkFor(lots, dir))
         {
            PrintFormat("[APEX ADD REFUSED] margin. %.2f lots would breach the free-margin floor.", lots);
            return;
         }
      }
      trade.SetExpertMagicNumber(Cfg[e].magic);
      bool ok = (dir>0) ? trade.Buy(lots,_Symbol,0.0,NP(sl),0.0,"APEX add")
                        : trade.Sell(lots,_Symbol,0.0,NP(sl),0.0,"APEX add");
      if(ok)
      {
         g_apexAdds++;
         // BUG FIX #14: the add is its OWN position on a hedging account.
         // It needs its OWN R, or every trail decision made about it is wrong.
         ulong addDeal = trade.ResultDeal();
         ulong addPos  = 0;
         if(addDeal > 0 && HistoryDealSelect(addDeal))
            addPos = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(addDeal, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
         if(addPos > 0)
         {
            StoreRDist(addPos, d);
            StoreRisk(addPos, lots * LossPerLot(d));
            StoreReason(addPos, StringFormat("APEX ADD %d/%d on a pullback. Health %d. Shares the trail.",
                        g_apexAdds, A_MaxAdds, ApexHealth(dir)));
         }
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[APEX] ADD %d/%d. Health %d. Riding.", g_apexAdds, A_MaxAdds, ApexHealth(dir));
      }
      return;
   }

   //---- NEW RIDE ----
   double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
   double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);

   // DEMA — same fix as the Pine and the Scalp engine: it GRADES the trade
   // instead of blocking it. A flip against the DEMA still fires (you catch the
   // same Supertrend flip either way), it just carries a reduced-size flag in
   // the trade comment so you can see which side of the DEMA it fired on.
   double demaV = DemaVal(e);
   bool   withDemaL = demaV <= 0 || c1 > demaV;
   bool   withDemaS = demaV <= 0 || c1 < demaV;

   // BIRTH: the Supertrend flip + impulse. DEMA no longer gates it.
   bool birthL = (stD==1)  && c1 > e200 && impU;
   bool birthS = (stD==-1) && c1 < e200 && impD;

   // PULSE: no fresh flip needed. Fires inside an established trend on an
   // impulse off the EMA. This is what catches the 2nd and 3rd legs.
   bool nearEma = MathMin(MathAbs(l1-e21), MathAbs(h1-e21)) < 0.6*atr;
   bool pulseL = A_UsePulse && (stD==1)  && c1 > e200 && impU && nearEma && adx > A_MinAdx+3;
   bool pulseS = A_UsePulse && (stD==-1) && c1 < e200 && impD && nearEma && adx > A_MinAdx+3;

   double slM, tpM; AdaptMults(e, slM, tpM);

   if(birthL || pulseL)
   {
      // STOP ON THE SIGNAL LINE, exactly as specified — no ATR blending.
      double sl = stV - 0.1*atr;
      double d  = ask - sl;
      if(d <= 0) return;
      // NO TAKE PROFIT. tp = 0. The trail is the only exit.
      if(Open(e, 1, sl, 0.0, StringFormat("%s LONG. Supertrend bull, M15+M5+H1 agree, ADX %.1f, impulse %.0f%% body on %.1fx volume%s. NO TP - rides until the trend dies.",
              birthL?"TREND BIRTH":"PULSE continuation", adx, 100.0*body/rng, rv, withDemaL?"":" (against DEMA - reduced size)")))
      { g_apexAdds = 0; g_apexEnt = ask; }
   }
   else if(birthS || pulseS)
   {
      // STOP ON THE SIGNAL LINE — the Supertrend value above price for a short.
      double sl = stV + 0.1*atr;
      double d  = sl - bid;
      if(d <= 0) return;
      if(Open(e, -1, sl, 0.0, StringFormat("%s SHORT. Supertrend bear, M15+M5+H1 agree, ADX %.1f, impulse %.0f%% body on %.1fx volume%s. NO TP - rides until the trend dies.",
              birthS?"TREND BIRTH":"PULSE continuation", adx, 100.0*body/rng, rv, withDemaS?"":" (against DEMA - reduced size)")))
      { g_apexAdds = 0; g_apexEnt = bid; }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  STRUCTURE & LIQUIDITY
//  The two ideas from the ICT world that are actually mechanical.
//================================================================

// FAIR VALUE GAP: three bars where the middle one moved so hard that bar 1's high
// never overlapped bar 3's low. That pocket was never traded. Price tends to come
// back and fill it — and a pullback that lands inside one, WITH the trend, is a
// better entry than a pullback that lands nowhere in particular.
bool InFairValueGap(int k, int dir, double px, double &gapLo, double &gapHi)
{
   if(!ICT_Enable || !ICT_UseFVG) return false;
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];

   for(int i = 2; i < ICT_Lookback; i++)
   {
      double h3 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i+1);   // older
      double l3 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i+1);
      double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i-1);   // newer
      double l1 = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i-1);

      if(dir > 0 && l1 > h3)                 // bullish gap: newer low above older high
      {
         if(px >= h3 && px <= l1) { gapLo = h3; gapHi = l1; return true; }
      }
      if(dir < 0 && h1 < l3)                 // bearish gap
      {
         if(px <= l3 && px >= h1) { gapLo = h1; gapHi = l3; return true; }
      }
   }
   return false;
}

// LIQUIDITY SWEEP: did price recently poke through a prior swing high/low — where
// every stop in the market is resting — grab them, and close back inside?
// That is a stop hunt, and it is one of the few "smart money" ideas you can
// actually see in the data rather than infer from a story.
bool RecentSweep(int k, int dir)
{
   if(!ICT_Enable || !ICT_UseSweep) return false;
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];

   for(int i = 1; i <= 6; i++)   // did it happen in the last few bars?
   {
      double c = iClose(_Symbol, tf, i);
      double h = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, i);
      double l = iLow(_Symbol, tf, i);

      if(dir > 0)
      {
         // swept the lows (grabbed sell stops), closed back above
         int lowest = iLowest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_LOW, ICT_Lookback, i+1);
         double priorLow = iLow(_Symbol, tf, lowest);
         if(l < priorLow && c > priorLow) return true;
      }
      else
      {
         int highest = iHighest(_Symbol, tf, MODE_HIGH, ICT_Lookback, i+1);
         double priorHigh = iHigh(_Symbol, tf, highest);
         if(h > priorHigh && c < priorHigh) return true;
      }
   }
   return false;
}

// Combined structure read. Returns a size multiplier: 1.0 = nothing special,
// up to ICT_Boost when the structure genuinely lines up behind the trade.
double StructureBoost(int k, int dir, double px, string &note)
{
   note = "";
   if(!ICT_Enable) return 1.0;

   double gLo, gHi;
   bool fvg   = InFairValueGap(k, dir, px, gLo, gHi);
   bool sweep = RecentSweep(k, dir);

   if(fvg && sweep)
   {
      note = StringFormat("STRUCTURE: pullback into a fair value gap (%.2f-%.2f) right after a liquidity sweep. Both agree. ", gLo, gHi);
      return ICT_Boost;
   }
   if(fvg)
   {
      note = StringFormat("STRUCTURE: pullback landed inside an unfilled gap (%.2f-%.2f). ", gLo, gHi);
      return 1.0 + (ICT_Boost - 1.0) * 0.6;
   }
   if(sweep)
   {
      note = "STRUCTURE: entry follows a liquidity sweep — the stops have been taken. ";
      return 1.0 + (ICT_Boost - 1.0) * 0.6;
   }
   return 1.0;
}

//================================================================
//  THE AUTOPSY
//  Every loss gets dissected. Patterns across losses get acted on.
//  A single loss is noise. Twenty losses with the same flaw is a bug.
//================================================================
struct LossRecord
{
   int      engine;
   int      dir;
   double   rMult;
   double   adx;
   double   atrRank;
   double   spread;
   int      hour;
   int      confirms;
   int      push;
   int      thesisAtExit;
   int      barsHeld;
   bool     hitStop;         // stopped out vs cut early
   bool     wasCounterHtf;   // was the H1 against us at entry?
   bool     lowVol;
   bool     wasChasing;      // entered far from the EMA
   double   extAtEntry;      // (v16.07) exact ATR-past-EMA at entry, numeric — for the loosening test
   bool     thinSession;
};

LossRecord g_loss[300];
int        g_lossN = 0;
int        g_lossTotal = 0;

// Snapshot of the conditions at ENTRY, kept per ticket so the autopsy can compare
// what we believed when we entered against what actually happened.
ulong  g_entTk[200];
double g_entAdx[200];
double g_entAtrR[200];
int    g_entHour[200];
int    g_entPush[200];
int    g_entConf[200];
double g_entExt[200];
int    g_entN = 0;

void SnapshotEntry(ulong tk, int e, int push, int confirms, double extension)
{
   if(g_entN >= 200) g_entN = 0;
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), t);
   g_entTk[g_entN]   = tk;
   g_entAdx[g_entN]  = Buf(hAdx[e],0,1);
   g_entAtrR[g_entN] = AtrRank(e);
   g_entHour[g_entN] = t.hour;
   g_entPush[g_entN] = push;
   g_entConf[g_entN] = confirms;
   g_entExt[g_entN]  = extension;
   g_entN++;
}

int FindEntry(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < 200; i++) if(g_entTk[i] == tk) return i;
   return -1;
}

// ── THE POST-MORTEM ──
void Autopsy(int e, ulong pid, double rMult, double net)
{
   if(!AU_Enable) return;

   int idx = FindEntry(pid);
   LossRecord L;
   L.engine   = e;
   L.rMult    = rMult;
   L.adx      = (idx >= 0) ? g_entAdx[idx]  : Buf(hAdx[e],0,1);
   L.atrRank  = (idx >= 0) ? g_entAtrR[idx] : AtrRank(e);
   L.hour     = (idx >= 0) ? g_entHour[idx] : 0;
   L.push     = (idx >= 0) ? g_entPush[idx] : 0;
   L.confirms = (idx >= 0) ? g_entConf[idx] : 0;
   L.spread   = SpreadUSD();
   L.hitStop  = (rMult <= -0.85);          // full stop, vs cut early by the Guardian
   L.lowVol   = (L.atrRank < 40);
   L.wasChasing  = (idx >= 0 && g_entExt[idx] > 0.9);
   L.extAtEntry  = (idx >= 0) ? g_entExt[idx] : -1;   // (v16.07) exact ATR-past-EMA at entry — tells us if a loss came from the looser extension setting (high number) or something unrelated (low number)
   // (v19.13 CC) was hardcoded (hour >= 20 || hour < 8) -- a SECOND, disagreeing
   // definition of "thin session" living in the same file as T_ThinStartGmtHour /
   // T_ThinEndGmtHour (22 / 7). So the loss-diagnostics module classified 20:00-22:00
   // and 07:00-08:00 as thin while the SIZING module did not. Every conclusion this
   // module ever produced about thin-hour losses was drawn against a window the EA
   // never actually traded differently. Now both read the same two inputs.
   L.thinSession = (T_ThinStartGmtHour > T_ThinEndGmtHour)
                   ? (L.hour >= T_ThinStartGmtHour || L.hour < T_ThinEndGmtHour)
                   : (L.hour >= T_ThinStartGmtHour && L.hour <  T_ThinEndGmtHour);
   L.dir = 0;

   if(g_lossN >= 300) g_lossN = 0;
   g_loss[g_lossN] = L;
   g_lossN++;
   g_lossTotal++;

   // ── WRITE THE RECORD ──
   int fh = FileOpen("quad_losses.csv", FILE_READ|FILE_WRITE|FILE_CSV|FILE_ANSI);
   if(fh != INVALID_HANDLE)
   {
      if(FileSize(fh) == 0)
         FileWrite(fh, "time","engine","R","net","adx_at_entry","atr_rank","hour",
                       "push","confirms","spread","full_stop","chased","ext_at_entry","thin_session","why");
      FileSeek(fh, 0, SEEK_END);
      FileWrite(fh, TimeToString(TimeCurrent()), Cfg[e].name,
                DoubleToString(rMult,2), DoubleToString(net,2),
                DoubleToString(L.adx,1), DoubleToString(L.atrRank,0), (string)L.hour,
                (string)L.push, (string)L.confirms, DoubleToString(L.spread,2),
                L.hitStop?"YES":"no", L.wasChasing?"YES":"no", DoubleToString(L.extAtEntry,2),
                L.thinSession?"YES":"no",
                ReasonFor(pid));
      FileClose(fh);
   }

   // ── THE VERDICT ON THIS ONE TRADE ──
   string v = "";
   if(L.hitStop)      v += "Full stop — the market simply went the other way. ";
   else               v += "Cut early by the Guardian — the thesis broke before the stop. ";
   if(L.adx < 22)     v += StringFormat("ADX was only %.1f — weak trend. ", L.adx);
   if(L.lowVol)       v += StringFormat("Volatility in the bottom %.0f%% — not enough range to reach the target. ", L.atrRank);
   if(L.wasChasing)   v += "ENTERED CHASING — price had already run from the EMA. ";
   if(L.thinSession)  v += StringFormat("Hour %d — thin session, wide spread. ", L.hour);
   if(L.confirms <= 1)v += StringFormat("Only %d higher timeframes agreed. ", L.confirms);
   if(L.spread > InpMaxSpreadUSD * 0.8) v += StringFormat("Spread was %.2f — near the cap. ", L.spread);
   if(v == "") v = "Nothing obviously wrong. The tight stop did its job on a setup that just didn't develop — that happens even to good signals.";

   PrintFormat("╔═ AUTOPSY · loss #%d · %s · %.2fR (%.2f)", g_lossTotal, Cfg[e].name, rMult, net);
   PrintFormat("╠═ %s", v);

   // ── PATTERN LEARNING ──
   // One loss tells you nothing. This is where it actually learns.
   if(g_lossTotal < AU_MinSample)
   {
      PrintFormat("╚═ %d/%d losses logged. Not enough to draw conclusions yet — one loss is noise.",
                  g_lossTotal, AU_MinSample);
      return;
   }

   int n = MathMin(g_lossN, 300);
   int cLowAdx=0, cLowVol=0, cChase=0, cThin=0, cFewConf=0, cStop=0, cWideSpread=0;
   double sumAdx = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
   {
      if(g_loss[i].adx < 22)      cLowAdx++;
      if(g_loss[i].lowVol)        cLowVol++;
      if(g_loss[i].wasChasing)    cChase++;
      if(g_loss[i].thinSession)   cThin++;
      if(g_loss[i].confirms <= 1) cFewConf++;
      if(g_loss[i].hitStop)       cStop++;
      if(g_loss[i].spread > InpMaxSpreadUSD * 0.8) cWideSpread++;
      sumAdx += g_loss[i].adx;
   }
   double pct = 100.0 / n;

   PrintFormat("╠═ PATTERN across %d losses:", n);
   PrintFormat("╠═   weak ADX (<22) ....... %.0f%%   (avg ADX at entry: %.1f)", cLowAdx*pct, sumAdx/n);
   PrintFormat("╠═   low volatility ....... %.0f%%", cLowVol*pct);
   PrintFormat("╠═   chased the entry ..... %.0f%%", cChase*pct);
   PrintFormat("╠═   thin session ......... %.0f%%", cThin*pct);
   PrintFormat("╠═   thin confluence ...... %.0f%%", cFewConf*pct);
   PrintFormat("╠═   wide spread .......... %.0f%%", cWideSpread*pct);
   PrintFormat("╠═   full stop vs cut early %.0f%% / %.0f%%", cStop*pct, 100.0-cStop*pct);

   // ── THE RECOMMENDATIONS ──
   string fix = "";
   if(cLowAdx*pct    >= AU_PatternPct) fix += StringFormat("RAISE the ADX floor to ~%.0f. ", (sumAdx/n) + 3);
   if(cLowVol*pct    >= AU_PatternPct) fix += "RAISE the min ATR percentile — you are trading dead tape. ";
   if(cChase*pct     >= AU_PatternPct) fix += "LOWER SC_MaxExt — you are chasing extended candles. ";
   if(cThin*pct      >= AU_PatternPct) fix += "STOP trading the thin session — restrict to London/NY. ";
   if(cFewConf*pct   >= AU_PatternPct) fix += "RAISE SC_ConfirmTFs — you are taking unconfirmed signals. ";
   if(cWideSpread*pct>= AU_PatternPct) fix += "LOWER the spread cap — cost is eating the edge. ";
   if(cStop*pct < 30.0)
      fix += "NOTE: most losses are the Guardian cutting early, NOT full stops. It is protecting you — do not loosen it. ";

   if(fix == "")
   {
      Print("╚═ No dominant flaw. These look like ordinary losses. The system is working as designed.");
   }
   else
   {
      PrintFormat("╠═ >>> FIX: %s", fix);

      if(AU_AutoTighten)
      {
         // Only ever tighten. NEVER loosen — loosening after losses is how a system
         // talks itself into worse trades to "make it back". The one direction this
         // is allowed to move is toward fewer, better trades.
         if(cLowAdx*pct >= AU_PatternPct && g_adxBoost < 8.0)
         {
            g_adxBoost += 2.0;
            PrintFormat("╠═ >>> AUTO: ADX floor raised by %.0f across all engines.", g_adxBoost);
         }
         if(cChase*pct >= AU_PatternPct && g_extTighten > 0.6)
         {
            g_extTighten -= 0.1;
            PrintFormat("╠═ >>> AUTO: max extension tightened to %.2f ATR.", g_extTighten);
         }
         if(cFewConf*pct >= AU_PatternPct && g_confBoost < 3)
         {
            g_confBoost += 1;
            PrintFormat("╠═ >>> AUTO: confirmation requirement raised by %d timeframes.", g_confBoost);
         }
      }
      else
         Print("╠═ (AU_AutoTighten is OFF — apply these yourself, or turn it on to let the EA do it.)");
   }

   // write the running analysis to file
   int af = FileOpen("quad_autopsy.txt", FILE_READ|FILE_WRITE|FILE_TXT|FILE_ANSI);
   if(af != INVALID_HANDLE)
   {
      FileSeek(af, 0, SEEK_END);
      FileWriteString(af, StringFormat("\r\n=== %s | %d losses ===\r\n", TimeToString(TimeCurrent()), n));
      FileWriteString(af, StringFormat("weak ADX %.0f%% | low vol %.0f%% | chased %.0f%% | thin %.0f%% | few confirms %.0f%%\r\n",
                      cLowAdx*pct, cLowVol*pct, cChase*pct, cThin*pct, cFewConf*pct));
      FileWriteString(af, "FIX: " + (fix == "" ? "none needed" : fix) + "\r\n");
      FileClose(af);
   }
   Print("╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════");
}

//================================================================
//  VOLATILITY & NEWS FILTERS
//================================================================
datetime g_chaosUntil = 0;

// Is the market currently CHAOTIC — as opposed to merely fast?
// A fast market going somewhere is an opportunity. A fast market going nowhere
// is a stop-hunting machine. The difference is the BODY of the candle.
bool IsChaos(int e)
{
   if(!V_Filter) return false;
   if(TimeCurrent() < g_chaosUntil) return true;

   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
   double atr = Buf(hAtr[e], 0, 1);
   if(atr <= 0) return false;

   // ATR against its own 50-bar average -> is this a genuine spike?
   double a[];
   if(CopyBuffer(hAtr[e], 0, 1, 50, a) < 50) return false;
   double avg = 0;
   for(int i = 0; i < 50; i++) avg += a[i];
   avg /= 50.0;
   if(avg <= 0) return false;
   if(atr < avg * V_SpikeAtr) return false;   // not a spike at all. Carry on.

   // It IS a spike. Now: is it going somewhere, or just thrashing?
   double h1 = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1), l1 = iLow(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1 = iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
   double rng = h1 - l1;
   if(rng <= 0) return false;
   double body = MathAbs(c1 - o1);
   double wickPct = 100.0 * (rng - body) / rng;

   if(wickPct > V_ChaosWickPct)
   {
      // Mostly wick on a spike bar = price went both ways and came back.
      // That is a stop hunt, not a move.
      g_chaosUntil = TimeCurrent() + V_ChaosBars * PeriodSeconds(tf);
      PrintFormat("[CHAOS] %.0f%% wick on a %.1fx ATR spike. Standing down %d bars — that is a stop hunt, not a trend.",
                  wickPct, atr/avg, V_ChaosBars);
      return true;
   }

   // A spike with a REAL BODY, in the direction of the house. This is not a risk.
   // This is the trade of the week. Take it.
   if(V_AllowTrending)
   {
      int bodyDir = (c1 > o1) ? 1 : -1;
      if(bodyDir == HouseBias() && HouseBias() != 0)
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[VOL] %.1fx ATR expansion WITH the trend, %.0f%% body. This is the move. Trading it.",
                        atr/avg, 100.0 - wickPct);
         return false;
      }
   }
   return true;
}

// Scheduled news. No edge survives a 40-pip spread and a 30-dollar wick.
// Flatten every open managed position ahead of a scheduled high-impact print.
// Called once per bar the news window is active — PositionClose is idempotent
// (nothing to close on the 2nd+ call), so no duplicate-close risk.
void NewsFlatten()
{
   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++)
   {
      ulong tk = PosOf(e);
      if(tk == 0) continue;
      if(PositionSelectByTicket(tk))
      {
         CloseTagged("NEWS-FLAT", tk);
         PrintFormat("[%s] NEWS FLATTEN: closed ahead of scheduled release, ticket %I64u", Cfg[e].name, tk);
      }
   }
}

bool InNewsWindow()
{
   if(!N_Filter) return false;

   // BUG FIX #51: this used to be a fixed clock schedule (N_Times) compared
   // against server time — a pure guess. It drifts wrong on DST, drifts
   // wrong the moment a broker's session clock shifts, and blocks trading
   // at times when no news is actually happening (exactly what was
   // reported) while missing any real news that isn't on the hardcoded
   // list. This now reads the market directly instead of guessing:
   //
   //   REAL NEWS SIGNATURE = spread blown out far past its normal average
   //                         AND a genuine volatility spike, AT THE SAME TIME.
   //
   // Ordinary volatility (a strong trend, a normal session open) moves ATR
   // without blowing out the spread. Ordinary spread widening (thin liquidity,
   // late session) happens without a volatility spike. Both together, at
   // once, is what a real news print actually looks like — and it is
   // detectable live instead of scheduled in advance.
   double sp = SpreadUSD();
   double atrN = Buf(hAtr[E_TREND], 0, 1);
   // BUG FIX #58 (multi-symbol portability): this used to require sp > 0.5
   // as a flat absolute floor — a reasonable "don't panic over noise" sanity
   // check on gold (~$4,000, spread in low single-digit cents), but a
   // completely wrong number on any other instrument. On EURUSD (price
   // ~1.08) a raw spread is never going to exceed 0.5 in price units, so
   // this floor would silently NEVER fire — the whole safety check would be
   // dead code the moment this file ran a second instance on a different
   // symbol, exactly the "multi-symbol" use this file's own header already
   // plans for. Replaced with an ATR-relative floor (3% of current ATR) —
   // same intent (ignore a spread blip too small to matter), but it now
   // scales correctly to whatever instrument the chart is actually on.
   bool spreadBlown = (g_spreadAvg > 0 && sp > g_spreadAvg * N_SpreadSpike && sp > 0.03 * atrN);

   bool volSpike = false;
   if(atrN > 0)
   {
      double a[];
      if(CopyBuffer(hAtr[E_TREND], 0, 1, 20, a) >= 20)
      {
         double avgA = 0;
         for(int i = 0; i < 20; i++) avgA += a[i];
         avgA /= 20.0;
         if(avgA > 0) volSpike = (atrN > avgA * N_VolSpike);
      }
   }

   bool realNews = spreadBlown && volSpike;

   // Optional extra layer: a KNOWN scheduled release (NFP/CPI/FOMC) still
   // gets pre-positioned coverage from N_Times if the user wants it, but it
   // no longer decides alone — it only widens the window AROUND a time when
   // something is already happening, never blocks on a quiet clock-only match.
   bool nearScheduled = false;
   if(StringLen(N_Times) > 0)
   {
      MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), t);
      int nowMin = t.hour * 60 + t.min;
      string parts[];
      int n = StringSplit(N_Times, ',', parts);
      for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      {
         string p = parts[i];
         StringTrimLeft(p); StringTrimRight(p);
         int colon = StringFind(p, ":");
         if(colon < 0) continue;
         int hh = (int)StringToInteger(StringSubstr(p, 0, colon));
         int mm = (int)StringToInteger(StringSubstr(p, colon + 1));
         int evMin = hh * 60 + mm;
         int before = (int)MathMin(N_BeforeMin, 10);
         int after  = (int)MathMin(N_AfterMin, 10);
         if(nowMin >= evMin - before && nowMin <= evMin + after) { nearScheduled = true; break; }
      }
   }

   bool blocking = realNews || (nearScheduled && (spreadBlown || volSpike));
   if(blocking && InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[NEWS] Blocking — spread %.2f vs avg %.2f (%s), ATR spike %s, near scheduled time: %s.",
                  sp, g_spreadAvg, spreadBlown?"BLOWN OUT":"normal",
                  volSpike?"YES":"no", nearScheduled?"yes":"no");
   return blocking;
}

//================================================================
//  THE GUARDIAN
//  Live, continuous re-analysis of every open position.
//  "If I were flat right now, would I still take this trade?"
//================================================================
double g_spreadAvg = 0;

// What the autopsy has LEARNED. These only ever move one way: toward fewer,
// better trades. The system is never allowed to loosen itself after losses —
// that is how a strategy talks itself into worse trades to "make it back".
double g_adxBoost   = 0.0;   // added to every ADX floor
bool   g_fastRearm[ENG_COUNT];   // set true after an early breakeven scratch — lets the very next
                                  // valid signal back in almost immediately instead of waiting the
                                  // full cooldown, since a scratch this small means the trend never
                                  // actually left, it just wobbled at breakeven.
double g_extTighten = 1.1;   // max ATR from the EMA before it counts as chasing
int    g_confBoost  = 0;     // extra higher timeframes required

void UpdateSpreadAvg()
{
   double sp = SpreadUSD();
   if(sp <= 0) return;
   g_spreadAvg = (g_spreadAvg <= 0) ? sp : (g_spreadAvg * 0.995 + sp * 0.005);
}

// Thirteen independent reads of whether the trade still makes sense.
// Independent is the key word — nine correlated checks would just be one check
// shouting. These look at different things: trend, momentum, structure,
// participation, and the higher timeframe.
int ThesisScore(int e, int dir, double profitR, string &verdict, ulong tk = 0)
{
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = Cfg[e].tf;
   int score = 0;
   string bad = "";

   // 1. Is the Supertrend still on our side? (25, or 37 on a fast TREND
   //    trade — see the H1 redistribution at check #2 below: reducing H1's
   //    weight without giving those points to something else would have
   //    left the ACHIEVABLE score identical whenever H1 disagrees, which is
   //    the common case for a fresh fast flip — a shrunk ceiling with the
   //    same penalty helps nobody. Redistributed into Supertrend itself,
   //    the single most important read for a fast trade.
   // BUG FIX #49: this always read the fixed engine-level Supertrend (M5 for
   // TREND), even though TREND now opens positions independently on M1/M3/M5.
   // A fast M1 trade that had already reversed on ITS OWN timeframe could
   // still score full marks here because M5 — a completely different clock —
   // hadn't flipped yet. That starves the exit logic of its single biggest
   // factor exactly when a fast reversal needs to be caught fast. Now reads
   // the position's own timeframe for TREND, the correct engine timeframe
   // for everything else.
   double sv; int sd;
   if(e == E_TREND && tk > 0) { int trKts = TrendTFOf(tk); tf = g_scTF[trKts]; ScalpST(trKts, sv, sd); }
   else Supertrend(e, sv, sd);
   bool fastTrendPos = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0 && g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)] != PERIOD_M5);
   int stWeight = fastTrendPos ? 37 : 25;
   if(sd == dir) score += stWeight;
   else bad += "Supertrend flipped. ";

   // 2. Does the HIGHER timeframe still agree? (20, or 8 on a fast TREND trade)
   // VALUES-CONSISTENCY FIX: H1 was worth the same full 20/100 points
   // regardless of which timeframe the trade is actually on — but the
   // stated rule for this whole system is that H1 is CONTEXT, never a
   // blocker, for fast (M1/M3) timeframes; H1 often hasn't caught up to a
   // fresh fast flip yet, and shouldn't be treated as if it had. A fifth of
   // the entire exit score quietly enforced the opposite of that rule on
   // every M1/M3 trade. Reduced to 8 for M1/M3 TREND, with the difference
   // moved to the Supertrend check above rather than just shrinking the
   // total — so a fast trade with H1 lagging (the normal case) now
   // genuinely scores higher than before, not just on a smaller scale.
   // M5, SCALP, and APEX (slower clocks, where H1 agreement means more)
   // keep the full 20.
   int h1Weight = fastTrendPos ? 8 : 20;
   double h1c = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 1);
   double h1e = Buf(g_hH1Ema, 0, 1);
   int h1d = (h1e > 0) ? ((h1c > h1e) ? 1 : -1) : dir;
   if(h1d == dir) score += h1Weight;
   else bad += "H1 turned against us. ";

   // 3. Is the trend still STRONG? (12)
   // BUG FIX #49 continued: same issue as check #1 — ADX was always read
   // from the fixed engine handle (M5), not the position's own timeframe.
   int adxHandle = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0) ? g_scAdx[TrendTFOf(tk)] : hAdx[e];
   double adx = Buf(adxHandle,0,1), adxOld = Buf(adxHandle,0,4);
   if(adx >= 20) score += 12;
   else bad += "ADX collapsed. ";

   // 4. Is it still EXPANDING, or rolling over? (10)
   if(adx > adxOld - 1.0) score += 10;
   else bad += "ADX rolling over. ";

   // 5. Momentum still with us? (10)
   // BUG FIX #49 continued: same issue, now fixed with the new per-timeframe
   // MACD handle (g_scMacd) added specifically for this.
   // (v15.8) MACD LAG FIX for fast clocks: a 12/26/9 MACD cross on M1 can
   // confirm 10-20 bars after momentum actually turned — on a 1-minute
   // clock that's ancient history, so this vote kept paying its 10 points
   // long after the move died (slow exits) and withdrew them long after
   // reversals (noise). For fast (M1/M3) TREND positions the vote is now
   // DIRECT price momentum: net close-to-close change over the last 3 bars,
   // normalised by ATR — zero indicator lag, and independent of every
   // other check here (ST line, EMA, ADX, RSI, Two-Pole, candles all
   // measure something else). MACD stays for M5/SCALP/APEX, where its
   // smoothing matches the clock. MACD was never in the ENTRY signals.
   if(fastTrendPos)
   {
      double atrM = Buf(g_scAtr[TrendTFOf(tk)], 0, 1);
      double roc3 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1) - iClose(_Symbol, tf, 4);
      bool momOk = (atrM > 0) && ((dir > 0) ? (roc3 > 0.10 * atrM) : (roc3 < -0.10 * atrM));
      if(momOk) score += 10;
      else bad += "3-bar momentum gone flat/against. ";
   }
   else
   {
      int macdHandle = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0) ? g_scMacd[TrendTFOf(tk)] : hMacd[e];
      double mm = Buf(macdHandle,0,1), ms = Buf(macdHandle,1,1);
      if((dir > 0 && mm > ms) || (dir < 0 && mm < ms)) score += 10;
      else bad += "MACD crossed against. ";
   }

   // 6. Price still the right side of the EMA50? (8)
   // BUG FIX #54: same class of bug as #49 above (checks #1/#3/#5) — this
   // always read hEma50[e], the FIXED engine-level M5 handle, even for a
   // TREND position actually running on M1 or M3. Comparing an M1 close
   // against an M5 EMA50 is comparing two different clocks; for two of
   // TREND's three timeframes this check was silently reading the wrong
   // data on every single tick. Now uses the position's own timeframe EMA50
   // (g_scE50), exactly like the Supertrend/ADX/MACD reads already do.
   int e50Handle = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0) ? g_scE50[TrendTFOf(tk)] : hEma50[e];
   double c1 = iClose(_Symbol, tf, 1);
   double e50 = Buf(e50Handle,0,1);
   if((dir > 0 && c1 > e50) || (dir < 0 && c1 < e50)) score += 8;
   else bad += "Lost the EMA50. ";

   // 7. STRUCTURE — are we still making higher lows / lower highs? (8)
   double sw1 = (dir > 0) ? iLow(_Symbol,tf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_LOW,4,1))
                          : iHigh(_Symbol,tf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_HIGH,4,1));
   double sw2 = (dir > 0) ? iLow(_Symbol,tf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_LOW,4,6))
                          : iHigh(_Symbol,tf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_HIGH,4,6));
   if((dir > 0 && sw1 >= sw2) || (dir < 0 && sw1 <= sw2)) score += 8;
   else bad += "Structure broke. ";

   // 8. PARTICIPATION — a trend on no volume is a drift, not a trend. (7)
   double rv = RelVol(tf, 1);
   if(!G_ExitOnVolDry || rv >= 0.75) score += 7;
   else bad += "Volume dried up. ";

   // 9. EXHAUSTION — new extreme on WEAKER momentum is the classic warning
   //    that the move is finishing. This is the one that gets you out at the top. (0 or penalty)
   double hNow = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,1), hPrev = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,5);
   double lNow = iLow(_Symbol,tf,1),  lPrev = iLow(_Symbol,tf,5);
   // BUG FIX #54c: same fix as #54 above, applied to the divergence check —
   // was reading hRsi[e] (fixed M5) regardless of which timeframe the TREND
   // position actually lives on.
   int rsiHandle = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0) ? g_scRsi[TrendTFOf(tk)] : hRsi[e];
   double r1 = Buf(rsiHandle,0,1), r5 = Buf(rsiHandle,0,5);
   bool diverge = G_ExitOnDiverge && ((dir > 0 && hNow > hPrev && r1 < r5 - 3.0) ||
                  (dir < 0 && lNow < lPrev && r1 > r5 + 3.0));
   if(!diverge) score += 0;   // no bonus — this is a penalty check only
   else { score -= 15; bad += "DIVERGENCE: new extreme on weaker momentum. "; }

   // 10. FAST-TAPE STALL CHECK (M1). Reads 1-6 already cover the position's
   //     own timeframe and H1 — but both only refresh once per M5 (or H1) bar,
   //     which can be too slow to catch a trade stalling out in real time.
   //     M1 is the fastest read available: has price made ANY net progress in
   //     our favour over the last several M1 bars, or has it simply gone flat
   //     since entry? A flat M1 tape after entry is the earliest, sharpest
   //     signal that this specific push isn't developing — well before ADX,
   //     H1, or the M5 structure checks above would notice. This is a penalty
   //     check only (like divergence, #9) so it never inflates a genuinely
   //     healthy trade's score — it only catches the stall case.
   double m1c1 = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, 1);   // last CLOSED M1 bar, not the forming one
   double m1sum = 0; int m1n = 0;
   for(int mi = 5; mi <= 9; mi++)
   {
      double v = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_M1, mi);
      if(v > 0) { m1sum += v; m1n++; }
   }
   // BUG FIX #61: same category of bug the chop-cluster fix already called
   // out elsewhere — this compares against M1 bars 5-9 back with NO regard
   // for whether the trade has existed that long. A trade opened 2 minutes
   // ago gets judged against M1 price action from before it even existed,
   // exactly the "closes at the beginning" pattern. Now requires at least
   // 9 real minutes since entry before this check applies at all.
   long secsHeld61 = (tk > 0) ? (TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME)) : 999999;
   if(m1c1 > 0 && m1n > 0 && secsHeld61 >= 9 * 60)
   {
      double m1avg = m1sum / m1n;   // average of bars 5-9 back, not one noisy point
      bool stalling = (dir > 0) ? (m1c1 <= m1avg) : (m1c1 >= m1avg);
      if(stalling && profitR < 0.5)
      { score -= 10; bad += "M1 tape flat since entry — not developing. "; }
   }

   // 11. CHOP AFTER PROFIT — check #10 above only applied early (profitR <
   //     0.5), so a trade that ran into real profit and THEN stalled into
   //     chop got no penalty from it at all. That is the case the user
   //     actually described: a trend that was genuinely working, then
   //     stopped continuing — the market saying "done" after already
   //     paying out, not a trade that never got going. Giving back real
   //     profit to a stall is worse than an early trade not working, so
   //     this checks specifically for that: real net progress vs range
   //     traveled, on the position's own timeframe, once profit is on the
   //     table. Same signature as the entry-side and Pine health() fixes.
   if(profitR >= 0.5)
   {
      double hi6P = iHigh(_Symbol,tf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_HIGH,6,1));
      double lo6P = iLow(_Symbol,tf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,tf,MODE_LOW,6,1));
      double rangeP = hi6P - lo6P;
      double netP = MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol,tf,1) - iClose(_Symbol,tf,6));
      bool choppyP = rangeP > 0 && (netP / rangeP) < 0.40;
      if(choppyP)
      { score -= 20; bad += "Chopping after profit — stopped continuing. "; }
   }

   // 12. CANDLESTICK REJECTION — ported from Pine, which already checked
   // this on the open trade (candleAgainstTrade); the EA never did. A real
   // bearish engulfing/pin-bar/doji-at-extreme against a long (or the
   // mirror for a short) on the position's own timeframe is concrete price
   // evidence the move may be turning, on top of everything the indicators
   // above already say. Same modest weight Pine uses (a nudge, not a verdict).
   if(CandleAgainst(tf, dir))
   { score -= 8; bad += "Candlestick rejection against the position. "; }

   // 13. TWO-POLE TURNED AGAINST — ported from Pine's exit-evidence use of
   // Two-Pole (tpAgainst), which the EA never had. The whole point of
   // Two-Pole is that it turns BEFORE Supertrend confirms — so a turn
   // against this position, on its own timeframe, right now, is some of
   // the earliest evidence available that the move may be finishing.
   // Same standing as the candlestick check above: a nudge, not a verdict.
   if(T_UseTwoPole && TwoPoleTurnedAgainstTf(tf, dir))
   { score -= 10; bad += "Two-Pole turned against the position. "; }

   verdict = (bad == "") ? "thesis intact" : bad;
   return (int)MathMax(0, MathMin(100, score));
}

// Runs on EVERY position, EVERY tick. This is the active defence.
// SAFETY NET. No engine places pending orders any more, but a stray one can still
// exist — left by an older build, placed by hand, or created by a broker-side
// event. If the daily loss limit fires and something is still resting on the
// book, it can fill AFTER the halt and walk straight through it. So: sweep.
void KillAllPendings()
{
   for(int i = OrdersTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong ot = OrderGetTicket(i);
      if(!OrderSelect(ot)) continue;
      if(OrderGetString(ORDER_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(OrderGetInteger(ORDER_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;   // ours only, never the user's
      trade.OrderDelete(ot);
   }
}

// The no-opposing-direction guard lives inside Open(). A PENDING order never goes
// through Open(), so it could fill against a live position and hedge us by
// accident. Same safety net.
void EnforceNoOpposingPendings()
{
   if(!InpNoOpposing) return;
   int net = NetDirection();
   if(net == 0) return;

   for(int i = OrdersTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong ot = OrderGetTicket(i);
      if(!OrderSelect(ot)) continue;
      if(OrderGetString(ORDER_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(OrderGetInteger(ORDER_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;

      ENUM_ORDER_TYPE t = (ENUM_ORDER_TYPE)OrderGetInteger(ORDER_TYPE);
      int oDir = (t == ORDER_TYPE_BUY_STOP  || t == ORDER_TYPE_BUY_LIMIT)  ?  1 :
                 (t == ORDER_TYPE_SELL_STOP || t == ORDER_TYPE_SELL_LIMIT) ? -1 : 0;
      if(oDir == 0) continue;

      if((net > 0 && oDir < 0) || (net < 0 && oDir > 0))
      {
         trade.OrderDelete(ot);
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("Deleted a stray pending %s — it would have opened against live %s exposure.",
                        oDir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", net > 0 ? "LONG" : "SHORT");
      }
   }
}

void Guardian(int e, ulong tk, int dir, double entry, double R, double profitR)
{
   if(!G_Enable || R <= 0) return;
   long guardAge = TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   if(T_MinHoldSecs > 0 && guardAge < (long)T_MinHoldSecs) return;   // (v17.50) Guardian ran BEFORE inMinHold, so a 1-second-old trade could be closed before the grace applied

   string why;
   int score = ThesisScore(e, dir, profitR, why, tk);

   // ── HARD EXITS: these do not care about the score or the profit ──

   // The spread has blown out. Something is wrong — news, a gap, a broker issue.
   // Whatever it is, you do not want to be holding through it on leverage.
   // BUG FIX #58b: same issue as #58 above — this required sp > 0.5 as a
   // flat absolute floor, meaningless off gold's price scale. R (this
   // trade's own stop distance) is already correctly scaled to whatever
   // instrument this position is actually on, so it's used as the
   // reference instead — the spread has to be a genuinely meaningful slice
   // of the trade's own risk unit (8%), not an arbitrary gold-sized number.
   double sp = SpreadUSD();
   if(g_spreadAvg > 0 && sp > g_spreadAvg * G_SpreadPanic && sp > 0.08 * R)
   {
      PrintFormat("[%s] SPREAD PANIC: %.2f vs normal %.2f. Flattening — something is wrong.",
                  Cfg[e].name, sp, g_spreadAvg);
      CloseTagged("SPREAD-PANIC", tk);
      return;
   }

   // The H1 flipped against us. Softened (v12): H1 alone against was closing
   // valid M1/M3/M5 flip trades because H1 hadn't caught up yet — the whole
   // point of trading fast timeframes is catching the turn BEFORE H1 confirms.
   // Now requires H1 against AND at least one other confirmation (M15 also
   // against, OR the trade already showing a real loss). H1 catching up
   // slowly to a fresh M3 trade is not evidence to close it.
   if(G_ExitOnHtfFlip)
   {
      double h1c = iClose(_Symbol, PERIOD_H1, 1);
      double h1e = Buf(g_hH1Ema, 0, 1);
      if(h1e > 0)
      {
         int h1d = (h1c > h1e) ? 1 : -1;
         if(h1d != dir && profitR > -0.5)
         {
            // second-opinion check: is M15 ALSO against, or is this trade actually losing?
            double m15st; int m15sd; Supertrend(E_APEX, m15st, m15sd);
            bool m15Against = (m15sd != 0 && m15sd != dir);
            bool realLoss   = (profitR < -0.3);
            if(m15Against || realLoss)
            {
               PrintFormat("[%s] H1 FLIPPED against us at %+.1fR, and %s. This trade is now counter-trend. Out.",
                           Cfg[e].name, profitR,
                           m15Against ? "M15 agrees against" : "the trade is already losing");
               CloseTagged("H1-FLIP", tk);
               return;
            }
            // else: H1 alone against a fresh fast-timeframe trade → wait, don't close.
         }
      }
   }

   // ── GRADED RESPONSE ──
   // ROOT-CAUSE FIX (live-data diagnosis): G_MinProfitR defaults to -0.3,
   // meaning this full-close could fire the moment a trade is barely
   // negative — which is true for almost EVERY trade in its first couple
   // of minutes, before it has had any real chance to develop. Worse, two
   // of ThesisScore's penalty checks (H1 disagreement, "M1 tape flat") are
   // MORE likely to be true on a brand-new trade than a mature one — a
   // fresh fast-timeframe flip often hasn't dragged H1 around yet, and a
   // trade that's 2 minutes old has, by definition, not developed. That
   // combination is exactly the reported live pattern: trades scoring
   // below the exit floor and closing within their first few minutes,
   // before the actual trend even gets going. This adds a genuine minimum
   // age — measured in the position's OWN bars, so it scales correctly
   // across M1/M3/M5 — before the cumulative SCORE is allowed to force a
   // full close. Real hard exits above (spread panic, the second-opinion
   // H1+M15+realloss check, the unconditional flip-close elsewhere) are
   // NOT affected — they stay fully live from bar one, because they are
   // each a single, unambiguous, real signal, not an accumulation of
   // still-settling soft evidence.
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES gTf = (e == E_TREND && tk > 0) ? g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)] : Cfg[e].tf;
   datetime gOpenTime = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   long gBarsHeld = (TimeCurrent() - gOpenTime) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(gTf), 1);
   bool gPastGrace = (gBarsHeld >= G_MinBarsBeforeExit);

   // Below the exit floor, and the loss is contained: the premise is dead. Leave.
   if(gPastGrace && score < G_ExitBelow && profitR >= G_MinProfitR)
   {
      PrintFormat("[%s] THESIS DEAD (%d/100) at %+.1fR. %s Closing — I would not take this trade now.",
                  Cfg[e].name, score, profitR, why);
      CloseTagged("THESIS-DEAD", tk);
      return;
   }

   // Breaking down, and we are in profit: bank half, let the rest prove itself.
   if(score < G_PartialBelow && profitR >= 0.6 && (!AlreadyBooked(tk) || T_StepPartials))
   {
      double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      // (v18.05) was a HALF of the position on one event; now one 0.01 step.
      double half = StepBank(tk, vol, MathFloor((vol / 2.0) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep);
      if(half >= g_lotMin && vol - half >= g_lotMin)
      {
         if(trade.PositionClosePartial(tk, half))
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] THESIS BREAKING (%d/100) at +%.1fR. %s Banked half. The rest can prove itself.",
                        Cfg[e].name, score, profitR, why);
            MarkBooked(tk);   // do not do this twice on the same position
         }
      }
   }

   // Weakening: pull the stop in hard. Give it much less room to go wrong.
   // BUG FIX #39: this required profitR > 0.3, so a trade that stalled flat
   // or dipped slightly negative right after entry — before ever reaching
   // 0.3R — got NO tightening from the thesis score, on top of getting no
   // Supertrend trail either (see fix #38). That is precisely the "signal
   // fired, price went flat/rolled over" case: the thesis score can see the
   // trend dying in real time, but was blocked from acting until the trade
   // had already produced profit. Now it can tighten as soon as the loss is
   // contained (matches G_MinProfitR, the same floor the exit branch above
   // already uses), not only once in profit.
   if(score < G_TightenBelow && profitR >= G_MinProfitR)
   {
      double atr = Buf(hAtr[e],0,1);
      double px  = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID) : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
      double sl  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      double tp  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_TP);
      double tight = (dir > 0) ? px - 0.8 * atr : px + 0.8 * atr;
      double newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(sl, tight) : MathMin(sl, tight);
      if(MathAbs(newSL - sl) > _Point)
      {
         ClampStops(dir, px, newSL, tp);
         bool ok = (dir > 0) ? (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID) - newSL) >= g_minStop
                             : (newSL - SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK)) >= g_minStop;
         if(ok)
         {
            if(ModifySLTP(tk, newSL, tp, "thesis-weakening pull-in") && InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[%s] thesis weakening (%d/100). Stop pulled in to %.2f. %s",
                           Cfg[e].name, score, newSL, why);
         }
      }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  PER-ENGINE POSITION MANAGEMENT
//  Every engine gets its OWN exit logic. This is the whole point.
//================================================================
// BUG FIX #15 (SEVERE) — THE WORST ONE IN THE FILE.
//
// ManageEngine() called PosOf(e), which returns the FIRST position it finds
// with that magic. On a NETTING account that is fine — there is only ever one.
// But most MT5 gold brokers are HEDGING accounts, and on a hedging account
// every APEX pyramid add opens its OWN separate position with the same magic.
//
// So: the first APEX position got the widening trail, the breakeven, the health
// exit, everything. And every pyramid add after it got NOTHING. No trail, no
// breakeven, no exit — just its initial stop, sitting there forever while the
// engine believed it was managing them.
//
// The pyramid is the entire reason APEX exists. It was silently broken on the
// account type you are most likely to be using.
//
// Now: ManageEngine loops EVERY position belonging to the engine, and each one
// carries its own R.
// (v16.46) Range-scalp clock multiplier. M1 keeps the original 0.8R fast bank.
// Slower clocks earn proportionally more room before the chop-scalp cap applies.
double RangeScalpClockMult(ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf)
{
   switch(tf)
   {
      case PERIOD_M1:  return 1.00;   // 0.8R = 4.8 pts
      case PERIOD_M3:  return 1.75;   // 1.4R = 12.6 pts
      case PERIOD_M5:  return 4.00;   // (v16.69) 2.50 -> 4.00 = 3.2R = ~38 pts. His 28 Jul M5 chart shows
                                     // ordinary chop legs of 10-25 points and real legs of 50-68. Banking an
                                     // M5 trade at 24 points was clipping the middle of the moves that pay.
      default:         return 5.00;   // (v16.69) 3.00 -> 5.00 for M15+, same reasoning scaled up
   }
}

void ManagePosition(int e, ulong tk);

void ManageEngine(int e)
{
   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[e].magic) continue;
      ManagePosition(e, tk);
   }
}


//================================================================
//  (v19.06 BV)  THE PER-TRADE LOCK — his 26 Aug data, one mechanism
//
//  HIS PANEL, SPLIT BY EXIT BUCKET. Peak = net + give-back:
//     BASKET-LOCK   15 trades   GBP24.60 of peak   avg 1.64   KEPT 98%
//     SL-HIT       109 trades  GBP176.41 of peak   avg 1.62   KEPT 0%
//  Identical opportunity per trade. Opposite outcome. GBP176 gone.
//
//  WHY. Three reasons, all arithmetic:
//   1. Every per-trade banking threshold is 3.0 points -- T_BeArmPts,
//      T_TrailArmPts, T_GivebackMidPts. At 0.01 lots that is GBP2.20; at 0.02,
//      GBP4.40. The average trade PEAKED at GBP1.62. None of them can arm.
//   2. Even if one did, CloseTagged() carries T_MinWinnerExitPts = 6.00 points
//      = GBP4.40, which vetoes closing any winner holding less than that. So a
//      GBP1.62 peak could not be banked by anything.
//   3. BASKET-LOCK is on the exempt list for that floor. That is the entire
//      reason it kept 98% while everything else kept nothing.
//  So those 109 trades had no exit available except their own stop loss.
//
//  THE FIX IS NOT A NEW IDEA. It is BASKET-LOCK applied to ONE ticket:
//     · a floor anchored to THIS TRADE'S peak, not to its entry
//     · armed at T_TradeLockArmBands noise bands, giving back one
//     · closed at MARKET, not left to a resting stop the spread can undercut
//     · and TAG-EXEMPT from the winner floor, like BASKET-LOCK
//
//  HIS OWN IDEA, CORRECTED. He asked for "SL at GBP1 profit, trailing up".
//  Right instinct, wrong unit. A flat GBP1 is another fixed ruler: unreachable
//  on a dead night, a joke on a news spike. One noise band at today's M1 ATR
//  (~0.35) is 0.51 points = GBP0.37 at 0.01 lots, so this arms at GBP0.75 and
//  banks peak minus GBP0.37 -- which IS about a pound today, and is derived
//  rather than typed. And because the floor is anchored to the peak it RISES:
//  a trade peaking at GBP8 banks GBP7.63. It protects the small ones without
//  capping the big ones, which is the thing a flat GBP1 stop cannot do.
//
//  WHAT THIS COSTS. It will close some trades that would have recovered. That
//  is the trade being made deliberately: on 26 Aug the recovery rate of a
//  given-back peak was zero out of 109.
//  OFF-SWITCH: T_TradeLock = false.
//================================================================
// (v19.06 BV) inputs and registry are declared up at the PtsScale block, because
// the CloseTagged() floor exemption at ~line 8205 reads T_TradeLock and MQL5
// requires declaration before use. Same class as the v18.73 ScalpST forward
// declaration and the v19.05 Buf() one -- declaration order, third time.

// Same slot discipline as PeakR: a slot is only reused once its ticket is no
// longer an open position. The v17.18 blind-wrap bug is not being repeated here.
double TradePeakCash(ulong tk, double nowCash)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_tlN; i++)
      if(g_tlTk[i] == tk)
      { if(nowCash > g_tlPk[i]) g_tlPk[i] = nowCash; return g_tlPk[i]; }
   for(int i = 0; i < g_tlN; i++)
      if(SlotDead(g_tlTk[i], tk))
      { g_tlTk[i] = tk; g_tlPk[i] = nowCash; return nowCash; }
   if(g_tlN < 128) { g_tlTk[g_tlN] = tk; g_tlPk[g_tlN] = nowCash; g_tlN++; return nowCash; }
   PrintFormat("[TRADE LOCK] table FULL -- #%I64u untracked, its peak is unprotected.", tk);
   return nowCash;
}

// (v19.10 BZ) full reasoning at the T_InfantCut inputs. No MAE registry is needed:
// we test the CURRENT floating loss on every tick, so the first tick that breaches
// the level is the one that acts. The peak comes from the registry TRADE-LOCK
// already maintains, which is what keeps the two mechanisms from fighting.
void InfantCutCheck(ulong tk)
{
   if(!T_InfantCut) return;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;
   long ot  = (long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   int  age = (int)(TimeCurrent() - ot);
   if(age > T_InfantSecs) return;              // grown up; not this rule's business
   double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   double now = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
   if(vol <= 0.0 || now >= 0.0) return;        // not adverse, nothing to judge
   double bandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[0], 0, 1));
   if(bandPts <= 0.0) return;
   double band  = MathAbs(LossPerLot(bandPts))  * vol;
   double spCsh = MathAbs(LossPerLot(SpreadPtsNow())) * vol;
   if(band <= 0.0) return;
   double cutAt = MathMax(T_InfantBands * band, T_InfantSpreadMult * spCsh);
   if(cutAt <= 0.0) return;
   double pk = TradePeakCash(tk, now);
   if(pk >= cutAt * T_InfantGreenFrac) return; // it DID work once -> TRADE-LOCK owns it
   if(-now < cutAt) return;
   PrintFormat("[INFANT CUT] #%I64u is %.2f down at %ds old, cut level %.2f "
               "(%.1f bands = %.2f, %.1f spreads = %.2f) and its best was only %.2f. "
               "In his 26 Aug data the median winner's worst moment was 0.00 -- a trade "
               "this far adverse this early is not a winner having a wobble.",
               tk, now, age, cutAt, T_InfantBands, T_InfantBands * band,
               T_InfantSpreadMult, T_InfantSpreadMult * spCsh, pk);
   CloseTagged("INFANT-CUT", tk);
}

// (v19.17 CG) full reasoning at the T_ProvePress inputs. Runs AFTER InfantCutCheck
// so a trade that failed its first minute is already gone and can never be pressed.
ulong  g_prTk[128];
int    g_prN = 0;
int    g_prDay = 0;
datetime g_prDate = 0;

bool ProveAlreadyPressed(ulong tk)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_prN; i++) if(g_prTk[i] == tk) return true;
   return false;
}

void ProvePressCheck(int e, ulong tk)
{
   if(!T_ProvePress || T_ProveTargetLots <= 0.0) return;
   MqlDateTime dt; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), dt);
   datetime d0 = StringToTime(StringFormat("%04d.%02d.%02d", dt.year, dt.mon, dt.day));
   if(d0 != g_prDate) { g_prDate = d0; g_prDay = 0; g_prN = 0; }
   if(g_prDay >= T_ProveMaxPerDay) return;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;
   if(ProveAlreadyPressed(tk)) return;

   long ot  = (long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   int  age = (int)(TimeCurrent() - ot);
   if(age < T_ProveSecs) return;                    // not old enough to have proved anything
   if(age > T_ProveSecs + 30) return;               // window passed; do not press a stale trade

   double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   double now = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
   if(vol <= 0.0 || vol >= T_ProveTargetLots - 1e-9) return;

   // The trade must be at or above water NOW. Its worst moment is already handled:
   // InfantCutCheck runs first and closes anything that went meaningfully adverse,
   // so simply still being here at T_ProveSecs is most of the evidence.
   if(now < -T_ProveMaxAdverse) return;

   int    dir  = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   double add  = T_ProveTargetLots - vol;
   double step = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP);
   if(step > 0.0) add = MathFloor(add / step) * step;
   double aMin = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN);
   if(add < aMin - 1e-9) return;

   // Open() is Open(e, dir, sl, tp, why, tag) -- it sizes ITSELF via LotFor and
   // cannot be handed a volume, so the add goes through the same CTrade path the
   // APEX scale-add uses. (Fifth signature/order trap this session; I checked it
   // before writing this time.)
   double psl = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
   if(psl <= 0.0) return;                      // never add to an unprotected position
   if(T_AddsRespectCaps)
   {
      if(DirExposureFull(dir, add))
      {
         PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS REFUSED] direction ceiling. %.2f wanted, %.2f open this way.",
                     add, OpenLotsThisDir(dir));
         return;
      }
      if(!MarginOkFor(add, dir))
      {
         PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS REFUSED] margin. %.2f lots would breach the free-margin floor.", add);
         return;
      }
   }
   PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS] #%I64u is %ds old, %+.2f, never scratched -> adding %.2f to reach %.2f, "
               "stop shared at %.2f. In his 26 Aug book, trades that had not gone adverse by 60s won "
               "82.6%% of the time; trades that had won 6.2%%. This is the fakeout filter he asked "
               "for, one minute late.",
               tk, age, now, add, T_ProveTargetLots, psl);
   trade.SetExpertMagicNumber(Cfg[e].magic);
   bool okAdd = (dir > 0) ? trade.Buy(add, _Symbol, 0.0, NP(psl), 0.0, "PROVE-PRESS")
                          : trade.Sell(add, _Symbol, 0.0, NP(psl), 0.0, "PROVE-PRESS");
   if(okAdd)
   {
      if(g_prN < 128) g_prTk[g_prN++] = tk;
      g_prDay++;
   }
   else
      PrintFormat("[PROVE-PRESS] send failed, retcode %d. The base position is untouched.",
                  trade.ResultRetcode());
}

void TradeLockCheck(ulong tk)
{
   if(!T_TradeLock) return;
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;
   double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
   double now = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
   if(vol <= 0.0) return;
   double pk  = TradePeakCash(tk, now);
   // one noise band, priced at THIS position's own size -- the same helper pair
   // BASKET-LOCK uses, so the two mechanisms cannot drift apart.
   double bandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[0], 0, 1));
   if(bandPts <= 0.0) return;
   double band = MathAbs(LossPerLot(bandPts)) * vol;
   if(band <= 0.0) return;
   double armAt = MathMax(T_TradeLockMinCash, T_TradeLockArmBands * band);
   if(pk < armAt) return;
   // (v19.07 BW) PROPORTIONAL. A flat band would cap every runner -- see the harm
   // audit at the inputs. One band is only the FLOOR of the allowance.
   double give    = MathMax(T_TradeLockGiveBands * band, T_TradeLockGivePct * pk);
   double floorAt = pk - give;
   // armAt is at least 2 bands and we give back 1, so the floor is always at
   // least one band of REAL money. It can never arm and close on the same tick.
   if(floorAt <= 0.0) return;
   if(now > floorAt)  return;
   PrintFormat("[TRADE LOCK] #%I64u peaked at %.2f, fell to %.2f, floor was %.2f. "
               "Banking at market. (%.2f lots, one band = %.2f, arm was %.2f.) "
               "This is the exit those 109 SL-HIT trades did not have.",
               tk, pk, now, floorAt, vol, band, armAt);
   CloseTagged("TRADE-LOCK", tk);
}

void ManagePosition(int e, ulong tk)
{
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;

   int    dir   = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
   double entry = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
   double sl    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
   double tp    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_TP);
   double bid   = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);
   double ask   = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
   double px    = (dir > 0) ? bid : ask;
   double atr   = (e == E_TREND) ? Buf(g_scAtr[TrendTFOf(tk)],0,1) : Buf(hAtr[e],0,1);
   if(atr <= 0 || sl == 0) return;

   TrackProgress(tk, dir, px);

   // (v18.29) the loss cap runs FIRST, before any trail/partial/hold logic, so a
   // trade that is already past the cap cannot be talked into staying open by a
   // hold vote. Returns after closing.
   MaxLossCheck(e, tk);
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;   // it was closed above

   // (v19.10 BZ) the infant cut runs before the lock: a trade that has never been
   // green has no peak for the lock to protect, and 77% of his loss lives here.
   InfantCutCheck(tk);
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;   // scratched above

   // (v19.17 CG) press AFTER the infant cut, so only survivors are ever pressed.
   ProvePressCheck(e, tk);
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;

   // (v19.06 BV) the per-trade lock runs SECOND, for the same reason the loss cap
   // runs first: a peak that has already been given back must not be talked into
   // staying open by a hold vote further down. Returns after closing.
   TradeLockCheck(tk);
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;   // banked above

   // BUG FIX #14: R now comes from the PER-TICKET store. The old version
   // back-solved it from the TP — impossible for APEX (tp = 0 by design), and
   // wrong for any position whose TP was later modified by the dynamic-TP logic.
   // It also used ONE global (g_apexR) for every APEX position, so after the
   // first pyramid add, every trail trigger and breakeven was computed from the
   // wrong R.
   double R = RDistOf(tk);
   if(R <= 0)
   {
      // first sight of this ticket (EA restarted, or a pending just filled):
      // reconstruct it once, then remember it.
      double tpR = (e==E_TREND) ? T_Tp3R : (e==E_SCALP) ? SC_TpR : 0.0;   // APEX has no TP, by design
      if(tp > 0 && tpR > 0) R = MathAbs(tp - entry) / tpR;
      if(R <= 0)            R = MathAbs(entry - sl);
      if(R <= 0)            R = 1.3 * atr;   // never let R be zero
      StoreRDist(tk, R);
   }

   double profitR = ((dir > 0) ? (px - entry) : (entry - px)) / R;

   // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #1 — SPREAD-AWARE ENTRY GRACE (the false-stop fix) ═══
   // Your complaint: "the broker's buy price differs from the real price, so
   // at the very start the EA thinks we're already in a small loss / near
   // stop." That is exactly right and it was real. profitR above measures
   // from YOUR fill (entry, which includes the spread you paid) against the
   // current bid/ask — so the instant a trade opens it shows roughly -1
   // spread of "loss" before price has done anything at all. On a tight
   // signal-line stop that spread can be 10-25% of 1R, and every early
   // management branch below (chop-cluster, stall, giveback arming, the
   // Guardian's "already losing" reads) was seeing a trade "going wrong"
   // when nothing had happened except the cost of entry.
   //
   // spreadR = how much of 1R the current spread eats. A trade that is only
   // down by about the spread, in its first few bars, has NOT gone against
   // us — it just hasn't overcome its own entry cost yet. The early-exit
   // logic uses profitRAdj (profitR with the spread added back) during the
   // grace window, so a brand-new trade is judged on real price movement,
   // not on the toll it paid to get in. The actual stop on the broker is
   // untouched — this only changes how the soft early-exit brains read the
   // trade. Cash risk, the hard SL, everything else: unchanged.
   double spreadNow = SpreadUSD();
   double spreadR   = (R > 0) ? spreadNow / R : 0.0;
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES graceTf = (e == E_TREND) ? g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)] : Cfg[e].tf;
   long   barsHeldGrace = (TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME)) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(graceTf),1);
   bool   inSpreadGrace  = (barsHeldGrace <= 2);   // first ~2 bars on the trade's own timeframe
   double profitRAdj = (inSpreadGrace) ? profitR + spreadR : profitR;

   // (v17.38) MINIMUM-HOLD GRACE — see the T_MinHoldSecs note at its declaration.
   // True while the trade is too young for any DISCRETIONARY exit to act on it.
   // Deliberately does NOT suppress the hard stop, Friday flatten, or gap close.
   long   posAgeSecs   = TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   // (v17.60) The grace is now whichever is LONGER: the seconds figure, or
   // T_MinHoldBars bars on the trade's OWN clock. Seconds alone is the wrong
   // unit — 120s is 2 bars on M1 but under half a bar on M5, so the same number
   // meant two completely different amounts of patience depending on which clock
   // opened the trade. Bars is the unit the signal actually thinks in.
   int    mhK          = (e == E_TREND) ? TrendTFOf(tk) : -1;
   if(e == E_TREND && (mhK < 0 || mhK >= SC_TFN)) mhK = SC_TFN - 1;
   long   mhBarSecs    = (mhK >= 0) ? (long)PeriodSeconds(g_scTF[mhK]) : (long)PeriodSeconds(Cfg[e].tf);
   long   mhMinSecs    = MathMax((long)T_MinHoldSecs, (long)T_MinHoldBars * MathMax(mhBarSecs, 1));
   // ══════════ (v19.11 CA) THE GRACE WINDOW IS INVERTED ══════════
   // T_MinHoldSecs = 180. The calibrated average hold time on his 26 Aug tickets
   // is 240 SECONDS. So three quarters of the average trade's entire life sits
   // inside a window where inMinHold stands down every discretionary exit, and
   // the only escape hatch is being worse than -T_MinHoldDangerR (1.20R), which
   // is most of the way to the stop anyway. Of 32 exit mechanisms in this file,
   // exactly two could act during the period in which 77% of the day's loss was
   // generated: the hard stop, and MAX-LOSS. That is why the attribution reads
   // SL-HIT 109 and MAX-LOSS 37 -- not because those were the right exits, but
   // because they were the ONLY exits available.
   //
   // AND THE EVIDENCE SAYS THE GRACE IS BACKWARDS. Measured on his own tickets,
   // own-exit prices excluded: the MEDIAN WINNER'S WORST MOMENT IS GBP0.00, at
   // 60s, at 120s and at 300s. Winners in this system do not need room. Losers
   // took a median GBP1.12 against them inside the first minute alone. So the
   // benefit of the doubt is being handed to precisely the population that never
   // earns it, and withheld from nothing.
   //
   // THE CHANGE, deliberately narrow: a fresh trade keeps its full grace while it
   // is going its way, or only trivially against. Once it is more than
   // T_MinHoldGiveR adverse, the grace is void and the normal exit stack applies
   // at whatever age it is. The 0.35R default is set just above two spreads so
   // quote noise can never void a grace period -- the same floor reasoning as the
   // infant cut, which handles the harder first-120-second case.
   //
   // HONEST COST: winners' adverse excursion is 0.00 at the median but GBP0.67 at
   // the 75th percentile, so roughly a quarter of winners will have their grace
   // voided at some point. Voided grace is not an exit -- the 30 mechanisms still
   // have to satisfy their own conditions. But some winners will be cut that were
   // not before. That is the trade, made on a 0.00-vs-1.12 median separation.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_MinHoldNeedsGreen = false restores v19.10 behaviour exactly.
   bool   mhVoided     = T_MinHoldNeedsGreen && (profitR < -T_MinHoldGiveR);
   bool   inMinHold    = (T_MinHoldSecs > 0 || T_MinHoldBars > 0)
                         && (posAgeSecs < mhMinSecs)
                         && (profitR > -T_MinHoldDangerR)
                         && !mhVoided;
   if(mhVoided && posAgeSecs < mhMinSecs && InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[%s] GRACE VOIDED at %+.2fR, %ds old. A fresh trade this far adverse has "
                  "forfeited it -- median winner adverse excursion in his data is 0.00. "
                  "The full exit stack is live from here.",
                  Cfg[e].name, profitR, (int)posAgeSecs);

   // (v17.40 — his exact words: "if I made a sell and it closed asap but then 3
   // or 5 shows a sell it needs to make sure it doesn't close.") A trade whose
   // direction is CONFIRMED BY ANOTHER CLOCK is not a failing trade — it is the
   // same idea, agreed on by a slower timeframe. Closing it on this clock's own
   // wobble throws away the exact confirmation the system exists to find.
   // Any OTHER clock's Supertrend agreeing with the trade now stands the
   // discretionary exits down, the same way the min-hold grace does.
   // Protection is untouched: hard stop, Friday flatten, gap close, and the
   // danger floor below all still fire regardless.
   bool otherClockAgrees = false;
   if(T_MultiClockHold && e == E_TREND && profitR > -T_MinHoldDangerR)
   {
      for(int ck = 0; ck < SC_TFN; ck++)
      {
         if(ck == TrendTFOf(tk)) continue;             // not this trade's own clock (v17.44 fix — trK isn't declared yet at this point in the function)
         double svA; int sdA; ScalpST(ck, svA, sdA);
         if(sdA != 0 && sdA == dir) { otherClockAgrees = true; break; }
      }
      if(otherClockAgrees && InpVerboseLog)
         PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: another clock's Supertrend agrees with this %s at %+.2fR — "
                     "discretionary exits stood down, stop still live.",
                     Cfg[e].name, dir>0?"BUY":"SELL", profitR);
   }
   // (v17.59, his repeated ask: "what confirmations tell us to hold — is the
   // DEMA showing the same, how strong is the DEMA") FIFTH HOLD VOTE: DEMA SLOPE.
   // demaSlope was already computed at ENTRY as a +10/-12 confidence tweak and
   // then never consulted again — the hold decision had four votes (ADX regime,
   // structure, DEMA SIDE, cross-clock) and none of them measured whether the
   // DEMA is actually MOVING the trade's way. Side alone says "price is above a
   // flat line"; slope says "the trend itself is still building". Deliberately
   // NOT MACD: MACD is EMA-derived, lags on M1, and flips constantly, so it
   // would manufacture exits rather than holds. This is the same DEMA already
   // on his chart, read over 3 bars, and it only ever ADDS a reason to hold —
   // it can never close a trade.
   bool demaHold = false;
   if(e == E_TREND && T_DemaSlopeHold)
   {
      int dhK = TrendTFOf(tk);
      if(dhK < 0 || dhK >= SC_TFN) dhK = SC_TFN - 1;
      double dNow = DemaValSc(dhK), dPast = Buf(g_scDema[dhK], 0, 4);
      double dAtr = Buf(g_scAtr[dhK], 0, 1);
      if(dNow > 0.0 && dPast > 0.0 && dAtr > 0.0)
      {
         double slope = dNow - dPast;
         // must be a real slope, not indicator noise: at least this fraction of
         // one ATR of travel over three bars, in the trade's direction.
         if(MathAbs(slope) >= T_DemaSlopeMinAtr * dAtr && ((dir > 0 && slope > 0) || (dir < 0 && slope < 0)))
         {
            demaHold = true;
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: DEMA still sloping %s with this %s (%.2f pts over 3 bars = %.2fx ATR) at %+.2fR — the trend is still building.",
                           Cfg[e].name, slope > 0 ? "UP" : "DOWN", dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL",
                           MathAbs(slope), MathAbs(slope) / dAtr, profitR);
         }
      }
   }
   // (v17.62) SIXTH HOLD VOTE: unfilled imbalance behind the trade.
   bool fvgHold = false;
   if(e == E_TREND && T_FvgHold)
   {
      int fvK = TrendTFOf(tk);
      if(fvK < 0 || fvK >= SC_TFN) fvK = SC_TFN - 1;
      double gSz = 0.0;
      if(FvgSupports(g_scTF[fvK], fvK, dir, px, gSz))
      {
         fvgHold = true;
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: unfilled %s FVG of %.2f pts behind this %s at %+.2fR — the move had displacement, not drift.",
                        Cfg[e].name, dir > 0 ? "bullish" : "bearish", MathAbs(gSz), dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", profitR);
      }
   }
   // (v17.64) SEVENTH HOLD VOTE: is there still room in front of the trade?
   bool roomHold = false;
   g_holdToTarget = false;
   if(e == E_TREND && T_RoomToRun)
   {
      int rmK = TrendTFOf(tk);
      if(rmK < 0 || rmK >= SC_TFN) rmK = SC_TFN - 1;
      double rmPts = 0.0, rmLvl = 0.0;
      if(RoomToRun(g_scTF[rmK], rmK, dir, px, rmPts, rmLvl))
      {
         roomHold = true;
         // (v18.21) THE HOLD ESTIMATE HE ASKED FOR — "our hold confirmations
         // clearly aren't working, they need to give us an estimate to hold at."
         // RoomToRun() already measures the distance to the prior swing and the
         // log already calls it "this trade's realistic goal" -- but it was only
         // ever used as a boolean VOTE. The number was computed, printed, and
         // thrown away. It is now an actual target: while the trade has travelled
         // less than T_HoldTargetFrac of that measured room, discretionary exits
         // are stood down. The stop, the trade's own flip, news and gap are not
         // affected. This is the mechanism that makes it a trend follower instead
         // of a candle scalper. OFF-SWITCH: T_HoldToTarget = false.
         if(T_HoldToTarget && rmPts > 0.0)
         {
            double travelled = (px - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)) * dir;
            double target    = rmPts * T_HoldTargetFrac;
            if(travelled > 0.0 && travelled < target)
            {
               g_holdToTarget = true;
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD TO TARGET: %.2f of %.2f pts travelled toward the swing at %.2f "
                              "(%.0f%% of the %.0f%% goal). Discretionary exits stood down; stop still live.",
                              Cfg[e].name, travelled, target, rmLvl,
                              100.0 * travelled / MathMax(target, 0.01), 100.0 * T_HoldTargetFrac);
            }
         }
         if(InpVerboseLog)
         {
            if(rmPts < 0.0)
               PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: no prior swing %s of this %s in %d bars — open air, the goal is wherever the trend stops.",
                           Cfg[e].name, dir > 0 ? "high above" : "low below", dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", T_RoomLookback);
            else
               PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: %.2f pts of room to the prior swing %s at %.2f — that is this trade's realistic goal, not yet reached.",
                           Cfg[e].name, rmPts, dir > 0 ? "high" : "low", rmLvl);
         }
      }
   }
   // (v17.86, his ask: RSI to identify strong trends / filter noise, but "you
   // cannot reduce signal quality for OTHER signals" and "better wrong than
   // late") RSI is added ONLY as an eighth HOLD vote -- same rule as every other
   // vote here: it can add a reason to hold, it can NEVER block or delay an entry.
   // No entry gate reads it. This gives you the noise-filtering benefit (a trade
   // with RSI momentum behind it holds through pullbacks) without slowing down
   // or filtering the entries you explicitly want kept fast and inclusive.
   bool rsiHold = false;
   if(e == E_TREND && T_RsiHold)
   {
      int rsK = TrendTFOf(tk);
      if(rsK < 0 || rsK >= SC_TFN) rsK = SC_TFN - 1;
      double rsiNow = iRSI_Val(rsK);
      if(rsiNow > 0.0)
      {
         bool rsiWithUs = (dir > 0) ? (rsiNow >= T_RsiHoldLevel) : (rsiNow <= (100.0 - T_RsiHoldLevel));
         if(rsiWithUs)
         {
            rsiHold = true;
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD: RSI %.1f still confirms this %s — momentum has not turned.",
                           Cfg[e].name, rsiNow, dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL");
         }
      }
   }
   // (v17.89 AUDIT FIX A) holdGrace was "ANY ONE of six". RSI>=55, room-to-run and
   // a 0.10-ATR DEMA slope are each true almost continuously in a live trend, so
   // one vote being enough meant the discretionary exits were effectively off and
   // profit was given back. Now: the fresh-trade grace (inMinHold) still stands
   // ALONE -- a new trade is protected exactly as before, that behaviour is
   // untouched -- but after the grace expires it takes TWO independent votes to
   // keep standing the exits down. Two agreeing reads is real evidence; one is a
   // coin-flip dressed as confirmation.
   int holdVotes = 0;
   if(otherClockAgrees) holdVotes++;
   if(demaHold)         holdVotes++;
   if(fvgHold)          holdVotes++;
   if(roomHold)         holdVotes++;
   if(rsiHold)          holdVotes++;
   // (v17.92 HOLD-001) A quorum of 2 fixed saturation but not vote QUALITY.
   // roomHold and rsiHold are near-permanently true inside a trend, and demaHold
   // at T_DemaSlopeMinAtr = 0.10 is easy to satisfy -- so "any 2" was routinely
   // satisfied by the three weakest reads while the two carrying real independent
   // information (another CLOCK agreeing, or an unfilled imbalance behind the
   // trade) sat idle. Those two cannot both be true by accident; the other three
   // can. The quorum now requires at least one of them, so standing the exits
   // down always rests on a genuine second opinion rather than on two easy ones.
   bool strongVote = otherClockAgrees || fvgHold;

   // ══════ (v18.20) THE HOLD QUORUM WAS KEEPING LOSERS ALIVE ══════
   // EVIDENCE, his own log, on the counter-trend BUY that lost:
   //     HOLD VOTES 3/2 (clock:1 dema:1 fvg:0 room:1 rsi:0)   at -0.74R
   //     HOLD: another clock's Supertrend agrees with this BUY at -0.74R
   //     HOLD: DEMA still sloping UP with this BUY ... at -0.74R
   // Three votes stood the exits down on a trade already 0.74R underwater, and
   // two of the three were the correlated pair: "another clock's Supertrend" and
   // "price vs DEMA". The entry required Supertrend and DEMA to agree, so in the
   // moments after entry those two votes are near-guaranteed to fire together --
   // they are one fact counted twice. T_MinHoldDangerR only resumes normal rules
   // at -1.20R, so the whole band from break-even to -1.20R was protected by
   // manufactured confidence.
   //
   // TWO CHANGES, both narrow:
   //  1. Below break-even the quorum needs T_HoldVotesLosing (default 3) rather
   //     than T_HoldVotesNeeded (2). Holding a winner needs a reason; holding a
   //     LOSER needs a better one.
   //  2. The correlated pair counts ONCE when the trade is underwater. clock and
   //     dema together contribute one vote, not two, so a hold has to rest on
   //     genuinely independent evidence (imbalance, room, momentum).
   // Winners are untouched: at or above break-even the original tally applies,
   // because cutting winners short is the other failure and it is worse.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_HoldLoserStrict = false restores v18.19 behaviour.
   int  holdVotesEff = holdVotes;
   int  votesNeededEff = T_HoldVotesNeeded;
   if(T_HoldLoserStrict && profitR < 0.0)
   {
      if(otherClockAgrees && demaHold) holdVotesEff--;      // the correlated pair, counted once
      votesNeededEff = MathMax(T_HoldVotesNeeded, T_HoldVotesLosing);
      if(InpVerboseLog && holdVotes > 0)
         PrintFormat("[%s] LOSING-TRADE HOLD TEST at %+.2fR — %d effective votes (clock+dema merged), "
                     "%d required. %s",
                     Cfg[e].name, profitR, holdVotesEff, votesNeededEff,
                     (holdVotesEff >= votesNeededEff) ? "still holding" : "exits released");
   }
   bool holdGrace  = inMinHold || g_holdToTarget
                     || (holdVotesEff >= votesNeededEff && (strongVote || !T_HoldNeedStrong));
   // (v18.80 AG) the same quorum WITHOUT the fresh-trade timer. Used only by the
   // early-fail cut, so a trade that has met every one of early-fail's own
   // conditions is not additionally held by a longer, unrelated clock.
   // See the note at T_FailCutOwnGrace.
   bool holdGraceVotes = g_holdToTarget
                     || (holdVotesEff >= votesNeededEff && (strongVote || !T_HoldNeedStrong));
   if(holdVotes > 0 && InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[%s] HOLD VOTES %d/%d (clock:%d dema:%d fvg:%d room:%d rsi:%d)",
                  Cfg[e].name, holdVotes, T_HoldVotesNeeded, otherClockAgrees, demaHold, fvgHold, roomHold, rsiHold);
   if(inMinHold && InpVerboseLog)
      PrintFormat("[%s] min-hold grace: %ds old at %+.2fR — discretionary exits stood down, stop still live.",
                  Cfg[e].name, (int)posAgeSecs, profitR);

   // (v14 #7) FRIDAY FLATTEN — close everything late Friday. A weekend gap on
   // gold can blow straight through any stop; no open risk is carried into it.
   if(T_FridayFlat)
   {
      MqlDateTime ftm2; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), ftm2);
      if(ftm2.day_of_week == 5 && ftm2.hour >= T_FridayFlatHour)
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] FRIDAY FLATTEN at %+.1fR — no position rides the weekend gap.", Cfg[e].name, profitR);
         ClearPeakR(tk);
         CloseTagged("FRI-FLAT", tk);
         return;
      }
   }

   // ── THE GUARDIAN RUNS FIRST ──
   // Before any engine-specific trail logic, ask whether this trade should still
   // exist at all. If the Guardian closes it, everything below is moot.
   // (#1) During the spread-grace window the Guardian sees profitRAdj, so a
   // trade that is only "down" by the entry spread in its first couple of
   // bars is not treated as an already-losing position and cut prematurely.
   Guardian(e, tk, dir, entry, R, profitRAdj);
   if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) return;   // the Guardian closed it

   // Small bonus lot when the DEMA independently confirms the direction we're
   // already in — separate profit track, banks itself on a small move.
   DemaTopUp(e, tk, dir);
   DemaTopUpBank(e, tk, dir);

   double newSL = sl;

   //---- E0 TREND: Supertrend trail. This is what lets it run to 4.5R. ----
   if(e == E_TREND)
   {
      // BUG FIX #38: the original stop is the SIGNAL LINE at entry, and the
      // Supertrend line only ever moves in the trade's favour once price is
      // running — but before T_Tp1R (1.5R) NOTHING re-read it. A trade that
      // stalls flat or rolls over between 0 and 1.5R sat on the original wide
      // stop the entire time, with no protection engaging at all — this is
      // the exact "BUY signal, price stays flat / rolls over" failure case.
      // The signal-line stop philosophy itself already says "the stop rides
      // the line" — this was never applied below 1.5R. Now it is applied from
      // the very first bar: if the Supertrend line has moved to a tighter,
      // still-favourable level than the original stop, the stop follows it —
      // same "only ever ratchets in our favour" rule as the Pine, just active
      // the whole time instead of switching on at 1.5R. Breakeven and the
      // wider late-stage trail below still only arrive at T_Tp1R, exactly as
      // before — this only closes the gap where a stall gave back ground
      // with no active defence at all.
      int trK = TrendTFOf(tk);
      // (v17.18) g_scTF[trK] was indexed with no bound check at all. TrendTFOf
      // cannot currently return out of range, but this is the first read of the
      // whole management block and an out-of-range index here would throw
      // "array out of range" and ABORT OnTick — leaving the position completely
      // unmanaged for that tick. Cheap insurance on the single most important
      // index in the file.
      if(trK < 0 || trK >= SC_TFN) trK = SC_TFN - 1;
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES trTf = g_scTF[trK];

      // PEAK-PROFIT GIVEBACK GUARD (fix #52, refined) — the direct fix for
      // "peaked at 9 quid, closed at 2." Track this trade's own best R
      // reached, and if profit falls back too far from that peak, close
      // now rather than waiting for the Supertrend line to eventually catch
      // up. Only arms once T_GivebackMinR is on the table.
      //
      // RE-AUDIT FIX: a flat 35% giveback regardless of peak size could
      // conflict with the widening-trail mechanism above, which deliberately
      // gives a large, genuinely running trend MORE room the deeper into
      // profit it goes (a 6R+ trend pulls back harder than a 2R one, on
      // purpose). A flat percentage guard could force-close a real trend at
      // the same tolerance as a modest one, undermining that design. The
      // allowed giveback now scales the same direction as the trail: tighter
      // for a modest peak (protect it fast, matches the user's 9-to-2 case,
      // which peaked in the 2-5R modest range), wider for a large one (let
      // it breathe, matches the widening trail's own room) — so the two
      // safety nets now agree instead of potentially fighting each other.
      UpdatePeakR(tk, profitR);
      double peakR = PeakR(tk);

      // ═══ (v16.25) MARKET-STRUCTURE READ ON THE OPEN TRADE ═══
      // Two jobs, both from the trader's own reasoning:
      //  (a) PATIENCE — an M3/M5 trade will NOT show profit immediately,
      //      because M1-speed noise moves against it first. While the swing
      //      structure on ITS OWN timeframe is still intact, that noise is
      //      noise BY DEFINITION, so for its first T_PatienceBars bars the
      //      fast banks (range-scalp / small-move / chop-stall) stand down
      //      and the trade is allowed to breathe. The STOP still protects it.
      //  (b) CHoCH EXIT — the objective line in the sand. When price closes
      //      beyond the protective swing, the trend is damaged: bank what's
      //      there rather than "telling ourselves it's just a pullback".
      bool structIntact = true;
      bool structPatient = false;
      bool healthyTrend = false;
      // (v17.11 FAIL-OPEN FIX) structIntact initialises to TRUE and was only
      // ever assigned inside this branch. If the clock registry could not
      // resolve trK (slot lost / not yet mapped) it stayed TRUE, which stands
      // the giveback guard and the ratchet tightening DOWN for the whole life
      // of that position — the two instruments that protect an open runner,
      // silently disabled by a bookkeeping miss. Unknown structure must fail
      // SAFE (guards active), not open.
      // (v17.18) NOTE ON A BAD v17.11 FIX OF MY OWN: this guard was written to
      // make unknown structure fail SAFE, but it was DEAD CODE — TrendTFOf()
      // never returns a negative value, it returns SC_TFN-1 on a miss. The real
      // defect was the silent M5 fallback, now handled in TrendTFOf itself.
      // Kept as a genuine belt-and-braces bound check, not as the fix.
      if(e == E_TREND && (trK < 0 || trK >= SC_TFN)) { structIntact = false; trK = SC_TFN - 1; }
      if(e == E_TREND && trK >= 0)
      {
         structIntact = StructureIntact(trTf, dir);
         // (v16.26) HEALTHY TREND — the fix for "it doesn't follow trends to a
         // good peak". While the regime is TRENDING and the swing structure is
         // still INTACT, this trade is in a live, healthy trend: the small
         // fast banks (range-scalp / small-move-candle / chop-stall) are CHOP
         // tools and have no business firing here. They stand down entirely,
         // and the trade is handed to the instruments that are actually built
         // to ride a runner: the trailing stop, the peak-lock, the giveback
         // guard, a genuine CHoCH, or the opposite Supertrend flip. This is
         // what lets a real trend run to its peak instead of being clipped at
         // +0.3R by a single candle in a trend that is still perfectly alive.
         int rgTrade = T_RegimeEngine ? RegimeOf(g_scAdx[trK], trTf) : 0;
         // (v17.11 DEAD-LOGIC FIX) This read `&& structIntact`, which made
         // healthyTrend a strict SUBSET of structIntact. Every downstream test
         // is the disjunction `healthyTrend || structIntact` (ratchet loosening,
         // L~9710) or its negation `!healthyTrend && !structIntact` (structure-
         // break cut, L~9423) — and A||B collapses to B when A implies B. So the
         // ADX/regime term contributed NOTHING to either decision: it was
         // impossible for healthyTrend to be true while structIntact was false.
         // T_TrendRideGuard and every regime threshold were inert for both.
         // Dropping the redundant conjunct is what makes the documented intent
         // ("healthyTrend is the ADX read, structIntact is the chop read") real.
         healthyTrend = T_TrendRideGuard && (rgTrade > 0);
         if(T_StructurePatience && structIntact)
         {
            datetime opT = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            int barsOwn = (int)((TimeCurrent() - opT) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1));
            structPatient = (barsOwn < T_PatienceBars);
         }
         // ═══ (v16.36) EARLY-FAILURE CUT — the single highest-leverage change ═══
         // Decomposing a day's P&L shows one cohort dominates everything: trades
         // that NEVER reach breakeven and therefore ride all the way to the full
         // stop. At a ~5pt signal-line stop that is ~GBP3.75 each; on 80 trades a
         // day a 30-40% full-stop share is GBP90-120 of losses the winners must
         // clear before anything is earned. Between a 25% and a 40% full-stop
         // share the day swings by ~GBP45 — more than any entry filter or sizing
         // tweak can move it. Halving that one term is worth ~GBP45-60/day.
         //
         // This is NOT a tighter stop (he has ruled that out, correctly — a tight
         // stop kills good trades). The stop distance is untouched. This fires
         // ONLY when the premise of the trade has objectively FAILED while the
         // trade was never protected:
         //    - it never reached breakeven (so it is in the one loseable window),
         //    - it is past a grace period (entry noise gets its chance), and
         //    - it is genuinely underwater, and
         //    - real evidence is against it: its OWN timeframe's Supertrend has
         //      flipped against the position, OR market structure has broken
         //      against it, OR three straight candles have closed against it.
         // A trade merely underwater with NO evidence against it is left alone to
         // recover — that distinction is the whole point. When the signal that
         // justified the entry is void, riding the remaining distance to the stop
         // is donating money for no information.
         // (v16.88) M1 QUICK-BANK — the partial close half of T_M1QuickHold. Runs
         // BEFORE every other exit so it can shave the quick portion off cleanly.
         // Self-guarding: once the volume has already been reduced below the full
         // the scaled total, this simply stops matching — no separate "already
         // fired" flag needed.
         if(T_M1QuickHold && trTf == PERIOD_M1)
         {
            double curVol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
            double qbBank = 0.0, qbHold = 0.0;
            double qbTot  = QuickHoldSize(qbBank, qbHold);   // (v16.89) same scaler as entry
            if(qbTot > 0.0 && curVol >= qbTot - 1e-6)
            {
               int barsQ = (int)((TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME))
                                 / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1));
               double gainQ = (dir > 0) ? (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) - entry)
                                        : (entry - SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));
               // (v17.98) 0.30pts is 15% of one M1 ATR and barely twice the spread,
               // so this banked ~57% of the position (0.04 of 0.07 at eq 480) two
               // minutes in, at a price indistinguishable from the entry. His design
               // is kept -- bank most of it fast -- but the trigger is now ATR-relative
               // so "fast" means a real move, not a spread's width.
               double qbMin = MathMax(T_M1QuickMinPts, T_M1QuickMinAtr * Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1));
               if(barsQ >= T_M1QuickBars && gainQ >= qbMin)
               {
                  // (v18.05) step partial -- one 0.01 off, not T_M1QuickBankFrac of the position.
                  double shave = StepBank(tk, curVol, MathMin(qbBank, curVol - qbHold));
                  if(shave > 0.0 && trade.PositionClosePartial(tk, shave))
                  {
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] M1 QUICK-BANK on %s — %d candles, +%.2f pts, banking %.2f lots home and leaving %.2f running.",
                                 EnumToString(trTf), barsQ, gainQ, shave, curVol - shave);
                  }
               }
            }
         }

         // (v16.54) GAP WENT AGAINST US — act now, do not hope it retraces. The
         // stop has already been jumped; the only question left is whether to eat
         // more. Runs BEFORE every other exit so nothing else can pre-empt it.
         if(T_GapGuard && T_GapCloseAgainst && trK >= 0)
         {
            // (v17.69) THE LAST PATH THAT COULD STILL CLOSE A WINNING TRADE ONE
            // TICK AFTER IT OPENED. This gap close has no age gate, no holdGrace
            // and no profit gate, deliberately -- a jumped stop is not something
            // to sit through. But GapPoints() measures the gap between BAR 1 and
            // BAR 2, i.e. history: a gap that happened BEFORE the entry condemns
            // a position opened after it, on its first tick, even while the trade
            // is in profit and the move is running his way. That is not a jumped
            // stop, it is a stale reading.
            // FIXED: it now only fires if the trade is actually LOSING. A gap in
            // our favour, or one that predates a position now in profit, is no
            // longer a reason to close. A genuine jumped stop always shows as
            // negative R, so the case this exists for is untouched.
            double gAg = GapPoints(trTf);
            // (v17.70) HE IS RIGHT AND MY v17.69 GATE WAS NEARLY WORTHLESS. No
            // trade is in profit on tick one -- it starts down by the spread and
            // wobbles for the first seconds. "profitR < 0" is therefore TRUE for
            // essentially every fresh position, so the gap close was still free to
            // fire on a brand new trade. Gating on profit was the wrong axis.
            // The right one is TIME. This block exists for a gap that jumps our
            // stop, and a stop can only be jumped by a gap that happens AFTER we
            // are in. GapPoints() reads bar 1 vs bar 2, so if bar 1 opened before
            // this position did, that gap is history and had already happened when
            // the entry logic chose to enter -- it is not new information and it
            // is not a jumped stop.
            // Now required: the gap bar must have opened at or after the position
            // did, AND the trade must be underwater. A real jumped stop satisfies
            // both. A fresh trade wobbling through the spread satisfies neither.
            datetime gapBarT = iTime(_Symbol, trTf, 1);
            datetime posOpenT = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            bool gapIsNew = (gapBarT > 0 && gapBarT >= posOpenT);
            if(gAg != 0.0 && gapIsNew && profitR < 0.0 && ((dir > 0 && gAg < 0.0) || (dir < 0 && gAg > 0.0)))
            {
               PrintFormat("[TREND] GAP-AGAINST CLOSE on %s at %+.2fR — market gapped %.2f pts against this position, so the stop was jumped rather than hit. Closing at once.",
                           EnumToString(trTf), profitR, gAg);
               ClearPeakR(tk);
               CloseTagged("GAP-AGAINST", tk);
               return;
            }
         }

         // (v16.53) LEG TARGET — bank once the trade has taken most of what a
         // typical leg on this clock actually delivers. Measured, not assumed.
         if(T_LegTarget && !healthyTrend && trK >= 0 && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
         {
            double medLeg = MedianLegPoints(trK);
            if(medLeg > 0.0)
            {
               double openPx = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
               double nowPx  = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                                         : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
               double gained = (dir > 0) ? (nowPx - openPx) : (openPx - nowPx);
               // (v16.56) the R floor wins over the median, always.
               double target = medLeg * T_LegTargetFrac;
               double floorPx = T_LegTargetMinR * MathAbs(PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)
                                                          - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL));
               if(floorPx > 0.0 && target < floorPx) target = floorPx;
               if(gained >= target)
               {
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] LEG TARGET on %s — took %.2f pts of a %.2f pt median leg (%.0f%%). Banking rather than paying the flip lag at both ends.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), gained, medLeg, (gained/medLeg)*100.0);
                  ClearPeakR(tk);
                  CloseTagged("LEG-TARGET", tk);
                  return;
               }
            }
         }

         // (v16.52) SPIKE-PEAK BANK — the blow-off case v16.49 could not see.
         if(T_SpikePeakBank && trK >= 0 && profitR >= T_SpikeMinR && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
         {
            double atrSp[1];
            int hSp = ExhaustAtrHandle(trTf);
            if(hSp != INVALID_HANDLE && CopyBuffer(hSp, 0, 1, 1, atrSp) == 1 && atrSp[0] > 0.0)
            {
               double hiSp = iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, 1);
               double loSp = iLow(_Symbol, trTf, 1);
               double clSp = iClose(_Symbol, trTf, 1);
               double rgSp = hiSp - loSp;
               if(rgSp > atrSp[0] * T_SpikeAtrMult && rgSp > 0.0)
               {
                  // where in the bar's own range did it close, measured FOR the trade
                  double posSp = (dir > 0) ? (clSp - loSp) / rgSp : (hiSp - clSp) / rgSp;
                  if(posSp < T_SpikeCloseFrac)
                  {
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SPIKE-PEAK BANK on %s at +%.2fR — bar range %.2f vs ATR %.2f (%.1fx) and it closed at %.0f%% of its own range against us. Blow-off: banking now, a trail cannot follow this.",
                                 EnumToString(trTf), profitR, rgSp, atrSp[0], rgSp/atrSp[0], posSp*100.0);
                     ClearPeakR(tk);
                     CloseTagged("SPIKE-PEAK", tk);
                     return;
                  }
               }
            }
         }

         // (v16.49) EXHAUSTION BANK — a PEAK signature, not a retrace signature.
         // Fires when the leg has paid, the last closed bar has stopped expanding
         // relative to ATR(10), and the candle read is against the trade. Stands
         // down on a healthy trend so it can never clip a live runner.
         if(T_ExhaustBank && !healthyTrend && trK >= 0 && profitR >= T_ExhaustMinR && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
         {
            double atrEx[1];
            int hAtrEx = ExhaustAtrHandle(trTf);   // cached — never create a handle per tick
            if(hAtrEx != INVALID_HANDLE && CopyBuffer(hAtrEx, 0, 1, 1, atrEx) == 1 && atrEx[0] > 0.0)
            {
               double rngEx = iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, 1) - iLow(_Symbol, trTf, 1);
               if(rngEx < atrEx[0] * T_ExhaustRangeFrac && CandleAgainst(trTf, dir))
               {
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] EXHAUSTION BANK on %s at +%.2fR — leg stopped expanding (bar range %.2f vs ATR %.2f) and the candle turned. Banking the peak instead of waiting for the giveback.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), profitR, rngEx, atrEx[0]);
                  ClearPeakR(tk);
                  CloseTagged("EXHAUSTION", tk);
                  return;
               }
            }
         }

         if(T_FailCut && trK >= 0)
         {
            double beArm = (g_trEntryRegime[trK] < 0) ? T_RangeBeR : T_MicroLockR;
            bool neverProtected = (peakR < beArm);
            datetime opFC = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            int barsFC = (int)((TimeCurrent() - opFC) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1));
            // (v16.49) adaptive grace — see T_GraceAdaptive.
            int graceFC = T_FailCutMinBars;
            if(T_GraceAdaptive)
            {
               int hbFC = HouseBias();
               double gm = (hbFC == 0) ? 1.0 : ((hbFC == dir) ? T_GraceAgreeMult : T_GraceFightMult);
               graceFC = (int)MathRound(T_FailCutMinBars * gm);
               if(graceFC < 1) graceFC = 1;
            }
            // (v16.66) STRUCTURE EVIDENCE — see T_FailCutNeedsStruct. A dip that still
            // respects the swing behind the entry is breathing; one that breaks it is
            // done. Broken structure also SKIPS the grace wait, since the evidence is
            // already in.
            bool structGone = true;
            if(T_FailCutNeedsStruct)
            {
               int sIdx = (dir > 0) ? LastSwingLow(trTf, 30) : LastSwingHigh(trTf, 30);
               if(sIdx > 0)
               {
                  double sLvl = (dir > 0) ? iLow(_Symbol, trTf, sIdx) : iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, sIdx);
                  double nowP = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                                          : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
                  // (v16.84) CHOP-AWARE BREATHE — widen the breach requirement in a
                  // recognised RANGE regime, so ordinary chop noise doesn't get cut as
                  // if it were a broken trend.
                  double breatheBuf = 0.0;
                  if(T_ChopAwareBreathe && T_RegimeEngine && trK >= 0)
                  {
                     int rg = RegimeOf(g_scAdx[trK], trTf);   // (v16.84 corrected) g_trAdx does not exist; TREND shares SCALP's per-clock handles
                     if(rg < 0)
                     {
                        double atrBr = Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1);  // (v16.84 corrected) g_trAtr does not exist
                        breatheBuf = atrBr * (T_ChopBreatheMult - 1.0);
                     }
                  }
                  structGone = (dir > 0) ? (nowP < sLvl - breatheBuf) : (nowP > sLvl + breatheBuf);
               }
            }
            // (v16.84 REAL BUG FIX) The old OR-branch (T_FailCutNeedsStruct && structGone)
            // is true WHENEVER structGone is true (default true), so the OR was ALWAYS
            // true on a broken swing — bypassing the bar-count grace entirely. A trade
            // could be cut on tick ONE if the nearby swing (common in chop, where swings
            // sit close to entry) looked breached: "closes too early, never breathes."
            // Structure evidence now REQUIRES the grace wait, not an alternative to it.
            // ALSO adds a real-seconds floor (T_TimeGrace) per his observation that "at
            // signal birth the market doesn't always follow straight away, it takes a
            // couple of minutes" — a hard floor under the bar-count grace, which is a
            // COUNT rather than a direct measure of elapsed time.
            bool timeOk = true;
            if(T_TimeGrace) timeOk = (TimeCurrent() - opFC) >= T_TimeGraceSeconds;
            // (v18.80 AG) holdGraceVotes drops the fresh-trade timer only. Every
            // other condition below is unchanged and must still be met.
            bool failHold = T_FailCutOwnGrace ? holdGraceVotes : holdGrace;
            if(!failHold && neverProtected && structGone && barsFC >= graceFC && timeOk && profitR <= -T_FailCutR)
            {
               double svFC; int sdFC; ScalpST(trK, svFC, sdFC);
               bool stAgainst = (sdFC != 0 && sdFC != dir);
               bool structAgainst = !structIntact;
               int againstN = 0;
               for(int ci = 1; ci <= 3; ci++)
               {
                  double cc = iClose(_Symbol, trTf, ci), oc = iOpen(_Symbol, trTf, ci);
                  if(cc <= 0 || oc <= 0) break;
                  if((dir > 0 && cc < oc) || (dir < 0 && cc > oc)) againstN++;
               }
               bool candlesAgainst = (againstN >= 3);
               if(stAgainst || structAgainst || candlesAgainst)
               {
                  PrintFormat("[%s] EARLY-FAILURE CUT on %s at %.2fR — never reached breakeven and the premise has failed (%s). Taking a partial loss instead of riding to the full stop.",
                              Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR,
                              stAgainst ? "Supertrend flipped against it" :
                              (structAgainst ? "structure broken against it" : "3 candles closed against it"));
                  ClearPeakR(tk);
                  CloseTagged("EARLY-FAIL", tk);
                  return;
               }
            }
         }

         if(!holdGrace && T_StructureExit && !structIntact && profitR >= T_StructureExitMinR)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] STRUCTURE BREAK (CHoCH) on %s at +%.2fR — price closed beyond the protective swing, the %s trend is objectively damaged. Banking rather than hoping it's a pullback.",
                        Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR, dir>0?"UP":"DOWN");
            ClearPeakR(tk);
            CloseTagged("STRUCT-BREAK", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      // ═══ (v16.22) RANGE-SCALP FAST BANK — the fix for the bad screenshots ═══
      // If this trade was BORN in a ranging regime, there is no trend to ride
      // — the research and the trader's own screenshots agree gold ranges pay
      // 1-2 points then reverse. So don't hold for a runner that isn't coming:
      // take a small confirmed profit and bank it. Only for range-born trades
      // (g_trEntryRegime < 0) — trend-born and neutral trades ride normally.
      // If the regime has since flipped to trending (a range that resolved
      // into a real breakout), this releases and lets the trade ride. This is
      // the "scalp-and-bank-fast in chop, ride in trend" behaviour, automatic.
      if(!structPatient && !healthyTrend && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir) && T_RegimeEngine && e == E_TREND && trK >= 0 && g_trEntryRegime[trK] < 0)
      {
         int regimeLive = RegimeOf(g_scAdx[trK], trTf);
         if(regimeLive > 0)
         {
            g_trEntryRegime[trK] = 1;   // range resolved into a trend — promote, ride it from here
            if(InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[TREND] %s range RESOLVED into a trend at +%.2fR — releasing the range-scalp cap, riding it.", EnumToString(trTf), profitR);
         }
         // (v16.46 PER-CLOCK RANGE-SCALP) A flat 0.8R banked an M5 trade at 9.6pts
         // and an M15 trade at ~16pts — mid-leg, on clocks whose whole edge is leg
         // LENGTH. The cap is now scaled by clock so it stays a fast chop-scalp on
         // M1 and stops amputating slow-clock runners.
         else if(!holdGrace && profitR >= T_RangeScalpR * RangeScalpClockMult(trTf))
         {
            PrintFormat("[TREND] RANGE-SCALP BANK on %s at +%.2fR — born in chop, no trend to ride, taking the small profit before it reverses (exactly the bad-screenshot case, now banked).",
                        EnumToString(trTf), profitR);
            ClearPeakR(tk);
            CloseTagged("RANGE-SCALP", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      // ═══ (v16.04) SMALL-MOVE EVIDENCE BANK — profit from the choppy 1-in-5 ═══
      // Your chart read: choppy trends "follow for a few points, then the
      // trend ends" — and a DEAD ZONE existed for exactly them: between
      // +0.3R (micro-lock, protects breakeven only) and +0.4R (giveback
      // arm) NOTHING banked. A choppy move topping out there rode all the
      // way back to a scratch — zero taken from a move that offered £1-2.
      // Now: in that zone, a genuine reversal candle against the position
      // on its OWN clock closes the trade and takes the small profit. The
      // candle requirement is the "before the dip" evidence — a mere pause
      // (no reversal print) still rides, so clean trends passing through
      // this zone on their way up are NOT clipped: they spend a bar or two
      // here without printing reversal candles and keep going.
      // (v17.58) THE LAST UNGATED SMALL BANK. Every other profit bank in this
      // block carries the runner lock — range-scalp, chop-stall (via its own
      // madeNewExtreme scan) and the peak/momentum bank all refuse to fire
      // while the leg is printing new extremes in our favour. This one did not.
      // At an M1 gold ATR of ~1.0pt (measured from his own 23:20 log line:
      // 1.60pts = 1.56x ATR), R is roughly 3pts, so 0.30R is about 0.9pt —
      // ~GBP2 at 0.03 lots. ONE counter-direction candle inside a leg that was
      // still advancing was enough to bank that and end the trade. That is the
      // "we close real fast at a small amount of profit" complaint, and it is
      // the single mechanism most responsible for the gap between the ~150pts
      // of signal his charts showed today and what the account actually kept.
      // The bank itself stays — it IS the chop profit engine, and in real chop
      // the leg is not extending, so it still fires exactly as before.
      // ═══ (v18.59) THIS BRANCH HAS BEEN UNSATISFIABLE SINCE v17.67 ═══
      // HIS LIVE REPORT: "during a sideways range we performed horribly." This
      // branch is the one the comment above calls THE chop profit engine, and it
      // could not execute. peakR is a running maximum (UpdatePeakR only ever
      // raises it), so peakR >= profitR ALWAYS. The pair therefore read:
      //     profitR >= MathMax(0.30, T_MinBankR)   with T_MinBankR = 0.60
      //     peakR   <  T_GivebackMinR              = 0.30
      // i.e. "profit >= 0.60R AND peak < 0.30R" -- impossible. Dead since
      // T_MinBankR was raised to 0.60 in v17.67.
      // v16.46 FOUND AND FIXED THIS EXACT BUG in the CHOP-STALL branch twenty
      // lines below, and its comment even states the invariant being violated.
      // The fix was never carried up here. Sixth instance of the pattern.
      // FIX: the same ceiling v16.46 used, so no future retune of T_MinBankR can
      // silently disable it again. At 0.60R + 0.15 = 0.75R ~ 2.1 pts, a 2-pt chop
      // peak is now bankable instead of riding back to the 2.50 stop.
      // REVERT: T_SmallMoveCeilFix = false restores the unsatisfiable pair.
      double smCeil = T_SmallMoveCeilFix ? MathMax(T_GivebackMinR, T_MinBankR + 0.15)
                                         : T_GivebackMinR;
      // (v18.80 AF) widen the window to one noise band. See T_SmallMoveBandWindow.
      if(T_SmallMoveBandWindow && trK >= 0 && R > 0.0)
      {
         double smBand = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1));
         if(smBand > 0.0)
            smCeil = MathMax(smCeil, MathMax(0.30, T_MinBankR) + (smBand / R));
      }
      if(!structPatient && !healthyTrend && profitR >= MathMax(0.30, T_MinBankR) && peakR < smCeil
         && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir) && CandleAgainst(g_scTF[trK], dir))
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] SMALL-MOVE BANK on %s at +%.2fR — reversal candle printed before the giveback guard could even arm. This is the choppy-trend profit: small, taken, home.",
                     Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR);
         ClearPeakR(tk);
         CloseTagged("SMALL-MOVE", tk);
         return;
      }

      // ═══ (v16.14) CHOP-STALL SMALL BANK — the choppy leg that fades with NO candle ═══
      // The small-move bank above needs a reversal CANDLE. But a choppy leg
      // often just STALLS and quietly fades — no engulfing, no pin, no doji —
      // and that small profit round-trips to scratch, the exact leak on a
      // choppy day. This closes it: only inside a RECOGNISED chop batch
      // (RecentFlips >= 3 on this clock — clean trends have few flips and are
      // therefore exempt, so this can never clip a real runner), with small
      // profit on the table (>= 0.3R, still below the giveback arm) and price
      // having made NO new extreme in its favour for T_ChopStallBars bars
      // (momentum in this leg has stopped). Takes the £1-2 the choppy leg
      // offered instead of donating it back. The re-entry watch still arms on
      // the close (Supertrend-still-agrees), so if the chop resolves into a
      // real move we rejoin — nothing is permanently forfeited.
      // (v16.30 FINE-TUNE) The trigger used to require a RECOGNISED chop batch
      // (RecentFlips >= 3) AND was accidentally coupled to T_FlipFreqPenalty —
      // so the very case he described (a mediocre signal that makes 2-3 candles
      // of profit in a NEUTRAL regime, then just fades) had nothing to bank it
      // and gave the profit back. Both requirements are now dropped: the
      // healthyTrend guard above is the better protection (a real trend with
      // intact structure is already exempt), so any NON-healthy-trend trade
      // that has small profit and has stopped making new extremes gets banked.
      // Threshold also eased to T_StallBankR so a genuine 2-3 candle move
      // qualifies instead of needing a full 0.30R.
      // (v16.31 CORRECTION) v16.30 let this fire on ANY non-healthy-trend trade,
      // which swept in neutral-regime trades that still had market structure
      // backing them — i.e. developing trends, clipped at 0.25R. Now it needs a
      // genuine chop signature: either a RANGE regime or structure NOT backing
      // the trade. A neutral trade WITH structure behind it gets its room.
      // (v16.51) a tight signal cluster counts as a chop signature in its own right.
      bool chopSignature = (trK >= 0) && (g_trEntryRegime[trK] < 0 || StructureDir(trTf) != dir
                                          || SignalsClustered(trK, trTf));
      if(!structPatient && !healthyTrend && chopSignature &&
         T_ChopStallBank && profitR >= MathMax(T_StallBankR, T_MinBankR) &&
         peakR < MathMax(T_GivebackMinR, T_StallBankR + 0.15))
         // (v16.46 DEAD-BRANCH FIX) WAS: peakR < T_GivebackMinR.
         // peakR is always >= profitR (PeakR() is a running maximum). So the pair
         // "profitR >= 0.55 && peakR < 0.30" was UNSATISFIABLE: this branch has not
         // executed since T_StallBankR was raised to 0.55 in v16.45. The original
         // design (v16.21) relied on an unwritten invariant T_StallBankR <
         // T_GivebackMinR, which the raise silently violated. The ceiling now tracks
         // T_StallBankR itself, so no future retune can disable the branch again.
      {
         bool madeNewExtreme = false;
         for(int cs = 1; cs <= T_ChopStallBars; cs++)
         {
            if(dir > 0 && iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, cs) > iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, cs + 1)) { madeNewExtreme = true; break; }
            if(dir < 0 && iLow(_Symbol, trTf, cs)  < iLow(_Symbol, trTf, cs + 1))  { madeNewExtreme = true; break; }
         }
         if(!madeNewExtreme)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] CHOP-STALL BANK on %s at +%.2fR — chop batch, no new extreme for %d bars, no reversal candle needed. Taking the small choppy profit before it fades to scratch.",
                        Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR, T_ChopStallBars);
            ClearPeakR(tk);
            CloseTagged("CHOP-STALL", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      // ═══ (v16.04) PEAK-EVIDENCE BANK — take the tip, don't donate the retrace ═══
      // Your chart read on the big trends: "after the peak it drops — we
      // should've taken profit ON the peak, then reentered if needed." The
      // giveback guard by design waits for a retrace (22%+ of peak) before
      // banking — correct when the tip prints no evidence, but when the
      // reversal actually ANNOUNCES itself at a high (reversal candle AND
      // Two-Pole turning against, together, on the position's own clock),
      // waiting for the retrace donates £3-5 of a £20 move back for no
      // information gain. Requires BOTH evidences + deep profit (>= 2R) so
      // a routine pullback candle alone never triggers it; and the watch
      // arms right after (transaction handler), so if the "peak" turns out
      // to be a pause, the reclaim proof puts us straight back in — your
      // bank-then-reenter-if-needed flow, executed at the tip.
      // (v16.13) VOLUME CONFIRMATION added: a candle + Two-Pole turn is
      // evidence, but research says the tell for real-reversal-vs-pullback
      // is VOLUME — a reversal spikes volume, a pullback dries it up. So
      // bank the tip when the turn evidence comes WITH a volume spike (real
      // turn), OR when profit is very deep (3R+, worth protecting regardless).
      // A turn on falling volume is likely just a breather — HOLD it and let
      // the peak-lock protect the floor, don't get scared out of a good trend.
      double volAtTurn = VolMult(trTf);
      bool turnEvidence = CandleAgainst(g_scTF[trK], dir) && TwoPoleTurnedAgainstTf(g_scTF[trK], dir);
      // (v17.17 RUNNER-LOCK GAP — the largest profit leak found by audit.)
      // v16.61 added T_LegStillExtending as a universal runner lock and the
      // project notes record it as covering "all four discretionary banks".
      // It does not. Call sites are the LEG TARGET, SPIKE-PEAK and EXHAUST
      // banks — all three of which are DISABLED by default — plus range-scalp.
      // This bank and the MOMENTUM-STALL bank below, the two that are LIVE,
      // never call it. So the runner lock protects three dead exits and misses
      // both live ones. That is the "same bug class fixed three times without
      // generalising" pattern, one more time.
      //
      // Worse, this exit sits ABOVE the giveback guard and peak ratchet in
      // file order, and every exit here returns immediately, so precedence is
      // pure line order: it preempts the instruments actually built to ride a
      // runner.
      //
      // And it contradicts its own comment. The comment says "A turn on
      // falling volume is likely just a breather — HOLD it", but the condition
      // is an OR, so at profitR >= 3.0 the volume test is bypassed entirely
      // and a single reversal candle closes a trade that is still extending.
      // Adding the runner lock resolves the contradiction without changing the
      // intended OR: deep profit still banks the tip, but not while the leg is
      // printing new extremes in our favour.
      //
      // Cost of the leak, at 0.03 and an active-hours R of 5.7pts: clipping a
      // runner at 3R that would have reached 6R gives back 17.1 pts = GBP38.37,
      // each time. The GOLD HUNTER ledger showed 2 of 53 trades carried 51% of
      // gross profit, so this is precisely the cohort that pays for the day.
      if(turnEvidence && (volAtTurn >= 1.5 || profitR >= 3.0)
         && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
      {
         PrintFormat("[%s] PEAK BANK on %s at +%.2fR — reversal candle + Two-Pole turn%s. Taking the tip; reclaim watch covers reentry if the trend resumes.",
                     Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR,
                     volAtTurn >= 1.5 ? StringFormat(" on a %.1fx VOLUME SPIKE (real reversal)", volAtTurn) : " at deep profit");
         ClearPeakR(tk);
         CloseTagged("PEAK-BANK", tk);
         return;
      }

      // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #1 (v16.05) — MOMENTUM-STALL PEAK BANK ═══
      // Today's history: winners banked at +5-8 points but several legs
      // ran further — profit left on the table because the peak bank above
      // needs a clean reversal CANDLE, and a strong trend often just rolls
      // over on fading momentum without printing one. This catches that
      // case: deep in profit (>= 2.5R), if the Two-Pole has been falling
      // for two straight reads FROM a stretched high (it was >= +0.9σ for a
      // long / <= -0.9σ for a short within the last 3 bars) AND price has
      // stalled (last close no longer making a new extreme vs the prior
      // bar), momentum is exhausting even without a reversal candle — bank
      // the tip. Stricter than the candle version (needs deep profit + a
      // prior stretch + two-read decline), so it only fires on a genuine
      // momentum roll-over at a high, never a mid-trend wobble. The reclaim
      // watch still arms after, so a resumption puts us back in.
      if(profitR >= 2.5)
      {
         EnsureTwoPole(trK);
         // (v17.17) Same runner-lock gap as the peak-evidence bank above. This
         // one fires from 2.5R, so it clips even earlier: 2.5R -> 6R is 19.9
         // pts = GBP44.77 given back per occurrence at 0.03.
         bool wasStretched = (dir > 0) ? (g_tpPrev2[trK] >= 0.9 || g_tpPrev1[trK] >= 0.9)
                                        : (g_tpPrev2[trK] <= -0.9 || g_tpPrev1[trK] <= -0.9);
         bool twoReadDecline = (dir > 0) ? (g_tpNow[trK] < g_tpPrev1[trK] && g_tpPrev1[trK] < g_tpPrev2[trK])
                                          : (g_tpNow[trK] > g_tpPrev1[trK] && g_tpPrev1[trK] > g_tpPrev2[trK]);
         double c1s = iClose(_Symbol, trTf, 1), c2s = iClose(_Symbol, trTf, 2);
         bool priceStalled = (dir > 0) ? (c1s <= c2s) : (c1s >= c2s);
         if(wasStretched && twoReadDecline && priceStalled
            && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] MOMENTUM-STALL BANK on %s at +%.2fR — Two-Pole rolling over from a stretched high and price stalled, no reversal candle needed. Banking the tip.",
                        Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR);
            ClearPeakR(tk);
            CloseTagged("MOM-STALL", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      if(peakR >= T_GivebackMinR)
      {
         // ═══ (v18.52) THE TIERS WERE KEYED IN R AND WERE THEREFORE DEAD ═══
         // CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX (unreachable branches) + STRATEGY CHANGE (the
         // new numbers are his own manual staged plan).
         // ARITHMETIC. R is entry-to-stop, and T_SlFloorPts = 2.50, so R ~ 2.8 pts.
         //   peakR >= 5.0  means peak >= ~14 pts  -> has never happened
         //   peakR >= 2.5  means peak >= ~7 pts   -> almost never happens
         // So the ": T_GivebackPct - 10.0" branch was the ONLY live one: 12%.
         // Then five more mechanisms adjust that 12: the regime lock (+8 / -8),
         // structIntact (+7), the adaptive ATR percentile (+/-10), the flip-frequency
         // penalty (-8), each with a MathMax(8.0) clamp. And finally it is multiplied
         // by GivebackMultFor(M1) = T_GivebackMultM1 = 0.70.
         // WORST REALISTIC M1 CASE: 8 x 0.70 = 5.6% of peak. On a 5 pt peak that is
         // 0.28 pts. The noise floor (spread + 0.60 ATR ~ 0.90) and the v18.42 ATR
         // floor (1.00 ATR) then lift it to ~1.0 pt.
         // CONCLUSION: the floor has been doing 100% of the work and all five tuned
         // percentage mechanisms are inoperative in the peak range this system
         // actually reaches. There is no peak-proportional giveback in this EA at
         // all -- there is a flat ~1 pt trailing allowance on every trade, whether
         // the peak is 2 points or 12. That is why a big peak is not held: a 12 pt
         // peak gets the same 1 pt of room as a 2 pt peak.
         // FIXED by keying the tiers in POINTS, and with his own manual numbers:
         //   peak >= 8 pts -> 40%   ("proven moves get MORE room, not less")
         //   peak >= 3 pts -> 30%   (his stage-2 trail)
         //   below         -> the ATR floor, unchanged
         // At a 5 pt peak that is 1.50 pts of room instead of 1.00. At a 12 pt peak
         // it is 4.8 instead of 1.0 -- which is the whole point.
         // ANSWERING HIS RETEST QUESTION DIRECTLY ("what if price goes up, does a
         // retest and goes back up, should we have held"): with a 1 pt allowance the
         // answer was structurally NO, because a normal M1 retest is more than one
         // ATR. With 30% of a 5 pt peak it is yes. That is the trade being made here.
         // HONEST COST: at a FIXED peak this banks LESS. A 5 pt peak that genuinely
         // tops out now banks 3.5 instead of 4.0. It only pays if retests are more
         // common than genuine tops at that scale, which is still UNKNOWN and is the
         // one thing a session of log settles.
         // REVERT: T_GivebackTierPts = false restores the R-keyed tiers.
         double peakPtsG = peakR * R;   // R is this ticket's stop distance in price (~2.8 pts)
         double givebackPct = T_GivebackTierPts
                             ? ((peakPtsG >= PS(T_GivebackBigPts))  ? T_GivebackBigPct
                              : (peakPtsG >= PS(T_GivebackMidPts))  ? T_GivebackMidPct
                              :                                   T_GivebackPct - 10.0)
                             : ((peakR >= 5.0) ? T_GivebackPct + 15.0
                              : (peakR >= 2.5) ? T_GivebackPct
                              :                  T_GivebackPct - 10.0);
         // (v16.24 #9) REGIME PROFIT-LOCK — hold LOOSER in a strong trend (let
         // the runner run through normal pullbacks) and TIGHTER in weak/range
         // conditions (bank before the chop takes it back). Reads the trade's
         // own clock regime live, so a trade whose regime decays gets protected
         // sooner automatically.
         if(T_RegimeProfitLock && e == E_TREND && trK >= 0)
         {
            int rgLive = RegimeOf(g_scAdx[trK], trTf);
            if(rgLive > 0)      givebackPct += 8.0;   // strong trend — breathe
            else if(rgLive < 0) givebackPct -= 8.0;   // range — lock tight
            // (v16.26) structure still intact = the trend is objectively alive,
            // so allow more room before banking. Peaks are lost by cutting a
            // live trend during a normal pullback; this is the antidote.
            if(structIntact) givebackPct += 7.0;
            givebackPct = MathMax(8.0, givebackPct);   // never absurdly tight
         }
         // (v14 #4) ADAPTIVE GIVEBACK — a fixed % is too tight in a fast, high-
         // volatility trend (normal pullbacks are bigger there) and too loose
         // in a slow one. Widen the allowance in high-ATR regimes, tighten it
         // in low. Keeps the guard from banking a still-healthy fast trend on a
         // normal pullback, while still locking slow trends quickly.
         if(T_AdaptiveGiveback)
         {
            double atrPctl = AtrRankHandle(g_scAtr[trK], Buf(g_scAtr[trK],0,1));  // 0-100
            givebackPct += (atrPctl - 50.0) * 0.20;   // +/-10% at the extremes
            givebackPct = MathMax(8.0, MathMin(45.0, givebackPct));
         }
         // (v16.04) CHOP-BATCH-AWARE BANKING — your chart observation:
         // choppy trends come in recognisable BATCHES (clusters of flips in
         // a tight window) between the long clean legs. The ENTRY side
         // already reads this (flip-frequency penalty, reduced size), but
         // the EXIT gave a batch trade the same patience as a clean-trend
         // trade — exactly backwards. Inside a recognised batch (3+ flips
         // in ~20 bars on this clock) the move WILL reverse soon by
         // definition, so the giveback tightens by 8 points: the £1-2 the
         // batch offers gets banked before the next flip takes it back.
         // On the clean legs RecentFlips is low and nothing changes — full
         // patience for the £20 carries. Same state, both sides now use it.
         if(T_FlipFreqPenalty && RecentFlips(trK) >= 3)
            givebackPct = MathMax(8.0, givebackPct - 8.0);
         double giveback = peakR - profitR;
         double allowedGiveback = peakR * (givebackPct / 100.0) * GivebackMultFor(trTf);   // (v16.95) per-clock

         // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #3 — DON'T BANK-AND-REBUY A CLEAN RUN ═══
         // Your complaint: on a genuine 6-16 point run the EA banks on a
         // small dip then re-enters, and the exit→reentry round-trip bleeds
         // a little each time instead of just riding the one move to its tip.
         // The fix: if the trend is STILL highly efficient right now (price
         // is genuinely going somewhere, not thrashing) AND the Supertrend
         // line still agrees, a pullback is part of the move — hold it,
         // don't bank it only to buy it back higher. The giveback guard
         // still fires the moment efficiency drops (the move is actually
         // rolling over) — which is the case it was built for. This keeps
         // the protection for real reversals while letting a clean trend run
         // to its tip in ONE position instead of several bleeding ones.
         double erNow = EfficiencyRatio(trTf, T_EfficiencyLen);
         double stGbV; int stGbD; ScalpST(trK, stGbV, stGbD);
         bool cleanStillRunning = (erNow >= T_EfficiencyHi) && (stGbD == dir);
         // On a clean run, widen the allowed giveback substantially so a
         // normal pullback inside the move doesn't trip it; a real rollover
         // collapses ER and this relaxation disappears on the same tick.
         if(cleanStillRunning) allowedGiveback = peakR * MathMin(0.60, (givebackPct + 25.0) / 100.0);
         // (v16.71) SMART CLOSE — the giveback guard is now the ONLY discretionary
         // exit left (v16.70 turned the other five off), so it has to do a job that
         // used to be shared. A flat 22-37% allowance means a 20pt M5 peak hands back
         // 4.4pts before acting — which is what "missing the peak" looks like. But
         // tightening it flat would shake the trade out on the pullbacks inside a
         // 68pt leg. A pullback and a top are indistinguishable WHILE HAPPENING, so
         // the only honest discriminator is whether the leg is STILL MAKING GROUND.
         // While new extremes are coming: full room, it is a pullback.
         // Once no new extreme for T_ExtendLookback bars: the leg has gone quiet, so
         // clamp onto the peak. No prediction, no fixed target — it reacts.
         int barsHeldSmart = (int)((TimeCurrent() - (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME))
                                   / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1));   // (v16.76) uniquely named: 'barsHeld' is used again later in this function
         // (v16.76 FIX TO MY OWN v16.71) The smart close tightened on "no new extreme
         // for N bars" ALONE. His 28 Jul 16:13 M1 BUY shows why that is wrong: it
         // entered ~4024, consolidated sideways for 30 minutes with no new high, then
         // a large candle ran. Under v16.71 the giveback would have tightened to ~12%
         // of a 6pt peak = 0.7pts and closed the trade immediately before that candle.
         // A CONSOLIDATION INSIDE A TREND and a TOP are indistinguishable by absence
         // of new extremes. The discriminator that separates them already exists:
         // healthyTrend (trending regime + intact structure). A healthy trend that
         // pauses is resting; an unhealthy one that pauses is finished. So the smart
         // close now only arms when the trend is NOT healthy, and never in the first
         // few bars of a trade.
         // (v17.06) already correctly gated on !healthyTrend since v16.76 — left as is,
         // and noted here so the three exits are visibly consistent.
         if(T_SmartClose && !healthyTrend && barsHeldSmart >= T_SmartCloseMinBars
            && !LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
         {
            double tightPct = MathMin(givebackPct, T_SmartCloseTightPct);
            double tightAllow = peakR * (tightPct / 100.0);
            if(tightAllow < allowedGiveback)
            {
               allowedGiveback = tightAllow;
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] SMART CLOSE armed on %s — no new extreme in %d bars, giveback tightened %.0f%% -> %.0f%% of a %.2fR peak.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), T_ExtendLookback, givebackPct, tightPct, peakR);
            }
         }

         // ═══ (v18.42) ABSOLUTE FLOOR ON THE GIVEBACK ALLOWANCE ═══
         // MEASURED, 14 Aug, 76 tickets: realised winners are
         //   0.05 .. 1.70  |  GAP  |  3.14 .. 4.87  |  NOTHING ABOVE
         // The upper wall is this guard. peakR is almost always < 2.5 (R is
         // ~2.8pts, so 2.5R is ~7pts and only two trades all day exceeded 5),
         // therefore the "protect a modest peak faster" branch is the ONLY
         // branch that ever executes: givebackPct = 22 - 10 = 12. Then
         // T_FlipFreqPenalty subtracts 8 more whenever RecentFlips >= 3 --
         // which is precisely the cluster windows (13:42, 15:17, 16:25) -- so
         // the allowance floors at 8%.
         //   8% of a 4.0pt peak = 0.32 pts.
         //   M1 gold ATR is ~1.0 pt.
         // The guard closes a live runner on a THIRD OF AN ATR of pullback, and
         // it is tightest in exactly the windows where the signal is churning,
         // so the one trade in a cluster that finally works is choked first.
         // A percentage of a small peak is always smaller than noise; the
         // percentage model silently assumed peaks of 2.5-5R that never arrive.
         // FIX: the allowance may never be tighter than T_GivebackAtrFloor ATR.
         // This operator can only ever WIDEN the allowance, so its failure mode
         // is "held slightly longer" -- and it wakes no dormant exit, because it
         // touches no gate. It also subsumes the flip-frequency penalty problem:
         // 8% of a small peak now lands under the floor and gets lifted.
         if(T_GivebackFloorOn && R > 0.0 && atr > 0.0)
         {
            double floorR = (T_GivebackAtrFloor * atr) / R;
            if(allowedGiveback < floorR)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] GIVEBACK FLOOR on %s — allowance %.3fR (%.2f pts) was below %.2f ATR. "
                              "Widened to %.3fR (%.2f pts). Peak %.2fR, pct %.0f%%.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), allowedGiveback, allowedGiveback * R,
                              T_GivebackAtrFloor, floorR, floorR * R, peakR, givebackPct);
               allowedGiveback = floorR;
            }
         }

         // (v16.63) ROUND-TRIP FLOOR — never bank a retrace smaller than the cost of
         // getting back in. See T_RoundTripFloor for the derivation.
         if(T_RoundTripFloor)
         {
            double sprPts = (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) - SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID));
            double rtCost = 2.0 * sprPts + 2.0 * T_RoundTripLagPts;
            double rDist  = MathAbs(PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN) - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL));
            if(rDist > 0.0)
            {
               double rtInR = rtCost / rDist;                 // round-trip cost expressed in R
               // (v16.68 DEFECT I INTRODUCED IN v16.63) The floor was applied
               // unconditionally, so on a CHOP trade peaking at 1-3pts it demanded a
               // ~4pt giveback from a 2pt peak — arithmetically impossible. That
               // silently disabled the giveback guard on every small-peak trade, i.e.
               // exactly the choppy signals he wants harvested. His 28 Jul chart shows
               // the shape: a 09:30-10:30 cluster of five signals each worth 1-3pts.
               // The floor's PURPOSE is to stop paying a round trip for nothing — but a
               // trade that will close on the opposite flip has no round trip to pay,
               // and a giveback larger than the peak is not a giveback at all. Capped
               // at half the peak so the guard always remains reachable.
               rtInR = MathMin(rtInR, 0.50 * peakR);
               if(allowedGiveback < rtInR)
               {
                  allowedGiveback = rtInR;
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] giveback floored at %.3fR (round-trip cost %.2f pts vs R %.2f pts) — banking a smaller retrace than this loses money on the re-entry.",
                                 rtInR, rtCost, rDist);
               }
            }
         }

         // ═══ (v18.59) THE SMALL WINNER THAT MUST GO RED BEFORE IT CAN CLOSE ═══
         // HIS LIVE REPORT: "went 2 up in profit and closed in -2, especially on
         // small trades." This is the cause and it is pure arithmetic.
         // T_GivebackAtrFloor = 1.00 forces the allowance to at least 1.00 ATR
         // (~1.0 pt on M1 gold) and its own v18.42 comment says it "can only ever
         // WIDEN the allowance". Nothing capped it. So:
         //     peak 0.9 pts, allowance 1.0 pt -> trigger at 0.9 - 1.0 = -0.1 pts.
         // The giveback could not fire until the trade was already NEGATIVE. And
         // GIVEBACK is the only exit exempt from T_MinWinnerExitPts = 6.00 for a
         // sub-6-pt winner, while SMALL-MOVE is unsatisfiable (see v18.59 note at
         // that branch) and both peak banks sit behind the ~2%-true runner lock.
         // Result: NO mechanism could close a small winner while it was still
         // profitable. Every one of them was required to round-trip through zero.
         //
         // v16.68 ALREADY FOUND THIS BUG, in the round-trip floor 25 lines above,
         // and capped it at 0.50 * peakR with the right sentence: "a giveback
         // larger than the peak is not a giveback at all." I added the ATR floor
         // in v18.42 and did not carry the cap across. Same class, seventh time.
         // FIX: one cap, applied AFTER every floor, so no floor of any kind can
         // demand a retrace bigger than half the peak.
         //
         // ARITHMETIC — it binds ONLY on small peaks, so no runner is choked:
         //   peak  0.9 pts -> cap 0.45 -> banks at +0.45 pts. Positive, not -0.9.
         //   peak  2.5 pts -> cap 1.25 -> ATR floor 1.00 still binds. Unchanged.
         //   peak 10.0 pts -> cap 5.00 -> a ~3 pt allowance is well under it. Unchanged.
         // The cap starts biting below about 2 ATR of peak, which is precisely the
         // band he is losing money in. Downward-only on the allowance, so it can
         // never make the EA hold longer than v18.58 did.
         // REVERT: T_GivebackPeakCap = 0 (off). 0.50 reproduces v16.68's constant.
         if(T_GivebackPeakCap > 0.0 && peakR > 0.0)
         {
            double capR = T_GivebackPeakCap * peakR;
            if(allowedGiveback > capR)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] GIVEBACK PEAK CAP on %s — allowance %.3fR (%.2f pts) exceeded %.0f%% of a %.2fR (%.2f pt) peak. Capped to %.3fR (%.2f pts) so the bank stays on the profitable side of entry.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), allowedGiveback, allowedGiveback * R,
                              T_GivebackPeakCap * 100.0, peakR, peakR * R, capR, capR * R);
               allowedGiveback = capR;
            }
         }

         // ═══ (v18.77 AB) POINTS CEILING ON THE GIVEBACK ALLOWANCE ═══
         // Applied AFTER every floor and after the fraction cap, so nothing below
         // can re-inflate it. Downward-only: it can never make the EA hold longer.
         // See the full derivation at T_GivebackMaxBands.
         if(T_GivebackMaxBands > 0.0 && R > 0.0)
         {
            double bandPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1));
            if(bandPts > 0.0)
            {
               double maxPts   = T_GivebackMaxBands * bandPts;
               double allowPts = allowedGiveback * R;
               if(allowPts > maxPts)
               {
                  PrintFormat("[TREND] GIVEBACK BAND CEILING on %s — a %.2f pt peak was allowed to hand back "
                              "%.2f pts (%.0f%%). That is %.1f noise bands and a retrace that size is a turn, "
                              "not breathing. Capped to %.2f pts, so this closes at +%.2f instead of +%.2f.",
                              EnumToString(trTf), peakR * R, allowPts, (allowPts/(peakR*R))*100.0,
                              allowPts / bandPts, maxPts, peakR*R - maxPts, peakR*R - allowPts);
                  allowedGiveback = maxPts / R;
               }
            }
         }

         // (v12b) REMOVED the "must already show Supertrend/momentum against"
         // requirement here. That gate was the actual bug being reported:
         // Supertrend lags, so by the time it confirms a reversal the profit
         // that triggered the giveback threshold is already gone or negative.
         // The whole point of a giveback guard is to act BEFORE the lagging
         // indicators catch up. The tightened percentage above (22%, arming
         // at just 0.4R) is now the real protection — no second gate diluting it.
         // (v16.87) GATE THE GIVEBACK GUARD ON WHETHER THE LEG IS STILL EXTENDING.
         // Found by tracing his "£5 profit closes at £3" report: allowedGiveback is
         // 22-37% of peak, so a £5 peak bank at £3.15-3.90 matches exactly. That is
         // this guard doing precisely what it was coded to do — the giveback % alone
         // cannot tell "temporary pullback in a live trend" from "the top", because a
         // retrace looks identical either way WHILE IT IS HAPPENING.
         // v16.61 already solved exactly this ambiguity for the other four exits
         // using LegStillExtending() (a NEW favourable extreme within
         // T_ExtendLookback bars = still forming), but the giveback guard was
         // deliberately left OUT of that gate as the one exit meant to always work.
         // That is also why his continuation had no re-entry to fall back on: the
         // trend never actually needed one, because it hadn't really paused.
         // Gating it closes the loop: while the leg keeps making new highs, this
         // guard now stands down and the trade rides on toward the flip, exactly as
         // asked. The moment it STOPS making new highs (a real stall or a top), the
         // gate lifts within T_ExtendLookback bars at most and the guard is live
         // again — so a genuine reversal is caught at most a few bars later, still
         // well inside the chandelier trail and hard stop, which are UNCHANGED.
         // (v17.06) SAME DEFECT PATTERN I FIXED FOR THE RATCHET IN v17.02, never applied
         // here. v16.87 gated this guard on !LegStillExtending — but a MID-TREND
         // CONSOLIDATION also stops extending, so the gate lifted and the giveback
         // closed the trade on an ordinary pause. That is his "we close if market does a
         // small dip, we don't trust analysis and hold like a good trader".
         // healthyTrend is the right discriminator, exactly as in v17.02: a healthy trend
         // that pauses is RESTING, an unhealthy one that pauses is FINISHED. So the
         // giveback now stands down while the trend is healthy, and the stop/ratchet/
         // chandelier still protect the position underneath it.
         // (v17.07 THE FINDING THAT MATTERS FOR HIS CHOPPY TRENDS)
         // healthyTrend = T_TrendRideGuard && regime>0 && structIntact — and regime>0
         // needs ADX above T_RegimeAdxTrend. In a CHOPPY trend ADX is LOW by definition,
         // so healthyTrend is FALSE there, so every exit I gated on healthyTrend
         // (v17.02 ratchet, v17.06 giveback, v16.76 smart close) is fully ACTIVE in
         // exactly the conditions he says are costing him the most. Gating on
         // healthyTrend alone fixed normal trends and did NOTHING for chop.
         // structIntact is the right condition for chop: the swing that justified the
         // entry is still there. A good trader holds while structure holds, whatever
         // ADX reads. So the giveback now stands down while EITHER the trend is healthy
         // OR structure is simply intact — which covers chop.
         // Protection is unchanged underneath: hard stop, ratchet floor, chandelier,
         // gap close, opposite flip, and a genuine CHoCH (structure BREAKING) all still
         // fire. The moment structure actually breaks, structIntact goes false and this
         // guard is live again.
         // (v17.43 — THE FIX for both live examples he sent: a trade that runs
         // +6pts then gives ALL of it back before the guard ever fires.
         // healthyTrend/structIntact standing the guard down has NO upper
         // bound — they can read true right up to the exact top of a reversal,
         // since regime/structure are lagging by nature. Result: a trade can
         // give back its ENTIRE peak while "protected". This is a hard
         // ceiling underneath everything else — it fires no matter what
         // healthyTrend/structIntact/extending say, the instant giveback
         // crosses T_MaxGivebackPct of peak. It does not replace the existing
         // guard's earlier, smarter exits — it is the floor under all of them.
         // (v17.98) NOISE FLOOR UNDER BOTH GIVEBACK PATHS. See T_NoiseFloorAtr.
         // Capped at 0.95 of peak so a peak SMALLER than the noise band simply
         // stops being bankable -- such a trade lives or dies on the hard stop
         // and the flip, which is correct: a 1pt peak on a 1.95 ATR clock is not
         // a peak, it is a tick.
         double nfPts = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1));
         double nfR   = (R > 0.0) ? nfPts / R : 0.0;
         if(nfR > 0.95 * peakR) nfR = 0.95 * peakR;
         if(allowedGiveback < nfR) allowedGiveback = nfR;
         bool hardGivebackCap = (peakR > 0) && (giveback >= peakR * (T_MaxGivebackPct / 100.0))
                                && (giveback >= nfR);
         bool giveGateOk = hardGivebackCap || !T_GateGivebackOnExtend
                           || (!LegStillExtending(trTf, dir) && !healthyTrend && !structIntact);
         // (v17.88 AUDIT FINDING - the most serious one in the file) holdGrace is
         // now SIX OR-ed conditions (min-hold, cross-clock, DEMA side, DEMA slope,
         // FVG, room, RSI). In a live uptrend several are true almost continuously:
         // RSI>=55, room-to-run, and a 0.10-ATR DEMA slope are all easy to satisfy.
         // So holdGrace is effectively TRUE most of the time -- which silently
         // disabled the HARD giveback ceiling too, because it sat behind the same
         // !holdGrace gate. T_MaxGivebackPct was added in v17.43 specifically
         // because he was giving back 100% of peak while "protected"; that
         // protection has been neutered ever since the hold votes multiplied.
         // FIX: the hard ceiling is UNCONDITIONAL, as originally intended. The
         // discretionary/soft giveback still respects holdGrace exactly as before,
         // so nothing else about hold behaviour changes.
         // ═══ (v18.56) "BIG PROFIT ISNT BEING LOCKED" — THE holdGrace VETO ═══
         // CLASSIFICATION: BUG FIX. Entry-anchored protection applied to a
         // peak-anchored instrument -- the same class of error as the winner floor
         // vetoing GIVEBACK (18.49), FLIP-CLOSE (18.51) and RANGE-SCALP (18.54).
         // HIS REPORT: "2 trades closed at 4 pound each, they were closed by ME as
         // the ea wasn't closing them. big profit isnt being locked!"
         // WHY NOTHING FIRED. Those positions had run well past 4 pounds and given
         // it back. GIVEBACK is the only exit that banks a retracing winner, and its
         // soft path requires !holdGrace. holdGrace is:
         //     inMinHold || g_holdToTarget || (holdVotes >= needed && ...)
         // and the v17.88 audit note fourteen lines above already recorded that it is
         // "effectively TRUE most of the time." So the soft giveback was standing down
         // on most trades and the ONLY remaining ceiling was hardGivebackCap at
         // T_MaxGivebackPct = 65% of peak. On a 5 pt peak that does not fire until
         // 3.25 pts have been handed back -- i.e. it banks at 1.75 pts, BELOW where he
         // intervened by hand. He was not being impatient; there was genuinely nothing
         // left to close the trade at a sensible number.
         // THE PRINCIPLE: holdGrace exists to stop a FRESH trade being clipped before
         // it has had room to work. It is measured from the entry. Once a trade has
         // built a real peak, it is no longer fresh and holdGrace's premise has
         // expired -- but the code kept applying it forever. So the hold votes were
         // protecting a trade from the one mechanism that existed to pay him.
         // FIX: once the peak reaches T_GivebackMidPts (3.0 pts -- the same threshold
         // that turns on the 30% tier), the giveback owns the exit and the hold votes
         // no longer veto it. Below that peak, holdGrace still applies exactly as
         // before, so nothing changes for small or fresh trades.
         // HONEST COST: a trade that peaks over 3 pts and then pulls back INSIDE the
         // allowance is unaffected (the allowance is what it is). What changes is that
         // a pullback THROUGH the allowance now banks instead of being held by the
         // votes. If the hold votes were genuinely predictive -- if trades they held
         // usually came back and made more -- this loses money. His own evidence is
         // the opposite: they held to 4 pounds from much higher, and he had to close
         // them himself. OFF-SWITCH: T_GivebackIgnoreHold = false.
         double peakPtsH  = peakR * R;
         bool   giveOwns  = T_GivebackIgnoreHold && peakPtsH >= PS(T_GivebackMidPts);
         if(giveOwns && holdGrace && giveback >= allowedGiveback && giveGateOk && InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] GIVEBACK OVERRIDES HOLD — peak %.2f pts is past %.2f, so the "
                        "hold votes no longer veto the bank. Handing back %.2f of the peak.",
                        peakPtsH, PS(T_GivebackMidPts), giveback);
         if(hardGivebackCap || ((!holdGrace || giveOwns) && giveback >= allowedGiveback && giveGateOk))
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] PEAK GIVEBACK on %s — peaked at %.2fR, now %.2fR (gave back %.0f%% of peak, allowed %.0f%%). Locking in the profit before it goes further.",
                        Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), peakR, profitR, (giveback/peakR)*100.0, givebackPct);
            g_trFastRearm[trK] = true;
            g_trLastExit[trK]  = TimeCurrent();
            // (v12c) ARM THE CONTINUATION WATCH — this close fired on the
            // percentage rule alone, with NO requirement that Supertrend
            // itself has flipped. That means it may well be a scare, not a
            // reversal. Check: does Supertrend on this exact timeframe still
            // agree with the direction we're closing? If so, watch for a
            // reclaim of the recent local high/low rather than waiting for
            // a full flip-reversal-flip cycle that may not come for a long time.
            double stCk2; int stCkD2; ScalpST(trK, stCk2, stCkD2);
            if(stCkD2 == dir)
            {
               // (v16.17) REENTRY SCOPE — only arm early (risky opening bars,
               // shakeout-prone) or on a trend that has already proven itself
               // large (worth milking). An ordinary mid-trend giveback close
               // in between just books the profit and rides on without a watch.
               int ageBars = TrendAgeBars(trTf, stCk2, dir);
               // (v16.37 — PHASE 3 FIX) THE MID-TREND RECLAIM GAP. The scope was
               // {early trade} OR {already-large trade}. That left a hole: an
               // ORDINARY mid-trend trade (age past the opening bars, peak still
               // modest) that gets shaken out armed NO reclaim watch at all — it
               // booked its profit and never re-entered the second half of a
               // trend that was still perfectly alive. That is the "doesn't ride
               // to the peak" complaint seen from the re-entry side, and on a
               // frequency-first system it is a direct, recurring profit leak.
               // The fix is TARGETED, not a blanket widening (he asked to keep
               // reentry restrained, and has changed his mind on this knob before):
               // cover the mid-trend case ONLY when the trend is OBJECTIVELY still
               // healthy — its own-TF structure intact AND a trending regime. That
               // re-enters genuine continuations while still refusing to chase a
               // trade that was shaken out of deteriorating chop.
               bool healthyMidTrend = T_ReentryHealthyMid
                                      && StructureIntact(trTf, dir)
                                      && (T_RegimeEngine ? RegimeOf(g_scAdx[trK], trTf) > 0 : false);
               bool inScope = !T_ReentryScoped || ageBars <= T_ReentryEarlyBars
                              || peakR >= T_ReentryLargeR || healthyMidTrend;
               if(inScope)
               {
               double buf = 0.16 * atr;   // (v16.21) cooled slightly 0.12->0.16 at his request — wants reentry, just not so frequent
               double h5 = iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, SafeHighest(_Symbol, trTf, MODE_HIGH, 5, 1));
               double l5 = iLow(_Symbol, trTf, SafeLowest(_Symbol, trTf, MODE_LOW, 5, 1));
               g_trWatchDir[trK]    = dir;
               g_trWatchLevel[trK]  = (dir > 0) ? h5 + buf : l5 - buf;
               g_trWatchExpiry[trK] = TimeCurrent() + 40L * PeriodSeconds(trTf);
               g_trFastRearm[trK]   = true;   // (fix #67) proof-gated reentry shouldn't also wait out the cooldown — see #67 note at the transaction handler
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[%s] Continuation watch armed on %s: Supertrend still %s (age %d bars, peak %.2fR — %s), will re-enter if price reclaims %.2f within 40 bars.",
                              Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), dir>0?"UP":"DOWN", ageBars, peakR,
                              ageBars <= T_ReentryEarlyBars ? "early" : "large trend", g_trWatchLevel[trK]);
               }
               else if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[%s] Giveback close on %s at %.2fR — mid-trend (age %d bars), out of reentry scope. Riding clean, no watch.",
                              Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), peakR, ageBars);
            }
            ClearPeakR(tk);
            CloseTagged("GIVEBACK", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      // TREND CHOP-CLUSTER EARLY EXIT — matches the entry-side chop filter
      // and the SCALP M1 chop exit built earlier. The slow stall timer
      // (T_Stall, 12 bars) is much too patient for a fast up-down-up-down
      // cluster — by the time it fires, several reversals may have already
      // happened. This reads the same net-move/range-traveled signature at
      // the position's OWN timeframe, and only acts once the loss is
      // contained (never panics a fresh trade on one noisy bar) and only
      // when TWO consecutive candles have gone against the trade — a single
      // normal pullback inside a real trend will not trip this.
      //
      // LIVE-DATA FIX: raised from 6 to 9 bars. 6 bars often isn't enough
      // real time for the Supertrend line to show meaningful slope right
      // after a fresh flip — the line is naturally sticky for the first
      // several bars of any new trend, fresh or fake. Judging "stalled" at
      // 6 bars in was, in effect, judging trends before they'd had a fair
      // chance to show their hand — part of the reported "closes at the
      // beginning" pattern. 9 bars gives a genuinely young but real trend
      // more room, while still catching actual chop well before the slow
      // 12-bar stall timer would.
      datetime openTime = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
      long barsHeld = (TimeCurrent() - openTime) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1);   // (v18.69 F) guarded, as v18.67 guarded its neighbour
      if(barsHeld >= 9 && profitR > -0.6 && profitR < 0.6)
      {
         double c1Tr = iClose(_Symbol,trTf,1), o1Tr = iOpen(_Symbol,trTf,1);
         double c2Tr = iClose(_Symbol,trTf,2), o2Tr = iOpen(_Symbol,trTf,2);
         bool ctr1 = (dir > 0) ? (c1Tr < o1Tr) : (c1Tr > o1Tr);
         bool ctr2 = (dir > 0) ? (c2Tr < o2Tr) : (c2Tr > o2Tr);
         double hi6Tr = iHigh(_Symbol,trTf,SafeHighest(_Symbol,trTf,MODE_HIGH,6,1));
         double lo6Tr = iLow(_Symbol,trTf,SafeLowest(_Symbol,trTf,MODE_LOW,6,1));
         double rangeTr = hi6Tr - lo6Tr;
         double netTr = MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol,trTf,1) - iClose(_Symbol,trTf,6));
         bool choppyTr = rangeTr > 0 && (netTr / rangeTr) < 0.40;
         // BUG FIX #60 — don't cut a healthy trend that merely paused. Two
         // red candles inside a live uptrend is a normal pause, not a
         // reversal. The Supertrend LINE itself must have genuinely failed
         // to advance since we started watching this trade — not "the
         // current value happens to sit below a price extreme from 3 bars
         // ago" (the old check), which was comparing the line to price, not
         // to its own past position, and could misfire on a perfectly
         // healthy young trend. Snapshot the line once when eligibility
         // first begins; compare against that snapshot from then on.
         double stNowCl; int stNowDir; ScalpST(trK, stNowCl, stNowDir);
         bool haveSnap = StSnapExists(tk);
         if(!haveSnap) StSnapSet(tk, stNowCl);   // first eligible check — just start watching, don't judge yet
         double stSnap = StSnapGet(tk);
         double advance = (dir > 0) ? (stNowCl - stSnap) : (stSnap - stNowCl);
         // "stalled" = the line has made less than a fifth of an ATR of
         // real progress in our favour since we started watching — a
         // genuinely advancing trend clears this easily; real chop won't.
         bool lineStalled = haveSnap && (stNowDir != dir || advance < 0.2 * atr);
         if(!holdGrace && haveSnap && ctr1 && ctr2 && choppyTr && lineStalled)
         {
            PrintFormat("[%s] CHOP CLUSTER on %s at %+.2fR (held %d bars) — going nowhere AND the Supertrend line has genuinely stalled since we started watching. Out.",
                        Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), profitR, (int)barsHeld);
            StSnapClear(tk);
            CloseTagged("CHOP-CLUSTER", tk);
            return;
         }
      }
      else
      {
         // Not eligible right now (too young, or already showing enough
         // movement either way) — clear any stale snapshot so a future
         // eligibility window starts fresh rather than comparing against a
         // long-stale reference point.
         StSnapClear(tk);
      }

      // ══════ (v18.33) THE 8-QUID TRADE THAT PAID 2.50 ══════
      // "I just had a trade sitting at 8 pound, we closed it off at 2.50."
      // At 0.05 lots that is ~1.6 points of open profit banked at ~0.5. Nothing in
      // the exit ARBITER did this, because the arbiter can only veto CloseTagged()
      // calls -- and this was not a close call at all. It was the TRAIL: the stop
      // was ratcheted up behind price, price pulled back a point, and the BROKER
      // closed the position at the stop. A broker-side stop hit cannot be vetoed by
      // any logic in this file, which is why every hold mechanism I have added this
      // session was powerless against it.
      // Two causes, both here:
      //   1. T_TrailArmR = 0.35 -- with a 2.5 pt stop the trail starts adjusting at
      //      0.9 points of profit, i.e. inside the first candle.
      //   2. Arming on R alone ignores absolute distance, so in quiet tape the trail
      //      arms almost immediately and then sits a fraction of a point away.
      // FIX: the trail may not arm until the trade shows BOTH T_TrailArmR of R AND
      // T_TrailArmPts of actual points. Until then the original entry stop holds and
      // the trade is allowed to breathe. This is the single highest-value execution
      // change in this build for his "let it run to 3-5 points" goal.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_TrailArmPts = 0.
      bool trailArmed = (profitR >= T_TrailArmR);
      if(trailArmed && T_TrailArmPts > 0.0)
      {
         double tpts = (dir > 0) ? (px - entry) : (entry - px);
         // (v18.63) peak-relative arm. A 3.00 pt arm means the trail never engages
         // on a trade whose whole life is 2 pts -- and the average peak in his
         // logged session was 3.63, so the trail was arming late or not at all on
         // most trades, leaving only the entry stop and the micro-lock. Capped at
         // T_PeakGateFrac of the peak: a 5.6 pt peak arms at 3.00 as before, a
         // 2.0 pt peak arms at 1.20 instead of never.
         double tArm = PS(T_TrailArmPts);
         if(T_PeakGateTrail && T_PeakGateFrac > 0.0)
         {
            // (v18.65) was PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TICKET), which silently
            // returns 0 if the selection has moved on -- PeakPtsOf(0) then returns
            // 0.0 and the gate quietly stops adapting. tk is the enclosing loop's
            // own ticket and is always right.
            double tpk = PeakPtsOf(tk);
            if(tpk > 0.0) tArm = MathMin(tArm, T_PeakGateFrac * tpk);
         }
         if(tpts < tArm)
         {
            trailArmed = false;
            if(InpVerboseLog && GateSayOnce(trK >= 0 ? trK : 0, dir, 1, trTf))
               PrintFormat("[TREND] TRAIL NOT ARMED: %+.2f pts of profit, needs %.1f. The entry stop holds "
                           "and the trade breathes. A trail that arms this early is what banked 1.6 pts at 0.5.",
                           tpts, tArm);
         }
      }
      if(T_TrailOnST && trailArmed)
      {
         double stVe; int stDe; ScalpST(trK, stVe, stDe);
         if(stVe > 0)
         {
            double bufE = MathMax(SpikeBufferTF(trTf, atr), T_SlNoiseBuf*atr);

            // (v12) ENTRY BREATHING ROOM DECAY. Initial stop was placed
            // wider (T_SlNoiseBuf + T_SlEntryBuf) to survive the noise-dip
            // right after entry. Now ratchet the extra room away: use the
            // MAX of a bars-based decay and a profit-based decay, so once
            // the trade is clearly working (profitR >= 0.8) the buffer
            // collapses to normal immediately, while a trade still stuck
            // in the noise zone in its first few bars keeps the full extra
            // room. Never widens the trail — only steps it in.
            datetime openTimeBuf = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            long barsHeldBuf = (TimeCurrent() - openTimeBuf) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(trTf), 1);   // (v18.69 F) guarded, as v18.67 guarded its neighbour
            double byBars   = MathMax(0.0, 1.0 - (double)barsHeldBuf / MathMax(T_SlEntryBufBars, 1));
            double byProfit = MathMax(0.0, 1.0 - profitR / 0.8);
            double roomFrac = MathMin(1.0, MathMax(byBars, byProfit));
            bufE += T_SlEntryBuf * atr * roomFrac;

            // GRADUATED MOMENTUM-FADE PROTECTION (audit priority: exits).
            // Between "trend fully intact" and "trend flipped" there is a
            // zone where momentum is measurably fading but no flip has fired
            // yet — this is where a +10 quietly bleeds back to +3. Detect
            // that fade and TIGHTEN the trail proportionally, so profit is
            // protected earlier without hard-closing a trade that may still
            // run. Only acts once there is real profit to protect.
            if(profitR >= 0.8)
            {
               int fade = 0;
               double mmF = Buf(g_scMacd[trK],0,1), mmF2 = Buf(g_scMacd[trK],0,3);
               // MACD histogram shrinking toward zero in our favour direction
               if(dir > 0 && mmF < mmF2 && mmF > 0) fade++;
               if(dir < 0 && mmF > mmF2 && mmF < 0) fade++;
               // ADX falling = trend strength bleeding out
               if(Buf(g_scAdx[trK],0,1) < Buf(g_scAdx[trK],0,3)) fade++;
               // RSI rolling over from an extreme (overbought long / oversold short)
               double rsiF = Buf(g_scRsi[trK],0,1), rsiF2 = Buf(g_scRsi[trK],0,2);
               if(dir > 0 && rsiF2 > 68 && rsiF < rsiF2) fade++;
               if(dir < 0 && rsiF2 < 32 && rsiF > rsiF2) fade++;
               // each fade signal pulls the trail in by 20% of the buffer,
               // so with all 3 fading the stop sits almost on the line —
               // catching the giveback early. Never loosens (max 60% tighter).
               bufE *= (1.0 - 0.20 * MathMin(fade, 3));
            }

            double floorStE = (dir > 0) ? stVe - bufE : stVe + bufE;
            bool validE = (dir > 0) ? (floorStE < px && floorStE > newSL)
                                     : (floorStE > px && floorStE < newSL);
            // (v17.91 ROOT CAUSE OF THE 2-POINT EXITS) floorStE is the SUPERTREND
            // LINE minus a buffer. Every mechanism after this uses MathMax, i.e. it
            // can only tighten. On a running move the ST line lags ~T_StMultiplier
            // (2.0) x ATR behind price, while the Chandelier stop below sits ~3x ATR
            // under the PEAK -- so the ST line is always the tighter of the two and
            // MathMax picks it every single time. The result: the trend-following
            // stop that exists in this file is permanently overridden by the ENTRY
            // indicator, and the stop ends up parked exactly at the depth of an
            // ordinary M1 pullback (~1-2 ATR). That is why a 10-40 point move exits
            // at ~2 points, identically, regardless of how far the move was going.
            // The Supertrend line is an ENTRY signal. It is not a stop distance.
            // FIX: while the leg is still making new extremes AND the trade has not
            // yet matured (peak < T_TrailMatureR), the ST-line trail stands down and
            // the wider Chandelier/structure stop is left in charge -- which is what
            // it was built for. Once the move matures, the ST line resumes and banks
            // the peak exactly as before. Nothing else in the chain changes.
            bool trailImmature = (trK >= 0 && LegStillExtending(trTf, dir)
                                  && peakR < T_TrailMatureR);
            if(validE && !trailImmature) newSL = floorStE;
            else if(validE && GateSayOnce(trK >= 0 ? trK : 0, dir, 5, trTf))
               PrintFormat("[TREND] ST-LINE TRAIL STOOD DOWN at %+.2fR — leg still extending, "
                           "wider trend stop stays in charge (ST line would have sat %.2f pts from price).",
                           peakR, MathAbs(px - floorStE));
         }
      }

      // ═══════ (v18.61) THE MISSING PRICE-ANCHORED TRAIL ═══════
      // HIS LIVE EVIDENCE, 19 Aug 18:10. SELL 0.02 entry 4485.93, price 4481.53,
      // session low 4481.19 -- 4.40 pts of open profit, 4.74 of peak -- and the SL
      // sitting at 4485.30. That is entry + spread, i.e. the MICRO-LOCK, untouched.
      // 4.40 points of profit protected by 0.63.
      //
      // WHY. Only two things in this file can move a stop in profit:
      //   1. the SUPERTREND-LINE trail above -- which v17.91 correctly makes STAND
      //      DOWN while the leg is still extending, because the ST line is an entry
      //      indicator and parks the stop at the depth of an ordinary pullback;
      //   2. the MICRO-LOCK -- which goes to entry+spread and then never moves.
      // v17.91's comment says that when the ST trail stands down "the wider
      // Chandelier/structure stop is left in charge". IT IS NOT. No price-anchored
      // trail exists in this path, so during exactly the phase that matters -- a
      // live leg making new extremes -- NOTHING follows price. That is his "our good
      // trades are not being hit at the peak", and it is the same mechanism that
      // turned +GBP15 into +GBP5.
      //
      // FIX: a real Chandelier. Anchor to the best extreme since entry, stand off by
      // T_TrailRoomAtr ATR (1.30 -- the input that was written for this and never
      // wired to an anchor), ratchet only. It runs when the ST trail is standing
      // down, which is the gap.
      //
      // ARITHMETIC ON HIS OPEN TRADE. Low 4481.19, M1 ATR ~1.8 -> standoff 2.34.
      // Stop = 4481.19 + 2.34 = 4483.53, locking 2.40 pts instead of 0.63. At 0.02
      // that is GBP3.53 protected instead of GBP0.93; at his effective double fill,
      // GBP7.06 instead of GBP1.85.
      //
      // WHY IT CANNOT CAUSE THE 2-POINT EXITS v17.91 FIXED. Those came from the ST
      // line, which sits ~1.2 x ATR from the WRONG side of price. This anchors to the
      // PEAK and stands off 1.30 ATR, so it is strictly looser than the ST line was
      // and it never tightens below the micro-lock. It only ever ratchets.
      //
      // DISCLOSURE -- INTERACTION WITH MY OWN v18.60 EDIT. trailImmature depends on
      // LegStillExtending(), which v18.60 changed from "any of 6 bars" (~98% true) to
      // "the last closed bar" (~50%). That means the ST-line trail, which used to
      // stand down almost always, will now engage about half the time -- re-opening
      // the v17.91 bug. This edit is what makes v18.60 safe: with a real price
      // anchor ratcheting underneath, an ST-line trail that engages early can no
      // longer be the only thing holding the stop. Ship them together or not at all.
      // REVERT: T_PeakTrailOn = false.
      // ═══ (v18.72 E) BREAKEVEN ON PROOF, NOT ON TIME ═══════════════════════
      // See note (E). T_HardLockArmPts = 6.0 arms above the reach of most trades
      // (his average offered is 2.72 pts), so his open book showed trades at
      // +4.11 with stops still 3.02 BELOW entry. This arms at +3.0 -- once the
      // trade has beaten its own average -- and locks entry + 0.50, which covers
      // the round trip instead of sitting exactly at flat. A trade lingering red
      // has never been +3.0, so this never touches it. Ratchet only: it can only
      // move the stop in our favour and never past an existing, tighter lock.
      if(T_BeArmPts > 0.0)
      {
         double bePk = PeakPtsOf(tk);
         if(bePk >= PS(T_BeArmPts))
         {
            double beSL = (dir > 0) ? entry + PS(T_BeLockPts) : entry - PS(T_BeLockPts);
            bool better = (dir > 0) ? (beSL > newSL) : (newSL <= 0.0 || beSL < newSL);
            bool safe   = (dir > 0) ? (beSL < px)    : (beSL > px);
            if(better && safe)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[BREAKEVEN] peak %.2f pts >= %.2f arm -- stop to entry %+.2f (%.2f), was %.2f. "
                              "The trade proved itself first; a lingering trade is never touched by this.",
                              bePk, PS(T_BeArmPts), PS(T_BeLockPts), beSL, newSL);
               newSL = beSL;
            }
         }
      }
      // ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      if(T_PeakTrailOn && trailArmed && atr > 0.0)
      {
         datetime oT   = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
         // (v18.67) PeriodSeconds() returns 0 on a bad/unset timeframe -- that is a
         // divide-by-zero, which in MQL5 gives a silent inf/garbage bar count fed
         // straight into iHighest(). Guarded.
         int      psec  = PeriodSeconds(trTf);
         if(psec <= 0) psec = 60;
         int      nB    = (int)MathMin(400, MathMax(2, (TimeCurrent() - oT) / psec + 2));
         double   room  = MathMax(T_TrailRoomAtr, 0.10) * atr;
         // (v18.92 AV) cap the give-back at one noise band. See the note at the input.
         if(T_TrailRoomBandCap)
         {
            double nfTr = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
            if(nfTr > 0.0 && room > nfTr)
            {
               if(InpVerboseLog)
                  PrintFormat("[TRAIL] room capped at one noise band: %.2f -> %.2f pts.", room, nfTr);
               room = nfTr;
            }
         }
         // (v18.66) in thin hours 1.30 x ATR can be under half the spread, which
         // hands every winner straight back to the broker. Spread floor.
         if(T_TrailSpreadMult > 0.0)
         {
            double spR = SpreadPtsNow();
            if(spR > 0.0) room = MathMax(room, T_TrailSpreadMult * spR);
         }
         double   cand  = 0.0;
         if(dir > 0)
         {
            int ih = iHighest(_Symbol, trTf, MODE_HIGH, nB, 0);
            if(ih >= 0) cand = iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, ih) - room;
            // (v18.62) my own v18.61 log bug: newSL was assigned before being printed
            // as the "was" value, so it printed the new number twice. Captured first.
            if(cand > newSL && cand < px) { double wasSL = newSL; newSL = cand;
               if(InpVerboseLog) PrintFormat("[TREND] PEAK TRAIL: high %.2f - %.2f ATR room = SL %.2f (%.2f pts locked, was %.2f).",
                                             iHigh(_Symbol,trTf,ih), T_TrailRoomAtr, cand, cand - entry, wasSL); }
         }
         else
         {
            int il = iLowest(_Symbol, trTf, MODE_LOW, nB, 0);
            if(il >= 0) cand = iLow(_Symbol, trTf, il) + room;
            if(cand > 0.0 && cand < newSL && cand > px) { newSL = cand;
               if(InpVerboseLog) PrintFormat("[TREND] PEAK TRAIL: low %.2f + %.2f ATR room = SL %.2f (%.2f pts locked).",
                                             iLow(_Symbol,trTf,il), T_TrailRoomAtr, cand, entry - cand); }
         }
      }

      // (v14 #3) MICRO-LOCK — the cheapest loss cut there is. Once even a
      // small profit exists, never let the trade go fully red again: park the
      // stop at entry+spread. Turns a lot of "gave back a small winner into a
      // loss" trades into scratch-or-better. Fires earlier than the 0.6R BE.
      // (v16.27) CHOPPY-TRADE BREAKEVEN RESCUE — his rule for chop: "as long as
      // we take some profit or at least break even". A range-born trade that
      // ticks up then rolls over used to give the whole thing back and pay a
      // full stop. Now, in a RANGE regime the breakeven lock arms MUCH earlier
      // (T_RangeBeR, default 0.15R instead of 0.30R), so the typical choppy
      // wiggle exits at ~scratch instead of -1R. Trending trades are untouched:
      // arming BE early on a real trend is how you get stopped out of runners.
      // (v16.30 FINE-TUNE) "if it can't make profit it shouldn't risk" — the
      // early breakeven arm was limited to trades BORN in a range. But a
      // mediocre signal in a NEUTRAL regime deserves the same treatment: if it
      // is not a confirmed healthy trend, get the stop to breakeven as soon as
      // there is anything to protect, so the worst realistic outcome is a
      // scratch rather than a full stop. A confirmed healthy trend is still
      // excluded on purpose — arming breakeven early on a real runner is
      // exactly how you get shaken out of the trades that pay for the day.
      // (v16.31 CORRECTION) This was widened in v16.30 to every non-healthy-trend
      // trade — wrong, and worse than before. His rule was about CHOPPY trades.
      // A NEUTRAL regime is frequently where a trend is being BORN (the whole
      // point of the breakout-birth read), so arming breakeven at 0.15R there
      // scratches out of exactly the trades that were about to run. Back to
      // range-BORN trades only, which is what he actually asked for.
      double microArm = T_MicroLockR;
      if(T_RangeBreakeven && e == E_TREND && trK >= 0 && g_trEntryRegime[trK] < 0)
         microArm = T_RangeBeR;

      // (v16.35 IMPROVEMENT 1) POINT-BASED BREAKEVEN ARM — shortens the ONLY
      // window in which this system can lose. Once the stop reaches breakeven the
      // trade cannot finish red, so the entire loss exposure is the period BEFORE
      // that. The R-based arm alone leaves that window elastic: R is measured off
      // the signal-line stop, so on a wide-ATR bar 0.30R can be several points of
      // travel, and the trade stays loseable much longer than on a quiet bar. This
      // adds an ABSOLUTE trigger — as soon as profit clears the spread by
      // T_BeArmPoints of price, breakeven arms regardless of R. Whichever
      // condition fires first wins, so the loss window can only ever get SHORTER,
      // never longer.
      double profitPts = profitR * R;                       // profit expressed in price
      // (v16.42) VOLATILITY-ADAPTIVE BREAKEVEN ARM — the fix for the CHOPPY day,
      // which is the weakest day type and also the one with the most signals.
      // T_BeArmPoints alone is ABSOLUTE (1.20 pts). In quiet/choppy conditions M1
      // ATR can be 0.3-0.5 pts, so 1.20 pts is 3-4x ATR away — breakeven almost
      // never armed before the flip failed, and the day bled ~2pt losses on
      // 50-plus trades. Now the arm fires at whichever comes FIRST: the fixed
      // point trigger, or T_BeArmAtr x ATR. In low volatility the ATR route arms
      // far sooner, turning a large block of choppy-day losers into scratches.
      // Floored just above the spread so it can never arm at a level that would
      // be hit by the bid/ask alone. HONEST TRADE-OFF: arming sooner also scratches
      // some trades that would have recovered and run — but a scratch beats a
      // loss, and this is exactly the "loss only at the beginning" principle
      // applied where the losses actually are. T_BeArmAtr = 0 disables the ATR
      // route and restores pure point-based behaviour.
      double armPts = T_BeArmPoints;
      if(T_BeArmAtr > 0.0 && atr > 0.0)
      {
         double propArm = MathMax(T_BeArmAtr * atr, 0.15);
         // (v17.20) mode 0 keeps the original MIN (can only arm sooner);
         // mode 1 uses the proportional value directly, so the arm is the same
         // fraction of a bar in every session instead of 4-5x tighter in the
         // active hours. See the T_BeArmMode note at the input declaration.
         armPts = (T_BeArmMode == 1) ? propArm : MathMin(armPts, propArm);
      }
      bool armByPoints = (armPts > 0.0) && (profitPts >= SpreadUSD() + armPts);

      // ══════════ (v18.93 AX) STACK LADDER-OUT — HIS DIRECT ASK ══════════
      // "we enter with multiple positions of smaller lots so it's easier to take
      //  partials... how do we claim it all"
      //
      // THE PROBLEM A TRAIL CANNOT SOLVE. Every trailing floor in this build is
      // capped by the noise band -- v17.98 and v18.73(K) both enforce it, and
      // correctly: a floor inside the band is taken by an ordinary pullback. So on
      // a 2.4 pt peak with a 1.3 pt band, the BEST any trail can lock is ~1.1 pts.
      // Measured on his 4636.20 stack: peak 2.38, closed 0.83. The trail was not
      // broken. It was at its floor.
      //
      // WHAT MULTIPLE POSITIONS BUY YOU. One position must choose between banking
      // the peak and riding the runner. THREE positions do not have to choose --
      // but only if they exit at DIFFERENT prices. Three fills closed at the same
      // price is arithmetically identical to one fill of three times the size; it
      // captures nothing extra. Today they all exit together, so the stack is
      // currently doing nothing except multiplying the money on one decision.
      //
      // SO: the OLDEST fill of a stack banks at a fixed TARGET, and the rest stay
      // on the trail. A target can act inside the band because it is not a floor
      // being chased -- nothing can pull back into it.
      //
      // THE NUMBER, derived, not invented. One noise band is the width of ordinary
      // noise, so a close inside it is not profit. TWO bands is the first level at
      // which a close is unambiguously the move rather than the noise. On his tape
      // that is 2.6-3.8 pts, which sits exactly on his observed chop peaks (2.38,
      // 2.62, 3.38) and well under his trend peaks (4.23, 8.12) -- so it banks the
      // chop and lets the trends run on the remaining fills.
      //
      // NEVER fires on a lone position: if there is nothing else open in the same
      // direction there is no ladder, only an early exit, and that is not the ask.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_StackLadderOut = false.
      if(T_StackLadderOut && T_StackBankBands > 0.0 && e == E_TREND && atr > 0.0)
      {
         double lbBand = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
         double lbNeed = T_StackBankBands * lbBand;
         if(lbBand > 0.0 && profitPts >= lbNeed)
         {
            // am I the OLDEST same-direction fill, and is there anyone behind me?
            datetime myT   = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            int      older = 0, sameDir = 0;
            for(int li = PositionsTotal() - 1; li >= 0; li--)
            {
               ulong lt = PositionGetTicket(li);
               if(lt == 0 || lt == tk) continue;
               if(!PositionSelectByTicket(lt)) continue;
               if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
               if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) != Cfg[E_TREND].magic) continue;
               int ld = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
               if(ld != dir) continue;
               sameDir++;
               if((datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME) < myT) older++;
            }
            PositionSelectByTicket(tk);   // restore the selection this block relies on
            if(sameDir >= 1 && older == 0)
            {
               PrintFormat("[%s] STACK BANK — #%I64u is the first of %d fills and is +%.2f pts, "
                           "clear of %.1f noise bands (%.2f pts each). Taking this one at the target "
                           "and leaving the other %d on the trail. A trail cannot lock inside the "
                           "band; a target can.",
                           Cfg[e].name, tk, sameDir + 1, profitPts, T_StackBankBands, lbBand, sameDir);
               ClearPeakR(tk);
               CloseTagged("STACK-BANK", tk);
               return;
            }
         }
      }

      if(T_MicroLock && (profitR >= microArm || armByPoints))
      {
         double mlLevel = (dir > 0) ? entry + SpreadUSD() : entry - SpreadUSD();
         // (v16.66) CONTINUOUS PEAK RATCHET — see T_PeakRatchet. Lifts the floor to
         // entry + a fraction of the PEAK profit reached, so a trade that touched
         // +2.5pts banks a share of it instead of scratching at breakeven.
         if(T_PeakRatchet && T_PeakRatchetFrac > 0.0 && R > 0.0)
         {
            double peakPts = PeakR(tk) * R;
            if(peakPts > SpreadUSD())
            {
               double rFrac   = RatchetFracFor(trTf);   // (v16.95) per-clock
               // (v17.02 FIX TO MY OWN v17.01 — CAUGHT ON HIS 29 Jul 19:00-20:11 M1 CHART)
               // v17.01 tightened the ratchet whenever the leg stopped making new
               // extremes. But a MID-TREND CONSOLIDATION also stops making new
               // extremes, and tightening there kills the trade before the second
               // half of the move. On that chart: entry 4010.5, peak 4023 (+12.5),
               // the 19:30-19:47 consolidation dipped to 4015.5, then price ran to
               // 4040.7.
               //   loose 0.30 -> floor 4014.25, dip SURVIVES  -> +30.2pts = GBP67.77
               //   tight 0.75 -> floor 4019.88, dip STOPS OUT -> + 9.4pts = GBP21.04
               // I made this exact mistake in v16.71 and fixed it in v16.76 by
               // requiring !healthyTrend. Same fix, same reason: a HEALTHY trend that
               // pauses is RESTING; an unhealthy one that pauses is FINISHED. So the
               // tightening now only applies when the trend is not healthy, and the
               // loosening still applies whenever the leg is actively extending.
               // (v17.02, corrected on the second pass) Leaving the base fraction alone
               // during a healthy pause was STILL too tight: M1's base is 0.60 (set in
               // v16.95 to bank fast moves near their peak), which puts the floor at
               // 4018.00 against a 4015.5 dip — stopped out again. The base fraction
               // and the trend-holding fraction are simply different jobs.
               // FINAL SHAPE — healthyTrend is the primary discriminator, extension is
               // secondary:
               //   healthy (running OR pausing) -> LOOSE. It is a real trend; give it
               //     room. floor 4014.25 vs a 4015.5 dip = SURVIVES, captures 30.2pts.
               //   not healthy, still extending  -> base. Might be something, no proof.
               //   not healthy, stopped          -> TIGHT. Bank the peak; this is the
               //     whipsaw case he wants profit from.
               if(T_ExtendAwareRatchet)
               {
                  // (v17.29 — his direct ask: "make sure we know when to hold longer
                  // even after a dip if something else provides confirmation.") ADX-regime
                  // (healthyTrend) and swing-structure (structIntact) are both ALREADY votes
                  // for holding through a dip. This adds a THIRD, genuinely independent one:
                  // DEMA(200) SIDE. It is independent because it is neither an oscillator
                  // (ADX) nor a swing-geometry read (structure) — it is the slow moving
                  // average the whole system already treats as the macro trend filter
                  // everywhere else (onDemaSide gates entries, the against-DEMA risk trim
                  // exists specifically because DEMA agreement/disagreement is informative).
                  // Reusing DemaVal(), not duplicating it — same lesson as every other
                  // "don't re-derive a reader you already have" fix this session.
                  // (v17.41 — his ask: "if I made a sell and it closed asap but then
                  // 3 or 5 shows a sell it needs to make sure it doesn't close.")
                  // A 4th hold vote: if ANY OTHER clock's Supertrend currently agrees
                  // with this trade's direction, the idea is confirmed on more than one
                  // timeframe, so the ratchet loosens and the trade gets room. This is
                  // a HOLD reason only — it never opens a trade and never blocks one.
                  bool otherClockAgrees = false;
                  for(int ck = 0; ck < SC_TFN; ck++)
                  {
                     if(ck == trK) continue;
                     double svX; int sdX; ScalpST(ck, svX, sdX);
                     if(sdX == dir) { otherClockAgrees = true; break; }
                  }
                  // v17.46, his direct ask: "it can hover around the DEMA line, so we
                  // have to know when to hold" — a bare greater/less-than test flickers true/false every
                  // tick when price is sitting right on the line, which flickers this whole
                  // vote and makes the ratchet's behaviour depend on noise rather than on
                  // trend. A small ATR-scaled buffer means "on the right side" has to mean
                  // something, not just win a coin-flip against the exact line.
                  double demaHold  = DemaVal(E_TREND);
                  double demaAtrH  = Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1);
                  double demaBuf   = (demaAtrH > 0) ? 0.10 * demaAtrH : 0.0;
                  bool   demaConfirmsHold = (demaHold > 0) &&
                                             ((dir > 0) ? (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) > demaHold + demaBuf)
                                                        : (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK) < demaHold - demaBuf));
                  if(healthyTrend || structIntact || demaConfirmsHold || otherClockAgrees)      rFrac *= T_RatchetLooseMult;   // (v17.07/17.29) structIntact covers CHOP, demaConfirmsHold is the new 3rd vote
                  // (v17.12) HTF hold-duration modulation. This is the ONLY place
                  // higher-timeframe structure is allowed to change behaviour, and
                  // it changes only HOW LONG we hold — never whether we enter.
                  // T_HtfLogOnly=true measures the score in the journal while
                  // leaving the ratchet untouched, so it can be validated before
                  // it is trusted (this system's F grade is validation, so a new
                  // mechanism ships with its own off-switch by default policy).
                  if(T_HtfHold && !T_HtfLogOnly)
                  {
                     // (v17.28 SEVERE BUG, MINE, FOUND FROM HIS REPORT "trades never
                     // hold more than a few candles" — live AND backtest, both, which
                     // ruled out a tester artefact and pointed straight back at the
                     // exit logic.) rFrac is the FRACTION OF PEAK LOCKED AS THE FLOOR:
                     // higher rFrac = floor closer to the peak = TIGHTER, less room.
                     // lower rFrac = floor closer to entry = LOOSER, more room.
                     // I wrote `rFrac *= T_HtfLooseMult` (1.35, >1) for the ALIGNED
                     // case and `rFrac *= T_HtfTightMult` (0.75, <1) for the OPPOSED
                     // case — which INCREASES rFrac (tightens) exactly when the trade
                     // is aligned with the higher timeframe and should get MORE room,
                     // and DECREASES rFrac (loosens) exactly when it is fighting the
                     // higher timeframe and should be banked sooner. Backwards, on
                     // every single ratchet-managed trade, since T_HtfHold defaults
                     // true. This also runs AFTER the healthyTrend||structIntact LOOSE
                     // multiplier a few lines up, so it was silently re-tightening
                     // exactly the healthy trends that multiplier had just loosened.
                     // FIX: divide instead of multiply, so the input names keep their
                     // stated meaning exactly as declared.
                     int hb = HtfBias(dir, trTf);   // (v17.34) scoped to the calling clock's own timescale
                     if(hb >=  1) rFrac /= T_HtfLooseMult;   // aligned: room to run -> LOOSEN (shrink rFrac)
                     if(hb <= -1) rFrac /= T_HtfTightMult;   // opposed: no room     -> TIGHTEN (grow rFrac)
                  }
                  else if(LegStillExtending(trTf, dir)) { /* base fraction, unchanged */ }
                  else                                  rFrac *= T_RatchetTightMult;
               }
               if(rFrac > 0.95) rFrac = 0.95;           // never lock the entire peak — always leave headroom
               double lockPts = MathMax(SpreadUSD(), rFrac * peakPts);
               // (v17.98) the ratchet floor may not sit inside the noise band below
               // the peak. rFrac 0.60 on a 3pt peak locked +1.80 and left 1.20pts of
               // room against a 1.50pt noise band -- guaranteed stop-out on an
               // ordinary pullback. Now it locks at most peak - noise, and on a peak
               // too small to clear the band it reverts to breakeven+spread and stops
               // ratcheting at all.
               double nfRat = NoiseFloorPts(Buf(g_scAtr[trK], 0, 1));
               if(nfRat > 0.0 && (peakPts - lockPts) < nfRat)
                  lockPts = MathMax(SpreadUSD(), peakPts - nfRat);
               // ══════════════ v18.73 (K) BUG FIX — THE NOISE FLOOR WAS ONE-SIDED ══════════════
               // The clause above only ever LOOSENS the floor: when the proportional
               // giveback is SMALLER than the noise band it pushes the floor down so an
               // ordinary pullback cannot take it. Correct. But nothing capped the
               // giveback from ABOVE, so `(1 - rFrac) * peak` kept scaling with the peak
               // forever. rFrac ~0.58 on his 20 Aug tape:
               //     peak 17.00 -> gave back 7.14 pts, band was 3.04  -> 4.10 donated
               //     peak 13.79 -> gave back  5.79 pts, band was 3.04  -> 2.75 donated
               // Measured against his tickets (entry 4522.85 peak 4539.85 exit 4530.92,
               // and entry 4526.06 peak 4539.85 exit 4534.11):
               //     18:02:27  +8.07 (47% of peak)  ->  +13.96 (82%)   +5.89 pts
               //     18:02:36  +8.05 (58% of peak)  ->  +10.75 (78%)   +2.70 pts
               // The band IS the required breathing room -- that is what the clause above
               // asserts. Giving back more than the band is a donation, not protection.
               // NOTE THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE: on a SMALL peak the proportional floor
               // is the higher of the two and still wins this MathMax, so chop behaviour
               // is byte-for-byte unchanged. peak 3.4 -> 0.58*3.4 = 1.97 vs 3.4-1.9 = 1.50,
               // proportional wins. This change can only ever bite on peaks larger than
               // roughly 2x the noise band. Blast radius is large winners only.
               // OFF-SWITCH: T_RatchetCapGiveback = false.
               if(T_RatchetCapGiveback && nfRat > 0.0 && (peakPts - lockPts) > nfRat)
                  lockPts = MathMax(lockPts, peakPts - nfRat);
               double rLevel  = (dir > 0) ? entry + lockPts : entry - lockPts;
               mlLevel = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(mlLevel, rLevel) : MathMin(mlLevel, rLevel);
            }
         }
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, mlLevel) : MathMin(newSL, mlLevel);

         // ── (v16.77) SCALE-ADD — see T_ScaleAdd ──────────────────────────────
         // Fires ONLY here, inside the block that has just proved the floor sits
         // above entry. The add's stop is that same floor, so its worst case is the
         // floor distance rather than the full 6/9/12pt stop.
         bool m1QuickHoldActive = (T_M1QuickHold && trTf == PERIOD_M1);
         if(T_ScaleAdd && T_ScaleAddLots > 0.0 && AddCountFor(tk) < T_ScaleAddMax && !m1QuickHoldActive)
         {
            double gainedPts = (dir > 0) ? (SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID) - entry)
                                         : (entry - SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK));
            bool floorAboveEntry = (dir > 0) ? (mlLevel > entry) : (mlLevel < entry);
            // (v16.78) CHOP GUARD ON THE ADD — his 28 Jul 17:20-19:05 M1 chop chart.
            // The two losing legs there NEVER reached +1pt, so the add would not have
            // armed on either: chop that fails early costs nothing extra. The residual
            // risk is the leg that touches the trigger and then reverses immediately —
            // base +0.35, add -0.65, net about -GBP0.71 instead of +GBP0.26.
            // LegStillExtending() removes exactly that case: it requires a NEW extreme
            // in the trade's favour within the last few bars, so the add only goes on
            // while the leg is actively making ground, never while it stalls at the
            // trigger. Costs a few adds on legs that pause then resume; those are
            // picked up by the reclaim watch instead.
            if(gainedPts >= PS(T_ScaleAddTrigPts) && floorAboveEntry
               && (!T_ScaleAddNeedExtend || LegStillExtending(trTf, dir)))
            {
               double addTot = OpenLotsTotal() + T_ScaleAddLots;
               // (v18.76) THE SCALE-ADD NOW ASKS THE SAME TWO QUESTIONS A BASE
               // ENTRY ASKS. It previously checked InpMaxTotalLots (0.25) and
               // nothing else -- not T_MaxDirLots (0.10), not margin. So the
               // directional ceiling could be passed silently, one 0.02 at a
               // time, by the one mechanism that fires at NEW EXTREMES.
               // OFF-SWITCH: T_AddsRespectCaps = false.
               bool addCapsOk = true;
               if(T_AddsRespectCaps)
               {
                  if(DirExposureFull(dir, T_ScaleAddLots))
                  {
                     addCapsOk = false;
                     PrintFormat("[SCALE-ADD REFUSED] direction ceiling. %.2f lots wanted on top of %.2f already open this way, cap %.2f. This add fires on a NEW EXTREME, which is exactly the chase guard X refuses for ordinary adds.",
                                 T_ScaleAddLots, OpenLotsThisDir(dir), T_MaxDirLots);
                  }
                  else if(!MarginOkFor(T_ScaleAddLots, dir))
                  {
                     addCapsOk = false;
                     PrintFormat("[SCALE-ADD REFUSED] margin. %.2f lots would breach the free-margin floor.", T_ScaleAddLots);
                  }
               }
               if(addCapsOk && addTot <= InpMaxTotalLots + 1e-9)
               {
                  double addPx = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                           : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
                  double addSl = NP(mlLevel);
                  trade.SetExpertMagicNumber(Cfg[e].magic);
                  bool addOk = (dir > 0) ? trade.Buy (T_ScaleAddLots, _Symbol, 0.0, addSl, 0.0, "scale-add")
                                         : trade.Sell(T_ScaleAddLots, _Symbol, 0.0, addSl, 0.0, "scale-add");
                  if(addOk)
                  {
                     MarkAdd(tk);
                     // register the new ticket on the SAME clock so the standard
                     // ManagePosition loop picks it up (not the old pyramid path)
                     for(int q = PositionsTotal()-1; q >= 0; q--)
                     {
                        ulong at = PositionGetTicket(q);
                        if(at == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(at)) continue;
                        if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[e].magic &&
                           (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME) >= TimeCurrent()-2 && at != tk)
                        { StoreTrendTF(at, trK); break; }
                     }
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SCALE-ADD on %s — base is %.2f pts onside with its floor at %.2f (above entry %.2f), so adding %.2f lots with the SAME floor as its stop. Total size now %.2f. Added risk is the floor distance, not the full stop.",
                                 EnumToString(trTf), gainedPts, addSl, entry, T_ScaleAddLots, addTot);
                  }
                  else
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] SCALE-ADD refused by broker on %s (err %d).", EnumToString(trTf), GetLastError());
                  PositionSelectByTicket(tk);   // restore selection for the rest of ManagePosition
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // (v16.35 IMPROVEMENT 2) SCRATCH-PLUS — turn the zero-return trades into
      // small wins. The micro-lock parks the stop at exactly entry+spread, so a
      // trade that runs to +0.8R and then rolls over all the way back exits for
      // GBP0.00: it paid the spread and handed back everything. Across ~80 trades
      // a day a large share finish exactly there, which is a big block of the day
      // returning nothing. This adds one more rung: from T_ScratchPlusR upward the
      // floor sits a little ABOVE breakeven, so those same trades finish small-
      // POSITIVE instead of flat. That is precisely the "profit by a little or a
      // lot, but profit" property the strategy is built around. Deliberately armed
      // later than the breakeven rung and kept small, so there is real separation
      // between price and stop and it does not clip a trade that recovers.
      if(T_ScratchPlus && atr > 0 && profitR >= T_ScratchPlusR)
      {
         double spLevel = (dir > 0) ? entry + SpreadUSD() + T_ScratchPlusAtr * atr
                                    : entry - SpreadUSD() - T_ScratchPlusAtr * atr;
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, spLevel) : MathMin(newSL, spLevel);
      }

      // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #4 (v15.6: GRADUATED — see fix #64 at the inputs) ═══
      // Holds a fraction of the best R this trade ever reached, from
      // T_PeakLockArmR upward. The fraction now SCALES with how far the
      // trade has run: ~0.45 of peak at the 1R arm point (a young trend
      // needs room to pull back and keep going — this is what "ride the
      // trend" means), climbing linearly to 0.75 by 5R (a mature runner
      // banks near its tip). Only ratchets forward, never loosens.
      if(T_PeakLock)
      {
         double pkNow = PeakR(tk);
         if(pkNow >= T_PeakLockArmR && R > 0)
         {
            double t64 = MathMin(1.0, (pkNow - T_PeakLockArmR) / MathMax(5.0 - T_PeakLockArmR, 0.1));
            double frac = T_PeakLockFracLo + (T_PeakLockFracHi - T_PeakLockFracLo) * t64;
            double lockR = pkNow * frac;
            double lockLevel = (dir > 0) ? entry + lockR * R : entry - lockR * R;
            newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, lockLevel) : MathMin(newSL, lockLevel);
         }
      }

      // ═══ IMPROVEMENT (v16.14 RESEARCH #3) — CHANDELIER VOLATILITY TIP-LOCK ═══
      // Classic Chandelier Exit: trail an EXTRA floor a volatility distance
      // below the HIGHEST HIGH reached since entry (above the lowest low for a
      // short), using the live ATR. Taken as the tighter of this and every
      // existing floor (peak-lock, step-lock, signal-line trail) so it can only
      // ever bank MORE, never loosen. Unlike the R-from-entry peak-lock it
      // anchors to the real price extreme and CONTRACTS as ATR dries up near a
      // top — exactly where gold rolls over — banking closer to the tip on the
      // big parabolic runs. Loose (3x ATR) + armed only from decent profit, so
      // it protects the tip without clipping a healthy ride. atr here is the
      // engine-aware ATR resolved at the top of ManagePosition.
      if(T_ChandelierLock && atr > 0)
      {
         double pkCh = PeakR(tk);
         if(pkCh >= T_ChandelierArmR)
         {
            ENUM_TIMEFRAMES chTf = (e == E_TREND) ? g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)] : Cfg[e].tf;
            // (v16.22) ADAPTIVE MULTIPLIER (research-backed): tighten the trail
            // when ADX is RISING (strong/accelerating trend — secure the gains
            // close) and widen it when ADX is FALLING (weakening/choppy — give
            // it room so a normal wobble doesn't clip a still-valid trade). A
            // fixed multiplier is the documented weakness of stock Supertrend.
            int chAdxH = (e == E_TREND) ? g_scAdx[TrendTFOf(tk)] : hAdx[e];
            double adxN = Buf(chAdxH, 0, 1), adxP = Buf(chAdxH, 0, 4);
            double chMult = T_ChandelierMult;
            if(adxN > 0 && adxP > 0)
               chMult = (adxN >= adxP) ? T_ChandelierMult * 0.80    // ADX rising -> tighter, bank the accel
                                       : T_ChandelierMult * 1.20;   // ADX falling -> wider, survive the noise
            datetime openT = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
            int barsIn = (int)((TimeCurrent() - openT) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(chTf), 1)) + 1;
            barsIn = (int)MathMax(2, MathMin(barsIn, 300));
            if(dir > 0)
            {
               int hIdx = iHighest(_Symbol, chTf, MODE_HIGH, barsIn, 1);
               if(hIdx >= 0)
               {
                  double chLevel = iHigh(_Symbol, chTf, hIdx) - chMult * atr;
                  if(chLevel < px) newSL = MathMax(newSL, chLevel);   // never place the stop above current price
               }
            }
            else
            {
               int lIdx = iLowest(_Symbol, chTf, MODE_LOW, barsIn, 1);
               if(lIdx >= 0)
               {
                  double chLevel = iLow(_Symbol, chTf, lIdx) + chMult * atr;
                  if(chLevel > px) newSL = MathMin(newSL, chLevel);   // never place the stop below current price
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // (v14 #10) STEP-LOCK RATCHET — bank the floor upward in fixed R steps as
      // the runner grows, so a big move can't round-trip back through a step it
      // already cleared. At +2R the stop can't fall below +1R, at +3R not below
      // +2R, and so on. Complements the trail without loosening it.
      if(T_StepLock && profitR >= 2.0)
      {
         double stepR = MathFloor(profitR) - 1.0;   // cleared-step floor, one R behind the current whole R
         if(R > 0)
         {
            double stepLevel = (dir > 0) ? entry + stepR * R : entry - stepR * R;
            newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, stepLevel) : MathMin(newSL, stepLevel);
         }
      }

      // (v12d) EARLY BREAKEVEN LOCK — the single cheapest "tighter closes"
      // improvement there is: once the trade has shown 0.6R of real profit,
      // the stop moves to entry plus the spread. From that moment the trade
      // can only ever finish at zero-or-better. Waiting until T_Tp1R (1.5R)
      // meant a trade could reach 1.2R and still round-trip to a full loss —
      // exactly the "EA doesn't believe in its own confidence" complaint.
      if(T_EarlyBE && profitR >= T_EarlyBE_R)
      {
         double beLevel = (dir > 0) ? entry + SpreadUSD() : entry - SpreadUSD();
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, beLevel) : MathMin(newSL, beLevel);
      }

      if(profitR >= T_Tp1R)   // breakeven at TP1
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, entry) : MathMin(newSL, entry);

      // (v17.72) FOUND IT. His 13:01:40 buy: entry 4189.513, closed 13:02:50 at
      // 4190.010 for +1.99, and the ticket shows S/L 4190.010 — the trade was
      // taken out BY ITS OWN STOP, which had been ratcheted to half a point above
      // entry 70 seconds in. His push notification even caught it mid-move:
      // "position buy 0.04 modified: sl 4189.987". Not one discretionary exit was
      // involved, which is why every grace, vote and floor I added left this
      // untouched: stops are deliberately never suppressed.
      // The flaw is not that it locks profit, it is WHERE. T_EarlyBE arms at 0.6R
      // (~1.8pts at an M1 ATR of ~1.0) and parks the stop at entry+spread, i.e.
      // INSIDE the noise band the trade is still breathing in. A 2pt pullback is
      // routine on M1 gold and it scratches the trade for +0.5pt. That is the
      // "closes for pennies on a clean move" complaint, and it is arithmetic.
      // FIX: until the trade has proven itself (peak >= T_LockRoomUntilR), any
      // locked stop must keep at least T_LockRoomAtr x ATR of air between itself
      // and live price. It can still only ever tighten — never wider than the
      // stop already on the position — so protection is preserved, it simply
      // stops sitting where a normal wick will find it.
      // (v17.75, his report: "we keep closing way too early due to trailing")
      // The standoff now also applies WHILE THE LEG IS STILL EXTENDING, at any
      // profit level, using the wider T_TrailRoomAtr. Reason: the ratchet tightens
      // toward 0.25 ATR as a trade proves itself, which is correct for a leg that
      // has stalled and wrong for one still printing new extremes -- that is where
      // a 10pt move gets cut at 3. Once the leg stops extending the tight ratchet
      // returns and takes the peak, which is what it is for.
      bool trailExtending = (trK >= 0 && LegStillExtending(trTf, dir));
      if((T_LockRoomAtr > 0.0 && peakR < T_LockRoomUntilR) || (T_TrailRoomAtr > 0.0 && trailExtending))
      {
         int    lrK  = (trK >= 0 && trK < SC_TFN) ? trK : SC_TFN - 1;
         double atrL = Buf(g_scAtr[lrK], 0, 1);
         double pxL  = (dir > 0) ? bid : ask;
         double curSL = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
         if(atrL > 0.0 && pxL > 0.0)
         {
            double minGap = (trailExtending ? MathMax(T_TrailRoomAtr, T_LockRoomAtr) : T_LockRoomAtr) * atrL;
            if(dir > 0)
            {
               double capped = pxL - minGap;
               if(newSL > capped)
               {
                  double kept = (curSL > 0.0) ? MathMax(curSL, capped) : capped;
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] LOCK HELD BACK: stop would sit at %.2f, only %.2f pts under price. "
                                 "Placed at %.2f instead (%.2f x ATR of room) — a routine wick is not an exit.",
                                 newSL, pxL - newSL, kept, T_LockRoomAtr);
                  newSL = kept;
               }
            }
            else
            {
               double capped = pxL + minGap;
               if(newSL < capped)
               {
                  double kept = (curSL > 0.0) ? MathMin(curSL, capped) : capped;
                  if(InpVerboseLog)
                     PrintFormat("[TREND] LOCK HELD BACK: stop would sit at %.2f, only %.2f pts over price. "
                                 "Placed at %.2f instead (%.2f x ATR of room) — a routine wick is not an exit.",
                                 newSL, newSL - pxL, kept, T_LockRoomAtr);
                  newSL = kept;
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // (v12d) PARTIAL PROFIT BANKING — mirror of SCALP's BankAt1R, now for
      // TREND too. When size allows (>= 2x the minimum lot — i.e. any tier
      // trade at 0.02+, and every trade once the account has grown), bank a
      // third of the position at 1R. The rest rides the trend exactly as
      // before, but a third of every decent move is ALWAYS taken home even
      // if the rest round-trips. Cash flows in constantly — the "maximise
      // profit taken home" behaviour, structurally.
      if(T_BankPartial && profitR >= 1.0 && (!PartialDone(tk) || T_StepPartials))
      {
         double curLots = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
         if(curLots >= 2.0 * g_lotMin)
         {
            // (v18.05) was a THIRD on one event; now one 0.01 step, repeatable as
            // the trade keeps earning: 0.04 sheds 0.01/0.01/0.01 and rides 0.01.
            double cut = StepBank(tk, curLots, MathFloor((curLots / 3.0) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep);
            if(cut >= g_lotMin && trade.PositionClosePartial(tk, cut))
            {
               PositionSelectByTicket(tk);   // partial close changed the selected-position context — restore it
               MarkPartialDone(tk);
               PrintFormat("[%s] PARTIAL BANK on %s — closed %.2f of %.2f lots at +%.1fR. The rest rides.",
                           Cfg[e].name, EnumToString(trTf), cut, curLots, profitR);
            }
         }
      }

      // (v16.03) REMOVED: the v12d STAGNATION EXIT (close after 25 bars
      // within ±0.25R). Weakest of THREE overlapping going-nowhere timers:
      // the stall timer fires at 12 bars without a new extreme, the
      // chop-cluster at 9 bars with counter-candles and a stalled line — a
      // trade surviving both to sit pinned at zero for 25 bars is a
      // unicorn, and its stated purpose (a dead trade squatting on the
      // direction lock, costing the next trend) was largely solved by fix
      // #63, which lets a faster-clock reversal trade open anyway. Three
      // timers for one job is how self-conflicts like #64 happen; two
      // earn their keep, this one didn't.

      // (v12e) SUPER-CONFIDENT PYRAMID TOP-UP — the "when we're SUPERRR
      // confident, add another 0.01/0.02" mechanism, done the low-risk way:
      //   · only once the original trade is ≥ T_PyramidMinR in profit —
      //     which means the early breakeven lock (0.6R) is already on, so
      //     the ORIGINAL position is risk-free at this point
      //   · only if the confluence stack STILL agrees right now (fresh
      //     ConfluenceTier read — tier 2 minimum: M1/M3/M5 + M15)
      //   · the add is small and fixed (0.01, or 0.02 on a full tier-3
      //     stack), with its OWN stop at the current signal line — its
      //     worst case is covered several times over by the open profit
      //   · once per position, capped per day. Rare by design.
      // Net effect: extra size lands only on trades that are already
      // winning AND still fully confirmed — low risk, extra reward.
      // (v17.55) THE ADD NOW REQUIRES THE LEG TO STILL BE MAKING NEW EXTREMES —
      // his exact worry: "these reentries were super good but that was a very
      // strong trend; if it wasn't, they could have made a loss." The scale-add
      // path already carried this requirement (T_ScaleAddNeedExtend, default
      // true); the pyramid top-up did NOT, so it could add into a trade sitting
      // at +1.2R whose leg had already stopped advancing — i.e. the top of the
      // move. LegStillExtending() is pure price evidence (a new favourable
      // extreme within T_ExtendLookback bars), no ADX, nothing that can lag. A
      // chop leg fails it within a bar or two by definition, which is precisely
      // the case he is scared of. Everything else about the gate is unchanged.
      // ════════ (v18.22) STACKING — WHY IT NEVER HAPPENS ════════
      // "we're missing our stacking, on the straight trends we need to stack."
      // The mechanism EXISTS (this block) and is ON (T_Pyramid = true). It has
      // never fired, because FOUR conditions must all hold at once:
      //   1. profitR >= T_PyramidMinR (1.2R)
      //   2. !PyramidDone(tk)          -- ONE top-up per position, EVER
      //   3. LegStillExtending()
      //   4. ConfluenceTier() >= 2     -- a multi-timeframe confluence score
      // Condition 2 is the killer for what he wants: a hard limit of one add per
      // position means "stacking" was never possible by construction -- at best
      // 0.05 becomes 0.06, once. Condition 1 compounds it: at a 4 pt stop, 1.2R
      // is ~4.8 pts of profit before the FIRST add is even considered, by which
      // point most M1 legs are done.
      // CHANGES: T_PyramidMaxPerPos allows several adds per position (default 3),
      // and T_PyramidMinR drops to 0.6 so the first add lands while the leg is
      // still running rather than after it. Every add still requires the leg to be
      // extending AND confluence tier >= 2, so this stacks into strength only --
      // it is not permission to average into a loser.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_PyramidMaxPerPos = 1 restores the old one-and-done.
      if(T_Pyramid && profitR >= T_PyramidMinR
         && (PyramidCount(tk) < T_PyramidMaxPerPos)
         && LegStillExtending(trTf, dir))
      {
         MqlDateTime pt; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), pt);
         if(pt.day != g_pyrLastDay) { g_pyrLastDay = pt.day; g_pyrToday = 0; }
         if(g_pyrToday < T_PyramidMaxDay)
         {
            double pyrTierLots = 0.0;
            int pyrTier = ConfluenceTier(trK, dir, pyrTierLots);
            // (v18.35) THE REAL REASON STACKING NEVER HAPPENS.
            // ConfluenceTier() returns 2 or 3 only on a narrow multi-timeframe
            // agreement, and 1 on the common M5-strong case. So "pyrTier >= 2"
            // failed on almost every add opportunity even after v18.22 raised the
            // per-position limit and v18.33 lowered the R trigger. Three separate
            // changes to a mechanism whose actual gate was this line.
            // Under T_TakeEveryPine an add only needs the leg to still be extending
            // (checked above) and the trade's own clock to agree (checked below) --
            // which is what "stack into a running trend" means. Tier 3 still gets the
            // larger T_PyramidT3Lots, so quality still decides SIZE.
            // ═══ (v18.53) STACK ON THE PULLBACK, NOT AFTER THE CANDLE ═══
            // CLASSIFICATION: STRATEGY CHANGE, on his report: "we keep stacking at a
            // peak or after a massive candle when a reversal has come."
            // AtLegExtreme() above only asks "is price at least T_AddNoTopPts (2.00)
            // from the 30-bar extreme". On M1 with ATR ~1.0 that is two ATR, so a
            // single 3-4 point candle carries price far enough past the extreme to
            // SATISFY the guard on the very bar that ended the move. The guard tests
            // DISTANCE from the extreme and has no idea whether that distance was
            // created by a healthy pullback or by the reversal itself.
            // TWO CONDITIONS ADDED, both cheap:
            //  1. NOT ON AN OVERSIZED BAR. If the last closed bar is >= T_AddMaxBarAtr
            //     ATR, that is the reversal candle, not a pullback. Wait one bar.
            //  2. PULLBACK TOWARD THE DEMA, NOT AWAY FROM IT. A real pullback moves
            //     price back toward the mean; a reversal keeps going through it. So
            //     price must still be on the trade's own side of the DEMA. If we are
            //     long and price has already crossed below the DEMA, the leg is not
            //     pulling back, it is over -- and adding there is buying the top.
            // This is his own "stacking needs to occur before the big move not after":
            // the add now happens in the quiet retrace between legs, which is the only
            // place a stack has a leg in front of it.
            // HONEST COST: adds become rarer, and a genuinely strong trend that pulls
            // back only shallowly (never far enough off the extreme, never touching
            // the DEMA side test) may now get no add at all. Given adds have barely
            // fired at 0.01 forced size anyway, the downside is small and the upside
            // is not adding into reversals. OFF-SWITCH: T_AddPullbackOnly = false.
            double addBarR = (Buf(g_scAtr[trK],0,1) > 0.0)
                           ? (iHigh(_Symbol, trTf, 1) - iLow(_Symbol, trTf, 1)) / Buf(g_scAtr[trK],0,1)
                           : 0.0;
            double addDema = Buf(g_scDema[trK], 0, 1);
            double addPx   = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                       : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
            bool addOnSide = (addDema <= 0.0) || ((dir > 0) ? (addPx > addDema) : (addPx < addDema));
            bool addPullOk = (!T_AddPullbackOnly)
                          || (addBarR < T_AddMaxBarAtr && addOnSide);
            if(!addPullOk && InpVerboseLog)
               PrintFormat("[NO ADD] %s add held — last bar %.1f ATR (limit %.1f) or price %.2f "
                           "is the wrong side of the DEMA %.2f. Stacking waits for the pullback, "
                           "not the candle that ended the leg.",
                           dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", addBarR, T_AddMaxBarAtr, addPx, addDema);
            // (v18.64) the add path is where the 21:29 stack grew, because
            // AtLegExtreme only asks about distance from the extreme and nothing
            // counted total exposure. Ceiling applied here as well as at the
            // fresh-entry gate.
            if(pyrTier >= (T_TakeEveryPine ? 1 : 2) && addPullOk
               && !DirExposureFull(dir, (pyrTier == 3) ? T_PyramidT3Lots : T_PyramidLots)
               && !AtLegExtreme(trTf, dir, (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                                                    : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)))
            {
               double stP; int stPD; ScalpST(trK, stP, stPD);
               double atrP2 = Buf(g_scAtr[trK],0,1);
               if(stPD == dir && stP > 0 && atrP2 > 0)
               {
                  double slAdd = (dir > 0) ? stP - T_SlNoiseBuf*atrP2 : stP + T_SlNoiseBuf*atrP2;
                  g_forceLots = (pyrTier == 3) ? T_PyramidT3Lots : T_PyramidLots;
                  string whyP = StringFormat("PYRAMID top-up on %s: original at +%.1fR (risk-free), tier %d stack still agrees — adding %.2f",
                                             EnumToString(trTf), profitR, pyrTier, g_forceLots);
                  g_lastEntryK = trK;   // (fix #55) top-up fires on this same timeframe
                  bool okP = Open(e, dir, slAdd, 0.0, whyP, "TREND-PYR");
                  PositionSelectByTicket(tk);   // Open() changed the selected-position context — restore it
                  g_forceLots = 0.0;
                  g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0;
                  if(okP)
                  {
                     MarkPyramidDone(tk);
                     PyramidNote(tk);   // (v18.22) count this add so several are allowed per position
                     g_pyrToday++;
                  }
               }
            }
         }
      }

      // BUG FIX #53 (SEVERE): the flip-close below used to live INSIDE
      // "if(T_TrailOnST) { ... }". T_TrailOnST defaults to true so this was
      // invisible on stock settings, but the moment anyone turned the
      // Supertrend-trail toggle off — thinking it only disabled the trailing
      // stop — the EA silently stopped closing TREND positions on the
      // opposite Supertrend flip at all. That is the ONE exit the entire
      // spec calls unconditional ("ride to opposite signal" is the floor,
      // always). Pine's strategy.entry() reverses on the flip no matter what
      // any input is set to; the EA must match that. The Supertrend read
      // itself is now unconditional; only the WIDENING-ROOM TRAIL below it
      // stays gated behind T_TrailOnST, exactly as that input's name promises.
      double stV; int stD; ScalpST(trK, stV, stD);
      if(T_TrailOnST && profitR >= T_Tp1R && stV > 0)
      {
         double buf = SpikeBufferTF(trTf, atr);
         // WIDENING ROOM: a trend running at 5R+ pulls back harder than one
         // just past breakeven — same principle as APEX's widening trail.
         // This adds extra breathing room on TOP of the spike buffer, scaled
         // by how far into profit the trade already is, so a genuinely huge
         // trend doesn't get clipped by a stop sized for a small one. It only
         // ever ratchets forward with the Supertrend line — never gives back
         // ground already locked in.
         double roomMult = (profitR >= 5.0) ? 0.9 : (profitR >= 2.5) ? 0.5 : 0.0;
         double room = roomMult * atr;
         double floorSt = (dir > 0) ? stV - buf - room : stV + buf + room;
         bool valid = (dir > 0) ? (floorSt < px) : (floorSt > px);
         if(valid) newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, floorSt) : MathMin(newSL, floorSt);
      }
      // BUG FIX #41: this used to be gated behind profitR >= T_Tp1R, same
      // block as the trail above. That meant a Supertrend flip AGAINST the
      // trade before 1.5R was reached did NOT close the position — it just
      // sat there on the original stop. Worse: because PosOf(e)!=0 blocks
      // a new entry and DirectionAllowed() blocks the opposite direction,
      // the new opposite signal could not fire either. The EA was not
      // actually "riding to the opposite signal" the way the Pine spec
      // describes — only the Pine backtest reverses automatically
      // (strategy.entry does that internally); Open() here never did.
      // The flip-close now fires at ANY profit level, matching the locked
      // philosophy: the opposite signal is always the floor, the moment
      // it happens. The trail/breakeven above still only start at 1.5R —
      // this only fixes the exit-on-reversal, not the profit-taking.
      // (v17.51) THE 1-SECOND CLOSE. This flip-close had no age gate, no
      // holdGrace and no profit gate — and T_MicroBreakout (default TRUE)
      // opens trades DELIBERATELY against the confirmed Supertrend; its own
      // log line says "while Supertrend is still %s". So a micro-breakout BUY
      // is opened while stD == -1, and on the very next tick this block reads
      // stD == -1, calls it a flip against the trade, and closes it. Entry and
      // exit inside one second, nowhere near the stop, for the cost of the
      // spread. FIX: a flip against only means something if the Supertrend was
      // ever WITH the trade. StAgreed() latches that per ticket; until it is
      // true this path stands down for the min-hold window, and the hard stop
      // plus every other exit still apply.
      bool stFlipAgainst = ((dir > 0 && stD == -1) || (dir < 0 && stD == 1));
      bool stEverAgreed  = StAgreed(tk, dir, stD);
      // (v17.90) THE LAST UNGATED EXIT, and the one his own live trades convict.
      // Evidence: buy 0.08 4263.755 -> 4263.987 closed for +0.23 POINTS, and sell
      // 0.04 4260.199 -> 4260.921 cut at -0.72. Neither is reachable by the banks
      // (0.60R floor ~1.8pts), by BE (1.0R ~3pts), or by the ratchet (0.9xATR
      // standoff). Only this flip-close can fire at any profit whatsoever -- its
      // sole condition is that the Supertrend once agreed. On M1 the Supertrend
      // flips repeatedly inside normal chop, so it was closing live trades at
      // trivial profit while every other guard we built stood down politely.
      // That is the mechanical cause of ~2pt winners against ~2pt losers.
      // FIX: a bare flip is no longer sufficient on its own. To close, EITHER
      // the trade has real profit to protect (>= T_FlipCloseMinR), OR a SECOND
      // independent read must confirm the turn (price lost the DEMA side, or
      // structure broke). One indicator changing its mind is not a reversal.
      // The hard stop, gap close and Friday flatten are untouched, so downside
      // is unchanged -- this only stops the EA volunteering out of live trends.
      // (v17.96 BUG FIX -- my own inversion from v17.90, caught by his 12:29 trade
      // that closed at +0.36 points inside its first bar.)
      // WRONG: `|| !demaHold`. demaHold is false whenever the DEMA SLOPE has not yet
      // developed, which is the normal state of a brand-new trade -- it does NOT mean
      // the DEMA turned against us. So "confirmation" was satisfied by the ABSENCE of
      // a positive read rather than by any adverse evidence, and the flip-close fired
      // at any profit on any young trade.
      // ALSO WRONG: the stand-down required posAgeSecs >= T_MinHoldSecs, so the
      // youngest trades -- the ones needing protection most -- were the only ones it
      // did not protect. Inverted.
      // NOW: confirmation requires REAL adverse evidence -- price actually on the
      // wrong side of the DEMA, or structure genuinely broken -- or enough profit to
      // be worth protecting. And the stand-down applies at ANY age.
      double flipDema = (trK >= 0 && trK < SC_TFN) ? DemaValSc(trK) : 0.0;
      bool demaAgainst = (flipDema > 0.0) && ((dir > 0) ? (px < flipDema) : (px > flipDema));
      // (v18.06) HOLD QUORUM. This was ANY ONE of three votes, so a single
      // structure wobble closed a live trend trade at almost no profit -- his
      // "our normal trades are never caught fully / it didn't hold". It now takes
      // T_FlipCloseVotes agreeing votes. 1 = v18.05 behaviour.
      // ════ (v18.23) "WHAT PUSHES PAST THE SMALL REVERSAL AND WHAT DOESN'T" ════
      // EVIDENCE: his 4417 SELL closed at ~4416 on a counter-flip (the blue arrow),
      // then price fell to 4408.8. Roughly 8 points of the leg left on the table
      // because a single M1 counter-flip was treated as a reversal when it was a
      // pullback inside a live downtrend.
      //
      // BUG 1 — BEING IN PROFIT WAS A VOTE TO CLOSE.
      //   if(profitR >= T_FlipCloseMinR) flipVotes++;
      // Profit is not evidence the thesis is dead. This line meant a WINNING trade
      // needed only one more weak vote to be closed on a counter-flip, while a
      // losing one was protected. It had the logic exactly backwards, and it is the
      // single line most responsible for "we never hold". Profit now only ENABLES
      // the test -- it is no longer a reason to act.
      //
      // BUG 2 — NO DISTINCTION BETWEEN A PULLBACK AND A REVERSAL.
      // The discriminator was already in the file, unused for this purpose: the
      // DEMA. At 4416 price was still BELOW the DEMA, i.e. the downtrend the sell
      // was riding had not been broken -- a counter-flip there is a pullback. A
      // counter-flip that also carries price back ACROSS the DEMA is a reversal.
      // T_FlipCloseNeedDema requires that crossing before a counter-flip may close
      // a trade that is in profit. Losing trades are unaffected (they still close
      // on the old rules) and the hard stop is never touched.
      // OFF-SWITCHES: T_FlipCloseNeedDema = false, T_FlipProfitIsVote = true.
      int flipVotes = 0;
      if(T_FlipProfitIsVote && profitR >= T_FlipCloseMinR) flipVotes++;
      // (v18.40) only count the DEMA vote if being against the DEMA is NEW. A trade
      // opened counter-DEMA was against it at birth, so this is not evidence of a
      // reversal -- it is the reason the trade exists.
      if(demaAgainst && !BornAgainstDema(tk)) flipVotes++;
      else if(demaAgainst && GateSayOnce(trK >= 0 ? trK : 0, dir, 0, trTf))
         PrintFormat("[TREND] DEMA VOTE IGNORED: #%I64u was opened counter-DEMA, so being against the DEMA "
                     "now is not new information. It is not counted as a reason to close.", tk);
      if(!structIntact)              flipVotes++;
      // ══════ (v18.30) AUDIT CORRECTION — I MADE FLIP-CLOSE HARDER FOR LOSERS ══════
      // HIS TICKET: SELL 0.05 at ~4379.3, price rallied to 4381.0, an opposite BUY
      // flip printed, and the sell was neither closed nor was the buy taken.
      // MY BUG: v18.23 set T_FlipProfitIsVote = false to stop profit being a reason
      // to close a WINNER. But the vote was removed GLOBALLY, so a LOSING trade lost
      // a vote too -- it now needed both remaining votes (demaAgainst AND broken
      // structure) to act on an opposite flip. One of them alone was no longer
      // enough, so the sell sat there while the reversal ran.
      // An opposite Supertrend flip against a trade that is ALREADY LOSING is the
      // clearest reversal evidence this system produces. It should not need a
      // committee. T_FlipCloseVotesLosing = 1: one vote closes it.
      // Winners keep the strict 2-vote + DEMA-cross test from v18.23.
      int flipNeed = (profitR < 0.0) ? T_FlipCloseVotesLosing : T_FlipCloseVotes;
      bool flipConfirmed = (flipVotes >= flipNeed);
      if(flipConfirmed && T_FlipCloseNeedDema && profitR > 0.0 && !demaAgainst)
      {
         flipConfirmed = false;
         if(GateSayOnce(trK >= 0 ? trK : 0, dir, 6, trTf))
            PrintFormat("[TREND] PULLBACK, NOT REVERSAL: Supertrend flipped against this %s at %+.2fR, "
                        "but price is still on our side of the DEMA — the trend that opened this trade "
                        "is intact. HOLDING through the reversal. Stop is still live.",
                        dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", profitR);
      }
      if(stFlipAgainst && !flipConfirmed)
      {
         if(GateSayOnce(trK >= 0 ? trK : 0, dir, 5, trTf))
            PrintFormat("[TREND] FLIP-CLOSE HELD: Supertrend flipped but nothing confirms it "
                        "(%+.2fR, DEMA side %s, structure %s). One indicator turning is not a reversal.",
                        profitR, demaAgainst ? "AGAINST us" : "not against us", structIntact ? "intact" : "broken");
      }
      else if(stFlipAgainst && !stEverAgreed && posAgeSecs < (long)T_MinHoldSecs)
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[TREND] flip-close STOOD DOWN: Supertrend has never agreed with this %s (counter-trend birth entry, %ds old). Nothing flipped — there was no agreement to flip from.",
                        dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", (int)posAgeSecs);
      }
      else if(stFlipAgainst)
      {
         // A genuine flip means the market has already told us the
         // direction changed — that is not noise to wait out, it is the
         // exact opposite of a scratch. Fast-rearm THIS TIMEFRAME so the
         // new signal can fire on the very next bar instead of sitting
         // through the normal 3-bar cooldown — and only this timeframe;
         // fix #45 keeps M1/M3/M5 cooldowns independent of each other.
         g_trFastRearm[trK] = true;
         g_trLastExit[trK]  = TimeCurrent();
         CloseTagged("FLIP-CLOSE", tk); return;
      }

      // TRAILING TP: extend the target while momentum still agrees.
      // BUG FIX #66: same per-timeframe bug class as #49/#54 — this read
      // hMacd[e]/hAdx[e], the FIXED M5 engine handles, even for a TREND
      // position actually running on M1 or M3, so the "momentum still
      // agrees" read extending the target was on the wrong clock for two
      // of three timeframes. Now reads the position's own handles.
      if(T_DynamicTP && tp > 0)
      {
         double mm = Buf(g_scMacd[trK],0,1), ms = Buf(g_scMacd[trK],1,1);
         double adx2 = Buf(g_scAdx[trK],0,1), adxP = Buf(g_scAdx[trK],0,3);
         bool agrees = (dir > 0) ? (mm > ms && adx2 > adxP) : (mm < ms && adx2 > adxP);
         if(agrees && profitR >= T_Tp2R)
         {
            double ext = (dir > 0) ? entry + (T_Tp3R + 1.5) * R : entry - (T_Tp3R + 1.5) * R;
            bool better = (dir > 0) ? (ext > tp) : (ext < tp);
            if(better) tp = ext;
         }
      }
   }




   //---- E7 SCALP: OUT BEFORE THE TABLES TURN ----
   // This is the entire reason the engine exists. A scalp does not sit through a
   // reversal waiting for its stop — it takes the profit that is on the table and
   // leaves. Three exits, whichever comes first:
   //    1. Target (2R)
   //    2. MOMENTUM FADE — the push is over. Take what is there.
   //    3. Stop (rare, because #2 usually fires first)
   //
   // The fade exit is what turns "small loss" into "small profit" across hundreds
   // of trades. It is worth more than any entry filter.
   else if(e == E_SCALP)
   {
      int k = ScalpTFOf(tk);   // THIS position's own timeframe, not the last one opened
      if(k < 0 || k >= SC_TFN) k = SC_TFN - 1;   // fall back to the slowest of the 3 (M5), same intent as before
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES tf = g_scTF[k];

      // IMPROVEMENT 8: BANK HALF AT 1R.
      // You want a high win rate. This is the single most direct way to get one:
      // take half off at 1R and move the rest to breakeven. That half is now a
      // WIN, banked, and cannot be given back. The remaining half rides to 2R for
      // free — worst case it scratches at breakeven, so the trade can no longer
      // be a loser. It costs you a little on the biggest winners and it converts a
      // large number of would-be scratches and small losses into small wins.
      // ══════════ v18.73 (S) THE PARTIAL ARMED ABOVE MOST TRADES' REACH ══════════
      // profitR >= 1.0 means 1.0R, and R on his tape is the birth stop = ~3.08 pts.
      // Measured peak distribution from every ticket in this conversation:
      //   17.00, 13.79, 7.03, 6.80, 5.16, 3.40, 2.74, 2.69, 2.21, 1.49, 1.41,
      //   1.39, 1.36, ~1.0, ~0.5  -- median ~2.7 pts.
      // So the partial armed ABOVE the median trade. It is the §4 shape yet again:
      // a threshold set above the quantity it is measured against. His words:
      // "some make 2 quid some 6 some 40 we need to just claim that peak."
      // A partial is the ONLY mechanism that banks money with zero giveback -- a
      // trailing stop must by construction give back the noise band before it
      // triggers. So this is the mechanism for the 2-quid trades, and the ratchet
      // (O) is the mechanism for the 40-quid ones. They are different jobs.
      // ARM LEVEL IS DERIVED, not chosen: the noise band (spread + 0.60*ATR), the
      // same quantity every lock in this build now uses. It is the smallest move
      // that is provably not noise, so it is the earliest honest moment to bank.
      //   band ~2.05 pts -> reached by ~9 of the 15 measured peaks above, vs ~5 at 1.0R.
      // It banks a THIRD (StepBank), so the 17pt runner keeps two thirds riding.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_PartialAtBand = false (reverts to the 1.0R arm exactly).
      double bankArmR = 1.0;
      if(T_PartialAtBand && R > 0.0)
      {
         double bandBk = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
         if(bandBk > 0.0) bankArmR = MathMin(1.0, bandBk / R);
      }
      if(SC_BankAt1R && profitR >= bankArmR && (!AlreadyBooked(tk) || T_StepPartials))
      {
         double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
         // (v18.05) step partial, not half.
         double half = StepBank(tk, vol, MathFloor((vol / 2.0) / g_lotStep) * g_lotStep);
         if(half >= g_lotMin && (vol - half) >= g_lotMin)
         {
            if(trade.PositionClosePartial(tk, half))
            {
               MarkBooked(tk);
               PrintFormat("[SCALP] +1R. Banked half (%.2f lots). The rest rides free from breakeven.", half);
            }
         }
      }

      // ══════════════ v18.73 (L) BUG FIX — SCALP HAD NO SUB-BAND SCRATCH ══════════════
      // TREND's micro-lock reverts a peak too small to clear the noise band to
      // breakeven+spread (the v17.98 clause). That whole block lives inside
      // `if(e == E_TREND)` at line 13485, so SCALP never reached it. SCALP's only
      // rung was the bare `entry` below, armed at 0.7R ~= 2.2 pts, which his chop
      // trades do not reach -- so they took the full flip loss instead of scratching:
      //   20 Aug tickets: -1.38 x3, -1.55, -2.10 x2, -2.19, -2.32 x2, -2.74 x3.
      //   All BELOW R (~3.2), i.e. flip-closes on trades that had been green.
      // Arm level is NOT invented: it is nfSc, the same spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr*ATR
      // the TREND path already uses to decide what counts as more than noise. A peak
      // that clears the band has proved something; below it, nothing is claimable
      // because 60% of a sub-band peak is inside the round-trip spread.
      // This can only move the stop to entry+spread -- it never sets a profit target
      // and never closes a position, so a trade that dips and then runs still runs.
      // Its cost is the trades that dip through breakeven before continuing.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_ScalpScratch = false.
      double nfSc = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
      if(T_ScalpScratch && nfSc > 0.0 && R > 0.0)
      {
         double pkSc = PeakR(tk) * R;
         // (v18.73 Q) T_ScalpSubBandArmPts > 0 lowers the arm below the band. 0 = off,
         // in which case this is exactly the sub-band rule TREND already had.
         double armSc = (T_ScalpSubBandArmPts > 0.0) ? MathMin(nfSc, T_ScalpSubBandArmPts) : nfSc;
         if(pkSc >= armSc)
         {
            double scLvl = (dir > 0) ? entry + SpreadUSD() : entry - SpreadUSD();
            newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, scLvl) : MathMin(newSL, scLvl);
         }
      }
      // (v18.73) was a bare `entry`, which banks a scratch and then pays the spread
      // out of it -- the same missing-spread shape TREND's micro-lock already had
      // fixed. Neighbouring path, guard absent.
      // ══════════ v18.73 (O) BUG FIX — SCALP HAD NO PEAK RATCHET AT ALL ══════════
      // MY OWN DEFECT, and it is the §4 shape I had written down one session earlier.
      // I identified that the peak ratchet sits inside `if(e == E_TREND)` (line 13485),
      // then patched that same ratchet with (K) and told him it would fix his peak
      // capture. His fills are SCALP. (K) never reached them.
      // HIS TAPE PROVED IT, 21 Aug 16:54:52: sell 4584.20, low 4577.17 = 7.03 pts
      // available, closed at exactly +4.00.
      //     with a band-capped ratchet:  7.03 - 1.9 = +5.13
      //     hard lock staircase only:    6.0 + floor(1.03/2.0)*2.0 - 2.0 = +4.00
      // It closed at +4.00, so nothing but the hard lock was in the path.
      // Every SCALP stop-raise above anchors to `entry` or to CURRENT price. Not one
      // anchored to the trade's own PEAK, so a SCALP trade could not bank a share of
      // what it actually reached.
      // Same nfSc the (L) scratch above uses, same MathMax-of-two-floors shape as (K),
      // so on a small peak the proportional floor still wins and chop is unchanged.
      // Uses RatchetFracFor(tf) -- this clock's own fraction, not a new number.
      // OFF-SWITCH: T_ScalpRatchet = false.
      if(T_ScalpRatchet && T_PeakRatchet && R > 0.0 && nfSc > 0.0)
      {
         double pkRt = PeakR(tk) * R;
         if(pkRt > SpreadUSD())
         {
            double fRt = RatchetFracFor(tf);
            if(fRt > 0.95) fRt = 0.95;
            double lkRt = MathMax(SpreadUSD(), fRt * pkRt);
            // loosen if the floor would sit inside the band (the v17.98 rule)
            if((pkRt - lkRt) < nfSc) lkRt = MathMax(SpreadUSD(), pkRt - nfSc);
            // (K) tighten if the giveback EXCEEDS the band -- the other side
            if(T_RatchetCapGiveback && (pkRt - lkRt) > nfSc)
               lkRt = MathMax(lkRt, pkRt - nfSc);
            if(lkRt > SpreadUSD())
            {
               double rtLvl = (dir > 0) ? entry + lkRt : entry - lkRt;
               newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, rtLvl) : MathMin(newSL, rtLvl);
            }
         }
      }

      // (v18.76) SWITCHED, NOT CHANGED. See T_ScalpBeAt07R.
      // (v18.77 CORRECTION) THIS WHOLE BRANCH IS UNREACHABLE. InpUseScalp = false,
      // and the only Open() call in the file is Open(E_TREND, ...), so
      // ManagePosition() is never invoked with e == E_SCALP. Everything between
      // the `else if(e == E_SCALP)` and its closing brace is dead code, including
      // the v18.73 (O) peak ratchet and the (U) flip-DEMA guard. Left in place
      // rather than deleted: if InpUseScalp is ever turned on it all comes alive
      // at once, and deleting 270 lines is a bigger risk than labelling them.
      if(T_ScalpBeAt07R && profitR >= 0.7)
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, entry + SpreadUSD()) : MathMin(newSL, entry - SpreadUSD());
      if(profitR >= 1.2)
      {
         double sm, tm; AdaptMults(E_TREND, sm, tm);
         double tr = (dir > 0) ? px - 1.2*atr*sm : px + 1.2*atr*sm;
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, MathMax(entry, tr)) : MathMin(newSL, MathMin(entry, tr));
      }

      // BUG FIX #43 / IMPROVEMENT: M1 MICRO-CHOP EARLY EXIT.
      // On M1, price sometimes accumulates in a tight up-down-up-down cluster —
      // a few small pushes each way with no real follow-through. A Supertrend
      // flip out of that cluster is barely a flip; riding it to the OPPOSITE
      // signal (the normal exit) means waiting through the same chop again,
      // giving back whatever small profit was on the table for a trade that
      // was never going to be the big one anyway. The normal fade-exit below
      // already does this in principle, but it requires SC_FadeMinR (0.5R)
      // before it can act — too high a bar for a trade that only ever had a
      // small amount on the table to begin with. This fires ONLY on M1, at a
      // much lower bar, so a genuine micro-scalp can bank what little it has
      // and stand down instead of sitting through another round of chop.
      // BUG FIX (live-testing correction, same as the TREND version above):
      // required the trade to have actually been open at least 6 bars
      // before this can fire, so it can't misread pre-entry price action
      // (the setup that led to the flip) as post-entry chop.
      datetime openTimeSc = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
      long barsHeldSc = (TimeCurrent() - openTimeSc) / MathMax(PeriodSeconds(PERIOD_M1), 1);   // (v18.69 F) guarded
      if(tf == PERIOD_M1 && barsHeldSc >= 6 && profitR >= 0.15 && profitR < SC_FadeMinR)
      {
         double c1M = iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,1), o1M = iOpen(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,1);
         double c2M = iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,2), o2M = iOpen(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,2);
         // two counter-direction candles in the last two closed M1 bars = the
         // cluster is turning back on itself, exactly the chop pattern described.
         bool counter1 = (dir > 0) ? (c1M < o1M) : (c1M > o1M);
         bool counter2 = (dir > 0) ? (c2M < o2M) : (c2M > o2M);
         // CHOP SIGNATURE, not just a pullback: a genuine early push still nets
         // real distance even through one counter candle. Chop instead travels
         // back and forth and goes nowhere — net movement over the last 6 bars
         // is small relative to the total range covered getting there. Requiring
         // both this AND the counter-candle read stops a single normal pause
         // inside a real push from tripping this — it only fires on actual
         // accumulation, not a healthy trade taking a breath.
         double hi6 = iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,SafeHighest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,MODE_HIGH,6,1));
         double lo6 = iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,SafeLowest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,MODE_LOW,6,1));
         double rangeTravelled = hi6 - lo6;
         double netMove = MathAbs(iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,1) - iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M1,6));
         bool choppy = rangeTravelled > 0 && (netMove / rangeTravelled) < 0.45;
         if(counter1 && counter2 && choppy)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SCALP] M1 MICRO-CHOP at +%.2fR — small win, unlikely to run. Banking it, not riding this one.", profitR);
            CloseTagged("M1-MICROCHOP", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      // Supertrend flipped = the reason for the trade is gone. Leave. Now.
      double sv; int sd; ScalpST(k, sv, sd);
      if((dir > 0 && sd == -1) || (dir < 0 && sd == 1))
      {
         // ══════ v18.73 (U) THE BIGGEST SINGLE LEAK IN THIS FILE ══════
         // TREND requires T_FlipCloseVotes (2) AND a DEMA cross before a counter-flip
         // may close a WINNER, on the stated principle "one indicator turning is not
         // a reversal". SCALP closed a winner on a BARE flip, unconditionally. §4:
         // the mechanism exists on one path and is absent on its neighbour.
         // THE COMMENT ABOVE THIS BLOCK CLAIMED A REPLACEMENT THAT DOES NOT EXIST --
         // it says what holds a winner is "the giveback-capped peak trail, note (I)",
         // but (I) was WITHDRAWN in v18.71 as redundant. So since v18.71 nothing at
         // all has been holding a winning scalp through a pullback.
         // HIS TICKET, 21 Aug 17:22:57: buy 4590.86, closed 4595.88 (+5.02) on a
         // counter-flip printed at ~4596.5 mid-rally. The rally then ran to 4602.89.
         //   available  4590.86 -> 4602.89 = 12.03 pts
         //   captured   5.02 pts = 42%
         //   left on the table at 0.05 lots = GBP 25.69 on ONE trade
         // THIS IS NOT THE v18.70 (H) MISTAKE. That used an M5 confirmation, and he
         // correctly rejected it because M5 is a lagging aggregate of M1 and delays
         // every exit. This reads the DEMA on THE TRADE'S OWN CLOCK -- zero timeframe
         // lag -- and it is the same reader (DemaValSc) the ratchet's hold vote uses.
         // ASYMMETRIC ON PURPOSE, exactly as TREND is: a flip against a LOSER still
         // closes instantly. Only a WINNER has to show that price actually crossed
         // the DEMA before a single flip is allowed to end it.
         // OFF-SWITCH: T_ScalpFlipNeedDema = false (restores the bare flip exactly).
         bool holdPullback = false;
         if(T_ScalpFlipNeedDema && profitR > 0.0)
         {
            double dmSc = DemaValSc(k);
            if(dmSc > 0.0)
            {
               double bidNow = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
               double askNow = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
               // still on the trade's side of the DEMA = pullback, not reversal
               bool demaAgainstSc = (dir > 0) ? (bidNow < dmSc) : (askNow > dmSc);
               if(!demaAgainstSc)
               {
                  holdPullback = true;
                  if(GateSayOnce(k, dir, 7, tf))
                     PrintFormat("[SCALP] PULLBACK, NOT REVERSAL: Supertrend flipped against this %s "
                                 "at %+.2fR but price is still on its own side of the %s DEMA (%.2f). "
                                 "Holding. The peak ratchet (O) still governs the floor, so this "
                                 "cannot give back more than the noise band.",
                                 dir > 0 ? "BUY" : "SELL", profitR, EnumToString(tf), dmSc);
               }
            }
         }
         if(!holdPullback)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SCALP] Supertrend flipped on %s at %.1fR. Out.", EnumToString(tf), profitR);
            CloseTagged("SCALP-ST-FLIP", tk);
            return;
         }
      }

      if(SC_FadeExit && profitR >= SC_FadeMinR)
      {
         double adxN = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,1), adxP = Buf(g_scAdx[k],0,3);
         double e21N = Buf(g_scE21[k],0,1);
         double c1   = iClose(_Symbol,tf,1), o1 = iOpen(_Symbol,tf,1);
         double c2   = iClose(_Symbol,tf,2);

         // three independent signs the push is finished
         bool adxRolling = (adxN < adxP - 1.0);                            // trend strength decaying
         bool lostEma    = (dir > 0) ? (c1 < e21N) : (c1 > e21N);          // gave up the EMA21
         bool counterBar = (dir > 0) ? (c1 < o1 && c1 < c2) : (c1 > o1 && c1 > c2);  // it turned

         int fading = (adxRolling?1:0) + (lostEma?1:0) + (counterBar?1:0);
         // ═══ IMPROVEMENT #6 — the fade needs price to actually turn ═══
         // adxRolling and lostEma are both lagging momentum reads that can
         // flash while price is STILL making new highs in a strong push —
         // firing the fade exit on a trade that hasn't actually turned yet
         // (part of the "closes too early" pattern). Require a real counter
         // candle (price genuinely rejecting) as a NECESSARY condition, not
         // just one of three interchangeable votes. So the exit still needs
         // two signs, but one of them must be price itself turning — never
         // two lagging indicators alone while the move is still going.
         if(fading >= 2 && counterBar)
         {
            PrintFormat("[SCALP] MOMENTUM FADE on %s at +%.1fR. %s%s%s Taking it before it turns.",
                        EnumToString(tf), profitR,
                        adxRolling?"ADX rolling over. ":"",
                        lostEma?"Lost the EMA21. ":"",
                        counterBar?"Counter candle. ":"");
            CloseTagged("MOM-FADE", tk);
            return;
         }
      }
   }


   //---- E6 APEX: NO TAKE PROFIT. A trail that WIDENS as profit grows. ----
   else if(e == E_APEX)
   {
      double openR = ((dir > 0) ? (px - entry) : (entry - px)) / MathMax(R, _Point);

      // THE TRAIL WIDENS. Wider, not tighter. This is the whole thesis: a
      // tightening trail is how you get shaken out of the trade of the year.
      double tM = (openR >= 5.0) ? A_TrailLate : (openR >= 2.0) ? A_TrailMid : A_TrailEarly;

      if(openR >= A_BeAtR)
      {
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, entry) : MathMin(newSL, entry);
         int look = 10;
         double anchor = (dir > 0)
            ? iHigh(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,SafeHighest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,MODE_HIGH,look,1))
            : iLow(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,SafeLowest(_Symbol,PERIOD_M15,MODE_LOW,look,1));
         double chand = (dir > 0) ? anchor - tM*atr : anchor + tM*atr;
         newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, chand) : MathMin(newSL, chand);
      }

      // Supertrend as a second floor
      double sv; int sd; Supertrend(e, sv, sd);
      if(sv > 0 && openR >= 1.0)
      {
         double buf = SpikeBuffer(e);
         double floorSv = (dir > 0) ? sv - buf : sv + buf;
         bool valid = (dir > 0) ? (floorSv < px) : (floorSv > px);
         if(valid) newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, floorSv) : MathMin(newSL, floorSv);
      }

      // Exit on the DEATH of the trend, not on a price level.
      int h = ApexHealth(dir);
      bool stAgainst = (dir > 0 && sd == -1) || (dir < 0 && sd == 1);
      if(stAgainst || h < A_HealthMin)
      {
         // BUG FIX #17: this closed the ONE position it was looking at. On a
         // hedging account the pyramid adds are separate positions — they would
         // have been left open, riding a trend the engine had already declared
         // dead. Close the whole book for this engine.
         PrintFormat("[APEX] CLOSE ALL at %.1fR. %s (health %d). Trend is done.",
                     openR, stAgainst ? "Supertrend flipped" : "health collapsed", h);
         for(int q = PositionsTotal()-1; q >= 0; q--)
         {
            ulong qt = PositionGetTicket(q);
            if(qt==0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(qt)) continue;
            if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
            if(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC) == Cfg[E_APEX].magic) CloseTagged("APEX-HEALTH", qt);
         }
         g_apexAdds = 0;
         return;
      }
      tp = 0;   // there is no take profit here and there never will be
   }


   //---- apply the current stop/target FIRST, before any force-close checks ----
   // BUG FIX #42: stall-exit and max-hold used to run BEFORE this block, so a
   // trade that stalled on the same tick the trail had just tightened would
   // close at raw market price — the tighter stop computed above never got
   // committed first. Committing it here, before the force-close checks below,
   // means the position is always carrying its best-known protection at the
   // moment any close decision is made — never worse than what was earned.
   //
   // BUG FIX #10 (SEVERE): if a PositionModify was rejected (too close to price,
   // requote, market closed) the old code just returned and tried again next
   // tick — which is fine. But if the position had NO stop at all (broker
   // stripped it, or the original order filled without one), nothing here would
   // ever put one on. A stopless gold position is how an account dies in one
   // candle. This guarantees a stop exists, always.
   if(sl == 0)
   {
      double emergency = (dir > 0) ? px - 2.0 * atr : px + 2.0 * atr;
      double t0 = tp;
      ClampStops(dir, px, emergency, t0);
      if(ModifySLTP(tk, emergency, t0, "emergency stop attach"))
         PrintFormat("[%s] EMERGENCY STOP attached — this position had none.", Cfg[e].name);
      return;
   }

   // ══════════ (v18.57) THE HARD PROFIT LOCK — HIS OWN IDEA, AND THE ══════════
   // ══════════ ONLY THING IN THIS FILE THAT CANNOT BE VETOED    ══════════
   // HIS WORDS: "maybe when we go into 10 pound profit we move our sl into 10 pound
   // profit so its secured yk" -- and he had to close two trades by hand because
   // nothing else would.
   // WHY THIS SUCCEEDS WHERE FIVE PREVIOUS FIXES ONLY HELPED: every other exit in
   // this EA goes through CloseTagged(), and CloseTagged() can be vetoed by
   // T_MinWinnerExitPts, by T_ExitMinR, by the arbiter, and the giveback can be
   // vetoed by holdGrace. I have now had to exempt GIVEBACK (18.49), FLIP-CLOSE
   // (18.51), RANGE-SCALP (18.54) and the hold votes (18.56) one at a time, because
   // the same entry-anchored protections keep blocking peak-anchored instruments.
   // A BROKER-SIDE STOP LOSS IS NOT AN EXIT PATH. It sits at the broker. No guard,
   // vote, floor, grace or arbiter in these 15,000 lines can reach it. So once the
   // profit is locked here, it is locked -- and that is exactly what "big profit
   // isnt being locked" needed.
   // THE LADDER, in points, at his 0.02 (1 pt ~ GBP 1.48):
   //   profit >= T_HardLockArmPts (6.0, ~GBP 9)  -> stop moves to lock 6.0 - 2.0 = 4.0
   //   then every T_HardLockStepPts (2.0) further -> the lock follows, 2.0 behind
   // So at +12 pts the stop guarantees +10; at +20 it guarantees +18. It ONLY ever
   // ratchets toward profit -- MathMax/MathMin against both the existing stop and the
   // computed newSL -- so it can never widen risk and can never fight the trail.
   // WHY 2.0 PTS OF GIVE AND NOT 0: a stop 0.2 pts behind price is scratched by the
   // next tick, which is the T_MicroLockR mistake at a bigger number. 2.0 pts is two
   // M1 ATR, outside the noise, and it still guarantees two thirds of a 6 pt peak.
   // WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it does not cap the upside. The trade still runs to the
   // 8 pt target, the giveback, or a flip. This only removes the outcome where a
   // trade that showed real profit closes at or below break-even.
   // HONEST COST: a trade that reaches +6 and then retraces 2 pts before continuing
   // to +15 is now stopped at +4 instead of riding. That is a real cost and it is the
   // reason the give is 2.0 and the arm is 6.0 rather than tighter -- a 2 pt pullback
   // after a 6 pt run is not routine noise on M1, it is most of a leg's worth.
   // If the log shows [HARD LOCK] stops being hit ahead of continuations, raise
   // T_HardLockGivePts to 3.0 before touching anything else.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_HardLockOn = false. Nothing else in this build depends on it.
   if(T_HardLockOn && T_HardLockArmPts > 0.0)
   {
      double lockPx  = (dir > 0) ? bid : ask;
      double gainPts = (dir > 0) ? (lockPx - entry) : (entry - lockPx);
      if(gainPts >= PS(T_HardLockArmPts))
      {
         // how many whole steps past the arm point we are
         double steps   = MathFloor((gainPts - PS(T_HardLockArmPts)) / MathMax(PS(T_HardLockStepPts), 0.1));
         double lockAt  = PS(T_HardLockArmPts) + steps * PS(T_HardLockStepPts) - PS(T_HardLockGivePts);
         // ══════════ v18.73 (P) BUG FIX — THE STAIRCASE GIVES BACK A FLAT 2 PTS ══════════
         // With Arm=6.0, Step=2.0, Give=2.0 this returns EXACTLY +4.00 for every gain
         // from 6.00 to 7.99, because steps=0 across that whole range. Confirmed six
         // times on his tape: 20 Aug three winners at +4.00, 20 Aug 18:18:43 at +4.00,
         // 21 Aug 16:54:52 two fills at +4.00 off a 7.03 pt peak.
         // Same principle as (K) and (O): the noise band is the required breathing
         // room, so giving back more than the band is a donation. This takes whichever
         // floor is HIGHER, so the staircase still governs whenever it is the tighter
         // of the two and the mechanism can never end up looser than it is today.
         //   gain 7.03, band 1.9  -> staircase 4.00, band floor 5.13  -> 5.13  (+1.13)
         //   gain 13.79, band 3.04 -> staircase 10.00, band floor 10.75 -> 10.75 (+0.75)
         // OFF-SWITCH: T_HardLockBandCap = false (reverts to the pure staircase).
         if(T_HardLockBandCap)
         {
            double nfHl = NoiseFloorPts(atr);
            if(nfHl > 0.0 && (gainPts - lockAt) > nfHl)
               lockAt = MathMax(lockAt, gainPts - nfHl);
         }
         if(lockAt > 0.0)
         {
            double lockSL = (dir > 0) ? entry + lockAt : entry - lockAt;
            double before = newSL;
            newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, lockSL) : MathMin(newSL, lockSL);
            if(sl > 0.0)   // never loosen a stop that is already tighter
               newSL = (dir > 0) ? MathMax(newSL, sl) : MathMin(newSL, sl);
            if(MathAbs(newSL - before) > _Point)
               PrintFormat("[%s] HARD LOCK — #%I64u is +%.2f pts, stop moved to guarantee +%.2f pts "
                           "(was %.2f, now %.2f). A broker stop cannot be vetoed by any hold vote, "
                           "floor or arbiter, so this profit is now banked whatever happens next.",
                           Cfg[e].name, tk, gainPts, lockAt, before, newSL);
         }
      }
   }

   if(MathAbs(newSL - sl) > _Point || MathAbs(tp - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_TP)) > _Point)
   {
      ClampStops(dir, px, newSL, tp);
      bool skipModify = false;
      if(dir > 0 && (bid - newSL) < g_minStop) skipModify = true;
      if(dir < 0 && (newSL - ask) < g_minStop) skipModify = true;
      if(!skipModify) ModifySLTP(tk, newSL, tp, "main trail/ratchet");   // (v17.33) now retries + always logs on failure
   }

   //---- universal: stall + max hold. APEX is EXEMPT - it is supposed to ride. ----
   ENUM_TIMEFRAMES stallTf = (e == E_TREND) ? g_scTF[TrendTFOf(tk)] : Cfg[e].tf;
   if(e != E_APEX && Stalled(tk, (e==E_TREND)?T_Stall:8, stallTf))
   {
      if(!CloseTagged("STALL", tk))
         PrintFormat("[%s] STALL CLOSE REJECTED: %d %s — retrying next tick.", Cfg[e].name,
                     trade.ResultRetcode(), trade.ResultRetcodeDescription());
      else if(InpVerboseLog)
         PrintFormat("[%s] stalled out — no new extreme, cutting it.", Cfg[e].name);
      return;
   }

   datetime ot = (datetime)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
   if(e != E_APEX && (TimeCurrent() - ot) > (long)Cfg[e].maxHold * PeriodSeconds(stallTf))
   {
      // (v18.17) per-clock horizon in minutes, replacing the bars-based number.
      // The trade's own clock. Cfg[e].tf is the engine's configured clock; that is
      // the right source here because MAX-HOLD is evaluated inside the engine
      // loop for engine e.
      ENUM_TIMEFRAMES hTf = Cfg[e].tf;
      int    holdMins = (hTf == PERIOD_M1) ? T_HoldMinsM1
                      : (hTf == PERIOD_M5) ? T_HoldMinsM5
                      : (hTf == PERIOD_M3) ? T_HoldMinsM1
                      : T_HoldMinsM15;
      long   ageSecs  = (long)TimeCurrent() - (long)PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TIME);
      if(ageSecs < (long)holdMins * 60)
      {
         if(InpVerboseLog)
            PrintFormat("[%s] MAX-HOLD deferred — %d min old, the %s horizon is %d min. Still holding.",
                        Cfg[e].name, (int)(ageSecs / 60), EnumToString(hTf), holdMins);
         return;
      }
      CloseTagged("MAX-HOLD", tk);
      if(InpVerboseLog) PrintFormat("[%s] max hold — %d min on %s", Cfg[e].name, (int)(ageSecs/60), EnumToString(hTf));
      return;
   }
}

//================================================================
//  MANUAL TRADE MONITOR
//  Works out WHICH of our engines' conditions were true when you
//  opened it, tells you what setup you actually took, and (option)
//  manages it with that engine's rules.
//================================================================
string InferSetup(int dir)
{
   // BUG FIX #47: this used to read ONLY the M5 Supertrend (via the old
   // single-timeframe Supertrend(E_TREND,...) call), even though TREND now
   // trades M1/M3/M5 independently. A manual trade agreeing with M1 and M3
   // but not the slower M5 was being called "counter-trend, lowest-
   // expectancy" — actively wrong, since it agreed with two of the three
   // timeframes the EA itself acts on. Now checks all three and reports
   // which ones actually agree, instead of treating M5 as the only truth.
   double stV1; int stD1; ScalpST(0, stV1, stD1);   // M1
   double stV3; int stD3; ScalpST(1, stV3, stD3);   // M3
   double stV5; int stD5; ScalpST(2, stV5, stD5);   // M5
   int agree = (dir>0?1:-1);
   int agreeCount = ((stD1==agree)?1:0) + ((stD3==agree)?1:0) + ((stD5==agree)?1:0);
   string tfAgree = StringFormat("M1 %s / M3 %s / M5 %s",
       stD1==1?"UP":"DOWN", stD3==1?"UP":"DOWN", stD5==1?"UP":"DOWN");

   double adx = Buf(hAdx[E_TREND],0,1);
   double rsi = Buf(hRsi[E_TREND],0,1);
   double e21 = Buf(hEma21[E_TREND],0,1);
   double e200= Buf(hEma200[E_TREND],0,1);
   double atr = Buf(hAtr[E_TREND],0,1);
   double c   = iClose(_Symbol,PERIOD_M5,1);
   CalcVWAP();
   double dev = (g_vwapSd > 0) ? (c - g_vwap) / g_vwapSd : 0;
   double rv  = RelVol(PERIOD_M5,1);

   string s = "";
   // "With trend" now means agreeing with the MAJORITY of the three
   // timeframes (2 of 3 or better), not just M5 alone.
   bool withTrend = agreeCount >= 2;

   if(withTrend && adx >= 18)
      s = StringFormat("TREND-style %s (%s). ADX %.1f. Manage it like E0: trail the Supertrend, no fixed target — ride until the opposite signal.",
          dir>0?"long":"short", tfAgree, adx);
   else if(MathAbs(dev) >= 2.0 && adx < 20)
      s = StringFormat("VWAP-FADE style. Price %.1f SD from VWAP with ADX %.1f (no trend). Target should be VWAP %.2f — do NOT trail this.",
          dev, adx, g_vwap);
   else if((dir > 0 && rsi < 32) || (dir < 0 && rsi > 68))
      s = StringFormat("EXHAUSTION-style %s. RSI %.0f, volume %.1fx. Fixed target ~2R, no trail.",
          dir>0?"long":"short", rsi, rv);
   else if(!withTrend)
      s = StringFormat("COUNTER-TREND %s (%s — only %d/3 timeframes agree), ADX %.1f. This is the lowest-expectancy thing you can do. Keep the stop tight.",
          dir>0?"long":"short", tfAgree, agreeCount, adx);
   else
      s = StringFormat("UNCLASSIFIED. %s, ADX %.1f, RSI %.0f, %.1f SD from VWAP.",
          tfAgree, adx, rsi, dev);
   return s;
}

void MonitorManual()
{
   if(!InpWatchManual) return;

   for(int i = PositionsTotal()-1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(tk)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      long m = PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC);
      if(EngineOf(m) >= 0) continue;          // one of ours

      int    dir   = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE)==POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      double entry = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double sl    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SL);
      double tp    = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_TP);
      double bid   = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_BID);
      double ask   = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol,SYMBOL_ASK);
      double px    = (dir>0) ? bid : ask;
      double atr   = Buf(hAtr[E_TREND],0,1);

      // classify once
      if(ReasonFor(tk) == "")
      {
         string why = InferSetup(dir);
         StoreReason(tk, "MANUAL: " + why);
         PrintFormat("=== YOUR TRADE #%I64u %s @ %.2f ===", tk, dir>0?"BUY":"SELL", entry);
         PrintFormat("    %s", why);
         if(sl == 0) Print("    WARNING: no stop loss on this position.");
         if(sl != 0 && tp != 0)
         {
            double rr = MathAbs(tp-entry) / MathMax(MathAbs(entry-sl), _Point);
            PrintFormat("    R:R 1:%.2f. Break-even win rate needed: %.0f%%.", rr, 100.0/(1.0+rr));
            if(rr < 1.5) Print("    That R:R needs a >40% win rate. Widen the target or tighten the stop.");
         }
      }

      if(!InpManageManual || atr <= 0) continue;

      // EMERGENCY STOP FOR NAKED MANUAL TRADES — a manual position with no SL
      // used to get only a logged warning and zero actual protection: it could
      // run to any loss untouched. If manual management is on and there's no
      // stop, attach a protective one at a sensible ATR distance so the trade
      // is never left completely naked. After this, the breakeven/trail logic
      // below takes over normally.
      if(sl == 0)
      {
         double emgSL = (dir > 0) ? entry - InpManualEmgAtr * atr : entry + InpManualEmgAtr * atr;
         double t0 = tp;
         ClampStops(dir, px, emgSL, t0);
         if(ModifySLTP(tk, emgSL, t0, "manual naked-position emergency stop"))
         {
            PrintFormat("[MANUAL] Attached emergency stop at %.2f (%.1f x ATR) to your naked %s — it had no protection.",
                        emgSL, InpManualEmgAtr, dir>0?"BUY":"SELL");
            sl = emgSL;   // continue into the trail logic below with the new stop
         }
         else continue;   // couldn't attach even after retries — try again next tick
      }

      // BUG FIX #20: TrackProgress() has 200 slots and RECYCLES the oldest when
      // full. Manual/foreign positions were being written into the same array as
      // the engines' — so a busy manual session could evict an engine's ticket,
      // and that engine's stall-exit would silently stop working on a live trade.
      // Manual positions get their own trail logic below; they do not need the
      // engine stall tracker, so they no longer pollute it.

      // protect it: breakeven, then trail
      if(sl != 0)
      {
         double R = MathAbs(entry - sl);
         double pR = ((dir>0)?(px-entry):(entry-px)) / MathMax(R, _Point);
         double newSL = sl;
         if(pR >= 1.0) newSL = (dir>0) ? MathMax(newSL, entry) : MathMin(newSL, entry);
         if(pR >= 1.5)
         {
            double tr = (dir>0) ? px - InpManualTrailAtr*atr : px + InpManualTrailAtr*atr;
            newSL = (dir>0) ? MathMax(newSL, tr) : MathMin(newSL, tr);
         }
         if(MathAbs(newSL - sl) > _Point)
         {
            ClampStops(dir, px, newSL, tp);
            if(dir>0 && (bid-newSL) < g_minStop) continue;
            if(dir<0 && (newSL-ask) < g_minStop) continue;
            ModifySLTP(tk, newSL, tp, "manual trail");   // (v17.33) result was previously discarded entirely
         }
      }
   }
}

//================================================================
//  OPTIONAL: TRADINGVIEW SIGNAL FILE
//  Set up a webhook -> file bridge and drop lines into
//  MQL5/Files/pine_signals.csv. MUST match the Pine alert() format exactly:
//     BUY,4102.50,4099.80,15,24.3,72
//     SIDE, ENTRY, SIGNAL-LINE STOP, SIGNAL TF, ADX, CONFIDENCE
//  No TP field — the Pine has none by design (ride to the opposite signal).
//  A timestamp is NOT sent by the Pine; this bridge stamps arrival time itself
//  and uses that for the staleness check below.
//================================================================
datetime g_lastPine = 0;

void ReadPineSignals()
{
   if(!InpReadPineFile) return;
   if(TimeCurrent() - g_lastPine < 10) return;
   g_lastPine = TimeCurrent();

   int fh = FileOpen(InpPineFileName, FILE_READ|FILE_CSV|FILE_ANSI|FILE_SHARE_READ);
   if(fh == INVALID_HANDLE) return;

   static datetime seen = 0;
   datetime newest = seen;

   while(!FileIsEnding(fh))
   {
      // (v15) Now up to eight fields, matching the Pine's alert() line:
      //   SIDE, ENTRY, SIGNAL-LINE STOP, TF, ADX, CONF, TAG, TIER
      //   TAG  = NORMAL | REENTRY   (REENTRY = close-and-reenter reclaim)
      //   TIER = 0 | 2 | 3          (Pine's big-move flag; maps to size here)
      // TAG and TIER are OPTIONAL — a v14-format 6-field line still parses.
      string side = FileReadString(fh);
      string ent  = FileReadString(fh);
      string slS  = FileReadString(fh);
      string tfS  = FileReadString(fh);
      string adxS = FileReadString(fh);
      string confS= FileReadString(fh);
      string tagS = FileIsEnding(fh) ? "NORMAL" : FileReadString(fh);
      string tierS= FileIsEnding(fh) ? "0"      : FileReadString(fh);

      // The Pine does not send a timestamp, so arrival time IS the timestamp —
      // read once per poll (the 10s gate above), so a line can only be acted on
      // within one poll window of appearing. That is the staleness control.
      datetime t = TimeCurrent();
      if(t <= seen) continue;
      newest = MathMax(newest, t);

      int dir = (StringFind(side, "BUY") >= 0) ? 1 : -1;
      double sl = StringToDouble(slS);
      double tp = 0.0;   // the Pine never sends a TP — ride to the opposite signal, by design
      double tvEntry = StringToDouble(ent);
      int pineTier   = (int)StringToInteger(tierS);
      bool isReentry = (StringFind(tagS, "REENTRY") >= 0);
      // A signal needs a real entry and a real stop. TP is intentionally NOT
      // required — requiring tp>0 here would silently reject every valid
      // ride-to-flip signal, which is the entire point of the strategy.
      if(sl <= 0 || tvEntry <= 0) continue;

      // BUG FIX #33: a TradingView signal is generated on a bar close, travels
      // through a webhook, hits a file, and gets read up to 10 seconds later.
      // Gold can move $3 in that window. The old code took TV's SL and TP and
      // fired at the CURRENT market price — so you could inherit a stop that was
      // now on the wrong side, or a target already behind you.
      // Open() now rejects a wrong-sided stop (fix #31), but we should also
      // refuse a signal whose price has already moved past usefulness.
      double pxNow = (dir > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK)
                               : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
      double atrNow = Buf(hAtr[E_TREND], 0, 1);
      if(tvEntry > 0 && atrNow > 0 && MathAbs(pxNow - tvEntry) > 0.5 * atrNow)
      {
         PrintFormat("[TV] SKIP: signal price %.2f, market is now %.2f (%.1f ATR away). Chasing it is not the trade.",
                     tvEntry, pxNow, MathAbs(pxNow - tvEntry) / atrNow);
         continue;
      }

      // (v12) If Pine tagged this a tier setup, feed that tier into LotFor via
      // the same tier state variables the native TREND path uses — the size
      // ends up bigger for a tier 2/3 setup, same rules, same ceiling checks.
      if(InpUseConfluenceTiers && pineTier >= 2)
      {
         double pineLots = (pineTier == 3) ? TierMaxLots
                        : (pineTier == 2) ? TierConfidentLots : TierBaseLots;
         // gate tier 3 behind proven expectancy, same as native
         if(pineTier == 3)
         {
            bool proven = (Stat[E_TREND].trades >= TierGateMinN &&
                           (Stat[E_TREND].netR / MathMax(Stat[E_TREND].trades, 1)) >= TierGateExpR);
            if(!proven) pineLots = TierConfidentLots;
         }
         g_lastTier = pineTier;
         g_lastTierSizeReq = pineLots;
      }

      // Route it through the TREND engine's risk model.
      if(EngineReady(E_TREND) && PosOf(E_TREND) == 0)
      {
         bool opened = Open(E_TREND, dir, sl, tp,
             StringFormat("TRADINGVIEW: %s%s%s TF %s, ADX %s, conf %s",
                          side,
                          isReentry ? " REENTRY" : "",
                          pineTier >= 2 ? StringFormat(" TIER %d", pineTier) : "",
                          tfS, adxS, confS));
         if(opened && g_lastTier >= 2) g_tierToday++;
      }
      g_lastTier = 0; g_lastTierSizeReq = 0.0; g_sizeReqIsExact = false; g_lastConfMult = 1.0;
   }
   FileClose(fh);
   seen = newest;
}

//================================================================
//  MAIN
//================================================================
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//|  DAILY STATS PANEL  (v17.97, open item 1)                        |
//|                                                                  |
//|  Why this lives in MT5 and not Pine: Pine can only count trades  |
//|  inside the loaded bar window. The EA sees every deal the broker  |
//|  ever wrote.                                                     |
//|                                                                  |
//|  DESIGN RULES THIS OBEYS                                         |
//|  1. Aggregates per DEAL_POSITION_ID. A 1R partial plus its       |
//|     runner is ONE trade, not two. Volume-weighted average entry  |
//|     and exit, so pyramids and multi-deal fills collapse          |
//|     correctly. This is the metric he specified.                  |
//|  2. Reads HISTORY, not the EA's own counters. It shares no state  |
//|     with Stat[], g_bookedTk[] or the other registries, so it     |
//|     cannot inherit a booking bug — and it disagrees loudly if    |
//|     one exists.                                                  |
//|  3. Recomputed from scratch every refresh, never accumulated.    |
//|     Idempotent: double-counting is structurally impossible.      |
//|  4. "Points" here means PRICE UNITS (USD/oz), the same unit as   |
//|     T_LegNoisePts, SpreadUSD() and PeakR()*R everywhere else in  |
//|     this file. NOT MT5 _Point.                                   |
//|                                                                  |
//|  The MFE block is the one part that must be sampled live (peak   |
//|  excursion is not in deal history). It uses its OWN table, not    |
//|  PeakR(), because PeakR is R-based and ClearPeakR() wipes it on   |
//|  close — exactly the value the panel needs after the close.      |
//|  Slots free themselves on close and the table NEVER blind-wraps. |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
input group "=== (v17.97) DAILY STATS PANEL ==="
input bool   InpStatsPanel     = true;   // draw the daily panel
input double InpPanelRefLot    = 0.03;   // flat lot used for the money column
// (v19.00) 1 -> 4. The panel rebuilt its objects once a second for twelve hours.
// Four seconds is still live to the eye and is the single biggest heat saving here.
input int    InpPanelRefreshS  = 4;      // (v18.73 M) 2 -> 1. TimeCurrent() is integer seconds, so a gap of 2 meant the box could lag ~2s on top of not being live at all.
input bool   InpPanelAllMagics = false;  // true = include manual/other-EA trades
// (v18.76) 0.0 -> 2.00. Asked for five times, never set. DISPLAY ONLY -- this
// value is read by the panel and by nothing that places or manages an order.
// It makes the panel's "green X/Y" row read: how many trades ever reached +2.0
// points. That single ratio settles the stop-distance question with arithmetic.
input double InpPanelGreenPts  = 2.00;   // "went green" threshold, 0 = use live spread
input int    InpPanelX         = 12;     // panel x
input int    InpPanelY         = 22;     // panel y
input bool   InpStatsSinceAttach = true;  // (v18.07) panel counts from when the EA was attached, not from midnight
input int    InpPanelFont      = 9;      // font size
input color  InpPanelInk       = clrGainsboro;
input double InpTarget1        = 50.0;   // target ladder, account currency at the flat lot
input double InpTarget2        = 100.0;
input double InpTarget3        = 150.0;
input double InpTarget4        = 200.0;
input int    InpNetFontBoost   = 11;     // (v18.07) how much larger the big NET readout is than the panel text
input double InpPanelGoalPts   = 20.0;   // daily target in NET POINTS, 0 = off
input double InpPanelGoalCcy   = 0.0;    // daily target in account currency at the flat lot, 0 = off
input color  InpPanelBg        = C'20,24,32';

#define QSP_PFX      "QSP_"
#define QSP_ROWS     34
#define QSP_FOLD_MAX 512
#define QSP_POS_MAX  512

// ── folded peaks: kept AFTER the position closes so the panel can pair
//    realised points against peak-available points for the same id ──
ulong  g_qspFoldId[QSP_FOLD_MAX];
double g_qspFoldPk[QSP_FOLD_MAX];
int    g_qspFoldN  = 0;
datetime g_qspFoldDay = 0;
bool   g_qspWarnLive = false, g_qspWarnFold = false;
int    g_qspRowY = 0;

datetime QSP_Midnight()
{
   MqlDateTime t; TimeToStruct(TimeCurrent(), t);
   t.hour = 0; t.min = 0; t.sec = 0;
   return StructToTime(t);
}

double QSP_GreenThresh()
{
   if(InpPanelGreenPts > 0) return InpPanelGreenPts;
   double s = SpreadUSD();
   return (s > 0) ? s : 0.05;
}

void QSP_Fold(ulong pid, double peak)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_qspFoldN; i++)
      if(g_qspFoldId[i] == pid) { if(peak > g_qspFoldPk[i]) g_qspFoldPk[i] = peak; return; }
   if(g_qspFoldN >= QSP_FOLD_MAX)
   {
      // no blind wrap. Drop it, say so once, and let the panel show the shortfall
      // in the tracked count rather than corrupting an existing record.
      if(!g_qspWarnFold)
      { Print("QSP: fold table full — MFE stats now under-count. Restart the EA to clear."); g_qspWarnFold = true; }
      return;
   }
   g_qspFoldId[g_qspFoldN] = pid;
   g_qspFoldPk[g_qspFoldN] = peak;
   g_qspFoldN++;
}

double QSP_FoldPeak(ulong pid, bool &found)
{
   for(int i = 0; i < g_qspFoldN; i++)
      if(g_qspFoldId[i] == pid) { found = true; return g_qspFoldPk[i]; }
   found = false;
   return 0;
}

// Sampled every tick. Tracks peak favourable excursion in points for every open
// position, then folds the peak into the day table the moment it closes.
void QSP_TrackLive()
{
   double bid = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID);
   double ask = SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
   if(bid <= 0 || ask <= 0) return;

   char seen[QSP_LIVE_MAX];
   ArrayInitialize(seen, 0);

   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong tk = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(tk == 0) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(!InpPanelAllMagics && EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;

      int    dir = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
      double op  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN);
      double px  = (dir > 0) ? bid : ask;          // the price we could exit AT
      double pts = (px - op) * dir;

      int s = -1;
      for(int j = 0; j < QSP_LIVE_MAX; j++)
         if(g_qspDir[j] != 0 && g_qspTk[j] == tk) { s = j; break; }
      if(s < 0)
      {
         for(int j = 0; j < QSP_LIVE_MAX; j++)
            if(g_qspDir[j] == 0) { s = j; break; }
         if(s < 0)
         {
            if(!g_qspWarnLive)
            { Print("QSP: live MFE table full (128 open positions) — peak untracked."); g_qspWarnLive = true; }
            continue;
         }
         g_qspTk[s] = tk; g_qspDir[s] = dir; g_qspPeak[s] = pts;
      }
      if(pts > g_qspPeak[s]) g_qspPeak[s] = pts;
      seen[s] = 1;
   }

   for(int j = 0; j < QSP_LIVE_MAX; j++)
   {
      if(g_qspDir[j] == 0 || seen[j] == 1) continue;
      if(PositionSelectByTicket(g_qspTk[j])) continue;   // still open, just not in the loop this pass
      QSP_Fold(g_qspTk[j], g_qspPeak[j]);
      g_qspTk[j] = 0; g_qspPeak[j] = 0; g_qspDir[j] = 0;  // slot freed on close
   }
}

// (v18.00) ALL-TIME, since the first deal on the account. Same per-POSITION_ID
// points method as the daily block, but no day filter and no MFE. Rescanned once
// a minute, not every refresh -- a full history scan is not a 2-second job.
datetime g_qspAttach = 0;   // (v18.03) set in PanelInit -- "since attached" means since
                            // the EA went on this chart, not since the account opened. The
                            // 19-trade count he saw was the account's whole history.
double   g_atPts = 0, g_atCash = 0;
int      g_atN = 0;
datetime g_atStamp = 0;

void QSP_AllTime()
{
   if(g_atStamp > 0 && TimeCurrent() - g_atStamp < 60) return;
   g_atStamp = TimeCurrent();
   if(!HistorySelect(g_qspAttach, TimeCurrent() + 3600)) return;   // (v18.03) since ATTACH, not account start

   static ulong  apid[4096];
   static double avIn[4096], apIn[4096], avOut[4096], apOut[4096], acash[4096];
   static int    adir[4096];
   static char   aodd[4096];
   int n = 0, total = HistoryDealsTotal();

   for(int i = 0; i < total; i++)
   {
      ulong d = HistoryDealGetTicket(i);
      if(d == 0) continue;
      if(HistoryDealGetString(d, DEAL_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      long dt = HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_TYPE);
      if(dt != DEAL_TYPE_BUY && dt != DEAL_TYPE_SELL) continue;
      if(!InpPanelAllMagics && EngineOf(HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      ulong id = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
      if(id == 0) continue;

      int s2 = -1;
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) if(apid[j] == id) { s2 = j; break; }
      if(s2 < 0)
      {
         if(n >= 4096) break;                  // no wrap: stop and report what we have
         s2 = n; n++;
         apid[s2] = id; avIn[s2] = 0; apIn[s2] = 0; avOut[s2] = 0;
         apOut[s2] = 0; acash[s2] = 0; adir[s2] = 0; aodd[s2] = 0;
      }
      double vol = HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_VOLUME);
      double prc = HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_PRICE);
      long   en  = HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_ENTRY);
      acash[s2] += HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_PROFIT)
                 + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_SWAP)
                 + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_COMMISSION);
      if(en == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
      {
         if(adir[s2] == 0) adir[s2] = (dt == DEAL_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         avIn[s2] += vol; apIn[s2] += prc * vol;
      }
      else if(en == DEAL_ENTRY_OUT || en == DEAL_ENTRY_OUT_BY)
      { avOut[s2] += vol; apOut[s2] += prc * vol; }
      else aodd[s2] = 1;
   }

   double pts = 0, cash = 0; int cnt = 0;
   for(int j = 0; j < n; j++)
   {
      if(avIn[j] <= 0 || avOut[j] <= 0) continue;       // still open
      cash += acash[j]; cnt++;
      if(aodd[j] == 1 || avOut[j] > avIn[j] + 0.0000001) continue;
      pts += (apOut[j] / avOut[j] - apIn[j] / avIn[j]) * adir[j];
   }
   g_atPts = pts; g_atCash = cash; g_atN = cnt;
}

void QSP_Row(int idx, string txt, color ink)
{
   if(idx < 0 || idx >= QSP_ROWS) return;
   string nm = QSP_PFX + "R" + IntegerToString(idx);
   // (v18.00) FROM HIS SCREENSHOT: ten rows reading "Label". An MT5 OBJ_LABEL with
   // an empty OBJPROP_TEXT falls back to displaying its type name, so blanking the
   // spare rows printed "Label" ten times. Spare rows are now DELETED, not blanked.
   if(txt == "")
   {
      if(ObjectFind(0, nm) >= 0) ObjectDelete(0, nm);
      return;
   }
   if(ObjectFind(0, nm) < 0)
   {
      ObjectCreate(0, nm, OBJ_LABEL, 0, 0, 0);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_CORNER, CORNER_LEFT_UPPER);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_XDISTANCE, InpPanelX + 9);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_YDISTANCE, InpPanelY + 7 + idx * g_qspRowY);
      ObjectSetString (0, nm, OBJPROP_FONT, "Consolas");
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_FONTSIZE, InpPanelFont);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_SELECTABLE, false);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_HIDDEN, true);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_BACK, false);
   }
   ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_COLOR, ink);
   ObjectSetString (0, nm, OBJPROP_TEXT, txt);
}

string QSP_Sgn(double v, int dp)
{
   return (v >= 0 ? "+" : "") + DoubleToString(v, dp);
}

// (v18.07) His ask: "net needs to be net of when I apply to the chart -- a new
// version means I've made updates and therefore want a fresh record." The panel
// counted from midnight, so the previous build's trades polluted the new build's
// record. Now the window starts at ATTACH when InpStatsSinceAttach is true, so
// dropping a new .ex5 on the chart zeroes the scoreboard.
void QSP_Recompute()
{
   datetime d0 = QSP_Midnight();
   if(InpStatsSinceAttach && g_qspAttach > d0) d0 = g_qspAttach;
   if(d0 != g_qspFoldDay) { g_qspFoldDay = d0; g_qspFoldN = 0; g_qspWarnFold = false; }

   // 7-day lookback so a position opened yesterday still has its ENTRY deals
   // available for the volume-weighted average. Anything older is flagged, not
   // silently mispriced.
   if(!HistorySelect(d0 - 7 * 86400, TimeCurrent() + 3600)) return;

   static ulong    pid[QSP_POS_MAX];
   static double   vIn[QSP_POS_MAX], pIn[QSP_POS_MAX], vOut[QSP_POS_MAX], pOut[QSP_POS_MAX], cash[QSP_POS_MAX];
   static int      dirP[QSP_POS_MAX];
   static datetime tOut[QSP_POS_MAX];
   static char     odd[QSP_POS_MAX];
   int n = 0;

   int total = HistoryDealsTotal();
   for(int i = 0; i < total; i++)
   {
      ulong d = HistoryDealGetTicket(i);
      if(d == 0) continue;
      if(HistoryDealGetString(d, DEAL_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      long dt = HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_TYPE);
      if(dt != DEAL_TYPE_BUY && dt != DEAL_TYPE_SELL) continue;   // skip balance/credit rows
      if(!InpPanelAllMagics && EngineOf(HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;

      ulong id = (ulong)HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_POSITION_ID);
      if(id == 0) continue;

      int s = -1;
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) if(pid[j] == id) { s = j; break; }
      if(s < 0)
      {
         if(n >= QSP_POS_MAX) continue;
         s = n; n++;
         pid[s] = id; vIn[s] = 0; pIn[s] = 0; vOut[s] = 0; pOut[s] = 0;
         cash[s] = 0; dirP[s] = 0; tOut[s] = 0; odd[s] = 0;
      }

      double vol = HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_VOLUME);
      double prc = HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_PRICE);
      long   en  = HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_ENTRY);

      cash[s] += HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_PROFIT)
               + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_SWAP)
               + HistoryDealGetDouble(d, DEAL_COMMISSION);

      if(en == DEAL_ENTRY_IN)
      {
         if(dirP[s] == 0) dirP[s] = (dt == DEAL_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
         vIn[s] += vol; pIn[s] += prc * vol;
      }
      else if(en == DEAL_ENTRY_OUT || en == DEAL_ENTRY_OUT_BY)
      {
         vOut[s] += vol; pOut[s] += prc * vol;
         datetime dtm = (datetime)HistoryDealGetInteger(d, DEAL_TIME);
         if(dtm > tOut[s]) tOut[s] = dtm;
      }
      else odd[s] = 1;   // DEAL_ENTRY_INOUT — a netting reversal. Cannot be priced
                         // as one clean position; counted and excluded, never guessed.
   }

   int    closed = 0, wins = 0, losses = 0, flats = 0, excl = 0;
   double won = 0, lost = 0, wPts = 0, wVol = 0, realCash = 0;
   int    mfeN = 0, green = 0;
   double mfeSum = 0, netTracked = 0;
   int    openN = 0;
   double openPts = 0, openCash = 0;
   double gT = QSP_GreenThresh();

   for(int s = 0; s < n; s++)
   {
      bool stillOpen = PositionSelectByTicket(pid[s]);
      if(stillOpen)
      {
         if(vIn[s] > 0)
         {
            openN++;
            openCash += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
            int    dr = (PositionGetInteger(POSITION_TYPE) == POSITION_TYPE_BUY) ? 1 : -1;
            double px = (dr > 0) ? SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_BID)
                                 : SymbolInfoDouble(_Symbol, SYMBOL_ASK);
            openPts += (px - PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PRICE_OPEN)) * dr;
         }
         continue;
      }
      if(tOut[s] < d0) continue;                 // closed on an earlier day
      if(vOut[s] <= 0 || vIn[s] <= 0) continue;  // nothing to price

      if(odd[s] == 1 || vOut[s] > vIn[s] + 0.0000001)
      { excl++; closed++; realCash += cash[s]; continue; }   // entry deals older than
                                                            // the lookback, or a reversal

      closed++;
      realCash += cash[s];
      double pts = (pOut[s] / vOut[s] - pIn[s] / vIn[s]) * dirP[s];

      if(pts > 0)      { wins++;   won  += pts; }
      else if(pts < 0) { losses++; lost += pts; }
      else               flats++;

      wPts += pts * vOut[s];
      wVol += vOut[s];

      bool haveMfe = false;
      double pk = QSP_FoldPeak(pid[s], haveMfe);
      if(haveMfe)
      {
         mfeN++; mfeSum += pk; netTracked += pts;
         if(pk >= gT) green++;
      }
   }

   double net     = won + lost;
   double lotAvg  = (wVol > 0) ? wPts / wVol : 0;
   double flatCcy = net * LossPerLot(1.0) * InpPanelRefLot;
   double winPct  = (wins + losses > 0) ? 100.0 * wins / (wins + losses) : 0;
   double capPct  = (mfeSum > 0)        ? 100.0 * netTracked / mfeSum    : 0;
   double grnPct  = (mfeN > 0)          ? 100.0 * green / mfeN           : 0;
   string ccy     = AccountInfoString(ACCOUNT_CURRENCY);

   color okC = C'120,220,140', badC = C'235,120,120', dimC = C'130,140,155';
   color netC = (net > 0) ? okC : (net < 0 ? badC : InpPanelInk);

   int r = 0;
   QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("QUAD %s  %s  since %s", EA_BUILD, _Symbol,
           TimeToString(d0, InpStatsSinceAttach ? (TIME_DATE|TIME_MINUTES) : TIME_DATE)), InpPanelInk);
   // (v18.03) stripped on his ask. Points-won / points-lost / lot-wtd / net@0.03
   // were four ways of saying what NET POINTS and REALISED already say.
   QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("%d trades  %d-%d  %.0f%% win", closed, wins, losses, winPct),
           (winPct >= 50 ? okC : badC));
   // (v18.73 M) SAME BUG AS THE CORNER BOX, ITS NEIGHBOUR. I patched QSP_NetCorner
   // and left this row -- the one actually inside the panel he reads -- still built
   // from closed positions only. His report: "the quad profit box is not updating
   // neither is net". Two boxes, one fix applied, the neighbour missed. §4 again.
   QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("NET  %s pts   %s %s",
                             QSP_Sgn(T_NetBoxLive ? net + openPts : net, 2),
                             QSP_Sgn(T_NetBoxLive ? realCash + openCash : realCash, 2), ccy), netC);
   // ══════════ (v18.91 AQ) LEDGER + MISSED POINTS ══════════
   // Deliberately drawn directly under NET so the two are read together. LEDGER
   // is counted as each trade closes; NET is rebuilt from history every refresh.
   // If these two disagree, or one moves and the other does not, that is the
   // answer to "the net box does not load" -- and it is on screen, not inferred.
   if(T_DayLedger)
   {
      // ══════ (v19.00) PLAIN ENGLISH. He said the panel was unreadable, so every
      // row below is a full sentence in pounds or points, no abbreviations, no
      // percentages he has to decode, no jargon. ══════
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("TODAY   %s %s from %d closed trades",
                                QSP_Sgn(g_ldgCash, 2), ccy, g_ldgN),
              (g_ldgCash > 0 ? okC : (g_ldgCash < 0 ? badC : InpPanelInk)));
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("GOLD MOVED %.0f pts today. WE TOOK %.0f pts.",
                                g_travelPts, g_ldgPts),
              (g_ldgPts > 0 ? okC : (g_ldgPts < 0 ? badC : dimC)));
      QSP_Row(r++, g_bskArmed
                   ? StringFormat("OPEN TRADES BEST %s %s — %s IS NOW SAFE",
                                  QSP_Sgn(g_bskPeak, 2), ccy, QSP_Sgn(g_bskFloor, 2))
                   : StringFormat("OPEN TRADES BEST %s %s — nothing locked yet (needs %s)",
                                  QSP_Sgn(g_bskPeak, 2), ccy, QSP_Sgn(T_BasketArmCash, 2)),
              (g_bskArmed ? okC : dimC));
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("OPEN RIGHT NOW %s %s (best today %s)",
                                QSP_Sgn(g_ldgBaskNow, 2), ccy, QSP_Sgn(g_ldgBaskPk, 2)),
              (g_ldgBaskNow > 0 ? okC : (g_ldgBaskNow < 0 ? badC : dimC)));
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("BEST MOMENT OF EVERY TRADE ADDED UP: %s %s",
                                QSP_Sgn(g_ldgPeakCash, 2), ccy),
              dimC);
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("SO WE HANDED BACK %s %s on %d trades",
                                QSP_Sgn(g_ldgMissCash, 2), ccy, g_ldgMissN),
              (g_ldgMissCash > 0 ? badC : dimC));
      // (v19.07 BW) the distribution, so no future session has to guess at it.
      // (v19.09 BY) day quality, in one line. If this says CHOP, expect nothing.
      double erP   = EfficiencyRatio(PERIOD_M1, T_EfficiencyLen);
      double rngP  = (g_travelHi > 0.0 && g_travelLo > 0.0) ? (g_travelHi - g_travelLo) : 0.0;
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("DAY QUALITY: efficiency %.3f | %.0f pts moved for %.1f pts of range = %.1f:1 churn",
                                erP, g_travelPts, rngP,
                                (rngP > 0.01 ? g_travelPts / rngP : 0.0)),
              (erP >= T_ErFullSize ? okC : (erP <= T_ErDeadSize ? badC : dimC)));
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("PEAKS: %d trades over 0.50, %d over 1, %d over 2, %d over 5 (of %d)",
                                g_pkHist50, g_pkHist100, g_pkHist200, g_pkHist500, g_pkHistN),
              dimC);
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("WENT GREEN THEN CLOSED RED: %d trades, %s %s of peak thrown away",
                                g_pkLostN, QSP_Sgn(g_pkLostCash, 2), ccy),
              (g_pkLostCash > 0 ? badC : dimC));
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("BIGGEST SINGLE GIVE-BACK %.1f pts, closed by %s",
                                g_ldgTopMiss, (g_ldgTopWhy == "" ? "nothing yet" : g_ldgTopWhy)),
              (g_ldgMiss > 0 ? badC : dimC));
   }

   // (v18.00) simplified on his ask. "peak avail" was the sum of the best price
   // every trade reached -- i.e. what was on the table. One line now says it in
   // plain words: we kept X% of what the trades offered.
   if(mfeN > 0)
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("kept %.0f%% of %s offered  green %d/%d",
                                capPct, QSP_Sgn(mfeSum, 1), green, mfeN),
              (capPct >= 50 ? okC : badC));
   if(openN > 0)
      QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("open %d  float %s pts %s %s", openN,
                                QSP_Sgn(openPts, 2), QSP_Sgn(openCash, 2), ccy), dimC);

   // ── WHICH EXIT IS KILLING THE MOVES ──────────────────────────────────────
   // Ranked by points left on the table, because that is the quantity that
   // matters, not how often a path fires. "~TAG" rows are SUPPRESSED families:
   // count only, so the cost of switching them off stays visible.
   if(g_xUsed > 0)
   {
      QSP_Row(r++, "-- EXIT ATTRIBUTION --------------", dimC);
      int ord[QSP_TAGS];
      for(int a = 0; a < g_xUsed; a++) ord[a] = a;
      for(int a = 0; a < g_xUsed - 1; a++)
         for(int b = a + 1; b < g_xUsed; b++)
         {
            double la = g_xPeak[ord[a]] - g_xPts[ord[a]];
            double lb = g_xPeak[ord[b]] - g_xPts[ord[b]];
            if(lb > la) { int sw = ord[a]; ord[a] = ord[b]; ord[b] = sw; }
         }
      int shown = 0;
      for(int a = 0; a < g_xUsed && shown < 6 && r < QSP_ROWS - 5; a++)
      {
         int  q = ord[a];
         bool sup = (StringGetCharacter(g_xTag[q], 0) == '~');
         if(sup) QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("%-13s %2d suppressed", g_xTag[q], g_xN[q]), dimC);
         else    QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("%-13s %2d %6s pts  lost %s", g_xTag[q], g_xN[q],
                                           QSP_Sgn(g_xPts[q], 1),
                                           QSP_Sgn(g_xPts[q] - g_xPeak[q], 1)), InpPanelInk);
         shown++;
      }
   }

   // ── DAILY GOAL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   // Two independent goals; either can be off. The points goal is the honest one
   // (lot-independent). The money goal is quoted at the flat reference lot, so it
   // does not move when the ladder changes size.
   bool goalOn  = (InpPanelGoalPts > 0.0 || InpPanelGoalCcy > 0.0);
   bool ptsHit  = (InpPanelGoalPts > 0.0) && (net     >= InpPanelGoalPts);
   bool ccyHit  = (InpPanelGoalCcy > 0.0) && (flatCcy >= InpPanelGoalCcy);
   bool metAll  = ((InpPanelGoalPts <= 0.0) || ptsHit) && ((InpPanelGoalCcy <= 0.0) || ccyHit);
   if(goalOn)
   {
      if(metAll) QSP_Row(r++, "*** DAILY GOAL MET ***", okC);
   }

   // (v18.00) TARGET LADDER, his ask. Money at the flat reference lot, so the rung
   // you are on does not move when the ladder resizes the position.
   double rung[4]; rung[0] = InpTarget1; rung[1] = InpTarget2;
   rung[2] = InpTarget3; rung[3] = InpTarget4;
   string lad = "";
   int hitTop = 0;
   for(int q = 0; q < 4; q++)
   {
      if(rung[q] <= 0) continue;
      bool hit = (flatCcy >= rung[q]);
      if(hit) hitTop = (int)rung[q];
      lad += StringFormat("%s%d%s ", hit ? "[" : " ", (int)rung[q], hit ? "]" : " ");
   }
   QSP_Row(r++, "-- TARGETS (" + ccy + ") ---------------", dimC);
   QSP_Row(r++, lad, (hitTop > 0) ? okC : InpPanelInk);

   // ALL-TIME, since the account opened
   QSP_Row(r++, "-- SINCE ATTACHED ----------------", dimC);
   QSP_Row(r++, StringFormat("%d trades   %s pts   %s %s", g_atN, QSP_Sgn(g_atPts, 1),
                             QSP_Sgn(g_atCash, 2), ccy),
           (g_atCash >= 0) ? okC : badC);
   // (v18.00) the "EA lock +500%" row is gone -- at the default it was a number
   // that can never be reached and it told him nothing. Only the halt STATE is
   // worth a line, and only when it is not normal.
   if(g_locked)    QSP_Row(r++, "PROFIT LOCK ACTIVE - no new trades", okC);
   else if(g_halt) QSP_Row(r++, "DAILY LOSS HALT - no new trades", badC);
   for(; r < QSP_ROWS; r++) QSP_Row(r, "", dimC);

   // ══════════════ v18.73 (M) BUG FIX — THE NET BOX WAS NOT LIVE ══════════════
   // His report: "the net and profit box don't update quickly / are slow." It is
   // not slow -- it was never live. `net` and `realCash` are built from the loop
   // above, which `continue`s on stillOpen, so both are CLOSED positions only.
   // openPts/openCash were already computed 40 lines up and were only ever used
   // for the separate "open ... float" row. So while a trade ran, the corner box
   // did not move at all; it jumped at close. That is exactly the symptom of
   // "13 quid in profit" never appearing anywhere he was looking.
   // No new quantity is invented here -- it passes the two that already exist.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_NetBoxLive = false (reverts to closed-only).
   // ══════ (v18.98 BG) THE NET BOX NOW READS THE LEDGER ══════
   // Reported dead five times. `net` and `realCash` above come from QSP_Recompute's
   // 7-day HistorySelect() rebuild, which returns SILENTLY if the history read
   // fails -- leaving the last drawn objects untouched, which on screen is
   // indistinguishable from a frozen box. The ledger counts the same money as each
   // deal closes, with no history scan and nothing that can fail quietly.
   // T_NetBoxFromLedger = false restores the old source if the two ever disagree.
   // (v19.02 BK) HIS SCREENSHOT: the header read "0 trades 0-0 0% win / NET +0.00"
   // while the ledger row directly beneath it read "-8.61 GBP from 57 closed trades"
   // and the footer read "56 trades". Three counters, three answers. The header is
   // the 7-day HistorySelect() rebuild and it found NOTHING -- which is the frozen
   // NET box, proven, not inferred. The ledger and the corner box are both correct.
   // Everything below now reads the ledger.
   double boxPts  = T_NetBoxFromLedger ? g_ldgPts  : net;
   double boxCash = T_NetBoxFromLedger ? g_ldgCash : realCash;
   color  boxInk  = T_NetBoxFromLedger
                    ? (boxCash > 0 ? okC : (boxCash < 0 ? badC : InpPanelInk))
                    : netC;
   if(T_NetBoxLive) QSP_NetCorner(boxPts + openPts, boxCash + openCash, ccy, boxInk);
   else             QSP_NetCorner(boxPts, boxCash, ccy, boxInk);

   // ══════════ v18.75 (Z) STOP GUESSING AT THE NET BOX ══════════
   // He has reported "net/profit box not updating" four times. I have patched two
   // printers (the corner box and the in-panel NET row) from reading the code, and
   // it is still wrong, which means my READING is wrong somewhere. So instead of a
   // third blind patch, this prints the exact numbers the panel is drawing, every
   // refresh, to the Experts log.
   // That splits the problem in two, which a screenshot of a frozen box cannot:
   //   - numbers MOVE in the log but not on screen  -> the drawing is broken
   //     (object not redrawn / off-screen / wrong corner / font colour)
   //   - numbers are FROZEN in the log too           -> the computation is broken
   //     (the history scan, the day filter, or openPts/openCash)
   // It costs one Print per refresh and nothing else. Turn it off once fixed.
   // OFF-SWITCH: T_LogPanelNet = false.
   if(T_LogPanelNet)
      PrintFormat("[PANEL] closed_pts=%.2f closed_cash=%.2f | open_n=%d open_pts=%.2f "
                  "open_cash=%.2f | BOX_SHOWS pts=%.2f cash=%.2f | live=%s refresh=%ds",
                  net, realCash, openN, openPts, openCash,
                  T_NetBoxLive ? net + openPts : net,
                  T_NetBoxLive ? realCash + openCash : realCash,
                  T_NetBoxLive ? "yes" : "no", InpPanelRefreshS);
   ChartRedraw(0);
}

void PanelInit()
{
   g_qspAttach = TimeCurrent();
   g_qspRowY = InpPanelFont + 7;
   ArrayInitialize(g_qspDir, 0);
   ArrayInitialize(g_qspTk, 0);
   ArrayInitialize(g_qspPeak, 0);
   g_qspFoldN = 0; g_qspFoldDay = 0;
   if(!InpStatsPanel) return;

   string bg = QSP_PFX + "BG";
   if(ObjectFind(0, bg) < 0) ObjectCreate(0, bg, OBJ_RECTANGLE_LABEL, 0, 0, 0);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_CORNER, CORNER_LEFT_UPPER);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_XDISTANCE, InpPanelX);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_YDISTANCE, InpPanelY);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_XSIZE, 20 + InpPanelFont * 25);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_YSIZE, 14 + QSP_ROWS * g_qspRowY);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_BGCOLOR, InpPanelBg);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_BORDER_TYPE, BORDER_FLAT);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_COLOR, C'60,70,85');
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_BACK, false);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_SELECTABLE, false);
   ObjectSetInteger(0, bg, OBJPROP_HIDDEN, true);
   QSP_Recompute();
}

// (v18.04) his ask: NET, bottom-right, nothing else. Separate object so the main
// panel can stay top-left. Measured in the lots actually traded (realised cash),
// with the points figure beside it.
void QSP_NetCorner(double netPts, double cash, string ccy, color ink)
{
   string nm = QSP_PFX + "NET";
   if(ObjectFind(0, nm) < 0)
   {
      ObjectCreate(0, nm, OBJ_LABEL, 0, 0, 0);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_CORNER, CORNER_RIGHT_LOWER);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_ANCHOR, ANCHOR_RIGHT_LOWER);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_XDISTANCE, 14);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_YDISTANCE, 14);
      ObjectSetString (0, nm, OBJPROP_FONT, "Consolas");
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_FONTSIZE, InpPanelFont + InpNetFontBoost);   // (v18.07)
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_SELECTABLE, false);
      ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_HIDDEN, true);
   }
   ObjectSetInteger(0, nm, OBJPROP_COLOR, ink);
   ObjectSetString (0, nm, OBJPROP_TEXT,
      StringFormat("NET  %s %s   (%s pts)", QSP_Sgn(cash, 2), ccy, QSP_Sgn(netPts, 1)));
}

void PanelDeinit()
{
   ObjectsDeleteAll(0, QSP_PFX);
}

void PanelTick()
{
   if(!InpStatsPanel) return;
   // (v18.69 B) only sample here when the top-of-tick sampler is switched off,
   // so the peak is never counted twice in one tick.
   if(!T_TrackPeaksAlways) QSP_TrackLive();
   QSP_AllTime();                         // cached, 60s
   static datetime last = 0;
   int gap = (InpPanelRefreshS < 1) ? 1 : InpPanelRefreshS;
   if(TimeCurrent() - last < gap) return;
   last = TimeCurrent();
   QSP_Recompute();
}

// ══════════ v18.73 (R) BASKET GIVEBACK — HIS BIGGEST UNADDRESSED COMPLAINT ══════════
// "WE NEED TO LOOK AT TOTAL PROFITS WHEN STACKING WE KEEP MAKING LOSS not every
//  trade will continue going up some make a pound profit some 20 it's just how we
//  CLOSE."
// He is right and there was NO basket-level logic in this file at all -- I grepped
// for it. Every protective mechanism (ratchet, hard lock, micro-lock, breakeven)
// arms off ONE POSITION'S OWN ENTRY. On a stack of fills spread across several
// points of entry that produces exactly the failure he describes:
//   20 Aug 21:58:42 -- nine 0.02 fills, entries 4524.71-4524.85. Price ran, the
//   best-entry fills armed, the worst-entry fills never reached their arm level,
//   and when it reversed all nine went to -4.4 each. The BASKET had been green.
// A per-position arm cannot see the basket. This can: it tracks the peak of TOTAL
// floating money and closes everything when the give-back exceeds the noise band
// converted to money at the CURRENT basket size.
// DERIVATION -- no invented number. The threshold is the same noise band every
// other lock in this build now uses (spread + T_NoiseFloorAtr*ATR), priced in
// money: band_pts * GBP_per_point_per_lot * total_lots.
//   21 Aug 16:54:52 stack, 0.03 lots: peak 7.03pts = GBP15.47, band 2.05pts on
//   0.03 lots = GBP4.51 -> closes near GBP11 instead of the GBP8.79 he got.
//   His "+13 -> -7" report at ~0.08 lots: band-money = GBP12.05, so it would have
//   closed near GBP1 instead of -GBP7. Swing ~GBP8.
// HONEST LIMIT: whether a LARGE stack should use a TIGHTER price retrace than a
// small one (because the same price move costs N times the money) is UNMEASURED.
// I have not scaled it, because I cannot derive the scaling from his tickets.
// T_BasketMinPeakCcy stops it acting on noise-sized baskets.
// OFF-SWITCH: T_BasketGiveback = false.
void BasketGivebackGuard()
{
   if(!T_BasketGiveback) return;
   static double pk = 0.0;
   double tot = 0.0, lots = 0.0;
   int n = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      if(!PositionSelectByTicket(PositionGetTicket(i))) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
      tot  += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
      lots += PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      n++;
   }
   if(n < 2 || lots <= 0.0) { pk = 0.0; return; }   // basket = 2+ fills, by definition
   if(tot > pk) pk = tot;
   if(pk < T_BasketMinPeakCcy) return;

   double atrB  = Buf(hAtr[E_SCALP], 0, 1);
   double bandP = NoiseFloorPts(atrB);
   if(bandP <= 0.0) return;
   double perPt = LossPerLot(1.0) * lots;              // money per 1 pt across the basket
   double allow = bandP * perPt;
   if(allow <= 0.0) return;

   if((pk - tot) >= allow)
   {
      PrintFormat("[BASKET] giveback %.2f >= band %.2f (%.2f pts x %.2f lots). "
                  "Peak was %.2f, now %.2f across %d fills. Closing the basket — "
                  "per-position arms cannot see this.", pk - tot, allow, bandP, lots, pk, tot, n);
      for(int j = PositionsTotal() - 1; j >= 0; j--)
      {
         ulong tkB = PositionGetTicket(j);
         if(!PositionSelectByTicket(tkB)) continue;
         if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
         if(EngineOf(PositionGetInteger(POSITION_MAGIC)) < 0) continue;
         CloseTagged("BASKET-GIVEBACK", tkB);
      }
      pk = 0.0;
   }
}

//================================================================
//  (v18.76) LIVE MARGIN WATCH — the guard that only existed pre-send
//================================================================
// See the note at T_LiveMarginWatch for why this exists and what it costs.
// NO TYPED THRESHOLD. The test is: if price moved T_LiveMarginBands x the noise
// band further against every losing open position, would free margin go below
// zero? If yes, we choose the exit instead of letting the broker choose it.
// It closes ONE position per pass -- the worst loser, which is the same one the
// broker would take first -- then re-measures on the next tick. It never touches
// a position that is in profit while any losing position is still open.
double MW_NoiseBandPts()
{
   // same accessor guard X uses at line ~8674, so the band is identical
   double a = Buf(hAtr[E_SCALP], 0, 1);
   double n = NoiseFloorPts(a);
   if(n <= 0.0) n = SpreadUSD() + 1.50;      // T_LegNoisePts fallback, same as NoiseFloorPts
   return n;
}

void LiveMarginWatch()
{
   if(!T_LiveMarginWatch) return;
   double free = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_MARGIN_FREE);
   double eq   = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(eq <= 0.0) return;

   double band = MW_NoiseBandPts() * MathMax(T_LiveMarginBands, 0.0);
   if(band <= 0.0) return;

   // What one more adverse band would cost the OPEN BOOK, in account currency.
   // Only positions currently losing are counted as getting worse: a winner
   // moving against us is already inside its own floor.
   double adverse = 0.0;
   ulong  worstTk = 0; double worstPl = 0.0; int nOpen = 0;
   for(int i = PositionsTotal() - 1; i >= 0; i--)
   {
      ulong t = PositionGetTicket(i);
      if(t == 0 || !PositionSelectByTicket(t)) continue;
      if(PositionGetString(POSITION_SYMBOL) != _Symbol) continue;
      nOpen++;
      double vol = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_VOLUME);
      double pl  = PositionGetDouble(POSITION_PROFIT) + PositionGetDouble(POSITION_SWAP);
      // (v18.91 AR) BUG FIX. The comment four lines up says only LOSING positions
      // are counted as getting worse. The code counted every open position,
      // winners included, so `adverse` was overstated and this could close the
      // worst trade while the book was in no danger at all. Code now matches the
      // stated intent. OFF-SWITCH: T_MarginWatchLosersOnly = false.
      if(!T_MarginWatchLosersOnly || pl < 0.0)
         adverse += LossPerLot(band) * vol;
      if(pl < worstPl) { worstPl = pl; worstTk = t; }
   }
   if(nOpen == 0 || worstTk == 0) return;      // nothing open, or nothing losing

   if((free - adverse) >= 0.0) return;         // one more band still fits. Do nothing.

   PrintFormat("[MARGIN WATCH] free margin %.2f, equity %.2f, %d positions open. One more %.2f pt band "
               "against the book costs %.2f, which puts free margin at %.2f. Closing the worst position "
               "(#%I64u, %.2f) BEFORE the broker chooses. This is not an opinion about the trade.",
               free, eq, nOpen, band, adverse, free - adverse, worstTk, worstPl);
   CloseTagged("MARGIN-WATCH", worstTk);
}

void OnTick()
{
   // (v18.69 B) PEAK FIRST, BEFORE ANY EXIT VOTES. See the note at
   // T_TrackPeaksAlways. This used to run last, inside PanelTick(), behind the
   // panel's on/off switch -- so the peak-relative gates read a stale or zero
   // peak, or died entirely when the panel was off.
   if(T_TrackPeaksAlways) QSP_TrackLive();
   LedgerTravel();   // (v18.98 BF) how far the market moved
   LedgerBasket();   // (v18.94 BA) the total, sampled every tick

   // (v18.76) before any exit vote and before any entry: if the book cannot
   // survive one more noise band, we pick the exit rather than the broker.
   LiveMarginWatch();

   // (v18.73 R) basket giveback, immediately after the peak sampler and before any
   // per-position exit vote — so the stack's own total is checked while it is still
   // green, not after the individual arms have failed to see it.
   BasketGivebackGuard();

   UpdateShadows();   // (v16.26) track what the SKIPPED signals would have made
   MqlDateTime g; TimeToStruct(TimeGMT(), g);
   if(g.day != g_lastDay)
   {
      g_lastDay  = g.day;
      // BUG FIX #21: this snapshotted EQUITY, which includes the floating P/L of
      // any position still open across the day roll. If a trade was $30 underwater
      // at midnight, the whole day's loss limit was measured from an already-
      // depressed baseline — so the "6% daily stop" silently became a 6% stop on
      // top of a loss you had already taken. BALANCE is the honest baseline: it
      // is realised money and does not move with open trades.
      g_dayStart = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_BALANCE);
      g_dayPnl   = 0;
      g_halt = false; g_locked = false;
      // BUG FIX #8: this used to clear Stat[e].disabled every single day. The
      // auto-disable is EXPECTANCY based and expectancy is CUMULATIVE — so an
      // engine proven to be bleeding got resurrected every morning and the
      // whole safety layer was decorative. Disabled now means disabled until
      // you restart the EA. Only the short consec-loss PAUSE resets daily.
      for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++) Stat[e].pauseUntil = 0;
      // (v16.22 #10) reset per-regime learning each day — it should adapt to
      // TODAY's tape, not average across weeks where conditions differ.
      for(int r = 0; r < 3; r++) { g_regR[r] = 0.0; g_regN[r] = 0; }
      Print("--- new day ---");
   }

   double eq = AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY);
   if(g_dayStart > 0)
   {
      // ══════════════════ BUG FIX #19 (SEVERE) ══════════════════
      // g_halt only made EngineReady() return false — i.e. it stopped NEW
      // market entries. It did nothing at all about the ORB stop orders and
      // SWEEP limits already RESTING ON THE BROKER. Those would still fill,
      // happily, after the daily loss limit had been breached.
      //
      // Your "hard daily stop" could be walked straight through by up to six
      // pending orders that were placed before it triggered. Now the halt
      // sweeps the book.
      if(InpDailyHaltEnable && eq <= g_dayStart * (1.0 - InpMaxDailyLossPct/100.0) && !g_halt)
      {
         g_halt = true;
         Print("DAILY LOSS LIMIT HIT. Halting, and cancelling every resting order.");
         KillAllPendings();
      }
      if(eq >= g_dayStart * (1.0 + InpMaxDailyProfitPct/100.0) && !g_locked)
      {
         g_locked = true;
         Print("DAILY PROFIT LOCKED. Cancelling resting orders — you have made your day.");
         KillAllPendings();
      }
   }

   // management runs every tick. Entries only on a new bar.
   UpdateSpreadAvg();
   for(int e = 0; e < ENG_COUNT; e++) ManageEngine(e);
   StopForensics();              // (v16) delivers the shakeout/reversal verdict on recorded losses — cheap: skips records whose 10-bar window isn't up
   Diagnostics();                // "why am I not trading?" — answered, every 5 min
   EnforceNoOpposingPendings();  // BUG FIX #5: pendings were bypassing the direction guard
   MonitorManual();
   ReadPineSignals();

   static datetime lastM3 = 0, lastM5 = 0, lastM15 = 0;
   datetime b3  = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M3, 0);
   datetime b5  = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M5, 0);
   datetime b15 = iTime(_Symbol, PERIOD_M15, 0);


   bool newM3  = (b3  != lastM3);
   bool newM5  = (b5  != lastM5);
   bool newM15 = (b15 != lastM15);
   if(newM3)  lastM3  = b3;
   if(newM5)  lastM5  = b5;
   if(newM15) lastM15 = b15;

   if(newM5)  RefreshStops();

   // Publish this instance's own open risk so sibling instances (Silver,
   // GBPJPY charts running the same EA) can see the true combined total.
   if(InpMultiSymbolCap) PublishMyRisk();

   // ═══════════════════ NEWS-SPIKE PROTECTION ═══════════════════
   // An existing open trade was never protected from the spike itself —
   // N_CloseOpen was a dead input that did nothing. Now it actually flattens
   // the book before a CPI/NFP/FOMC print, using the same N_BeforeMin buffer,
   // so you are flat BEFORE the spread blows out and price jumps, not caught inside it.
   if(N_CloseOpen && InNewsWindow())
      NewsFlatten();

   // ═══════════════════ THREE ENGINES, ONE DIRECTION ═══════════════════
   // They do not compete. They are the SAME trade at three speeds:
   //
   //   SCALP (M1-M15) : takes the small pushes inside the move, many times a day
   //   TREND (M5)     : takes the pullback, holds for 4.5R
   //   APEX  (M15)    : takes the whole move, no target, rides until it dies
   //
   // When gold trends, all three are on the same side at different horizons — so
   // the profits STACK instead of cancelling. That is the whole design.
   Engine_Scalp();               // scans M1+M3+M5+M15 itself, every tick
   Engine_Trend();                // scans M1+M3+M5 itself, every tick — same pattern as Scalp
   if(newM15) Engine_Apex();

   PanelTick();   // (v17.97) daily stats panel — last, so it reflects this tick
}
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
